// AUTO-GENERATED authored deep overlay for the test screen: concept-from-scratch +
// why EVERY wrong option is wrong, for the constructed mocks that had only one-line explanations
// (pep 1/2, MET mock 1/3/4, diagnostic, bio, nimhans). Keyed by mock id -> ordered array
// index-matched to data-all.js (stem-guarded in explainHTML).
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"pep-mock-1":[{"q":"Which ministry is the Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) under?","concept":"The Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) began in 1986 as a registered society and became a statutory body through the Rehabilitation Council of India Act, 1992 (RCI Act 1992). An amendment in 2000 widened its role to include promoting research in rehabilitation and special education. Its jobs are to regulate training programmes for rehabilitation professionals, recognise qualifications (such as the M.Phil and the Professional Diploma in Clinical Psychology), and maintain the Central Rehabilitation Register (CRR) of qualified professionals. Because the RCI deals with disability and rehabilitation, it sits under the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment, within its Department of Empowerment of Persons with Disabilities (Divyangjan). The same department administers the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RPwD) Act 2016 and the National Trust Act 1999. Bodies under the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare include the National Medical Commission (NMC Act 2019), NIMHANS and the Central Mental Health Authority set up under the Mental Healthcare Act (MHCA) 2017. The Ministry of Education oversees the UGC and NCERT, and the Ministry of Labour and Employment runs employment and social security schemes. An easy rule: medical councils follow Health, the disability and rehabilitation council follows Social Justice. This is why an Indian clinical psychologist, though working in hospitals and named as a mental health professional in the MHCA 2017, is registered through a disability-focused body rather than a health one. Option d is right because the RCI is a statutory body under the Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment, through its Department of Empowerment of Persons with Disabilities.","traps":{"a":"The Ministry of Education oversees school and higher education through bodies such as the University Grants Commission (UGC), NCERT and the National Council for Teacher Education. It tempts because the RCI recognises university courses and special-education teacher training such as the B.Ed in Special Education, so it looks like an education regulator. But its parent is the disability department, and RCI course recognition is separate from UGC approval.","b":"The Ministry of Health and Family Welfare controls the National Medical Commission, NIMHANS, the National Mental Health Programme (1982) and the machinery of the Mental Healthcare Act 2017, such as the Central and State Mental Health Authorities. It is the most tempting distractor because clinical psychologists work in hospitals and are named as mental health professionals in the MHCA 2017. But the RCI itself is not a health ministry body.","c":"The Ministry of Labour and Employment handles employment, wages, industrial relations and social security bodies such as ESIC and EPFO. Its link to disability is limited to employment services for persons with disabilities. It tempts because 'rehabilitation' can sound like the vocational rehabilitation of workers, but the RCI regulates the training and registration of professionals, which is not a labour function."}},{"q":"Q) Who coined the term “mental tests”?","concept":"James McKeen Cattell (1890) coined the phrase 'mental tests' in his paper 'Mental Tests and Measurements', published in the journal Mind. Cattell had earned his doctorate under Wilhelm Wundt (who founded the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879) and then worked with Francis Galton in England, so he combined Wundt's laboratory precision with Galton's interest in individual differences. Galton had already measured thousands of people in his Anthropometric Laboratory (1884) in London, assuming that keen senses meant a keen intellect. Cattell's ten tests followed the same logic: grip strength, rate of movement, two-point sensation threshold, pressure causing pain, weight discrimination, reaction time to sound, colour naming, line bisection, judging ten seconds of time, and memory span for letters. The approach collapsed when Clark Wissler (1901), Cattell's own student at Columbia, found that these scores barely correlated with college grades or with each other. The real turn came from Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905), whose scale sampled higher processes such as judgement, comprehension and reasoning; the 1908 revision introduced mental age. William Stern (1912) proposed dividing mental age by chronological age, Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied this by 100 as the IQ of the Stanford-Binet, and David Wechsler (1939) replaced the ratio with the deviation IQ. Examiners like to ask who coined 'mental tests' (Cattell), who is the father of psychometrics (Galton), and who built the first practical intelligence test (Binet). Option b is right because Cattell introduced the term 'mental tests' in his 1890 paper.","traps":{"a":"Alfred Binet, with Theodore Simon, produced the first practical intelligence test in 1905 to identify French schoolchildren who needed special education, and introduced mental age in the 1908 revision. His items tested reasoning and judgement rather than the senses. It tempts because Binet is the best-known name in early testing, but the phrase 'mental tests' was already in print in Cattell's 1890 paper.","c":"David Wechsler published the Wechsler-Bellevue scale in 1939, followed by the WISC (1949) and the WAIS (1955). He defined intelligence as the global capacity to act purposefully, think rationally and deal effectively with the environment, and replaced the ratio IQ with the deviation IQ (mean 100, SD 15). It tempts because his scales dominate clinical testing today, but he came half a century after the term.","d":"Lewis Terman of Stanford University revised the Binet-Simon scale into the Stanford-Binet (1916), popularised the IQ (mental age divided by chronological age, times 100) and began a famous longitudinal study of gifted children in 1921. It tempts because he turned testing into a mass movement in America, but he adapted an existing test and borrowed the IQ idea from William Stern rather than coining 'mental tests'."}},{"q":"Q) Who was the psychologist that introduced the term scholar-professional for the new model of training?","concept":"Models of clinical psychology training answer one question: how far should a clinician be a researcher and how far a practitioner. The first formal answer came from the Boulder Conference (1949) in Colorado, which adopted the scientist-practitioner model, built on the 1947 training report of a committee chaired by David Shakow: the clinician earns a PhD, is trained equally in research and practice, and uses science to guide therapy. Critics felt this produced researchers who did little practice and practitioners who did little research. Gordon F. Derner of Adelphi University in New York argued for a professional model whose main aim is skilled practice informed by scholarship rather than original research, and introduced the term scholar-professional for it. The Adelphi programme is often described as the first university-based professional school of psychology. The idea was endorsed at the Vail Conference (1973), which approved the practitioner-scholar model and the PsyD degree; the first PsyD programme began at the University of Illinois in 1968 under Donald Peterson, and the first freestanding professional school, the California School of Professional Psychology, opened in 1969. A later reaction, the clinical scientist model, put research first and is linked to Richard McFall's 'Manifesto for a Science of Clinical Psychology' (1991). For variants, remember the pairs: Boulder, scientist-practitioner, PhD; Vail, practitioner-scholar, PsyD; Derner, scholar-professional; McFall, clinical scientist; Lightner Witmer (1896), first psychological clinic. Option a is right because Gordon Derner introduced the term scholar-professional for the professional model of training.","traps":{"b":"Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, with landmarks such as The Interpretation of Dreams (1900) and the structural model of id, ego and superego (1923). Analysts were trained through personal analysis and supervision in psychoanalytic institutes, but Freud played no part in the American debate on university training models. It tempts only because he is the most famous name in clinical work and feels like a safe guess for anything about therapy.","c":"'F. Skinner' is B. F. Skinner, who developed radical behaviourism and operant conditioning, set out in The Behavior of Organisms (1938), and introduced schedules of reinforcement and the operant chamber. His work fed into behaviour therapy and applied behaviour analysis, not into naming training models for clinicians. It tempts because behavioural methods are a large part of clinical training, and the truncated name may confuse candidates.","d":"Carl Gustav Jung founded analytical psychology after breaking with Freud around 1913, and introduced the collective unconscious, archetypes, complexes and the introversion and extraversion attitudes in Psychological Types (1921). He wrote nothing on scholar-professional university education. It tempts because, like Freud, he is a famous clinical theorist whose name feels right for any question about therapists and their training."}},{"q":"Q) Which Act mandates all rehabilitation professionals to be registered with the RCI?","concept":"The Rehabilitation Council of India Act, 1992 turned the RCI, which had existed as a registered society since 1986, into a statutory body, and it is the source of the registration mandate. The Act empowers the RCI to regulate training courses, recognise qualifications and maintain the Central Rehabilitation Register (CRR). Its key rule is that a person without a recognised qualification and registration on the CRR may not practise as a rehabilitation professional, and doing so is a punishable offence. An amendment in 2000 added the promotion of research in rehabilitation and special education. Clinical psychologists are among the professionals it covers, so an Indian clinical psychologist needs an RCI-recognised M.Phil or equivalent and a CRR number. Examiners pair this with laws that look similar. The Mental Health Act 1987 (misnamed in one option) replaced the Indian Lunacy Act 1912 and dealt with admission procedures and licensing of psychiatric hospitals. It was repealed by the Mental Healthcare Act (MHCA) 2017, which is rights-based, effectively decriminalised attempted suicide (Section 115), introduced advance directives and nominated representatives, and defines a clinical psychologist through RCI-recognised qualifications. The Persons with Disabilities Act 1995 was replaced by the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RPwD) Act 2016, which raised the recognised disabilities from 7 to 21. So the MHCA 2017 relies on RCI qualifications, but only the RCI Act creates the duty to register. Option d is right because the RCI Act 1992 makes registration with the RCI compulsory for anyone practising as a rehabilitation professional.","traps":{"a":"The Mental Healthcare Act 2017 is a rights-based law under the Health ministry: right to access mental healthcare, advance directives, nominated representatives, Mental Health Review Boards and the effective decriminalisation of attempted suicide. It names clinical psychologists as mental health professionals by referring to RCI-recognised qualifications, which is why it tempts. But it borrows the RCI standard; it does not create the duty to register.","b":"The Persons with Disabilities (Equal Opportunities, Protection of Rights and Full Participation) Act 1995 recognised seven disabilities, including mental illness and mental retardation, and provided for education, job reservation and a Chief Commissioner for Persons with Disabilities. It tempts because it is a disability law under the same ministry as the RCI. But it came after the RCI Act, did not set up registration, and was replaced by the RPwD Act 2016.","c":"The law of 1987 was the Mental Health Act 1987, so this option's title is itself wrong. That Act replaced the Indian Lunacy Act 1912, regulated the licensing of psychiatric hospitals and procedures for voluntary and involuntary admission, and was repealed by the MHCA 2017. It tempts because candidates remember a mental health law from the 1980s, but it said nothing about registering rehabilitation professionals."}},{"q":"Q) How do clinicians tend to conceive assessment information?","concept":"When a clinician gets a test result, the question is what the result stands for. Norman D. Sundberg and Leona E. Tyler (1962) described three ways clinicians conceive assessment information: as samples, correlates or signs, and the three differ in how much inference they need. A sample treats the test behaviour as a small piece of the very behaviour you want to know about. A typing test samples typing; observing a child's tantrums in class samples tantrums. Inference is minimal, and this is the logic of behavioural assessment. A correlate treats the score as something shown statistically to go with an outside criterion, whether or not the content looks related. The empirical keying of the MMPI by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) is the classic case: an item is scored on the depression scale simply because depressed patients answered it differently from controls. A sign treats the response as an indicator of an underlying, unobserved state or trait, such as reading Rorschach responses as signs of thought disorder or ego strength. This is the most inferential level and typical of projective and psychodynamic assessment. Marvin Goldfried and Ronald Kent (1972) used the same contrast to separate behavioural assessment (sample) from traditional personality assessment (sign). A neighbouring idea is the comparison of clinical and statistical (actuarial) prediction by Paul Meehl (1954), who showed that formula-based prediction usually equals or beats clinical judgement. Option d is right because clinicians conceive assessment information as samples, correlates or signs.","traps":{"a":"Factual, hypothetical or imaginary is not a recognised framework in assessment. It sounds like a scale of how real or certain information is, and 'hypothetical' echoes the real practice of forming working hypotheses from test data. That echo is why it tempts. But the standard triad from Norman Sundberg and Leona Tyler (1962) sorts information by level of inference (sample, correlate, sign), not by how true it is.","b":"Predictions, estimates and suggestions describe what a clinician does with data after interpreting it, for example predicting relapse or suggesting a treatment. Prediction is central to the clinical versus statistical prediction debate started by Paul Meehl (1954), which makes this option sound academic. It tempts for that reason, but it describes the outputs of assessment, not the ways the information itself is conceived.","c":"Examples, prototypes and categories come from concept formation in cognitive psychology, especially the prototype theory of Eleanor Rosch (1975), in which a category is organised around its best example. Prototype matching does appear in diagnosis, which is why it tempts. But it describes how diagnostic categories are formed and recognised, not how individual pieces of test information are interpreted by the clinician."}},{"q":"Q) Which of the following affect factors do NOT affect the outward objective expression of immediate emotion?","concept":"The mental status examination separates mood from affect. Mood is the sustained, pervasive emotional state the patient experiences and reports in their own words ('I feel low'), described by its quality, intensity, persistence over days or weeks, and diurnal variation. Affect is the outward, observable expression of emotion during the interview, the moment-to-moment display the examiner sees in face, voice and posture. A common teaching analogy says mood is the climate and affect is the weather. Affect has several parameters. Quality is the dominant tone (euthymic, depressed, anxious, irritable, elated). Range is the variety of emotion shown: full, restricted, blunted or flat, where flat affect is a negative symptom of schizophrenia. Reactivity, or mobility, is how readily expression shifts with the conversation; rapid excessive shifts are labile affect, and failure to brighten is non-reactive affect. Intensity, stability, appropriateness to the situation and congruence with thought content are also rated. Examiners also test reactivity in depression: in DSM-5 (2013), mood reactivity, brightening in response to positive events, is the core of the atypical features specifier, while its loss marks melancholia. Persistence, by contrast, is about how long a state lasts, so it belongs to mood, the inner climate, and cannot describe a display seen in the next few seconds. Related terms include alexithymia, difficulty identifying and describing feelings, named by Peter Sifneos (1973). Option b is right because persistence describes the sustained internal state of mood, not the immediate outward expression of emotion that defines affect.","traps":{"a":"Quality of affect is the dominant emotional tone the examiner observes: euthymic, depressed, anxious, irritable, elated or perplexed. It is the first thing recorded under affect and is plainly part of the outward expression of immediate emotion, so it cannot be the exception. It tempts because 'quality' is also used to describe mood, and candidates who mix up mood and affect may pick it.","c":"Range of affect is the variety of emotional expression shown during the interview, graded as full or broad, restricted (constricted), blunted and flat. Flat affect, with almost no expression, is a classic negative symptom of schizophrenia. Range is observed directly, moment to moment, so it belongs to outward affect. It tempts because a blunted range can look like a long-standing trait rather than an immediate display.","d":"Reactivity is how readily emotional expression changes in response to the conversation or events, for example a depressed patient who smiles at a joke. Excessive reactivity is lability; absent reactivity points to melancholic depression in DSM-5. Although the option says 'of mood', reactivity is seen on the spot, so it describes outward expression. It tempts because the phrase 'of mood' makes it sound like a mood-only feature."}},{"q":"Which aspect of cognitive function is assessed by the Glasgow Coma Scale?","concept":"The Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) was published by Graham Teasdale and Bryan Jennett (1974) of Glasgow as a simple, reliable bedside way to grade level of consciousness, mainly after head injury. Consciousness has two parts: arousal or wakefulness, maintained by the ascending reticular activating system in the brainstem, and awareness, the content of consciousness, served by the cerebral hemispheres. The GCS scores three observed behaviours. Eye opening is scored 1 to 4 (spontaneous, to sound, to pain, none). Best verbal response is scored 1 to 5 (oriented, confused, inappropriate words, incomprehensible sounds, none). Best motor response is scored 1 to 6 (obeys commands, localises pain, withdraws, abnormal flexion, extension, none). Totals run from 3 to 15. A score of 13 to 15 indicates mild, 9 to 12 moderate and 3 to 8 severe head injury, and 8 or less is usually taken as coma. In the mental status examination consciousness is assessed first, because every later cognitive test assumes the patient is awake. Clinical labels move from alert to drowsy, obtunded, stuporous and comatose, and clouding of consciousness with fluctuating attention is the hallmark of delirium. Neighbouring domains have their own tests: attention and concentration by digit span and serial sevens, memory by immediate, recent and remote recall, and intelligence by standardised tests such as the Wechsler scales. Examiners may also ask about the AVPU scale (alert, voice, pain, unresponsive). Option a is right because the Glasgow Coma Scale measures level of consciousness through eye, verbal and motor responses.","traps":{"b":"Attention is the ability to focus on a stimulus and sustain that focus. In the MSE it is tested by digit span (a normal forward span is at least five digits), serial sevens, or spelling WORLD backwards. A patient can be fully conscious yet inattentive, as in mania or ADHD. It tempts because attention is impaired in delirium and closely tied to consciousness, but the GCS does not test it.","c":"Memory is assessed in the MSE as immediate retention (repeating digits or words at once), recent memory (recall of three objects after about five minutes, recent events) and remote memory (personal and historical facts). The Mini-Mental State Examination of Marshal Folstein and colleagues (1975) includes such items. It tempts because the GCS verbal score asks about orientation, which overlaps with memory, but recall is not scored.","d":"Intelligence is the general capacity to reason, learn and solve problems. It is estimated clinically from education, vocabulary and general knowledge, and measured formally by tests such as the Wechsler scales or, in India, Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests. It tempts because the GCS appears in lists of cognitive functions, but a coma scale says nothing about intellectual level; a highly intelligent person in coma scores 3."}},{"q":"What is assessed in the Insight category of the cognitive assessment?","concept":"Insight, in psychiatry, is the patient's awareness and understanding of their own illness. Aubrey Lewis (1934) defined it as 'a correct attitude to a morbid change in oneself'. Anthony David (1990) showed that insight has three separable dimensions: recognising that one has a mental illness, being able to relabel unusual experiences such as hallucinations as abnormal, and accepting treatment. In the mental status examination, insight is commonly graded in six levels, as in Kaplan and Sadock's textbook: complete denial of illness; slight awareness of being sick and needing help while denying it at the same time; awareness of being sick but blaming others, external factors or physical causes; awareness that the illness is due to something unknown in oneself; intellectual insight, where the patient admits the illness and its link to their own feelings but does not use this to change; and true emotional insight, which leads to change in behaviour. Poor insight is common in schizophrenia and mania and predicts poor treatment adherence. Insight is recorded near the end of the examination, next to judgement, the ability to assess a situation and act appropriately. Do not confuse it with anosognosia, unawareness of a neurological deficit such as hemiplegia, described by Joseph Babinski (1914). Rating scales include the Scale to Assess Unawareness of Mental Disorder by Xavier Amador and colleagues (1993) and the Beck Cognitive Insight Scale of Aaron T. Beck and colleagues (2004). Option c is right because insight refers to the degree of awareness a patient has regarding their own illness.","traps":{"a":"Orientation is awareness of time, place and person, tested by asking the date, the location, and who the patient and others are. Orientation to time is usually lost first and to person last, a useful pattern in delirium and dementia. It tempts because the word 'awareness' appears in both definitions, but orientation is awareness of one's surroundings, while insight is awareness of being ill.","b":"Level of consciousness is the degree of arousal and awareness, from alert through drowsy and stuporous to coma, graded objectively by the Glasgow Coma Scale of Graham Teasdale and Bryan Jennett (1974). It is assessed first in the cognitive examination, because all other tests depend on it. It tempts because consciousness and insight both sound like awareness, but a fully alert patient can still have no insight.","d":"Immediate retention and recall is the registration stage of memory, tested by asking the patient to repeat digits or three words at once and recall them a few minutes later. It reflects attention and short-term memory and sits under the memory heading of the MSE. It tempts as a filler because it is a cognitive function listed in the same section, but it has nothing to do with awareness of illness."}},{"q":"What is flight of ideas?","concept":"Flight of ideas is a formal thought disorder, a disturbance of the form or flow of thinking rather than its content. Thoughts come so fast that speech jumps from one idea to the next before any is finished, so the patient never reaches the goal of the original idea. Unlike the breakdowns seen in schizophrenia, the links between successive ideas can usually be followed: they are made through chance associations, rhymes and clang associations, puns, or things in the room that catch attention (distractibility). It is the classic thought disorder of mania and comes with pressure of speech; the subjective version is racing thoughts. DSM-5 (2013) lists flight of ideas or racing thoughts among the criterion B symptoms of a manic episode. A milder form in hypomania, where speech wanders but still gets there, is called prolixity. Examiners build variants from neighbouring terms. Circumstantiality is over-inclusion of unnecessary detail with eventual arrival at the point. Tangentiality departs from the question and never returns. Loosening of associations, the term of Eugen Bleuler (1911), means links the listener cannot follow, typical of schizophrenia. Perseveration is repetition of the same word or idea despite a new question, seen in organic brain disease. Poverty of speech is reduced spontaneous speech, seen in depression and, as alogia, in schizophrenia. The Thought, Language and Communication scale of Nancy Andreasen (1979) defines many of these. Option b is right because flight of ideas is a rapid shift from one idea to another without any of them being completed.","traps":{"a":"Repeating the same idea or word over and over, even when a new question is asked, is perseveration. It reflects difficulty in shifting mental set and is a classic sign of organic brain disease, especially frontal lobe damage and dementia. It tempts because both perseveration and flight of ideas are abnormalities of the flow of ideas, but perseveration is stuck on one idea, while flight of ideas races through many.","c":"Loading speech with irrelevant or unnecessary details before eventually reaching the point is circumstantiality. The goal is reached, but slowly and by a long detour. It is seen in epilepsy, obsessional personalities and some people with intellectual disability. It tempts because both involve speech that wanders from the point, but circumstantiality is slow and overdetailed, while flight of ideas is fast and never arrives.","d":"Lack of spontaneity in speech is poverty of speech: brief, unelaborated replies given only when asked. It is seen in depression with psychomotor retardation and, as alogia, among the negative symptoms of schizophrenia. It tempts as the paired opposite in a list of speech disturbances, but flight of ideas is overproductive and pressured, the very reverse of reduced spontaneous speech."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT included in the General Principles of the APA's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct?","concept":"The American Psychological Association (APA) published its first ethics code in 1953, and the current Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct was adopted in 2002, with amendments in 2010 and 2016. The Code has two parts. The General Principles are aspirational: they describe ideals psychologists should aim for and are not themselves enforceable rules. The Ethical Standards are enforceable and grouped into ten sections, from Resolving Ethical Issues and Competence through Privacy and Confidentiality, Research and Publication, Assessment and Therapy. There are exactly five General Principles, lettered A to E. A is Beneficence and Nonmaleficence: benefit those you work with and do no harm. B is Fidelity and Responsibility: establish trust, clarify roles, manage conflicts of interest and accept responsibility for one's conduct. C is Integrity: promote accuracy, honesty and truthfulness. D is Justice: all persons are entitled to equal access to and benefit from psychology, and psychologists guard against bias and the limits of their competence. E is Respect for People's Rights and Dignity: privacy, confidentiality, self-determination and sensitivity to cultural and individual differences. Equity is an everyday idea close to Justice, which is why it works as a distractor, but it is not the title of any principle. Neighbouring lists are tested too: Tom Beauchamp and James Childress (1979) gave four principles of biomedical ethics (autonomy, nonmaleficence, beneficence, justice), and Karen Kitchener (1984) proposed five for counselling psychology by adding fidelity. Option d is right because Equity is not one of the five General Principles; the fairness principle is called Justice.","traps":{"a":"Beneficence and Nonmaleficence is Principle A of the APA Code: psychologists strive to benefit those they work with, take care to do no harm, resolve conflicts among obligations in a way that minimises harm, and stay alert to how their own health may affect their work. It is genuinely in the list, so it cannot be the answer. It tempts because it pairs two ideas, which can make it look like an invented heading.","b":"Fidelity and Responsibility is Principle B: psychologists establish relationships of trust, uphold professional standards, clarify roles, manage conflicts of interest, cooperate with other professionals, and are encouraged to give some time for little or no fee. It is a real principle. It tempts because 'fidelity' is less familiar to students than terms like confidentiality or consent, so it may sound made up.","c":"Integrity is Principle C: psychologists promote accuracy, honesty and truthfulness, do not steal, cheat or misrepresent facts, keep their promises, and must weigh carefully any use of deception in research. It is a real principle. It tempts because integrity is also a common virtue word, and some candidates assume that a short one-word title, like Integrity or Equity, must be the odd one out."}},{"q":"Which General Principle emphasizes the importance of promoting accuracy, honesty, and truthfulness in the science, teaching, and practice of psychology?","concept":"The Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct of the American Psychological Association (adopted 2002, amended 2010 and 2016) opens with five aspirational General Principles, lettered A to E, followed by ten sections of enforceable Ethical Standards. Principle C, Integrity, is the honesty principle. Its text says psychologists seek to promote accuracy, honesty and truthfulness in the science, teaching and practice of psychology; they do not steal, cheat or engage in fraud, subterfuge or intentional misrepresentation of fact; and they keep their promises and avoid unwise or unclear commitments. It also covers deception: where deception may be ethically justified to maximise benefit, as in some research, psychologists must consider whether it is needed, weigh its consequences, and correct any mistrust it causes. The enforceable standards that put Integrity into practice include the rules on deception in research, debriefing, and not fabricating data or plagiarising. Variants give a principle's key words and ask for its name. Beneficence and Nonmaleficence (A): benefit, do no harm, safeguard welfare. Fidelity and Responsibility (B): trust, professional relationships, roles, conflicts of interest, responsibility to society. Integrity (C): accuracy, honesty, truthfulness. Justice (D): fairness, equal access and quality, guarding against bias. Respect for People's Rights and Dignity (E): privacy, confidentiality, self-determination, cultural and individual differences. The commonest confusion is between B and C, because trust sounds like honesty; trust concerns relationships and obligations, honesty concerns truthful information. Option c is right because Integrity is the principle that promotes accuracy, honesty and truthfulness in the science, teaching and practice of psychology.","traps":{"a":"Beneficence and Nonmaleficence (Principle A) asks psychologists to benefit those they work with and do no harm, safeguard the welfare and rights of clients and research participants, and guard against misuse of their influence. It is about welfare and harm, not truth. It tempts because it is the first and most familiar principle, and because harming people through deception sounds like a beneficence issue.","b":"Fidelity and Responsibility (Principle B) is about establishing relationships of trust, upholding professional standards, clarifying roles, managing conflicts of interest and accepting responsibility for one's behaviour. It is the strongest distractor, because 'fidelity' means faithfulness and sounds close to honesty. But its focus is trust and obligations within relationships and to society, while the truthfulness of information belongs to Integrity.","d":"Respect for People's Rights and Dignity (Principle E) concerns the worth of all people, their rights to privacy, confidentiality and self-determination, special safeguards for vulnerable persons, and awareness of differences such as age, gender, culture, religion, disability and language. It tempts because confidentiality and informed consent feel like honesty issues, but this principle protects rights and dignity, not the accuracy of information."}},{"q":"How does the deontological approach to ethics differ from the utilitarian perspective?","concept":"Ethical theories differ on what makes an action right. Deontological ethics, from the Greek deon (duty), holds that some acts are right or wrong in themselves, whatever their results. Its best-known form comes from Immanuel Kant (1785) in the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals: the categorical imperative says act only on a rule you could will to be a universal law, and treat people always as ends, never merely as means. On this view lying is wrong even if a lie would produce a good outcome. William David Ross (1930) softened this with prima facie duties, such as fidelity, non-maleficence and justice, which can conflict and must be weighed. Utilitarianism is the main consequentialist theory: an act is right if it produces the greatest good for the greatest number. Jeremy Bentham (1789) proposed a felicific calculus that adds up pleasures and pains, essentially a cost-benefit analysis, and John Stuart Mill (1861) distinguished higher from lower pleasures. Act utilitarianism judges each act; rule utilitarianism judges rules by their usual consequences. Clinical ethics uses both. Confidentiality and informed consent are duty-based, while breaking confidentiality to protect a third party, as after the Tarasoff ruling (1976) in California, rests on weighing harms and benefits. A third approach, virtue ethics from Aristotle (fourth century BCE), asks what a good person would do rather than which rule or outcome applies. Option b is right because deontology judges actions by their intrinsic rightness or wrongness, while utilitarianism judges them by a cost-benefit analysis of their consequences.","traps":{"a":"This option swaps the two theories. Cost-benefit analysis of outcomes is the core of utilitarianism (Jeremy Bentham, 1789; John Stuart Mill, 1861), while moral duties and obligations are the core of deontology (Immanuel Kant, 1785). It tempts because it uses all the right terms, and candidates who remember the key words but not which theory owns which can pick it in a hurry. Always check the pairing.","c":"Saying the two approaches are identical is plainly false. They are usually presented as the two main rival families of normative ethics: deontology is non-consequentialist and judges the act itself, while utilitarianism is consequentialist and judges only outcomes. They can disagree sharply, for example on whether to lie to save a life. It tempts only as a filler for someone who knows neither theory.","d":"Only deontology is built on moral duties and obligations. Utilitarianism has one overarching aim, to maximise overall good, and treats any rule as useful only if it serves that aim, as in rule utilitarianism. It tempts because rule utilitarianism follows rules that look like duties, and because both theories tell people what they ought to do. But the source of the ought differs: duty versus consequences."}},{"q":"What are the four pillars of principle-based ethics?","concept":"Principle-based ethics, also called principlism, comes from Tom L. Beauchamp and James F. Childress (1979) in Principles of Biomedical Ethics. They proposed four mid-level principles that people holding different moral theories can agree on and apply to clinical cases. Respect for autonomy means respecting a competent person's right to make informed decisions about their own life; it underlies informed consent, confidentiality and truth telling. Non-maleficence means do no harm, the old maxim primum non nocere. Beneficence means acting for the person's good, preventing and removing harm, and balancing benefits against risks. Justice means fair distribution of benefits, risks and costs, and treating similar cases alike. The principles are prima facie: none automatically wins, so in a dilemma they must be weighed. A depressed client refusing treatment, for example, pits autonomy against beneficence. Examiners build variants from neighbouring lists. The Belmont Report (1979), on research ethics in the United States, gave three principles: respect for persons, beneficence and justice. Karen Kitchener (1984) proposed five for counselling psychology: autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice and fidelity. The APA Ethics Code (2002) has five General Principles, including Integrity, which is why integrity is planted in the wrong options here. In India, the ICMR National Ethical Guidelines (2017) govern research with human participants. Remember the four with the letters A, B, N, J: autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice. Option c is right because beneficence, non-maleficence, justice and respect for autonomy are the four pillars set out by Beauchamp and Childress.","traps":{"a":"This list keeps autonomy, beneficence and non-maleficence but replaces justice with integrity. Integrity, meaning accuracy and honesty, is Principle C of the APA Ethics Code (2002), not one of the biomedical pillars of Tom Beauchamp and James Childress (1979). It tempts because candidates who study both lists tend to merge them, and integrity sounds like a natural ethical pillar.","b":"This list keeps autonomy, beneficence and justice but drops non-maleficence and adds a vague 'respect'. Respect is already part of the first pillar, properly called respect for autonomy, so the list counts one principle twice and loses another. It tempts because 'respect' appears in the correct full title and in the APA principle of Respect for People's Rights and Dignity.","d":"This list has three correct pillars but swaps justice for integrity. Justice, the fair distribution of benefits, burdens and resources, is one of the four pillars, while integrity belongs to the APA General Principles. It tempts more than the other distractors because it keeps the precise wording 'respect for autonomy', so it looks exact. Check that justice is present before choosing."}},{"q":"Which types of psychotherapy are most effective in treating survivors of sexual assault?","concept":"Sexual assault is one of the traumas most likely to lead to post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). Ann Burgess and Lynda Holmstrom (1974) described rape trauma syndrome, with an acute disorganisation phase followed by a long-term reorganisation phase, and later research showed that many survivors develop PTSD, depression and substance problems. The treatments with the strongest evidence are trauma-focused. Trauma-focused cognitive behavioural therapy is a family that includes Cognitive Processing Therapy, developed by Patricia Resick and Monica Schnicke (1992) for rape survivors, which targets stuck points such as self-blame; Prolonged Exposure, set out by Edna Foa and Barbara Rothbaum (1998) in Treating the Trauma of Rape, which uses imaginal and in vivo exposure to extinguish fear; and, for children, TF-CBT. Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR), introduced by Francine Shapiro (1989), has the client hold the traumatic memory in mind while following bilateral stimulation such as eye movements. WHO guidance (2013) recommends trauma-focused CBT and EMDR for adult PTSD. Psychodynamic psychotherapy has a weaker evidence base for PTSD, but it is used to work on shame, relationships and longer-term personality effects, which is why it appears in the keyed list. Therapies without a trauma focus, such as general CBT, mindfulness-based approaches, acceptance and commitment therapy, interpersonal therapy and dialectical behaviour therapy, can help with associated problems but are not first-line PTSD treatments. Option c is right because it is the only list that includes the two trauma-specific, evidence-based treatments, trauma-focused CBT and EMDR.","traps":{"a":"This list has psychodynamic therapy, general CBT and mindfulness-based therapy. General CBT, as set out by Aaron T. Beck (1979) for depression, is not the same as trauma-focused CBT, which deliberately processes the trauma memory. Mindfulness-based approaches, from the stress reduction programme of Jon Kabat-Zinn (1979), reduce general distress but are not first-line PTSD treatment. It tempts because CBT sounds evidence-based, but the trauma focus is missing.","b":"Interpersonal therapy, set out by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (1984), treats depression by working on grief, role disputes, role transitions and interpersonal deficits. Dialectical behaviour therapy, by Marsha Linehan (1993), was built for chronic suicidality and borderline personality disorder. Both may help survivors with depression or self-harm, which is why the list tempts, but neither is a trauma-focused treatment for PTSD itself.","d":"This list is all general or third-wave CBT: standard CBT, mindfulness-based therapy, and acceptance and commitment therapy, developed by Steven Hayes and colleagues (1999), which builds psychological flexibility through acceptance and values. They are modern and research-based, which is why the list tempts. But none of them requires processing the trauma memory, and none has the PTSD evidence of trauma-focused CBT or EMDR."}},{"q":"What is the main goal of TF-CBT?","concept":"Trauma-Focused Cognitive Behavioural Therapy (TF-CBT) was developed by Judith Cohen, Anthony Mannarino and Esther Deblinger and set out in their manual Treating Trauma and Traumatic Grief in Children and Adolescents (2006). It is a short-term treatment for children and adolescents, roughly 3 to 18 years old, with PTSD symptoms after events such as sexual abuse, and a non-offending parent or caregiver takes part. Its components are remembered by the acronym PRACTICE: Psychoeducation and Parenting skills, Relaxation, Affective expression and modulation, Cognitive coping, Trauma narrative and processing, In vivo mastery of trauma reminders, Conjoint child-parent sessions, and Enhancing safety and future development. These fall into three phases: stabilisation and skills, trauma narrative and processing, and integration and consolidation. Gradual exposure runs through every component, but its peak is the trauma narrative, in which the child describes the traumatic experience in detail over several sessions, often as a book, while the therapist helps identify and correct distorted thoughts such as self-blame. Repeated, controlled recall reduces the distress and avoidance linked to the memory, which is desensitisation. The coping skills of the first phase exist to prepare the child for this work. Examiners contrast TF-CBT with EMDR of Francine Shapiro (1989), which uses bilateral stimulation; Prolonged Exposure for adults, described by Edna Foa and Barbara Rothbaum (1998); and Cognitive Processing Therapy of Patricia Resick and Monica Schnicke (1992), which works mainly on beliefs. Option c is right because building the trauma narrative and desensitising the child to traumatic memories is the central, defining goal of TF-CBT.","traps":{"a":"Closure and relief describe a hoped-for outcome of any trauma therapy, not the specific mechanism of TF-CBT. 'Closure' is also a technical term in EMDR, where phase 7 returns the client to calm at the end of each session. It tempts because it sounds compassionate and goal-like, but it names no technique; examiners want the process that produces relief, which is narrative and desensitisation.","b":"Emotional regulation skills belong to the early stabilisation phase of TF-CBT (Relaxation and Affective modulation in PRACTICE). Mindfulness is the core of other models, such as dialectical behaviour therapy by Marsha Linehan (1993) and the mindfulness-based therapies. It tempts because these skills are taught in TF-CBT, but they prepare the child for trauma processing rather than forming the main goal, and mindfulness is not a defining component.","d":"Teaching coping skills is a real and important part of TF-CBT, covered by the Relaxation, Affective modulation and Cognitive coping components, so this is the strongest distractor. But these skills form the first phase, preparing the child to tolerate trauma processing. A treatment that taught only coping skills would be skills-based, not trauma-focused; the trauma narrative is what makes TF-CBT trauma-focused."}},{"q":"What is the correct sequence of the eight phases of EMDR?","concept":"Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR) was developed by Francine Shapiro, who in 1987 noticed that quick eye movements seemed to reduce the distress of disturbing thoughts, and who published the first controlled study in 1989. Her Adaptive Information Processing model holds that trauma memories are stored in an unprocessed form, and that bilateral stimulation (eye movements, taps or tones) helps link them to adaptive memory networks. The standard protocol has eight phases. Phase 1, history taking and treatment planning, identifies targets: past events, present triggers and future templates. Phase 2, preparation, explains the method and builds stabilising resources such as the safe or calm place. Phase 3, assessment, activates the target: an image, a negative cognition, a desired positive cognition rated on the Validity of Cognition (VoC) scale from 1 to 7, the emotions, a rating on the Subjective Units of Disturbance (SUD) scale of Joseph Wolpe (1969) from 0 to 10, and the body location. Phase 4, desensitisation, uses sets of bilateral stimulation until SUD falls to 0 or 1. Phase 5, installation, strengthens the positive cognition towards a VoC of 7. Phase 6, body scan, clears residual tension. Phase 7, closure, returns the client to calm at the end of every session. Phase 8, reevaluation, checks the results at the start of the next session. Option b is right because it is the only list with all eight phases in Shapiro's order, with closure ending each session as phase 7 and reevaluation opening the next session as phase 8.","traps":{"a":"This sequence starts with preparation and pushes history taking to phase 3, after assessment. In the protocol of Francine Shapiro you cannot prepare a client or assess a target before taking a history and choosing targets, so history taking is always phase 1. It also puts closure before body scan. It tempts because preparation sounds like a natural first step in any therapy.","d":"This sequence makes preparation phase 1 and history taking phase 2, and puts body scan before installation. Body scan only makes sense after the positive cognition is installed, since it checks whether any disturbance remains in the body while the client holds the target and the positive cognition together. It tempts because it contains all eight correct names, so only careful checking of order reveals the errors.","c":"This list has the first six phases right but swaps the last two, putting reevaluation at 7 and closure at 8. In the protocol of Francine Shapiro, closure ends every session (phase 7) and reevaluation opens the next session to check whether the gains held (phase 8), so reevaluation must come last. It tempts because it differs from the correct order only at the very end, where readers stop checking."}},{"q":"How does the syndromal approach of classifying psychiatric disorders differ from the historical approach?","concept":"Psychiatric classification can be organised by cause (etiology) or by description (signs, symptoms and course). In general medicine the ideal is etiological: general paresis of the insane was a puzzling syndrome until Hideyo Noguchi and J. W. Moore (1913) found Treponema pallidum in patients' brains, after which it became a disease defined by its cause. For most mental disorders, however, causes are still unknown. Older classifications, which this question calls the historical approach, sorted disorders by known or presumed causes: organic versus functional, or, in DSM-I (1952) and DSM-II (1968), categories shaped by psychodynamic theory, with many disorders labelled 'reactions' and neuroses defined by unconscious conflict. Clinicians could not reliably agree on such presumed causes, so diagnostic reliability was poor. The syndromal or descriptive approach groups disorders by clusters of signs and symptoms that occur together and share a course, without assuming a cause. Its roots lie in the separation of dementia praecox from manic-depressive insanity by Emil Kraepelin (1899), based on symptoms and outcome. It became dominant with the research criteria of John Feighner and colleagues (1972) and with DSM-III (1980), led by Robert Spitzer, which was explicitly atheoretical and used operational criteria; ICD-10 (1992), DSM-5 (2013) and ICD-11 follow it. Etiology survives in a few places, such as organic disorders, substance-induced disorders, PTSD and adjustment disorder, which require a cause or stressor in the definition. Option b is right because the syndromal approach rests on clinical signs and symptoms, while the historical approach classified disorders by known or presumed etiology.","traps":{"a":"This option reverses the two definitions. Symptom-based description is the syndromal approach of DSM-III (1980) onwards, and classification by cause is the older, etiological style. It tempts because it uses the right two ideas, and because 'syndrome' can sound technical and causal. Remember: a syndrome is a cluster of signs and symptoms whose cause is unknown or unspecified; once the cause is known it becomes a disease.","c":"The syndromal approach is not absent from psychiatry; it is the backbone of both current systems, DSM-5 (2013) and ICD-11, where nearly every disorder is defined by a list of symptoms, a minimum duration and impairment. It is the older, cause-based approach that has largely been abandoned. It tempts only because the word 'historical' suggests a traditional, established method that is still in use.","d":"No rule restricts syndromal classification to medical illness or the historical approach to psychiatry. Both approaches are used across medicine: many medical conditions began as syndromes, for example AIDS before HIV was identified, and moved to causal definitions. It tempts because candidates may link the word 'syndrome' to medical labels such as Down syndrome and assume psychiatry must use something else."}},{"q":"Which category of mental disorders in ICD-10 includes delirium and dementia?","concept":"Chapter V of ICD-10, published by the World Health Organization (1992) and coded with the letter F, divides mental and behavioural disorders into blocks of ten. F00 to F09 covers organic, including symptomatic, mental disorders: those with a demonstrable cause in cerebral disease, brain injury or a systemic illness affecting the brain. Symptomatic means the brain is affected secondarily by a disease elsewhere in the body, such as hypothyroidism. The block contains F00 dementia in Alzheimer's disease, F01 vascular dementia, F02 dementia in other diseases (for example Pick's, Huntington's, Parkinson's or HIV disease), F03 unspecified dementia, F04 organic amnesic syndrome, F05 delirium not induced by alcohol and other psychoactive substances, F06 other organic disorders (hallucinosis, catatonic, delusional, mood and anxiety disorders), and F07 personality and behavioural disorders due to brain disease. A classic variant trap: delirium in alcohol withdrawal (delirium tremens) is coded F10.4, and alcohol-induced amnesic (Korsakoff) syndrome F10.6, because substance causes go to F10 to F19. The other blocks are F20 to F29 schizophrenia, schizotypal and delusional; F30 to F39 mood; F40 to F48 neurotic, stress-related and somatoform; F50 to F59 behavioural syndromes with physiological disturbances; F60 to F69 adult personality and behaviour; F70 to F79 mental retardation; F80 to F89 psychological development; F90 to F98 childhood-onset behavioural and emotional disorders. ICD-11 (adopted 2019) and DSM-5 (2013) drop the word organic and speak of neurocognitive disorders. Option a is right because delirium and dementia are organic mental disorders, classified in F00 to F09.","traps":{"b":"F10 to F19 covers mental and behavioural disorders due to psychoactive substance use. The third character names the substance (F10 alcohol, F11 opioids, F12 cannabinoids) and the fourth the clinical state (.0 intoxication, .2 dependence, .3 withdrawal, .4 withdrawal with delirium). It tempts because substance-induced delirium and Korsakoff syndrome are coded here, but delirium and dementia as categories sit in F00 to F09.","c":"F20 to F29 contains schizophrenia (F20), schizotypal disorder (F21), persistent delusional disorders (F22), acute and transient psychotic disorders (F23), induced delusional disorder (F24) and schizoaffective disorders (F25). These are functional psychoses with no demonstrable brain disease as cause. It tempts because delirium can include hallucinations and delusions, but psychotic features caused by brain disease are coded F06, not in the F20s.","d":"The mood block actually runs F30 to F39 (the option misprints its start), covering manic episode (F30), bipolar affective disorder (F31), depressive episode (F32), recurrent depressive disorder (F33) and persistent mood disorders such as dysthymia (F34). It tempts because dementia often begins with depressive symptoms and severe depression can mimic dementia (pseudodementia). But an organic mood disorder is coded F06.3, not here."}},{"q":"What is the correct sequence of steps involved in working on self-acceptance for LGBTQ individuals?","concept":"Affirmative therapy treats same-sex attraction and gender diversity as normal variations and sees much of the client's distress as a product of stigma. The background is examinable. The American Psychiatric Association removed homosexuality from the DSM in 1973; DSM-III (1980) kept ego-dystonic homosexuality, which was dropped in 1987. ICD-10 (1992) kept egodystonic sexual orientation (F66.1), and ICD-11 removed that group. In India, the Supreme Court in Navtej Singh Johar v. Union of India (2018) decriminalised consensual same-sex relations, and the Indian Psychiatric Society stated in 2018 that homosexuality is not an illness. The minority stress model of Ilan Meyer (2003) explains why sexual minorities show more mental health problems: distal stressors (discrimination, violence) and proximal stressors (expecting rejection, concealment, internalised homophobia) add to ordinary life stress. Self-acceptance work builds on this model in a set order. The therapist first raises awareness of and explores sexual minority stress, so clients can see where their pain comes from. Next comes validation of their feelings and identity. This prepares the key reframing, the shift from ego-dystonicity (believing something is wrong with me) to social-dystonicity (recognising that the conflict lies in society's prejudice). Therapeutic or support groups then reduce isolation, and finally clients are connected to gay-affirmative resources and community. A related exam favourite is the six-stage identity formation model of Vivienne Cass (1979): confusion, comparison, tolerance, acceptance, pride and synthesis. Option c is right because it gives the sequence that begins with minority stress, moves through validation and reframing, and ends with groups and community resources.","traps":{"a":"This sequence opens with validation, which is right in spirit but too early, and brings in the therapist's 'expert position', meaning professional authority used to correct myths, such as stating that homosexuality is not a disorder. Expert position is a useful technique but not a step in the keyed sequence, and the list omits the central shift from ego-dystonicity to social-dystonicity. It tempts because validation sounds like a natural first step.","b":"This sequence starts with mirroring, a term from the self psychology of Heinz Kohut (1971) for reflecting back the client's worth, and puts the reframing from ego-dystonicity to social-dystonicity second, before minority stress has been explored. It ends with awareness of minority stress, which should come first, and leaves out validation and resources. It tempts because it contains the key reframing phrase.","d":"This sequence leads with the therapist's expert position, places awareness of minority stress fourth, and leaves out the reframing from ego-dystonicity to social-dystonicity altogether. Starting from authority rather than the client's own experience runs against affirmative practice, which first explores how stigma has shaped the client's distress. It tempts because it ends correctly, with connection to gay-affirmative resources."}},{"q":"How is normality defined in the Subjective Model of normality?","concept":"There is no single definition of normality, so textbooks describe models, each with its own criterion. Daniel Offer and Melvin Sabshin (1966) set out four perspectives: normality as health (the absence of psychopathology, the traditional medical view), normality as utopia (an ideal of optimal functioning), normality as average (the statistical middle of a bell curve), and normality as process (healthy development across the life span). Other textbooks add the subjective model and the social or normative model. In the subjective model, a person is normal if they feel no distress, suffer no disability in daily functioning, and do not seek help. The judgement rests on the individual's own experience: if I am not troubled and not asking for help, I am normal. Its strength is respect for personal experience; its weakness is that people with antisocial personality disorder, mania, or delusions with poor insight may feel no distress, while someone grieving normally may feel great distress. The medical model asks whether a diagnosable disorder or pathology is present. The ideal model asks whether functioning is optimal, as in the six criteria of positive mental health of Marie Jahoda (1958), including self-actualisation, autonomy and environmental mastery. The social model asks whether behaviour conforms to cultural norms, which vary across cultures and eras. The harmful dysfunction analysis of Jerome Wakefield (1992) tries to combine a scientific element (dysfunction) with a value element (harm). Option b is right because the subjective model defines normality as the absence of distress, disability or help-seeking behaviour.","traps":{"a":"Optimal functioning is the ideal or utopian model, called normality as utopia by Daniel Offer and Melvin Sabshin (1966), and the basis of the positive mental health criteria of Marie Jahoda (1958). Very few people meet it, so most would count as abnormal. It tempts because it can be described from the person's point of view, but it sets a high external ideal rather than asking about personal distress.","c":"Absence of any psychiatric disorder or psychopathology is the medical model, which Daniel Offer and Melvin Sabshin (1966) called normality as health. Normality is judged by a clinician from signs and symptoms, not by the person's own experience. It tempts because the words 'absence of' appear in both definitions, but the subjective model asks whether the person feels distressed, not whether a diagnosis can be made.","d":"Socially acceptable behaviour is the social or normative model, which judges normality by conformity to the norms of a culture at a given time. Its classic weakness is cultural relativity: hearing the voices of ancestors may be normal in one community and pathological in another. It tempts because social judgement is often how families first label a person abnormal, but the standard is external, not subjective."}},{"q":"Which of the following is not one of the gunas of the individual Jiva?","concept":"The three gunas come from Samkhya, one of the six orthodox (astika) schools of Indian philosophy, traditionally traced to the sage Kapila and set out systematically in the Samkhya Karika of Ishvarakrishna. Samkhya is dualist: purusha is pure consciousness, and prakriti is matter or nature. Prakriti is made of three gunas (literally 'strands' or 'qualities'). Sattva is lightness, clarity, knowledge and calm. Rajas is activity, passion, desire and restlessness. Tamas is heaviness, inertia, ignorance and dullness. Before creation the three are in balance; when the balance is disturbed, prakriti evolves into intellect, ego, mind, senses and the physical elements. Because every jiva (the individual embodied self) is purusha bound up with prakriti, every person carries all three gunas, and temperament depends on which one dominates at a given time. The Bhagavad Gita, chapter 14, describes the sattvic, rajasic and tamasic person, and chapter 17 extends this to food, faith and charity. Modern Indian psychology treats the triguna model as a personality typology and has turned it into self-report inventories. Examiners build variants from neighbouring lists, so keep them apart: the three doshas of Ayurveda (vata, pitta, kapha) are bodily humours, not gunas; the four purusharthas are goals of life (dharma, artha, kama, moksha); and ahimsa (non-violence) is the first of the five yamas in the Yoga Sutras of Patanjali, alongside satya, asteya, brahmacharya and aparigraha. Ahimsa is also the central vow of Jainism. Option d is right because ahimsa is an ethical restraint and not one of the three gunas of sattva, rajas and tamas.","traps":{"a":"Rajas is a genuine guna. It stands for activity, passion, desire and restlessness, and in Samkhya it is the force that sets prakriti in motion. The Bhagavad Gita (chapter 14) links it to craving and attachment to work and its rewards. It cannot answer a 'not one of the gunas' stem. It tempts only if you misread the stem as asking which option is a guna.","b":"Tamas is a genuine guna, the quality of heaviness, inertia, darkness, ignorance and sleepiness. In Samkhya it is the principle of resistance that holds things in place; in the Gita the tamasic person is lazy, confused and careless. It belongs to the triad, so it is not the exception. It tempts because 'tamas' sounds negative, and candidates sometimes assume a negative quality cannot be a basic constituent.","c":"Sattva is a genuine guna, the quality of lightness, purity, clarity, knowledge and harmony. Indian psychology treats a sattva-dominant temperament as the healthiest, calm and balanced. It is part of the triad, so it cannot be the odd one out. It tempts because sattva sounds like a virtue, and a candidate may wrongly group it with ethical ideals such as ahimsa rather than with the gunas."}},{"q":"What is the Sanskrit term for the waking state according to the Mandukya Upanishad?","concept":"The Mandukya Upanishad is the shortest of the principal Upanishads, only twelve verses, and belongs to the Atharva Veda. It analyses the Self (Atman) through four states of consciousness and maps them onto the sacred syllable Om, split into A, U, M and the silence after them. The first state is jagrat, the waking state; the Self here is called Vaishvanara or Vishva (spelt Viswa in many papers). It is 'outward-knowing', experiencing gross external objects through the senses, and corresponds to the letter A. The second is svapna, the dream state; the Self is Taijasa, 'inward-knowing', enjoying subtle mental images, and corresponds to U. The third is sushupti, deep dreamless sleep; the Self is Prajna, a unified mass of consciousness with no desire and no dream, corresponding to M. The fourth is Turiya, literally 'the fourth', which is not really a state but the pure, non-dual witness underlying the other three, matching the silence after Om. Gaudapada's Mandukya Karika, the earliest systematic Advaita Vedanta text, is a commentary on this scheme. Later Vedanta links the first three states to the three bodies: gross (sthula), subtle (sukshma) and causal (karana). For exams, separate the names of the states (jagrat, svapna, sushupti, turiya) from the names of the experiencing Self in each (Vishva, Taijasa, Prajna); variants switch between the two lists. Option a is right because Vishva (Viswa), also called Vaishvanara, is the Upanishad's name for the Self in the waking state, and it is the only waking-state term among the options.","traps":{"b":"Taijasa ('the luminous one', from tejas, light) is the Self in svapna, the dream state. It is inward-knowing: it experiences subtle images made by the mind from waking impressions, and it corresponds to the letter U of Om. It does not answer a question about waking. It tempts because it is listed second, straight after Vishva, and candidates who memorise the sequence loosely swap the first two.","c":"Prajna is the Self in sushupti, deep dreamless sleep, where there is neither desire nor dream. The Upanishad calls it a unified mass of consciousness, full of bliss, and links it to the letter M of Om. Later Vedanta pairs it with the causal body. It is not the waking state. It tempts because the word prajna also means wisdom or awareness in general, which sounds like wakefulness.","d":"Turiya means 'the fourth'. It is pure consciousness, the non-dual Atman that witnesses waking, dream and deep sleep but is none of them, and it matches the silence after Om. The Upanishad describes it mostly by negation: neither inward-knowing nor outward-knowing. It is not the waking state. It tempts because it is the most famous term in the text and sounds like a heightened, fully awake state."}},{"q":"What is the principle of disinterestedness that the Gita is based on?","concept":"The Bhagavad Gita is a 700-verse dialogue between Krishna and the warrior Arjuna, set in the Bhishma Parva of the Mahabharata. Arjuna refuses to fight because he fears the results of killing his kin; Krishna's answer is a theory of action. Its core is verse 2.47: you have a right to your action, never to its fruits; do not let the fruit be your motive, and do not cling to inaction either. This is nishkama karma, literally 'desireless action': doing your duty (svadharma) fully and well while giving up attachment to success or failure. Its opposite is sakama karma, action done for a desired reward. The Gita adds samatva (evenness of mind in gain and loss) and lokasangraha (acting for the welfare of the world). It describes several paths (margas): karma yoga, the path of action; jnana yoga, the path of knowledge and discrimination; bhakti yoga, the path of loving devotion; and dhyana yoga, meditation. Nishkama karma is the attitude that turns ordinary work into karma yoga, so the two are linked but not identical: karma yoga is the path, nishkama karma is the principle of disinterestedness that drives it. Bal Gangadhar Tilak's Gita Rahasya (1915) read the whole text as a call to such selfless action. Indian psychologists discuss the idea as a buffer against outcome anxiety and burnout, close to Western ideas of process focus and intrinsic motivation. Option d is right because the stem asks for the specific principle of disinterested action, and that is nishkama karma, action without attachment to its fruits.","traps":{"a":"Bhakti yoga is the path of loving devotion to a personal God, developed in Gita chapter 12 and later in the bhakti movements. It is a path (marga), not the principle of acting without desire for results, though a devotee may offer every action to God. It tempts because the Gita's closing teaching (18.66) asks Arjuna to surrender to Krishna, and many readers see devotion as its main message.","b":"Jnana yoga is the path of knowledge: discriminating the eternal Self (Atman) from the changing body and mind, taught in chapter 2 and developed in Advaita Vedanta. It aims at liberation through insight, not at a rule for how to act. It tempts because the Gita's first teaching to Arjuna, that the Self cannot be killed, is a jnana argument, and nishkama karma is presented right after it.","c":"Karma yoga is the path of action, the discipline of doing one's duty as a spiritual practice, developed mainly in chapters 3 and 5. It is the broader path; nishkama karma is the specific attitude of disinterest in results that makes action into yoga. It tempts most strongly because the two are taught together and many textbooks use them as synonyms. A stem asking for the 'principle of disinterestedness' wants nishkama karma."}},{"q":"What is the focus of Mahayana Buddhism?","concept":"Buddhism began with Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, in north India around the fifth century BCE. Its core teaching is the Four Noble Truths (suffering, its cause in craving, its cessation, and the Eightfold Path) and the Middle Way between indulgence and harsh asceticism. Within a few centuries of his death the tradition divided. The older stream, surviving today as Theravada ('teaching of the elders') in Sri Lanka and Southeast Asia, keeps the Pali canon and holds up the arhat, the person who reaches nirvana through his or her own effort. Mahayana ('great vehicle') arose around the start of the Common Era and spread to China, Korea, Japan and Tibet. Its defining ideal is the bodhisattva, a being who postpones final nirvana in order to free all sentient beings from suffering, guided by karuna (compassion) and prajna (wisdom). Mahayana followers called the older path Hinayana ('lesser vehicle'), a term now considered pejorative. Mahayana philosophy produced two major schools: Madhyamaka of Nagarjuna (around the second century CE), which teaches sunyata, the emptiness of all phenomena, and Yogacara, which holds that experience is 'mind only'. Vajrayana, the tantric Buddhism of Tibet, grew out of Mahayana and adds mantra, mandala and ritual as fast methods to enlightenment. Variants often ask about the bodhisattva, the arhat, or which school stresses sunyata, so link each term to its tradition. Option c is right because Mahayana's central focus is the bodhisattva aim of collective freedom from suffering and teaching all beings the way to enlightenment, rather than individual liberation alone.","traps":{"a":"Tantric rituals, mantras, mandalas and guru initiation are the marks of Vajrayana ('diamond vehicle'), the form of Buddhism dominant in Tibet. Vajrayana grew out of Mahayana and shares its bodhisattva ideal, but its distinctive ritual methods are not the focus of Mahayana as a whole. It tempts because Tibetan Buddhism is the most visible Mahayana-related tradition, so candidates assume its rituals define Mahayana.","b":"This describes a caricature of the older Theravada stream, which keeps the Pali Tripitaka as its canon and emphasises monastic discipline and individual liberation as an arhat. Even there, strict asceticism is not the goal: the Buddha himself rejected extreme self-mortification in favour of the Middle Way. It tempts because textbooks contrast Mahayana with a more conservative, monk-centred tradition, and this option sounds like that contrast.","d":"Voidness or emptiness (sunyata) of both inner and outer phenomena is a real Mahayana doctrine, taught by Nagarjuna's Madhyamaka school and the Perfection of Wisdom sutras: nothing has a fixed, independent essence. But it is a philosophical tenet within Mahayana, not its defining focus or aim. It tempts most because it is true of Mahayana, so you must choose the broader orientation (collective liberation) over a single doctrine."}},{"q":"Which pitaka in the Tripitaka is a rulebook for monks and nuns?","concept":"The Tripitaka (Pali Tipitaka, 'three baskets') is the canon of Buddhist scripture. Tradition says that soon after the Buddha's death the first council at Rajagriha fixed the teachings orally: the monk Upali recited the rules of discipline and Ananda, the Buddha's attendant, recited the discourses. The Pali version was first written down in Sri Lanka in the first century BCE. The first basket is the Vinaya Pitaka, the basket of discipline. It contains the monastic code (the Patimokkha), the story of how each rule arose, and procedures for ordination and community life; in Theravada the code has 227 rules for monks (bhikkhus) and more for nuns (bhikkhunis). The second is the Sutta Pitaka, the basket of discourses, holding the Buddha's sermons in five collections (nikayas): Digha, Majjhima, Samyutta, Anguttara and Khuddaka; the Dhammapada and the Jataka tales sit in the Khuddaka. The third is the Abhidhamma Pitaka, the 'higher teaching', a systematic analysis of mind and matter that lists moments of consciousness (citta) and mental factors (cetasika), which is why it is often called the first Buddhist psychology. Remember the pattern: Vinaya equals rules, Sutta equals sermons, Abhidhamma equals analysis of mind. Variants may ask which basket contains the Dhammapada (Sutta), which matters most for Buddhist psychology (Abhidhamma), or which was recited by Upali (Vinaya). Mahayana schools also accept further sutras outside the Pali canon. Option a is right because the Vinaya Pitaka is the disciplinary rulebook for monks and nuns, setting out the Patimokkha rules and monastic procedures.","traps":{"b":"The Sutta Pitaka is the basket of discourses: thousands of sermons and dialogues of the Buddha and his chief disciples, arranged in five nikayas, including the Dhammapada and the Jataka tales. It teaches doctrine (dhamma) such as the Four Noble Truths and dependent origination, not the monastic code. It tempts because it is the largest and best-known basket, and some suttas do describe how monks should behave.","c":"The Abhidhamma Pitaka, the 'higher teaching', is a later scholastic analysis that breaks experience into momentary states of consciousness (citta), mental factors (cetasika), matter and nirvana. It is the basket psychology students meet most often, as an early theory of mind. It is philosophy and psychology, not a rulebook. It tempts because its formal, list-heavy style looks like a code of rules.","d":"'None of the above' would be right only if no basket contained the monastic rules. The Vinaya Pitaka is exactly that rulebook, so this option fails. It tempts candidates who are unsure of the three names, or who think the rules for monks lie outside the canon in a separate code, since the Patimokkha is often recited and printed as a standalone text."}},{"q":"What are the three principal aspects of the human being in Sufi psychology?","concept":"Sufism (tasawwuf) is the mystical, inward tradition of Islam. Its psychology, built up by classical writers such as Abu Hamid al-Ghazali (1058 to 1111) and summarised for modern psychologists by Robert Frager in Heart, Self and Soul (1999), describes the person through three principal aspects. Nafs is the self or ego, the seat of desires, appetites and selfish drives; it is not evil in itself but must be trained. The tradition names its stages from the Quran: nafs al-ammara (the commanding self that urges wrongdoing), nafs al-lawwama (the self-reproaching self, close to conscience) and nafs al-mutmainna (the self at peace). Qalb, often written ghalb from its Persian pronunciation, is the heart: not the physical organ but the spiritual centre of perception, intuition and love, which can be veiled by the nafs or polished like a mirror to reflect the divine. Ruh is the spirit, the divine breath God blew into Adam, which longs to return to its source. Growth means purifying the nafs so that the heart opens and the ruh can guide the person. Do not confuse these inner aspects with the stages of the Sufi path, commonly listed as sharia (religious law), tariqa (the mystical way under a sheikh), haqiqa (inner truth) and marifa (direct knowledge of God). Sharia is an external code of conduct, not a part of the human being. Option a is right because nafs, qalb (ghalb) and ruh, the self, heart and spirit, are the three principal aspects of the human being in Sufi psychology.","traps":{"b":"This option keeps qalb (ghalb, the heart) and nafs (the self) but replaces ruh, the spirit, with sharia. Sharia is Islamic religious law, the external code of worship and conduct and the first stage of the Sufi path, not an inner aspect of the person. It tempts because two of the three terms are correct and sharia is the most familiar Islamic word, so partial recall picks it.","c":"This option keeps nafs (the self) and ruh (the spirit) but drops qalb, the heart, which is the centre of Sufi psychology, and adds sharia, the religious law. Sharia governs outward behaviour; the heart is where Sufis locate spiritual perception and love. It tempts because nafs and ruh are the two terms most often contrasted as lower and higher self, so the missing middle term is easy to forget.","d":"This option keeps ruh (spirit) and qalb (heart) but leaves out nafs, the ego or lower self whose training is the main work of the Sufi path, and inserts sharia, the religious law. Without nafs the model loses its account of desire and inner struggle. It tempts because it contains the two 'higher' aspects, and a candidate may wrongly think the law, not the ego, is the third element."}},{"q":"What is the innermost being in the concentric system of Integral Yoga?","concept":"Integral Yoga (Purna Yoga) is the system of Sri Aurobindo (1872 to 1950), the nationalist turned philosopher and yogi of Pondicherry, set out in The Life Divine and The Synthesis of Yoga. It aims not only at individual liberation but at transforming the whole of human nature, and it is a key source for Indian psychology. Aurobindo described the personality on two axes. The concentric system runs from outside to inside. The outermost ring is the outer or surface being: the physical (body), the vital (life-energy, desires, emotions, drives) and the mind (thought, will, reason). Behind it lies the inner or subliminal being, with an inner mind, inner vital and inner physical that are wider and subtler. At the very centre is the psychic being (chaitya purusha), the evolving soul, a spark of the divine that grows across lives and can guide the outer nature once it comes forward. The vertical system runs from below to above: the subconscient and inconscient lie below ordinary mind, while higher planes rise above it through higher mind, illumined mind, intuition and overmind to the supermind. Change follows a triple transformation: psychic (bringing the psychic being forward), spiritual (opening to higher planes) and supramental. A useful neighbour is the five koshas of the Taittiriya Upanishad (annamaya, pranamaya, manomaya, vijnanamaya, anandamaya), another layered model of the person that moves from body inwards to bliss. Option a is right because in Aurobindo's concentric system the psychic being, the inmost soul, lies at the centre, behind the outer layers of body, vital and mind.","traps":{"b":"In Integral Yoga the mind (thought, reason, will and perception) is one of the three layers of the outer or surface being, together with the vital and the physical. It is the highest part of ordinary nature but lies on the outer ring of the concentric system, not at its centre. It tempts because Western psychology often treats the mind as the core of the self, and Aurobindo's vertical system places higher mental planes above it.","c":"The vital is Aurobindo's term for the life-energy layer: desires, emotions, ambitions, drives and sensations, roughly matching prana in Indian thought. It sits between the mind and the body in the outer being and must be purified so that the psychic being can lead. It is not the innermost centre. It tempts because the vital feels intensely personal and close to who we are, so it can be mistaken for the core self.","d":"The physical or body is the most external layer of the outer being in the concentric system, the material base that Integral Yoga ultimately also seeks to transform. It is the outermost, not the innermost, part. It tempts candidates who reverse the scale, since layered models such as the five koshas start from the body (annamaya kosha) and list it first, which can be misread as most central."}},{"q":"When was the National Institute of Mental Health and Neurosciences established?","concept":"The National Institute of Mental Health and Neurosciences (NIMHANS), Bengaluru, is India's leading centre for psychiatry, clinical psychology, neurology and neurosurgery. Its roots lie in the Bangalore Lunatic Asylum of the mid nineteenth century, which later became the Mysore Government Mental Hospital. In the mid 1950s this hospital was developed into the All India Institute of Mental Health (AIIMH), a national teaching and research centre for psychiatry and clinical psychology. In 1974 the AIIMH and the mental hospital merged to form NIMHANS as an autonomous institute. It became a deemed university in 1994, and the NIMHANS Bengaluru Act 2012 declared it an Institute of National Importance. Sources differ on the 'established' year: NIMHANS histories count from 1954 (AIIMH) or 1974 (NIMHANS), and some question banks print 1955. Learn the parallel dates for the other entrance-exam institutes: the Central Institute of Psychiatry (CIP), Ranchi, was founded in 1918 as the Ranchi European Lunatic Asylum, and IHBAS, Delhi, grew out of the Hospital for Mental Diseases, Shahdara, and was set up as an institute in 1993. Common distractor years come from Indian psychology history: Girindrasekhar Bose founded the Indian Psychoanalytical Society in 1922, and the University of Calcutta opened India's first department of psychology in 1916. Today the RCI Act 1992 regulates the clinical psychology training these institutes offer. Option b is right because, among the years given, 1955 is the only one at the institutional origin of NIMHANS, the founding of the All India Institute of Mental Health in the mid 1950s (most official histories say 1954).","traps":{"a":"1922 is the year Girindrasekhar Bose founded the Indian Psychoanalytical Society in Calcutta, one of the earliest landmarks of Indian psychology. It has nothing to do with NIMHANS or the All India Institute of Mental Health. It tempts because it sits in the cluster of early twentieth century history-of-psychology dates that candidates memorise together, so it feels familiar without being linked to any mental health institute.","c":"1924 is not a founding year for NIMHANS, the All India Institute of Mental Health or the other major mental health institutes in the syllabus: CIP Ranchi dates from 1918, and NIMHANS from 1954 and 1974. It works only as a filler year near other early twentieth century dates. It tempts candidates who vaguely remember that NIMHANS has roots in an older Mysore mental hospital and so guess a pre-independence date.","d":"1916 is when the University of Calcutta set up India's first department of psychology, which is why the year appears in Indian psychology history questions. It is unrelated to NIMHANS, which grew from a hospital in Bangalore, not a university department. It tempts because it is a genuine and frequently tested 'first' in Indian psychology, and candidates link any early landmark with the most famous institute."}},{"q":"What are the goodness or quality criteria for knowledge according to positivism?","concept":"This question comes from the paradigm tables of Egon Guba and Yvonna Lincoln (1994), 'Competing paradigms in qualitative research', in the Handbook of Qualitative Research. They compare four paradigms (positivism, postpositivism, critical theory and constructivism) first on three basic questions: ontology (what is real), epistemology (how the knower relates to the known) and methodology (how we find out). Positivism, named by Auguste Comte (1830s), holds a naive realist ontology (a real, knowable world), an objectivist epistemology (a detached observer can find value-free facts) and an experimental, mostly quantitative methodology that verifies hypotheses. Postpositivism keeps realism but makes it critical and stresses falsification, an idea from Karl Popper (1934). Guba and Lincoln then compare the paradigms on practical issues. For goodness or quality criteria, positivism and postpositivism use conventional benchmarks of rigour: internal validity, external validity, reliability and objectivity. Critical theory uses historical situatedness, erosion of ignorance and misapprehensions, and action stimulus. Constructivism uses trustworthiness and authenticity. Trustworthiness had been set out by Lincoln and Guba (1985) as parallel criteria: credibility (for internal validity), transferability (for external validity), dependability (for reliability) and confirmability (for objectivity). On knowledge accumulation, positivism sees accretion of 'building blocks' and cause-effect generalisations; critical theory, historical revisionism and generalisation by similarity; constructivism, more informed reconstructions and vicarious experience. Variant questions mix rows (aim, nature of knowledge, voice, values, ethics), so learn each row by paradigm. Option b is right because Guba and Lincoln list internal and external validity, reliability and objectivity as the positivist goodness or quality criteria.","traps":{"a":"Historical situatedness is the first goodness criterion of critical theory in Guba and Lincoln (1994): good inquiry must take account of the historical, social, economic and gender context of what it studies, and should also erode ignorance and stimulate action. Positivism, by contrast, seeks context-free, timeless laws. It tempts because it sounds like a scholarly quality standard and appears in the same row of the table.","c":"In Guba and Lincoln's (1994) table, 'generalization by similarity' sits in the knowledge-accumulation row under critical theory, beside historical revisionism: insights transfer to settings whose historical and structural conditions are similar. Positivist generalisation is instead statistical and law-like, building cause-effect linkages. It tempts because generalisation is a core positivist concern, and the phrase sounds like external validity.","d":"Vicarious experience belongs to constructivism in the knowledge-accumulation row of Guba and Lincoln (1994): knowledge grows as readers vicariously enter rich case descriptions and form more informed reconstructions, the logic of the naturalistic case study. It is a way of transferring understanding, not a positivist standard of rigour. It tempts because 'experience' sounds empirical, and empiricism is linked with positivism."}},{"q":"What is the voice in inquiry according to constructivism?","concept":"Egon Guba and Yvonna Lincoln (1994), in their chapter 'Competing paradigms in qualitative research', compared four inquiry paradigms: positivism, postpositivism, critical theory and constructivism. A paradigm, for them, is a basic belief system defined by its answers to three questions: ontological, epistemological and methodological. Constructivism holds a relativist ontology (realities are multiple, local, socially constructed), a transactional and subjectivist epistemology (findings are created through the interaction of inquirer and participants) and a hermeneutic, dialectical methodology (constructions are elicited, compared and refined through dialogue). Its aim is understanding and reconstruction, and its quality is judged by trustworthiness and authenticity. One row of their table, 'voice', asks what stance the inquirer takes. Positivism and postpositivism give the 'disinterested scientist' who informs decision makers, policy makers and change agents, standing apart from the values of the people studied. Critical theory gives the 'transformative intellectual', an advocate and activist who exposes oppression and works for emancipation, in the tradition of Paulo Freire (1970). Constructivism gives the 'passionate participant', a facilitator of multivoice reconstruction, who is personally involved and helps different stakeholders hear and revise each other's constructions. This links to the fourth generation evaluation of Guba and Lincoln (1989), where the evaluator negotiates among stakeholder claims and concerns. Variant questions ask the same row for another paradigm or swap in rows such as values (excluded versus formative) or ethics, so memorise the table by columns. Option c is right because in Guba and Lincoln's scheme the constructivist inquirer's voice is the 'passionate participant' who facilitates multivoice reconstruction.","traps":{"a":"The 'disinterested scientist' who informs decision makers, policy makers and change agents is the positivist and postpositivist voice in Guba and Lincoln (1994). The inquirer stands apart, keeps values out, and hands objective findings to those who act on them. Constructivists reject this detachment, since findings are co-created. It tempts because it is the default picture of a researcher in most psychology training.","b":"The 'transformative intellectual' as advocate and activist is the critical theory voice in Guba and Lincoln (1994), drawing on Frankfurt School critical theory, feminism and Paulo Freire's emancipatory pedagogy. The inquirer aims to expose structures of oppression and change them. Constructivists facilitate many voices rather than advocate one position. It tempts because constructivism and critical theory share a subjectivist epistemology and often appear together.","d":"'None of the above' would be right only if no option matched the constructivist voice. Choice c reproduces Guba and Lincoln's own wording for constructivism, the 'passionate participant' facilitating multivoice reconstruction, so this option fails. It tempts candidates who know constructivists reject the detached scientist but cannot recall the exact phrase, and who suspect the other labels are invented."}},{"q":"What was Socrates' method of gaining knowledge?","concept":"Socrates (c. 470 to 399 BCE) wrote nothing; we know his method mainly through the dialogues of his student Plato. His way of gaining knowledge was the elenchus (Greek for refutation or cross-examination), also called the Socratic method or Socratic dialectic. It works in steps. A person states a belief, often a definition, such as 'courage is a kind of endurance' in the Laches or 'piety is what the gods love' in the Euthyphro. Socrates then asks a series of questions that lead the person to accept further statements, and finally shows that these statements contradict the original claim. The result is aporia, a state of puzzlement in which the person realises he does not know what he thought he knew. Socrates claimed to know only that he knew nothing, and he called himself a midwife (maieutics) who helps others give birth to ideas already latent in them. Knowledge, for him, was reached by reason and dialogue, not by the senses, and his central concern was ethics: virtue is knowledge, and the unexamined life is not worth living. Examiners build variants around his successors. Plato developed the theory of Forms and of knowledge as recollection (anamnesis), a rationalist and nativist position. Aristotle (384 to 322 BCE) turned to observation and classification of the natural world, the root of empiricism. Much later, Francis Bacon's Novum Organum (1620) promoted induction and experiment, which grew into experimental science. Option a is right because the elenchus, systematic questioning that exposes contradictions in a belief, was the defining Socratic method of seeking knowledge.","traps":{"b":"Observation and categorization describes the method of Aristotle (384 to 322 BCE), Plato's student, who collected and classified plants, animals and political constitutions and treated the senses as the starting point of knowledge. This is the root of empiricism and of later natural science, not of Socratic dialogue. It tempts because Socrates, Plato and Aristotle are taught as one chain, and examiners like to swap their methods.","c":"Intuition means grasping a truth directly, without step-by-step reasoning. In philosophy it is linked with later rationalists such as René Descartes (Meditations, 1641), who treated clear and distinct ideas as self-evident. Socrates did the opposite: he distrusted confident first impressions and tested them by slow questioning. It tempts because Socrates spoke of an inner divine voice (daimonion) that warned him, which sounds intuitive.","d":"Experimentation means deliberately manipulating a variable and measuring its effect under controlled conditions, the method that Francis Bacon (1620) championed and that psychology adopted when Wilhelm Wundt opened his Leipzig laboratory in 1879. Socrates never manipulated anything; his only tool was conversation. It tempts because his questioning is loosely described as 'testing' ideas, and because candidates assume any method of gaining knowledge must be scientific."}},{"q":"What did Descartes' dualism influence?","concept":"René Descartes (1596 to 1650) is the father of modern rationalism and the classic source of mind-body dualism, set out in his Meditations (1641) and The Passions of the Soul (1649). He divided reality into two substances: an unextended thinking mind (res cogitans) and an extended, mechanical body (res extensa). The two interact, he proposed, through the pineal gland. This split influenced psychology along two lines. First, because the body was a machine, its actions could be explained mechanically. Descartes described the reflex, in which an external stimulus moves 'animal spirits' through the nerves to produce a movement with no mind involved. This mechanistic, stimulus-response view of the body fed reflexology and later behaviourism, which John B. Watson (1913) launched by treating behaviour as mechanical responses to stimuli. Second, Descartes held that the mind has innate ideas (God, self, unity, the axioms of geometry) that are not learned from experience. This nativist strand passed through Immanuel Kant to the Gestalt psychologists, such as Max Wertheimer (1912), who argued that the mind organises perception by inborn principles. Variants may ask about interactionism versus psychophysical parallelism (Gottfried Leibniz), or about 'cogito ergo sum' from the Discourse on Method (1637). Remember that Descartes founded rationalism, and that empiricism, from John Locke (1690), was largely a reply to his innate ideas. Option a is right because Cartesian mechanism shaped behaviourism and Cartesian nativism shaped Gestalt psychology.","traps":{"b":"Psychoanalysis, founded by Sigmund Freud (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900), centres on unconscious conflict, and humanistic psychology, led by Abraham Maslow and Carl Rogers in the 1950s and 1960s, centres on growth and self-actualisation. Neither is built on the Cartesian picture of a conscious mind plus a machine body; Freud in fact challenged Descartes' equation of mind with consciousness. It tempts because both are major schools every candidate recognises.","c":"Existentialism (Martin Heidegger, Jean-Paul Sartre) and phenomenology (Edmund Husserl) are philosophical movements. Husserl did start from Descartes' method of doubt in his Cartesian Meditations (1931), but both movements tried to overcome the mind-body split by studying lived, embodied experience. They are not the psychological schools that inherited Cartesian mechanism and nativism. It tempts because Husserl openly named Descartes as his starting point.","d":"Rationalism (knowledge comes from reason and innate ideas) is the philosophical position Descartes himself founded, and empiricism (knowledge comes from sense experience), from John Locke (1690) onward, grew largely as a reply to his innate ideas. These are theories of knowledge that came before psychology, not schools that his dualism later shaped. It tempts because Descartes is the textbook rationalist and appears in every rationalism versus empiricism question."}},{"q":"What is the basic premise of Structuralism?","concept":"Structuralism was the first formal school of psychology. Its name came from Edward Bradford Titchener (1898), an Englishman who trained under Wilhelm Wundt at Leipzig and then taught at Cornell University. Wundt had founded the first psychology laboratory in 1879 and called his own system voluntarism; Titchener narrowed it into structuralism. The subject matter was conscious experience, defined as experience dependent on the experiencing person. The aim was a kind of chemistry of the mind: break consciousness into its basic elements, then find out how they combine. Titchener stated three problems: reduce conscious processes to their simplest components, determine the laws by which these elements are combined and connected, and relate them to their physiological conditions. He named three elements: sensations (the basis of perception), images (the basis of ideas) and affective states or feelings (the basis of emotion). Each element had attributes such as quality, intensity, duration and clearness. The method was analytic introspection by trained observers, who had to avoid the stimulus error, that is, describing the object ('a red apple') instead of the raw experience. The school faded after Titchener's death in 1927. Know the reactions to it: functionalism (William James, 1890; John Dewey) asked what consciousness is for; behaviourism (John B. Watson, 1913) rejected introspection; Gestalt psychology (Max Wertheimer, 1912) rejected elementism; psychoanalysis studied the unconscious. Option d is right because structuralism's stated premise was to describe the components of consciousness and how they are combined and connected.","traps":{"a":"Social structures and institutions (family, class, caste, economy) shaping behaviour is the subject of sociology, for instance the work of Émile Durkheim (The Division of Labour in Society, 1893), Talcott Parsons (The Social System, 1951), or the structural anthropology of Claude Lévi-Strauss (1958). These share only the word 'structure' with Titchener's school, which looked inward at the elements of individual consciousness. It tempts purely because of the name.","b":"The power of unconscious processes is the premise of psychoanalysis, founded by Sigmund Freud (1900), who argued that most mental life lies outside awareness. Titchener's structuralism studied only conscious experience that a trained observer could report; it had no place for an unconscious. It tempts because psychoanalysis also describes a 'structure' of the mind, the id, ego and superego of Freud's structural model (1923).","c":"Free will and personal agency are the premise of humanistic psychology (Carl Rogers, Abraham Maslow) and of existential psychology (Rollo May), the 'third force' of the 1950s and 1960s. Structuralism was strictly descriptive and elementistic; it did not deal with choice, values or the whole person. It tempts because Wundt called his own system voluntarism, a word that sounds as if it is about free will."}},{"q":"What is Sullivan's interpersonal theory?","concept":"Harry Stack Sullivan, an American psychiatrist, set out his interpersonal theory most fully in The Interpersonal Theory of Psychiatry (1953), published after his death in 1949. He belonged to the neo-Freudian, social-psychological group with Karen Horney (1945) and Erich Fromm (1941), who moved away from Sigmund Freud's biological drives. For Sullivan, personality cannot be studied in an isolated individual. He defined it as 'the relatively enduring pattern of recurrent interpersonal situations which characterize a human life'. A person exists only in a field of relations with others, real or imagined, so personality is an energy system in constant exchange with its social environment, an open system rather than a closed container of instincts. Key terms recur in exams. Tensions are of two kinds: needs (for tenderness, intimacy) and anxiety, which passes from mother to infant through empathy. Dynamisms are recurring patterns of behaviour; the self-system protects against anxiety using security operations such as selective inattention. Personifications are images of self ('good-me', 'bad-me', 'not-me') and of others. Experience develops through three modes: prototaxic (undifferentiated flow), parataxic (private, illogical links, the source of parataxic distortion, close to transference) and syntaxic (consensually validated, shared through language). He described developmental epochs from infancy to late adolescence and the therapist's role as participant observer. Contrast this with Freud's intrapsychic model and with the behaviourism of B. F. Skinner (1938). Option d is right because Sullivan saw personality as an open system that exists only in continuing interaction with its interpersonal environment.","traps":{"a":"The importance of unconscious processes is the defining claim of Sigmund Freud's psychoanalysis (1900): behaviour is driven by repressed, mainly sexual and aggressive, wishes within the individual's mind. Sullivan kept some dynamic ideas, such as selective inattention, but moved the focus from intrapsychic drives to relationships between people. It tempts because Sullivan trained in the psychoanalytic tradition and is grouped with the neo-Freudians.","b":"Social structures and institutions (class, economy, religion, the state) are the level of analysis of sociology and, within psychology, of writers such as Erich Fromm (1941), who linked character to the social and economic system. Sullivan worked at a smaller scale: the concrete relationship between two or more persons, starting with mother and infant. It tempts because both views are 'social' alternatives to Freud.","c":"Studying observable behaviour in social settings describes a behaviourist or social learning approach, for instance Albert Bandura's (1977) social learning theory or experimental social psychology. Sullivan studied inner experience too, including anxiety, personifications and modes of experience, inferred through the therapist's participant observation. It tempts because 'interpersonal' sounds like 'social behaviour', yet Sullivan's theory is about personality, not observable acts alone."}},{"q":"What are the five fundamental needs identified by Erich Fromm?","concept":"Erich Fromm, a German-born psychoanalyst and social philosopher, was one of the neo-Freudians who placed personality in its social and historical setting. In Escape from Freedom (1941) he argued that modern people, freed from medieval ties, feel isolated and may flee from freedom through authoritarianism, destructiveness or automaton conformity. In The Sane Society (1955) he proposed that humans, unlike animals, have lost instinctive harmony with nature; reason and self-awareness create an existential dilemma, and from it arise five distinctly human needs. Relatedness is the need to unite with others, best met through productive love rather than submission or domination. Transcendence is the need to rise above our passive, created status, met by creating or, failing that, by destroying. Rootedness is the need for belonging and a sense of home, met by brotherliness rather than fixation on mother, family or nation. Sense of identity is the need to experience oneself as a distinct individual, rather than through herd conformity. Frame of orientation is the need for a stable map of the world and an object of devotion, met best by reason rather than irrational belief. Fromm also described character orientations: four non-productive types (receptive, exploitative, hoarding and marketing) and the healthy productive orientation. Do not confuse these needs with the ranked hierarchy of Abraham Maslow (1943) or with self-determination theory, which also uses the word relatedness. Option d is right because relatedness, transcendence, rootedness, identity and orientation are exactly Fromm's five existential needs.","traps":{"a":"Safety, love and belonging, esteem and self-actualisation are levels of Abraham Maslow's (1943) hierarchy of needs; the full model starts with physiological needs at the base. Maslow ranked needs so that lower ones must be largely met before higher ones dominate. Fromm's needs are not ranked and arise from the human existential condition. It tempts because Maslow's list is the best-known five-item needs list in psychology.","b":"Competence, autonomy and relatedness are the three basic psychological needs of self-determination theory by Edward Deci and Richard Ryan (1985), which explains intrinsic motivation. Self-esteem and purpose are padding. The option borrows 'relatedness', Fromm's first need, to look familiar. It tempts because it shares one term with the correct list and sounds like a modern humanistic account of human needs.","c":"Hunger, thirst, shelter, rest and reproduction are biological or physiological needs shared with other animals, the drives of Clark Hull's (1943) drive-reduction theory and the base of Maslow's pyramid. Fromm deliberately separated these animal needs from the distinctly human, existential needs that come from self-awareness and reason. It tempts because 'fundamental needs' sounds as if it should mean survival needs."}},{"q":"Who coined the term \"Mom-ism\" to describe the cultural idealization of motherhood?","concept":"Momism is a term from mid-twentieth-century American cultural criticism. The word itself first appeared in the writer Philip Wylie's best-selling polemic Generation of Vipers (1942), which mocked the sentimental cult of 'Mom' as a smothering, self-righteous figure who kept her sons dependent. The idea was taken into psychiatry by Edward A. Strecker, a professor of psychiatry at the University of Pennsylvania who advised the US armed forces during the Second World War. In Their Mothers' Sons (1946) he argued that many men rejected or discharged from military service for psychiatric reasons were the products of 'moms' who had failed to emotionally wean them. Strecker gave momism its clinical form, and he is the name this exam associates with the term. The concept belongs to a family of mother-blaming ideas that clinical psychology later rejected: the schizophrenogenic mother of Frieda Fromm-Reichmann (1948); the cold 'refrigerator' parent linked with Leo Kanner, who first described autism in 1943, and with Bruno Bettelheim (1967); and the double bind hypothesis of Gregory Bateson and colleagues (1956). Each blamed a parenting style for a severe disorder, and family and genetic research did not support them. Contrast these with Karen Horney (1885 to 1952), who proposed womb envy and challenged Freudian views of women, and with the mother archetype of Carl Gustav Jung (1875 to 1961). Option d is right because, among the names listed, Edward Strecker is the one who brought momism into psychiatry, although strictly Philip Wylie coined the word.","traps":{"a":"Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) placed the mother at the centre of development through the Oedipus complex and the early mother-infant bond, but he did not coin momism. The word comes from American social criticism of the 1940s, after Freud's death. It tempts because Freud is the default answer for any question about mothers and sons, and Strecker's argument used loosely psychoanalytic language about dependency.","b":"Carl Gustav Jung (1875 to 1961) described the mother archetype, a universal image in the collective unconscious with both nourishing and devouring sides, and the mother complex. These are archetypal ideas, not cultural criticism of American mothers, and he did not use the term momism. It tempts because Jung's devouring or terrible mother sounds similar to the smothering 'mom' of Wylie and Strecker.","c":"Karen Horney (1885 to 1952) challenged Freud's view of women, proposed womb envy as a counterpart to penis envy, and explained neurosis through basic anxiety arising from parental indifference. She wrote about cultural influences on women but did not coin momism. It tempts because she is the only woman theorist in the list and is closely linked with motherhood, culture and feminine psychology."}},{"q":"Which of the following effects is characterized by a discriminatory employment pattern that keeps workers, mainly women, in the lower ranks of the job scale?","concept":"Gender inequality at work is described through a set of glass and floor metaphors that examiners like to mix. The sticky floor refers to a discriminatory employment pattern that keeps workers, mainly women, in the lowest-paid, low-status, low-mobility jobs at the bottom of the job scale, such as clerical, cleaning, care and retail work. The term is usually credited to the sociologist Catherine White Berheide (1992). It is about being stuck at the start: lack of training, part-time or informal contracts, no promotion ladders, and family responsibilities that limit mobility. The glass ceiling, a phrase popularised by a Wall Street Journal article in 1986, is the invisible barrier that stops qualified women and minorities from reaching top management even after they climb the middle ranks. The glass cliff, shown by Michelle Ryan and Alexander Haslam (2005) in their study of large UK firms, is the tendency to appoint women to leadership when an organisation is in crisis, so their position is precarious and failure more likely. The maternal wall, described by the legal scholar Joan C. Williams, is bias against workers who are mothers or become pregnant, who are assumed to be less committed. The glass escalator of Christine Williams (1992) is the reverse case: men in female-dominated jobs such as nursing are moved up quickly. Related ideas include the motherhood penalty and the leaky pipeline. Option c is right because the sticky floor is the pattern that holds women in the lower ranks rather than blocking them near the top.","traps":{"a":"The glass ceiling is an invisible but real barrier near the top of an organisation that blocks qualified women and minorities from senior management and boardroom posts. The metaphor was popularised by a 1986 Wall Street Journal article. It concerns the upper ranks, while this stem is about the lower ranks. It tempts because it is the most famous gender-discrimination metaphor and is often used loosely for any barrier.","b":"The maternal wall, a term from the legal scholar Joan C. Williams, is discrimination against employees who are pregnant, take maternity leave or have caregiving duties, based on the stereotype that mothers are less competent or committed. It can strike at any level of the job scale and is defined by parenthood, not rank. It tempts because it is a real women-specific barrier that also limits progress.","d":"The glass cliff, identified by Michelle Ryan and Alexander Haslam (2005), is the over-appointment of women to top leadership roles when a company is already in crisis or performing poorly, which makes those positions risky and failure likely. It concerns women who have reached the top, not those kept at the bottom. It tempts because it shares the 'glass' family name with the glass ceiling."}},{"q":"How is relative poverty measured?","concept":"Poverty is measured in two basic ways. Absolute poverty means lacking the income needed for a fixed minimum of food, shelter and other necessities, defined by a poverty line that does not move with the living standards of the society. The World Bank's international poverty line, a fixed number of dollars a day adjusted for purchasing power, and India's lines from the Tendulkar Committee (2009) and the Rangarajan Committee (2014) are absolute measures. Relative poverty means having so much less than others in one's own society that one cannot take part in its ordinary way of life. The sociologist Peter Townsend built this idea of relative deprivation in Poverty in the United Kingdom (1979). It is measured as the percentage of the population whose income falls below a fixed proportion of the median income: the OECD usually uses 50 percent of median household income, and the European Union uses 60 percent. The median is used because a few very rich households pull the mean upward. Because the line rises as the society gets richer, relative poverty is really a measure of inequality, and it may not fall during economic growth. Examiners also test types of poverty: situational poverty is caused by a specific event such as job loss, illness or divorce and is often temporary, while generational poverty lasts two or more generations, a distinction popularised by Ruby Payne (1996). Oscar Lewis proposed the culture of poverty (1959). Option b is right because relative poverty is the share of people below a fixed fraction of median income.","traps":{"a":"Situational poverty is poverty caused by a particular event, such as job loss, death of an earner, illness, divorce or a natural disaster, and it is often temporary; it is contrasted with generational poverty, as in Ruby Payne (1996). It is a type or cause of poverty, not a measurement method, and it says nothing about comparison with median income. It tempts because it sounds technical and 'relative to circumstances'.","c":"The percentage of the population living in rural areas is a measure of urbanisation (or its reverse), not of poverty. Rural areas often have higher poverty rates, and India has historically drawn separate rural and urban poverty lines, which is why the option feels plausible. But location alone does not define anyone as poor, absolutely or relatively. It tempts candidates who link poverty with villages.","d":"The percentage of the population living in urban areas is the urbanisation rate, a demographic indicator. Urban poverty exists, for example in slums, and is measured separately, but living in a city does not itself make a person poor. The option mirrors the rural one and is included as filler. It tempts only candidates who confuse where poverty is measured with how it is measured."}},{"q":"What are the three components of Deiner's tripartite model of subjective wellbeing?","concept":"Subjective well-being (SWB) is the scientific term for what people call happiness: how a person evaluates his or her own life, both in thought and in feeling. Ed Diener set out the tripartite model in a Psychological Bulletin review (1984), and it remains the standard definition. The three components are: life satisfaction, a cognitive, global judgement of one's life against one's own standards; frequent positive affect, such as joy, interest and contentment; and infrequent negative affect, such as sadness, anxiety and anger. High SWB means high life satisfaction, high positive affect and low negative affect. A key finding is that positive and negative affect are partly independent, as Norman Bradburn (1969) had shown, so reducing distress does not automatically create joy. Measurement follows the model: the five-item Satisfaction With Life Scale of Diener, Robert Emmons, Randy Larsen and Sharon Griffin (1985) for the cognitive part, and the PANAS of David Watson, Lee Anna Clark and Auke Tellegen (1988) for affect. Some writers add domain satisfactions (work, family, health). Contrast SWB, which is hedonic well-being, with eudaimonic models: the psychological well-being of Carol Ryff (1989), with six dimensions (self-acceptance, positive relations, autonomy, environmental mastery, purpose in life, personal growth), and the PERMA model of Martin Seligman (2011). Related ideas include the hedonic treadmill of Philip Brickman and Donald Campbell (1971). Option c is right because Diener's cognitive component is life satisfaction, joined by the presence of positive affect and the absence of negative affect.","traps":{"a":"Physical wellness (health, fitness, absence of illness) is a domain of life that people may be satisfied with, and it appears in multidimensional wellness models from health promotion. Diener's cognitive component is not a judgement about one particular domain; it is a global evaluation of life as a whole. It tempts because the two affect components are copied correctly, so only the first term differs.","b":"Spiritual wellness, a sense of meaning and connection with something larger, belongs to holistic wellness models and to research on spirituality and religiosity. It is not part of Diener's definition, which is deliberately content-free: people judge life by their own standards. It tempts because it keeps the two correct affect components and swaps only the cognitive part, and because meaning appears in eudaimonic models.","d":"Social wellness, or social well-being, is a separate model by Corey Keyes (1998) with five parts: social integration, acceptance, contribution, actualisation and coherence. Diener's cognitive component is global life satisfaction, not social functioning. It tempts because it keeps the two correct affect components and because positive relations with others appear in Carol Ryff's psychological well-being, a neighbouring model."}},{"q":"What is the set point theory of weight?","concept":"Set point theory holds that each person has a biologically regulated body weight, or more exactly a level of body fat, which the body defends much as a thermostat defends a temperature. Its roots are in the lipostatic hypothesis of Gordon Kennedy (1953), who proposed that the brain monitors fat stores and adjusts intake. Richard Nisbett (1972) used the idea to argue that many obese people are biologically underweight relative to their own higher set point. The set point is thought to be shaped by genes and early development. When weight falls below it, the body compensates: hunger rises, and energy expenditure drops more than the loss of tissue alone predicts. Rudolph Leibel, Michael Rosenbaum and Jules Hirsch (1995) showed that maintaining a 10 percent weight loss reduced daily energy expenditure beyond what was expected, while weight gain raised it. The discovery of leptin by Jeffrey Friedman's group (1994), a hormone released by fat cells that signals the hypothalamus, gave the theory a mechanism. This explains why most diets fail in the long run. Critics prefer a settling point model, in which weight drifts to a balance shaped by the food environment rather than a fixed target. Know the neighbouring ideas: restraint theory and disinhibition (C. Peter Herman and Janet Polivy, 1975), social contagion of obesity (Nicholas Christakis and James Fowler, 2007), and weight cycling. Option b is right because set point theory states that each person has a biological weight and resists weight loss by lowering energy expenditure.","traps":{"a":"Stress and anxiety disinhibiting eating comes from the restraint theory of C. Peter Herman and Janet Polivy (1975): chronic dieters hold back eating by cognitive control, and stress, alcohol or a broken diet rule releases that restraint, producing overeating (the 'what-the-hell effect'). This is a psychological account of eating, not a biologically defended weight. It tempts because it also explains why dieting often fails.","c":"Obesity spreading through social networks is the social contagion finding of Nicholas Christakis and James Fowler (2007), who analysed Framingham Heart Study data and found that a person's chance of becoming obese rose sharply if a friend, sibling or spouse became obese. It is a social and environmental explanation, not a biological set point. It tempts because it is a well-known modern theory of weight gain.","d":"Weight cycling, or yo-yo dieting, is repeated loss and regain. Animal work by Kelly Brownell and colleagues (1986) suggested that each cycle made later weight loss slower and regain faster, partly through lower metabolic rate; human evidence is mixed. It is a separate hypothesis about the effects of dieting history. It tempts because it resembles set point compensation, but it does not state the core claim of a biologically defended weight."}},{"q":"What is Buber's concept of the 'I-Thou' attitude?","concept":"Martin Buber, an Austrian-born Jewish philosopher, described two basic attitudes a person can take toward the world in his book I and Thou (Ich und Du, 1923). In the I-It attitude, the other is experienced as an object: something to be observed, measured, categorised, used or analysed into parts. Much of daily life, and all of science, properly runs on I-It. In the I-Thou (I-You) attitude, the other is met as a whole, unique person in a direct, mutual, present relationship, with no purpose beyond the meeting itself. Buber wrote that 'all real living is meeting', and held that the self becomes fully a self only through such relation. For psychology this became the basis of the dialogical and existential view of therapy: the therapist does not only diagnose the client but meets and confirms the client in the totality of his or her being, as a unique person embedded in relationships. Buber held a public dialogue with Carl Rogers in 1957 on whether the therapy relationship could be fully mutual; the core conditions Rogers described the same year (unconditional positive regard, empathy and congruence) are close to the I-Thou stance. The idea also shaped existential therapists such as Rollo May (1958) and Irvin Yalom (1980), and Gestalt therapy's emphasis on contact. Contrast it with the nomothetic approach, which seeks general laws and treats people as instances of categories, and with reductionism, which explains a person by breaking him into parts. Option b is right because I-Thou means validating the client as a whole, unique and socially embedded person.","traps":{"a":"Experiencing the person as an objective entity to be systematised and analysed is exactly Buber's I-It attitude, the opposite pole of I-Thou. In I-It the other becomes a thing to know or use, as in a purely diagnostic or nomothetic stance. Buber did not condemn I-It, which science needs, but it is not genuine meeting. It tempts because it is drawn from the same theory and uses Buber's own contrast.","c":"Breaking the individual down into universal parts is reductionism or elementism, the method of structuralism and of trait or factor approaches that describe people by common dimensions. It is closest to the I-It attitude, and the opposite of meeting a person as a unique whole. It tempts because it sounds academic and systematic, and candidates may think a philosophical concept must involve analysis into components.","d":"'None of the above' is a filler option. It would be right only if no listed choice captured I-Thou, but option b clearly states Buber's idea of meeting and confirming the whole, unique person. It tempts candidates who notice that b uses therapy language ('client') that Buber himself did not use in 1923, and who conclude that no option is faithful to the original philosophy."}},{"q":"What type of information is included in AXIS III of the DSM-IV-TR?","concept":"The multiaxial system was introduced by the American Psychiatric Association in DSM-III (1980) and kept in DSM-IV (1994) and its text revision, DSM-IV-TR (2000). It asked the clinician to record information on five axes, so that a diagnosis captured the whole clinical picture rather than a single label. Axis I: clinical disorders and other conditions that may be a focus of clinical attention, such as schizophrenia, major depressive disorder, anxiety disorders and substance use disorders. Axis II: personality disorders and mental retardation, plus prominent maladaptive personality features and defence mechanisms. Axis III: general medical conditions potentially relevant to understanding or managing the mental disorder, such as hypothyroidism in a patient with depression, or a head injury. Axis IV: psychosocial and environmental problems, such as bereavement, unemployment, housing or legal problems. Axis V: Global Assessment of Functioning (GAF), a single rating on a 100-point scale. A memory aid: I clinical, II personality and retardation, III medical, IV stressors, V functioning. DSM-5 (2013) abolished the multiaxial system: Axes I to III were merged into one list of diagnoses, Axis IV became notes on psychosocial factors and Z codes, and the GAF was dropped, with the WHODAS 2.0 suggested instead. Mental retardation was renamed intellectual disability. ICD-10 (1992) and ICD-11 (adopted 2019, in force 2022) do not use these axes. Option c is right because Axis III of DSM-IV-TR records general medical conditions.","traps":{"a":"Personality disorders and mental retardation were placed on Axis II, to make sure that long-standing patterns were not overlooked when an Axis I disorder drew attention. Examples are borderline personality disorder and mild mental retardation, now called intellectual disability (intellectual developmental disorder) in DSM-5 (2013). It tempts because Axis II is the axis people remember best, and the order of axes is easy to mix up.","b":"Clinical psychiatric diagnoses, the main mental disorders such as schizophrenia, bipolar disorder, major depression and anxiety disorders, were recorded on Axis I. This was the primary diagnostic axis and listed everything except personality disorders and mental retardation. It tempts because 'clinical diagnosis' sounds like the most important information, and some candidates assume medical and psychiatric conditions shared an axis.","d":"Psychosocial and environmental problems, such as death of a family member, divorce, job loss, poverty, discrimination or problems with the legal system, were recorded on Axis IV. They are stressors that can influence diagnosis, treatment and prognosis. It tempts because Axes III and IV are adjacent and both describe context around the mental disorder; one records physical illness, the other records life stressors."}},{"q":"What is the term used to describe the inherited vulnerability?","concept":"The diathesis-stress model explains psychological disorders as the product of two things: a predisposition and a trigger. Diathesis comes from the Greek for disposition or arrangement; in older medicine it meant a constitutional tendency to a disease. In psychopathology it is the term for an inherited or early-acquired vulnerability, usually genetic or biological, though cognitive styles and early adversity can also count. Stress is the environmental or life event that acts on the diathesis to produce the disorder. Neither is enough alone: a person with a strong diathesis may need only mild stress, while a person with a weak diathesis can stay well unless stress is severe. Paul Meehl (1962) proposed an inherited neural defect, schizotaxia, which can develop into schizotypy and, under stress, into schizophrenia. Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977) restated this as the vulnerability-stress model, in which vulnerability is a relatively enduring trait while episodes come and go. Evidence includes identical-twin concordance for schizophrenia of roughly 40 to 50 percent, well short of 100, so environment matters. Cognitive diatheses are used for depression in the cognitive model of Aaron T. Beck (1967) and the hopelessness theory of Lyn Abramson, Gerald Metalsky and Lauren Alloy (1989). A broader frame is the biopsychosocial model of George Engel (1977). Distinguish the terms: diathesis is the technical name for the inherited predisposition, the stressor is the precipitating event, and vulnerability is the broader everyday word. Option c is right because diathesis is the technical term for an inherited vulnerability.","traps":{"a":"A stressor is the environmental event or demand that sets off a stress response, such as bereavement, job loss or conflict; Hans Selye (1956) and Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984) built models of how people respond to it. In the diathesis-stress model the stressor is the precipitating, external half, not the inherited predisposition. It tempts because 'stressor' is the other half of the same model.","b":"Vulnerability is the general word for susceptibility, and Joseph Zubin and Bonnie Spring (1977) used it in their vulnerability-stress model. But it is the broader term, covering inherited and acquired weaknesses, and the stem asks for the technical name of an inherited vulnerability. It tempts strongly because it simply repeats a word from the stem; examiners often place the stem's own word as a decoy.","d":"'Enduring factor' is not a standard term in the model. Zubin and Spring did describe vulnerability as a relatively enduring trait, while stress and episodes are time-limited, and case formulation speaks of predisposing (enduring) versus precipitating and maintaining factors. It tempts because a diathesis is indeed enduring, but this descriptive phrase is not the name of the concept."}},{"q":"The greater the intensity of a stimulus, greater the probability that a learned response will be elicited. This is called","concept":"Clark L. Hull built the most formal learning theory of the neo-behaviourist period, a hypothetico-deductive drive-reduction theory, in Principles of Behavior (1943) and later in A Behavior System (1952). He wrote behaviour as equations linking stimulus inputs to response outputs through intervening variables. The core is reaction potential, sEr, the tendency of a stimulus to evoke a learned response. In the later version, sEr = sHr × D × V × K. Here sHr is habit strength, built up by the number of reinforced trials; D is drive, a state of need such as hours of food deprivation; V is stimulus-intensity dynamism, the principle that the more intense the stimulus, the greater the reaction potential and so the probability that the learned response is elicited; and K is incentive motivation, the size or quality of the reward, added after Leo Crespi (1942) showed that changing reward size changes performance at once. Because the terms multiply, if any one is zero, no response occurs. Reaction potential is then reduced by inhibition: reactive inhibition (Ir), a fatigue-like state from responding, and conditioned inhibition (sIr). Oscillation (sOr) adds random variation, and a response appears only if reaction potential exceeds the reaction threshold (sLr). Hull measured response strength by latency, amplitude and resistance to extinction. Kenneth Spence (1956) refined incentive motivation, and John Dollard and Neal Miller (1950) carried the theory into personality. Option a is right because Hull's name for the principle that a more intense stimulus raises the probability of the learned response is stimulus-intensity dynamism.","traps":{"b":"Incentive motivation (K) is Hull's term for the effect of the amount or quality of reward on reaction potential, added after Leo Crespi (1942) found that rats ran faster as soon as reward size was increased. It concerns the reinforcer that follows the response, not the strength of the stimulus that elicits it. It tempts because K and V sit side by side in the same Hullian equation.","c":"Response amplitude is one of Hull's measures of response strength, the magnitude of the response, such as the number of drops of saliva or the force of a lever press, alongside latency, probability and resistance to extinction. It is a dependent variable, an outcome, not the name of a principle relating stimulus intensity to responding. It tempts because a stronger stimulus may indeed produce a larger response.","d":"Reaction threshold (sLr) in Hull's system is the minimum reaction potential that must be exceeded before any response appears. It is a fixed cut-off, not a law about stimulus intensity. In sensory psychology a threshold is also the minimum stimulus intensity detected, as in the psychophysics of Gustav Fechner (1860), which makes the option sound related to stimulus intensity. That overlap is why it tempts."}},{"q":"The sudden increase in performance of an organism upon reinforcement, despite never being reinforced in the previous trials is _____________","concept":"Latent learning is learning that takes place without reinforcement and is not shown in behaviour until a reason to use it appears. It was demonstrated by Hugh Blodgett (1929) and most famously by Edward C. Tolman and Charles Honzik (1930) at Berkeley. Three groups of hungry rats ran a complex maze. One group found food in the goal box every day and gradually made fewer errors. A second group was never rewarded and improved only a little. The third group was not rewarded for the first ten days and behaved like the unrewarded group; when food was introduced on day 11, its errors dropped sharply on the very next trials, matching or bettering the always-rewarded group. The sudden jump showed that the rats had been learning the maze all along; reinforcement affected performance, not learning. Tolman (1948) explained this with the cognitive map, an internal representation of the layout of the environment, and called his position purposive behaviourism. This challenged the reinforcement theories of Edward Thorndike (1898) and Clark Hull (1943), and was an early root of cognitive psychology. The learning-performance distinction also appears in Albert Bandura's (1965) Bobo doll study: children who saw a model punished imitated less, but when offered rewards they all reproduced the aggression. Other variants: place learning versus response learning, insight learning (Wolfgang Köhler, 1925) and incidental learning. Option d is right because a sudden rise in performance once reinforcement is introduced, after unreinforced trials, is the hallmark of latent learning.","traps":{"a":"Observational learning is learning by watching a model, described by Albert Bandura in social learning theory (1977) through four processes: attention, retention, reproduction and motivation. The learner acquires behaviour without performing it or being reinforced directly. The stem, however, describes an organism's own unrewarded trials followed by reinforcement, with no model involved. It tempts because Bandura also separated learning from performance.","b":"Discrimination learning is learning to respond to one stimulus and not to a similar one, because only the first (the discriminative stimulus or S+) is reinforced, as when the dogs of Ivan Pavlov (1927) learned to salivate to one tone but not another. It needs differential reinforcement from the start, not unreinforced trials followed by reward. It tempts because it also involves trials, mazes and choices.","c":"Vicarious reinforcement means that seeing another person rewarded for a behaviour raises the observer's tendency to perform it, as in Albert Bandura's (1965) Bobo doll study, where children who saw the model rewarded copied more aggression. It requires a model who is reinforced. In the stem the organism itself had never been reinforced and then was. It tempts because it explains behaviour shown without direct reinforcement."}},{"q":"Cocktail party effect is better explained by","concept":"The cocktail party effect is the ability to follow one conversation in a noisy room while still noticing important material, such as your own name, in a conversation you were ignoring. Colin Cherry (1953) studied it with dichotic listening and shadowing: a different message goes to each ear, and the listener repeats one aloud. Listeners could report almost nothing of the meaning of the unattended message, not even a change of language, but noticed physical changes such as a male voice becoming female or a tone. Donald Broadbent (1958) explained this with his filter model, an early-selection theory: a selective filter acts like an all-or-none switch, passing one channel on the basis of physical features and blocking the rest before meaning is analysed. The problem was breakthrough. Neville Moray (1959) found that about a third of listeners heard their own name in the unattended ear, and Anne Treisman (1960) showed listeners following a story when it moved to the wrong ear. Treisman's attenuation model (1964) replaced the switch with an attenuator: unattended messages are turned down, not cut off, and words with low thresholds (one's name, danger words, expected words) can still reach awareness. J. Anthony Deutsch and Diana Deutsch (1963) proposed late selection, in which all input is analysed for meaning. Capacity theory (Daniel Kahneman, 1973) and automatic versus controlled processing (Walter Schneider and Richard Shiffrin, 1977) deal with resource limits. Option b is right because Treisman's attenuator model explains how one's name breaks through an unattended conversation.","traps":{"a":"The switch model is Donald Broadbent's (1958) filter theory, in which a selective filter acts like an all-or-none switch that passes one channel by physical features and blocks the others completely before meaning is processed. It explains why Cherry's listeners missed the unattended content, but it cannot explain breakthrough of one's own name, the core of the cocktail party effect. It tempts because it was the first formal model of selective attention.","c":"Automatic versus controlled processing is the dual-process account of Walter Schneider and Richard Shiffrin (1977): practised tasks become fast, parallel and effortless, while novel tasks need slow, serial, capacity-limited control. The Stroop effect (John Ridley Stroop, 1935) is the classic example. The model is about practice and effort, not about how an unattended channel is filtered. It tempts because detecting one's own name feels automatic.","d":"Capacity theory is Daniel Kahneman's (1973) resource model in Attention and Effort: attention is a limited pool of mental effort, shared among tasks by an allocation policy that depends on arousal, enduring dispositions and momentary intentions. It explains dual-task interference and divided attention rather than which item in an ignored message breaks through. It tempts because Kahneman's model is the standard alternative to filter theories."}},{"q":"Which of the following is a factor of the extent of lateral inhibition","concept":"Lateral inhibition is the process by which an excited neuron reduces the activity of its neighbours. It was first measured precisely by Haldan Keffer Hartline and Floyd Ratliff in the eye of the horseshoe crab, Limulus, in the 1950s; Hartline shared the 1967 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine. Limulus is useful because each ommatidium (a simple eye unit) has its own receptor with a large nerve fibre that can be recorded, and neighbouring receptors are linked by a lateral plexus that carries inhibition sideways. The classic experiment: light on receptor A alone produces a high firing rate in A's fibre. When light is also shone on a neighbouring receptor B, A's firing drops. The more intense the light on B, the stronger the inhibition of A. So the extent of lateral inhibition depends on how strongly the inhibiting cell is stimulated, and it also falls off with distance between the two cells. In the human retina the same job is done by horizontal cells and amacrine cells, which spread signals sideways between photoreceptors, bipolar cells and ganglion cells. Lateral inhibition creates centre-surround receptive fields, first described in the cat by Stephen Kuffler (1953). It explains Mach bands (Ernst Mach, 1865), the Hermann grid illusion and simultaneous contrast, all of which exaggerate brightness differences at borders. Variant questions ask what lateral inhibition does (enhances edges and contrast) or which cells carry it. Option b is right because the extent of lateral inhibition depends on the intensity of light falling on the inhibiting cell.","traps":{"a":"Time difference between two lights describes temporal factors studied in apparent motion, such as the phi phenomenon of Max Wertheimer (1912), and in visual masking, where a second stimulus shortly after the first reduces its visibility. In Hartline's Limulus experiments the lights were presented together, and the variable that changed inhibition was the intensity on the neighbouring receptor. It tempts because two-light procedures appear in both kinds of experiment.","c":"Wavelength determines the hue we see and which cone type responds most, the basis of the Young-Helmholtz trichromatic theory (Thomas Young, 1802) and of colour-opponent cells. In Hartline's work the strength of lateral inhibition depended on how much light reached the inhibiting receptor, not on its colour. It tempts because wavelength is a basic property of light and the option copies the 'inhibitory cell' wording.","d":"Frequency of a light wave is the same physical property as wavelength seen the other way (frequency equals the speed of light divided by wavelength), so it again codes colour, not intensity. Do not confuse it with firing frequency, the rate at which a neuron fires, which is how inhibition is measured in Limulus recordings. It tempts because 'frequency' appears in nerve recordings and the option mimics the correct wording."}},{"q":"Lateral inhibition ________","concept":"Lateral inhibition means that when a neuron is excited, it suppresses the firing of its neighbours through sideways connections. In the vertebrate retina these connections are made by horizontal cells and amacrine cells; in the horseshoe crab, Limulus, Haldan Keffer Hartline and Floyd Ratliff measured the effect directly in the 1950s. Its main effect is to exaggerate differences. Consider a border between a light area and a dark area. A receptor just inside the light side receives strong inhibition from its bright neighbours on one side but weak inhibition from its dark neighbours on the other, so it fires more than receptors deeper in the light area. A receptor just inside the dark side gets extra inhibition from its bright neighbours, so it fires less than receptors deeper in the dark. The result is a bright stripe and a dark stripe along the border, the Mach bands described by Ernst Mach (1865). The edge is sharpened, which helps us find the boundaries of objects. The same mechanism is used to explain simultaneous contrast (a grey square looks darker on a white background than on a black one) and the grey spots of the Hermann grid (Ludimar Hermann, 1870). At the level of receptive fields, lateral inhibition builds the centre-surround organisation of retinal ganglion cells described by Stephen Kuffler (1953). Overall brightness is handled by light adaptation and lightness constancy, and colour by cone types and opponent processes (Ewald Hering, 1878). Option a is right because lateral inhibition increases contrast at borders, so it enhances edge perception.","traps":{"b":"Dulling edge perception is the exact reverse of what lateral inhibition does. Edges blur with optical problems such as refractive error or cataract, or when contrast is low, not through lateral inhibition, which sharpens borders by exaggerating differences in firing. It tempts because the word 'inhibition' suggests weakening or reducing, so candidates assume the effect on perception must be a loss.","c":"Lowering overall brightness is not the function of lateral inhibition. Overall sensitivity to light level is controlled by light and dark adaptation (pupil size, bleaching and regeneration of photopigment), and perceived lightness stays fairly constant through lightness constancy. Lateral inhibition does reduce firing in some cells, but its job is to change relative contrast at borders. It tempts because inhibition does lower absolute firing.","d":"Colour perception depends on three cone types (trichromatic theory, Thomas Young, 1802) and on opponent processing (red-green, blue-yellow) proposed by Ewald Hering (1878). Opponent cells do use a form of inhibition, which is why the option sounds possible, but the textbook function of lateral inhibition in brightness vision is to enhance edges and contrast, not colour. It tempts because inhibition appears in both explanations."}},{"q":"Very threatening or anxiety provoking material is often unable to gain access to conscious awareness. This is called","concept":"Repression is the central defence mechanism of psychoanalysis. Sigmund Freud developed it from his work with Josef Breuer in Studies on Hysteria (1895) and later called it the cornerstone on which the whole structure of psychoanalysis rests. In repression, thoughts, memories, wishes or feelings that would cause intolerable anxiety, guilt or shame are kept out of conscious awareness automatically and unconsciously. The person does not choose to forget and does not know that anything has been pushed away. The repressed material stays active in the unconscious and returns in disguised form: in dreams, slips of the tongue and neurotic symptoms. Freud distinguished primary repression, which keeps some content from ever becoming conscious, from repression proper, which pushes back material that was once conscious. Anna Freud (1936) placed repression first among the defences in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence. The key exam contrast is suppression: a conscious, deliberate decision to put a thought out of mind, which George Vaillant (1977) ranked among the mature defences. Denial refuses to accept an external reality; dissociation splits off whole parts of experience. In cognitive psychology, Michael Anderson and Collin Green (2001) showed with the think/no-think paradigm that deliberately suppressing retrieval reduces later recall, a laboratory form of motivated forgetting. Whether traumatic memories are truly repressed and later recovered remains debated, as in the false memory work of Elizabeth Loftus (1993). Option b is right because the automatic, unconscious exclusion of threatening material from awareness is repression.","traps":{"a":"Suppression (spelt 'supression' in the option) is the conscious, voluntary effort to push a disturbing thought out of mind, for example deciding not to think about an exam result until evening. George Vaillant (1977) counted it among the mature defences because the person knows what is set aside and can return to it. The stem describes material that cannot reach awareness, which is unconscious. It tempts because both words mean keeping things out of mind.","c":"Inhibition is a general term: in memory research it covers retroactive and proactive interference and retrieval inhibition, as in the think/no-think studies of Michael Anderson and Collin Green (2001); in psychoanalysis Freud wrote of inhibitions as restrictions of ego functions (1926). It is not the specific defence that keeps threatening material out of consciousness. It tempts because it sounds like a neutral scientific synonym for repression.","d":"Motivated forgetting is the broad umbrella term for forgetting driven by a wish to avoid unpleasant memories. It includes conscious suppression and laboratory retrieval inhibition as well as repression. The stem names the specific Freudian mechanism, defined by the unconscious, automatic exclusion of anxiety-provoking material. It tempts because repression is often given as an example of motivated forgetting, so the broader category looks correct."}},{"q":"Icon and echo are part of","concept":"Sensory memory, also called the sensory register, is the first stage in the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968). It holds a brief, literal copy of incoming sensory input, large in capacity but very short-lived, so that attention can select what goes on to short-term memory. Ulric Neisser (1967) named the visual and auditory forms: iconic memory (the icon) and echoic memory (the echo). George Sperling (1960) demonstrated iconic memory. He flashed a grid of 12 letters for 50 milliseconds. With whole report, people recalled only about four letters. With partial report, a tone after the display told them which row to recall, and they could report most letters of any cued row, so nearly the whole display had been available. If the cue was delayed by about a second, the advantage vanished, showing that the icon lasts roughly a quarter to half a second. Echoic memory lasts longer, about two to four seconds, as Christopher Darwin, Michael Turvey and Robert Crowder (1972) showed with an auditory 'three-eared man' version of partial report. The echo explains why we can catch what someone just said when we were not listening. Compare the later stages: short-term memory holds about seven plus or minus two items (George A. Miller, 1956) for around 18 to 30 seconds without rehearsal; working memory (Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch, 1974) adds active processing; long-term memory is large and durable. Option d is right because the icon and the echo are the visual and auditory stores of sensory memory.","traps":{"a":"Long-term memory is the relatively permanent store of very large capacity, divided by Endel Tulving (1972) into episodic and semantic memory and later contrasted with procedural or implicit memory. Information reaches it from short-term memory through rehearsal and deeper encoding (levels of processing, Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart, 1972). Icons and echoes fade within seconds and never reach it in raw form. It tempts only as filler.","b":"Working memory is the model of active short-term processing by Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974), with a central executive, phonological loop and visuospatial sketchpad, plus the episodic buffer added in 2000. The phonological loop and sketchpad sound like auditory and visual stores, which is why the option tempts. But they hold coded, rehearsable information for active work, not a raw sensory trace.","c":"Short-term memory is the second store in the model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), holding about seven plus or minus two items (George A. Miller, 1956) for up to about 18 to 30 seconds without rehearsal. Only attended information enters it from the sensory register. The icon and echo come before this stage. It tempts because both stores are brief and candidates confuse 'very brief' with 'short-term'."}},{"q":"REM sleep is NOT characterized by","concept":"Sleep is measured by polysomnography: EEG for brain waves, EOG for eye movements and EMG for muscle tone. Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) discovered REM sleep when they saw bursts of rapid, jerky eye movements in sleeping subjects, and William Dement and Kleitman (1957) showed that people woken from REM usually report vivid dreams. Sleep has two kinds. NREM sleep has three stages in current scoring: N1 (light sleep, theta waves), N2 (sleep spindles and K-complexes) and N3 (slow-wave sleep with high-amplitude, low-frequency delta waves). The deeper NREM gets, the more synchronised the EEG becomes: large groups of cortical neurons fire in unison, producing big, slow waves. REM sleep is different. Its EEG is desynchronised: low-voltage, fast, mixed-frequency activity with sawtooth waves that looks almost like waking. The body, however, is paralysed: atonia of skeletal muscles is produced by circuits in the pons acting through the medulla, which is why we do not act out dreams; loss of this atonia causes REM sleep behaviour disorder. Other REM signs are irregular breathing and heart rate, genital arousal and poor temperature control. Because the brain looks awake while the body is limp, Michel Jouvet (1959) called it paradoxical sleep. Cycles last about 90 minutes, and REM periods grow longer towards morning, taking up about 20 to 25 percent of adult sleep. Option d is right because EEG synchrony marks slow-wave NREM sleep, whereas REM shows a desynchronised, wake-like EEG, so synchrony is the feature REM lacks.","traps":{"a":"Lack of muscle tone (atonia) is a true REM feature: brainstem circuits in the pons, acting through the medulla, inhibit spinal motor neurons so skeletal muscles go limp, sparing the eyes and diaphragm. Its loss causes REM sleep behaviour disorder, in which people act out dreams. Since the stem asks what REM is NOT, a true feature is wrong. It tempts because 'lack' sounds like an absent quality.","b":"Dreaming is strongly tied to REM: in the work of William Dement and Nathaniel Kleitman (1957), most people woken from REM reported vivid, story-like dreams. Dreams also occur in NREM, but they are usually shorter and more thought-like. Because REM is the classic dream state, this is a true characteristic. It tempts candidates who know NREM dreaming exists and over-apply that fact.","c":"Rapid eye movements give REM its name: Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman (1953) saw bursts of darting, conjugate eye movements under closed lids, recorded by electro-oculogram. They are the defining sign of this stage, so they cannot be what REM lacks. It tempts only candidates who miss the capitalised NOT in the stem and choose the most obvious feature."}},{"q":"The chemical that is responsible for feeling drowsy is","concept":"Sleepiness is explained by the two-process model of Alexander Borbely (1982). Process S is homeostatic sleep pressure: it builds the longer you stay awake and falls during sleep, especially slow-wave sleep. Process C is the circadian rhythm, set by the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus, which decides when in the 24-hour day sleep should occur. Adenosine is the best known chemical marker of Process S. Active neurons and glia use ATP for energy, and adenosine is a by-product. During long waking it accumulates outside cells, especially in the basal forebrain. It acts on A1 receptors to inhibit wake-promoting cholinergic neurons and on A2A receptors to excite sleep-promoting neurons, such as those of the ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO). The result is a growing feeling of drowsiness. During sleep adenosine is cleared, so you wake refreshed. Caffeine is the classic proof: it blocks adenosine receptors, so the pressure signal is masked, and drowsiness returns once caffeine wears off. Do not confuse adenosine with melatonin, the pineal hormone that signals darkness and the timing of sleep (Process C), not accumulated tiredness. The wake systems include orexin from the lateral hypothalamus, histamine from the tuberomammillary nucleus, noradrenaline from the locus coeruleus, serotonin from the raphe nuclei and acetylcholine from the basal forebrain and brainstem. The VLPO switches them off, a mutual inhibition that Clifford Saper and colleagues (2001) called the sleep flip-flop switch. Option a is right because adenosine accumulates with time awake and directly produces sleep pressure, the feeling of drowsiness.","traps":{"b":"Acetylcholine, released by neurons in the basal forebrain and the pedunculopontine and laterodorsal tegmental nuclei of the brainstem, promotes cortical arousal. It is high in waking and in REM sleep, where it drives the wake-like EEG, and low in slow-wave sleep. It produces alertness, not drowsiness. It tempts because it is linked to REM sleep, so candidates associate it with sleep in general.","c":"Serotonin from the raphe nuclei is most active in waking, lower in NREM and nearly silent in REM sleep, so it is a 'REM-off' signal, not a drowsiness chemical. It is also the precursor of melatonin in the pineal gland. It tempts because Michel Jouvet's research in the 1960s proposed a serotonin theory of sleep, and the melatonin link sounds sleep-related, but later work showed serotonin mainly supports waking.","d":"Orexin (hypocretin), discovered in 1998 by two independent teams, is made by neurons in the lateral hypothalamus and stabilises wakefulness by exciting the other arousal systems. Loss of orexin neurons causes narcolepsy type 1, with sleep attacks and cataplexy. So orexin opposes drowsiness; suvorexant, an orexin-receptor blocker, is even used as a sleeping pill. It tempts because it is clearly a sleep-wake chemical."}},{"q":"Raphe nuclei produces which neurotransmitter","concept":"The raphe nuclei are a chain of cell groups along the midline (raphe means 'seam') of the brainstem, running through the medulla, pons and midbrain. They are the main source of serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine, 5-HT) in the brain. Annica Dahlstrom and Kjell Fuxe (1964) mapped the brain's monoamine neurons with a fluorescence method and labelled the serotonin groups B1 to B9; most lie in the raphe. The rostral groups, the dorsal and median raphe, send axons to almost the whole forebrain, including cortex, hippocampus, amygdala and hypothalamus; the caudal groups project to the spinal cord, where they modulate pain and motor output. Serotonin is made from the amino acid tryptophan via 5-HTP and broken down to 5-HIAA, which can be measured in cerebrospinal fluid. Its functions include mood, impulse control and aggression, appetite, pain, and sleep: raphe neurons fire fast in waking, slowly in NREM and almost not at all in REM. SSRIs such as fluoxetine act by blocking serotonin reuptake. Examiners pair each nucleus with its transmitter, so learn the list: locus coeruleus, norepinephrine; substantia nigra and ventral tegmental area, dopamine; lateral hypothalamus, orexin; tuberomammillary nucleus, histamine; basal forebrain (nucleus basalis of Meynert), acetylcholine; pineal gland, melatonin. A common twist is the reverse question: which nucleus is the main serotonin source, or which transmitter falls when the raphe is lesioned. Option a is right because the raphe nuclei are the brain's principal serotonergic nuclei, producing serotonin and distributing it throughout the brain and spinal cord.","traps":{"b":"Norepinephrine (noradrenaline) in the brain comes mainly from the locus coeruleus, a small blue-tinged nucleus in the dorsal pons, labelled A6 by Annica Dahlstrom and Kjell Fuxe (1964). It drives vigilance, attention and the stress response. It is not made by the raphe. It tempts because the locus coeruleus also sits in the brainstem, close to the raphe, and both are monoamine arousal systems with wide projections.","c":"Orexin (hypocretin) is a neuropeptide made only by a small group of neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. It excites the raphe, locus coeruleus and other arousal centres to stabilise wakefulness, and its loss causes narcolepsy type 1. So orexin acts on the raphe but is not produced there. It tempts because orexin and serotonin appear together in sleep-wake chapters, and the raphe is one of orexin's targets.","d":"Brain histamine is made by the tuberomammillary nucleus in the posterior hypothalamus, which promotes wakefulness. That is why older antihistamines that cross the blood-brain barrier, such as diphenhydramine, cause drowsiness. It is not a raphe product. It tempts because histamine is another monoamine arousal transmitter with wide projections, listed in the same sleep-wake tables as serotonin from the raphe."}},{"q":"a pictorial display of a person's family relationships and medical history is ___________","concept":"A genogram is a structured, multigenerational diagram of a family that records both who is related to whom and what has happened to them: births, deaths, marriages, divorces, illnesses, psychiatric disorders, substance use, migrations and the quality of relationships. Its theoretical base is the family systems theory of Murray Bowen (1978), whose idea of multigenerational transmission holds that patterns such as anxiety, emotional cut-offs and triangles pass down the generations. The standard method was set out by Monica McGoldrick and Randy Gerson in Genograms in Family Assessment (1985). A genogram usually covers at least three generations. Squares stand for males, circles for females, an X marks a death, a horizontal line joins partners and vertical lines lead to children; the index person (identified patient) is drawn with a double outline. Special lines show close, conflictual, distant, cut-off or fused relationships. Clinicians use it in family therapy, psychiatric history-taking, genetic counselling and general practice, to spot repeating patterns and to engage the family. Neighbouring tools appear as variants. A pedigree chart is the medical genetics version, tracing how one inherited trait passes through a family, without relational detail. An ecomap, created by Ann Hartman (1978), shows the family's links with outside systems such as school, work, health services and friends. A sociogram, from Jacob L. Moreno (1934), maps likes and choices within a group, not a family. A family tree lists ancestry only. Option a is right because a genogram is the pictorial display that combines family relationships with medical and psychological history across generations.","traps":{"b":"A family tree is a genealogical chart of descent: who was born to whom across generations, often kept for heritage or inheritance. It records names, dates and lineage but not illnesses, emotional relationships or patterns of conflict and closeness. It tempts because a genogram looks like a family tree at first glance, and in everyday speech people call any family diagram a family tree.","c":"Family history is the part of a clinical case history that records illnesses, psychiatric disorders, suicides and substance use among relatives, usually written as text or a list. It is information, not a standardised pictorial display; a genogram is one way to draw it. It tempts because the stem mentions medical history, and 'family history' is exactly what a clinician collects before drawing the diagram.","d":"A gene tree is a phylogenetic diagram in evolutionary biology showing how copies of a particular gene diverged across species or populations over time. It concerns molecular ancestry, not a person's family relationships or clinical history. It tempts because 'gene' echoes the 'gen' in genogram and suggests medical genetics, so candidates guessing from the word root may pick it."}},{"q":"Low levels of which neurotransmitter leads to aggression","concept":"The best supported neurochemical finding on aggression is that low serotonin (5-HT) activity goes with impulsive, reactive aggression. The evidence started with cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) levels of 5-HIAA, the main breakdown product of serotonin. Marie Asberg and colleagues (1976) found low CSF 5-HIAA in depressed patients who made violent suicide attempts. Gerald Brown and colleagues (1979) found that military men with a history of aggression had lower CSF 5-HIAA, and Markku Linnoila and colleagues (1983) showed the drop was strongest in impulsive violent offenders rather than those who planned their crimes. Animal work agrees: monkeys with low serotonin are more aggressive and take more risks. The usual explanation is that serotonin, acting on the prefrontal cortex, supports impulse control, so low levels weaken the brakes on anger. This is why SSRIs are sometimes used for impulsive aggression and irritability. Keep the neighbours apart. Aggression is linked to high, not low, dopamine activity, and dopamine-blocking antipsychotics reduce it. Norepinephrine and epinephrine rise in hot, affective aggression, as part of the fight-or-flight response described by Walter Cannon (1915). Testosterone is linked to dominance, and a low-activity MAOA gene interacts with childhood maltreatment to predict antisocial behaviour, as Avshalom Caspi and colleagues (2002) showed. Separate impulsive (reactive) from premeditated (instrumental) aggression: the serotonin link holds mainly for the impulsive type. Option a is right because low serotonin is the neurotransmitter deficit most consistently linked to aggression, especially impulsive aggression, in human and animal research.","traps":{"b":"Dopamine, from the substantia nigra and ventral tegmental area, drives reward, motivation and movement. Where it is linked to aggression, the link runs the other way: increased dopamine activity, as with amphetamine or in mania and psychosis, raises aggression, and dopamine-blocking antipsychotics calm it. Low dopamine is linked to Parkinson's disease and anhedonia, not aggression. It tempts because dopamine is the best known 'behaviour' transmitter.","c":"Epinephrine (adrenaline) is mainly a hormone of the adrenal medulla. It prepares the body for fight or flight, as described by Walter Cannon (1915), raising heart rate, blood pressure and blood glucose during anger and fear. Aggression goes with surges, not low levels, of epinephrine. It tempts because anger feels like an 'adrenaline rush', so candidates connect adrenaline with aggression without checking the direction.","d":"Norepinephrine (noradrenaline), from the locus coeruleus and sympathetic nerves, raises arousal, vigilance and readiness for action. Affective aggression is associated with heightened, not low, noradrenergic activity, and beta-blockers such as propranolol are sometimes used to reduce aggression after brain injury. It tempts because it is a monoamine studied alongside serotonin in aggression research, so the candidate must check the direction of the link."}},{"q":"people with blindness caused by damage to the visual cortex can recognize facial expressions of emotion even though they have no conscious awareness of looking at a person’s face. This is called ________________","concept":"Blindsight is the ability of people with damage to the primary visual cortex (V1, striate cortex) to respond accurately to stimuli in the blind part of their visual field while denying that they see anything. Ernst Poppel, Richard Held and Douglas Frost (1973) first showed residual eye movements to unseen targets, and Lawrence Weiskrantz and colleagues (1974) named the effect after testing patient DB, who could point to and discriminate stimuli he insisted he could not see. Affective blindsight extends this to emotion. Beatrice de Gelder, Weiskrantz and colleagues (1999) showed that patient GY, blind in one half-field after V1 damage, could guess the emotional expression of faces shown to his blind field well above chance. Imaging suggests a subcortical route that bypasses V1: retina to superior colliculus to pulvinar of the thalamus to the amygdala, which responds to fearful faces even without awareness. This fits the 'low road' for fear described by Joseph LeDoux (1996). Separate it from neighbouring disorders. Hemispatial neglect, usually after right parietal damage, is a failure of attention to the left side of space, not blindness; yet neglected items can still be processed implicitly, as in the 'burning house' study of John Marshall and Peter Halligan (1988). Prosopagnosia is a failure to recognise familiar faces after fusiform damage, sometimes with covert recognition shown by skin conductance. Anton syndrome is the reverse of blindsight: cortically blind patients who deny their blindness. Option a is right because the stem describes non-conscious recognition of facial emotion after visual cortex damage, which is exactly affective blindsight.","traps":{"b":"'Affect neglect' is not an established neuropsychological term. It borrows the word neglect from hemispatial neglect, a disorder of attention after right parietal damage in which patients ignore the left side of space even though their vision is intact. In the stem the problem is cortical blindness, not inattention, and the preserved ability is emotion recognition. It tempts because neglect patients also process unseen information implicitly.","c":"Emotional neglect is a form of child maltreatment: caregivers persistently fail to meet a child's needs for warmth, attention and comfort. It is measured, for example, by a subscale of the Childhood Trauma Questionnaire and is a risk factor for later depression and attachment problems. It has nothing to do with brain damage or vision. It tempts because it pairs 'emotional' with a clinical word, matching the emotion theme of the stem.","d":"'Motivational blindsight' is an invented label. Blindsight itself was named by Lawrence Weiskrantz and colleagues (1974), and the version for facial emotion is affective blindsight; nothing in the stem concerns motivation, drives or goals. It tempts because it keeps the correct key word 'blindsight', so a candidate who remembers only that part may pick it. The emotional content of the faces points to 'affective'."}},{"q":"The smallest bone in the body is","concept":"The middle ear holds the three smallest bones of the body, the auditory ossicles, linked in a chain from the eardrum to the inner ear. The malleus (hammer) is attached to the tympanic membrane; the incus (anvil) sits in the middle; and the stapes (stirrup) has a footplate that fits into the oval window of the cochlea. The stapes is the smallest bone in the human body, about 3 millimetres long. The ossicles solve an impedance problem: sound travels in air but the cochlea is filled with fluid, so most sound energy would otherwise be reflected. The chain concentrates force from the large eardrum onto the small oval window and adds a small lever effect, raising pressure roughly twenty times. Two tiny muscles damp the chain in the acoustic reflex: the stapedius, the smallest skeletal muscle, supplied by the facial nerve, and the tensor tympani, supplied by the trigeminal nerve. Clinical links: otosclerosis fixes the stapes in the oval window and causes conductive hearing loss, treated by stapedectomy. Conductive loss arises in the outer or middle ear; sensorineural loss in the cochlea or auditory nerve. In the inner ear, hair cells on the basilar membrane turn vibration into nerve impulses. Know the other extreme too: the femur is the longest and strongest bone. Spelling matters in the distractor: carpals are wrist bones, while a carpel is part of a flower. Option c is right because the stapes, the stirrup-shaped ossicle that presses on the oval window, is the smallest bone in the human body.","traps":{"a":"The malleus (hammer) is the first and largest of the three ossicles. Its handle is attached to the inner surface of the eardrum, so it picks up the drum's vibrations and passes them to the incus; the tensor tympani muscle pulls on it. It is small but clearly larger than the stapes. It tempts because candidates remember that an ear ossicle is the smallest bone but not which one.","b":"The incus (anvil) is the middle ossicle, linking the malleus to the stapes through tiny joints. It is larger than the stapes, though smaller than the malleus. It tempts because candidates who know the answer is an ossicle often guess the middle one, and because many remember the order hammer, anvil, stirrup without recalling that size falls from the first bone to the last.","d":"Carpal bones are the eight small bones of the wrist (scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform, trapezium, trapezoid, capitate and hamate); the pea-shaped pisiform is the smallest of them, still far larger than the stapes. 'Carpel', as spelled here, is actually the female part of a flower. It tempts because the carpals are famous as small bones, and the misspelling hides the error."}},{"q":"The _______ is rich in _________ and is responsible for colour and detailed vision","concept":"The retina has two kinds of photoreceptor, the basis of the duplex theory of vision. Rods, about 120 million in each eye, contain rhodopsin and are extremely sensitive to light, so they serve night (scotopic) vision. Many rods converge onto one ganglion cell, which pools weak signals but blurs detail, and rods do not signal colour. Cones, about 6 million, work in bright (photopic) light. There are three types, sensitive to short, medium and long wavelengths, and comparing their outputs gives colour vision. The fovea is a small pit at the centre of the macula, on the visual axis. Its centre is packed with cones and contains no rods, and the inner retinal layers are pushed aside so light reaches the cones directly. In the fovea each cone connects almost one-to-one with its own ganglion cell, so acuity is highest there; we move our eyes to put objects of interest on the fovea. Away from the fovea rods dominate, which is why a faint star is easier to see slightly off-centre. The blind spot (optic disc) is where ganglion cell axons leave the eye as the optic nerve; it has no rods or cones at all, and the brain fills in the gap. Exam variants include dark adaptation (cones adapt within minutes, rods take about 30 minutes), the shift of peak sensitivity towards blue at dusk described by Jan Evangelista Purkinje (1825), and macular degeneration, which destroys central detailed vision. Option c is right because the fovea is rich in cones, and cones provide colour and fine detailed vision.","traps":{"a":"The blind spot (optic disc) is where ganglion cell axons gather and leave the eye as the optic nerve, about 15 degrees to the nasal side of the fovea on the retina. It has no photoreceptors at all, neither cones nor rods, so it gives no vision, let alone colour or detail. The second half, cones, is correct. It tempts because candidates who know cones are right may grab the first option containing them.","b":"The fovea is the right place but the wrong cell. Rods, rich in rhodopsin, serve dim-light vision; they are colour-blind and, because many converge on one ganglion cell, give poor acuity. They are absent from the centre of the fovea and most dense about 20 degrees out in the periphery. It tempts because rods are the more numerous photoreceptor, so 'rich in rods' sounds plausible for any retinal area.","d":"Both halves are wrong. The blind spot contains no photoreceptors, so it cannot be rich in rods, and rods would not give colour or detail anyway, since they serve sensitive but low-acuity, colourless night vision. It tempts only by guesswork, because both words are familiar retina terms and a candidate unsure of the structure may pair them at random."}},{"q":"Which of the following follows the all or none law","concept":"The all-or-none law states that a response either occurs at full strength or does not occur at all; a stronger stimulus does not make it bigger. Henry Pickering Bowditch (1871) first described it for heart muscle, Keith Lucas (1909) showed it for skeletal muscle fibres, and Edgar Adrian (1914) showed it for nerve. The ionic basis was worked out by Alan Hodgkin and Andrew Huxley (1952) on the squid giant axon. A neuron at rest sits at about minus 70 millivolts, held by the sodium-potassium pump and potassium leak channels. Inputs at the dendrites produce graded potentials: excitatory postsynaptic potentials (EPSPs) depolarise, inhibitory postsynaptic potentials (IPSPs) hyperpolarise, and their size varies with input strength. They spread passively, fade with distance and summate over space and time. If the summed input depolarises the axon hillock to threshold, about minus 55 millivolts, voltage-gated sodium channels open in a positive feedback loop and an action potential fires, peaking near plus 30 to 40 millivolts; potassium outflow then repolarises the membrane, often with a brief hyperpolarisation. Each spike has the same size and travels along the axon without decrement. Stimulus intensity is coded instead by firing rate and by the number of neurons recruited. The absolute refractory period blocks a second spike, while during the relative refractory period only a stronger stimulus can trigger one. Myelin speeds conduction by saltatory jumps between nodes of Ranvier. Option a is right because action potentials fire at full, fixed amplitude once threshold is reached and not at all below it, which is the all-or-none law.","traps":{"b":"An excitatory postsynaptic potential is a small, local depolarisation produced when a transmitter such as glutamate opens cation channels on the postsynaptic membrane. It is graded: more transmitter gives a bigger EPSP, and EPSPs summate in space and time. Only if their sum reaches threshold at the axon hillock does an all-or-none spike follow. It tempts because it is excitatory and can trigger an action potential.","c":"An inhibitory postsynaptic potential is a small, local hyperpolarisation, usually caused by GABA or glycine opening chloride or potassium channels. It moves the membrane away from threshold, making firing less likely. Its size is graded by the amount of transmitter, and it summates with EPSPs, so it does not obey the all-or-none law. It tempts because it is a named potential in the same chapter as the action potential.","d":"The resting membrane potential is the steady voltage of about minus 70 millivolts across an inactive neuron's membrane, kept by the sodium-potassium pump, selective potassium leak and trapped negative proteins inside. It is a baseline state, not a signal, so it has no 'all' or 'none'. It tempts because it is a fixed, stable value, and a fixed size is easily mistaken for the all-or-none property of spikes."}},{"q":"Schwan cells are found in","concept":"Glial cells support neurons, and two kinds make myelin, the fatty insulating sheath that lets impulses jump between gaps called nodes of Ranvier (saltatory conduction). Which cell does it depends on location. In the peripheral nervous system (PNS), meaning the cranial and spinal nerves and ganglia outside the brain and spinal cord, myelin is made by Schwann cells, named after Theodor Schwann (1839), who described them in his work on cell theory. Each myelinating Schwann cell wraps one segment of a single axon, while non-myelinating Schwann cells enclose bundles of thin unmyelinated axons. In the central nervous system (CNS), meaning the brain and spinal cord, myelin is made by oligodendrocytes, and one oligodendrocyte can myelinate segments of many different axons. Other glia complete the picture: in the CNS, astrocytes (blood-brain barrier, ion and transmitter balance), microglia (immune defence) and ependymal cells (lining the ventricles); in the PNS, satellite cells around neuron bodies in ganglia. Schwann cells guide the regrowth of cut peripheral axons, which is why peripheral nerves can regenerate while CNS damage usually does not. Disease contrasts are favourite questions: multiple sclerosis attacks CNS myelin, while Guillain-Barre syndrome and Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease affect peripheral nerves; a vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma) is a benign tumour of Schwann cells on the eighth cranial nerve. One trap: the optic nerve is called a nerve but is CNS tissue, myelinated by oligodendrocytes. Option a is right because Schwann cells are found in the peripheral nerves outside the skull and spine, where they form the myelin sheath.","traps":{"b":"The brain is central nervous system tissue. Its axons are myelinated by oligodendrocytes, each of which sends processes to wrap segments of many axons, and its white matter owes its colour to that oligodendrocyte myelin. Schwann cells normally do not occur there. It tempts because myelin is discussed mainly in relation to brain white matter and diseases such as multiple sclerosis, so candidates link all myelin with the brain.","c":"The spinal cord is also part of the central nervous system, so its tracts are myelinated by oligodendrocytes, not Schwann cells. The boundary lies where the nerve roots leave the cord: beyond that transition zone, the roots and spinal nerves are peripheral and do contain Schwann cells. It tempts because spinal nerves and the spinal cord sound alike, and candidates forget that the cord itself is CNS.","d":"The basal ganglia (caudate nucleus, putamen and globus pallidus, functionally linked with the subthalamic nucleus and substantia nigra) are grey-matter nuclei deep in the brain, involved in movement and habit, and damaged in Parkinson's and Huntington's diseases. They lie inside the central nervous system, so any myelin there comes from oligodendrocytes. It tempts only as a specific-sounding brain structure for candidates unsure of the CNS and PNS split."}},{"q":"Group of cell bodies in the PNS is called_______","concept":"Neuroanatomy uses different words for the same kind of structure depending on whether it lies in the central nervous system (CNS: brain and spinal cord) or the peripheral nervous system (PNS: everything outside them, including cranial and spinal nerves and the autonomic system). Two kinds of structure matter. The first is a cluster of neuron cell bodies, which looks grey because cell bodies are unmyelinated. In the CNS such a cluster is a nucleus, for example the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus or the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus. In the PNS it is a ganglion (plural ganglia), for example the dorsal root ganglia that hold the cell bodies of sensory neurons, or the sympathetic chain ganglia beside the spinal cord. The second kind is a bundle of axons, which looks white because of myelin. In the CNS it is a tract (the corticospinal tract, the optic tract). In the PNS it is a nerve (the sciatic nerve, the vagus nerve). A useful grid: CNS has nuclei and tracts, PNS has ganglia and nerves. One exception catches students: the basal ganglia are clusters of cell bodies deep inside the cerebrum, so strictly they are CNS nuclei, and many anatomists prefer the term basal nuclei; the old name survives by tradition. Examiners build variants by asking for the CNS equivalent (nucleus), the PNS axon bundle (nerve) or the CNS axon bundle (tract). Option b is right because a group of neuronal cell bodies in the peripheral nervous system is called a ganglion, so the plural answer is ganglia.","traps":{"a":"Nuclei (singular nucleus) are indeed clusters of neuronal cell bodies, but the term is reserved for the central nervous system, as in the hypothalamic or thalamic nuclei. The stem specifies the PNS, where the same structure is called a ganglion. It tempts because it describes the right kind of structure in the wrong location, and the misnamed basal ganglia blur the rule further.","c":"Tracts are bundles of myelinated axons running inside the central nervous system, such as the corticospinal or optic tract, and they form white matter. They contain no cell bodies and they are not in the PNS, so they fail the stem twice. They tempt because tract, nerve, nucleus and ganglion are taught together as one confusing set of terms.","d":"A nerve is a bundle of axons in the peripheral nervous system, such as the sciatic or vagus nerve, wrapped in connective tissue. It is the right location but the wrong component: nerves carry fibres, while the cell bodies of those fibres sit in ganglia or in the CNS. It tempts because 'nerve' is the most familiar PNS word for students."}},{"q":"Corpus callosum is a part of","concept":"The brain develops from the neural tube. Early in embryonic life its front end swells into three primary vesicles: the prosencephalon (forebrain), the mesencephalon (midbrain) and the rhombencephalon (hindbrain). These then divide into five secondary vesicles, each with fixed adult derivatives that examiners love to test. The telencephalon becomes the two cerebral hemispheres: the cerebral cortex, the basal ganglia, the hippocampus and amygdala, the olfactory bulbs, the lateral ventricles and the white matter commissures that join the hemispheres, chiefly the corpus callosum and the anterior commissure. The diencephalon becomes the thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus (including the pineal gland) and subthalamus, around the third ventricle. The mesencephalon stays as the midbrain: tectum (superior and inferior colliculi), tegmentum, substantia nigra and cerebral aqueduct. The rhombencephalon splits into the metencephalon (pons and cerebellum) and the myelencephalon (medulla oblongata). The corpus callosum is the largest commissure in the brain, a broad band of roughly 200 million axons that lets the hemispheres share information. Its role became famous through the split-brain studies of Roger Sperry and Michael Gazzaniga in the 1960s, for which Sperry shared the 1981 Nobel Prize: when the callosum is cut to control epilepsy, information shown only to the right hemisphere cannot be named by the left-hemisphere language system. A quick rule: cerebrum means telencephalon, the 'thalamus' family means diencephalon. Option b is right because the corpus callosum connects the two cerebral hemispheres and develops with them from the telencephalon, the front part of the forebrain.","traps":{"a":"The diencephalon, the rear part of the forebrain, gives rise to the thalamus, hypothalamus, epithalamus with the pineal gland, and subthalamus, arranged around the third ventricle. The corpus callosum arches directly above the thalamus, so they look like neighbours on a midline section, but it belongs to the hemispheres, not the diencephalon. It tempts because both are forebrain divisions lying close together.","c":"The mesencephalon is the midbrain. It contains the tectum (superior colliculi for visual reflexes, inferior colliculi for auditory relay), the tegmentum, the substantia nigra whose dopamine neurons degenerate in Parkinson's disease, and the cerebral aqueduct. It has no commissure joining the cerebral hemispheres. It tempts only because its name sounds similar to the other 'encephalon' options.","d":"The metencephalon is the front part of the hindbrain and develops into the pons and the cerebellum. The pons does carry large crossing fibre bundles, but these link the cerebral cortex to the cerebellum, not one hemisphere to the other. It tempts because crossing white matter in the pons can be confused with the callosum's role as the main bridge between hemispheres."}},{"q":"Which of the following is responsible for coordinating movement","concept":"Movement is produced by a team of structures, and exams test who does what. The primary motor cortex, in the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe (Brodmann area 4), issues the command for voluntary movement; it is mapped as the motor homunculus described by Wilder Penfield and Theodore Rasmussen (1950), and each hemisphere controls the opposite side of the body. The basal ganglia help select and initiate movements and suppress unwanted ones; damage gives Parkinson's disease (too little movement) or Huntington's disease (too much). The cerebellum, at the back of the brain under the occipital lobes, is the coordinator. It receives a copy of the motor command and continuous feedback from muscles, joints and the vestibular system, compares intended with actual movement, and corrects errors in timing, force, direction and balance. It is also central to motor learning, such as learning to ride a bicycle. It controls the same side of the body, so a right cerebellar lesion affects the right limbs. Cerebellar damage does not paralyse; it produces ataxia: a wide-based staggering gait, intention tremor, dysmetria (overshooting a target in the finger-nose test), dysdiadochokinesia and nystagmus. Alcohol intoxication mimics this, which is why roadside sobriety tests use cerebellar tasks. Jeremy Schmahmann and Janet Sherman (1998) described the cerebellar cognitive affective syndrome, showing roles in planning, language and emotion too. Option a is right because the cerebellum is the structure that coordinates and smooths movement, integrating feedback to keep actions accurate and balanced.","traps":{"b":"The primary motor cortex (precentral gyrus, Brodmann area 4) sends the commands that start voluntary movement down the corticospinal tract to the opposite side of the body. Damage causes weakness or paralysis, not clumsiness. It initiates movement, while the cerebellum coordinates it. It tempts because the word 'motor' makes it sound like the answer to any movement question.","c":"The medulla oblongata is the lowest part of the brainstem and holds the cardiovascular and respiratory centres, plus reflexes such as coughing, sneezing, swallowing and vomiting. It is also where most motor fibres cross over (the pyramidal decussation). It does not coordinate skilled movement. It tempts because it is a hindbrain structure next to the cerebellum and appears in the same brainstem list.","d":"The pons, meaning bridge, links the cerebral cortex to the cerebellum through the pontine nuclei and middle cerebellar peduncles, and contributes to breathing and REM sleep. It passes motor information to the cerebellum but does not do the coordinating itself. It tempts because it develops from the same metencephalon as the cerebellum and is directly wired to it."}},{"q":"Which is called the master gland","concept":"The endocrine system is a set of glands that release hormones into the blood. The pituitary gland (hypophysis) is a pea-sized gland that hangs from the base of the brain by a stalk (the infundibulum) and sits in a bony pocket called the sella turcica. It is classically called the master gland because many of its hormones are tropic: they control other endocrine glands. The anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) secretes thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH, acting on the thyroid), adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH, acting on the adrenal cortex), follicle-stimulating and luteinizing hormones (FSH and LH, acting on the gonads), plus growth hormone and prolactin. The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) releases oxytocin and vasopressin. The subtlety examiners use is that the pituitary is itself controlled by the hypothalamus, which sends releasing hormones such as CRH and TRH through a portal blood system and sends nerve fibres directly into the posterior lobe. For that reason some books call the hypothalamus the 'master of the master gland'. Together they form axes such as the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, central to the stress response described by Hans Selye (1936). Keep the other options separate: the thalamus is a sensory relay, not a gland, and the pineal gland secretes melatonin for circadian rhythm. If a question asks which structure controls the pituitary, answer hypothalamus; if it asks for the master gland, answer pituitary. Option a is right because the pituitary is the conventional textbook master gland, since its tropic hormones regulate the thyroid, adrenals and gonads.","traps":{"b":"The hypothalamus is a small region of the diencephalon that regulates hunger, thirst, temperature and sexual behaviour, and controls the pituitary through releasing hormones and direct nerve fibres. That is why some texts call it the 'master of the master gland'. It is neural tissue and not the gland conventionally given the title. It tempts strongly because it truly sits above the pituitary in the chain of command.","c":"The thalamus is a paired relay station in the diencephalon that passes almost all sensory information except smell on to the cortex, for example vision via the lateral geniculate nucleus and hearing via the medial geniculate nucleus. It is not an endocrine gland and secretes no hormones. It tempts mainly because its name and location sound similar to the hypothalamus.","d":"The pineal gland, in the epithalamus, secretes melatonin in darkness and helps regulate the sleep-wake cycle and seasonal rhythms. It is a true endocrine gland but controls no other glands, so it cannot be the master gland. René Descartes (1649) famously called it the principal seat of the soul. It tempts because it is a well-known gland located inside the brain."}},{"q":"The posterior pituitary produces","concept":"The pituitary has two lobes with completely different origins. The anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) is true glandular tissue that manufactures its own hormones under hypothalamic control. The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) is really an extension of the brain: it contains the axon terminals of large neurosecretory cells whose cell bodies lie in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus. These neurons synthesise two small peptide hormones, transport them down their axons and store them in the posterior lobe, which releases them into the blood when the neurons fire. So, strictly, the posterior pituitary stores and releases rather than produces, although exam stems still say 'produces' or 'secretes'. The two hormones are oxytocin and vasopressin. Oxytocin causes uterine contractions during labour and milk ejection during breastfeeding, and in social neuroscience it is linked to bonding and trust. Vasopressin, also called antidiuretic hormone (ADH), makes the kidneys reabsorb water and, at high doses, constricts blood vessels; lack of it causes diabetes insipidus, and alcohol suppresses it, which is why drinking increases urination. Compare the anterior lobe's list (GH, prolactin, TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH) so that variants asking which hormone is NOT posterior are easy. Distractor chemicals include orexin (hypocretin), a lateral hypothalamic peptide for wakefulness and appetite, and norepinephrine, a catecholamine from the locus coeruleus, sympathetic nerves and adrenal medulla. Option a is right because oxytocin and vasopressin are the only two hormones released by the posterior pituitary, even though the hypothalamus actually synthesises them.","traps":{"b":"Oxytocin is correct, but orexin (also called hypocretin) is a neuropeptide made by neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. It promotes wakefulness and food seeking, and loss of orexin neurons causes narcolepsy with cataplexy. It acts within the brain and is not stored in or released by the posterior pituitary. It tempts because it pairs a right hormone with another hypothalamic peptide.","c":"Vasopressin is correct, but 'vasodilators' is not a hormone at all; it is a general label for any substance that widens blood vessels, such as nitric oxide. Vasopressin actually does the opposite, constricting vessels at high doses, which is where its name comes from. It tempts because the partner word sounds like vasopressin's family and half the pair is right.","d":"Vasopressin is correct, but norepinephrine is a catecholamine released by sympathetic nerve endings, by the adrenal medulla as a hormone, and by locus coeruleus neurons as a brain neurotransmitter. It has nothing to do with the posterior pituitary. It tempts because norepinephrine, like vasopressin, raises blood pressure, so the pair feels physiologically consistent."}},{"q":"Which of the following are directly involved in triggering eating","concept":"Eating is controlled by a balance between orexigenic signals, which start hunger and feeding, and anorexigenic signals, which produce satiety and stop it. Early research led to the dual-centre hypothesis of Eliot Stellar (1954): lesions of the lateral hypothalamus made rats stop eating (a hunger centre), while lesions of the ventromedial hypothalamus made them overeat and become obese (a satiety centre). The modern picture centres on the arcuate nucleus of the hypothalamus, which holds two opposing neuron groups. One group makes neuropeptide Y (NPY) and agouti-related peptide (AgRP); injecting NPY into the hypothalamus makes even well-fed animals eat heavily, so NPY is one of the most powerful feeding triggers known. The other group makes pro-opiomelanocortin (POMC) and cocaine- and amphetamine-regulated transcript (CART), which suppress feeding. Body signals tune these groups. Ghrelin from the empty stomach is the main peripheral hunger hormone and excites NPY neurons. Leptin, released by fat cells in proportion to fat stores and identified by Jeffrey Friedman's laboratory (1994), inhibits NPY and stimulates POMC and CART, signalling long-term satiety; mice lacking leptin are massively obese. Cholecystokinin (CCK), released by the duodenum when fat and protein arrive, signals short-term fullness through the vagus nerve. A tidy exam list: triggers are NPY, AgRP, ghrelin and orexin; stoppers are leptin, CCK, CART, POMC, peptide YY and insulin. Option d is right because neuropeptide Y is the only listed signal that directly stimulates feeding; the other three are satiety signals.","traps":{"a":"Leptin is a hormone secreted by adipose (fat) cells in proportion to fat stores. It acts on the arcuate nucleus to inhibit NPY neurons and activate POMC and CART neurons, so it reduces appetite and signals long-term energy sufficiency. It suppresses rather than triggers eating. It tempts because it is the most famous appetite hormone, and students mix it up with ghrelin, the hunger hormone.","b":"CART, cocaine- and amphetamine-regulated transcript, is a peptide made alongside POMC in arcuate neurons. It is anorexigenic: it reduces food intake, which fits the appetite loss produced by stimulant drugs. It is activated by leptin and opposes NPY. It tempts because its name is unfamiliar, so a candidate who knows only that it is a hypothalamic feeding peptide may guess it promotes eating.","c":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) is a gut hormone released from the small intestine when fats and proteins arrive after a meal. It slows stomach emptying, triggers bile and enzyme release, and signals fullness to the brainstem through the vagus nerve, ending the meal. It is a short-term satiety signal, so it stops eating. It tempts because it is directly involved in feeding control, just in the opposite direction."}},{"q":"What new method did Wechsler invent for obtaining the IQ score in the Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scales?","concept":"Intelligence scores have been expressed in two ways. The first was the ratio IQ. Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905) built the first practical intelligence test, and their 1908 revision introduced mental age: the age level whose typical items a child can pass. William Stern (1912) proposed dividing mental age by chronological age, and Lewis Terman (1916) multiplied by 100 in the Stanford-Binet, giving IQ = MA/CA x 100. A 10-year-old with a mental age of 12 scores 120. The ratio IQ breaks down for adults, because mental age stops rising in the mid-teens while chronological age keeps increasing, so an unchanged adult would appear to decline every year. Its spread also varied across ages, so the same IQ meant different things at different ages. David Wechsler solved this in the Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scale (1939) with the deviation IQ, which this item calls Standard IQ. A person's raw score is compared with the scores of people of the same age in the norm sample and converted to a standard score with a mean of 100 and standard deviation of 15. So 115 is one standard deviation above the mean (about the 84th percentile) and 130 is two (about the 98th). Wechsler also split the test into verbal and performance scales. The Stanford-Binet adopted the deviation IQ in 1960, and all modern scales such as the WAIS and WISC use it. Option a is right because Wechsler's innovation was the deviation, or standard score, IQ, which compares a person with same-age peers instead of using the mental age ratio.","traps":{"b":"An 'age-relative formula' IQ describes the older ratio approach, in which a score is computed by dividing mental age by chronological age. That formula was proposed by William Stern (1912) and used by Lewis Terman (1916) in the Stanford-Binet. Wechsler replaced it precisely because it fails for adults. It tempts because the deviation IQ is also age based, since it uses same-age norms.","c":"Mental age IQ is the ratio IQ: mental age, introduced in the 1908 Binet-Simon scale, divided by chronological age and multiplied by 100. It was the standard method before Wechsler and the very method he abandoned, because mental age plateaus in adolescence. It tempts because 'mental age' is the best-known historical term in intelligence testing and appears in every textbook account.","d":"'Physical age IQ' is not a recognised psychometric method. The nearest real term is chronological age, the denominator of the old ratio IQ, but nobody computes an IQ from physical age alone. It is a filler distractor. It tempts candidates who vaguely remember that some kind of age entered the old formula and cannot recall the exact term."}},{"q":"Which test can diagnose if an examinee consistently makes more errors on one side of the body than the other?","concept":"Neuropsychologists use lateralised testing to locate brain damage. The key principle is crossed control: touch, pain and position sense travel up pathways that cross the midline, so the left side of the body is represented in the right parietal lobe (the postcentral gyrus, primary somatosensory cortex) and vice versa. If a patient consistently makes more errors on one side of the body, the opposite hemisphere is suspected. The Finger Localization Test of Arthur Benton and colleagues (1983) applies this logic. The examiner touches one or two of the patient's fingers, with the hand first visible and then hidden, and the patient names or points to the fingers touched. Each hand is scored separately. Errors confined to one hand suggest a lesion in the opposite hemisphere, while bilateral finger agnosia is classically linked to dominant parietal damage and forms part of Gerstmann syndrome (finger agnosia, left-right confusion, agraphia and acalculia), described by Josef Gerstmann (1930). Similar side-by-side comparisons appear in the sensory-perceptual examination of the Halstead-Reitan Battery. Contrast this with attention tests, which measure a capacity rather than a side. The Continuous Performance Test of Haldor Rosvold and colleagues (1956) measures sustained attention and impulsivity, and the Test of Everyday Attention of Ian Robertson and colleagues (1994) measures selective, sustained and switching attention through everyday tasks. The attention disorder that is lateralised is unilateral neglect, assessed with cancellation and line bisection tasks. Option a is right because the Finger Localization Test scores each hand separately, so a consistent excess of errors on one side reveals lateralised sensory dysfunction.","traps":{"b":"The Continuous Performance Test, introduced by Haldor Rosvold and colleagues (1956), presents a long stream of stimuli and asks the person to respond only to a target. Omission errors index inattention and commission errors index impulsivity, which is why it is widely used in ADHD assessment. It measures vigilance over time, not differences between the two sides of the body. It tempts because it also counts errors.","c":"A sustained attention test measures the ability to keep focus on a dull task over a long period, as in the Mackworth Clock of Norman Mackworth (1948). Performance declines with time on task, called the vigilance decrement. Such tests give an overall attention score, not a left versus right comparison. It tempts because the stem's 'consistently makes more errors' sounds like a performance-over-time measure.","d":"The Test of Everyday Attention, by Ian Robertson and colleagues (1994), uses realistic tasks such as searching a map, searching a telephone directory and counting lift (elevator) tones to assess selective attention, sustained attention and attentional switching. It is ecologically valid but not designed to compare body sides. It tempts because it is a well-known named neuropsychological test and sounds comprehensive enough to detect anything."}},{"q":"What is NOT an example of executive function?","concept":"Executive functions are the higher-order control processes that manage other cognitive abilities when behaviour must be goal-directed, flexible and new rather than routine. They depend mainly on the prefrontal cortex, especially the dorsolateral region, and its loops with the basal ganglia and thalamus. Muriel Lezak (1982) grouped them into volition, planning, purposive action and effective performance, while Akira Miyake and colleagues (2000) identified three separable but related core functions: inhibition, shifting (cognitive flexibility) and updating of working memory. Higher abilities such as planning, abstract reasoning, concept formation and problem solving are built from these. Classic tests make the idea concrete: the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test of David Grant and Esta Berg (1948) for concept formation and set shifting, the Tower of London of Tim Shallice (1982) for planning, the Stroop test of J. Ridley Stroop (1935) for inhibition, Trail Making Part B for switching, and verbal fluency for strategic retrieval. Damage produces the dysexecutive syndrome, famously seen in Phineas Gage after his 1848 accident: impulsivity, poor planning, concrete thinking and perseveration, with basic perception and memory often intact. That last point is the key to this item. Basic sensory and perceptual processes, such as hearing and recognising sounds, are lower-level, modality-specific functions handled by posterior and temporal cortex, for example the primary auditory cortex in Heschl's gyrus. They supply the information that executive processes act on, but they are not executive themselves. Option d is right because auditory processing is a basic perceptual function of the temporal lobe, while conceptualization, planning and logical analysis are all executive functions.","traps":{"a":"Conceptualization, the ability to form abstract concepts and see the rule behind examples, is a core executive ability. It is measured by the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test, where the person must discover the sorting rule, and by similarities tasks. Frontal patients show concrete thinking here. It is not the exception the stem asks for. It tempts because it sounds like general thinking rather than control.","b":"Planning, which means formulating a sequence of steps toward a goal and holding it in mind, is the textbook example of an executive function. The Tower of London task of Tim Shallice (1982) was designed to measure it, and frontal lobe damage impairs it. It belongs inside the category, not outside. It tempts only through careless reading of the word NOT in the stem.","c":"Logical analysis, meaning reasoning, evaluating information and drawing valid inferences, is a higher-order process that relies on executive control and working memory in the prefrontal cortex. Neuropsychologists list it with abstraction and judgement among executive abilities. So it is an example, not the exception. It tempts because some students think of logic as general intelligence rather than an executive function."}},{"q":"What is the purpose of the STAI?","concept":"Anxiety can be understood in two senses. State anxiety is a temporary emotional condition: tension, apprehension and worry with autonomic arousal, felt right now and changing with the situation, such as just before an exam. Trait anxiety is a relatively stable personality disposition: a general tendency to perceive situations as threatening and to respond with state anxiety more often and more strongly. Charles Spielberger built his state-trait theory of anxiety on this distinction and, with Richard Gorsuch and Robert Lushene (1970), published the State-Trait Anxiety Inventory (STAI); the revised Form Y appeared in 1983. It has 40 self-report items in two 20-item scales. The state scale asks how the person feels right now, at this moment, and the trait scale asks how they generally feel. Each item is rated on a four-point scale, so each scale ranges from 20 to 80. Because the state scale is sensitive to change, it is used to measure the effect of stress, therapy or relaxation, while the trait scale identifies anxiety-prone people. Examiners set the STAI against other inventories by purpose. The Eysenck Personality Questionnaire of Hans Eysenck and Sybil Eysenck (1975) measures psychoticism, extraversion and neuroticism. Validity scales, such as the L, F and K scales of the MMPI of Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943), check how honestly someone answered. Broad inventories measure normal or abnormal personality as a whole. Option c is right because the STAI was designed specifically to measure and separate state anxiety from trait anxiety.","traps":{"a":"Measuring both normal and abnormal personality describes broad multiscale inventories, for example combining a normal-range test such as the Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire of Raymond Cattell (1949) with a clinical test such as the MMPI. The STAI measures one construct, anxiety, in two forms. It tempts because trait anxiety is a personality variable, so the STAI can look like a personality test.","b":"Assessing the validity of responses is the job of validity scales, which detect lying, random answering, faking bad or defensiveness. The classic examples are the L (lie), F (infrequency) and K (correction) scales of the MMPI of Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943). The STAI has no such purpose. It tempts because many self-report inventories include some check on response validity.","d":"Psychoticism, extraversion and neuroticism are the three super-factors of the PEN model, measured by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire of Hans Eysenck and Sybil Eysenck (1975), which also has a lie scale. Neuroticism overlaps with trait anxiety, but the EPQ covers broad personality, not the state versus trait distinction. It tempts because neuroticism and trait anxiety correlate strongly."}},{"q":"Which strategy did Comrey pursue in developing his 180-item test?","concept":"Personality inventories are built by a few classic strategies, and exams ask you to match a test to its strategy. In the logical-content or rational strategy the author writes items that obviously reflect the trait, as in the Personal Data Sheet that Robert Woodworth (1917) built to screen soldiers. In the theoretical strategy items come from a theory, as in the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule of Allen Edwards (1954), based on the need theory of Henry Murray (1938). In the criterion-group or empirical keying strategy, items are kept only if they separate a criterion group from controls, whatever their content, as in the MMPI of Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943). In the factor-analytic strategy, factor analysis (introduced by Charles Spearman, 1904) is used to find clusters of items that correlate highly with each other and weakly with others; each cluster defines a factor, and scales are built around the factors. The Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire of Raymond Cattell (1949) is the classic example. Andrew Comrey's Comrey Personality Scales (1970) follow the same path. Comrey first grouped items into small factored homogeneous item dimensions (four items each, two positively and two negatively worded) and then factor analysed these dimensions. The result is a 180-item test answered on seven-point scales, with eight main personality scales, such as Trust versus Defensiveness and Extraversion versus Introversion, plus two validity scales. Option c is right because Comrey derived his 180-item scales by factor analysis of item and item-cluster intercorrelations, the defining feature of the factor-analytic strategy.","traps":{"a":"'Correlational' describes a research design that measures how variables co-vary without manipulating them, not a recognised test-construction strategy. Factor analysis does start from a matrix of correlations, which is why this tempts, but the named strategy is factor-analytic because it goes further and extracts latent factors that define the scales. Choosing 'correlational' confuses the raw input with the method used.","b":"Criterion-referencing means interpreting a score against a fixed standard of mastery (such as 80 percent correct on a driving test) rather than against other people, so it belongs to achievement testing. The personality strategy it resembles is criterion keying (empirical keying), used in the MMPI of Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943). It tempts because the two 'criterion' terms sound alike.","d":"An experimental strategy means manipulating an independent variable and observing its effect on a dependent variable under controlled conditions. Personality inventories are not built that way; their items are selected by logic, theory, criterion groups or factor analysis. Experiments may later help validate a test, but they do not construct it. It tempts as a familiar research word for unsure candidates."}},{"q":"The tendency to explain others’ actions as stemming from dispositions even in the presence of situational causes is","concept":"Attribution theory, founded by Fritz Heider (1958), asks how people explain behaviour: by internal or dispositional causes (personality, attitudes, ability) or by external or situational causes (pressure, roles, luck). Edward Jones and Keith Davis (1965) proposed correspondent inference theory, describing when we infer that an act corresponds to an underlying disposition, for example when it is freely chosen and unexpected. In practice people over-infer. In the classic study of Edward Jones and Victor Harris (1967), students read essays praising or criticising Fidel Castro. Even when told the writer had been assigned the position, readers still judged that the writer held that attitude. Lee Ross (1977) named this the fundamental attribution error, and Daniel Gilbert and Patrick Malone (1995) used the label correspondence bias, explaining it by a two-step process: we automatically make a dispositional inference and correct for the situation only if we have time and mental resources. Most textbooks treat the two names as synonyms. The bias is weaker in collectivist cultures, where people attend more to context. Know the neighbours. The actor-observer bias of Edward Jones and Richard Nisbett (1971) says we blame situations for our own behaviour but dispositions for others'. The self-serving bias credits our successes to ourselves and blames our failures on circumstances. The ultimate attribution error of Thomas Pettigrew (1979) extends the error to whole groups. Option c is right because correspondence bias is exactly the tendency to explain others' actions by their dispositions even when clear situational causes are present.","traps":{"a":"Belief bias is a reasoning error in which people judge the logical validity of an argument by whether its conclusion is believable. For example, a valid syllogism with an unbelievable conclusion is often rejected, as shown by Jonathan Evans, Julie Barston and Paul Pollard (1983). It concerns deductive reasoning, not explaining other people's behaviour. It tempts because it is another named bias.","b":"The self-serving bias is the tendency to attribute our own successes to internal causes such as ability and effort and our own failures to external causes such as bad luck or an unfair test. It protects self-esteem and concerns explanations of our own outcomes, while the stem is about others' actions. It tempts because it also involves choosing between dispositional and situational causes.","d":"Confirmation bias is the tendency to seek, notice and remember information that supports what we already believe and to ignore disconfirming evidence. Peter Wason (1960) demonstrated it with the 2-4-6 rule discovery task, where people tested only examples that fitted their hypothesis. It is about testing beliefs, not attributing behaviour. It tempts because it is the most famous cognitive bias."}},{"q":"Which of the following is false about social loafing","concept":"Social loafing is the tendency for people to exert less effort on a collective task, where individual contributions are pooled and cannot be identified, than when working alone. The first evidence came from Max Ringelmann (1913), who found that people pulling a rope in groups pulled less hard per person as group size grew. Part of this was coordination loss, but Bibb Latané, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) showed real motivation loss: blindfolded participants who merely believed others were shouting with them shouted less loudly. Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993) reviewed many studies and proposed the collective effort model: people work hard only when they expect their effort to matter and to lead to outcomes they value. This predicts the conditions that reduce loafing, which are exactly what this question lists. Loafing drops when individual output is identifiable and evaluated, when the task is challenging, appealing or involving, when members believe high effort will be rewarded, when the group is cohesive or made of friends, and in collectivist cultures. The key link is evaluation apprehension, the concern about being judged, described by Nickolas Cottrell (1968). Loafing happens precisely because pooling removes evaluation apprehension; restoring it by making contributions identifiable cuts loafing. Contrast social facilitation, from Norman Triplett (1898) and Robert Zajonc (1965): when individual performance is observed, arousal rises and simple tasks improve. Related effects include the free-rider and sucker effects. Option c is right because it is the false statement: groups loaf less when evaluation apprehension is present, not absent.","traps":{"a":"This statement is true. When a task is challenging, appealing or personally involving, people care about the outcome and keep working hard even in a group, a finding consistent with the collective effort model of Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993). Since the stem asks for the false statement, this cannot be the answer. It tempts only candidates who miss the word 'false'.","b":"This statement is true. According to the collective effort model of Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993), effort depends on expecting that one's effort matters and leads to valued rewards. When members are convinced high effort will pay off for them or the group, loafing falls. It tempts because 'rewards' sounds like an extrinsic shortcut that might not apply to group work.","d":"This statement is true. Loafing is lower in cohesive groups, such as groups of friends, because members value the group and do not want to let each other down. Cross-cultural research also shows less loafing in collectivist cultures, where group identity is strong. It tempts because some students expect friends to relax together, but cohesion actually increases effort."}},{"q":"Persuasion though systematic processing is the __________________ and  involves _________________","concept":"Two dual-process models explain how persuasive messages change attitudes. The Elaboration Likelihood Model of Richard Petty and John Cacioppo (1986) proposes two routes. On the central route, people think carefully about the message, scrutinise the strength and logic of the arguments and generate their own favourable or unfavourable thoughts; strong arguments persuade and weak ones can backfire. On the peripheral route, people do little thinking and are swayed by surface cues such as the attractiveness or fame of the source, the number of arguments, or a catchy slogan. Which route is taken depends on motivation (personal relevance, need for cognition) and ability (knowledge, time, freedom from distraction). Central-route change is more lasting, more resistant to counter-persuasion and more predictive of behaviour. The Heuristic-Systematic Model of Shelly Chaiken (1980) makes the same distinction in different words. Systematic processing is careful, effortful evaluation of message content and corresponds to the central route. Heuristic processing relies on simple decision rules or mental shortcuts, such as 'experts are usually right' or 'more arguments means a better case', and corresponds to the peripheral route. Both models build on the Yale communication research of Carl Hovland and colleagues (1953), which studied who says what to whom. A typical variant swaps the terms: if a stem mentions mental shortcuts or celebrity endorsement, the answer is the peripheral route or heuristic processing. Option a is right because systematic processing is the central route to persuasion, and it involves careful attention to the content and quality of the arguments.","traps":{"b":"Peripheral route with mental shortcuts is a correct pairing, but it describes heuristic processing, not systematic processing. On the peripheral route of the Elaboration Likelihood Model (Richard Petty and John Cacioppo, 1986), people rely on cues such as source attractiveness or the sheer number of arguments. It tempts because the pair is internally consistent, so a candidate who misreads 'systematic' picks it.","c":"This mixes the two routes. The central route is right for systematic processing, but it does not involve mental shortcuts; shortcuts, or heuristics, define the peripheral route and the heuristic mode of Shelly Chaiken (1980). On the central route people elaborate on argument quality. It tempts because one half is correct, so a candidate who checks only the first blank picks it.","d":"This also mixes the routes. Careful attention to arguments is the hallmark of the central route, while the peripheral route is defined by low elaboration and reliance on superficial cues. A peripheral route with careful attention is a contradiction in terms. It tempts because the second half is correct, so a candidate who checks only the second blank falls for it."}},{"q":"Trivialization and self-affirmation can reduce","concept":"Cognitive dissonance theory was proposed by Leon Festinger (1957). Dissonance is the unpleasant tension felt when two cognitions (beliefs, attitudes or knowledge of one's own behaviour) are inconsistent, for example 'I smoke' and 'smoking kills'. Because the tension is aversive, people are motivated to reduce it. The classic demonstration is by Leon Festinger and James Carlsmith (1959): students paid only 1 dollar to tell the next participant that a boring task was interesting later rated the task as more enjoyable than those paid 20 dollars, because the small payment gave insufficient justification for the lie. Festinger listed direct routes: change the behaviour, change one of the cognitions, or add new consonant cognitions that justify the act. Later research added indirect routes that reduce the discomfort without resolving the inconsistency. Trivialization, studied by Linda Simon, Jeff Greenberg and Jack Brehm (1995), means deciding that the inconsistent attitudes or behaviours are unimportant, so the inconsistency does not matter. Self-affirmation, from the self-affirmation theory of Claude Steele (1988), means affirming some other valued part of the self (for example 'I am a caring parent'), so overall self-integrity is restored and the specific inconsistency stops hurting. Exam variants use neighbours: effort justification (Elliot Aronson and Judson Mills, 1959), post-decision dissonance, the induced compliance paradigm, and the rival self-perception theory of Daryl Bem (1967), which explains the same findings without any tension. Option a is right because trivialization and self-affirmation are recognised indirect strategies people use to reduce cognitive dissonance.","traps":{"b":"Compliance is changing behaviour in response to a direct request, often won through techniques such as the foot-in-the-door of Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser (1966). It is an outcome of social influence, not an aversive state that people try to reduce. Induced compliance can create dissonance, which then needs reducing. It tempts because compliance appears in classic dissonance experiments.","c":"Reactance, from Jack Brehm (1966), is the motivational state aroused when a person feels their freedom of choice is threatened, leading them to resist or do the opposite, as when a ban makes something more attractive. It is reduced by restoring freedom, not by trivialization or self-affirmation. It tempts because Jack Brehm also co-authored the trivialization research on dissonance.","d":"Self-regulation is the process of controlling one's own thoughts, emotions and behaviour in pursuit of goals, studied for example in the ego depletion work of Roy Baumeister and colleagues (1998). It is a capacity people exercise, not a tension they reduce. It tempts because self-affirmation contains 'self' and is sometimes discussed in self-regulation research on coping with threats."}},{"q":"What are the three components of empathy?","concept":"Empathy is the capacity to understand and share another person's emotional state, and most modern accounts treat it as having several parts rather than being one ability. The social psychology textbook of Robert A. Baron and Nyla R. Branscombe, widely used for Indian entrance exams, describes three components. The emotional aspect is feeling what the other person feels, a partly automatic sharing of their emotion. The cognitive component is understanding what the other person thinks and feels, also called perspective taking or empathic accuracy, close to the theory of mind idea of David Premack and Guy Woodruff (1978). Empathic concern is the third part: other-oriented warmth and compassion for the person's welfare. The empathy-altruism hypothesis of C. Daniel Batson (1981) holds that empathic concern is what produces truly selfless helping. The Interpersonal Reactivity Index of Mark Davis (1980) measures four related facets: perspective taking, fantasy, empathic concern and personal distress. Counselling texts sometimes describe a different trio with a behavioural or communicative part, meaning conveying understanding back to the client, as in the accurate empathic understanding that Carl Rogers (1957) listed among the core conditions of therapy; that describes empathic skill in therapy, not the component model of empathy itself. Keep empathy separate from sympathy (feeling sorry for someone without sharing the feeling) and from personal distress (self-focused discomfort that makes people escape rather than help). Option c is right because the three components of empathy are the emotional aspect, the cognitive component and empathic concern.","traps":{"a":"This option includes 'positivity', which is not a component of empathy in any standard model. Positivity is a general tendency to evaluate things favourably, and cheerful positivity can even block attention to another's distress. Empathic concern and the emotional aspect are genuine elements, which makes the list look plausible. It tempts because two of its three terms are real empathy vocabulary.","d":"This list drops the emotional aspect, which every model treats as the core of empathy: sharing the other person's feeling. Without it, cognitive understanding plus concern plus behaviour could describe a skilled but emotionally detached helper. It tempts because each term on its own is real empathy vocabulary, and empathic concern is sometimes loosely treated as the emotional part.","b":"This trio (emotional, cognitive, behavioural) is the general affect, cognition and behaviour template applied to empathy, as in counselling texts where empathy has to be communicated back to the client, following Carl Rogers (1957). It is not the three-component model of empathy, where the third part is empathic concern, the compassion that C. Daniel Batson (1981) linked to altruistic helping. It tempts because the ABC triad fits almost any attitude question."}},{"q":"__________ is the tendency for an individual surrounded by a group of strangers to hesitate and not help because none of the bystanders knows for sure what is happening","concept":"Bystander intervention research began after the 1964 murder of Kitty Genovese in New York, reported (with exaggeration) as witnessed by 38 neighbours who did nothing. John Darley and Bibb Latané (1968) showed experimentally that the presence of others reduces helping, the bystander effect. Bibb Latané and John Darley (1970) then proposed a five-step decision model: notice the event, interpret it as an emergency, assume personal responsibility, know how to help, and decide to help. Different mechanisms block different steps. Pluralistic ignorance blocks step two, interpretation. Emergencies are ambiguous, so each bystander looks at the others for clues; everyone tries to look calm, so each concludes nobody else is worried and that nothing serious is happening. In the smoke-filled room study of Latané and Darley (1968), 75 percent of participants who were alone reported the smoke, but only 10 percent did when sitting with two passive confederates. Diffusion of responsibility blocks step three: the more bystanders, the less personal responsibility each feels. In the seizure study, about 85 percent of participants who believed they were the only listener helped, against about 31 percent who believed four others could hear. A third mechanism is audience inhibition (evaluation apprehension), the fear of looking foolish by overreacting. Pluralistic ignorance also describes students who stay silent in a confusing lecture, each assuming the others understand. Option b is right because the stem describes bystanders hesitating because none of them knows for sure what is happening, which is pluralistic ignorance, a failure to interpret the situation as an emergency.","traps":{"a":"The bystander effect is the overall finding, from John Darley and Bibb Latané (1968), that a person is less likely to help in an emergency when other people are present than when alone. It is the outcome, not the mechanism. The stem specifies a particular cause, uncertainty about what is happening, which names a specific explanation. It tempts because it is the umbrella term.","c":"Diffusion of responsibility is the mechanism in which each bystander feels less personally obliged to act because responsibility is shared among everyone present, so each assumes someone else will help. It blocks the step of taking responsibility, after the event is already seen as an emergency. In the stem the problem is uncertainty about what is happening. It tempts as the best-known bystander mechanism.","d":"'Amotivation to help' is not a recognised concept in bystander research. Amotivation is a term from the self-determination theory of Edward Deci and Richard Ryan (1985), meaning a lack of any intention to act, often because the activity seems pointless. Bystanders in emergencies are not unmotivated in this sense; they are uncertain or inhibited. It tempts as plausible-sounding filler."}},{"q":"choose the correct arrangement of events in group formation","concept":"Bruce Tuckman (1965) reviewed studies of small groups, including therapy and training groups, and proposed that groups develop through a predictable sequence. Forming is the orientation stage: members are polite, uncertain and dependent on the leader, testing what behaviour is acceptable. Storming is the conflict stage: members resist the task demands and each other, compete for roles and influence, and express disagreement. Norming follows when conflict is resolved: cohesion grows, roles and rules are accepted, and members share opinions openly. Performing is the stage of mature functioning: the structure now supports the task, energy goes into the work and problems are solved together. Bruce Tuckman and Mary Ann Jensen (1977) added a fifth stage, adjourning, when the task is complete and the group disbands; some writers call it mourning because members may feel loss. The order is easy to recall because of the rhyme: forming, storming, norming, performing, adjourning. The logic is also intuitive: conflict must be worked through before norms settle, norms must exist before effective performance, and ending can only come last. Not every group passes neatly through every stage, and some cycle back to storming. An alternative is the punctuated equilibrium model of Connie Gersick (1988), in which project groups show long periods of inertia broken by a burst of change around the midpoint of their deadline. Variant questions ask which stage involves conflict (storming), cohesion and role acceptance (norming) or disbanding (adjourning). Option b is right because it is the only sequence that follows Tuckman's order of forming, storming, norming, performing and adjourning.","traps":{"a":"This sequence places adjourning third, before norming and performing. Adjourning, added by Bruce Tuckman and Mary Ann Jensen (1977), is the termination stage when the group disbands after finishing its task, so it must come last. A group cannot end and then develop norms and perform. It tempts because the first two stages are correct, so a quick scan looks right.","c":"This sequence puts performing second and storming fourth, which reverses Tuckman's logic. Effective performance depends on settled norms, and norms emerge only after the conflict of storming has been resolved. A group cannot perform well before working through its disagreements. It tempts because it starts correctly with forming and ends correctly with adjourning, so only the middle looks scrambled.","d":"This sequence puts norming before storming and adjourning before performing. In the model of Bruce Tuckman (1965), conflict comes first and norms are agreed only after it is worked through, and adjourning is always the final stage. It also contains a spelling error ('stroming'), a hint it was carelessly built. It tempts because some real groups do agree on rules early."}},{"q":"The phenomenon in which groups make more extreme decisions than as individuals is called","concept":"Group polarization is the tendency for group discussion to strengthen the average initial leaning of members, so the group's decision is more extreme, in the same direction, than the average of their individual views. Its history begins with James Stoner (1961), whose master's thesis found that groups advised riskier courses of action on choice dilemmas than individuals did: the risky shift. Later studies found that groups whose members started out cautious became more cautious, so the shift was not always toward risk. Serge Moscovici and Marisa Zavalloni (1969) showed that French students mildly positive about their president and mildly negative about Americans became more extreme on both after discussion, and group polarization replaced risky shift as the general term. David Myers and George Bishop (1970) showed that discussion among like-minded students widened the gap in racial attitudes. Two explanations are standard. Informational influence (persuasive arguments): discussion exposes members to new arguments mostly supporting the shared view. Normative influence (social comparison): members want to be seen as good examples of the group's valued position, so they shift further toward it. Distinguish groupthink, described by Irving Janis (1972) from the Bay of Pigs fiasco: a cohesive group seeking agreement suppresses dissent and decides badly, with symptoms such as an illusion of invulnerability and self-censorship. Groupthink is about poor decision quality; polarization is about extremity. Option a is right because group polarization is the established term for groups reaching more extreme decisions than their members would as individuals.","traps":{"b":"'Extremity' is an ordinary word describing how far an attitude is from neutral, and attitude extremity is a studied property of attitudes, but it is not the name of a group phenomenon. The process by which discussion makes group decisions more extreme is called group polarization. It tempts because the stem says 'more extreme', so the matching word feels like a direct echo.","c":"Groupthink, described by Irving Janis (1972), is a mode of thinking in highly cohesive, insulated groups with directive leaders, where the desire for consensus overrides realistic appraisal of alternatives, as in the Bay of Pigs invasion. It concerns poor decision quality and suppressed dissent, not extremity as such. It tempts because both are group decision biases and groupthink can produce extreme decisions.","d":"Conformity is changing one's behaviour or beliefs to match a group, through normative or informational influence, as in the line-judgement studies of Solomon Asch (1951) and the autokinetic studies of Muzafer Sherif (1935). Conformity pulls people toward the group norm, which usually makes judgements converge rather than become extreme. It tempts because normative influence is also one explanation of polarization."}},{"q":"The research method that writes about people and cultures is called","concept":"Qualitative research has several traditions, each with its own focus. Ethnography, from the Greek for 'writing about people', is the study and written description of a culture or social group from the inside. It grew out of anthropology, especially the long-term fieldwork of Bronislaw Malinowski (1922) among the Trobriand Islanders. The ethnographer immerses in the setting for months or years, uses participant observation, informal interviews and field notes, and aims to capture the shared beliefs, rituals, language and everyday practices of the group. Clifford Geertz (1973) called the goal thick description: explaining not just what people do but what it means to them. Ethnographers distinguish the emic (insider) from the etic (outsider) view, terms coined by Kenneth Pike (1954). Psychology uses ethnography in cultural and community research, for example studying healing practices at a dargah or daily life on a psychiatric ward. Know the neighbours. Phenomenology, from the philosophy of Edmund Husserl (1913), describes the essence of individuals' lived experience of a phenomenon; interpretative phenomenological analysis was developed by Jonathan Smith (1996). Grounded theory, from Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss (1967), builds a theory inductively from data through constant comparison and theoretical sampling. Narrative analysis studies the stories people tell about their lives, focusing on structure and meaning. A case study examines one person or unit in depth. A quick rule: culture means ethnography, experience means phenomenology, theory means grounded theory, story means narrative. Option b is right because ethnography is the method that literally writes about people and their culture, describing a group's shared way of life.","traps":{"a":"Narrative analysis studies the stories individuals tell about their experiences, examining plot, sequence and how people construct identity and meaning through storytelling. Its unit is the individual's story, often a life history or illness narrative, not a culture. It tempts because ethnographers also collect stories, and 'writes about people' sounds like storytelling. The defining feature here, writing about cultures, belongs to ethnography.","c":"Phenomenology, rooted in the philosophy of Edmund Husserl (1913), aims to describe the essence of how individuals experience a particular phenomenon, such as grief or chronic pain, by bracketing the researcher's assumptions. Its focus is lived subjective experience, not a culture or a group's shared practices. It tempts because both are qualitative, interview-based approaches that try to capture an insider's view.","d":"Grounded theory, developed by Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss (1967), builds a new theory inductively from data, using coding, constant comparison and theoretical sampling until saturation. Its product is an explanatory theory, not a cultural description. It tempts because grounded theory studies often involve fieldwork and observation in social settings, much as ethnography does, and both are classic qualitative traditions."}},{"q":"Which of the sampling methods involves a two stage method","concept":"Sampling methods split into probability methods, where every unit has a known, non-zero chance of selection, and non-probability methods, where it does not. In simple random sampling each unit is drawn directly from a complete list. Stratified random sampling first divides the population into internally similar subgroups (strata) such as gender or class, then draws a random sample from every stratum. If each stratum's share of the sample matches its share of the population, it is proportionate; if small strata are oversampled so they can be analysed separately, it is disproportionate, and results are weighted later. Either way every stratum is sampled and individuals are picked in one round of selection. Cluster sampling works the other way round. The population is divided into naturally occurring groups (clusters) such as schools, villages or hospital wards, each ideally a mixed, miniature version of the population. Clusters, not individuals, are drawn at random. In one-stage cluster sampling everyone in the chosen clusters is studied; in two-stage cluster sampling a random sample of individuals is then drawn inside each chosen cluster. Adding more levels (districts, then villages, then households) gives multistage sampling, used in large Indian surveys such as the National Family Health Survey. Cluster sampling is cheap when no list of individuals exists, but less precise than a simple random sample of the same size. Memory aid: strata are all sampled from; clusters are themselves sampled. Option c is right because cluster sampling first selects groups and then, in its standard two-stage form, samples units within those chosen groups.","traps":{"a":"Proportionate stratified random sampling divides the population into internally similar strata (for example urban and rural) and draws a random sample from each stratum in proportion to its population share. Every stratum is represented, and individuals are chosen directly within strata in one round of random selection. It tempts because stratifying first and then sampling feels like two steps, but the strata themselves are never sampled.","b":"Disproportionate stratified random sampling also samples from every stratum, but deliberately takes a larger fraction from small strata (for example a minority group) so each can be analysed on its own; results are then weighted back to population proportions. Like the proportionate version, it selects individuals directly within all strata rather than sampling groups first. It tempts because examiners pair it with option a to offer two plausible stratified choices.","d":"Quota sampling is a non-probability method: the researcher fixes quotas for categories (say 50 men and 50 women) and interviewers fill them with whoever is convenient. It imitates stratified sampling without random selection, so sampling error cannot be estimated. It tempts because some marketing research texts (Naresh Malhotra) call it 'two-stage restricted judgmental sampling', but psychology exams key cluster sampling as the two-stage method."}},{"q":"A t-distribution is a similar to a normal distribution in the way, where ______________ .","concept":"The t-distribution was published in 1908 by William Sealy Gosset, a chemist at the Guinness brewery in Dublin, under the pen name 'Student', which is why it is called Student's t. He needed to test means from very small samples, where the population standard deviation is unknown and has to be estimated from the sample. That estimate adds extra uncertainty, and the t-distribution builds it in. Like the standard normal (z) distribution, t is continuous, bell-shaped, unimodal and perfectly symmetrical around a mean of zero, so mean, median and mode coincide and there is no skew. The differences lie in spread and tails. There is a family of t curves, one for each value of degrees of freedom (df, n minus 1 for a single sample). With few df the curve is flatter in the middle with heavier tails, so it is leptokurtic, and its variance, df divided by (df minus 2), is greater than 1, the variance of z. As df grows the curve tightens toward the normal; by about 30 df the two are close, and at infinite df they are identical. The practical effect is larger critical values: about 2.26 at 9 df (two-tailed, .05) against 1.96 for z, so small samples need bigger differences to reach significance. Contrast the chi-square and F distributions, which are positively skewed and never negative. Option b is right because, of the properties offered, symmetry about the mean is the one the t-distribution shares with the normal distribution.","traps":{"a":"'None of the above' would be right only if the t and normal distributions shared none of the listed properties. They do share one: both are perfectly symmetrical, bell-shaped curves centred on zero. This option tempts candidates who remember that t differs from the normal in tails and spread, and then overgeneralise to 'they share nothing'. Always test each listed property before choosing 'none of the above'.","c":"Kurtosis describes how heavy the tails and how sharp the peak of a distribution are; the normal is mesokurtic, with excess kurtosis of zero. The t-distribution is leptokurtic, with heavier tails and an excess kurtosis of 6 divided by (df minus 4) when df exceeds 4, so kurtosis is not the same. It tempts because both curves look like bells at a glance, and the gap shrinks as df rises.","d":"Variability means spread, measured by variance or standard deviation. The standard normal has a variance of 1, while the t-distribution has a variance of df divided by (df minus 2), which is always larger than 1 when df exceeds 2. The extra spread reflects the added uncertainty of estimating the population standard deviation from a sample. It tempts because the difference becomes tiny once df is large."}},{"q":"the df for 'within groups' in one way ANOVA with three levels on intelligence and 10 samples per level is","concept":"Analysis of variance (ANOVA), set out by Ronald A. Fisher (1925), tests whether three or more group means differ by splitting total variability into two parts. Between-groups variability reflects differences among the group means (any treatment effect plus error). Within-groups variability reflects how scores spread around their own group mean (error only). Each part has its own degrees of freedom (df), the number of values free to vary once a statistic is fixed. With k groups and N total scores the formulas are: df between = k minus 1; df within (also called df error or residual) = N minus k, because one df is lost for each group mean estimated; df total = N minus 1. The parts always add up: (k minus 1) plus (N minus k) equals N minus 1. In this stem the single independent variable has three levels, so k = 3, and 10 participants per level give N = 30. So df between = 2, df within = 30 minus 3 = 27, and df total = 29 (check: 2 + 27 = 29). The result is reported as F(2, 27), and the critical F at the .05 level is about 3.35. Examiners vary this item by asking for df between, df total, or the error df in a repeated-measures design, where the same people serve in every condition and error df becomes (k minus 1)(n minus 1). Option b is right because within-groups df equals N minus k, which here is 30 minus 3 = 27.","traps":{"a":"29 is the total degrees of freedom, N minus 1 = 30 minus 1. Total df treats all scores as one sample and equals the sum of between (2) and within (27) df. It tempts because N minus 1 is the most familiar df formula from one-sample t-tests, so candidates subtract one by habit instead of subtracting one for each group mean estimated.","c":"30 is simply N, the total number of observations (3 groups times 10). A sample size is never itself a df value in ANOVA, because at least one df is always used up when a mean is estimated from the data. It tempts candidates who multiply 3 by 10 correctly and then forget the subtraction step, especially under time pressure in a numerical item.","d":"31 does not match any ANOVA df in this design; it would require adding one to N, which no df formula does. Degrees of freedom are always reduced, never increased, by the parameters estimated from the data. It tempts only as an arithmetic slip, such as confusing N plus 1 with N minus 1, and is included mainly to catch guessing."}},{"q":"What is the degrees of freedom on an independent samples t-test with a total sample size of 25 in each group.","concept":"Degrees of freedom (df) are the number of scores free to vary once certain statistics are fixed. If five scores must have a mean of 10, four can take any value, but the fifth is then determined, so df = 4. Each time a mean is estimated from a sample, one df is used up. This gives the df rules for the three t-tests, all based on the distribution published by William Sealy Gosset (1908) under the name 'Student'. In a one-sample t-test one mean is estimated, so df = n minus 1. In a paired (related or dependent) samples t-test the analysis runs on the difference scores, so df = number of pairs minus 1. In an independent samples t-test two separate group means are estimated, so df = (n1 minus 1) plus (n2 minus 1) = N minus 2, where N is the total of both groups. Here each group has 25 people, so N = 50 and df = 25 + 25 minus 2 = 48. Adding the df of each group gives the same result: 24 + 24 = 48. When group variances are unequal, the test proposed by Bernard L. Welch (1947) uses an adjusted, usually non-integer df. Watch the wording in variants: 25 matched pairs would give 24, and 25 people in total would give 23. Option a is right because an independent samples t-test with 25 in each group has df = n1 + n2 minus 2 = 50 minus 2 = 48.","traps":{"b":"24 is n minus 1 for a single group of 25. It would be the correct df for a one-sample t-test on 25 people, or for a paired samples t-test with 25 matched pairs, where the analysis runs on 25 difference scores. It tempts because the stem says '25 in each group', and candidates apply n minus 1 to one group instead of combining both groups.","c":"49 is N minus 1 for all 50 people treated as one sample. It subtracts only one df, as if a single mean had been estimated, whereas an independent samples t-test estimates two group means and so loses two df. It tempts because N minus 1 is the most memorised df formula, and 49 is close enough to 48 to look right.","d":"25 is the size of one group, not a degrees-of-freedom value. Degrees of freedom are always reduced by the number of means estimated, so a raw sample size cannot serve as df. It tempts because it is the only number printed in the stem, and a hurried reader may take 'total sample size of 25' as the answer itself."}},{"q":"F ratio in one-way ANOVA is given by","concept":"The F ratio is the test statistic of analysis of variance (ANOVA), the method Ronald A. Fisher developed for agricultural experiments and set out in 1925; George W. Snedecor named the statistic F in his honour in 1934. ANOVA partitions the total sum of squares (SS total) into SS between, the spread of group means around the grand mean, and SS within, the spread of individual scores around their own group mean. Each SS is divided by its degrees of freedom to give a mean square (MS), which is a variance estimate: MS between = SS between divided by (k minus 1), and MS within = SS within divided by (N minus k). The logic is signal over noise. MS within reflects only random error. MS between reflects that same error plus any real treatment effect. So F = MS between divided by MS within. If the null hypothesis is true, both estimate the same error variance and F is close to 1; a real effect inflates the numerator and pushes F well above 1. F can never be negative because both terms are variances; its distribution is positively skewed and defined by two df values, written F(df between, df within). With only two groups, F equals t squared. Mean square total is not part of the ratio; SS total appears instead in effect sizes such as eta squared (SS between divided by SS total). Option a is right because F is defined as mean square between groups divided by mean square within groups.","traps":{"b":"MS total over MS within is not a standard statistic. Total variability is the starting point that ANOVA splits into between and within parts, so placing it in the numerator mixes error back into the signal and the comparison loses its logic. It tempts because SS total does appear in ANOVA tables and in eta squared (SS between divided by SS total).","c":"MS within over MS total also has no role in ANOVA. Dividing error by total variability resembles the proportion of unexplained variance (1 minus eta squared, which is computed from sums of squares, not mean squares). It tempts because it looks like a part-to-whole ratio, which is how effect sizes are built, but F compares two independent variance estimates rather than a part with its whole.","d":"MS within over MS between is the F ratio turned upside down. Putting error variance on top would make large treatment effects produce small values, the reverse of how F is read against its critical value. It tempts because candidates remember that both mean squares appear in the formula and simply forget which one is the numerator. Memory aid: signal (between) over noise (within)."}},{"q":"The most appropriate non-parametric alternative for a related samples t-test when using an ordinal level of variable with some magnitude is","concept":"Parametric tests such as the t-test assume interval or ratio data and roughly normal distributions. When those assumptions fail, or the data are ordinal, non-parametric (distribution-free) tests are used, usually working on ranks. Choosing one depends on two questions: are the samples independent or related, and how much information do the scores carry? For two related samples (the same people measured twice, or matched pairs) the parametric test is the paired samples t-test. Its non-parametric alternatives are the sign test and the Wilcoxon matched-pairs signed-rank test of Frank Wilcoxon (1945). The sign test records only whether each difference is positive or negative and checks the split against the binomial distribution, so it discards size. The Wilcoxon test keeps it: find each pair's difference, drop zero differences, rank the absolute differences from smallest to largest, attach the original signs, and sum the positive and negative ranks separately. The smaller sum, T, is compared with a critical value. Because larger differences carry more weight, it is more powerful than the sign test. Sidney Siegel (1956) stressed that it needs data whose differences can be ordered by size, which is the 'ordinal with some magnitude' in the stem. Parallel tests for other designs: Kruskal-Wallis, from William Kruskal and W. Allen Wallis (1952), for three or more independent groups, and the Friedman test, from Milton Friedman (1937), for three or more related conditions. Option b is right because the Wilcoxon signed-rank test is the related samples alternative that uses both the direction and the magnitude of differences.","traps":{"a":"The Mann-Whitney U test, from Henry B. Mann and Donald R. Whitney (1947), is the non-parametric alternative to the independent samples t-test. It pools scores from two separate groups, ranks them together, and checks whether one group's ranks are systematically higher. It ignores pairing, so it suits different people in each group, not matched or repeated measures. It tempts because it is the best-known rank test and is equivalent to Wilcoxon's rank-sum test.","c":"The chi-square test, from Karl Pearson (1900), compares observed and expected frequencies of nominal categories, either for goodness of fit or for independence in a contingency table. It works on counts, not ranked differences, and the standard version assumes independent observations, so it cannot handle paired ordinal magnitudes. It tempts because it is the most familiar non-parametric test and a common default guess for any non-parametric question.","d":"The sign test also handles two related samples, so it is the closest distractor. But it uses only the direction of each difference (plus or minus) and tests the split with the binomial distribution, ignoring how large each difference is. That wastes information and lowers power. It tempts because it is a genuine paired alternative; the phrase 'with some magnitude' in the stem is what rules it out in favour of Wilcoxon."}},{"q":"According to Wallas' model of the creative process, what is the second stage?","concept":"Graham Wallas, an English social psychologist and a co-founder of the London School of Economics, proposed the best-known stage model of creative thinking in The Art of Thought (1926). He built it from the self-reports of creative thinkers, especially the physicist Hermann von Helmholtz and the mathematician Henri Poincare, who described solutions arriving suddenly after a period away from the problem. The four stages, in order, are: (1) Preparation, the conscious, effortful work of defining the problem, gathering information and trying solutions, often ending in an impasse. (2) Incubation, setting the problem aside while the mind turns to other things; Wallas believed unconscious or fringe-conscious processing continued meanwhile. (3) Illumination, the sudden 'aha' or flash of insight when the idea enters awareness, sometimes announced by a vague feeling Wallas called intimation. (4) Verification, the conscious testing, checking and refining of the idea. A handy mnemonic is PIIV. Modern research supports a modest incubation effect, often explained by forgetting misleading cues or fading fixation rather than hidden full-scale problem solving. Neighbouring ideas used in variants: insight learning in the chimpanzee studies of Wolfgang Kohler (1917); divergent and convergent thinking from J. P. Guilford (1950); the five-stage version of Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi (1996), which adds evaluation and elaboration; and functional fixedness, the block incubation can relieve. Exams also ask for the first stage, the last stage, or the stage matching a description. Option b is right because incubation is the second of Wallas's four stages, coming after preparation and before illumination.","traps":{"a":"Illumination is the third stage in Wallas (1926): the sudden flash of insight, the 'aha' moment, when the solution breaks into awareness after incubation. It is often preceded by intimation, a vague feeling that an idea is about to arrive. It tempts because illumination is the most dramatic and memorable stage, and candidates who remember insight following a break may misplace it as the second stage.","c":"Verification is the fourth and final stage in Wallas (1926): the conscious, deliberate testing, checking and refining of the idea produced by illumination, for example a mathematician working through the full proof. It brings back the effortful, logical thinking of preparation. It tempts because verification sits in the same list as the other stages, and a candidate unsure of the order can place it anywhere.","d":"Preparation is the first stage in Wallas (1926): defining the problem, collecting information and making conscious attempts at a solution, often until reaching a frustrating impasse. Incubation follows it. It tempts because preparation naturally feels like the start of the process, and candidates who know the stage names but not their order, or who misread 'second', pick it."}},{"q":"What is the illumination stage of Wallas' model of the creative process?","concept":"Graham Wallas (1926), in The Art of Thought, described creative thinking in four stages, drawing on reports by Hermann von Helmholtz and Henri Poincare of solutions that arrived suddenly after a rest from the problem. Preparation is conscious work: defining the problem, collecting information and trying solutions. Incubation is the period when the problem is set aside and attention turns elsewhere, while some unconscious processing is thought to continue. Illumination is the third stage: the sudden 'aha' or 'eureka' moment when a solution, or the key idea for one, appears in consciousness, often unexpectedly, as in Poincare's account of grasping a mathematical idea just as he stepped onto a bus. Wallas noted that illumination may be preceded by intimation, a fringe feeling that an answer is near. Verification is the final stage, in which the idea is tested, worked out and refined. Illumination is the experience psychologists study as insight. Wolfgang Kohler (1917) saw it when his chimpanzee Sultan suddenly joined two sticks to reach fruit, and Karl Duncker (1945) showed that insight comes from restructuring the problem. Insight problems are solved by such restructuring, unlike analytic problems solved step by step. Examiners build variants by giving a description and asking for the stage: initial attempts (preparation), leaving the problem aside (incubation), the flash of insight (illumination), testing the solution (verification). Option d is right because illumination is the stage at which insight into the problem is achieved and the solution suddenly becomes conscious.","traps":{"a":"Formulating the problem and making initial attempts to solve it describes preparation, the first stage in Wallas (1926). This is conscious, deliberate effort: gathering knowledge, defining the goal and trying approaches, often until the person gets stuck. It tempts because preparation is when the person works hardest on the problem, and candidates may wrongly link active effort with the arrival of the solution.","b":"Testing and carrying out the solution describes verification, the fourth and final stage in Wallas (1926). Here the insight produced during illumination is checked for accuracy, worked out in detail and applied, using deliberate, logical thinking. It tempts because verification deals with the solution and follows straight after illumination, so a candidate who thinks loosely of 'the solution stage' may choose it.","c":"Leaving the problem while considering other things describes incubation, the second stage in Wallas (1926). The problem is set aside, and unconscious processing, release from fixation or forgetting of misleading cues may prepare the ground for insight. It tempts because incubation leads directly into illumination, and many candidates merge the rest period and the sudden insight that follows it into a single stage."}},{"q":"According to Sternberg's investment theory, how many distinct but interrelated resources are required for creativity?","concept":"Robert J. Sternberg and Todd I. Lubart proposed the investment theory of creativity in 1991 and expanded it in their book Defying the Crowd (1995). Its central metaphor comes from finance: creative people 'buy low and sell high' in the world of ideas. They pursue ideas that are unknown or out of favour, persuade others of their worth despite resistance, then move on once the idea becomes popular. Sternberg and Lubart argue that creativity needs a confluence of six distinct but interrelated resources: (1) intellectual abilities, namely synthetic ability to see problems in new ways, analytic ability to judge which ideas are worth pursuing, and practical-contextual ability to sell ideas to others; (2) knowledge of the field, which helps but can also entrench old ways of thinking; (3) thinking styles, especially a legislative style that prefers making one's own rules; (4) personality, including willingness to overcome obstacles, take sensible risks and tolerate ambiguity; (5) motivation, mainly intrinsic and task-focused; and (6) environment, a setting that supports and rewards creative ideas. Confluence means the resources combine in complex ways: some need a minimum threshold and one can partly compensate for another. Distinguish neighbouring models: the componential model of Teresa Amabile (1983), with domain-relevant skills, creativity-relevant processes and intrinsic task motivation, later adding the social environment; the systems model of Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi (1988), with person, domain and field; and the four stages of Graham Wallas (1926). Option d is right because the investment theory names exactly six resources: intellectual abilities, knowledge, thinking styles, personality, motivation and environment.","traps":{"a":"Five is not the count in the investment theory, which lists six resources. Five-part lists elsewhere cause the slip: the Big Five personality traits, in which openness to experience is the trait most linked to creativity, or the five-stage creative process of Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi (1996). It tempts candidates who recall most of the list but forget one resource, usually environment or thinking styles.","b":"Four is not correct either. Four is the number of stages in the creative process of Graham Wallas (1926), namely preparation, incubation, illumination and verification, and of the components in the later version of Teresa Amabile's componential model, which added the social environment. It tempts because these four-part creativity models are taught in the same chapter, so the numbers are easily swapped.","c":"Seven overstates the count; the investment theory has six resources. Seven is the number of intelligences in the original multiple intelligences theory of Howard Gardner (1983), before he added naturalist intelligence. It tempts because Gardner and Sternberg are both taught as intelligence theorists who also wrote on creativity, so their lists are easily mixed up under exam pressure."}},{"q":"What is the difference between functional fixedness and mental set?","concept":"Both terms describe ways past experience blocks problem solving, a family often called fixation. Mental set is the broader idea: a tendency to keep using a problem-solving strategy that worked before, even when a simpler or better one is available. The classic demonstration is the water jar problem of Abraham S. Luchins (1942). Participants used three jars of given sizes to measure out exact amounts of water. The first several problems could only be solved with a long formula (B minus A minus 2C). Later problems could be solved with a simple one (A minus C), but most participants who had practised the long method kept using it, and some failed a problem only the simple method could solve. Luchins called this the Einstellung effect. Functional fixedness is a narrower block that concerns objects: the tendency to see an object only in terms of its usual function. Karl Duncker (1945) showed it with the candle problem: given a candle, matches and a box of tacks, people struggled to fix the candle to a wall because they saw the box only as a container, not a shelf, and did better when the box was presented empty. Norman Maier (1931) found a similar effect in the two-string problem, where pliers could serve as a pendulum weight. Some textbooks treat functional fixedness as a special case of mental set. Option c is right because functional fixedness concerns how we think about physical objects, while mental set concerns the rigid use of problem-solving strategies.","traps":{"a":"This option reverses the two definitions. It assigns problem-solving strategies to functional fixedness and physical objects to mental set, the opposite of the classic studies: the candle problem of Karl Duncker (1945) concerned an object's usual function, and the water jars of Abraham S. Luchins (1942) concerned a habitual method. It tempts because it uses the right two ideas, objects and strategies, and only careful reading reveals the swap.","b":"Saying there is no difference is wrong, though it holds a grain of truth. Both are forms of fixation in which past experience blocks a fresh approach, and some textbooks describe functional fixedness as a special type of mental set. But they are defined separately: one concerns the uses of objects, the other concerns strategies. It tempts candidates who remember only that both are obstacles to problem solving.","d":"This option borrows a perception term. Top-down processing means interpretation guided by prior knowledge and expectations, as in recognising a word from context; it is not the defining feature of functional fixedness, which is seeing an object only in its usual use. The second half, applying strategies too rigidly, does describe mental set. It tempts because one half is right and the jargon sounds sophisticated."}},{"q":"Why does functional fixedness occur?","concept":"Functional fixedness is the tendency to see an object only in terms of its usual function, which blocks you from noticing a new use that would solve the problem. Karl Duncker (1945) demonstrated it with the candle problem: given a candle, a box of tacks and some matches, people must fix the candle to a wall so that it burns without dripping. The solution is to empty the box, tack it to the wall and use it as a shelf. People solve it far less often when the box is presented full of tacks, because it is then encoded as a container, not a platform. Norman Maier (1931) found a similar block in the two-string problem, where a pair of pliers must be used as a pendulum weight. Functional fixedness is a special case of mental set, the readiness to attack a problem in the way that worked before, shown by Abraham Luchins (1942) with his water-jar problems. Both are failures caused by knowledge, not by its absence. In cognitive terms this is top-down processing: thinking and perception driven by stored concepts, schemas and expectations, as opposed to bottom-up processing, driven by the raw features of the stimulus. Tim German and Margaret Defeyter (2000) found that five-year-olds are less fixed than six- and seven-year-olds, who have formed firmer ideas of what objects are for. Option b is right because functional fixedness arises when prior knowledge of an object's usual use, a top-down influence, dominates and hides its other possible uses.","traps":{"a":"A lack of problem-solving skill would predict that experts and older children suffer least, but functional fixedness works the other way: it grows out of knowledge about how objects are normally used. German and Defeyter (2000) found older children more fixed than younger ones, and skilled adults routinely fail Duncker's candle problem. It tempts because any failure to solve a problem looks, on the surface, like a skills deficit.","c":"Underestimating the environment is not the mechanism. In the candle problem every needed object is in plain view and people do attend to it; the difficulty is that they encode the box only as a container. The obstacle is internal, a fixed mental representation, not neglect of the situation. It tempts because the solution does involve using the environment creatively, so the option sounds related to the outcome.","d":"Limited access to tools describes an external shortage of resources, not a cognitive bias. In the studies of Duncker (1945) and Maier (1931) all the necessary objects were provided; participants failed because they could not see a familiar object serving a new function. Functional fixedness is about how we represent what we have, not how much we have. It tempts because the classic examples all involve tools and objects."}},{"q":"What are heuristics in problem-solving?","concept":"In problem solving, a heuristic is a rule of thumb or mental shortcut that usually leads to a good answer quickly but carries no guarantee of success. The term was popularised by the mathematician George Polya (1945) in How to Solve It and formalised by Allen Newell and Herbert A. Simon (1972) in Human Problem Solving, who described problem solving as a search through a problem space from an initial state to a goal state. The contrast concept is the algorithm: a step-by-step procedure that, if followed correctly, always produces the solution, such as trying every combination of a lock or applying a formula. Algorithms are guaranteed but can be slow, or impossible when the problem space is huge; heuristics are fast and economical but can fail. Simon's idea of bounded rationality explains why people rely on heuristics: we have limited time, memory and information, so we satisfice rather than optimise. Common problem-solving heuristics are means-ends analysis, working backward, hill climbing, setting subgoals and analogy. In judgement and decision making, Amos Tversky and Daniel Kahneman (1974) described the availability, representativeness, and anchoring and adjustment heuristics, and showed the systematic biases they produce. A variant question may ask which strategy guarantees a solution (algorithm) or which is a shortcut that may fail (heuristic). Neither is simply better: the choice depends on the cost of errors and of time. Option a is right because a heuristic is a general rule that is usually, but not always, right, trading certainty for speed.","traps":{"b":"A method that always produces a solution is the definition of an algorithm, the systematic procedure that exhausts possibilities or applies a fixed formula, such as checking every possible arrangement of an anagram. Heuristics carry no such guarantee; they can lead to dead ends or errors. It tempts because heuristics often do work, and candidates confuse 'usually works' with 'always works', which is exactly the distinction being tested.","c":"Neither strategy is better in general. Heuristics save time and effort, but algorithms guarantee a solution, so the right choice depends on the problem, the size of the search space and the cost of mistakes. Newell and Simon (1972) treated both as tools for searching a problem space. It tempts because textbooks praise heuristics for efficiency, which candidates misread as overall superiority.","d":"Accuracy is the algorithm's strength, not the heuristic's. A correctly applied algorithm reaches the right answer every time, while heuristics trade some accuracy for speed. Tversky and Kahneman (1974) showed that judgement heuristics such as availability and representativeness produce systematic biases, which is direct evidence that they are less accurate. It tempts because heuristics feel intuitive and are often good enough in daily life."}},{"q":"What is the hill-climbing heuristic in problem-solving?","concept":"Hill climbing is a problem-solving heuristic in which, at each step, you choose the move that seems to bring you closest to the goal state. It comes from the problem-space approach of Allen Newell and Herbert A. Simon (1972): every problem has an initial state, a goal state and operators that transform one state into another, and a heuristic is a rule for choosing among operators. The name is an analogy: a climber in fog who always steps uphill will usually reach the top. Hill climbing is fast and needs little memory, because you only compare the current state with the goal. Its weakness is that some problems require a move that temporarily takes you further from the goal. The classic illustration is the Hobbits and Orcs (missionaries and cannibals) river-crossing problem, where people get stuck at the step that requires carrying creatures back across the river. A hill climber can also be trapped on a local peak. Neighbouring heuristics that examiners swap in: means-ends analysis, in which you find the biggest difference between the current and goal states and set subgoals to reduce it, as in Newell and Simon's General Problem Solver; working backward from the goal; breaking a problem into subgoals; analogy, which reuses the solution to an earlier similar problem; and trial and error, which tries options with no guiding rule. Means-ends analysis tolerates detours that hill climbing avoids. Option d is right because hill climbing means choosing, at each point, the option that moves you closer to the goal state.","traps":{"a":"Using the solution of an earlier, similar problem is the analogy heuristic. Its success depends on spotting the deep structural similarity between problems, as Mary Gick and Keith Holyoak (1980) showed with Duncker's radiation problem: people rarely used a helpful military story unless given a hint. Hill climbing uses no earlier problem; it only compares the current state with the goal. It tempts because analogy is another familiar named heuristic.","b":"Breaking a problem into smaller parts and solving each separately is the subgoal strategy, also called problem decomposition, and it is the core of means-ends analysis described by Newell and Simon (1972). The Tower of Hanoi is the classic example, where you first aim to free and move the largest disc. Hill climbing sets no subgoals; it simply takes the locally best step. It tempts because both are goal-directed heuristics.","c":"Trying different solutions until one works is trial and error, the strategy Edward Thorndike (1898) observed in cats escaping from his puzzle boxes. It has no guiding rule for which attempt to make next, whereas hill climbing evaluates each possible move against the goal and picks the one that reduces the distance most. It tempts because hill climbing also proceeds step by step and sometimes fails."}},{"q":"What are the three aspects of intelligence in Sternberg's Triarchic Theory?","concept":"Robert J. Sternberg (1985) proposed the triarchic theory of intelligence in Beyond IQ, arguing that IQ tests measure only a narrow slice of intelligent behaviour. Triarchic means ruled by three, and the theory has three subtheories. The componential subtheory covers the internal mental mechanisms of thinking: metacomponents (planning, monitoring and evaluating problem solving), performance components (carrying out the strategy) and knowledge-acquisition components (learning new information). It yields analytical intelligence, the kind IQ tests and school examinations reward. The experiential subtheory covers how a person handles novelty and how well they automatise familiar tasks; it yields creative intelligence. The contextual subtheory covers adapting to, shaping, or selecting one's environment; it yields practical intelligence, close to street smarts or tacit knowledge. Sternberg later broadened this into the theory of successful intelligence, a balance of analytical, creative and practical abilities used to reach one's goals within a cultural context. Know the neighbouring theories used as distractors: Charles Spearman's (1904) general factor g; Louis L. Thurstone's (1938) primary mental abilities; Howard Gardner's (1983) multiple intelligences, which began with seven (linguistic, logical-mathematical, spatial, musical, bodily-kinaesthetic, interpersonal, intrapersonal) and later added naturalist; and Peter Salovey and John Mayer's (1990) emotional intelligence. Do not confuse the triarchic theory with Sternberg's separate triangular theory of love (1986), built from intimacy, passion and commitment. Option b is right because the three aspects of Sternberg's triarchic theory are componential, experiential and contextual.","traps":{"a":"Linguistic, logical-mathematical and spatial are three of the multiple intelligences of Howard Gardner (1983), whose original list also included musical, bodily-kinaesthetic, interpersonal and intrapersonal, with naturalist added later. Gardner argued for relatively independent intelligences, citing evidence such as brain damage and savant syndrome. It tempts because Gardner and Sternberg are the two best known challengers to g and are taught together, so their lists are easily swapped.","c":"Social, emotional and spiritual are separate constructs from different authors: social intelligence was described by Edward Thorndike (1920), emotional intelligence by Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990) and popularised by Daniel Goleman (1995), and spiritual intelligence is a later popular idea. None is a Sternberg subtheory. It tempts because Sternberg's practical intelligence sounds social, and these terms appear in the same chapter on intelligence beyond IQ.","d":"Physical, cognitive and emotional are broad domains of human development used in lifespan textbooks, not components of any theory of intelligence. Sternberg's three parts all concern mental functioning: internal mechanisms of thought, the handling of novelty, and adaptation to context. It tempts because it sounds like a complete, balanced list of human abilities and contains the word 'cognitive', which candidates link with intelligence."}},{"q":"What are the five major parts of emotional intelligence, according to Goleman?","concept":"Emotional intelligence (EI) was first defined by Peter Salovey and John D. Mayer (1990) as the ability to monitor one's own and others' feelings and emotions, to discriminate among them and to use this information to guide thinking and action. Daniel Goleman, a psychologist and science journalist, popularised the idea in his best seller Emotional Intelligence (1995), adapting Salovey's framework into five domains: knowing one's emotions (self-awareness, recognising a feeling as it happens), managing emotions (handling feelings appropriately, such as soothing anxiety or anger), motivating oneself (marshalling emotions toward a goal, delaying gratification and controlling impulses), recognising emotions in others (empathy) and handling relationships (managing emotions in others, social competence). In Working with Emotional Intelligence (1998) Goleman relabelled these for the workplace as self-awareness, self-regulation, motivation, empathy and social skills, and he later merged them into four domains. Know the families of models. Ability models, such as Mayer and Salovey's (1997) four-branch model (perceiving, using, understanding and managing emotions), treat EI as a set of mental abilities measured by performance tests such as the MSCEIT. Mixed models, such as Goleman's and Reuven Bar-On's (1997) EQ-i, blend abilities with personality traits and motivation and are measured largely by self-report. The roots of the idea lie in Howard Gardner's (1983) interpersonal and intrapersonal intelligences and Edward Thorndike's (1920) social intelligence. Option b is right because it reproduces Goleman's original 1995 list of five domains in his own wording: knowing our emotions, managing them, motivating ourselves, recognising others' emotions and handling relationships.","traps":{"a":"This list mixes Howard Gardner's (1983) interpersonal and intrapersonal intelligences with generic labels such as cognitive and communication skills. Gardner's two personal intelligences are ancestors of emotional intelligence, but this is not Goleman's model, and cognitive skill belongs to IQ, the very thing EI was defined against. It tempts because interpersonal and intrapersonal sound like the self and other halves of Goleman's scheme.","c":"Self-awareness, self-regulation, motivation, empathy and social skills is Goleman's own workplace relabelling in Working with Emotional Intelligence (1998), so it is not a false description of his ideas. The key prefers b because b reproduces the five domains in the exact wording of his 1995 book. It tempts strongly because this later version is the one used in most management and leadership training today.","d":"IQ, EQ, a social quotient, CQ and a spiritual quotient are popular quotient labels for separate constructs: general intelligence, emotional intelligence itself, social intelligence, cultural intelligence and spiritual intelligence. They are rival or related constructs, not five parts of EI, and the list even contains EQ, the very thing being divided. It tempts because it is a neat list of five that includes the right term."}},{"q":"What are the two basic mental operations that Piaget hypothesized to be involved in learning?","concept":"Jean Piaget (1936) explained cognitive growth as adaptation, the same principle that drives biological evolution. The basic unit of knowledge is the schema (or scheme): an organised pattern of action or thought, such as sucking or grasping in infancy, or a concept of dog later on. Adaptation works through two complementary processes. Assimilation means interpreting new experience in terms of an existing schema: a toddler who knows 'doggie' calls a cow 'doggie'. Accommodation means modifying an existing schema, or creating a new one, when the experience does not fit: the child learns that cows are different and forms a new category. When existing schemas handle experience well, the child is in equilibrium; a mismatch creates disequilibrium, and the drive to restore balance through assimilation and accommodation is called equilibration, the motor of development. Organisation is the related tendency to combine schemas into larger, coordinated systems. Together these processes produce the four stages: sensorimotor (birth to about 2 years), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (from about 11). Exam variants ask which process changes a schema (accommodation), which fits experience into an unchanged schema (assimilation), or which names the balancing process (equilibration). Piaget saw the child as a little scientist who constructs knowledge through action, which is why his view is called constructivism. Option c is right because Piaget proposed assimilation and accommodation as the two basic operations of adaptation through which learning and cognitive development occur.","traps":{"a":"Encoding and retrieval are stages of memory in the information-processing approach: encoding turns input into a storable form, storage holds it, and retrieval brings it back, as in the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968). They describe memory processes, not Piaget's mechanisms of schema change. It tempts because information processing is the main modern alternative to Piaget in cognitive development chapters.","b":"Attention and perception are cognitive processes that select and interpret sensory input. They matter in every theory of learning but are not Piaget's named mechanisms; in his theory, perception is itself organised by schemas through assimilation. Information-processing researchers, not Piaget, made attention central to development. It tempts because attention and perception sound like the fundamental mental operations any theory of learning must include.","d":"Sensory input and motor output describe the reflex arc and the content of Piaget's first stage, the sensorimotor period, in which infants know the world through senses and actions. They are the raw material of early schemas, not the two processes that change schemas. It tempts because the word sensorimotor is strongly linked with Piaget, so a sensory and motor pairing looks Piagetian."}},{"q":"What are parapraxes, according to Freud's theory?","concept":"Parapraxes are the small errors of everyday life that Sigmund Freud (1901) analysed in The Psychopathology of Everyday Life: slips of the tongue, slips of the pen, misreadings, mishearings, forgetting of names or appointments, and mislaying objects. The English word parapraxis was introduced by Freud's translator James Strachey for the German Fehlleistung, literally faulty action. Freud's claim was that these errors are not random. They are compromise formations: an unconscious wish, impulse or thought that has been repressed slips past the ego's defences and interferes with what we consciously intend to say or do. A chairman who opens a meeting by declaring it closed reveals that he wishes it were over. Parapraxes therefore join dreams (the royal road to the unconscious in Freud's 1900 book on dreams), free association and neurotic symptoms as windows into unconscious content, and they illustrate psychic determinism, the principle that no mental event is accidental. The hidden content need not be sexual; it can be hostility, anxiety, rivalry or any other repressed wish. Neighbouring concepts used to build variants: the conscience and the ego-ideal, the two parts of the superego, formed through childhood punishment and praise respectively; repression, the defence that keeps material unconscious; and the topographic model of conscious, preconscious and unconscious. Modern cognitive psychologists explain many slips as ordinary errors in speech planning, so treat Freud's account as theory, not settled fact. Option b is right because parapraxes are mistakes that reveal the unconscious.","traps":{"a":"This option is half right: parapraxes do include errors in speaking and writing. But Freud did not define them as sexual in nature. The hidden motive can be any repressed wish, such as hostility, fear or rivalry, not only sexual content, so the added qualifier is the trap. It tempts because Freud's theory is popularly reduced to sex, and slips with sexual meaning are the best known jokes about Freudian slips.","c":"Good behaviours for which children have been praised build the ego-ideal, one of the two subsystems of the superego described by Sigmund Freud (1923). Rewarded behaviour becomes an internal standard that produces pride when we meet it. This concerns moral development, not everyday mistakes. It tempts because it comes from the same chapter on Freud's structural model and uses the language of childhood learning.","d":"Behaviours for which children are punished form the conscience, the second subsystem of the superego in Freud's (1923) structural model; violating it produces guilt. It is a moral structure created through punishment and identification with parents, not an error that leaks out of the unconscious. It tempts because guilt and the unconscious are related Freudian ideas, and the option sounds like a technical definition."}},{"q":"What are Freudian slips, according to Freud's theory?","concept":"A Freudian slip is the everyday name for what Sigmund Freud (1901) called a parapraxis, described in The Psychopathology of Everyday Life. The term covers slips of the tongue and pen, misreadings, forgetting a familiar name, losing objects and other small faulty actions. Freud's argument rests on psychic determinism: no mental act is accidental, so an apparently random error must have a cause. In his view the cause is a repressed wish or thought in the unconscious, which breaks through the ego's censorship and distorts the intended act. A student who writes that she looks forward to a long and fruitless collaboration may be revealing doubt about the project. The slip is a compromise between the conscious intention and the unconscious impulse. Freud placed slips beside dreams, jokes and neurotic symptoms as evidence for an active unconscious, and analysts used them clinically much as they used free association. The hidden content can be aggressive, competitive, anxious or sexual; sex is not part of the definition. Variants of this question swap in Freud's structural terms: the id, the pleasure-seeking reservoir of drives; the ego, which follows the reality principle; and the superego with its two parts, the conscience (formed by punishment) and the ego-ideal (formed by praise). They may also ask about the topographic model, in which the preconscious holds material that can easily become conscious. Critics note that many slips are explained by ordinary errors in speech planning. Option a is right because a Freudian slip is a mistake that reveals the unconscious.","traps":{"b":"Freudian slips can occur in speech or writing, so the first half fits, but the qualifier 'sexual in nature' makes it wrong. Freud (1901) held that the hidden motive can be any repressed content, such as hostility, jealousy, anxiety or ambition, as well as sexual wishes. It tempts because popular culture treats every Freudian slip as a sexual joke and because sexuality is central to Freud's drive theory.","c":"Behaviours that earned praise in childhood are internalised as the ego-ideal, the part of the superego that holds standards of what we should be and rewards us with pride, according to Sigmund Freud (1923). It is a moral structure, not an error that betrays hidden wishes. It tempts because it is drawn from the same Freud chapter and the wording sounds like a definition of a Freudian term.","d":"Behaviours punished in childhood form the conscience, the part of the superego that forbids and punishes with guilt, in Freud's (1923) structural model. A slip may reveal a guilty wish, but the conscience itself is a set of internal prohibitions, not a mistake. It tempts because guilt, punishment and unconscious conflict all appear in discussions of slips, and the option uses Freud's technical vocabulary."}},{"q":"What is the phallic stage of development, according to Freud's theory?","concept":"Sigmund Freud set out the psychosexual stages in Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality (1905), naming the phallic stage in 1923. Each stage is defined by the erogenous zone through which the libido, the energy of the sexual instinct, seeks pleasure, and by a conflict that must be resolved; too much frustration or indulgence leaves a fixation that shapes adult personality. The oral stage (birth to about 18 months) centres on the mouth: sucking, biting and feeding. The anal stage (about 18 months to 3 years) centres on the anus, and its key conflict is toilet training. The phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) centres on the genitals. The child develops sexual feelings toward the opposite-sex parent and rivalry with the same-sex parent: the Oedipus complex in boys, with castration anxiety, and in girls what Carl Jung (1913) called the Electra complex, a term Freud rejected, with penis envy. The complex is resolved by repressing the incestuous wishes and identifying with the same-sex parent, which forms the superego and gender identity. The latency stage (about 6 years to puberty) is a quiet period in which sexual impulses are repressed and sublimated into schoolwork, friendships and play. The genital stage, from puberty, brings mature sexuality directed at partners outside the family. Exam variants match a stage to its zone, its conflict or its adult fixation, such as the oral character or the anal-retentive personality. Option c is right because the phallic stage is when incestuous Oedipal fantasies toward the opposite-sex parent arise.","traps":{"a":"The stage in which the mouth is the main erogenous zone is the oral stage, from birth to about 18 months, when pleasure comes from sucking, biting and feeding. Fixation here is linked with dependency and with oral habits such as smoking or overeating. It precedes the phallic stage by several years. It tempts because it is the first and most familiar stage, so candidates who only remember the stage list may guess it.","b":"Conflict over toilet training is the defining event of the anal stage, about 18 months to 3 years, when pleasure centres on retaining or expelling faeces. Harsh or lax training was thought to produce the anal-retentive (orderly, stingy, stubborn) or anal-expulsive (messy, careless) character. It tempts because it is the stage immediately before the phallic stage, and both involve parental control and conflict.","d":"Sublimation of the sex instinct describes the latency stage, from about 6 years to puberty, when sexual impulses are repressed and channelled into school learning, hobbies and same-sex friendships. Sublimation is the defence mechanism that redirects drives into socially valued activity. It tempts because the latency stage follows directly from the resolution of the phallic stage, so the two are easily confused."}},{"q":"What are the two parts of the superego, according to Freud's theory?","concept":"Sigmund Freud (1923) introduced the structural model of the mind in The Ego and the Id. The id, present at birth, is the reservoir of instinctual drives; it operates on the pleasure principle and seeks immediate gratification through primary process thinking. The ego develops to deal with reality; it operates on the reality principle and uses secondary process thinking, delaying gratification until a realistic outlet is found. The superego is the last structure to form, emerging at about age 5 to 6 as the child resolves the Oedipus complex by identifying with the same-sex parent and internalising parental and cultural standards. Freud described two subsystems of the superego. The conscience contains the prohibitions learned through punishment: what we must not do. When we violate it we feel guilt. The ego-ideal contains the positive standards learned through praise and reward: what we should strive to be. Living up to it brings pride and raised self-esteem. A harsh superego can be as troublesome as a strong id, producing excessive guilt and perfectionism, and the ego must mediate between id, superego and reality, using defence mechanisms when anxiety rises. Examiners build variants by asking which principle governs each structure, which structure forms last, or which part of the superego produces guilt. Remember the mapping: punishment builds the conscience and guilt; praise builds the ego-ideal and pride. Option d is right because Freud divided the superego into the conscience and the ego-ideal.","traps":{"a":"The id and the ego are the other two structures of Freud's (1923) tripartite model, not parts of the superego. The id is the inborn source of drives and the ego is the reality-oriented mediator; the superego stands beside them as the third structure. It tempts because the three are always taught together, so a candidate who remembers the model but not the superego's subdivisions picks the familiar pair.","b":"The pleasure principle and the reality principle are the operating rules of the id and the ego respectively, described by Sigmund Freud (1911): the id demands immediate gratification, while the ego delays it until reality allows. The superego follows neither; it operates on moral standards. It tempts because 'principle' sounds like a moral term, and these are among Freud's best known paired concepts.","c":"The ego and the unconscious mix Freud's structural model with his earlier topographic model of conscious, preconscious and unconscious. The ego is a separate structure, and the unconscious is a level of awareness, not a part of the superego. Large parts of both the ego and the superego themselves operate unconsciously. It tempts because the superego does work largely outside awareness, so 'unconscious' sounds plausibly related."}},{"q":"What is the anima, according to Jung's theory of archetypes?","concept":"Carl Gustav Jung, the Swiss psychiatrist who broke with Sigmund Freud in 1913 and founded analytical psychology, divided the psyche into the conscious ego, the personal unconscious (forgotten and repressed experiences organised into complexes) and the collective unconscious. The collective unconscious is inherited and shared by all humans; it contains archetypes, universal predispositions to perceive and respond in certain ways, which appear as recurring images in myths, dreams, religions and art. The persona is the mask or socially acceptable front we present to others, named after the masks worn by actors in Roman theatre. The shadow is the dark, primitive and socially unacceptable side of personality, which we tend to deny and project onto others. The anima is the feminine side of the male psyche, and the animus is the masculine side of the female psyche. Jung held that each sex carries psychological qualities of the other, partly from the species' long experience of living with the other sex, and that a man projects his anima onto the women he becomes infatuated with. Other archetypes include the great mother, the wise old man, the hero, and the self, which represents wholeness and the drive toward unity and is symbolised by the mandala. Integrating these opposites is part of individuation, the lifelong process of becoming a complete self. Exam variants ask which archetype is the mask, the dark side, the feminine element in men or the masculine element in women. Option b is right because the anima is the female element of a man.","traps":{"a":"The male element of a woman is the animus, the counterpart of the anima in Carl Jung's theory of archetypes. It represents masculine qualities such as reason and assertiveness in the female psyche and shapes how a woman relates to men. It tempts because anima and animus look almost identical and are always taught as a pair, so swapping them is the most common error on this question.","c":"The socially acceptable front that we present to others is the persona, named after the mask of the Roman actor. Jung saw it as necessary for social life but harmful when a person over-identifies with the role and loses touch with the real self. It is an archetype, but it concerns public image, not the opposite sex. It tempts because the persona is the first archetype listed in most textbooks.","d":"The dark and unacceptable side of personality is the shadow, which holds primitive, animal instincts and qualities the ego refuses to acknowledge. Jung said we project the shadow onto others, for example onto disliked people or groups. It tempts because the anima is also hidden and unconscious, and all three archetypes in these options come from the same list, so a vague memory of the list is not enough."}},{"q":"What are the two attitudes in Jung's theory of personality?","concept":"In Psychological Types (1921), Carl Gustav Jung proposed a typology built from two attitudes and four functions. The attitudes describe the direction in which psychic energy (libido) flows. In extraversion it flows outward, toward people, objects and events; in introversion it flows inward, toward thoughts, feelings and the subjective world. Jung held that everyone has both attitudes, but one is dominant and conscious while the other is less developed and lies in the unconscious. The four functions describe how we take in and process experience. Thinking and feeling are the rational, or judging, functions: thinking evaluates by logic and truth, feeling by value, like or dislike. Sensing and intuiting are the irrational, or perceiving, functions: sensing registers what is actually present through the senses, while intuition grasps possibilities and hunches beyond the data. Combining the two attitudes with the four functions gives eight psychological types, such as the extraverted thinking type. The Myers-Briggs Type Indicator, developed by Katharine Cook Briggs and Isabel Briggs Myers from the 1940s, built on Jung and added a judging versus perceiving scale. Hans Eysenck (1947) later treated extraversion and introversion as a continuous trait dimension with a biological basis, and the dimension survives today in the Big Five. Examiners build variants by asking which pair are the rational functions, which are irrational, or what an attitude means. Option c is right because the two attitudes in Jung's theory are extraversion and introversion.","traps":{"a":"Rational and irrational are not attitudes but Jung's labels for groups of functions. Thinking and feeling are rational because they make judgements; sensing and intuiting are irrational because they simply perceive without evaluating. Irrational here means non-judging, not illogical. It tempts because it is a genuine Jungian pair from the same theory and sounds like a basic orientation of personality.","b":"Sensing and intuiting are two of Jung's four functions, the irrational or perceiving pair. Sensing takes in concrete facts through the senses, while intuition perceives possibilities and patterns. They describe how information is gathered, not the direction in which psychic energy flows. It tempts because these terms are familiar from the Myers-Briggs S and N scales and appear in the same chapter as the attitudes.","d":"Thinking and feeling are the rational, or judging, functions in Jung's (1921) typology: thinking judges by logic, feeling by personal value. They are functions, not attitudes, and each can combine with either attitude, giving types such as the extraverted thinker or the introverted feeler. It tempts because thinking versus feeling is a well known Myers-Briggs scale and looks like a fundamental personality split."}},{"q":"Which of the following is the correct sequence of Erikson's stages of psychosocial development?","concept":"Erik H. Erikson (1950) described eight psychosocial stages across the lifespan in Childhood and Society. Each stage is a crisis between two opposite tendencies, and a favourable balance yields a basic strength or virtue. The order matters, because each stage builds on those before it, following the epigenetic principle. Learn the full list with ages and virtues. First, basic trust versus mistrust (birth to about 1 year): hope. Second, autonomy versus shame and doubt (1 to 3 years): will. Third, initiative versus guilt (3 to 6 years): purpose. Fourth, industry versus inferiority (6 years to puberty): competence. Fifth, identity versus role confusion (adolescence): fidelity. Sixth, intimacy versus isolation (young adulthood): love. Seventh, generativity versus stagnation (middle adulthood): care. Eighth, ego integrity versus despair (late adulthood): wisdom. The first four correspond roughly to Freud's oral, anal, phallic and latency stages and the fifth to the genital stage, but Erikson stressed social relationships and development after adolescence, which Freud did not. James Marcia (1966) later expanded the fifth stage into four identity statuses: diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium and achievement. Sequence questions list four stages and ask which list is in the right order; the safe method is to number each stage and check that the numbers rise. Here every list rises, so the item is flawed, and only the keyed list starts at stage 1 and includes its immediate successor. Option a is right because it lists trust, autonomy, identity and generativity in correct chronological order, beginning with the first stage.","traps":{"b":"This list begins with autonomy versus shame (stage 2), then industry versus inferiority (4), identity versus role confusion (5) and generativity versus stagnation (7). The numbers rise, so strictly it is also in correct order; the key rejects it presumably because it omits the first stage, trust versus mistrust. It tempts because it shares three stages with the keyed list and looks equally orderly.","c":"Initiative versus guilt (stage 3, about 3 to 6 years), industry versus inferiority (4), identity versus role confusion (5) and ego integrity versus despair (8) also run in rising order, so this list is not actually out of sequence. The key treats it as wrong, apparently because it starts midway rather than in infancy. It tempts because it spans early childhood to old age.","d":"Trust versus mistrust (stage 1), initiative versus guilt (3), intimacy versus isolation (6) and ego integrity versus despair (8) are also in rising order and even begin with the first stage. The key rejects it, perhaps because it skips autonomy, the stage that immediately follows trust. Strictly it is as correct as the keyed answer. It tempts because it opens with the first stage and ends with the last."}},{"q":"What are the four processes involved in observational learning, according to Bandura's social learning theory?","concept":"Observational learning, also called modelling or vicarious learning, is learning by watching others. Albert Bandura set it out in Social Learning Theory (1977), building on the Bobo doll experiments of Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961), in which children who watched an adult hit and kick an inflatable doll later imitated the same aggressive acts. Bandura identified four sub-processes that must all occur. Attentional processes: the observer must notice the model's behaviour; attention is greater for models who are attractive, powerful, similar to us or rewarded. Retention processes: the behaviour must be stored in memory as images or verbal codes, often helped by mental rehearsal. Production (motor reproduction) processes: the observer must be physically and cognitively able to turn the stored representation into action, refining it through feedback. Motivational processes: the observer must have a reason to perform, which comes from external reinforcement, vicarious reinforcement (seeing the model rewarded) or self-reinforcement. Bandura (1965) separated learning from performance: children who saw the model punished imitated less, yet reproduced the aggression readily when offered a reward, showing they had learned it. Neighbouring ideas: self-efficacy (Bandura, 1977), the belief that one can perform a behaviour; reciprocal determinism, the mutual influence of person, behaviour and environment; and the later renaming of the approach as social cognitive theory (1986). Contrast classical and operant conditioning, which require direct experience of stimuli or consequences. Option b is right because Bandura's four processes are attentional, retention, production and motivational.","traps":{"a":"Sensory, perceptual, cognitive and affective are broad levels of psychological functioning, roughly the route from receiving input to interpreting it, thinking about it and feeling about it. They are not Bandura's named sub-processes, though attention and retention involve perception and cognition. It tempts because the list sounds like a sensible account of how we take in a model's behaviour, and it has four items like the answer.","c":"Encoding, storage and retrieval are the three stages of memory in information-processing models such as that of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), with evaluation added as filler. They describe only what happens inside Bandura's retention stage, not the whole sequence from attention to motivation. It tempts because retention is one of the four processes and memory vocabulary sounds technical and scientific.","d":"Stimulus, response, reinforcement and extinction are the vocabulary of classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov) and operant conditioning (B. F. Skinner, 1938), where learning depends on direct experience of stimuli and consequences. Bandura's point was that people can learn without performing a response or being reinforced themselves. It tempts because reinforcement also appears in Bandura's motivational stage, and both theories sit in the learning chapter."}},{"q":"What are cardinal traits in Allport's theory?","concept":"Gordon W. Allport (1937) built the first major trait theory in Personality: A Psychological Interpretation. With Henry Odbert (1936) he combed an unabridged English dictionary and found about 18,000 words describing personal characteristics, the starting point of the lexical approach. Allport distinguished common traits, shared by many people in a culture and useful for comparing individuals, from personal dispositions, the traits unique to one individual. He ranked personal dispositions by how pervasive they are. A cardinal trait is so dominant that it shapes almost everything a person does; few people have one, and those who do may become known by it, as when we call someone Machiavellian or Christ-like. Central traits are the five to ten outstanding characteristics you would mention in a letter of recommendation, such as honest, warm or ambitious. Secondary traits are the least important, appearing only in certain situations and inconsistently, such as a preference for a type of food or impatience in queues. Allport championed the idiographic approach, the intensive study of one individual (as in his case study Letters from Jenny), against the nomothetic approach of comparing many people on common dimensions. He also proposed the functional autonomy of motives: adult motives can become independent of the childhood needs from which they arose. Later trait theorists, Raymond B. Cattell (1949) and Hans Eysenck (1947), used factor analysis to reduce traits to a few dimensions, leading eventually to the Big Five. Option c is right because cardinal traits are the most pervasive and powerful traits, dominating the whole of a person's life.","traps":{"a":"The least important traits, displayed inconspicuously and inconsistently, are Allport's secondary traits, such as liking spicy food or becoming impatient only in queues. They appear only in specific situations and tell us little about the whole person, the opposite end of the hierarchy from cardinal traits. It tempts because it is a genuine level of Allport's hierarchy, so it looks familiar to anyone who remembers the three tiers.","b":"The handful of outstanding traits that describe a person's behaviour are central traits, the five to ten characteristics a friend would list in a letter of recommendation. Everyone has central traits, whereas few people have a cardinal trait. It tempts strongly because 'outstanding' sounds like 'most powerful', and central traits are the middle tier that candidates most often confuse with cardinal traits.","d":"Traits shared by a number of people, such as the members of a culture, are Allport's common traits. They allow people to be compared on the same dimension, for example assertiveness, and fit the nomothetic approach. Allport contrasted them with personal dispositions, the unique traits of one person, which are then ranked as cardinal, central or secondary. It tempts because common traits appear in the same section."}},{"q":"What are L-data in Cattell's theory?","concept":"Raymond B. Cattell, whose Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF) appeared in 1949, built one of the most systematic trait theories using factor analysis, a statistical method that groups correlated variables into a smaller number of underlying factors. He argued that a theory of personality must be checked against three independent kinds of data, remembered as L, Q and T. L-data (life-record data) are information about a person's actual behaviour in everyday life, taken from records or from ratings by people who know the person well: school grades and absences, traffic offences, job history, or a teacher's ratings. Q-data (questionnaire data) are self-reports, in which people describe themselves on inventories such as the 16PF. T-data (objective test data) come from placing a person in a controlled, standardised situation and recording responses they cannot easily fake, such as reaction times or physiological measures. Cattell factor-analysed all three sources and found 16 source traits, the underlying stable structures (for example warmth, reasoning, emotional stability and dominance), which he contrasted with surface traits, clusters of observable behaviour that appear together. He also separated ability, temperament and dynamic (motivational) traits, and divided intelligence into fluid and crystallised forms (1963). His starting list of trait words came from the lexical work of Gordon Allport and Henry Odbert (1936). Examiners usually test the letter codes, so remember: L for life record, Q for questionnaire, T for test. Option c is right because L-data are information gathered about a person's life, such as from school records.","traps":{"a":"Data gathered from self-reports and questionnaires are Q-data in Cattell's scheme, collected with inventories such as the 16PF (1949). They rely on people describing themselves, so they can be distorted by poor self-insight or by faking good. It tempts because questionnaires are the most familiar way of measuring personality, and the 16PF is Cattell's best known instrument, so candidates assume his data must be questionnaire data.","b":"Data collected by placing a person in a controlled test situation and noting or rating responses are T-data, objective test data. Examples include reaction time, physiological responses or behaviour in a miniature situation, where the person cannot easily tell what is being measured and so cannot fake. It tempts because L could be misread as laboratory, and this option describes the most scientific-sounding data source.","d":"Cattell's 16 basic personality traits are his source traits, the underlying factors found by factor analysis, such as warmth, dominance and emotional stability, measured by the 16PF. They are the results of the analysis, not one of the three kinds of data on which it is based. It tempts because the number 16 is the most memorable fact about Cattell, so any option mentioning it looks safe."}},{"q":"What is the need for affiliation (n Aff) in Murray's theory?","concept":"Henry A. Murray (1938) set out his theory, which he called personology, in Explorations in Personality, written with colleagues at the Harvard Psychological Clinic. He defined a need as a force in the brain that organises perception, thinking and action so as to change an unsatisfying situation. He distinguished viscerogenic (primary, physiological) needs such as food and water from psychogenic (secondary, psychological) needs, and listed about twenty psychogenic needs, each written with n for need. Examples: n Affiliation (to draw near to and enjoy cooperation with others, to form friendships and remain loyal), n Achievement (to accomplish something difficult, to master and to excel), n Dominance (to control and influence others), n Exhibition (to make an impression, to be seen and heard, to excite and amuse), n Recognition (to gain approval and social status), n Autonomy, n Aggression, n Nurturance and n Order. Needs interact with press, the environmental forces that help or hinder need satisfaction, and a need-press combination is called a thema. To measure needs, Murray and Christiana Morgan (1935) developed the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), in which people tell stories about ambiguous pictures and project their needs into them. David McClelland (1953) later used TAT scoring to study n Achievement in depth, and his tradition focused on three needs: achievement, affiliation and power. Variants ask you to match a need to its description, or to separate needs from press. Option a is right because n Affiliation is the need for social interaction, friendship and companionship.","traps":{"b":"The need for power and control over others corresponds to Murray's n Dominance, the need to control, influence and direct others. David McClelland (1953) and his followers later studied it as the need for power (n Pow) in leadership. It is about controlling people, whereas affiliation is about being close to them. It tempts because affiliation, achievement and power form McClelland's famous trio, so the three are easily swapped.","c":"The need for recognition and success corresponds to two other Murray needs: n Achievement, the drive to accomplish difficult tasks and excel, and n Recognition, the desire for praise and social status. Both concern accomplishment and esteem, not warm relationships. It tempts because n Achievement is the most studied need in Murray's list, thanks to David McClelland (1953), and is often the first that candidates recall.","d":"The need for attention and excitement resembles Murray's n Exhibition, the need to make an impression, to be seen and heard, and to excite or amuse others, along with n Play. Exhibition uses people as an audience rather than seeking companionship with them. It tempts because attention-seeking involves other people, so it sounds social, like affiliation, even though the underlying motive is different."}},{"q":"What are the five needs in Maslow's hierarchy of needs, arranged in order from strongest at the bottom to weakest at the top?","concept":"Abraham H. Maslow (1943) proposed the hierarchy of needs in A Theory of Human Motivation and developed it in Motivation and Personality (1954). Needs are arranged by prepotency: lower needs are stronger and more urgent, so they dominate behaviour until they are reasonably satisfied, and only then do higher needs emerge. From the bottom: physiological needs (food, water, air, sleep, sex); safety needs (security, stability, freedom from fear and danger); belongingness and love needs (affection, friendship, family, acceptance by a group); esteem needs (self-respect, competence and achievement, plus respect and recognition from others); and self-actualisation (becoming everything one is capable of becoming). Maslow called the first four deficiency needs (D-needs), because their absence creates tension, and self-actualisation a growth or being need (B-need). He studied people he considered self-actualised, such as Abraham Lincoln and Eleanor Roosevelt, and described traits such as acceptance of self and others, spontaneity, autonomy and peak experiences. He did not insist that a lower need be completely satisfied; partial satisfaction is enough, and the order can vary for some people. Later he wrote of self-transcendence beyond self-actualisation. Neighbouring theories used in variants: Clayton Alderfer's (1969) ERG theory, which compresses the levels into existence, relatedness and growth and allows regression to lower needs; and Frederick Herzberg's (1959) two-factor theory of hygiene factors and motivators. The familiar pyramid drawing was popularised by others, not by Maslow himself. Option b is right because physiological, safety, belongingness and love, esteem and self-actualisation is the order from the strongest base to the weakest top.","traps":{"a":"Esteem, belongingness and love, safety, physiological, then self-actualisation scrambles the order: it runs the bottom four levels downward and then jumps to the top. In Maslow's (1943) hierarchy the base must be physiological needs, because they are the most prepotent. It tempts because it contains all five correct labels, so a candidate checking only for the right names, not the sequence, may choose it.","c":"Safety, physiological, esteem, belongingness and love, self-actualisation swaps two adjacent pairs: safety is placed below physiological needs, and esteem below belongingness. Maslow put physiological needs at the bottom because hunger and thirst override even safety, and he put love needs before esteem. It tempts because each swap is small and the first and last items look nearly right.","d":"Self-actualisation, esteem, belongingness and love, safety, physiological is the correct hierarchy read in reverse, from the top of the pyramid downward. The stem asks for the order from strongest at the bottom to weakest at the top, so the list must start with physiological needs. It tempts because the levels and their sequence are right, so only careful reading of the direction separates it from the answer."}},{"q":"What is learned helplessness, according to Seligman?","concept":"Learned helplessness was described by Martin E. P. Seligman and Steven F. Maier (1967) in experiments with dogs. In a triadic design, one group received electric shocks they could stop by pressing a panel, a yoked group received identical shocks they could not control, and a third group received no shock. Later all dogs were placed in a shuttle box where they could escape shock simply by jumping a low barrier. Dogs from the escapable and no-shock groups learned to escape quickly; most dogs from the inescapable group lay down and passively accepted the shocks. The key factor was not the shock but its uncontrollability: the animals had learned that their responses made no difference to outcomes. Seligman described three deficits: motivational (no attempt to escape), cognitive (difficulty learning that responses now work) and emotional (passivity and apparent distress). Donald Hiroto and Seligman (1975) produced similar effects in humans with uncontrollable noise and unsolvable problems. Lyn Abramson, Seligman and John Teasdale (1978) reformulated the theory in attributional terms: helplessness and depression follow when people explain bad events as internal, stable and global. This fed into the hopelessness theory of depression and Seligman's later work on explanatory style and learned optimism. Neighbouring concepts used as distractors are escape learning (ending an aversive stimulus), avoidance learning (preventing it) and pain-elicited aggression. Maier and Seligman (2016) later revised the account, arguing that passivity is the default response and that what is learned is control. Option a is right because learned helplessness is the acceptance of avoidable punishment after repeated exposure to unavoidable punishment.","traps":{"b":"Learning to escape punishment through trial and error is escape learning, the kind of instrumental learning Edward Thorndike (1898) observed when cats tried responses until one opened the puzzle box. In Seligman's study, dogs given escapable shock did exactly this. Helpless dogs failed to do it, which is the whole point. It tempts because the shuttle-box procedure involves escape, so the vocabulary overlaps with the correct answer.","c":"Learning to avoid punishment through reinforcement is avoidance learning, in which a warning signal allows the animal to act before the aversive event, and the response is maintained by negative reinforcement. O. Hobart Mowrer (1947) explained it with his two-factor theory. Helpless animals show the opposite: they fail to act even when avoidance is possible. It tempts because the same shuttle-box apparatus was used in avoidance research.","d":"Becoming aggressive in response to punishment is pain-elicited aggression, studied by Roger Ulrich and Nathan Azrin (1962), who found that rats given shocks attacked each other. It also relates to the frustration-aggression hypothesis of John Dollard and colleagues (1939). Learned helplessness produces passivity, not active attack. It tempts because both concern reactions to aversive stimulation, and both have been linked to clinical problems."}},{"q":"At which level of moral reasoning do people act under external controls and obey rules to avoid punishment or reap rewards?","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg (1958) extended Jean Piaget's (1932) work on moral judgement by presenting boys with moral dilemmas, the best known being the Heinz dilemma, in which a man must decide whether to steal an overpriced drug to save his dying wife. Kohlberg scored the reasoning behind the answer, not the yes or no decision, and proposed three levels with two stages each. Level I, preconventional morality (typical of children aged about 4 to 10): people act under external controls, obeying rules to avoid punishment or gain rewards. Stage 1 is orientation toward punishment and obedience; Stage 2 is instrumental purpose and exchange, as in you scratch my back and I scratch yours. Level II, conventional morality (from about age 10, often lasting into adulthood): people have internalised the standards of authority figures. Stage 3 is maintaining mutual relations and the approval of others; Stage 4 is social concern and conscience, or law and order. Level III, postconventional morality (adolescence onward, if ever): people judge by abstract principles. Stage 5 is morality of contract, individual rights and democratically accepted law; Stage 6 is morality of universal ethical principles. Carol Gilligan (1982) criticised the theory for neglecting an ethic of care, and later research found Stage 6 very rare. Variants describe a motive and ask for its level or stage; external rewards and punishments always point to Level I. Option a is right because obeying rules under external control to avoid punishment or gain reward defines Level I, preconventional morality.","traps":{"b":"Level II, conventional morality, describes people who have internalised the standards of authority figures. They follow rules because they want approval (Stage 3) or because doing one's duty and maintaining social order matter (Stage 4), not merely to avoid punishment. It tempts because conventional reasoners also obey rules, so the word 'obey' in the stem can mislead a candidate who ignores the phrase 'external controls'.","c":"Level III, postconventional morality, is reasoning by self-chosen principles: social contract and individual rights (Stage 5) and universal ethical principles such as justice and human dignity (Stage 6). A postconventional person may break a law that violates these principles, the opposite of obeying for reward or punishment. It tempts only because it is a genuine Kohlberg level and sounds the most advanced.","d":"None of the above would be correct only if no option described Kohlberg's level for externally controlled, punishment-avoiding and reward-seeking morality. Kohlberg named that level explicitly as Level I, preconventional morality, so a correct option is present. It tempts candidates who confuse Kohlberg with Piaget's (1932) heteronomous morality and therefore doubt that any listed Kohlberg level fits."}},{"q":"At which stage of Level II of Kohlberg’s moral development are people concerned with doing their duty, showing respect for higher authority, and maintaining the social order?","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg built his theory of moral development from his 1958 doctoral dissertation, extending the two-stage account in The Moral Judgment of the Child by Jean Piaget (1932). He gave boys moral dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma (should a man steal an overpriced drug to save his dying wife?) and scored the reasoning, not the yes or no answer. He described three levels, each with two stages, in an invariant sequence. Level I, preconventional: Stage 1, orientation toward punishment and obedience; Stage 2, instrumental purpose and exchange. Level II, conventional, where people have internalised the standards of authority figures and society: Stage 3, maintaining mutual relations, approval of others and the golden rule, often called the good boy, good girl orientation; Stage 4, social concern and conscience, also called the law-and-order orientation, in which the person values doing one's duty, respecting higher authority and maintaining the social order, because laws hold society together. Level III, postconventional: Stage 5, morality of contract, individual rights and democratically accepted law; Stage 6, morality of universal ethical principles. Most adolescents and adults reason at Stages 3 and 4; Stage 6 is rare. The option labels here follow the Human Development textbook of Diane E. Papalia and colleagues. Carol Gilligan (1982) criticised the scheme as biased toward a male ethic of justice and proposed an ethic of care. Option b is right because doing one's duty, respecting authority and maintaining social order define Stage 4, social concern and conscience, the second stage of Level II.","traps":{"a":"Maintaining mutual relations, approval of others and the golden rule describe Stage 3, the first stage of Level II, often called the good boy, good girl orientation. People here want to be seen as good by those close to them and judge acts by intentions and loyalty. It tempts because it is also conventional (Level II), so a candidate who knows the level but not the stage picks it.","c":"Morality of contract, individual rights and democratically accepted law is Stage 5, the first stage of Level III (postconventional). People see laws as social agreements that can be changed democratically when they fail to protect rights and welfare. It tempts because it also mentions law, but Stage 4 obeys law to keep order, while Stage 5 treats law as a changeable contract serving people.","d":"Morality of universal ethical principles is Stage 6, the highest stage of Level III (postconventional). The person follows self-chosen principles of justice, equality and human dignity, even against the law. Kohlberg found it rare and later dropped it from his standard scoring. It tempts because 'conscience' in the correct option sounds like inner principle, which candidates link to the highest stage."}},{"q":"How does Vygotsky's theory differ from Piaget's theory of cognitive development?","concept":"Jean Piaget and Lev Vygotsky are the two classic theorists of cognitive development. Piaget, whose The Origins of Intelligence in Children first appeared in 1936, saw the child as a lone scientist who builds knowledge by acting on the physical world. Through assimilation, accommodation and equilibration, children pass through four stages in a fixed order: sensorimotor (birth to about 2), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 onward). The stages describe general cognitive structures he believed appear in children everywhere. Vygotsky, a Russian psychologist who died in 1934, proposed a sociocultural theory, best known in English through Mind in Society (1978). He argued that higher mental functions begin on the social plane, in interaction with parents, teachers and more skilled peers, and are then internalised. What children learn, and the tools they think with, such as language, counting systems and literacy, are shaped by their culture, so many cognitive skills are culture-specific rather than universal. His key ideas include the zone of proximal development, scaffolding and private speech. On private speech they disagreed directly: Piaget saw it as egocentric speech that fades, while Vygotsky saw it as self-guiding speech that turns into inner speech. Both were interactionists: Piaget stressed maturation plus experience, and Vygotsky wove a natural (biological) line of development with a cultural line. Neither held a strictly predetermined or solely environmental view. Option c is right because Vygotsky held that skills learned through interaction with more competent people are often culture-specific, while Piaget proposed universal cognitive structures.","traps":{"a":"This option reverses the theorists. The claim that all children pass through the same stages in the same order belongs to Jean Piaget, with his four universal stages from sensorimotor to formal operational. Lev Vygotsky proposed no fixed universal stage sequence and stressed that development varies with culture and social interaction. It tempts because candidates remember that one theorist proposed universal stages but attach the claim to the wrong name.","b":"Lev Vygotsky did not think development was solely environmental. He described a natural, biological line of development interwoven with a cultural line, and saw the child as an active participant in social exchanges. Jean Piaget also combined maturation with experience. So this option misstates Vygotsky and blurs the real contrast. It tempts because Vygotsky is labelled sociocultural, which candidates overextend to mean environment only.","d":"Neither theorist saw development as purely predetermined. Lev Vygotsky saw it as shaped by social interaction and cultural tools, the opposite of a preset course. Jean Piaget's fixed stage order is the closer fit to 'predetermined', though he too gave experience a central role. The option therefore inverts the two. It tempts because both theorists discussed maturation and experience, so the terms sound plausible for either."}},{"q":"What is the zone of proximal development (ZPD)?","concept":"The zone of proximal development (ZPD) is the central idea in the sociocultural theory of Lev Vygotsky (1978). He defined it as the distance between a child's actual developmental level, shown by what the child can do alone, and the level of potential development, shown by what the child can do under adult guidance or with more capable peers. Tasks below the zone are already mastered and need no help; tasks above it are too hard even with help; tasks inside it are too hard to manage alone but achievable with support. Vygotsky argued that instruction should target this zone, because learning that runs slightly ahead of development pulls development forward: what a child can do with help today, the child can do alone tomorrow. He also noted that two children with the same test score may have zones of different width, so the zone reveals learning potential that static tests miss. Related terms: scaffolding, introduced by David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976), means adjusting help to the learner's level and withdrawing it as competence grows; the helper is often called the more knowledgeable other; guided participation, from Barbara Rogoff (1990), extends the idea to everyday shared activity. In assessment the ZPD underpins dynamic assessment, such as the test-teach-retest approach of Reuven Feuerstein (1979). Contrast Jean Piaget (1936), who stressed readiness and the child's own discovery. Option b is right because the ZPD is the range of tasks too complex to master alone but achievable with guidance and encouragement from a more skilful partner.","traps":{"a":"Tasks simple enough to need no help lie below the ZPD, at the child's actual developmental level, the zone of independent performance. These skills are already mastered, so teaching aimed here produces no new development. The wording 'too simple to be mastered alone' is also self-contradictory. It tempts because it mentions doing tasks alone, which is part of the ZPD definition, and a hurried reader may miss 'simple'.","c":"Tasks too complex even with guidance lie above the ZPD, beyond the child's level of potential development. Help at this point cannot produce learning, because the child lacks the prerequisite skills to use it; frustration follows rather than progress. It tempts because it shares the phrase 'too complex to be mastered' and mentions a more skilful partner, so only the word 'even' separates it from the correct definition.","d":"Learning through experience rather than instruction is closer to the view of Jean Piaget, who saw children as independent explorers constructing knowledge through their own activity, and to discovery learning in general. Vygotsky's ZPD is defined by assisted performance with a more capable partner, the opposite emphasis. It tempts because both theorists are constructivists and the option reads like a harmless general statement about learning."}},{"q":"What is the first grasp that infants develop?","concept":"Fine motor development in infancy is mainly the story of reaching and grasping, and it follows the proximodistal trend: control spreads from the trunk and shoulder outward to the hands and fingers, and within the hand from the little-finger (ulnar) side to the thumb (radial) side. Newborns show the palmar grasp reflex, an automatic closing of the fingers when the palm is stroked; it is a primitive reflex, not a voluntary grasp, and fades over the first few months. When true reaching appears around 3 to 4 months, the first voluntary grip is the ulnar grasp: a clumsy, claw-like movement in which the fingers close against the palm, with the object held on the little-finger side and the thumb hardly used. Soon infants adjust their grip to an object's size and shape and begin passing objects from hand to hand. Grasping then shifts toward the thumb side, through palmar, radial-palmar and radial-digital grips, until around the end of the first year the pincer grasp appears: thumb and index finger meet to pick up tiny objects such as a raisin. Henry M. Halverson (1931) mapped this sequence from film records of infants, and child development textbooks such as Laura E. Berk's summarise it. Variants ask which grasp comes last (pincer), which is a reflex (the palmar grasp reflex), or which principle explains the order (proximodistal). Option c is right because the ulnar grasp is the first voluntary grasp infants develop, appearing once reaching begins and well before the pincer grasp.","traps":{"a":"The palmar grasp first appears as a newborn reflex: the fingers close automatically around anything that touches the palm. As a reflex it is not counted as a developed, voluntary grasp, and the voluntary whole-hand palmar grip comes after the ulnar grasp. It tempts because the palmar grasp reflex is present at birth, so a candidate thinking of reflexes may call it the first grasp.","b":"The pincer grasp is the precise grip in which the tip of the thumb meets the tip of the index finger, used to pick up small objects such as a raisin or a crumb. It appears around the end of the first year and is the last of the infant grasps, not the first. It tempts because it is the best-known grasp and the one most often named in milestone charts.","d":"The radial grasp uses the thumb side of the hand, with the thumb starting to oppose the fingers, in the second half of the first year. It comes after the ulnar grasp, because control within the hand develops from the little-finger side toward the thumb side. It tempts because ulnar and radial are paired anatomical terms, and a candidate unsure of the direction may reverse them."}},{"q":"What is the clumsy, claw-like grip that that permits little tactile exploration?","concept":"Infant grasping develops in a predictable sequence. At birth the palmar grasp reflex closes the fingers automatically around anything placed in the palm; it fades in the early months and gives way to voluntary grasping once true reaching begins around 3 to 4 months. The first voluntary grip is the ulnar grasp, named after the ulna, the forearm bone on the little-finger side. It is a clumsy, claw-like motion in which the fingers close against the palm and the thumb plays little part. Because the object is clamped against the palm rather than held at the fingertips, the infant can do little tactile exploration of its texture or shape; the grip mainly serves to hold objects and bring them to the mouth. Over the next months control shifts toward the thumb (radial) side, through radial-palmar and radial-digital grips, as the thumb begins to oppose the fingers. Around 4 to 5 months infants also transfer objects between hands, and by the end of the first year they achieve the pincer grasp, using thumb and index finger to pick up tiny objects with fine control and to explore them in detail. The order reflects the proximodistal principle. The dynamic systems theory of Esther Thelen (1995) adds that each new motor skill is assembled by the infant from maturation, body growth, motivation and practice, rather than simply unfolding. Option b is right because the ulnar grasp is the clumsy, claw-like grip, with fingers closing against the palm, that permits little tactile exploration.","traps":{"a":"The pincer grasp is the mature precision grip in which the thumb and index finger meet, appearing around the end of the first year. It allows fine manipulation and detailed exploration of small objects, the opposite of a clumsy grip that permits little exploration. It tempts because 'pincer' and 'claw' both suggest a gripping animal limb, and pincer is the best-known grasp term.","c":"The radial grasp uses the thumb side of the hand, with the thumb beginning to oppose the fingers, and emerges after the ulnar grasp in the second half of the first year. It is more controlled than the claw-like ulnar grip and allows more handling of objects. It tempts because ulnar and radial are paired anatomical terms, and candidates unsure which side comes first may swap them.","d":"Digital grasps use the fingertips rather than the palm. The radial-digital grasp appears late in the first year, and the digital pronate grasp is a toddler's way of holding a crayon. Both give more fingertip control than the ulnar grasp, not less. It tempts because 'digital' can sound like crude clawing with the fingers, but fingertip grips are actually the more refined ones."}},{"q":"What is the language-making capacity (LMC) proposed by Dan Slobin?","concept":"How children acquire grammar so quickly is explained by competing theories. B. F. Skinner (1957), in Verbal Behavior, treated language as behaviour learned through reinforcement and imitation. Noam Chomsky (1959) attacked that view and argued that children are born with a language acquisition device (LAD), an inborn linguistic processor activated by verbal input, containing universal grammar, knowledge of rules common to all languages. Hearing speech simply tunes that built-in grammar, which explains why children produce forms they never heard, such as the overregularisation 'goed'. Dan Slobin offered a softer nativism. From research on children learning very different languages, he proposed operating principles (1973) and later the language-making capacity (LMC), set out in his 1985 cross-linguistic volumes. The LMC is a set of inborn cognitive and perceptual abilities that are highly specialised for language learning: strategies for analysing speech, such as paying attention to the ends of words, noticing word order and avoiding exceptions. Unlike the LAD, the LMC contains no ready-made grammatical rules; children use these strategies to work out the grammar of whatever language they hear. Neighbouring ideas for variant questions: the language acquisition support system of Jerome Bruner (1983), the social scaffolding caregivers provide; the critical period hypothesis of Eric Lenneberg (1967); and interactionist views that combine biology with input such as child-directed speech. Option c is right because Slobin defined the language-making capacity as a set of cognitive and perceptual abilities that are highly specialised for language learning, not as built-in grammatical knowledge.","traps":{"a":"An inborn linguistic processor activated by verbal input is the textbook definition of the language acquisition device (LAD) of Noam Chomsky, who argued against B. F. Skinner (1957) that grammar is too complex to learn by reinforcement alone. Slobin's capacity is also inborn, but it is a set of analysing strategies, not a dedicated grammar processor. It tempts because both ideas are nativist and both are described as inborn.","b":"Knowledge of rules common to all languages is universal grammar, the content Noam Chomsky placed inside the language acquisition device. It claims children are born knowing the general principles of grammar and need input only to set language-specific parameters. Slobin rejected built-in rules and proposed general operating principles for discovering them. It tempts because Slobin also studied universals through cross-linguistic research.","d":"'All of the above' would require Slobin's capacity to be Chomsky's inborn processor and universal grammar as well as specialised abilities. Options a and b describe Chomsky's language acquisition device and universal grammar, which Slobin deliberately softened into learning strategies rather than innate rules. It tempts because all three options sound nativist and Slobin is often taught as a modified LAD theorist."}},{"q":"What is the receptive-expressive gap ?","concept":"Language has two sides. Receptive language is comprehension: understanding words, sentences and gestures. Expressive (productive) language is producing them in speech, sign or writing. In development comprehension comes first, and the lag between the two is the receptive-expressive gap. Infants respond to their own name by about 6 months and understand some familiar words before their first birthday, but usually say their first words only around 12 months. Helen Benedict (1979) followed children through the one-word period and found they understood about 50 words roughly five months before they could say 50. Expressive vocabulary then accelerates in the vocabulary spurt around 18 months, followed by two-word telegraphic speech, described by Roger Brown (1973). The gap never fully closes: adults also recognise far more words than they use. One reason is that understanding needs only recognition of a stored word, while speaking requires recall plus motor planning of the vocal tract. The concept has clinical uses. Receptive vocabulary is measured by pointing tasks such as the Peabody Picture Vocabulary Test. A late talker who understands well differs from a child whose comprehension is also delayed; DSM-5 (2013) groups both under language disorder, replacing the older expressive and mixed receptive-expressive types. In adults, expressive aphasia, described by Paul Broca (1861), gives halting speech with fair comprehension, while receptive aphasia, described by Carl Wernicke (1874), gives fluent but empty speech with poor comprehension. Option a is right because the receptive-expressive gap is the difference between what infants understand and what they can produce in language.","traps":{"b":"General cognitive abilities have no standard receptive versus expressive split; the term refers specifically to language. Receptive and expressive are labels for understanding and producing words, not for perception versus reasoning or memory. It tempts because comprehension is itself a cognitive process, and 'receptive' can sound like taking in any information, so the broader wording seems a reasonable paraphrase.","c":"Motor skills are described as gross and fine, not as receptive and expressive. Speech production does need motor control of the lips, tongue and breath, which is one reason expression lags behind comprehension, but the gap itself is defined by language, not by motor development. It tempts because the motor demands of speaking are part of the explanation for the gap.","d":"Social skills are not split into receptive and expressive in this concept. The gap concerns language comprehension versus language production. Social-communicative milestones such as social smiling, joint attention and pointing are related but separate. It tempts because early language develops inside social interaction, and clinicians do speak of social communication, as in the DSM-5 diagnosis of social (pragmatic) communication disorder."}},{"q":"What are the four skill modules of DBT?","concept":"Dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha M. Linehan for chronically suicidal women who met criteria for borderline personality disorder, and set out in her treatment and skills training manuals (1993). It rests on a biosocial theory: an inborn emotional vulnerability (high sensitivity, intense reactions, slow return to baseline) meets an invalidating environment that dismisses or punishes emotional expression, producing pervasive emotion dysregulation. 'Dialectical' means balancing opposites, above all acceptance (drawn from Zen practice) and change (drawn from behaviour therapy). Standard DBT has four modes: individual therapy, a weekly skills group, telephone coaching between sessions, and a consultation team for therapists. The skills group teaches four modules. Two are acceptance skills: core mindfulness, including 'wise mind', the blend of emotion mind and reasonable mind; and distress tolerance, which teaches surviving crises without making them worse, plus radical acceptance. Two are change skills: emotion regulation, which teaches naming emotions, reducing vulnerability and acting opposite to an unhelpful urge; and interpersonal effectiveness, which teaches asking for what one needs and saying no while keeping relationships and self-respect, using acronyms such as DEAR MAN. DBT is now also used for self-harm, substance use and eating disorders. Neighbouring therapies supply the distractors: cognitive restructuring from the cognitive therapy of Aaron T. Beck (1976), relaxation training from Edmund Jacobson (1929), and acceptance and commitment therapy from Steven C. Hayes (1999), which has six core processes rather than four modules. Option b is right because DBT's four skill modules are mindfulness, interpersonal effectiveness, emotion regulation and distress tolerance.","traps":{"a":"This list keeps mindfulness and interpersonal effectiveness but swaps emotion regulation and distress tolerance for cognitive restructuring and relaxation. Cognitive restructuring, identifying and challenging distorted thoughts, is the core of Aaron T. Beck's cognitive therapy, and relaxation training comes from Edmund Jacobson's progressive muscle relaxation. DBT uses behavioural methods but does not name these as modules. It tempts because two of the four items are correct.","c":"This list keeps mindfulness and emotion regulation but adds cognitive restructuring and relaxation, dropping interpersonal effectiveness and distress tolerance. It reads like a generic cognitive behavioural package for anxiety: thought restructuring from Aaron T. Beck's cognitive therapy plus relaxation exercises. DBT is cognitive behavioural in origin, which is why the list sounds believable. It tempts because two items are genuine DBT modules and the others are common techniques.","d":"This list has three genuine DBT modules, interpersonal effectiveness, emotion regulation and distress tolerance, but replaces mindfulness with cognitive restructuring. Mindfulness is the core DBT module, taught first and revisited between the others, because the other skills depend on observing and describing experience without judgement. It tempts because it is three-quarters right, making it the closest distractor for a candidate who recalls only the change skills."}},{"q":"Which therapeutic technique involves asking a person to play the role of an exhibitionist even if they claim to suffer from severe inhibitions and excessive timidity?","concept":"Gestalt therapy was developed by Fritz Perls with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman; its founding text, Gestalt Therapy, appeared in 1951. It is an existential, experiential therapy centred on the here and now: the client is helped to become aware of what they are doing, feeling and avoiding, rather than to analyse why. Unfinished business (unexpressed feelings from the past) and disowned parts of the self are brought into present awareness through experiments rather than interpretation. Gerald Corey's textbook Theory and Practice of Counseling and Psychotherapy lists the standard exercises. The empty chair or internal dialogue sets up a conversation between conflicting parts, such as the top dog and the underdog. The reversal exercise rests on the idea that symptoms often reverse hidden impulses, so the client acts out the opposite of their usual style; Corey's own example is asking a person who claims severe inhibitions and excessive timidity to play an exhibitionist. The rehearsal exercise voices aloud the silent rehearsing people do before acting. The exaggeration exercise asks a client to amplify a gesture, posture or phrase, such as trembling hands or a clenched fist, until the feeling behind it becomes clear. Staying with the feeling asks the client to remain with an unpleasant emotion instead of escaping it. Future projection, by contrast, comes from the psychodrama of Jacob L. Moreno (1946). Option a is right because asking a timid, inhibited person to play an exhibitionist means acting out the opposite of their usual style, which is the reversal exercise, and Corey uses this exact example for it.","traps":{"c":"The rehearsal exercise brings into the open the silent rehearsing people do before acting, as they prepare how to come across and win approval. Clients say their rehearsed thoughts aloud and notice how much energy goes into playing a role. It involves no switch to an extreme or opposite role. It tempts because both exercises involve acting out roles, and 'rehearsal' sounds like practising a new behaviour.","d":"The future projection technique comes from psychodrama, developed by Jacob L. Moreno, rather than from the core Gestalt exercises. The client acts out a feared or hoped-for future event, such as a job interview, in the here and now, to clarify goals and rehearse responses. It does not involve playing a trait opposite to one's own. It tempts because it is also an enactment technique used in experiential therapies.","b":"The exaggeration exercise asks a client to amplify a gesture, posture or phrase they already show, such as trembling legs or a tight smile, until the feeling behind it becomes clear. It intensifies existing behaviour; it does not switch to the opposite. A timid person playing an exhibitionist is acting against type, which is reversal. It tempts because playing an exhibitionist sounds like an over-the-top, exaggerated performance."}},{"q":"Which type of therapeutic technique in REBT involves using imagery?","concept":"Rational emotive behaviour therapy (REBT) was founded by Albert Ellis, who began practising it as rational therapy in 1955 and adopted the current name in 1993. Its core is the ABC model: an Activating event (A) does not directly cause the emotional and behavioural Consequence (C); the Belief (B) about the event does. Irrational beliefs include rigid demands ('musts' and 'shoulds'), awfulising, low frustration tolerance and global rating of self or others. Therapy adds D, Disputing those beliefs, and E, a new Effective philosophy. Ellis held that thinking, feeling and acting interact, so REBT is multimodal, and Gerald Corey's textbook groups its techniques into three families. Cognitive methods include disputing irrational beliefs, cognitive homework, changing one's language, and psychoeducational methods such as books and recordings. Emotive techniques work through feeling and vivid experience: rational emotive imagery, humour, role playing and shame-attacking exercises. Behavioural methods include in vivo desensitisation, reinforcement and penalties, and skill training. In rational emotive imagery, developed by Maxie C. Maultsby Jr. and adopted by Ellis, clients vividly imagine one of the worst things that could happen, let themselves feel the unhealthy emotion (such as panic), then change it into a healthy negative emotion (such as concern) by changing their belief. It counts as emotive because it works by arousing and transforming feeling, not by verbal argument. Do not confuse it with the imagery work in the cognitive therapy of Aaron T. Beck (1976) or with systematic desensitisation. Option b is right because, in REBT, imagery-based methods such as rational emotive imagery belong to the emotive techniques.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive methods in REBT work through reason and argument: disputing irrational beliefs (asking where the evidence is that you must succeed), cognitive homework such as ABC worksheets, changing self-talk, and reading. They target beliefs directly through verbal challenge. It tempts because imagery is a mental activity and, in other approaches such as the cognitive therapy of Aaron T. Beck (1976), imagery work is often classed as cognitive.","c":"Behavioural techniques in REBT are action-based: in vivo desensitisation, staying in feared situations, operant reinforcement and penalties for doing or skipping homework, and skill training such as assertiveness. They change beliefs by having clients act against them in real life. It tempts because rational emotive imagery is practised as homework and resembles the imaginal exposure used in systematic desensitisation by Joseph Wolpe (1958).","d":"Psychoeducational methods teach clients the REBT model through books, recordings, lectures and workshops, so they understand the ABC theory and can apply it themselves. In Gerald Corey's classification they sit within the cognitive methods, not as a separate imagery-based family. It tempts because it sounds like a distinct category and is often listed alongside the others, so candidates may treat it as the catch-all."}}],"pep-mock-2":[{"q":"How many General Principles are included in the APA's Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct?","concept":"The American Psychological Association (APA) adopted its first ethics code in 1953; the current Ethical Principles of Psychologists and Code of Conduct was adopted in 2002 and amended in 2010 and 2016. It has four parts: an Introduction and Applicability section, a Preamble, the General Principles, and the Ethical Standards. The General Principles are five aspirational ideals, lettered A to E: A, Beneficence and Nonmaleficence (benefit people, do no harm); B, Fidelity and Responsibility (trust and professional obligations to society); C, Integrity (accuracy, honesty, truthfulness); D, Justice (fair access to and equal quality of services); and E, Respect for People's Rights and Dignity (privacy, confidentiality, self-determination, cultural differences). They are not enforceable rules and do not by themselves form the basis for sanctions. The enforceable part is the ten Ethical Standards, for example Standard 2 Competence, 3 Human Relations, 4 Privacy and Confidentiality, 9 Assessment and 10 Therapy. Examiners build variants from neighbouring frameworks. The Belmont Report (1979) has three principles: respect for persons, beneficence and justice. Tom Beauchamp and James Childress (1979) set out four principles of biomedical ethics: autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence and justice. Karen Kitchener (1984) proposed five moral principles for psychologists: autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, justice and fidelity. So remember: Belmont three, Beauchamp and Childress four, APA five principles plus ten standards. In India, clinical psychologists are registered by the Rehabilitation Council of India under the RCI Act 1992. Option c is right because the APA code contains exactly five General Principles, lettered A to E.","traps":{"a":"One would mean a single overarching rule, such as 'first, do no harm' (primum non nocere), which people often treat as the whole of medical ethics. The APA code instead separates its ideals into five lettered principles, and even nonmaleficence is paired with beneficence inside Principle A. It tempts candidates who remember that the code has a single Preamble, or who think of ethics as one master principle.","b":"Three is the number of principles in the Belmont Report (1979), the United States research ethics document: respect for persons, beneficence and justice. Those three underlie informed consent, risk-benefit assessment and fair selection of research participants, but they are not the APA's General Principles. It tempts because Belmont is taught in the same research ethics lectures, and its principles overlap in name with APA Principles A and D.","d":"Seven matches no part of the APA code. The code has five General Principles and ten enforceable Ethical Standards, so seven is neither count. It tempts candidates who blur the principles with the standards, or who mix Karen Kitchener's (1984) five moral principles with other lists. Remember the pattern: Belmont three, Beauchamp and Childress four, APA five principles and ten standards."}},{"q":"According to the United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child, what are children capable of?","concept":"The United Nations Convention on the Rights of the Child (UNCRC) was adopted by the UN General Assembly on 20 November 1989, and India ratified it in 1992. It defines a child as every human being below 18 years. Its rights are often grouped as survival, development, protection and participation, and four articles act as general principles: non-discrimination (Article 2), best interests of the child (Article 3), the right to life, survival and development (Article 6), and respect for the views of the child (Article 12). Article 12 is the one examined here. It says a child who is capable of forming his or her own views has the right to express them freely in all matters affecting the child, and those views must be given due weight according to age and maturity. In clinical practice this is the basis of assent. Consent is a legal permission that requires capacity and, for minors, normally comes from a parent or guardian. Assent is the child's own agreement, and dissent is the child's objection, which the clinician must hear and weigh even when the guardian has consented. A neighbouring idea is Gillick competence, from the English case Gillick v West Norfolk (1985), under which a minor with sufficient understanding may consent. In India, the Juvenile Justice Act 2015 and the POCSO Act 2012 use the same under-18 definition. Option c is right because the Convention treats children as capable of forming and voicing views, that is, giving assent or dissent, not as legally competent decision makers.","traps":{"a":"Understanding complex legal terminology is a test of legal literacy, which the Convention never assumes. Article 12 asks only that the child can form a view, and Article 13 protects freedom of expression in any form the child can use. Explaining matters in child-friendly language is the adult's duty, not a capacity expected of the child. It tempts because the Convention is a legal treaty, so a legal-sounding option looks relevant.","b":"Providing consent for psychotherapy is a legal act that requires capacity, and for children this authority usually rests with a parent or guardian. Some systems allow mature minors to consent, as in Gillick competence (1985), but that is national case law, not what the Convention says. It tempts because the Convention stresses children's voices, and candidates slide from 'has a voice' to 'can consent'. The correct concept is assent.","d":"Making important life decisions overstates the child's role. The Convention gives children a right to be heard, with due weight according to age and maturity, while best interests (Article 3) and parental guidance in line with evolving capacities (Article 5) keep major decisions with adults. It tempts because participation rights sound like autonomy, but participation means being consulted and taken seriously, not having the final say."}},{"q":"Under what circumstances therapists may accept gifts?","concept":"Gifts from clients are a classic boundary question. Thomas Gutheil and Glen Gabbard (1993) separated boundary crossings, departures from strict therapeutic distance that can be harmless or even helpful, from boundary violations, crossings that exploit or harm the client. A small gift is usually a crossing. The ethical question is not 'gifts, yes or no' but 'what does accepting or refusing mean for this client and this therapy'. The American Counseling Association Code of Ethics (2014, Standard A.10.f) asks counsellors to consider the therapeutic relationship, the monetary value of the gift, the client's motivation for giving it, and the counsellor's motivation for accepting or declining it. It also notes that in some cultures small gifts are a token of respect and gratitude. The APA code (2002, amended 2016) has no specific gift rule, but its standards on multiple relationships (3.05) and exploitative relationships (3.08) apply. So a gift may be accepted when it is small, freely offered, and when accepting it serves the client's welfare, for example when refusal would feel like rejection to a client working on low self-worth, and acceptance affirms that what they offer has value. Large gifts, repeated gifts, gifts that create obligation, and gifts linked to fees are red flags. Related concepts are dual relationships, barter (APA Standard 6.05, allowed only when not clinically contraindicated and not exploitative) and countertransference, since the therapist's wish to receive can bias judgment. Option c is right because it gives a clinical, client-centred reason for acceptance, that the gift will help promote the client's self-acceptance.","traps":{"a":"Without any specific reason means accepting gifts casually, as a social habit. Every code that addresses gifts asks the therapist to examine the value of the gift, the client's motive and the therapist's own motive before accepting. Unreflective acceptance risks sliding from a boundary crossing into a boundary violation, in the terms of Thomas Gutheil and Glen Gabbard (1993). It tempts because most small gifts are harmless and refusing them can seem cold.","b":"Only on festive occasions turns a clinical decision into a calendar rule. Festival sweets at Diwali or Eid may well be acceptable in India, since codes recognise the cultural meaning of gifts, but the occasion is not what justifies acceptance; the clinical meaning for the client is. The word 'only' also makes it a rigid rule. It tempts because cultural gift-giving is familiar and feels like the safest exception.","d":"In exchange for reducing session fees makes the gift a payment, which is barter. APA Standard 6.05 allows barter only when it is not clinically contraindicated and not exploitative, and a gift traded for a fee cut creates a business relationship and obligations on both sides. That is a multiple relationship and a conflict of interest, not a therapeutic gift. It tempts because it sounds like a fair, practical arrangement."}},{"q":"What is the impact of the Criminal Law Amendment Act on the age of statutory rape in India?","concept":"Statutory rape means sexual intercourse with a person below the legal age of consent, where the minor's agreement is legally irrelevant. In India, rape was defined in Section 375 of the Indian Penal Code 1860, and before 2013 the age of consent there was 16 years. After the December 2012 Delhi gang rape, the Justice J. S. Verma Committee (2013) reviewed sexual offence law, and Parliament passed the Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2013. This Act widened the definition of rape, added offences such as stalking, voyeurism and acid attack, and raised the age of consent from 16 to 18 years. That brought the Indian Penal Code in line with the Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act 2012, which already treated every person below 18 as a child and all sexual acts with them as offences. Neighbours an examiner may use: the marital rape exception, which the Supreme Court read down in Independent Thought v Union of India (2017) so that sex with a wife below 18 is rape; the Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2018, which raised punishments for rape of girls below 12 and below 16; and the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023, which replaced the Indian Penal Code and keeps the age at 18. For clinical psychologists, POCSO also imposes mandatory reporting, so a therapist who learns of sexual activity involving a minor has a legal duty to report it. Option b is right because the 2013 amendment increased the age of consent, and therefore the age for statutory rape, from 16 to 18 years.","traps":{"a":"It lowered the age from 18 to 16 reverses the real direction. The age of consent in the Indian Penal Code was 16 before 2013, and the amendment raised it to 18. It tempts because public debate since then, including calls for a 'Romeo and Juliet' exception for consensual adolescent relationships, has discussed lowering the age, and a candidate may mix up the debate with the law itself.","c":"It criminalized all consensual sex among young people overstates the law. The amendment fixed an age threshold of 18; it did not ban sexual activity between adults of any age. A real criticism is that, together with POCSO 2012, the law makes consensual relationships between two 17-year-olds an offence, which courts have commented on. That criticism makes this option tempting, but the Act changed only the age threshold.","d":"It did not have any impact is simply false. The 2013 amendment rewrote Section 375 of the Indian Penal Code, and one explicit change was raising the age of consent from 16 to 18. It tempts candidates who assume the age was already 18, because the POCSO Act 2012 had defined a child as anyone below 18 a year earlier, so the change can look like no change at all."}},{"q":"What does ethical competency refer to?","concept":"Ethical competency is the capacity to practise ethically, and it is treated as a cluster of abilities rather than one skill. In the competency cube model of Emil Rodolfa and colleagues (2005), ethical and legal standards and policy is one of the foundational competencies that run through every functional activity such as assessment, intervention and supervision. Most frameworks describe three linked parts. The first is knowledge: knowing the relevant codes, laws and guidelines, such as the APA Ethics Code (2002, amended 2016), the RCI Act 1992 and the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 (MHCA). The second is skill: recognising that a situation has an ethical dimension, analysing it, and resolving it, often through a step-wise decision-making model. The third is attitude and accountability: taking responsibility for one's own actions, seeking consultation, and correcting mistakes. James Rest (1986) gave the classic psychological account in his four-component model of moral behaviour: moral sensitivity (noticing the issue), moral judgment (deciding what is right), moral motivation (prioritising the ethical value) and moral character (having the persistence to act). Karen Kitchener (1984) added the distinction between intuitive and critical-evaluative levels of ethical reasoning. Knowledge without skill produces people who can quote the code but miss the dilemma; skill without responsibility produces rationalisation. Examiners also test the difference between ethical competence, a capacity, and ethical compliance, merely following rules to avoid sanction. Option d is right because ethical competency includes knowledge of codes, the ability to recognise, analyse and resolve ethical situations, and responsibility for one's own actions, so all three listed elements together define it.","traps":{"a":"Knowledge and understanding of ethics codes is the cognitive base of ethical competency, and it is genuinely part of it. On its own, though, it is incomplete: a clinician can know the APA code or RCI rules well and still fail to notice a dual relationship forming. It tempts because exam preparation stresses memorising codes, so knowledge feels like the whole of competence. In an 'all of the above' item it is only one piece.","b":"Ability to recognise, analyse and resolve ethical situations is the applied skill component, matching moral sensitivity and moral judgment in James Rest's (1986) four-component model. It is the heart of ethical decision making, but it leaves out knowledge of codes and personal accountability. It tempts because it is the longest and most complete-sounding option, which candidates often pick when they do not weigh the 'all of the above' choice.","c":"Responsibility for one's own actions is the accountability component: owning mistakes, seeking consultation and following through on decisions, close to moral character in James Rest's (1986) model. It is a real part of ethical competency but too narrow alone, since responsibility without knowledge or analysis can still produce poor decisions. It tempts because 'responsibility' is a common one-word definition of professional ethics."}},{"q":"According to Immanuel Kant's deontological approach, what are actions judged by?","concept":"Deontology is the family of ethical theories that judge an action by whether it conforms to a moral duty or rule, not by its results. Its classic form comes from Immanuel Kant in the Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals (1785). Kant argued that the only thing good without qualification is a good will, that is, acting from duty. An action has moral worth because of the principle (maxim) behind it, so lying is wrong in itself even if a lie would produce a good outcome. His test is the categorical imperative. The universal law formulation says act only on a maxim you could will to become a universal law. The humanity formulation says treat persons always as ends in themselves and never merely as means. Kant contrasted categorical imperatives with hypothetical imperatives, which are conditional ('if you want X, do Y'). The main rival is consequentialism, especially the utilitarianism of Jeremy Bentham (1789) and John Stuart Mill (1863), which judges acts by outcomes, the greatest happiness of the greatest number, and uses cost-benefit reasoning. A third family is virtue ethics, from Aristotle's Nicomachean Ethics (fourth century BCE), which asks what a person of good character would do. W. D. Ross (1930) later softened Kant with prima facie duties, such as fidelity and non-maleficence, that can conflict. In clinical ethics, informed consent and respect for autonomy are Kantian ideas, while triage is utilitarian. Option b is right because Kant judges actions by their intrinsic rightness or wrongness, that is, their conformity to duty, not by their consequences.","traps":{"a":"Their consequences is the criterion of consequentialism, the opposite of Kant's view. Utilitarians such as Jeremy Bentham (1789) and John Stuart Mill (1863) judge an action right if it produces the greatest happiness for the greatest number. Kant held that a good outcome cannot make a wrong act right. It tempts because consequentialism is the most intuitive everyday way of judging actions, and the two theories are always taught as a pair.","c":"Cost-benefit analysis is a practical method of consequentialist reasoning: add up expected benefits and harms and choose the option with the best balance. Jeremy Bentham's (1789) felicific, or hedonic, calculus is its classic form. Kant rejected this kind of calculation because it can justify using a person merely as a means. It tempts because risk-benefit weighing is common in clinical and research ethics, so it sounds like a respectable ethical standard.","d":"None of the above would be correct only if no listed option captured Kant's criterion. 'Their intrinsic goodness or badness' does capture it: Kant judged acts by their inherent rightness, meaning conformity to duty and the categorical imperative, regardless of outcome. It tempts candidates who find 'intrinsic goodness' vague, or who know that Kant focused on the good will and motive and so doubt that any option fits."}},{"q":"What is a potential consequence of poor awareness of the culture and prevalent familial practices in family therapy?","concept":"Family therapy treats the family, not the individual, as the unit of assessment and change, and every family therapy model carries assumptions about what a healthy family looks like. The structural family therapy of Salvador Minuchin (1974), for example, judges boundaries as clear, rigid or diffuse and labels very close families as enmeshed. If a therapist applies such norms without cultural awareness, normal cultural patterns get read as pathology. In an Indian joint family, adult children living with parents, elders taking decisions, a mother-in-law's authority, arranged marriage or shared finances may be normative, not signs of enmeshment, poor differentiation of self in the sense of Murray Bowen (1978), or dependency. The result is wrong assessments and misinterpretations, then goals that clash with the family's values, poor engagement and dropout. Monica McGoldrick, John Pearce and Joseph Giordano's Ethnicity and Family Therapy (1982) made this a core training issue. Derald Wing Sue, Patricia Arredondo and Roderick McDavis (1992) defined multicultural competence in three parts: awareness of one's own cultural assumptions and biases, knowledge of the client's worldview, and culturally appropriate skills. Neighbouring terms examiners use: emic (culture-specific) versus etic (universal) approaches, cultural humility, and the Cultural Formulation Interview of DSM-5 (2013). In India, Vidya Sagar at Amritsar Mental Hospital in the 1950s pioneered keeping families with patients during inpatient care, an early sign that Indian practice must work with, not against, family systems. Option a is right because poor awareness of culture and familial practices leads the therapist to misjudge normal practices as dysfunction, producing wrong assessments and misinterpretations.","traps":{"b":"Accurate understanding of the family's dynamics is what good cultural awareness produces, so it is the opposite of the consequence asked for. A therapist who knows, for instance, that elders' involvement in decisions is normal in many Indian families can separate culture from dysfunction. It tempts only through careless reading of the stem, which asks for the result of poor awareness, a negative consequence, not a goal of therapy.","c":"Effective communication with the family depends on understanding its language, hierarchy and norms of respect, for example whom to address first. Poor cultural awareness damages communication, so this is a benefit of competence, not a consequence of its absence. It tempts because communication is a core family therapy skill, emphasised by Virginia Satir in Conjoint Family Therapy (1964), and it appears in every list of therapy outcomes.","d":"Development of a strong therapeutic alliance is a positive outcome that culturally insensitive work actively threatens. Edward Bordin (1979) defined the alliance as agreement on goals and tasks plus an emotional bond, and goals clash when the therapist pathologises the family's values. It tempts because the alliance is the most studied common factor in therapy, so it sounds like a safe answer, but the stem asks for a harmful consequence."}},{"q":"Which type of psychotherapy is NOT commonly used to treat survivors of sexual assault?","concept":"Survivors of sexual assault have high rates of post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD), depression and shame, and the treatments with the strongest evidence are trauma-focused, meaning they work directly on the memory of the assault and the beliefs it created. Prolonged exposure, by Edna Foa and Barbara Rothbaum (1998), was developed with rape survivors and uses imaginal and in vivo exposure. Cognitive processing therapy, by Patricia Resick and Monica Schnicke (1992), was also first tested with rape survivors and targets 'stuck points' such as self-blame. Trauma-focused cognitive-behavioural therapy (TF-CBT), developed by Judith Cohen, Anthony Mannarino and Esther Deblinger (2006), is the standard for children and adolescents after sexual abuse. Eye movement desensitization and reprocessing (EMDR), by Francine Shapiro (1989), pairs recall of the trauma with bilateral stimulation and is recommended by the World Health Organization (2013) and NICE guidelines. Psychodynamic psychotherapy is widely used, especially for complex and long-standing effects of sexual trauma on relationships and identity, though its trial evidence is weaker. Interpersonal psychotherapy (IPT), by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman (1984), was built for depression and works on four problem areas: grief, role disputes, role transitions and interpersonal deficits. It is not trauma-focused and is not a standard treatment for assault survivors. One trial by John Markowitz and colleagues (2015) found IPT comparable to prolonged exposure for PTSD, so it may be treated as an emerging option in future. Option a is right because interpersonal psychotherapy is a depression-focused treatment that is not commonly used for sexual assault survivors, unlike TF-CBT, EMDR and psychodynamic therapy.","traps":{"b":"Psychodynamic psychotherapy explores how the assault connects with earlier relationships, unconscious conflict, shame and identity, and works these through within the therapeutic relationship. Its controlled-trial evidence for PTSD is weaker than that for trauma-focused CBT, but it is widely used with sexual trauma survivors, especially for long-term relational effects. It tempts because guidelines do not list it as first line, so candidates confuse 'not first line' with 'not commonly used'.","c":"EMDR, developed by Francine Shapiro (1989), asks the client to hold the trauma memory in mind while following bilateral stimulation such as side-to-side eye movements. It is recommended for PTSD by the World Health Organization (2013) and NICE, and is commonly used after rape and sexual abuse. It tempts because the eye movement procedure looks unusual and was once controversial, so candidates may assume it is a fringe therapy.","d":"TF-CBT, developed by Judith Cohen, Anthony Mannarino and Esther Deblinger (2006), combines psychoeducation, relaxation, affect regulation, cognitive coping, a trauma narrative and in vivo exposure, with caregiver sessions. It was designed for children and adolescents after sexual abuse and is a first-line, evidence-based treatment. It tempts only candidates who misread the NOT in the stem and choose the most familiar trauma treatment."}},{"q":"What is bilateral stimulation in EMDR?","concept":"Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing (EMDR) was developed by the psychologist Francine Shapiro, who noticed in 1987 that moving her eyes from side to side while thinking about a disturbing thought reduced its emotional charge; her first controlled study with trauma survivors appeared in 1989. Bilateral stimulation (BLS) is the signature ingredient: rhythmic, alternating left-right stimulation that the client attends to while holding a target memory in mind. The classic form is saccadic eye movement, the client's eyes following the therapist's fingers back and forth across the visual field in short sets. Alternating hand taps, tones through headphones or light bars are accepted variants. The principle is dual attention: the client stays anchored in the safe present while briefly contacting the traumatic image, its negative belief (for example 'I am helpless') and its body sensations. Shapiro explained the effect with her Adaptive Information Processing (AIP) model: trauma is stored in an unprocessed form, and BLS helps link it to adaptive memory networks. A rival working memory account says the eye movements load working memory, so the recalled image becomes less vivid and less emotional. EMDR runs in eight phases (history taking, preparation, assessment, desensitization, installation, body scan, closure, reevaluation), with distress rated on the 0 to 10 Subjective Units of Disturbance (SUD) scale. The World Health Organization (2013) recommends EMDR, along with trauma-focused CBT, for PTSD, although dismantling studies question how much the eye movements add beyond exposure. Option b is right because bilateral stimulation means the back-and-forth eye movements the client makes while focusing on a disturbing memory.","traps":{"a":"This option garbles the technique. Alternating taps on the hands or knees are an accepted variant of bilateral stimulation, but the defining feature is rhythmic left-right alternation paired with a target memory, not 'physical touch' and 'thoughts' in general. Touch has no therapeutic role of its own in EMDR. It tempts because candidates remember that taps are allowed and that the client thinks of something during each set.","c":"Medication combined with behavioural techniques describes drug treatment plus behaviour therapy for anxiety, for example an SSRI with graded exposure or with the systematic desensitization of Joseph Wolpe (1958). EMDR is a stand-alone psychotherapy and uses no drugs. It tempts because the full name of EMDR contains 'desensitization', a word borrowed from behaviour therapy, and because EMDR treats anxiety-related conditions such as PTSD.","d":"Hypnosis induces a trance of focused attention and heightened suggestibility, and 'accessing repressed memories' recalls Freud's early cathartic method and the controversial recovered-memory therapies of the 1990s. EMDR works on memories the client already has, in a fully alert state of dual attention, and does not aim to uncover hidden memories. It tempts because both hypnosis and EMDR guide attention and are used for trauma."}},{"q":"What do researchers mean by pathologization and depathologization of homosexuality?","concept":"Pathologization means defining a way of being as a disease, so it becomes something to diagnose, explain and treat. Depathologization is the reverse: formally removing that diagnostic label. Homosexuality is the classic case. Nineteenth-century sexology, such as Richard von Krafft-Ebing's Psychopathia Sexualis (1886), framed it as degeneracy; DSM-I (1952) listed it under sociopathic personality disturbance, and DSM-II (1968) among the sexual deviations. The evidence turned against this view. Evelyn Hooker (1957) gave projective tests to matched gay and heterosexual men, and expert judges could not tell the groups apart, showing no inherent maladjustment. Alfred Kinsey's reports (1948, 1953) showed that same-sex behaviour was common. After protests by gay activists, the American Psychiatric Association voted in December 1973, with the psychiatrist Robert Spitzer central to the change, to remove homosexuality from DSM-II, keeping 'sexual orientation disturbance' only for people distressed by their orientation. This survived as 'ego-dystonic homosexuality' in DSM-III (1980) and was dropped in DSM-III-R (1987). The World Health Organization removed homosexuality from ICD-10, approved in 1990. In India, the Supreme Court decriminalised consensual same-sex relations in Navtej Singh Johar v. Union of India (2018), and the Indian Psychiatric Society stated the same year that homosexuality is not a mental illness. Keep three neighbours apart: depathologization concerns diagnostic status; conversion therapy tries to change orientation and is now rejected as harmful; coming out is personal disclosure of identity. Option b is right because pathologization is classifying homosexuality as a mental disorder and depathologization is removing that classification.","traps":{"a":"'None of the above' is a catch-all distractor that is correct only when every listed choice is false. Here one listed choice states exactly what researchers mean: classifying homosexuality as a disorder, then removing that classification from DSM and ICD. It tempts candidates who find the long words 'pathologization' and 'depathologization' unfamiliar and assume the examiner is testing an obscure meaning that no option captures.","c":"Changing orientation from homosexual to heterosexual is the aim of conversion or 'reparative' therapy, which has used aversive conditioning, religious counselling and talk therapy. Professional bodies reject it as ineffective and harmful; in India the Madras High Court (2021) ordered action against it and the National Medical Commission (2022) declared it professional misconduct. It tempts because conversion therapy only made sense while homosexuality was pathologized.","d":"Coming out is the process of recognising one's own sexual orientation and disclosing it to others, described in stage models such as the homosexual identity formation model of Vivienne Cass (1979). It is a personal, developmental process, not a change in diagnostic manuals. It tempts because depathologization made coming out safer, and the two topics are discussed together in the LGBTQ+ mental health literature."}},{"q":"What are some defining features of minority stress?","concept":"Minority stress theory explains why lesbian, gay and bisexual (LGB) people show higher rates of depression, anxiety and substance use than heterosexual people. Virginia Brooks (1981) first used the term for lesbian women, and Ilan Meyer developed it, most influentially in his 1995 study of gay men and his 2003 review in Psychological Bulletin. It builds on social stress theory and on Erving Goffman's (1963) account of stigma. The core claim is that prejudice in the social environment creates extra stress, and that this excess stress, not sexual orientation itself, produces the health gap. Meyer gave minority stress three defining features: it is unique (added on top of the general stressors everyone faces), chronic (related to relatively stable underlying social and cultural structures), and socially based (stemming from social processes, institutions and structures beyond the individual, rather than from individual events or conditions). He arranged the stressors on a distal to proximal continuum. Distal stressors are objective events such as discrimination, harassment and violence; proximal stressors are internal, such as expecting rejection, concealing one's identity and internalized homophobia. Coping and community support can buffer these effects. Mark Hatzenbuehler (2009) added a psychological mediation framework linking stigma to emotion regulation and interpersonal problems. Contrast the transactional model of Richard Lazarus and Susan Folkman (1984), which locates stress in an individual's appraisal of a situation; minority stress locates the source in the social structure. Option b is right because Meyer defines minority stress as chronic and related to relatively stable underlying social and cultural structures.","traps":{"a":"This describes ordinary short-lived stress handled by individual coping, the territory of positive thinking and cognitive reframing. Minority stress is the opposite: chronic, because prejudice is built into lasting social structures, and not removable by changing one's thoughts alone. Meyer warned that treating it as a personal problem blames the victim. It tempts because coping and resilience do appear in the model, but only as buffers, not as a cure.","c":"Locating the stress in individual characteristics and personal choices is a person-centred view of stress, and it echoes the old pathologizing idea that something inside the minority person is faulty. Meyer's model says the source lies outside the individual, in stigma, prejudice and discrimination. It tempts because proximal stressors such as internalized homophobia and concealment do occur inside the person, but they are internalized forms of social stigma.","d":"This flatly negates Meyer's third defining feature, that minority stress is socially based, stemming from social processes, institutions and structures. Examiners build such options by adding a single 'not' to the textbook definition. It tempts careless readers who scan for the familiar phrase 'social processes, institutions, and structures', recognise it from their notes, and miss the negation at the start of the sentence."}},{"q":"Who is credited with coining the term \"clinical psychology\"?","concept":"Clinical psychology as a named discipline begins with Lightner Witmer (1867 to 1956). Witmer studied with James McKeen Cattell and took his PhD under Wilhelm Wundt at Leipzig in 1892, so he was trained in the experimental laboratory tradition. In March 1896, at the University of Pennsylvania, he opened the first psychological clinic, after a teacher brought him a boy, Charles Gilman, with severe spelling problems. In December 1896 he described his clinical method to the American Psychological Association, and in 1907 he founded the journal The Psychological Clinic, whose opening article, titled 'Clinical Psychology', put the term in print. For Witmer, clinical psychology meant applying scientific psychology to individual cases, mostly children with learning and behaviour difficulties, through assessment and remedial teaching. Examiners pair this with other firsts: Wundt's Leipzig laboratory (1879), Cattell's coining of 'mental test' (1890), the first practical intelligence scale by Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon (1905), and the scientist-practitioner model set at the Boulder conference (1949). The names in the other options built different things: Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis (The Interpretation of Dreams, 1900), Carl Rogers founded client-centred therapy (Client-Centered Therapy, 1951), and B. F. Skinner developed operant conditioning (The Behavior of Organisms, 1938). In India, the profession is regulated by the Rehabilitation Council of India under the RCI Act 1992; clinical psychologists must register with the RCI, which requires a recognised qualification such as the M.Phil in Clinical Psychology. Option c is right because Lightner Witmer coined the term 'clinical psychology' and founded the first psychological clinic in 1896.","traps":{"a":"Sigmund Freud (1856 to 1939) was a neurologist who founded psychoanalysis, with landmarks such as Studies on Hysteria (1895, with Josef Breuer) and The Interpretation of Dreams (1900). He shaped psychotherapy and psychiatry, but he worked within a medical framework and never named clinical psychology. It tempts because Freud is the most famous figure in clinical work, and many students assume he founded everything clinical.","b":"Carl Rogers (1902 to 1987) founded client-centred, later person-centred, therapy, built on empathy, unconditional positive regard and genuineness, and he pioneered recorded sessions and psychotherapy research. His key books appeared in 1942 and 1951, almost half a century after Witmer's clinic. It tempts because Rogers was a president of the American Psychological Association and a central figure in the growth of clinical and counselling psychology.","d":"'F. Skinner' is a slip for B. F. Skinner (1904 to 1990), the radical behaviourist who developed operant conditioning, the Skinner box and schedules of reinforcement (The Behavior of Organisms, 1938). His work later fed behaviour modification and token economies in hospitals. It tempts because applied behaviour analysis is clinically important and Skinner is a giant of twentieth-century psychology, but he did not coin the term."}},{"q":"What is countertransference in therapy?","concept":"Countertransference is the therapist's emotional reaction to the client. Sigmund Freud introduced the term in 1910 for feelings aroused in the analyst by the patient's influence on the analyst's unconscious, and he treated it as an obstacle that the analyst's own analysis should remove. It is the mirror image of transference, which Freud analysed in the Dora case (1905): the client unconsciously redirects feelings and attitudes from significant early figures, such as parents, onto the therapist, for example treating a neutral therapist as a critical father. In countertransference the direction reverses: the therapist experiences similar unconscious feelings toward the client, such as irritation, rescue fantasies, boredom, attraction or overprotectiveness, often because the client resembles someone in the therapist's own life. Later writers turned the concept from a problem into a tool. Donald Winnicott (1949) wrote on 'hate in the counter-transference', and Paula Heimann (1950) argued that the analyst's feelings are a source of information about the client's inner world. This broader 'totalistic' view counts all of the therapist's reactions, while the classical view counts only those arising from the therapist's unresolved conflicts. Managing countertransference requires self-awareness, personal therapy and supervision. Neighbouring terms that examiners swap in are resistance (the client's unconscious opposition to change), the working alliance (the collaborative bond), boundary violations (ethical breaches that unmanaged countertransference can lead to), and projective identification, described by Melanie Klein (1946). Option c is right because countertransference is when the therapist experiences similar, transference-like feelings towards the client, the reverse of the client's transference.","traps":{"a":"Fear of the unknown, low motivation and feeling overwhelmed are common client barriers to starting or staying in therapy, and in psychodynamic terms some of them appear as resistance, the client's unconscious opposition to change that Freud described. They concern the client's state, not the therapist's feelings. It tempts because it reads like a list of therapy-process problems from the same textbook chapter on the therapeutic relationship.","b":"Maintaining appropriate boundaries is an ethical and professional duty covering time, fees, touch, gifts, self-disclosure and dual relationships, set out in professional codes of conduct. It is related, because unexamined countertransference can lead to boundary crossings and violations, but it is a rule of conduct, not an emotional process. It tempts because textbooks often discuss countertransference and boundaries in the same section.","d":"This is the definition of transference: the client unconsciously transfers feelings or attitudes toward significant people in their life onto the therapist. Freud first saw it as a form of resistance, then as the central vehicle of analytic work, handled through interpretation. Countertransference runs in the opposite direction, from therapist to client. It tempts because it is a perfectly correct definition of the paired concept, so a hurried reader picks it."}},{"q":"Which biological system is implicated in obsessive-compulsive disorder?","concept":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) is defined by obsessions (intrusive, unwanted thoughts, images or urges that cause anxiety) and compulsions (repetitive acts or mental rituals done to neutralise them). The serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine, 5-HT) hypothesis is the classic biological account, and it rests on drug response. Clomipramine, a tricyclic antidepressant with strong serotonin reuptake blockade, reduced obsessions, while desipramine, a tricyclic that mainly blocks noradrenaline reuptake, did not. The selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs), such as fluoxetine, fluvoxamine and sertraline, then proved effective and became first-line drugs. Two clinical facts tie OCD to serotonin specifically: SSRIs work in OCD whether or not the patient is depressed, and they need higher doses and longer trials (often 8 to 12 weeks) than in depression. Brain imaging adds the circuit level: PET studies show overactivity in the cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical (CSTC) loop, especially the orbitofrontal cortex, anterior cingulate and caudate nucleus, and this activity falls after either SSRIs or exposure and response prevention. The other systems in the options matter, but less centrally. Glutamate is the main excitatory transmitter of the CSTC loop and a newer research target. Dopamine is relevant to OCD with tics, and low-dose antipsychotics are used to augment SSRIs in resistant cases. GABA, the main inhibitory transmitter, is the target of benzodiazepines, which ease general anxiety but not core obsessions. Examiners also link sudden childhood OCD to streptococcal infection (PANDAS, described by Susan Swedo, 1998). Option a is right because the central serotonergic (5-HT) system is the system most consistently implicated in OCD, shown by its selective response to serotonergic drugs.","traps":{"b":"The GABAergic system uses gamma-aminobutyric acid, the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter, acting on GABA-A receptors that benzodiazepines and barbiturates enhance. It is central to generalized anxiety, panic, alcohol withdrawal and sedation. Benzodiazepines may calm distress but do not reduce core obsessions and compulsions. It tempts because OCD was classed as an anxiety disorder until DSM-5 (2013) moved it to its own obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter.","c":"Glutamate is the main excitatory neurotransmitter and drives the cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical loop that is overactive in OCD. Raised glutamate in cerebrospinal fluid has been reported, and glutamate-modulating drugs such as memantine and N-acetylcysteine are being studied as add-ons. It tempts because it is a genuine, current research lead, but the established textbook system behind first-line treatment remains serotonin.","d":"Dopamine pathways underlie reward, movement and psychosis, and are the key system in schizophrenia and Tourette syndrome. In OCD, dopamine matters for the tic-related subtype and explains why low-dose antipsychotics such as risperidone or aripiprazole help when SSRIs fail. It tempts because compulsions look like habit-like behaviours of the basal ganglia, but dopamine is an augmentation target, not the primary system."}},{"q":"What are the clinical features of conversion disorder?","concept":"Conversion disorder is the modern name for what was once called hysteria: genuine loss or alteration of voluntary motor or sensory function, such as paralysis, tremor, non-epileptic seizures, blindness or numbness, not explained by neurological disease. Jean-Martin Charcot studied such patients at the Salpetriere in the 1870s and 1880s, and Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (Studies on Hysteria, 1895) popularised 'conversion' for the idea that unacceptable psychic conflict is converted into a bodily symptom. Freud separated primary gain (anxiety kept out of awareness) from secondary gain (attention, or relief from duties). The classic picture, still examined through ICD-10 (F44, dissociative (conversion) disorders) and DSM-IV, is: symptoms affecting motor or sensory function, sudden onset, a close time link to a significant psychosocial stressor, no intentional production, and sometimes la belle indifference, a surprising calm about the disability. DSM-5 (2013) added the name functional neurological symptom disorder (made the main title in DSM-5-TR, 2022), required positive evidence that the symptom is incompatible with recognised neurological disease, such as Hoover's sign in functional leg weakness, and dropped the requirements for a stressor and for ruling out feigning. Tell the neighbours apart by intention and motive: conversion symptoms are not intentional; factitious disorder symptoms are deliberately produced to occupy the sick role without external reward; malingering symptoms are deliberately produced for external reward such as compensation. Somatic symptom disorder centres on excessive worry about real symptoms. Option b is right because it lists the defining clinical triad: altered motor or sensory function, sudden onset, and development after a significant psychosocial stressor.","traps":{"a":"Normal examination and investigations are true of conversion disorder, but they are equally true of factitious disorder, malingering, somatic symptom disorder and illness anxiety, so this is not a defining feature. Modern practice also warns against diagnosis by exclusion alone; DSM-5 asks for positive signs of incompatibility with neurological disease. It tempts because it is a correct statement, yet it is incomplete and does not distinguish conversion from its neighbours.","c":"This is half right. DSM-IV did say conversion symptoms 'suggest a neurological or other general medical condition', but that phrase was its general definition of all somatoform disorders, so it does not single out conversion. The specific feature is that the symptoms affect voluntary motor or sensory function. It tempts because the rest of the option (sudden onset, psychosocial stressor) is word for word the same as the keyed answer.","d":"Intentional symptom production describes factitious disorder, where the person feigns or induces illness to occupy the sick role without external reward (its severe form named Munchausen syndrome by Richard Asher in 1951), and malingering, where the motive is external gain. Conversion symptoms are by definition not intentionally produced and typically follow a stressor closely. It tempts because 'no secondary gain' sounds like a familiar textbook phrase."}},{"q":"What is the characteristic belief associated with Koro?","concept":"Koro is a culture-bound syndrome, now called a cultural concept of distress, in which a person suddenly and intensely fears that the penis (in women, the vulva or nipples) is shrinking and retracting into the body, and that complete retraction will cause death. The name comes from the Malay and Indonesian region; in Chinese tradition it is suo yang ('shrinking of yang'), tied to beliefs about loss of vital essence. In the acute panic, the person or relatives may clamp or tie the organ to stop it disappearing. Koro occurs sporadically and also in epidemics spread by rumour, notably in Singapore (1967) and in north-eastern India and West Bengal, where Indian psychiatrists have documented outbreaks. DSM-IV listed it in its glossary of culture-bound syndromes, and DSM-5 (2013) gives it as an example of other specified obsessive-compulsive and related disorder, close to body dysmorphic disorder. Examiners rotate a set of culture-bound syndromes, so learn each by its core belief: Dhat syndrome (South Asia; anxiety about losing semen in urine, named by N. N. Wig in 1960); amok (Malaysia; brooding followed by a violent outburst and amnesia); latah (South-East Asia; startle-induced echolalia and echopraxia); taijin kyofusho (Japan; fear of offending others through one's body or gaze); windigo (Algonquian peoples; fear of becoming a cannibal monster); susto (Latin America; soul loss after a fright); and possession syndromes, now classed as dissociative trance or possession disorder. Option c is right because Koro is defined by the belief that the genitals are retracting into the body and that this may result in death.","traps":{"a":"Belief in possession by evil spirits describes possession syndromes, common in India as possession by a ghost, spirit or deity, and classed as trance and possession disorder (ICD-10 F44.3), a dissociative condition in which the person's usual identity is replaced by the possessing agent. It involves altered identity and trance, not fear of genital shrinkage. It tempts because both are culturally shaped conditions taught in the same lecture.","b":"A sense of impending doom tied to weather or atmospheric change is not a recognised culture-bound syndrome. It borrows the 'impending doom' of a panic attack and loosely echoes khyal cap ('wind attacks') among Cambodians, where a wind-like substance is believed to rise inside the body. Koro does involve panic and fear of death, which is why this tempts, but its specific content is genital retraction, not the environment.","d":"Believing that one is turning into a mythical creature or animal describes clinical lycanthropy (the delusion of becoming a wolf or other animal) or windigo among Algonquian peoples, the fear of being transformed into a cannibal monster. These concern transformation of identity, not shrinking organs. It tempts because koro, like these, involves a bizarre-sounding bodily belief rooted in cultural folklore."}},{"q":"What is the characteristic feature of Borderline Personality Disorder?","concept":"Borderline personality disorder (BPD) is a pervasive pattern, beginning by early adulthood, of instability in relationships, self-image and emotions, with marked impulsivity. Adolph Stern (1938) coined 'borderline' for patients he saw as lying on the border between neurosis and psychosis, and Otto Kernberg (1967) described borderline personality organization, marked by identity diffusion and primitive defences such as splitting. DSM-5 (2013) lists nine criteria, of which five are needed: frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment; unstable, intense relationships swinging between idealization and devaluation; identity disturbance; impulsivity in at least two self-damaging areas (spending, sex, substances, reckless driving, binge eating); recurrent suicidal behaviour or self-harm; affective instability; chronic emptiness; intense, inappropriate anger; and transient stress-related paranoia or dissociation. ICD-10 calls it emotionally unstable personality disorder, borderline type (F60.31), alongside an impulsive type. Marsha Linehan (1993) explained it through her biosocial theory, emotional vulnerability meeting an invalidating environment, and created dialectical behaviour therapy (DBT), the best-supported treatment. To separate the personality disorders used as options, look for the core motive. BPD and dependent personality disorder both fear being left, but dependent patients cling submissively, whereas borderline patients swing into rage and self-harm. Histrionic patients seek attention through shallow, dramatic emotion; antisocial patients disregard others' rights; avoidant patients (Cluster C) shun people from fear of criticism while wanting acceptance. Borderline, histrionic, antisocial and narcissistic make up Cluster B, the dramatic, emotional or erratic cluster. Option d is right because unstable relationships, impulsive behaviour and fear of abandonment are the hallmark features of BPD.","traps":{"a":"Social inhibition, fear of rejection and desire for acceptance describe avoidant personality disorder, a Cluster C (anxious, fearful) disorder marked by feelings of inadequacy and hypersensitivity to negative evaluation. The avoidant person withdraws from relationships to escape criticism, whereas the borderline person plunges into intense, stormy ones. It tempts because 'fear of rejection' sounds very like the borderline 'fear of abandonment'.","b":"Excessive attention-seeking, rapidly shifting but shallow emotion and theatrical behaviour describe histrionic personality disorder, another Cluster B disorder. The histrionic person needs to be the centre of attention and uses appearance and drama to get it, without the self-harm, emptiness and identity disturbance of BPD. It tempts because 'emotional volatility' overlaps with borderline affective instability and both belong to Cluster B.","c":"Disregard for and violation of others' rights, deceit and manipulation describe antisocial personality disorder, which DSM-5 diagnoses only from age 18 with evidence of conduct disorder before age 15. Hervey Cleckley (1941) described the related construct of psychopathy. It tempts because borderline patients can also be impulsive and are sometimes labelled manipulative, but their behaviour is driven by fear of abandonment, not callous exploitation."}},{"q":"What is Dysthymia?","concept":"Dysthymia (from Greek, 'bad state of mind') is chronic, low-grade depression. The term dysthymic disorder entered DSM-III (1980), replacing the older 'depressive neurosis', and DSM-5 (2013) merged it with chronic major depression under the name persistent depressive disorder (PDD). The DSM-5 criteria: depressed mood for most of the day, more days than not, for at least two years (one year in children and adolescents, where mood may be irritable), plus at least two of six symptoms: poor appetite or overeating, insomnia or hypersomnia, low energy, low self-esteem, poor concentration or indecisiveness, and hopelessness. The person is never free of symptoms for more than two months at a stretch, and there has never been a manic or hypomanic episode. ICD-10 codes dysthymia as F34.1 and places it, with cyclothymia (F34.0), among the persistent mood (affective) disorders. When a major depressive episode is superimposed on dysthymia, clinicians call it 'double depression'. The examiner's favourite trap is the set of chronic or recurrent mood conditions, which differ by polarity and severity: dysthymia is persistent mild depression only (unipolar); cyclothymia is at least two years of fluctuating mild depressive and hypomanic symptoms (bipolar spectrum, low severity); bipolar I disorder involves at least one manic episode; bipolar II disorder combines major depressive episodes with hypomania. Remember that the two-year rule applies to both dysthymia and cyclothymia, so duration alone cannot separate them; polarity does. Option c is right because dysthymia is defined as a persistent, chronic, mild depression without any manic or hypomanic phases.","traps":{"a":"Severe depression alternating with mania describes bipolar I disorder, the descendant of the 'manic-depressive insanity' of Emil Kraepelin (1899), in which at least one full manic episode occurs and major depressive episodes usually do too. Dysthymia is mild, chronic and purely depressive, with no manic phases. It tempts because 'dysthymia' sounds like a general term for disturbed mood, and candidates link any mood word with mood swings.","b":"Persistent instability of mood between mild depression and mild elation is cyclothymia (cyclothymic disorder), a bipolar spectrum condition lasting at least two years in adults, in which hypomanic and depressive symptoms never meet full episode criteria. It tempts because cyclothymia and dysthymia are both persistent mood disorders in ICD-10, share the two-year rule and are mild. The difference is polarity: cyclothymia goes up and down, dysthymia only down.","d":"Mild depression together with hypomania over two years is a bipolar spectrum picture, closest to cyclothymia, or to bipolar II disorder if full major depressive episodes occur. DSM-5 explicitly rules out persistent depressive disorder if there has ever been a manic or hypomanic episode. It tempts because it copies the correct 'persistent mild depression' and the two-year duration, then slips in hypomania."}},{"q":"According to Psychodynamic Theory, what is the primary defense mechanism used to deal with anxiety?","concept":"Defence mechanisms are unconscious strategies the ego uses to protect itself from anxiety arising from conflict between the id's impulses, the superego's demands and reality. Sigmund Freud put repression at the centre: in 1914 he called the theory of repression 'the corner-stone on which the whole structure of psycho-analysis rests', and his paper 'Repression' (1915) defined its essence as turning something away and keeping it at a distance from consciousness. Repression is motivated forgetting: threatening wishes, memories and feelings are pushed out of awareness, yet they stay active and return in disguised form as dreams, slips and symptoms. Freud separated primal repression, which keeps material from ever becoming conscious, from repression proper, which expels material that was once conscious. In Inhibitions, Symptoms and Anxiety (1926) he revised the order: anxiety is a signal that sets repression in motion, not its product. Anna Freud (1936) systematised the defences in The Ego and the Mechanisms of Defence and called repression the most efficacious and also the most dangerous of them. Contrast repression with suppression, the conscious, deliberate putting aside of thoughts, and with denial, the refusal to accept an external reality. George Vaillant (1977) ranked defences from psychotic and immature to neurotic and mature; repression sits at the neurotic level with displacement, isolation and reaction formation. Freud also tied specific defences to specific neuroses: displacement to phobias (Little Hans, 1909), isolation and undoing to obsessional neurosis, and conversion to hysteria. Option c is right because repression is the primary, foundational defence by which the ego keeps anxiety-provoking material out of consciousness.","traps":{"a":"Displacement shifts an impulse or emotion from its real, threatening target to a safer substitute, as when a man scolded by his boss shouts at his child. Freud used it to explain phobias: Little Hans (1909) displaced fear of his father onto horses. It is a real defence, but a secondary one that redirects material rather than barring it from awareness. It tempts because it is the textbook's most popular example of a defence.","b":"Conversion, in classical theory, is the mechanism of hysteria by which repressed conflict is transformed into a bodily symptom such as paralysis or blindness, as described by Josef Breuer and Sigmund Freud (1895). It is better seen as a form of symptom formation that follows repression, not as the basic defence against anxiety. It tempts because conversion disorder is well known and the word sounds like a defence.","d":"Isolation, or isolation of affect, separates an idea from its emotional charge, so a person can describe a traumatic event in a flat, detached way. Freud linked it, with undoing, to obsessional neurosis. It lets thoughts into consciousness stripped of feeling, so it is a later, partial defence, not the primary one. It tempts because 'isolation' sounds like shutting material away, which is what repression actually does."}},{"q":"What were the two clinical types differentiated by Emil Kraepelin?","concept":"Emil Kraepelin (1856 to 1926), a German psychiatrist who had trained in Wilhelm Wundt's laboratory, is called the father of modern psychiatric classification. Before him, diagnoses were mostly lists of symptoms. Kraepelin recorded thousands of cases on summary cards and followed patients over years, insisting that a disease is defined by its symptoms together with its course and outcome. On this basis, in the sixth edition of his textbook (1899), he divided the major psychoses into two groups, the Kraepelinian dichotomy. Dementia praecox (early dementia) began in youth and ran a chronic, deteriorating course; into it he merged hebephrenia, described by Ewald Hecker (1871), catatonia, described by Karl Kahlbaum (1874), and paranoid forms. Manic-depressive insanity ran in episodes with recovery in between and covered mania, melancholia and mixed states. Eugen Bleuler (1911) renamed dementia praecox 'schizophrenia', meaning a splitting of psychic functions, because many patients did not deteriorate and onset was not always early; he described the four A's (associations, affect, ambivalence, autism). Karl Leonhard (1957) later divided manic-depressive illness into unipolar and bipolar forms, a split that DSM-III (1980) adopted. The separation of schizophrenia spectrum disorders from mood disorders in DSM-5 and ICD-11 still follows Kraepelin, although schizoaffective disorder and shared genetic risk challenge the dichotomy. Variant questions ask who coined 'schizophrenia' (Bleuler), what criterion Kraepelin used (course and outcome), or who split unipolar from bipolar (Leonhard). Option c is right because the two clinical types Kraepelin differentiated were dementia praecox and manic-depressive illness.","traps":{"a":"Depression and anxiety are the two common non-psychotic conditions of modern psychiatry and primary care. Kraepelin's dichotomy concerned the major psychoses seen in asylums, and depression sat inside his manic-depressive insanity rather than being contrasted with anxiety. It tempts because depression and anxiety are the most familiar pair in present-day psychology, and the idea of depression also appears in the correct option.","b":"Schizophrenia is the 1911 name that Eugen Bleuler gave to Kraepelin's dementia praecox, and borderline personality disorder comes from Adolph Stern (1938) and Otto Kernberg (1967), entering DSM-III in 1980. Neither label belongs to Kraepelin's dichotomy, and a personality disorder is not a psychosis. It tempts because half of the option is the modern equivalent of one of Kraepelin's two categories.","d":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder and bipolar disorder are modern DSM labels. Bipolar disorder descends from Kraepelin's manic-depressive insanity through the unipolar and bipolar split of Karl Leonhard (1957), but OCD is a neurosis, not a psychosis, and was never one of Kraepelin's two groups. It tempts because one half again matches a Kraepelinian category in modern dress, so candidates who spot 'bipolar' stop reading."}},{"q":"What is the sheath/kosh surrounding atman that is involved in perceptual processing","concept":"The Pancha Kosha (five sheaths) model comes from the Taittiriya Upanishad and was elaborated in Advaita Vedanta texts. It describes the human being as the atman (Self) covered by five layers, from gross to subtle, like the layers of an onion. Annamaya kosha is the food sheath, the physical body. Pranamaya kosha is the vital sheath of breath and life energy, the five pranas working with the five organs of action. Manomaya kosha is the mental sheath: manas (mind) together with the five organs of perception (jnanendriyas: eyes, ears, nose, tongue, skin). It receives sense impressions, organises them into percepts, and is the seat of desire, emotion, doubt and indecision. Vijnanamaya kosha is the intellect sheath: buddhi with the organs of perception, which discriminates, judges, decides and carries the sense of agency. Anandamaya kosha, the innermost, is the bliss sheath, experienced in deep dreamless sleep. The Self is none of the sheaths, and Vedanta teaches discrimination (viveka) to stop identifying with them. The five sheaths map onto three bodies (sharira): gross (annamaya), subtle (pranamaya, manomaya, vijnanamaya) and causal (anandamaya). In Indian psychology and yoga therapy the model serves as a holistic framework for health, with practices aimed at each level, such as asanas for the body, pranayama for the vital sheath and meditation for the mental sheath. The key exam distinction is manas versus buddhi: manas perceives and wavers, buddhi judges and decides. Option d is right because the manomaya kosha, formed of manas and the organs of perception, carries out perceptual processing.","traps":{"a":"Ananda-maya kosha is the bliss sheath, the innermost and subtlest layer, identified with the causal body and with the peace of deep dreamless sleep. It lies closest to the atman and involves no sensory activity at all. It tempts because it is the best-known kosha, and candidates sometimes assume that the deepest layer controls everything, including perception.","b":"Vijnana-maya kosha is the intellect or wisdom sheath, formed by buddhi with the organs of perception. It discriminates, judges, decides and holds the sense of 'I am the doer', working on what manas has already perceived. It tempts because 'vijnana' means knowledge or understanding and perception sounds cognitive, but in this model judgment belongs to buddhi and perception to manas.","c":"Prana-maya kosha is the vital or energy sheath, made of the five pranas (prana, apana, vyana, udana, samana) working with the five organs of action. It governs breathing, circulation, digestion and other physiological functions and links body to mind. It tempts because the sense organs are alive and energised by prana, but the processing of sense impressions is assigned to the mental sheath."}},{"q":"Which school of Buddhism focuses on nirvana via collective suffering?","concept":"Buddhism began with Siddhartha Gautama, the Buddha, in north India around the fifth century BCE, and later divided into schools that differ in their ideal goal. Theravada ('teaching of the elders'), based on the Pali Canon and found today in Sri Lanka, Myanmar and Thailand, takes the arhat as its ideal: a person who has personally extinguished craving and reached nirvana through ethics, meditation and wisdom. Mahayana ('great vehicle') arose around the start of the Common Era, with texts such as the Perfection of Wisdom (Prajnaparamita) sutras and the Lotus Sutra, and philosophers such as Nagarjuna (around the second century CE). Its ideal is the bodhisattva, who vows to postpone final nirvana until all sentient beings are freed from suffering. Its central virtues are karuna (compassion) and prajna (wisdom), and its key doctrine is sunyata (emptiness). Mahayana writers called the earlier path Hinayana ('lesser vehicle'), a term now seen as pejorative. Vajrayana, the tantric form of Tibet, and Zen, which grew from Chinese Chan and stresses sudden insight through meditation, are later lineages that grew out of Mahayana and keep the bodhisattva ideal, but they are named for their methods. In psychology, Theravada vipassana and Zen fed mindfulness programmes such as Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction, founded by Jon Kabat-Zinn (1979), while Mahayana compassion practices inform compassion-focused interventions. The stem's 'collective suffering' means the shared suffering of all beings, from which Mahayana seeks collective liberation. Option b is right because Mahayana aims at nirvana for all beings together, through the bodhisattva who works for universal liberation.","traps":{"a":"Theravada, the 'teaching of the elders', is the oldest surviving school, based on the Pali Canon and followed in Sri Lanka, Myanmar and Thailand. Its ideal is the arhat, who reaches nirvana through his or her own practice of ethics, meditation and insight, so its focus is individual liberation. It tempts because it is the best-known 'original' Buddhism and is linked to the Four Noble Truths that every exam asks about.","c":"Vajrayana, the 'diamond' or 'thunderbolt vehicle', is tantric Buddhism, dominant in Tibet, Bhutan and Mongolia. It grew out of Mahayana and keeps the bodhisattva ideal, but it is defined by its esoteric methods: mantras, mandalas, visualisation, initiation by a guru and the aim of enlightenment within one lifetime. It tempts because it shares Mahayana compassion for all beings, yet the school identified with collective liberation is Mahayana itself.","d":"Zen is the Japanese form of Chinese Chan Buddhism, traditionally traced to Bodhidharma, which stresses seated meditation (zazen), koans and sudden, direct insight into one's true nature beyond scriptures. It is a Mahayana lineage and accepts the bodhisattva vow, but it is known for its meditative method rather than for the doctrine of collective salvation. It tempts because Zen is the most familiar Buddhist name in psychology."}},{"q":"The path to the cessation of suffering, as described in the fourth Noble Truth, is known as the _______.","concept":"The Four Noble Truths are the Buddha's first teaching, given in the Deer Park at Sarnath near Varanasi in the sermon called the Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta, 'setting in motion the wheel of the Dhamma'. They follow the logic of a physician: diagnosis, cause, prognosis and treatment. The First Truth is dukkha: life as ordinarily lived involves suffering or unsatisfactoriness, including birth, ageing, illness, death and separation from what we love. The Second is samudaya, the origin of suffering, which is tanha (craving or thirst) for sense pleasures, for existence and for non-existence. The Third is nirodha, the cessation of suffering when craving is fully extinguished, which is nirvana (Pali: nibbana). The Fourth is magga, the path leading to that cessation, the Noble Eightfold Path: right view, right intention, right speech, right action, right livelihood, right effort, right mindfulness and right concentration. These eight are grouped into three trainings: wisdom (panna), ethical conduct (sila) and concentration (samadhi). The path is also called the Middle Way, avoiding both sensual indulgence and extreme asceticism. Do not mix the truths with the three marks of existence: anicca (impermanence), dukkha (suffering) and anatta (non-self). Examiners also test the order: dukkha, samudaya, nirodha, magga. Clinical psychology meets this framework in mindfulness-based therapies, which draw on right mindfulness, and in the idea that suffering is maintained by craving and clinging, which parallels experiential avoidance in the acceptance and commitment therapy of Steven Hayes (1999). Option b is right because magga, meaning 'path', is the Fourth Noble Truth, the way to the cessation of suffering.","traps":{"a":"Samudaya means 'arising' or 'origin' and is the Second Noble Truth: suffering arises from tanha, craving for sense pleasure, for existence and for non-existence. It explains why suffering exists, not how to end it. It tempts because candidates remember that one truth deals with what to do about craving, and samudaya is the truth that actually names craving.","c":"Nirodha means 'cessation' and is the Third Noble Truth: suffering ends when craving is completely extinguished, the state called nirvana. It is the goal, not the route. The stem asks for the path to cessation, which is the Fourth Truth. It tempts because the stem itself contains the word 'cessation', so a candidate matching words picks nirodha instead of the path that leads there.","d":"Anicca means impermanence, one of the three marks of existence along with dukkha (suffering) and anatta (non-self). All conditioned things arise and pass away, which is why clinging to them causes suffering. It is a characteristic of reality, not one of the Four Noble Truths at all. It tempts because it is a familiar Pali term from the same chapter on Buddhist psychology."}},{"q":"According to the Bhagavad Gita, the Trigunas belong to","concept":"The Bhagavad Gita, a 700-verse dialogue between Krishna and Arjuna in the Bhishma Parva of the Mahabharata, borrows much of its metaphysics from Samkhya, the dualist school traditionally founded by the sage Kapila and set out systematically in Ishvarakrishna's Samkhya Karika. Samkhya holds that reality has two independent principles. Purusha is pure consciousness: the passive witness, unchanging and without qualities (nirguna). Prakriti is primordial nature or matter: active, changing and made of the three gunas. Sattva is lightness, clarity and harmony; rajas is activity, passion and restlessness; tamas is heaviness, inertia and darkness. In the unmanifest state the gunas are in balance; when the balance is disturbed, Prakriti evolves into buddhi (mahat), ahamkara, manas, the senses and the elements, so even the mind belongs to Prakriti. The Gita states the point directly in chapter 14, verse 5: sattva, rajas and tamas are 'born of Prakriti' and bind the embodied self to the body. Chapters 14, 17 and 18 apply the gunas to food, faith, charity, action and personality, and the ideal person is gunatita, one who rises above all three. Freedom comes when the self recognises that the gunas act and it only witnesses. Indian psychologists have built triguna personality scales on this model. Variant questions ask which principle is free of the gunas (Purusha), what Prakriti evolves into, or how the Gita's field (kshetra, body and nature) differs from its knower (kshetrajna, consciousness) in chapter 13. Option c is right because the Gita describes the three gunas as born of, and belonging to, Prakriti.","traps":{"a":"Samadhi is meditative absorption, the eighth and final limb of the eight-limbed yoga in Patanjali's Yoga Sutras, in which the mind becomes one with its object and ordinary distinctions dissolve. It is a state of consciousness reached by stilling the play of the gunas, not the principle to which they belong. It tempts because samadhi is a familiar Sanskrit term from the same Indian psychology unit.","b":"Purusha is pure consciousness in Samkhya and the Gita, the eternal witness that is nirguna, without qualities. The gunas belong to the other principle, and bondage arises only because Purusha wrongly identifies with their play. It tempts because Purusha and Prakriti are always taught as a pair, so a candidate who remembers the pair but not which side holds the gunas is left with a fifty-fifty guess.","d":"Samskaras are latent impressions left by past actions and experiences, stored in the mind (chitta) and shaping future tendencies, as described in Patanjali's Yoga Sutras. They are coloured by the gunas, but they are products of mental activity, not the ground to which the gunas belong. It tempts because samskaras, like the gunas, are used to explain personality and habitual behaviour in Indian psychology."}},{"q":"According to the Bhagavad Gita, what are the three elements of the mind?","concept":"Indian psychology describes the mind as an inner instrument, the antahkarana, distinct from the outer instruments, the senses. The Bhagavad Gita lists its parts when Krishna describes his lower nature in chapter 7, verse 4: earth, water, fire, air, space, manas, buddhi and ahamkara make up his eightfold Prakriti. So in the Gita the mind is not the true self; it is a subtle part of nature, while the self is the knower beyond it. Manas is the sensory mind: it receives input from the senses, attends, desires and wavers, the faculty of doubt and imagination (sankalpa and vikalpa). Buddhi is the intellect: it discriminates, judges and decides, and it is what yoga trains to be steady, as in the sthitaprajna, the person of steady wisdom, of chapter 2. Ahamkara is the ego or 'I-maker', which claims experiences and actions as 'mine' and creates the sense of being the doer. Chapter 3, verse 42 gives a hierarchy: the senses are high, the mind is higher than the senses, the intellect is higher than the mind, and the self is higher than the intellect. Samkhya also derives buddhi, ahamkara and manas from Prakriti, and later Vedanta adds a fourth part, chitta (the store of memory), giving a fourfold antahkarana. Examiners build distractors from other well-known Indian triads: the three gunas (qualities of nature), the purusharthas (goals of life) and metaphysical categories such as matter, consciousness and field. Option a is right because manas, buddhi and ahamkara are the three elements of the mind named in the Gita.","traps":{"b":"Prakriti (nature or matter) and purusha (consciousness) are the two ultimate principles of Samkhya, and kshetra (the field, meaning body and nature) is paired in Gita chapter 13 with kshetrajna, the knower of the field. These are metaphysical categories describing all of reality, not components of the mind. It tempts because the Gita discusses them at length and says the mind itself is part of prakriti and of the field.","c":"Dharma (duty, righteousness), artha (wealth) and kama (pleasure, desire) are three of the four purusharthas, the goals of human life, together called the trivarga; moksha (liberation) is the fourth. They describe what a person pursues, not what the mind is made of. It tempts because it is another famous Sanskrit triad and appears in the same syllabus unit on Indian psychology.","d":"Sattva, rajas and tamas are the three gunas, the qualities that make up Prakriti. They colour every mental state, so a mind can be called sattvic, rajasic or tamasic, but they are qualities operating in the mind, not its structural parts. It tempts because the gunas are the Gita's best-known psychological triad and are often presented as the basis of personality."}},{"q":"What are the four major goals of human life according to Indian culture?","concept":"The purusharthas, literally 'aims of a person', are the four goals that classical Hindu thought holds a full human life should pursue. Dharma is duty, righteousness and moral order, including the obligations of one's stage of life and social role. Artha is wealth, livelihood, power and material security. Kama is desire, pleasure, love and aesthetic enjoyment. Moksha is liberation from samsara, the cycle of birth and rebirth. The first three, called the trivarga, are worldly aims that must be balanced, with dharma regulating the pursuit of artha and kama; moksha is the transcendent fourth goal. Each has its classical texts: the Dharmashastras, such as the Manusmriti, for dharma; Kautilya's Arthashastra for artha; and Vatsyayana's Kamasutra for kama. The purusharthas are paired with the four ashramas, or stages of life: brahmacharya (student), grihastha (householder), vanaprastha (forest dweller, gradual withdrawal) and sannyasa (renunciant), with artha and kama most prominent for the householder and moksha for the renunciant. Psychologists often compare the scheme with the hierarchy of needs of Abraham Maslow (1943), likening moksha to the top of the hierarchy, self-actualisation and later self-transcendence, though the purusharthas are aims to be balanced rather than needs met in strict order. Distractors swap in plausible but non-canonical goals such as knowledge, fame, fitness or enlightenment; the test is to remember the Sanskrit four and translate them exactly. A simple memory aid is DAKM: dharma, artha, kama, moksha. Option c is right because duty, wealth, desires and liberation are the English equivalents of dharma, artha, kama and moksha.","traps":{"a":"Physical fitness and fame are modern-sounding substitutes, and neither is a purushartha. Fitness may be part of living dharmically, and fame loosely relates to artha, but the canonical list contains dharma and moksha, both missing here. Wealth and desires are correct, so the option looks half right. It tempts because it mixes two genuine goals with two everyday aims that many people actually pursue.","b":"Knowledge and enlightenment sound spiritual but are not the canonical terms. Knowledge (jnana) is a path to liberation, as in jnana yoga, not a separate goal, and while enlightenment loosely resembles moksha, the option drops dharma, the first and governing aim. It tempts because 'enlightenment' looks like a translation of moksha, and 'wealth, desires' are correct.","d":"This keeps dharma and kama but replaces artha with knowledge and moksha with fame or power. Liberation, the highest goal, is missing altogether, and wealth is missing too. It tempts because power is part of the broad meaning of artha (Kautilya's Arthashastra is a treatise on statecraft), so a candidate who half remembers artha as power may choose it."}},{"q":"Which of the following is an astik system in Indian thought?","concept":"Classical Indian philosophy divides its systems (darshanas, 'ways of seeing') into astika and nastika. The criterion is acceptance of the authority of the Vedas, not belief in God. The six astika (orthodox) schools come in three pairs: Nyaya (logic, attributed to Gautama) with Vaisheshika (atomism, Kanada); Samkhya (dualism of purusha and prakriti, Kapila) with Yoga (Patanjali's eight-limbed discipline of the mind); and Purva Mimamsa (ritual interpretation of the Vedas, Jaimini) with Uttara Mimamsa or Vedanta (Badarayana's Brahma Sutras). Vedanta interprets the Upanishads and has three main sub-schools. Advaita (non-dualism), systematised by Adi Shankaracharya around the eighth century CE, holds that Brahman alone is real, that the individual self (atman) is identical with Brahman, and that the world of multiplicity is maya. Vishishtadvaita (qualified non-dualism) is associated with Ramanuja, and Dvaita (dualism) with Madhva. The nastika (heterodox) schools reject Vedic authority: Buddhism, Jainism, the materialist Charvaka or Lokayata, and the Ajivikas. A classic trap is that astika does not mean theist: classical Samkhya and Purva Mimamsa are astika although they need no creator God, while Jainism and Buddhism are nastika despite rich ethical and spiritual systems. Sufism, the mystical tradition of Islam brought to India by orders such as the Chishtis, stands outside this Vedic classification altogether. In Indian psychology, the Advaita model of the self and the Yoga model of the mind are the most cited sources. Option b is right because Advaita-Vedanta accepts the authority of the Vedas and is one of the six orthodox (astika) darshanas.","traps":{"a":"Sufism (tasawwuf) is the mystical, devotional tradition within Islam, which reached India in the medieval period through orders such as the Chishti, Suhrawardi and Naqshbandi, and deeply influenced devotional poetry and music. It does not accept the Vedas and was never part of the astika and nastika classification, which applies only to Indian darshanas. It tempts because Sufi mysticism resembles Advaita in seeking union with the divine.","c":"Buddhism, founded by Siddhartha Gautama, rejects the authority of the Vedas, the Vedic ritual system and the idea of a permanent self (anatta), so it is classed as nastika (heterodox). It tempts because candidates wrongly equate astika with 'spiritual' or 'religious', and Buddhism is plainly both. It also arose in India, unlike Sufism, which makes it look like an orthodox Indian school.","d":"Jainism, whose twenty-fourth tirthankara Mahavira was a contemporary of the Buddha, rejects the authority of the Vedas and of a creator God, and teaches liberation through ahimsa, austerity and the three jewels (right faith, right knowledge, right conduct). It is therefore nastika. It tempts because Jainism accepts karma, rebirth and an eternal soul (jiva), ideas shared with the astika schools."}},{"q":"What are the three gunas in the Upanishads?","concept":"The three gunas, literally 'strands' or 'qualities', are the constituents of Prakriti, primordial nature, in Indian thought. The idea has roots in the Upanishads: the Chandogya Upanishad speaks of three colours (red, white and black) present in all things, the Shvetashvatara Upanishad describes an unborn nature of three colours, often read as the gunas, and later Upanishads such as the Maitri name sattva, rajas and tamas explicitly. Samkhya then systematised the theory, and the Bhagavad Gita gave it its best-known psychological form in chapters 14, 17 and 18. Sattva is purity, lightness, clarity, knowledge and harmony; its colour is white. Rajas is activity, passion, desire, ambition and restlessness; its colour is red. Tamas is inertia, darkness, dullness, ignorance and sloth; its colour is black. Every object, food, action and mental state contains all three in varying proportions, and personality is described by whichever guna dominates. The spiritual aim is to cultivate sattva and finally go beyond all three. Indian psychologists have built triguna personality inventories on this model. Do not confuse the gunas with the three doshas of Ayurveda (vata, pitta, kapha), which are bodily humours, although Ayurveda does treat rajas and tamas as the two mental doshas. Other triads that examiners use as distractors are purusha, prakriti and kshetra (metaphysical categories), atman, Brahman and maya (Vedantic ontology), and karma, dharma and moksha (action, duty and liberation). Option b is right because rajas, tamas and sattva are the three gunas, simply listed here in a different order.","traps":{"a":"Prakriti (nature), purusha (consciousness) and kshetra (the field, meaning body and nature as the arena of experience, from Gita chapter 13) are metaphysical categories, not qualities. The gunas are the stuff of which prakriti is made, so this option names their container rather than the gunas themselves. It tempts because prakriti and the gunas are taught together, and any list of Sanskrit metaphysical terms looks plausible.","c":"Atman (the individual self), Brahman (the absolute reality) and maya (the power by which the one appears as many) are the pillars of Vedantic, especially Advaita, ontology, and they come from the Upanishads themselves. They describe the nature of self and reality, not the qualities of nature. It tempts because the stem says 'in the Upanishads', and these three are the most famous Upanishadic concepts.","d":"Karma (action and its consequences), dharma (duty, moral order) and moksha (liberation from rebirth) are key ideas of Hindu ethics, with dharma and moksha also among the four purusharthas. They concern how to act and what to aim for, not the qualities that make up nature and mind. It tempts because this is another familiar Sanskrit triad with a spiritual tone."}},{"q":"Bavisha lost her job and has become so stressed that she cannot eat, has withdrawn, and cannot get herself to function, sunk in inertia. This dull, lethargic, cannot-act state reflects the dominance of which?","concept":"The three gunas, sattva, rajas and tamas, come from Samkhya philosophy, the dualist school traditionally traced to the sage Kapila and systematised in Ishvarakrishna's Samkhya Karika. Samkhya separates purusha, pure consciousness, from prakriti, primordial nature, and holds that prakriti is woven from these three strands, so everything in nature, including mind and personality, is a mixture of them. The Bhagavad Gita (chapter 14) applies them directly to human character. Sattva is lightness, clarity, calm, knowledge and balance. Rajas is activity, passion, desire, restlessness, ambition and anger. Tamas is heaviness, darkness, dullness, inertia, sloth, excess sleep, confusion and an inability to act. All three are always present; the dominant one colours a person's state at a given time, and the balance can shift. Ayurveda (Charaka Samhita) treats rajas and tamas as the two mental doshas, and modern Indian psychology uses the gunas as a personality typology with its own questionnaires. A useful clinical mapping for exams: withdrawal, psychomotor slowing, poor appetite and hopeless inactivity look tamasic; agitation, craving, irritability and frantic activity look rajasic; equanimity and insight look sattvic. Examiners build distractors from other Indian frameworks: the eight limbs (ashtanga) of Patanjali's Yoga Sutras, such as yama and pratyahara, which are practices, and the paths (margas) of the Gita, such as jnana, karma and bhakti, which are routes to liberation. A guna is a quality or state of mind; a limb or path is something you do. Option a is right because Bavisha's dull, lethargic, withdrawn, unable-to-act state is the textbook description of tamas dominance.","traps":{"b":"Yama is the first of the eight limbs (ashtanga) of Patanjali's Yoga Sutras: yama, niyama, asana, pranayama, pratyahara, dharana, dhyana and samadhi. The five yamas are ethical restraints: non-violence (ahimsa), truthfulness (satya), non-stealing (asteya), sexual restraint (brahmacharya) and non-possessiveness (aparigraha). Yama is a rule of conduct a person chooses to follow, not a mental state that overwhelms someone after a job loss. It tempts because it is a familiar Sanskrit term from the same Indian psychology unit.","c":"Pratyahara is the fifth limb of Patanjali's ashtanga yoga: deliberately withdrawing the senses from their objects so that attention turns inward, as preparation for dharana (concentration). It is a voluntary, trained discipline. Bavisha's withdrawal is involuntary, a collapse of energy and interest, not skilled sense control. It tempts because the stem uses the word 'withdrawn', and pratyahara is usually translated as 'withdrawal of the senses', so the surface match pulls candidates toward it.","d":"Jnana means knowledge, and jnana yoga is the path to liberation through discriminating the real from the unreal, one of the paths taught in the Bhagavad Gita alongside karma yoga (action) and bhakti yoga (devotion). Clear knowledge is a mark of sattva; tamas is exactly what clouds it. So jnana describes the opposite of Bavisha's state. It tempts as a well-known Indian psychology term for candidates who recognise the Sanskrit but not the guna framework."}},{"q":"What are the components of the Linga sharira (subtle body) according to the Pancha kosas?","concept":"Indian psychology describes the person through two overlapping schemes. The first is the three bodies (sharira traya): the gross body (sthula sharira), the subtle body (sukshma or linga sharira) and the causal body (karana sharira). The second is the five sheaths (pancha kosha) of the Taittiriya Upanishad, layered from outer to inner: annamaya kosha (the food or physical sheath), pranamaya kosha (the vital sheath of breath and the five pranas), manomaya kosha (the mental sheath of manas, emotion and sense processing), vijnanamaya kosha (the intellect sheath of buddhi, judgement and doership) and anandamaya kosha (the bliss sheath). Advaita Vedanta texts such as Tattva Bodha and Vivekachudamani, traditionally attributed to Adi Shankaracharya (8th century CE), map one scheme onto the other: annamaya forms the gross body; pranamaya, manomaya and vijnanamaya together form the subtle body; anandamaya forms the causal body. The subtle body survives death and carries karmic impressions (samskaras) into the next birth. Atman, the Self, is not a sheath at all; it is pure consciousness, the witness that the five sheaths cover. The subtle body can also be listed by its 17 parts: five sense organs (jnanendriyas), five organs of action (karmendriyas), five pranas, manas and buddhi. Exams also use the antahkarana, the inner instrument: manas, buddhi, ahankara and chitta. Option a is right because it names the three middle koshas that make up the linga sharira; the added Atman is best read as the witnessing Self within, not as a fourth sheath.","traps":{"b":"This list adds annamaya kosha, the outermost food sheath, which is made of and sustained by food. It is the gross physical body (sthula sharira) that perishes at death, so it cannot be part of the subtle body, which begins with the pranamaya kosha. It tempts because three of its four items are correct and the list runs neatly from the outer sheath inward in textbook order, so it looks complete.","c":"Ahankara (the I-maker or ego), manas (the receiving, doubting mind), buddhi (the deciding intellect) and chitta (the store of memories and impressions) are the four functions of the antahkarana, the inner instrument of Vedanta psychology. The antahkarana does belong to the subtle body, but this is a list of mental functions, not of koshas, so it does not answer a question framed by the pancha kosha model. It tempts because all four are clearly subtle, mental entities.","d":"Jnanendriyas (five sense organs), karmendriyas (five organs of action), manas, buddhi and ahankara are tattvas, the evolutes of Samkhya, where the subtle body (linga) also includes the five subtle elements (tanmatras). The Vedanta version has 17 parts: ten indriyas, five pranas, manas and buddhi. Either way this is a list of elements, not of koshas. It tempts because it really does describe subtle body contents, but in a different classification from the one the stem asks for."}},{"q":"What are Samskara according to the Pancha kosas?","concept":"The Pancha Kosha (five sheaths) model comes from the Taittiriya Upanishad, which describes the person as a set of nested sheaths around the Self (Atman). From outer to inner they are the annamaya kosha (food sheath, the physical body), pranamaya kosha (vital breath and energy), manomaya kosha (the mind that receives sense data and reacts), vijnanamaya kosha (intellect, discrimination, the sense of agency) and anandamaya kosha (the sheath of bliss). Vedanta maps these onto three bodies: the gross body (sthula sharira) is the food sheath; the subtle body (sukshma sharira) holds the vital, mental and intellectual sheaths; and the causal body (karana sharira) corresponds to the bliss sheath. Samskaras (from Sanskrit sam plus kr, 'to shape or put together') are the latent impressions that every action, thought and experience leaves behind. They lie dormant, then surface as habits, dispositions and automatic reactions. Clusters of samskaras form vasanas, enduring tendencies or desires. In the Yoga Sutras of Patanjali, samskaras explain why patterns repeat and why practice and detachment are needed to weaken old impressions and plant new ones. Indian psychology texts therefore translate samskara as past tendencies or nature: the residue of the past that colours present personality, close to the modern ideas of habit, implicit memory and conditioned disposition. Keep three things apart: a sheath (a layer of the person), a body (gross, subtle or causal) and samskara (the impressions carried within those layers). Option b is right because samskaras are the accumulated past tendencies that shape a person's present nature.","traps":{"a":"The gross body (sthula sharira) is the annamaya kosha, the physical body built and maintained by food, which is born, grows and dies. It is the outermost layer of the person, not the store of past impressions. It tempts because the question names the kosha framework, and the gross body is the first and most familiar layer students memorise from it.","c":"The sense organs (jnanendriyas: ears, skin, eyes, tongue, nose) and organs of action (karmendriyas) are instruments of perception and action. In Vedanta these subtle faculties are grouped with the vital, mental and intellectual sheaths of the subtle body. They take in present experience; samskaras are what that experience leaves behind. It tempts because impressions begin as sense contact, so the two feel linked.","d":"The subtle body (sukshma sharira) combines the vital, mental and intellectual sheaths. In Vedanta it is the vehicle that carries samskaras from one life to the next, so it is closely linked to them. But a vehicle is not its contents: the question asks what samskaras are, not where they reside. That close link makes it the strongest distractor."}},{"q":"What is the process of psycho-spiritual transformation in Sufism?","concept":"Sufism (tasawwuf) is the mystical, inward path within Islam. Indian psychology courses treat it as a psychology of transformation: a method for changing personality and consciousness, not just a set of beliefs. The central idea is the nafs, the lower or ego self. The Quran is read as describing stages of the nafs: the commanding self (nafs al-ammara) driven by impulse and desire, the blaming self (nafs al-lawwama) that notices its faults and feels remorse, and the tranquil self (nafs al-mutmainna) at peace with God. Later Sufi teachers added further stages. The seeker (salik) travels through stations (maqamat), stable qualities such as repentance, patience, trust and contentment that are earned by effort, and states (ahwal), passing experiences of grace such as longing or awe. Practices include dhikr (repeated remembrance of God), muraqaba (meditation), service, and guidance by a spiritual master (shaykh or pir), as in the Chishti order prominent in India. The goal is fana, the passing away of the ego self, followed by baqa, subsisting in God. A classic four-level map runs from shariah (law) to tariqah (path) to haqiqah (inner truth) to marifah (direct knowledge). Psychologically, this is a journey from ordinary surface awareness, dominated by desire and habit, toward deeper and higher levels of self-consciousness. Do not mix it with Buddhist psychology, whose path is built on the Four Noble Truths and the Noble Eightfold Path. Option b is right because it states the definition used in these texts: psycho-spiritual transformation as a journey from mundane surface awareness to the depths and heights of self-consciousness.","traps":{"a":"Guidelines and rules for the ordinary Muslim describe the shariah, the outward religious law covering prayer, fasting, charity and conduct. Sufis accept the shariah as the starting point, but the path of transformation begins where outward observance turns inward. The option describes rule-following, not a change in consciousness. It tempts because Sufism sits within Islam and any question on it brings Islamic law to mind.","c":"Moving from the outer layer of Islam to its inner dimension is a common general description of Sufism as the esoteric side of the faith, the step from shariah toward tariqah and haqiqah. It is not false, but it describes Sufism's place in religion, not the psychological process of transforming the self. It tempts because it sounds like a fair definition and is the closest rival to the keyed answer.","d":"The Four Noble Truths (suffering, its origin in craving, its cessation, and the path to cessation) are the core teaching of the Buddha, and the transformation they describe runs through the Noble Eightfold Path. They belong to Buddhist psychology, not Sufism. The option tempts because its first half, transformation of personality and consciousness, sounds exactly right before the wrong tradition is attached."}},{"q":"Which year saw the establishment of the first psychology department in India?","concept":"Academic psychology in India began at the University of Calcutta. An experimental psychology laboratory was set up there in 1915, and a separate Department of Psychology followed in 1916, the first in the country. The philosopher Brajendranath Seal drafted the 1916 syllabus on the model of the experimental psychology of Wilhelm Wundt, who had opened the first psychology laboratory at Leipzig in 1879. The department was led by Narendra Nath Sengupta (head from 1916), who had trained at Harvard under Hugo Munsterberg, a Wundt student brought to Harvard by William James in 1892. So early Indian psychology was laboratory based and Western in method, focused on perception, attention and psychophysics. Girindrasekhar Bose, a Calcutta physician who taught in the department and later headed it, founded the Indian Psychoanalytical Society in 1922. The University of Mysore followed with psychology teaching in 1924 under M. V. Gopalaswamy, who had studied in London with Charles Spearman, known for his 1904 g factor. The Indian Journal of Psychology began in the mid-1920s. Variant questions are built from this timeline, so learn it as a chain: Wundt 1879, Calcutta laboratory 1915, Calcutta department 1916, Indian Psychoanalytical Society 1922, Mysore 1924. A later line of questions concerns indigenisation: Durganand Sinha (1986) argued that borrowed Western models fitted Indian social reality poorly. Option a is right because the University of Calcutta set up India's first psychology department in 1916.","traps":{"b":"1924 is usually cited as the year the University of Mysore began psychology teaching and a laboratory under M. V. Gopalaswamy, who had trained in London with Charles Spearman. That makes Mysore the second major centre, eight years after Calcutta. It tempts because it is a real founding date from the same early period, and candidates who remember 'a department in the 1920s' pick it.","c":"1905 predates academic psychology in India. In psychology it is best known as the year Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon published the first practical intelligence scale in Paris; in Indian history it is the year of the partition of Bengal. It tempts because it is a famous psychology date and feels like a plausible 'early beginning' for the discipline in India.","d":"1938 is too late. By then Calcutta had taught psychology for over twenty years, Mysore had followed in 1924, and the Indian Psychoanalytical Society (1922) was well established. Psychology spread to many more universities in later decades, especially after independence. It tempts only as a guess for candidates who assume that academic psychology reached India late."}},{"q":"According to positivism, what is the nature of reality?","concept":"Research paradigms are sets of basic beliefs that answer three linked questions. Ontology asks what reality is. Epistemology asks what relationship the knower has with what can be known. Methodology asks how the inquirer can find it out. The standard comparison comes from Egon Guba and Yvonna Lincoln (1994), who set four paradigms side by side. Positivism, rooted in Auguste Comte's call in the 1830s for a science of society modelled on physics, holds naive realism: there is one real reality, driven by natural laws, and it can be apprehended as it is. Its epistemology is dualist and objectivist (the researcher stands apart and does not influence the object), and its findings are treated as true. Its method is experimental, verifying hypotheses with mainly quantitative data. Post-positivism holds critical realism: reality is real, but our grasp of it is imperfect and probabilistic, so findings are only probably true and hypotheses are tested by falsification, following Karl Popper (1934). Critical theory holds historical realism: a virtual reality shaped over time by social, political, cultural, economic, ethnic and gender values, studied through dialogue to expose and change power. Constructivism holds relativism: multiple local and specific constructed realities, with findings co-created by researcher and participant through hermeneutic and dialectical methods. A fast way to sort options: 'apprehendable' means positivism, 'imperfectly and probabilistically' means post-positivism, 'shaped by values' means critical theory, 'constructed' means constructivism. Option c is right because positivism's naive realist ontology says a real reality exists and can be apprehended.","traps":{"a":"A virtual reality shaped by social, political, cultural, economic, ethnic and gender values, crystallised over time, is the historical realism of critical theory in Egon Guba and Yvonna Lincoln (1994). It covers neo-Marxist, feminist and participatory inquiry aimed at exposing power. Positivism denies that values shape reality. It tempts because the option is long and detailed, which looks like a textbook definition.","b":"A real reality that can be apprehended only imperfectly and probabilistically is critical realism, the ontology of post-positivism. It keeps realism but admits that human observation is flawed, so findings are only probably true and are tested by falsification (Karl Popper, 1934). It tempts most because it differs from positivism by just one qualifier, 'imperfectly and probabilistically'.","d":"Only local and specific constructed realities is relativism, the ontology of constructivism, in which realities are mental constructions that differ across people and groups and are studied through hermeneutic and dialectical methods. It is the opposite end from positivism. It tempts because constructivism is the paradigm most often contrasted with positivism in qualitative research teaching."}},{"q":"What is the key goal of liberal feminism?","concept":"Feminist theories agree that women are disadvantaged but differ on the cause, and so on the cure. Exams test this by pairing each school with its goal. Liberal feminism is the oldest strand, growing from Enlightenment ideas of individual rights and reason. Mary Wollstonecraft (1792), in A Vindication of the Rights of Woman, argued that women appear inferior only because they are denied education. John Stuart Mill (1869), in The Subjection of Women, argued for legal equality. Betty Friedan (1963), in The Feminine Mystique, described the 'problem that has no name' among American housewives and helped found the National Organization for Women in 1966. The liberal diagnosis is that women are held back by unfair laws, customs and lack of opportunity, not by the system itself. So the cure is reform: equal rights, education, suffrage, equal pay, and access to jobs and public office, letting women enter and rise within mainstream institutions. Its rivals reject that strategy. Marxist feminism sees women's oppression as rooted in capitalism and class, and wants the system overthrown. Socialist feminism sees capitalism and patriarchy working together. Radical feminism sees patriarchy as the root and wants its basic assumptions challenged. Cultural feminism values traits linked with women and seeks to build a separate women's culture. The usual criticism is that liberal feminism mainly serves educated, middle-class women and leaves deeper structures untouched. Option c is right because liberal feminism's key goal is to integrate women into the existing structures of mainstream society through reform and equal opportunity.","traps":{"a":"Overthrowing the capitalist system is the aim of Marxist feminism, which follows Friedrich Engels (1884), The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State, in tracing women's subordination to private property and class society. On this view reform is not enough; the economic system must go. Liberal feminism wants change within existing institutions. It tempts because both are presented as equality movements.","b":"Building a women's culture is the aim of cultural feminism, which grew out of radical feminism in the 1970s. It celebrates qualities linked with women, such as care and cooperation, and builds women-centred spaces, art and communities rather than entering male institutions. That is the reverse of liberal feminism's integration. It tempts because it also sounds like empowerment.","d":"Recognising that women are oppressed by the capitalist system is the premise of Marxist and socialist feminism, which link women's unpaid domestic labour and low-paid work to the needs of capital. It is a diagnosis, not a liberal goal; liberals locate the problem in unequal laws and opportunity. It tempts because it sits beside 'overthrow capitalism' and splits candidates' attention."}},{"q":"What is the primary focus of radical feminism?","concept":"Radical feminism arose in the late 1960s and 1970s within the women's liberation movement in the United States and Britain. 'Radical' means going to the root, and for radical feminists the root of women's oppression is patriarchy: a system of male power that runs through the family, sexuality, religion, law and culture, older and deeper than capitalism. Kate Millett (1970), in Sexual Politics, argued that the relationship between the sexes is a political relationship of domination. Shulamith Firestone (1970), in The Dialectic of Sex, located it in reproduction and the family. The slogan 'the personal is political', popularised by Carol Hanisch's 1970 essay, captures the method: problems women face in private life (housework, sexuality, violence at home) are products of power, not personal failings. Consciousness-raising groups, where women compared experiences, were the main tool. Because patriarchy lives in everyday assumptions, radical feminists aim to challenge its basic premises (that men naturally lead, that women belong in the home, that male sexuality defines the norm) rather than simply win equal access. Contrast the neighbours. Liberal feminism wants women integrated into existing institutions through reform. Marxist feminism blames capitalism and class. Socialist feminism combines capitalism and patriarchy. Cultural feminism, which grew out of radical feminism, focuses on building a separate women's culture. Option c is right because radical feminism's primary focus is challenging the fundamental assumptions of patriarchy, which it sees as the root of women's oppression.","traps":{"a":"Recognising that women are oppressed by the capitalist system is the starting point of Marxist and socialist feminism. Marxist feminists, following Friedrich Engels (1884), see women's subordination as a product of private property and class. Radical feminists reject this, arguing that patriarchy predates capitalism and survives under any economic system. It tempts because both schools are critical of the status quo.","b":"Building a women's culture is the aim of cultural feminism, which developed from radical feminism in the mid-1970s and shifted from fighting patriarchy to valuing female traits and creating women-centred communities. Because the two share roots, textbooks sometimes blur them. The question asks for the primary focus of radical feminism itself, which is patriarchy. It tempts as the closest neighbour.","d":"Integrating women into mainstream society is the goal of liberal feminism (Mary Wollstonecraft, 1792; Betty Friedan, 1963), which seeks equal rights and opportunity through reform of existing institutions. Radical feminists criticise this as insufficient, since entering a patriarchal structure leaves its assumptions intact. It tempts because integration is the best known feminist goal."}},{"q":"What is the primary characteristic of absolute poverty?","concept":"Poverty can be measured against a fixed standard or against the rest of society, and exams test which is which. Absolute poverty uses a fixed, minimum standard: a person is poor if they lack the resources to obtain the basic necessities for survival, such as food, safe water, shelter, clothing, sanitation and basic health care. Seebohm Rowntree's study of York (1901) set an early example with a subsistence line based on the cost of a minimum diet and essentials. The United Nations World Summit for Social Development in Copenhagen (1995) defined absolute poverty as severe deprivation of basic human needs, including food, safe drinking water, sanitation, health, shelter, education and information. Internationally it is tracked through the World Bank's international poverty line, a daily income measured in purchasing power parity dollars. In India, poverty lines were estimated by expert groups such as the Tendulkar Committee (2009) and the Rangarajan Committee (2014). Relative poverty, by contrast, is defined against the typical living standard of one's society, for example income below 60 percent of the median; Peter Townsend (1979) described it as being unable to take part in the customs and activities normal in that society. Relative poverty therefore rises with inequality, while absolute poverty can fall even as inequality grows. Two further types appear as distractors: situational (transient) poverty after a shock such as job loss, illness or disaster, and generational (chronic) poverty passed down across generations. Option b is right because the defining characteristic of absolute poverty is lack of access to basic necessities.","traps":{"a":"Income inequality is the uneven distribution of income across a population, measured with tools such as the Lorenz curve and the Gini coefficient (Corrado Gini, 1912). It is the basis of relative poverty, where poverty is judged against others. Absolute poverty uses a fixed survival threshold, so a country can reduce it while inequality rises. It tempts because both terms appear in every poverty chapter.","c":"Temporary deprivation after an adverse event, such as a job loss, illness, crop failure or natural disaster, is situational or transient poverty. It is defined by its cause and duration, and families often recover once the shock passes. Absolute poverty is defined by the level of deprivation, whatever its cause. It tempts because people in situational poverty may briefly fall below the absolute line.","d":"Poverty handed down through generations is generational or chronic poverty, in which low education, poor health, debt and lack of assets keep successive generations poor. Oscar Lewis (1959) linked it to a 'culture of poverty', a contested idea. It describes persistence over time, not the survival threshold that defines absolute poverty. It tempts because chronic poverty is often also absolute."}},{"q":"How does eudemonic wellbeing conceptualize wellbeing?","concept":"Positive psychology recognises two traditions of well-being, both traced to Greek philosophy. The hedonic tradition, from early Greek thinkers who equated the good life with pleasure, says well-being is pleasure and the absence of pain. In psychology it became subjective well-being, defined by Ed Diener (1984) as high life satisfaction, frequent positive affect and infrequent negative affect. The eudaimonic tradition comes from the Nicomachean Ethics of Aristotle (4th century BCE), where eudaimonia means living well by realising one's daimon or true potential through virtuous activity. Feeling good is not the test; living in line with one's best capacities is. Carol Ryff (1989) turned this into six dimensions of psychological well-being: self-acceptance, positive relations with others, autonomy, environmental mastery, purpose in life and personal growth. Richard Ryan and Edward Deci (2001) linked eudaimonia to self-determination theory, in which well-being follows satisfaction of the needs for autonomy, competence and relatedness. Martin Seligman's authentic happiness model (2002) separated the pleasant life from the engaged and meaningful lives, and Christopher Peterson and Seligman (2004) classified 24 character strengths under six virtues, giving eudaimonic work its tools. The meaningful life, in Seligman's terms, means using one's strengths in service of something larger than oneself. For exam sorting: pleasure, relaxation, positive mood and satisfaction point to hedonic well-being; growth, meaning, virtue, strengths, purpose and contribution point to eudaimonic well-being. Income and possessions belong to neither; they are objective or economic indicators. Option c is right because eudaimonic well-being is about cultivating personal strengths and contributing to the greater good.","traps":{"a":"Measuring well-being by income and material possessions is an economic or objective-indicator approach, such as using GDP per head. Richard Easterlin (1974) showed that rising national income does not reliably raise reported happiness, the Easterlin paradox. Neither hedonic nor eudaimonic psychology defines well-being as wealth. It tempts because income is the most common everyday proxy for a good life.","b":"Focusing on pleasure and relaxation is the hedonic view, which treats well-being as feeling good: positive affect, comfort, enjoyment and freedom from distress. The subjective well-being model of Ed Diener (1984) belongs to this tradition. Eudaimonic theorists accept that pleasure matters but treat it as a by-product, not the definition. It tempts because relaxation sounds healthy and close to wellness.","d":"Equating well-being with pleasure and happiness is the core hedonic definition, the direct opposite of eudaimonia in every positive psychology text. Richard Ryan and Edward Deci (2001) frame the field around exactly this contrast. A person can be pleased yet not flourishing, or struggling yet growing. It tempts because 'eudaimonia' is often loosely translated as 'happiness', which makes the hedonic option look right."}},{"q":"What are some areas of post-traumatic growth?","concept":"Post-traumatic growth (PTG) is positive psychological change experienced as a result of struggling with a highly challenging life crisis. The term was introduced by Richard Tedeschi and Lawrence Calhoun in the mid-1990s, and their Posttraumatic Growth Inventory (1996) has 21 items grouped into five domains. Relating to others: closer relationships, more compassion, more willingness to accept help. New possibilities: new interests, roles or life paths. Personal strength: 'if I survived that, I can handle anything'. Spiritual change: deeper engagement with spiritual or existential questions. Appreciation of life: changed priorities and valuing everyday things. Growth comes not from the trauma itself but from the struggle with it, especially deliberate rumination that rebuilds a shattered set of core beliefs. Distress and growth can coexist; PTG is not the absence of PTSD. Know the neighbours. Resilience means bouncing back to the previous level of functioning; growth means going beyond it. Benefit finding is seeing positives in adversity, often studied in illness. Stress-related growth, measured by Crystal Park and colleagues (1996), is a similar idea for less extreme stress. Viktor Frankl (1946), in Man's Search for Meaning, is the classic forerunner, arguing that meaning can be found even in suffering. Critics note that self-reported growth may partly be a coping illusion. Exams usually test recall of the five domains, so learn them as a list. Option d is right because appreciation of life, relating to others, personal strength and spiritual change are all PTGI domains, making it the fullest correct list offered.","traps":{"a":"'None of the above' would be right only if no option listed real growth domains. Both b and d list genuine domains from the Posttraumatic Growth Inventory of Richard Tedeschi and Lawrence Calhoun (1996), so this is plainly wrong. It tempts candidates who notice that neither option lists all five domains and conclude that no option is complete enough to accept.","b":"Appreciation of life, personal strength and new possibilities are all genuine domains of the Posttraumatic Growth Inventory (Richard Tedeschi and Lawrence Calhoun, 1996), so this option is not false. It is simply less complete: it gives three of the five, while d gives four, adding relating to others and spiritual change. In single-best-answer format the fuller correct list wins. It tempts because every item in it is right.","c":"Material possessions, financial success and personal weakness are not growth domains; the inventory measures increased perceived strength, not weakness, and growth is psychological, not material. Slipping 'new possibilities' (a real domain) into the list makes it look plausible. It tempts candidates who skim, recognise one familiar term and miss the plainly wrong ones beside it."}},{"q":"How have physician recommendations and increased awareness contributed to higher exercise participation rates?","concept":"Health psychology explains why people take up exercise using models of health behaviour. The Health Belief Model, developed in the 1950s by US Public Health Service researchers including Godfrey Hochbaum and Irwin Rosenstock, says action depends on perceived susceptibility to a disease, perceived severity, perceived benefits of acting, perceived barriers, cues to action and, in later versions, self-efficacy. A doctor's recommendation is a classic cue to action, and information on how exercise lowers the risk of heart disease, diabetes, obesity and depression raises perceived benefits. Public-health campaigns work the same way at scale. The Theory of Planned Behaviour of Icek Ajzen (1991) adds that attitudes, subjective norms (what important others, including one's doctor, expect) and perceived behavioural control shape intention. Self-efficacy, from Albert Bandura (1977), is the belief that one can carry out the behaviour. The Transtheoretical Model of James Prochaska and Carlo DiClemente (1983) adds stages: precontemplation, contemplation, preparation, action and maintenance. Awareness moves people from precontemplation to contemplation. Knowledge alone rarely sustains change, because the gap between intention and action is large, so programmes add goal setting, social support and feedback. For this item, though, the mechanism is simple: physician advice and campaigns make people aware of the benefits of exercise, and greater awareness of benefits raises participation. The other choices either reverse the effect or deny that any answer applies. Option c is right because physician recommendations and awareness campaigns raise participation by increasing knowledge of the benefits of exercise.","traps":{"a":"Creating barriers would lower participation, the reverse of what the stem describes. In the Health Belief Model (1950s), perceived barriers such as lack of time, cost, fatigue or fear of injury are exactly what reduce health behaviour, and good advice aims to lower them. It tempts only candidates who recognise 'barriers' as a model term and attach it to the wrong effect.","b":"Discouraging people from physical activity contradicts both the content of medical advice and the observed rise in participation. Doctors may restrict exercise for a few medical conditions, but recommendations as a whole push people toward activity. This option is a reversal distractor. It tempts only through careless reading, since the stem already says that participation rose.","d":"'None of the above' is wrong because option c states the actual mechanism, greater awareness of the benefits of exercise. This catch-all is right only when every listed option fails. It tempts candidates who think awareness is too simple an explanation, knowing that knowledge alone rarely changes behaviour, and so reject all the listed options."}},{"q":"What eating disorder involves cycles of binge eating and purging through various methods?","concept":"Bulimia nervosa was first described as a separate syndrome by Gerald Russell (1979), who called it an ominous variant of anorexia nervosa. DSM-III (1980) listed 'bulimia', and DSM-III-R (1987) renamed it bulimia nervosa. In DSM-5 (2013) the diagnosis needs: recurrent binge eating, meaning eating within a discrete period an amount clearly larger than most people would, with a sense of loss of control; recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviour to prevent weight gain, such as self-induced vomiting, misuse of laxatives, diuretics or other medicines, fasting or excessive exercise; both occurring on average at least once a week for three months; self-evaluation unduly influenced by body shape and weight; and the episodes not occurring only during anorexia nervosa. Severity is rated by compensatory episodes per week: mild 1 to 3, moderate 4 to 7, severe 8 to 13, extreme 14 or more. Body weight is usually normal or above, which separates it from anorexia. Signs of repeated vomiting include Russell's sign (calluses on the knuckles), swollen parotid glands, dental enamel erosion and low potassium. Three neighbours build variants. Anorexia nervosa needs restriction leading to significantly low body weight, fear of weight gain and body image disturbance; its binge-eating/purging subtype can purge, but low weight decides the diagnosis. Binge-eating disorder has binges with distress but no regular compensation. Purging disorder, under other specified feeding or eating disorders, has purging without binges. Option b is right because recurrent cycles of binge eating followed by purging define bulimia nervosa.","traps":{"a":"Anorexia nervosa is defined by restriction of energy intake leading to significantly low body weight, an intense fear of gaining weight, and a disturbed experience of body shape (DSM-5, 2013). It has a binge-eating/purging subtype, so purging can occur, but the defining feature is low weight, not a binge and purge cycle. It tempts because the two disorders share body image concerns and can shade into each other.","c":"Binge-eating disorder, a full DSM-5 (2013) diagnosis, involves recurrent binges with loss of control and marked distress, at least once a week for three months, but without regular compensatory behaviour such as vomiting or laxatives. Many sufferers are overweight. It tempts because 'binge eating' is in its name and the stem begins with bingeing, yet it fails the purging half of the stem.","d":"'None of the above' is wrong because bulimia nervosa exactly matches cycles of binge eating and purging. It might tempt a candidate who notices that the option says 'Bulimia' rather than the full 'bulimia nervosa', or who knows that purging also appears in anorexia, and so doubts every option. The short name still refers to the same disorder."}},{"q":"According to Guthrie, which of the following is NOT improvable by practice?","concept":"Edwin R. Guthrie set out the simplest learning theory of the behaviourist era in The Psychology of Learning (1935). It rests on one law of contiguity: a combination of stimuli that has accompanied a movement will, on its recurrence, tend to be followed by that movement. No reinforcement is needed; pairing in time is enough. From this follows one-trial learning: the stimulus-movement bond reaches full strength on its first pairing. Practice does not strengthen a bond, and reward matters only because it changes the situation, so the last response made is preserved (the recency principle). To explain why performance still improves with practice, Guthrie separated three levels. Movements are specific muscle contractions, learned completely in one trial. Acts are classes of movements defined by their outcome, such as opening a door or typing a word, which can be achieved by many different movements in many situations. Skills are collections of acts, such as driving or playing cricket. Acts and skills improve with practice because each repetition attaches the needed movements to more of the stimulus combinations in which they must occur. Guthrie and George Horton (1946) supported this with cats escaping a puzzle box, whose escape movements became highly stereotyped. William Estes (1950) later formalised the idea in stimulus sampling theory. Contrast incremental theories: the law of effect of Edward Thorndike (1911) and the habit strength of Clark Hull (1943) both grow gradually with reinforced trials. Option c is right because Guthrie held that movements are learned fully in one trial, so practice cannot improve them.","traps":{"a":"Skills, in the scheme of Edwin Guthrie (1935), are large collections of acts, such as playing a musical instrument or driving a car. Because a skill must work across a huge range of stimulus situations, practice keeps attaching the right movements to new cues, so skills clearly improve with practice. It tempts because 'skill' sounds like a fixed, already learned unit.","b":"Acts are outcome-defined classes of movements, such as catching a ball or writing a name. The same act can be done with different movements in different situations, so practice improves it by connecting the right movement to each situation. Guthrie used this to reconcile one-trial learning with gradual learning curves. It tempts because acts sit close to movements in his hierarchy.","d":"'None of the above' would mean that everything improves with practice. That is the incremental view of Edward Thorndike (1911) and Clark Hull (1943), in which each reinforced trial strengthens a bond. Guthrie rejected it for movements, which are learned completely in one pairing. It tempts candidates who apply the everyday rule that practice improves everything."}},{"q":"The view of 'observational learning' where reinforcement is not required for learning a behaviour is given by","concept":"Observational learning, or modelling, is learning by watching another person (the model) and the consequences of their behaviour. Albert Bandura developed it into social learning theory (1977) and later social cognitive theory (1986). His key claim is that acquisition and performance are separate. A person can learn a behaviour simply by observing it, with no reinforcement for themselves; reinforcement, direct or vicarious, only affects whether the learned behaviour is later performed. The Bobo doll studies show this. Bandura, Dorothea Ross and Sheila Ross (1961) found that children who watched an adult attack an inflatable doll copied the specific aggressive acts. In Bandura (1965), children saw the model rewarded, punished or given no consequence. Those who saw punishment imitated less, but when every child was then offered an incentive to show what they remembered, all groups reproduced the behaviour about equally. Learning had happened without reinforcement. Bandura listed four processes: attention, retention, motor reproduction and motivation. Related ideas include vicarious reinforcement (seeing a model rewarded raises our tendency to copy) and self-efficacy (1977). Compare the neighbours. Neal Miller and John Dollard (1941), in Social Learning and Imitation, explained imitation by drive reduction: imitating was learned because it was reinforced. B. F. Skinner (1938) treated learning as shaped by reinforcement of the learner's own responses. Edward Tolman (1930) also showed learning without reward, but in latent maze learning, not imitation of a model. Option b is right because Bandura held that observational learning occurs without reinforcement, which affects performance rather than acquisition.","traps":{"a":"B. F. Skinner (1938, 1953) explained behaviour through operant conditioning, in which a response is learned because it is followed by reinforcement. In his view imitation is just another operant, strengthened when copying is rewarded. That is the opposite of reinforcement-free learning. It tempts because Skinner is the default name for any question that mentions reinforcement.","c":"Edward Tolman, with Charles Honzik (1930), showed latent learning: rats explored a maze without reward and formed a cognitive map, revealed only when food was introduced. That is also learning without reinforcement, but it comes from the animal's own exploration, not from watching a model. It tempts because 'no reinforcement needed' is Tolman's signature idea too.","d":"Neal Miller and John Dollard (1941) offered the first social learning theory, but in Hullian drive-reduction terms: imitation (matched-dependent behaviour) is learned because the imitator is reinforced for copying. So reinforcement is required. Bandura's departure from them is exactly this point. It tempts because their book title, Social Learning and Imitation, sounds like the right answer."}},{"q":"The sudden increase in performance of an organism upon reinforcement, despite never being reinforced in the previous trials is _____________","concept":"Latent learning is learning that takes place without reinforcement and stays hidden (latent) until an incentive gives the organism a reason to show it. Early evidence came from Hugh Blodgett (1929), and the classic study is Edward Tolman and Charles Honzik (1930). Three groups of rats ran a complex maze once a day. One group found food in the goal box every day; one never found food; the third found none for the first ten days and food from day eleven. The never-rewarded rats improved little, as did the third group before food appeared. Yet once rewarded, that group's errors dropped sharply on the very next day, matching or even beating the always-rewarded rats. They must have learned the maze layout during the unrewarded trials. Tolman (1948) called this internal representation a cognitive map, and the study supports the distinction between learning and performance: reinforcement drives performance, not learning itself. This challenged Clark Hull (1943) and others who held reinforcement necessary for learning. The signature in a stem is a sudden jump in performance once reward is introduced, after earlier unrewarded trials. Separate it from insight learning (Wolfgang Kohler, 1917), a sudden solution through restructuring a problem, and from the Crespi effect (Leo Crespi, 1942), a change in running speed when the size of an existing reward is shifted. The observational learning of Albert Bandura (1977) also separates learning from performance, but it involves a model. Option d is right because a sudden improvement once reinforcement arrives, after unrewarded trials, is the defining sign of latent learning.","traps":{"a":"Discrimination learning is learning to respond to one stimulus and not to another, because responses to one are reinforced and responses to the other are not; Ivan Pavlov (1927) showed it with dogs that learned to separate similar tones. It depends on differential reinforcement throughout, not on learning that appears only once reward begins. It tempts as a standard term from the same chapter.","b":"Vicarious reinforcement, from Albert Bandura (1965), is the effect of seeing a model rewarded: the observer becomes more likely to perform the modelled behaviour. The stem describes the organism's own trials and its own first reward, with no model at all. It tempts because it also involves reinforcement changing performance, and Bandura's work is famous for separating learning from performance.","c":"Observational learning, in the social learning theory of Albert Bandura (1977), is learning by watching a model, as in the Bobo doll studies. It shares the idea that learning can occur without reinforcement, but it needs another individual to observe. The stem describes a single organism's unrewarded practice followed by a sudden jump when rewarded. It tempts because both concepts separate learning from performance."}},{"q":"According to Skinner, the response rate _________ after extinction","concept":"In operant conditioning, as set out by B. F. Skinner (1938) in The Behavior of Organisms, extinction is the procedure of withholding reinforcement for a response that was previously reinforced. Before conditioning, every response already has a baseline frequency called the operant level: a rat in a Skinner box presses the lever now and then by chance. Reinforcement raises the rate far above that level. When reinforcement stops, the rate declines, often after a brief extinction burst (a temporary rise in rate and variability, sometimes with emotional behaviour), and eventually returns to roughly the operant level, its earlier pre-conditioning rate. On a cumulative record this appears as a curve that gradually flattens. Extinction is not the same as forgetting or erasing the response. Spontaneous recovery, first noted by Ivan Pavlov (1927) in classical conditioning, shows that responding can reappear after a rest. Work on renewal by Mark Bouton (2004) shows that an extinguished response can return when the context changes, which suggests extinction is new learning layered over the old. Extinction is also slower after intermittent than after continuous reinforcement, the partial reinforcement extinction effect, first shown by Lloyd Humphreys (1939). Contrast punishment, which adds an aversive consequence to suppress behaviour, and negative reinforcement, which strengthens behaviour by removing something unpleasant. Option c is right because Skinner described extinction as bringing the response rate back down to its earlier, pre-conditioning operant level, not to zero.","traps":{"a":"An increase in responding happens only briefly at the start of extinction, the extinction burst, when the expected reinforcer fails to arrive, as when people press a lift button repeatedly. It is a transient reaction, not the outcome of extinction. Over time the rate falls. It tempts because candidates who know about the burst mistake it for the end state.","b":"If the response rate stayed the same, reinforcement would not be controlling the behaviour at all. In the data of B. F. Skinner (1938), rate falls steadily once reinforcement stops, faster after continuous reinforcement and more slowly after partial reinforcement. Persistence after partial reinforcement can look like 'staying the same' in the short run, which is why this option tempts.","d":"Zero responding is the popular image of extinction, and many introductory books say the behaviour 'disappears'. Skinner's point was that the rate returns to the operant level, the low chance rate that existed before conditioning, since the organism emitted the response occasionally even then. Spontaneous recovery also shows the response is not erased. It tempts as the textbook shorthand."}},{"q":"The allocation policy in Kahneman's model includes","concept":"Daniel Kahneman (1973), in his book Attention and Effort, proposed the capacity or resource model of attention. Earlier filter models, from Donald Broadbent (1958) to the attenuation model of Anne Treisman (1964), treated attention as a bottleneck at one stage of processing. Kahneman instead treated attention as a single limited pool of mental effort that can be spread across several tasks at once, as long as total demand does not exceed supply. The size of the pool is not fixed: it rises and falls with arousal, so a moderately aroused person has more capacity than a drowsy one, while very high arousal impairs control. Capacity is distributed by the allocation policy, which has three inputs. Enduring dispositions are involuntary, long-standing rules of attention, such as automatic orienting to a sudden movement, a loud noise or one's own name. Momentary intentions are current, deliberate goals, such as 'listen to the left ear'. Evaluation of demands on capacity is a running check of how much effort each activity needs; if demand is too high, one task is dropped or done poorly, and rising demand also feeds back to raise arousal. Arousal mainly sets the total supply rather than being one of the three listed inputs. The model explains dual-task interference and why effort shows up as pupil dilation. Christopher Wickens (1984) later proposed multiple resource pools for different modalities, a common contrast question. Option d is right because Kahneman's allocation policy is governed by enduring dispositions, momentary intentions and the evaluation of demands.","traps":{"a":"This option keeps 'evaluation of demands' but changes the other two terms. 'Enduring temperament' borrows a personality word, whereas Kahneman meant enduring dispositions, automatic attention rules such as orienting to novelty or to your own name. 'Momentary distractions' reverses the idea: the policy follows momentary intentions, which are deliberate current goals, not passing distractions. It tempts because one term is exactly right and the other two sound very close.","b":"'Enduring temperament; momentary distractions; environmental demands' changes all three of Kahneman's terms. Temperament is a personality construct, distractions are what the policy guards against rather than a determinant of it, and 'environmental demands' points outward, whereas Kahneman meant the person's own evaluation of how much capacity a task requires. It tempts because each phrase sounds attention-related and the option keeps the same enduring, momentary, demands structure as the real list.","c":"This option gets 'momentary intentions' right but replaces the other two terms. 'Enduring temperament' should be enduring dispositions, and 'environmental demands' should be the evaluation of demands on capacity, an internal appraisal of the effort a task needs rather than a description of the surroundings. It tempts because it contains the correct middle term, which candidates often remember most clearly, and the substitutes look like harmless synonyms."}},{"q":"An idealized representative object of a category of objects is","concept":"Concepts let us treat different objects as members of one category. The classical view, going back to Aristotle, said a category is defined by necessary and sufficient features: a bachelor is an unmarried adult male. Eleanor Rosch (1973, 1975) showed that natural categories do not work like that. Membership is graded: people rate a robin as a better example of 'bird' than a penguin or an ostrich, verify 'a robin is a bird' faster, and list it first. Rosch explained this with the prototype, an idealised, abstract representative of the category that captures its central tendency, the average or most typical combination of features. A new item is categorised by its similarity to the prototype; typical members share many features with other members, which Rosch and Carolyn Mervis (1975) called family resemblance, a term borrowed from Ludwig Wittgenstein (1953). The prototype need not be any real member you have seen. A rival is exemplar theory (Douglas Medin and Marguerite Schaffer, 1978), which says we store many concrete instances and compare new items with all of them. Rosch also described levels of categories: superordinate (furniture), basic (chair) and subordinate (kitchen chair), with the basic level learned first and used most. In pattern recognition the same word appears beside templates, features and geons, which is how examiners build distractors: a template is an exact stored copy, a feature is one component, a geon is a three-dimensional building block. Option a is right because an idealised representative of a category, its best example, is the definition of a prototype.","traps":{"b":"A template is a stored, exact copy of a pattern. Template-matching theory says we recognise input by checking it against these copies for an exact fit, like a machine reading the printed numbers on a cheque. It fails because one object appears in endless sizes, angles and handwriting styles, which would need countless templates. A template is literal and specific; a prototype is abstract and averaged. It tempts because both are stored mental standards used for comparison.","c":"A feature is a single component attribute, such as a horizontal line, a curve or an angle. Feature-analysis models, such as the Pandemonium model of Oliver Selfridge (1959), recognise patterns by detecting features, supported by the feature-detector cells found by David Hubel and Torsten Wiesel (1959, 1962). A feature is a part, not a whole representative of a category. It tempts because a prototype is described as the most typical combination of features.","d":"Geons (geometric ions) are about 36 simple three-dimensional shapes, such as cylinders, cones, blocks and wedges, proposed by Irving Biederman (1987) in recognition-by-components theory. Objects are recognised by identifying their geons and how they are joined, much as letters combine into words. Geons are building blocks of individual object shapes, not idealised representatives of a category. It tempts because recognition by components sits in the same object-recognition chapter as prototypes."}},{"q":"In which of the following cases, would the sensitivity 'd'  be highest in a signal detection task?","concept":"Signal detection theory (SDT), brought into psychology by Wilson Tanner and John Swets (1954) and set out fully by David Green and John Swets (1966), separates two things that classical threshold methods mixed together: how well an observer can tell a signal from noise (sensitivity) and how willing they are to say 'yes' (response bias or criterion). On each trial the signal is present or absent and the observer says yes or no, giving four outcomes: hit (present, yes), miss (present, no), false alarm (absent, yes) and correct rejection (absent, no). The theory pictures two overlapping normal distributions of internal activity, one for noise alone and one for signal plus noise. Sensitivity, d' (d-prime), is the distance between their means in standard-deviation units, computed as z(hit rate) minus z(false-alarm rate). When hits greatly exceed false alarms, the distributions are far apart and d' is large. When hits equal false alarms, d' is zero: the observer is guessing. When false alarms exceed hits, d' is negative, which suggests confusion or reversed responding. Bias is a separate quantity, beta or c: a liberal observer says yes often, raising both hits and false alarms, so d' can stay unchanged. Examiners also ask about the ROC (receiver operating characteristic) curve, which plots hit rate against false-alarm rate; the more it bows toward the top left, the greater the sensitivity, while the diagonal means d' of zero. Option c is right because, with equal numbers of signal and noise trials, d' is positive and grows only when hits exceed false alarms.","traps":{"a":"If false alarms are slightly more than hits, the false-alarm rate is a little above the hit rate, so z(hit rate) minus z(false-alarm rate) is slightly negative. The observer is doing no better than guessing, and d' is near zero or just below it. It tempts because 'slightly' makes the difference sound trivial, and candidates who equate lots of 'yes' responding with alertness may think the observer is detecting well.","b":"When false alarms exceed hits, the observer says 'yes' more often on noise-only trials than on signal trials. That gives a negative d', meaning performance is worse than chance, usually from misunderstanding the task or reversing responses. Sensitivity is lowest here, not highest. It tempts because a high false-alarm count can look like an eager, responsive observer, but that eagerness reflects a liberal criterion (bias), not sensitivity.","d":"When hits equal false alarms, the hit rate equals the false-alarm rate, the two internal distributions overlap completely, and d' equals zero. The point lies on the diagonal of the ROC curve: the observer cannot tell signal from noise at all. It tempts because equality sounds balanced or ideal, and candidates may confuse a neutral response bias with good sensitivity, although bias and sensitivity are separate measures in SDT."}},{"q":"A Prototype is a/an _________ and ________ member of a category","concept":"This item tests the two defining properties of a prototype in the prototype theory of categorisation of Eleanor Rosch (1975). First, a prototype is abstract: it is a mental summary built from experience, not a specific concrete object you have met. Your prototype of 'dog' is not your neighbour's dog, and it may not match any real dog at all. Second, it is averaged: it represents the central tendency of the category, the most typical value on each feature, formed by pooling across the members encountered. Classic evidence comes from Michael Posner and Steven Keele (1968), who showed people distorted versions of dot patterns; later, participants classified the never-seen undistorted prototype as accurately as the patterns they had actually studied. That is only possible if the mind abstracted an average. Prototype theory predicts typicality effects: items close to the average (a robin for bird) are judged faster and more confidently than atypical items (a penguin). Contrast this with exemplar theory (Douglas Medin and Marguerite Schaffer, 1978), in which categories are stored as many concrete individual instances and their variability is kept rather than averaged away; exemplar models explain why we know that some categories are more variable than others. The classical definitional view has neither, only necessary and sufficient rules. So when a question pairs abstract versus concrete with averaged versus varied, abstract and averaged describes the prototype, while concrete and varied describes stored exemplars. Option d is right because a prototype is an abstract and averaged representation of the members of a category.","traps":{"a":"'Abstract; varied' gets the first half right, since a prototype is abstract, but 'varied' is wrong. A prototype compresses the variability among members into a single central-tendency summary; it does not preserve the variety. Keeping the spread of individual instances is a feature of the exemplar theory of Douglas Medin and Marguerite Schaffer (1978). It tempts because categories themselves are varied, so candidates attach the word to the representation instead of to the members.","b":"'Concrete; averaged' gets 'averaged' right but 'concrete' wrong. A prototype is not a particular, physically experienced member; it is an abstraction that may correspond to no real object, as the never-seen dot-pattern prototypes of Michael Posner and Steven Keele (1968) showed. It tempts because people often name a concrete best example, such as a robin, when asked for a typical bird, which blurs a typical exemplar with the abstract prototype.","c":"'Concrete; varied' describes the exemplar view, not prototype theory. In the exemplar approach of Douglas Medin and Marguerite Schaffer (1978), memory holds many specific, concrete instances along with their full variability, and a new item is compared with all of them. It tempts because it is the exact mirror image of the correct answer, so candidates who mix up prototype theory and exemplar theory choose it."}},{"q":"For perception of a contour, _________ and __________ are required","concept":"A contour is the boundary we see where one region of the visual field changes abruptly into another in brightness, colour or texture. Contours give objects their shape and allow figure-ground segregation, described by Edgar Rubin (1915). Without contours, form vanishes: in a Ganzfeld, a completely uniform field studied by Wolfgang Metzger (1930), observers see only a featureless fog. Two stimulus conditions must be met for a contour to register. First, the difference in stimulation must reach a minimal intensity, so the change crosses the detection threshold. The retina helps through lateral inhibition, which sharpens edges and produces Mach bands, described by Ernst Mach (1865). Second, the stimulus must be exposed for a minimum time, because contour formation takes time. Heinz Werner (1935) showed this with metacontrast masking: a black disc was briefly flashed and then followed by a ring whose inner edge matched the disc's outline, and the disc often was not seen, because the later contour disrupted the one still forming. The trade-off between intensity and duration is expressed in Bloch's law (Adolphe-Moise Bloch, 1885): for flashes shorter than about a tenth of a second, threshold depends on intensity multiplied by duration, so a weaker stimulus needs a longer exposure. Examiners contrast this with reaction time, which is the speed of the observer's response, not a property of the stimulus, and with illusory contours, such as the triangle of Gaetano Kanizsa (1955), seen where no physical edge exists. Option a is right because contour perception needs both a minimal intensity of stimulation and a minimum exposure time.","traps":{"b":"This option keeps the correct first condition, minimal stimulus intensity, but replaces exposure time with reaction time. Reaction time is the interval between stimulus onset and the observer's response, measured since Franciscus Donders (1868) with his subtraction method. It describes how fast a person responds, not what a stimulus needs in order to be seen, so it cannot be a condition for perceiving an edge. It tempts because it is half right.","c":"Minimum exposure time is a genuine requirement, but this option drops stimulus intensity and adds reaction time. A very faint edge would not be seen however long it was shown, so intensity cannot be left out, and reaction time is a measure of the response, not a condition of the stimulus. It tempts because exposure time is correct and the word 'time' appears twice, which makes the pair look like a matched set.","d":"Neither term is a requirement for contour perception. Reaction time is the speed of the observer's response, and movement is not needed because stationary edges are seen perfectly well. Movement matters for motion perception, as in the phi phenomenon of Max Wertheimer (1912), not for seeing a boundary. It tempts because 'minimum movement' sounds like the tiny eye movements discussed in the same perception chapter, and 'minimum' echoes the wording of the stem."}},{"q":"the theory which assumes that perceiving an object is by due to 'geons' is","concept":"Recognition by components (RBC) is the theory of object recognition proposed by Irving Biederman (1987). It says that the visual system recognises a three-dimensional object by breaking it into a small set of simple volumetric parts called geons, short for geometric ions. Biederman proposed about 36 geons, such as cylinders, cones, bricks and wedges, defined by non-accidental properties: features of edges that stay the same from almost any viewpoint, such as straightness, curvature, parallelism, symmetry and edges meeting at a vertex. A mug is a cylinder with a curved handle-shaped geon on its side; a pail is the same two geons with the handle attached on top. The arrangement matters as much as the parts, just as a few letters form many words. RBC explains viewpoint invariance: we recognise objects from new angles because the geons remain identifiable. In Biederman's degraded-picture studies, line drawings with segments deleted from the middle of contours were still recognised, but when the deletions removed the vertices and junctions that define geons, recognition failed. The main criticism is that RBC handles basic-level recognition (a chair) better than fine distinctions within a category (one face versus another), and it struggles with the view-dependent effects reported by Michael Tarr and Steven Pinker (1989). Examiners pair RBC with older pattern-recognition models: template matching (exact stored copies), feature analysis such as the Pandemonium model of Oliver Selfridge (1959), and prototype theory (Eleanor Rosch, 1975). Option b is right because recognition by components is the theory that explains object perception through geons.","traps":{"a":"The Pandemonium model of Oliver Selfridge (1959) is a feature-analysis model of pattern recognition, usually explained with letters. Image demons record the input, feature demons detect lines and angles, cognitive demons 'shout' for the letters matching those features, and a decision demon picks the loudest. It works on two-dimensional features, not three-dimensional volumetric parts. It tempts because it is also a parts-based model taught in the same chapter as geons.","c":"The prototype model of Eleanor Rosch (1975) recognises an item by comparing it with an abstract, averaged best example of its category, so a robin is recognised as a bird because it is close to the bird prototype. It is a theory of categorisation by similarity to a central tendency, not of building objects out of geons. It tempts because both theories explain how we identify objects as members of a type.","d":"Template matching proposes that the mind stores exact copies of patterns and recognises input when it fits one, as a machine reads the numbers printed on cheques. Its well-known weakness is that it needs a separate template for every size, orientation and style of an object. RBC was partly a solution to this problem, since a few geons combine to describe countless objects. It tempts because it is the most familiar recognition theory."}},{"q":"Which of the following depth perception cues requires both eyes?","concept":"Depth perception means seeing the world in three dimensions and judging distance, although each retinal image is flat. Cues are grouped as binocular (needing both eyes) or monocular (available to one eye). There are two binocular cues. Retinal or binocular disparity is the slight difference between the images on the two retinas, because the eyes are about six centimetres apart; the brain fuses them into one image with depth, a process called stereopsis, first demonstrated with the stereoscope of Charles Wheatstone (1838). Convergence is the inward turning of both eyes to fixate a near object; the brain uses the feedback from the eye muscles (an oculomotor, kinaesthetic cue) to judge distance, and it is useful only up to a few metres. Monocular cues include the oculomotor cue of accommodation, the change in the curvature of the lens to focus on near objects; pictorial cues, such as linear perspective, relative size, interposition (occlusion), texture gradient (James J. Gibson, 1950), aerial perspective, shading and height in the visual field; and movement-produced cues, such as motion parallax. A frequent trap is that convergence and accommodation are both oculomotor cues sensed through muscle feedback, so they are learned as a pair, yet accommodation happens within a single eye. Another variant asks which cues artists use: pictorial cues, because they work on a flat canvas. Option c is right because convergence depends on the angle between the lines of sight of the two eyes, so it requires both eyes.","traps":{"a":"Motion parallax is a monocular, movement-produced cue. When you move, for example while looking out of a train window, near objects seem to sweep past quickly in the opposite direction while distant objects move slowly or seem to travel with you. It works perfectly well with one eye closed. It tempts because 'parallax' sounds like a difference between two viewpoints, which is how binocular disparity is often described.","b":"Aerial or atmospheric perspective is a monocular pictorial cue: distant objects look hazier, less detailed and bluer because light is scattered by the air, dust and moisture between them and the viewer. Painters use it on a flat canvas, which shows that it needs only one eye. It tempts because the word 'perspective' sounds technical, and candidates may link it with stereoscopic viewing.","d":"Accommodation is a monocular oculomotor cue: the ciliary muscles change the curvature of the lens to focus on near or far objects, and the muscle feedback signals distance. It occurs within each eye separately and is useful only at short range. It tempts because it is taught alongside convergence as a pair of muscle-based cues, so candidates assume both of them need two eyes."}},{"q":"The process of conversion of stimulus input into electrical signal by the receptors is called","concept":"Sensation begins when a physical stimulus reaches a sense organ, but the brain cannot use light, sound waves or pressure directly; it works only with electrochemical neural signals. Transduction is the conversion of stimulus energy into these signals by specialised receptor cells. In the eye, rods and cones contain photopigments (rhodopsin in rods) that change shape when they absorb light, altering the receptor's membrane potential. In the ear, hair cells in the cochlea bend as the basilar membrane vibrates, opening ion channels. In the skin, mechanoreceptors such as Pacinian corpuscles respond to pressure and vibration; in taste and smell, chemoreceptors respond to molecules. The receptor first produces a graded receptor (generator) potential, which, if large enough, triggers action potentials in the sensory neurons that carry the message to the brain. Transduction is one link in a chain: detection by receptors, transduction, then coding, the way features such as intensity and pitch are represented in the rate and pattern of neural firing. Examiners pair it with neighbouring terms: sensation (detecting and encoding stimuli) versus perception (organising and interpreting them); the absolute threshold and the difference threshold, studied by Ernst Weber (1834) and Gustav Fechner (1860); and sensory adaptation, the reduced response to an unchanging stimulus. Distractors are usually words that sound similar, so learn the exact technical meaning of each. Option a is right because transduction is precisely the conversion of stimulus input into electrical signals by the receptors.","traps":{"b":"Transference is a psychoanalytic concept from Sigmund Freud, discussed in his paper 'The Dynamics of Transference' (1912): the client redirects feelings and attitudes from important early figures, such as parents, onto the therapist. Countertransference is the therapist's emotional reaction to the client. It belongs to psychotherapy, not to sensory physiology. It tempts because it begins with 'trans' and means carrying something across, which sounds like passing a signal along.","c":"Transformation means a change of form in general. In psychology it is best known from the transformational grammar of Noam Chomsky (1957), in which rules convert one sentence structure into another, such as active into passive, and in statistics, where raw scores are transformed into z scores. It is not the technical name for receptor conversion of energy. It tempts because transduction does involve changing one form of energy into another.","d":"Transfusion is a medical term for transferring blood or blood products from a donor into a patient's circulation. It has no meaning in sensory psychology at all. It tempts only because it shares the prefix 'trans' with the correct answer, a common trick in vocabulary-based items, so a candidate relying on a vague memory of 'trans-something' may pick it."}},{"q":"_________ are tiny rapid movements of the eyes to ensure ___________","concept":"Our eyes are never completely still. Saccades are fast, jerky, ballistic movements that jump the gaze from one point to another, about three or four times a second while reading or scanning a scene, and vision is suppressed during each jump (saccadic suppression). Between saccades the eyes rest in fixations, which last a few hundred milliseconds and are when most visual information is taken in. Even during fixation, the eyes make tiny involuntary movements: microsaccades, slow drift and high-frequency tremor, together called fixational eye movements or physiological nystagmus. Their job is to keep the retinal image moving slightly. This matters because receptors show sensory adaptation: a perfectly unchanging stimulus produces a declining response. Stabilised-image experiments, such as those of Lorrin Riggs and colleagues (1953) and Roy Pritchard (1961), used contact-lens devices so that the image moved with the eye and stayed on the same receptors. The image soon faded and disappeared, often in meaningful parts. Troxler fading, described by Ignaz Troxler (1804), is a related effect: a faint peripheral spot fades away when the gaze is held steady. So tiny rapid eye movements prevent adaptation and keep perception constant. Other eye movements used as distractors are smooth pursuit (tracking a moving target), vergence (convergence and divergence for depth) and the vestibulo-ocular reflex (stabilising gaze when the head moves). Option a is right because saccades, in their tiny form as microsaccades, are rapid eye movements that refresh the retinal image so that perception remains constant instead of fading through adaptation.","traps":{"b":"Fixation is the pause between saccades, when the gaze is held on one point for roughly a quarter of a second and most visual information is taken in. It is a relatively stable state, not a rapid movement, so it cannot fill the first blank. The tiny movements that occur during fixation are microsaccades, drift and tremor. It tempts because the second half of the option, constant perception, is correct.","c":"Saccades is correct for the first blank, but sensory adaptation is the wrong purpose. Sensory adaptation is the decline in receptor response to an unchanging stimulus, as when you stop noticing a smell after a few minutes. Tiny eye movements exist to prevent adaptation; stabilised retinal images fade precisely because adaptation is allowed to happen. It tempts because the term is closely tied to the correct explanation, only in reverse.","d":"This option is wrong on both counts. Fixation is the steady holding of gaze, not a rapid movement, and sensory adaptation is the fading of response to an unchanging stimulus, which eye movements work against rather than ensure. If the eyes truly fixated without any movement, adaptation would occur and the image would fade. It tempts because fixation and adaptation are both genuine terms from the same topic."}},{"q":"the component of working memory that is capable of mentally rotating an object is","concept":"Working memory is the small, active workspace in which information is held and manipulated during ongoing tasks. Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974) replaced the single short-term store of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968) with a multi-component model. The central executive is a limited-capacity attentional controller: it focuses, divides and switches attention and coordinates the subsystems, but stores nothing itself. The phonological loop holds speech-based information through a passive phonological store and an articulatory rehearsal process, the inner voice; it explains the word-length effect and the phonological similarity effect. The visuospatial sketchpad holds and manipulates visual and spatial information, the inner eye: imagining a route, counting the windows of your house, or mentally rotating a shape. Robert Logie (1995) divided it into a passive visual cache (form and colour) and an active inner scribe (spatial and movement processes). Baddeley (2000) later added the episodic buffer, which binds information from the subsystems and long-term memory into integrated episodes. Evidence for separate subsystems comes from dual-task studies: two verbal tasks interfere with each other, while a verbal and a spatial task interfere much less. Mental rotation itself was shown by Roger Shepard and Jacqueline Metzler (1971): the time taken to judge whether two three-dimensional figures were identical increased linearly with the angle between them, as if the image were turned at a steady rate. Option c is right because mentally rotating an object is visuospatial manipulation, the job of the visuospatial sketchpad.","traps":{"a":"The phonological loop is the verbal subsystem of working memory. Its phonological store holds speech-based material for about two seconds, and articulatory rehearsal refreshes it, as when you repeat a phone number to yourself. It explains the word-length effect and is disrupted by articulatory suppression, such as repeating 'the the the'. It handles sounds, not images, so it cannot rotate an object. It tempts because it is the best-known component.","b":"The central executive is the supervisory, attention-controlling component of the model of Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974). It allocates attention, switches between tasks and coordinates the phonological loop and visuospatial sketchpad, but it has no storage of its own and does not hold the image being rotated. It tempts because mental rotation is effortful and clearly needs executive control, so candidates credit the manager instead of the specialised workspace.","d":"Executive functioning is a broad neuropsychological umbrella term for frontal-lobe control processes, such as planning, inhibition, set-shifting and updating, assessed by tests like the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test. It is not a named component of the Baddeley and Hitch working memory model at all. It tempts because it sounds like the central executive, and spatial reasoning tasks sometimes appear in executive function test batteries."}},{"q":"The mnemonic where a sentence is framed using first letter of items on a list is called","concept":"Mnemonics are strategies that make material easier to remember by adding organisation, imagery or meaning. Two first-letter techniques are often confused. An acronym takes the first letter of each item and forms a single word or pronounceable string: VIBGYOR for the colours of the rainbow, or OCEAN for the Big Five personality traits. An acrostic takes the same first letters but builds a sentence or phrase in which each word begins with the cue letter: 'Every Good Boy Deserves Fruit' for the musical notes E, G, B, D, F, or 'My Very Educated Mother Just Served Us Nachos' for the planets. Acrostics are useful when the first letters do not make a word. Both work as retrieval cues and by chunking, but they help only if the items themselves are already familiar. Imagery-based mnemonics form the other family. The method of loci, credited in legend to the Greek poet Simonides of Ceos, places items at points along a familiar route. The pegword system uses a memorised rhyme (one is a bun, two is a shoe) and links each item to a peg by an image. The keyword method, studied by Richard Atkinson and Michael Raugh (1975), links a foreign word to a similar-sounding native word and an interactive image. The dual-coding theory of Allan Paivio (1971) explains why imagery helps: concrete items can be stored in both verbal and visual codes. Option b is right because a sentence built from the first letters of the items on a list is, by definition, an acrostic.","traps":{"a":"An acronym is formed when the first letters of the items create a single word or pronounceable letter string, such as VIBGYOR for the rainbow colours or OCEAN for the Big Five traits. It uses first letters exactly as the stem describes, but it produces a word, not a sentence. It tempts because acronym and acrostic both begin with 'acro' and both rely on first-letter cues.","c":"The pegword system is an imagery mnemonic. The learner first memorises a fixed rhyme of number pegs, such as one is a bun, two is a shoe, three is a tree, and then links each list item to its peg with a vivid interactive image. It suits ordered lists and allows recall by number. It uses rhymes and images, not first-letter sentences. It tempts because it is another named mnemonic for lists.","d":"The keyword method, studied by Richard Atkinson and Michael Raugh (1975), is used mainly for foreign vocabulary. The learner picks a native-language keyword that sounds like part of the foreign word and forms an image linking it to the meaning, for example linking the Spanish 'carta' (letter) to a cart carrying a letter. It relies on sound similarity and imagery, not first letters. It tempts because 'keyword' sounds like a word built from key letters."}},{"q":"In short term memory encoding is primarily ________","concept":"Encoding is the form in which information is stored. In the multi-store model of Richard Atkinson and Richard Shiffrin (1968), material passes from sensory memory to short-term memory (STM) and then to long-term memory (LTM), and each store tends to use a different code. Sensory memory keeps the raw, modality-specific form: iconic for vision and echoic for sound. STM is mainly acoustic. R. Conrad (1964) showed letters visually and found that recall errors were acoustic confusions, such as writing B for V or P for T, letters that sound alike rather than look alike. Alan Baddeley (1966) gave the classic contrast: immediate recall of word lists was much worse for acoustically similar words (cat, cap, can) than for dissimilar ones, while semantic similarity (big, large, huge) hardly mattered; when recall was delayed, testing LTM, the pattern reversed and semantic similarity caused the problems. So STM is primarily acoustic and LTM primarily semantic. These are tendencies, not absolutes: STM can hold visual codes, as when rotating a shape in the mind, and semantic codes for meaningful material. Other STM facts examiners ask about: a capacity of about seven plus or minus two items (George Miller, 1956), revised by Nelson Cowan (2001) to about four chunks; a duration of roughly 18 to 30 seconds without rehearsal, from the Brown-Peterson technique (John Brown, 1958; Lloyd and Margaret Peterson, 1959); and forgetting through decay and interference. Option a is right because the dominant code in short-term memory is acoustic, the sound of the material, as acoustic confusion errors show.","traps":{"b":"Visual encoding is used in sensory memory (iconic memory) and in the visuospatial sketchpad of working memory, and STM can hold visual images briefly. But it is not the primary code: even when letters are presented visually, R. Conrad (1964) found that people confuse letters that sound alike, not those that look alike. It tempts because many STM experiments present material visually, so candidates assume the stored code matches the input.","c":"Haptic encoding is coding through active touch, the sense we use to recognise objects by handling them. There is a brief haptic sensory store, and touch information can enter STM, but it is a minor, modality-specific code, not the primary form of short-term encoding for the verbal material used in memory research. It tempts mainly as an unfamiliar, technical-sounding term that candidates sometimes pick when unsure.","d":"Semantic encoding, storing material by its meaning, is the primary code of long-term memory. Alan Baddeley (1966) showed that semantically similar words such as big, large and huge disrupted delayed recall from LTM but barely affected immediate recall from STM. Meaning is also the deep level in the levels-of-processing framework of Fergus Craik and Robert Lockhart (1972). It tempts because meaning-based processing is linked with good memory in general."}},{"q":"Which sampling technique is most likely to result in a representative sample?","concept":"A sample is representative when its characteristics, such as age, gender, class and symptom severity, mirror those of the population it is drawn from, so that results can be generalised. Sampling methods fall into two families. In probability sampling, every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of being chosen, and selection is random. The main types are simple random sampling (lottery or random-number table), systematic sampling (every kth name after a random start), stratified random sampling (dividing the population into strata, such as urban and rural, and sampling randomly within each, often in proportion), cluster sampling (randomly selecting whole groups, such as schools or villages) and multistage sampling. Because chance alone decides who is chosen, systematic selection bias is removed, sampling error can be estimated, and inferential statistics such as confidence intervals rest on this assumption. In non-probability sampling, selection depends on the researcher's convenience or judgement, so some people have no chance of inclusion. Types include convenience or accidental sampling, purposive or judgement sampling, quota sampling (filling set numbers per subgroup, but non-randomly) and snowball sampling. These are cheaper and suit pilot work, qualitative studies and hidden populations, but they cannot guarantee representativeness. A common variant asks which method is best when the population has distinct subgroups: stratified random sampling. Another trap is quota sampling, which resembles stratified sampling but lacks random selection. Larger samples reduce random error but do not fix biased selection. Option c is right because random selection with known probabilities gives probability sampling the best chance of producing a representative sample.","traps":{"a":"Non-probability sampling is the umbrella term for methods in which selection is not random, so the probability of any person being chosen is unknown and some people have no chance at all. It includes convenience, purposive, quota and snowball sampling. These methods are practical and common in clinical research, but they are open to selection bias and do not support confident generalisation. It tempts because it is the broad category paired with the correct answer.","b":"Convenience or accidental sampling recruits whoever is easiest to reach, such as students in the researcher's own class or patients attending one hospital outpatient department on a given day. It is quick and cheap but systematically over-represents accessible, willing people, so results may not generalise. Many published psychology studies use convenience samples, which is exactly why it tempts: it is common, but common does not mean representative.","d":"Snowball sampling starts with a few participants who then refer others from their networks, so the sample grows like a rolling snowball. It is useful for hidden or hard-to-reach populations, such as drug users or sex workers, for whom no sampling frame exists. But referrals stay within connected social circles, so the sample is biased toward well-connected people. It tempts because it can produce large samples quickly."}},{"q":"According to Wallas' model of the creative process, what is the third stage?","concept":"Graham Wallas (1926), an English social psychologist and political scientist, described the creative process in his book The Art of Thought, drawing on the self-reports of famous scientists about how their discoveries came to them. He proposed four stages. Preparation is the first: consciously studying the problem, gathering information and trying solutions, usually without success. Incubation is the second: the person stops working on the problem consciously, and processing is thought to continue outside awareness. Illumination is the third: the sudden 'aha' moment when the solution appears, often unexpectedly, as in the story of Archimedes in his bath. Verification is the fourth: the idea is consciously tested, elaborated and put into usable form. Wallas also described intimation, a fringe feeling that a solution is about to arrive, as part of the shift into illumination, and some textbooks list it as a separate stage, making five; in that version illumination becomes the fourth stage, so read the options carefully. A simple mnemonic is PIIV: prepare, incubate, illuminate, verify. Related concepts used in variants include insight learning in the chimpanzee studies of Wolfgang Kohler (1925), the call for creativity research by J. P. Guilford (1950), who later distinguished divergent from convergent thinking, and functional fixedness from Karl Duncker (1945), which incubation is thought to help break by letting misleading approaches fade. Option b is right because illumination is the third of the four stages of Wallas, coming after preparation and incubation and before verification.","traps":{"a":"Incubation is the second stage in the model of Graham Wallas (1926). After intense conscious preparation, the person sets the problem aside, and unconscious processing, rest, or the fading of fixed wrong approaches is thought to prepare the ground for insight. It comes before illumination, not third. It tempts because candidates often remember incubation as the middle of the process and lose track of its exact position.","c":"Preparation is the first stage in the model of Graham Wallas (1926): defining the problem, gathering information, learning the relevant skills and making conscious attempts at a solution. It lays the groundwork without which incubation and illumination cannot happen, since insight comes to a prepared mind. It tempts because some candidates start counting from incubation, forgetting that preparation is the opening stage rather than a later one.","d":"Verification is the fourth and final stage in the model of Graham Wallas (1926): the insight is checked, tested, refined and put into communicable form, for example by proving a theorem or building a working version of an invention. It is conscious and effortful, like preparation. It tempts because candidates who misremember the order and put the 'aha' moment at the very end place verification third."}},{"q":"According to Piaget, how does cognitive development occur?","concept":"Jean Piaget (1936), the Swiss developmental psychologist, held a constructivist and interactionist view: children actively build their own understanding, and development comes from the interaction between biological maturation and experience with the environment. He named four factors: maturation of the nervous system, which sets the limits of what is possible at each age; experience, both physical (acting on objects) and logico-mathematical (reflecting on one's own actions); social transmission, meaning teaching and language; and equilibration, the self-regulating process that coordinates the other three. The mechanism works through schemas, organised patterns of action or thought. Assimilation fits new experience into an existing schema; accommodation changes the schema when the experience does not fit. A mismatch creates disequilibrium, which motivates the child to restore equilibrium at a higher level. This produces four invariant stages: sensorimotor (0 to 2 years), preoperational (2 to 7), concrete operational (7 to 11) and formal operational (11 onwards). Neighbouring theories supply the distractors. Arnold Gesell (1928) stressed maturation alone, development unfolding on a biological timetable. Strict behaviourists such as John B. Watson (1913) and B. F. Skinner (1938) stressed learning alone. Lev Vygotsky, whose work appeared in English as Mind in Society (1978), stressed social interaction and culture, with the zone of proximal development and scaffolding, a term introduced by David Wood, Jerome Bruner and Gail Ross (1976). Piaget saw children as little scientists working largely on their own; Vygotsky saw them as apprentices. Option c is right because Piaget explained cognitive development as the product of maturation and learning through experience together, with neither acting alone.","traps":{"a":"'Solely through maturation' is the maturationist position of Arnold Gesell (1928), who held that development unfolds on a genetic timetable with little role for experience. Piaget did think maturation sets limits on what a child can do at each stage, but he insisted that the child's own activity and experience are essential. It tempts because Piaget's fixed, age-linked sequence of stages looks biologically driven.","b":"'Solely through learning' is the strict empiricist or behaviourist view of John B. Watson (1913) and B. F. Skinner (1938): the child is shaped by conditioning and reinforcement, and development is the accumulation of learned responses. Piaget rejected this, arguing that children can learn only what their current cognitive structures allow, so maturation and readiness matter. It tempts because Piaget gave experience a central role, which can sound like learning theory.","d":"Development through socialisation and cultural practices is the sociocultural theory of Lev Vygotsky, published in English as Mind in Society (1978). He argued that higher mental functions begin as social interaction and are then internalised, with learning guided by more skilled others within the zone of proximal development. Piaget listed social transmission as one factor but saw it as secondary to the child's own activity. It tempts because both theorists are constructivists."}},{"q":"What are phonemes?","concept":"Psycholinguistics studies language at several levels, and examiners test whether you can match each term to its level. Phonology is the sound system. A phoneme is the smallest unit of sound that can change meaning in a particular language: /b/ and /p/ are different phonemes in English because 'bat' and 'pat' are different words. Phonemes have no meaning of their own. English has roughly 40 to 45 phonemes, and each language uses its own set; Japanese speakers, for example, do not treat English /r/ and /l/ as separate phonemes. Infants can at first discriminate speech sounds from all languages but by about 10 to 12 months tune in to the phonemes of their native language, as shown by Janet Werker and Richard Tees (1984). Morphology deals with morphemes, the smallest units that carry meaning: 'unhappiness' has three (un, happy, ness), and grammatical endings such as the plural -s are bound morphemes. Syntax is the set of rules for combining words and grammatical markers into sentences; Noam Chomsky (1957) made it central to linguistics. Semantics concerns the meaning of words and sentences. Pragmatics covers the social rules of language use, such as taking turns, politeness and adjusting speech to the listener, and it varies across cultures. A useful ladder: phonemes combine into morphemes, morphemes into words, and words into phrases and sentences under syntax, all used socially under pragmatics. Option b is right because phonemes are the basic units of sound used in a spoken language.","traps":{"a":"The smallest meaningful units of language are morphemes, not phonemes. A free morpheme can stand alone as a word, such as 'book', while a bound morpheme must attach to another, such as the plural -s or the prefix un-. 'Books' therefore has two morphemes but four phonemes. It tempts because both definitions use the word 'smallest', and candidates mix up the smallest sound unit with the smallest meaningful unit.","c":"Rules for combining words and grammatical markers into meaningful sentences define syntax, the grammar of word order and sentence structure. Syntax explains why 'the dog bit the man' and 'the man bit the dog' mean different things although they contain the same words. Noam Chomsky (1957) made syntax central to modern linguistics. It tempts because it is a correct definition from the same language chapter, only for a different level.","d":"Culturally specific rules for how language is used in social contexts define pragmatics: knowing how to take turns, be polite, make requests indirectly and adjust speech for a child versus an elder. Pragmatic deficits are prominent in autism spectrum disorder and define social (pragmatic) communication disorder in DSM-5 (2013). It tempts because it is a valid language definition placed beside the correct answer to test level matching."}},{"q":"which of the following F ratio values will always be insignificant despite the degrees of freedom","concept":"Analysis of variance (ANOVA), popularised by Ronald A. Fisher (1925), tests whether two or more group means differ; the letter F was named in his honour by George Snedecor (1934). ANOVA splits total variability into between-groups variance, reflecting any treatment effect plus chance, and within-groups variance, reflecting chance or error alone. Each is turned into a mean square by dividing its sum of squares by its degrees of freedom. The F ratio is mean square between divided by mean square within. If the null hypothesis is true, both mean squares estimate the same error variance, so F should be close to 1. A real effect inflates the numerator, so F rises above 1. Significance is judged by comparing the obtained F with the critical F from tables for the given numerator and denominator degrees of freedom. Because the test looks only at the upper tail and the critical value at conventional levels is always greater than 1, an F below 1 can never be significant, whatever the degrees of freedom. An F below 1 simply means that group differences are smaller than the variation within groups. Whether F values above 1, such as 1.2, 1.55 or 2, are significant depends on the degrees of freedom and alpha: with very large samples even a small F can exceed the critical value. Related facts: F cannot be negative, since variances are squared quantities, and with two groups F equals t squared. Option b is right because 0.99 is less than 1, so it can never exceed the critical F value.","traps":{"a":"An F of 2 means the between-groups variance is twice the within-groups variance. Whether that is significant depends on the degrees of freedom: with 2 and 12 degrees of freedom the critical F at .05 is about 3.89, so it fails, but with many groups and large samples the critical value falls below 2 and it passes. It tempts because 2 looks small and unimpressive, yet it is not always insignificant.","c":"An F of 1.55 is above 1, so the between-groups variance exceeds the error variance. With small samples it will not be significant, but critical values shrink toward 1 as both degrees of freedom grow, so with large enough numerator and denominator degrees of freedom, 1.55 can be significant. It tempts because candidates who know only the common critical values, usually between about 2 and 5, assume such a small F can never pass.","d":"An F of 1.2 is close to 1 and in ordinary studies will almost never be significant. Yet it is above 1, and with very large numerator and denominator degrees of freedom the critical F approaches 1, so 1.2 can reach significance. 'Always insignificant' must hold for every possible degrees of freedom, which only an F below 1 satisfies. It tempts because it is the smallest value above 1 and looks practically equal to chance."}},{"q":"Lack of organization and planning of dreams is due to low activation of ________","concept":"Dreams, especially in REM (rapid eye movement) sleep, are vivid, emotional and visual, yet bizarre, poorly organised, short on logic and planning, and accepted uncritically while they happen. Brain imaging explains this pattern. PET studies by Pierre Maquet and colleagues (1996) and Allen Braun and colleagues (1997) found that in REM sleep the limbic system, especially the amygdala, along with the brainstem and visual association areas, is highly active, while the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex is markedly deactivated. The prefrontal cortex supports executive functions: planning, working memory, logical sequencing, self-reflection and reality testing. With it offline, dream scenes jump without transition, impossible events go unquestioned, and we rarely recognise that we are dreaming. Lucid dreaming, in which the dreamer knows they are dreaming, is linked to partial reactivation of frontal areas. Braun and colleagues (1998) also showed a split within vision: the primary visual (striate) cortex was relatively deactivated while extrastriate cortex was active, fitting imagery generated internally rather than from the eyes. Theories built on this include the activation-synthesis hypothesis of J. Allan Hobson and Robert McCarley (1977), which says the forebrain makes a story out of random brainstem (pontine) activation, and the later AIM model of Hobson and colleagues (2000), describing brain states by activation, input source and neuromodulation. Mark Solms (2000) argued that dreaming depends on forebrain dopamine circuits, not just the pons. Option c is right because the lack of organisation and planning in dreams reflects low activation of the prefrontal cortex during REM sleep.","traps":{"a":"The striate cortex is the primary visual cortex (V1) in the occipital lobe, named for its striped appearance. It receives input from the lateral geniculate nucleus and handles basic features such as edges and orientation. In REM sleep it is relatively deactivated because no input arrives from the eyes, but its role is vision, not organising or planning dream narratives. It tempts because dreams are so strongly visual.","b":"The somatosensory cortex, in the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe, processes touch, temperature, pain and body position, laid out as the sensory homunculus mapped by Wilder Penfield and Edwin Boldrey (1937). It has no special role in the logic or planning of dreams. It tempts as a filler because dreams can include bodily sensations such as falling or flying, which seem to call for a body-sense area.","d":"The extrastriate cortex comprises the visual association areas beyond V1, such as V4 for colour and V5 (MT) for motion, which build complex images and recognise objects. Allen Braun and colleagues (1998) found it active during REM sleep, which helps explain vivid dream imagery. So it is highly activated, not low, and it serves imagery rather than planning. It tempts because its REM activity is often cited alongside the prefrontal finding."}},{"q":"Slow wave sleep occurs when neurons in the ____________________ become active","concept":"Sleep and waking are controlled by mutually inhibiting groups of neurons, described by Clifford Saper and colleagues (2001) as a 'flip-flop switch'. On the sleep side is the ventrolateral preoptic area (VLPO) of the anterior hypothalamus, identified as sleep-active by Saper's group in 1996. Its neurons release the inhibitory transmitters GABA and galanin onto the arousal systems: the histaminergic tuberomammillary nucleus, the noradrenergic locus coeruleus, the serotonergic raphe nuclei, cholinergic brainstem neurons, and the orexin (hypocretin) neurons of the lateral hypothalamus. When VLPO neurons fire, arousal is shut down and slow wave sleep (NREM stage N3, with high-amplitude delta waves) begins; when the arousal systems fire, they inhibit the VLPO and we wake. Because each side suppresses the other, transitions are quick, and orexin neurons stabilise the switch, which is why their loss produces narcolepsy. Earlier, Constantin von Economo, studying encephalitis lethargica from 1917, noticed that anterior hypothalamic damage caused insomnia while posterior damage caused excessive sleepiness. What pushes the switch toward sleep? Adenosine builds up with time awake (the homeostatic drive, Process S in the two-process model of Alexander Borbely, 1982) and promotes VLPO activity, while the suprachiasmatic nucleus supplies circadian timing (Process C). REM sleep has its own switch in the brainstem: REM-on neurons of the sublaterodorsal nucleus versus REM-off neurons in the ventrolateral periaqueductal gray. Option b is right because slow wave sleep starts when the GABAergic neurons of the ventrolateral preoptic area become active and inhibit the arousal centres.","traps":{"a":"The ventrolateral periaqueductal gray (vlPAG), in the midbrain around the cerebral aqueduct, contains GABAergic REM-off neurons. Together with the lateral pontine tegmentum they inhibit REM-on cells, so when they fall silent REM sleep can begin (Jun Lu and colleagues, 2006). The wider periaqueductal gray also controls pain modulation and defensive behaviour. It tempts because 'ventrolateral' appears in both this option and the correct one, but this region governs REM, not slow wave sleep.","c":"The sublaterodorsal nucleus (SLD) in the dorsal pons holds glutamatergic REM-on neurons. When they fire, REM sleep begins, and their projections to the medulla and spinal cord produce the muscle atonia of REM. Damage here releases movement during REM, a model for REM sleep behaviour disorder. It tempts because it is a genuine sleep-switch nucleus described in the same textbook passage, but it switches on REM sleep, not slow wave sleep.","d":"The suprachiasmatic nuclei (SCN) of the hypothalamus, sitting just above the optic chiasm, are the master circadian clock. They receive light input from melanopsin-containing retinal ganglion cells and set daily rhythms, including melatonin release by the pineal gland. They time when sleep is likely (Process C), but SCN activity does not itself generate slow wave sleep. It tempts because the SCN is the best known hypothalamic structure linked to sleep."}},{"q":"Study of monozygotic and dizygotic twins who are separated at  birth and reared apart","concept":"Behaviour genetics asks how much of the variation in a trait across people comes from genetic differences (heritability) and how much from environment. Since human breeding experiments are impossible, it relies on natural experiments in which genetic relatedness varies. Francis Galton (1875) first proposed comparing twins. Monozygotic (MZ, identical) twins come from one fertilised egg and share virtually all their genes; dizygotic (DZ, fraternal) twins come from two eggs and share about 50 percent of segregating genes, like ordinary siblings. In the classic twin study, if MZ pairs are more alike (more concordant) than DZ pairs, genes are implicated; Douglas Falconer (1960) estimated heritability as twice the difference between the MZ and DZ correlations. The strongest version studies twins separated early and reared apart, because their similarity cannot come from a shared home. The best known example is the Minnesota Study of Twins Reared Apart led by Thomas Bouchard from 1979, which found MZ twins reared apart correlated about .70 on IQ. Examiners build variants from the neighbouring designs. Family studies check whether a disorder runs in families; risk rises with relatedness, but genes and environment are confounded. Adoption studies compare adoptees with their biological and adoptive parents, as in Leonard Heston's (1966) study of children of mothers with schizophrenia. Linkage and association studies, including genome-wide association studies, locate specific genes. Twins reared apart are, strictly, a hybrid twin and adoption design. Option d is right because a study comparing MZ and DZ twins, including those reared apart, is by definition a twin study.","traps":{"a":"Family studies compare the rate of a trait or disorder in relatives of affected people with the rate in the general population. For schizophrenia, Irving Gottesman (1991) showed risk rising with genetic closeness, from about 1 percent in the population to about 10 percent in first-degree relatives. But families share homes as well as genes, so the two cannot be separated. It tempts because twins are relatives too, yet the stem specifies MZ and DZ twins.","b":"Adoption studies compare adopted children with their biological parents (who gave the genes) and adoptive parents (who gave the environment). Leonard Heston (1966) and the Danish studies of Seymour Kety (1968) found schizophrenia more common in biological relatives. This tempts strongly because twins 'separated at birth and reared apart' were in effect adopted, making such work a twin and adoption hybrid. But the stem's defining feature is the MZ versus DZ comparison, which marks a twin study.","c":"Linkage studies track whether a disorder is inherited together with a known DNA marker across generations of large families with several affected members. Co-inheritance suggests the responsible gene lies close to that marker on the same chromosome, expressed as a LOD score. They locate genes rather than estimate heritability. It tempts because it is another behaviour-genetic method taught alongside family, twin and adoption designs, but it does not involve twins reared apart."}},{"q":"Which of the following mechanoreceptors is involved in pain perception?","concept":"The skin contains several kinds of receptors, each tuned to a type of stimulus. Mechanoreceptors respond to physical deformation of tissue. Four specialised endings handle harmless touch: Meissner's corpuscles (light touch, flutter and low-frequency vibration; fast adapting), Merkel's discs (steady pressure, fine form and texture; slow adapting), Ruffini endings (skin stretch; slow adapting) and Pacinian corpuscles (deep pressure and high-frequency vibration; fast adapting). Fast adapting receptors signal change, slow adapting ones signal a continuing stimulus. Pain is handled differently. Nociceptors are free nerve endings: bare, unencapsulated branches of sensory neurons. They detect tissue-damaging mechanical, thermal and chemical stimuli, and other free endings signal temperature and itch. Many are polymodal, so a free ending that responds to crushing or pinching is both a mechanoreceptor and a nociceptor, which is why the stem can loosely call them mechanoreceptors. Two fibre types carry pain: thin myelinated A-delta fibres give fast, sharp first pain, and unmyelinated C fibres give slow, dull, burning second pain. Heat and capsaicin act on the TRPV1 channel in these endings. Pain is then modulated centrally, as in the gate control theory of Ronald Melzack and Patrick Wall (1965), in which activity in large touch fibres can close a spinal gate, explaining why rubbing an injury eases it. Exams vary this item by asking which receptor signals vibration (Pacinian), stretch (Ruffini) or texture (Merkel). Option c is right because free nerve endings are the receptors that signal pain.","traps":{"a":"Merkel's discs (Merkel cell-neurite complexes), described by Friedrich Merkel (1875), lie at the base of the epidermis, densely in the fingertips. They are slowly adapting, so they keep firing during steady pressure and signal fine form, edges and texture, as in reading Braille. They are low-threshold receptors that respond to harmless pressure. It tempts because they are a well known mechanoreceptor and hard pressure can hurt, but that pain is carried by free endings.","b":"Meissner's corpuscles are encapsulated receptors in the dermal papillae of hairless (glabrous) skin such as fingertips, palms and lips. They adapt rapidly, so they signal change: light stroking touch, flutter, low-frequency vibration and an object slipping in the hand. They respond to gentle stimulation and play no role in signalling tissue damage. They tempt because they are among the most sensitive touch receptors, and 'sensitive' is easily confused with 'painful'.","d":"Ruffini endings (Ruffini corpuscles) are spindle-shaped encapsulated receptors deep in the dermis and in joint capsules. They are slowly adapting and respond to skin stretch and sustained pressure, helping to sense finger position and the shape of grasped objects. Like the other corpuscles, they have a low threshold for harmless mechanical change. They tempt because older textbooks linked Ruffini endings with warmth, and candidates stretch that to pain, but neither is their job."}},{"q":"Norman Triplett observed _______________ tended to race _________ when racing in the presence of others","concept":"Social facilitation is the effect of the mere presence of others, as co-actors or as an audience, on individual performance. Its starting point is Norman Triplett (1898), often credited with the first social psychology experiment. Studying racing records of the League of American Wheelmen, Triplett noticed that cyclists rode faster when racing against others or with a pacer than when riding alone against the clock. He proposed 'dynamogenic' factors: the presence of a rival releases latent energy. He then tested this in the laboratory with children winding fishing reels, alone or in pairs; many wound faster in pairs, though some did worse. Floyd Allport later named the effect social facilitation. The mixed results were explained by Robert Zajonc (1965): the presence of others raises arousal, and arousal strengthens the dominant response. On simple or well-learned tasks the dominant response is correct, so performance improves; on complex or new tasks it is often wrong, so performance worsens (social inhibition). Zajonc and colleagues (1969) even showed this in cockroaches running simple versus complex runways. Nickolas Cottrell (1968) argued the arousal comes from evaluation apprehension, and Robert Baron (1986) proposed distraction-conflict theory. Do not confuse this with social loafing (Bibb Latane and colleagues, 1979), where people working on a pooled group task, as in Max Ringelmann's rope pulling, exert less effort because individual output cannot be identified. Option c is right because Triplett observed that cyclists raced faster in the presence of others than alone.","traps":{"a":"This keeps the correct subjects but reverses the direction. A slowing effect of others, called social inhibition, does occur, but on complex or poorly learned tasks, as Robert Zajonc (1965) explained through arousal strengthening the dominant response. Racing a bicycle is a simple, well-practised task, so arousal helped. It tempts because some of Triplett's own reel-winding children did worse in pairs, and because social loafing also describes reduced effort in groups.","b":"Both parts are wrong. Rats were not Triplett's subjects; his data came from human cyclists and then children winding fishing reels. Animal work on social facilitation came later, most famously the 1969 cockroach runway study by Robert Zajonc and colleagues, and the effect on a simple task was faster, not slower. It tempts because rats dominate early learning research, from Edward Tolman's (1948) maze studies to B. F. Skinner's (1938) operant chamber.","d":"This has the direction right but the subjects wrong. Faster performance in company was later shown in animals such as ants and cockroaches, the latter in the 1969 study by Robert Zajonc and colleagues, but Triplett (1898) worked with human cyclists' racing records and children's reel winding. It tempts because candidates remember 'faster' and that social facilitation was later demonstrated in animals, then attach the wrong species to Triplett."}},{"q":"What is systematic measurement error?","concept":"Classical test theory, built on the work of Charles Spearman (1904), says every observed score is the sum of a true score and error (X = T + E). Error comes in two kinds, and examiners like to contrast them. Random error is unpredictable: it pushes scores up on one occasion and down on another, through things like momentary fatigue, guessing, noise or lapses of attention. Because it is equally likely in either direction, it tends to cancel out across many measurements, and its main effect is to lower reliability (precision). Systematic error, also called bias or constant error, pushes scores in the same direction every time. A weighing scale that always reads 2 kg heavy, a translated questionnaire whose items are harder for one group, a lenient examiner, and social desirability responding are all examples. Because it repeats consistently, systematic error does not lower reliability; a biased scale can give the same wrong reading every time. Instead it threatens validity and accuracy, because the scores are consistently off target. The dartboard analogy helps: darts tightly grouped away from the bullseye show high reliability with systematic error, while darts scattered around the bullseye show random error. Systematic error can arise from the instrument, the observer, the procedure or the participant, and it cannot be removed simply by averaging more measurements; it needs calibration, standardisation, blinding or counterbalancing. Option b is right because systematic error is defined by being consistent in the same direction across measurements.","traps":{"a":"Error from random fluctuations is random (unsystematic) error. It arises from chance factors such as momentary fatigue, mood, distraction or guessing, and it varies from one measurement to the next. In classical test theory it is the E that lowers reliability and averages toward zero over many trials. It tempts because 'error' in everyday speech means random mistakes, and because both kinds of error are defined together in the same textbook section.","c":"Error that is inconsistent and changes direction across measurements is again random error, here defined by its behaviour rather than its cause. It is the direct opposite of systematic error, which keeps the same direction. Random error reduces reliability, whereas systematic error biases scores and reduces validity. It tempts because it mirrors the correct option word for word with one key change, so a reader who skims picks the wrong half of the pair.","d":"Participant bias, such as social desirability, acquiescence or reacting to demand characteristics as described by Martin Orne (1962), can indeed produce systematic error. But it is only one possible source, alongside the instrument, the observer and the procedure. The question asks what systematic error is, its defining property, not one of its causes. It tempts because it names a real bias and sounds more specific, which many candidates mistake for more correct."}},{"q":"Which of the following were flaws that David Wechsler aimed to rectify with his Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scales?","concept":"David Wechsler, chief psychologist at Bellevue Hospital in New York, published the Wechsler-Bellevue Intelligence Scale in 1939 because the leading test of the day, the Stanford-Binet of Lewis Terman (1916, revised with Maud Merrill in 1937), served adults poorly. His criticisms form a favourite exam list. First, the Binet was built for children: its items and norms did not suit or appeal to adults. Second, it leaned heavily on verbal material and word manipulation, penalising people with limited schooling or language. Third, it gave weight to speed, with timed items scattered through the scale, which handicapped older adults whose accuracy was intact. Fourth, it relied on mental age and the ratio IQ (mental age divided by chronological age, times 100) proposed by William Stern (1912); since mental age stops rising in the mid-teens, a ratio IQ makes little sense for adults. Wechsler's solutions became standard. He replaced the age scale, in which items are grouped by the age at which most children pass them, with a point scale, in which items of one type form a subtest and earn points. He added a Performance scale of nonverbal tasks such as Block Design and Picture Completion, drawing on the Army Beta of World War I. He replaced the ratio IQ with the deviation IQ, mean 100 and standard deviation 15, comparing each person with his or her own age group. These ideas carried into the WISC (1949) and WAIS (1955). Option a is right because it lists exactly the four flaws Wechsler set out to correct.","traps":{"b":"This reverses the first flaw. The Stanford-Binet descends from the 1905 scale of Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon, designed for schoolchildren, so appeal to children was never its weakness. It also substitutes 'math questions' for the real complaint about verbal emphasis, and 'physical age' for mental age, a term no IQ formula uses. Only 'speed over accuracy' is correct. It tempts because that one true phrase makes the whole option look partly right.","c":"This gets 'no appeal for adults' right, and vocabulary is at least a word task, but the last two parts are wrong. The Binet valued speed over accuracy, the reverse of what is written here, and it relied on mental age, not 'physical age'. It tempts because the first phrase matches the real list exactly and vocabulary items did feature in the Binet, so a reader who checks only the opening words is caught.","d":"This contains only one real flaw, reliance on mental age. The Binet did appeal to children, it did not emphasise history questions, and its timed items favoured speed rather than accuracy. Wechsler's concern was adults, which is why his first scale targeted them; his children's scale, the WISC, came only in 1949. It tempts because mental age is the most famous Binet concept, and seeing it named makes the option feel authoritative."}},{"q":"What is the formula for Wechsler's new method for obtaining the IQ score?","concept":"Two ways of expressing IQ appear in exams. The ratio IQ, proposed by William Stern (1912) and adopted by Lewis Terman for the 1916 Stanford-Binet, divides mental age by chronological age and multiplies by 100, so a ten-year-old performing like a typical twelve-year-old scores 120. It fails for adults, because mental age levels off in the mid-teens while chronological age keeps rising, so a ratio IQ would fall steadily with age. David Wechsler, building the Wechsler-Bellevue scale (1939), dropped mental age entirely. He described the IQ as the relation between the score a person actually attains and the mean score expected for people of the same age, which is the formula in this question: IQ equals actual score over expected mean score for age. A person scoring exactly at the age-group mean therefore sits at the centre of the scale. In practice the comparison is carried out with standard scores, the deviation IQ: the raw score is converted to a z score within the person's age group, z = (X minus age-group mean) divided by the age-group standard deviation, and then IQ = 100 + 15z. So an IQ of 115 is one standard deviation above age peers, and 130 is two above, roughly the top 2 percent. The Stanford-Binet adopted the deviation IQ in 1960 (with SD 16 until the fifth edition moved to 15). Option a is right because Wechsler's IQ divides the person's actual score by the expected mean score for his or her age group.","traps":{"b":"This inverts Wechsler's ratio by putting the expected mean on top. A person who outperformed age peers would then get a value below 1, so the brightest people would receive the lowest IQs. It mirrors a common slip with the ratio IQ of William Stern (1912), writing chronological age over mental age instead of mental age over chronological age. It tempts because it uses the same two terms and only their order changes.","c":"Multiplying the actual score by the age-expected mean has no psychometric meaning. The product grows with both terms, so a person in an age group with a high mean would gain IQ points simply for being that age, the opposite of comparing someone with peers. Every IQ formula compares two quantities, by division or in standard deviation units. It tempts only because the familiar ratio IQ formula ends in 'times 100'.","d":"Adding the expected mean for age to the actual score has no meaning either, because it does not compare the person with anyone. Two people of the same age would differ only by their raw scores, and the age norms would play no real role. It tempts because the modern deviation IQ formula does contain an addition, IQ = 100 + 15z, so a candidate who remembers a plus sign without the logic may pick it."}},{"q":"Which cognitive theories were used to derive the five factors of intelligence assessed in the SB5?","concept":"The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scales, Fifth Edition (SB5), developed by Gale Roid (2003), covers ages 2 to 85 and above. It measures five cognitive factors, each through a verbal and a nonverbal subtest, giving ten subtests in all: Fluid Reasoning, Knowledge, Quantitative Reasoning, Visual-Spatial Processing and Working Memory. These combine into a Full Scale IQ, a Verbal IQ and a Nonverbal IQ, each with mean 100 and standard deviation 15. Two routing subtests, Nonverbal Fluid Reasoning and Verbal Knowledge, set the starting level for the rest. The factor structure was built on two cognitive theories. The first is the Cattell-Horn-Carroll (CHC) model, which joins Raymond Cattell's (1963) distinction between fluid and crystallized intelligence, extended by John Horn, with the three-stratum theory of John B. Carroll (1993), drawn from his reanalysis of more than 460 factor-analytic data sets: general ability (g) at stratum III, broad abilities such as fluid reasoning, crystallized knowledge, quantitative knowledge and visual processing at stratum II, and narrow abilities at stratum I. Four SB5 factors map onto these broad abilities. The fifth, Working Memory, draws on the model of Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974), in which a central executive directs a phonological loop and a visuospatial sketchpad; this is why the SB5 tests working memory in both verbal and nonverbal forms. Variants ask how many factors the SB5 has, its routing subtests, or the theory behind the KABC-II or Woodcock-Johnson batteries. Option c is right because the SB5 factors were derived from Carroll's hierarchical model of abilities and Baddeley's working memory model.","traps":{"a":"Erik Erikson (1950) proposed eight psychosocial stages, each a crisis such as trust versus mistrust, and Lev Vygotsky (Mind in Society, 1978 in English) stressed that thinking develops through social interaction, introducing the zone of proximal development. Both are developmental theories about how people change, not factor models of ability structure, so neither supplied the SB5 factors. It tempts because both names are famous and Vygotsky's work is sometimes classed as cognitive.","b":"Jean Piaget, working from the 1920s onward, described four stages of cognitive development (sensorimotor, preoperational, concrete operational, formal operational), and Abraham Maslow (1943) proposed a hierarchy of needs rising to self-actualisation. Piaget explains how thinking changes qualitatively with age, and Maslow addresses motivation, not ability. Neither yields measurable factors like quantitative reasoning or working memory. It tempts because Piaget is the most famous 'cognitive' theorist and the Binet tradition tests children.","d":"B. F. Skinner (1938) developed operant conditioning, explaining behaviour through reinforcement history without reference to mental structures, and Sigmund Freud (1900, The Interpretation of Dreams) founded psychoanalysis, explaining behaviour through unconscious conflict. One is behaviourist and the other psychodynamic, so neither is a cognitive theory at all, and neither proposes ability factors. It tempts only as a pair of famous names for candidates who are guessing."}},{"q":"What are the two scales included in the KBIT-2?","concept":"The Kaufman Brief Intelligence Test, Second Edition (KBIT-2), by Alan S. Kaufman and Nadeen L. Kaufman (2004), is a short screening test of verbal and nonverbal intelligence for ages 4 to 90 that takes about 20 minutes. It revised the original K-BIT (1990). Its two scales follow the fluid and crystallized distinction of Raymond Cattell (1963) and John Horn. The Verbal or Crystallized scale has two subtests, Verbal Knowledge (pointing to the picture that matches a word or answers a question) and Riddles (naming a concept from verbal clues), which tap vocabulary, general information and verbal reasoning learned through schooling and culture. The Nonverbal or Fluid scale is Matrices, in which the examinee completes a visual pattern or analogy, tapping reasoning with new material that depends little on prior learning. The two scales combine into an IQ Composite with mean 100 and SD 15. Because the nonverbal task needs minimal language, a large gap between the scales can flag language difficulty or cultural disadvantage. Brief tests like this, or the Wechsler Abbreviated Scale of Intelligence (1999), are used for screening and research, not for diagnosing intellectual disability, which needs a full battery. Keep the Kaufman family straight: the K-ABC (1983) measured sequential versus simultaneous processing based on the neuropsychology of Alexander Luria (The Working Brain, 1973), the KABC-II (2004) added a CHC interpretation, and the KTEA is their achievement test of reading, mathematics and writing. Option d is right because the KBIT-2 consists of a Verbal or Crystallized scale and a Nonverbal or Fluid scale.","traps":{"a":"Reading comprehension and mathematics are school achievement areas, measured by achievement tests such as the Kaufman Test of Educational Achievement (KTEA) or the Wechsler Individual Achievement Test. Achievement tests measure what has been taught, whereas intelligence tests aim at reasoning capacity. It tempts because the Kaufmans did publish an achievement test, and because the gap between ability and achievement was long used to identify specific learning disorders.","b":"Quantitative and analytical sections recall aptitude tests like the GRE, whose older General Test had verbal, quantitative and analytical parts, and the triarchic theory of Robert Sternberg (1985) with its analytical intelligence. Quantitative Reasoning is also one of the five SB5 factors. The KBIT-2, however, has no quantitative or analytical scale. It tempts because both words sound like standard intelligence subscales from other well known tests.","c":"EQ, or emotional intelligence, was defined by Peter Salovey and John Mayer (1990) and popularised by Daniel Goleman (1995). It is measured with separate instruments such as the Mayer-Salovey-Caruso Emotional Intelligence Test (MSCEIT) or the Bar-On EQ-i, not by brief cognitive screeners, which measure only cognitive ability. It tempts because 'IQ and EQ' is a familiar pairing in popular psychology and sounds like a neat two-scale structure."}},{"q":"What was developed by Alan Kaufman to measure intelligence?","concept":"Alan S. Kaufman is an American psychometrician who, with his wife Nadeen L. Kaufman, produced a whole family of individually administered tests. Before that he worked with David Wechsler on the WISC-R (1974), and his book Intelligent Testing with the WISC-R (1979) taught clinicians to interpret patterns of subtest scores in the light of theory, not just the Full Scale IQ. The Kaufman Brief Intelligence Test (K-BIT, 1990; KBIT-2, 2004) is a quick screener of verbal (crystallized) and nonverbal (fluid) ability. The Kaufman Assessment Battery for Children (K-ABC, 1983) was built on the sequential versus simultaneous processing distinction from Alexander Luria (1973) and aimed to reduce cultural loading; the KABC-II (2004) offers both a Luria and a Cattell-Horn-Carroll interpretation. The Kaufman Adolescent and Adult Intelligence Test (KAIT, 1993) covers ages 11 and above, and the Kaufman Test of Educational Achievement (KTEA) measures academic skills. Distinguish these clinical, one-to-one tests from large-scale group admission tests. The SAT, first given in 1926 and developed for the College Board by Carl Brigham, and the ACT, founded by E. F. Lindquist in 1959, are United States college admission tests. The GRE, run by Educational Testing Service, is used for graduate admission. These are group aptitude or achievement tests used for selection, not intelligence tests authored by Kaufman. Variants ask who developed the K-ABC, which theory it rests on, or what the KBIT-2 scales are. Option b is right because the K-BIT, the Kaufman Brief Intelligence Test, was developed by Alan Kaufman with Nadeen Kaufman to measure intelligence.","traps":{"a":"The ACT is an American college admission test introduced in 1959 by E. F. Lindquist of the University of Iowa. It covers English, mathematics, reading and science, so it is closer to an achievement test of school learning than to an intelligence test, and it is given to large groups at once. It has no connection with Kaufman. It tempts because, like the SAT and GRE, it is a famous standardised test.","c":"The SAT was first administered in 1926 for the College Board, adapted by Carl Brigham from the Army Alpha tests of World War I. It is a group admission test of reading, writing and mathematics used by United States colleges, originally called the Scholastic Aptitude Test. Although it correlates with IQ, it is a selection test, not a Kaufman instrument. It tempts because its original name contained 'aptitude', which sounds like intelligence.","d":"The GRE (Graduate Record Examinations) is a group admission test for graduate school, run by Educational Testing Service, with verbal reasoning, quantitative reasoning and analytical writing sections. It assesses developed academic skills for selection and was not authored by any single psychologist, certainly not by Kaufman. It tempts because its 'reasoning' sections sound like intelligence subtests, and because it is the test most familiar to students planning graduate study abroad."}},{"q":"What is the RAT?","concept":"The RAT is the Remote Associates Test, devised by Sarnoff Mednick (1962) to measure creativity from his associative theory. Mednick defined creative thinking as forming associative elements into new combinations that are useful or meet some requirement, and argued that creative people have a flat associative hierarchy: besides the obvious, strong associations to a word, they can reach weak, remote ones. People with a steep hierarchy produce only the common responses and stop. Each RAT item gives three words that seem unrelated, and the testtaker must find a fourth word that links all three; for example, cottage, Swiss and cake are linked by cheese. The original college form was a timed, 40-minute paper-and-pencil test of about 30 items, scored as the number solved. Note the paradox examiners like: although it targets creativity, each RAT item has a single correct answer, so it relies on convergent thinking. Contrast it with divergent thinking tests, based on the distinction drawn by J. P. Guilford (1950, 1967), such as the Alternate Uses Task (list many uses for a brick) and the Torrance Tests of Creative Thinking by E. Paul Torrance (1966), scored for fluency, flexibility, originality and elaboration. Modern compound remote associate items are used in insight research to compare insight with step-by-step analytic solutions. Another classic creativity framework is the four-stage model of Graham Wallas (1926): preparation, incubation, illumination and verification. Option c is right because the RAT is a timed, 40-minute paper-and-pencil test.","traps":{"a":"'Rapid Action Team' is an invented expansion with no place in psychological testing. In India the similar-sounding Rapid Action Force is a riot-control wing of the Central Reserve Police Force, which may make the phrase feel familiar. In psychology, RAT stands for the Remote Associates Test of Sarnoff Mednick (1962). It tempts because acronym questions train candidates to look for an expansion, and this is the only option that offers one.","b":"This plays on the literal word. Rats are the classic laboratory animal of learning psychology, from the cognitive map studies of Edward Tolman (1948) to the operant chamber of B. F. Skinner (1938), so 'rat' feels at home in a psychology paper. But the capitalised RAT here is an acronym for a creativity test, the Remote Associates Test. It tempts candidates who do not recognise the acronym and fall back on the literal meaning.","d":"The RAT is not a device; it is a paper-and-pencil test made of printed word triads, although researchers now often present the items on computers. In computing, RAT means remote access trojan, a type of malware, which may explain the lure. Electronic recording tools in psychology include the polygraph and the EEG. It tempts because modern testing is often computerised, so 'electronic' sounds plausible for almost any test."}},{"q":"What is the purpose of the MMPI-2?","concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory was published by Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley (1943) at the University of Minnesota to help diagnose psychiatric patients. Its key innovation was empirical criterion keying: items were kept only if they statistically separated diagnosed patient groups from normal controls, whatever the item content seemed to mean. The MMPI-2 (1989, James Butcher and colleagues) restandardised it on a more representative US sample and has 567 true/false items for adults. It yields ten clinical scales: Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Masculinity-Femininity, Paranoia, Psychasthenia, Schizophrenia, Hypomania and Social Introversion, scored as T-scores (mean 50, SD 10), with T of 65 or more treated as clinically significant. Validity scales check test-taking attitude: L (lie), F (infrequency or faking bad), K (defensiveness), plus VRIN and TRIN for inconsistent responding. Clinicians interpret code types, the two highest clinical scales, such as 2-7 for anxious depression. Later versions are the MMPI-A (1992) for adolescents, the shorter MMPI-2-RF (2008) and the MMPI-3 (2020). Neighbours to separate: the Millon Clinical Multiaxial Inventory of Theodore Millon (1977) targets personality disorders; the 16PF of Raymond Cattell (1949) and the NEO-PI-R of Paul Costa and Robert McCrae (1992) measure normal personality; interest inventories guide careers. The MMPI is also used in personnel screening, custody and forensic work, but its core purpose remains clinical. Option a is right because the MMPI-2 was designed to assess psychopathology and so aid psychiatric diagnosis.","traps":{"b":"Educational assessment measures learning and school readiness, using achievement tests (for example the Wechsler Individual Achievement Test), aptitude tests and diagnostic tests for specific learning disorders. The MMPI-2 measures none of these; it is for adults 18 and over, and even the adolescent version, the MMPI-A, targets psychopathology, not learning. It tempts because school counsellors and educational psychologists also use personality tests, so candidates link testing in general with education.","c":"Physical health diagnosis is made through medical examination and laboratory tests, not a personality inventory. The MMPI-2 does have a Hypochondriasis scale (scale 1) and a Health Concerns content scale, but these measure psychological preoccupation with bodily symptoms, not actual disease. It tempts because those health-related scales, and the use of the MMPI in medical settings to screen for psychological factors in pain or illness, suggest a medical purpose.","d":"Career assessment uses interest inventories such as the Strong Interest Inventory, begun by Edward K. Strong (1927), and the Self-Directed Search based on the RIASEC theory of John Holland (1959), which match a person's interests to occupations. The MMPI-2 contains no occupational scales. It tempts because the MMPI is used in personnel screening for high-risk jobs such as police and pilots, but there it screens out psychopathology rather than guiding career choice."}},{"q":"What does answering 'false' to L Scale items in the MMPI-2 assert?","concept":"The MMPI-2 has validity scales that show how a person approached the test before any clinical scale is interpreted. The L (Lie) scale has 15 items, chosen rationally rather than by empirical keying, modelled on the character studies of Hugh Hartshorne and Mark May (1928). Each item describes a small fault or socially disapproved act that almost everyone will admit, such as occasionally putting off until tomorrow what ought to be done today, or sometimes getting angry. All L items are scored when answered 'false'. Answering 'false' therefore denies ordinary human weaknesses, which claims a level of virtue and self-control rarely seen in the culture. A few such answers are normal, but a high L score (T of 65 or more) points to a naive, unsophisticated attempt to look good; problems may be underreported and clinical scales falsely low. High L is common in custody evaluations, job applicants, and very conventional or devout people. Contrast the other validity scales: K measures subtler, more sophisticated defensiveness and is used to correct five clinical scales; S (Superlative Self-Presentation, James Butcher and Yossef Han, 1995) also detects over-favourable reporting; F (Infrequency) detects rare, extreme answers, suggesting faking bad, severe distress or random responding; VRIN and TRIN detect inconsistent and yea-saying or nay-saying responding; and the Cannot Say count flags omitted items. Option c is right because denying the common faults in L items asserts a degree of personal virtue rarely observed in the culture.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the meaning. Lacking virtue would be shown by admitting faults, that is, answering 'true' to L items, and doing so is the normal, honest pattern that produces a low or average L score. A low L does not mean a person lacks virtue; it suggests frank, sometimes self-critical responding. It tempts because 'false' sounds like a negative statement about the self, so candidates link it with something negative.","b":"Careless responding from not reading items properly is detected by the VRIN (Variable Response Inconsistency) scale, which counts contradictory answers to pairs of similar items, and by an elevated F scale. Random answering raises both, and L only by chance. Consistently denying the specific faults in the L items is a deliberate pattern of looking good, not carelessness. It tempts because any invalid profile is easy to blame on careless reading.","d":"Poor comprehension, from low reading ability, limited language or confusion, usually shows up as many omitted items (the Cannot Say count), inconsistent answers on VRIN, or a raised F scale. The L items are short and simple, so denying them reflects motive, not misunderstanding. It tempts because high L scores are more common in less educated, unsophisticated respondents, which candidates mistake for poor understanding rather than naive defensiveness."}},{"q":"Which of the following is not a factor that contributes to consistency in measurement?","concept":"Reliability is the consistency of measurement: whether a test gives the same result for the same person under the same conditions. In classical test theory (Charles Spearman, 1904), an observed score is a true score plus error, and reliability is the proportion of observed score variance that is true score variance. Anything that reduces error variance makes scores more consistent. Three classic contributors appear in this question. First, the stability of the construct itself: a stable trait such as intelligence gives consistent scores over time, while a fluctuating state such as mood or state anxiety changes even if the test is perfect, which is why the State-Trait Anxiety Inventory of Charles Spielberger (1970) measures the two separately. Second, consistency of administration: standard instructions, time limits, setting and examiner behaviour, which is the point of standardisation. Third, consistency of scoring: objective keys, clear rubrics and trained scorers, checked by inter-rater statistics such as Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960). Other factors that raise reliability, and often appear in variant questions, include test length (more items, as the Spearman-Brown formula shows), a heterogeneous sample with a wide score range, items of moderate difficulty, and homogeneous item content. Complexity of a measure is not a recognised source of consistency: a complex test can be highly reliable and a simple one unreliable, depending on how it is built, given and scored. Option d is right because the complexity of the measure is the only choice that is not a standard contributor to consistency.","traps":{"a":"Stability of the construct is a genuine contributor. If the trait itself changes between occasions, scores change and test-retest reliability falls, even with a perfect test. Traits such as intelligence are stable; states such as mood, fatigue or state anxiety fluctuate. It tempts in a 'not' question because candidates think of reliability as a property of the test alone, forgetting that the thing being measured must also hold still.","b":"Consistent administration is a genuine contributor. Standardised instructions, time limits, testing conditions and examiner behaviour remove variation that has nothing to do with the testtaker, which is the whole purpose of a standardised test. Differences in rapport or in how instructions are read add error variance. It tempts because it seems procedural rather than psychometric, so some candidates wrongly assume it does not affect reliability.","c":"Consistent scoring is a genuine contributor. When scoring is objective, as with multiple-choice keys, scorers agree; when it needs judgement, as with essays or projective tests like the Rorschach, scorer differences add error. That is why John Exner (1974) built the Comprehensive System for the Rorschach, and why inter-rater agreement is checked with Cohen's kappa. It tempts because scoring happens after testing and seems separate from measurement itself."}},{"q":"Which method of estimating reliability involves administering the same test twice to the same group of people and correlating their scores?","concept":"Reliability can be estimated in several ways, and each targets a different source of error. The test-retest method gives the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlates the two sets of scores with Pearson's r; the result is the coefficient of stability, and its error source is time sampling. Its problems are practice and memory (carryover) effects if the interval is short, and genuine change in the trait if it is long, so it suits stable traits such as intelligence rather than moods. The alternate or parallel forms method gives two equivalent versions of the test and yields a coefficient of equivalence, controlling memory for specific items. Internal consistency methods need only one administration: split-half reliability correlates two halves and corrects with the Spearman-Brown formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910); Kuder-Richardson formula 20 (G. Frederic Kuder and M. W. Richardson, 1937) handles right-or-wrong items; and coefficient alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) generalises KR-20 to any item format. Inter-rater reliability checks agreement between scorers. Item response theory, developed by Frederic Lord (1952) and by Georg Rasch (1960), is a different framework: it models the probability of each response from item difficulty, discrimination and person ability, and replaces a single reliability coefficient with a test information function. A useful memory hook is that only test-retest uses the identical test twice, separated by time. Option b is right because administering the same test twice to the same group and correlating the scores is the test-retest method.","traps":{"a":"The split-half method gives the test once, divides it into two halves (often odd versus even items), correlates the halves, and corrects the result upward with the Spearman-Brown formula because each half is only half as long. It measures internal consistency, so its error source is content sampling, not time. It tempts because it also involves correlating two sets of scores obtained from the same people.","c":"Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) is an internal consistency coefficient computed from a single administration, using the number of items, the item variances and the total score variance. It equals the mean of all possible split-half coefficients and generalises KR-20 to items with more than two response options. It tempts because it is the most widely reported reliability statistic, so candidates choose it for any reliability question.","d":"Item response theory (Frederic Lord, 1952; Georg Rasch, 1960) is a modern measurement framework, not a reliability procedure. It models the probability of a given response from item parameters (difficulty, discrimination, guessing) and the person's latent ability, and it underpins computerised adaptive testing. It does not need two administrations. It tempts because it is a sophisticated, current-sounding approach that candidates feel must be relevant to any measurement question."}},{"q":"Which of the following is a potential limitation of the internal consistency method of estimating reliability?","concept":"Internal consistency reliability estimates how well the items of a test hang together, using one administration only. Methods include split-half reliability with the Spearman-Brown correction, Kuder-Richardson formula 20 (G. Frederic Kuder and M. W. Richardson, 1937) for right-or-wrong items, coefficient alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) for any item format, and omega (Roderick McDonald, 1999). The error source these methods capture is content sampling: whether the particular items chosen measure the same thing. Their main limitation follows directly from the design. Because all the data come from a single sitting, internal consistency says nothing about stability across time; a mood questionnaire can have an alpha of .90 and still give very different scores next week. Time sampling error is the job of test-retest reliability, and differences between versions are the job of alternate forms reliability. Other cautions are worth knowing for variant questions. Alpha rises with the number of items, so a long test can show high alpha even when items are only modestly related. Alpha is lowered when a test is heterogeneous or multidimensional, yet a high alpha does not prove that a test measures a single dimension. Internal consistency should not be used for speeded tests, because unreached items at the end inflate the estimate. A one-item measure cannot be assessed this way. The keyed answer is the standard textbook limitation; strictly, the content and multiple-item statements also describe real properties, which makes 'All of the above' arguable. Option a is right because internal consistency is based on a single administration and therefore cannot show consistency over time.","traps":{"b":"Internal consistency is indeed affected by content: if the items sample different domains, inter-item correlations fall and alpha drops. But sensitivity to content heterogeneity is exactly what the method is designed to detect, so textbooks treat it as what internal consistency measures rather than as its limitation. Differences between versions of a test are the concern of alternate forms reliability. It tempts because the statement is true, which is why this key is debatable.","c":"Needing multiple items is literally true: with one item there is nothing to correlate, and alpha needs at least two. But almost every psychological test has many items, so this is a trivial condition of use rather than the recognised limitation. The textbook answer focuses on the lack of any information about stability over time. It tempts because the statement is factually correct, and factual correctness pulls candidates toward it.","d":"'All of the above' would be right only if every statement were accepted as a limitation. The key treats the single-administration problem as the one real limitation, and regards content sensitivity and the need for several items as properties rather than flaws. It tempts strongly here because the other two statements are technically true, so a careful reader may feel forced to include them; this is the arguable point in the item."}},{"q":"What is the major challenge with split-half reliability?","concept":"Split-half reliability is an internal consistency method: the test is given once, scored as two halves, and the two half-scores are correlated. Because each half is only half as long as the whole test, and shorter tests are less reliable, the half-test correlation underestimates full-test reliability and is stepped up with the Spearman-Brown formula of Charles Spearman and William Brown (1910), 2r / (1 + r). The central practical problem is how to split. The two halves must be as nearly equivalent as possible in content, difficulty and position, or the correlation will be lowered by differences between the halves rather than by unreliability. Splitting first half against second half is the worst choice, because items usually get harder, testtakers tire or warm up, and in a timed test many never reach the later items. Accepted solutions are the odd-even split, random assignment of items to halves, or matching items for content and difficulty. Different splits of the same test give different coefficients; a 10-item test can be split 126 ways. Lee Cronbach (1951) dealt with this by showing that coefficient alpha equals the average of all possible split-half coefficients. The formulas of Phillip Rulon (1939) and Louis Guttman (1945) estimate split-half reliability without assuming equal half variances. Keep the other designs separate: test-retest gives the same test twice across a time interval, and alternate forms gives two different versions of the test. Option b is right because dividing the test into two nearly equivalent halves is the major challenge of split-half reliability.","traps":{"a":"The Spearman-Brown formula (Charles Spearman and William Brown, 1910) corrects the half-test correlation to estimate full-length reliability: full r = 2r / (1 + r), where r is the correlation between halves. Once the halves are correlated, this is a simple calculation, a routine step rather than a challenge. It tempts because it is the most technical-sounding part of split-half reliability, and candidates assume the hard part must be the formula.","c":"Giving the same test to the same group twice, with a time interval between, is the test-retest method, which yields a coefficient of stability. Its own challenges are choosing the interval, practice and memory effects, and real change in the trait. Split-half needs only one administration, which is its main convenience. It tempts because the option accurately describes a real reliability procedure, just not the one in the stem.","d":"Giving two different versions of a test to the same group is the alternate (parallel) forms method, which yields a coefficient of equivalence. Its challenge is building two forms that are truly equal in content, difficulty and variance, which is costly. It tempts because that problem, constructing equivalent forms, closely resembles the split-half problem of constructing equivalent halves; split-half is essentially alternate forms built within one test."}},{"q":"If the half-test reliability coefficient is .60, what is the estimated reliability of the full test using the Spearman-Brown formula?","concept":"The Spearman-Brown prophecy formula, published separately by Charles Spearman and William Brown in 1910, predicts how reliability changes when a test is lengthened or shortened with comparable items. The general form is new r = kr / (1 + (k minus 1)r), where r is the current reliability and k is the factor by which length changes. In split-half reliability each half is half the length of the whole test, so the correlation between halves underestimates full-test reliability; doubling (k = 2) simplifies the formula to full r = 2r / (1 + r). Here: 2 times .60 = 1.20; 1 + .60 = 1.60; 1.20 divided by 1.60 = .75. So a half-test correlation of .60 implies a full-test reliability of .75. Notice the pattern: lengthening raises reliability, but with diminishing returns, and the result can never exceed 1. Exam variants use other values of k. Tripling a test with reliability .60 gives 1.80 / 2.20 = .82. Halving it (k = 0.5) gives .30 / .70 = .43. You can also solve for k: to raise reliability from .60 to .80, k = (.80 times .40) / (.60 times .20) = 2.67, so the test must be about 2.7 times as long. The formula assumes the added items are parallel to the existing ones and that the test is not speeded. For whole tests, coefficient alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) avoids depending on a single split. Option a is right because 2(.60) / (1 + .60) = 1.20 / 1.60 = .75.","traps":{"b":".82 is what Spearman-Brown gives if the test is tripled rather than doubled: 3(.60) / (1 + 2(.60)) = 1.80 / 2.20 = .82. Split-half correction always involves doubling, because each half is half the full test, so k is 2, not 3. It tempts because it is a genuine Spearman-Brown result and lies in the plausible range above .60, so candidates who apply the wrong k land on it.","c":".30 is simply .60 divided by 2. It moves the coefficient in the wrong direction: the full test is longer than either half, so its reliability must be higher than .60, not lower. Even shortening a .60 test to half length by Spearman-Brown would give .43, not .30. It tempts candidates who see the word 'half-test' and mechanically halve the number they are given.","d":".60 is the uncorrected correlation between the two halves, which is the input to the formula, not the answer. Leaving it uncorrected underestimates the reliability of the full-length test, because each half has only half the items and shorter tests are less reliable. It tempts candidates who forget that split-half coefficients must always be stepped up with the Spearman-Brown formula before being reported."}},{"q":"What is the purpose of averaging several splits in estimating the reliability of a psychological test according to Cronbach?","concept":"Split-half reliability depends on how the test is split: odd versus even items, first versus second half, or a random division can each give a different coefficient, and even a 10-item test can be split 126 ways. Any one split is therefore partly arbitrary. Lee J. Cronbach (1951), in his paper on coefficient alpha and the internal structure of tests, showed that alpha equals the mean of all possible split-half coefficients (computed with the formula of Phillip Rulon, 1939). Averaging across every split removes the luck of a particular division, so the estimate is more accurate and stable than any single split-half value. Alpha is calculated without actually splitting: alpha = (k / (k minus 1)) times (1 minus the sum of item variances divided by total score variance), where k is the number of items. For right-or-wrong items it reduces to Kuder-Richardson formula 20 (G. Frederic Kuder and M. W. Richardson, 1937). Points examiners use in variants: alpha rises with more items and with higher average inter-item correlation; values around .70 are often treated as acceptable for research and about .90 for individual clinical decisions; alpha is a lower bound to true reliability unless items are essentially tau-equivalent; and a high alpha does not prove the test measures a single dimension. Cronbach and colleagues (1972) later broadened reliability into generalizability theory, which separates several sources of error at once, such as raters, occasions and items. Option d is right because averaging several splits increases the accuracy of the reliability estimate.","traps":{"a":"Averaging split-half coefficients does not add dimensions. Dimensions are the underlying constructs a test measures, identified by factor analysis, as in the g factor of Charles Spearman (1904) or the primary mental abilities of Louis Thurstone (1938). Adding dimensions would actually lower alpha, which is highest when items measure one construct. It tempts because Cronbach's 1951 paper mentions the 'internal structure of tests', which sounds like dimensionality.","b":"This is the exact opposite of the purpose. Averaging many estimates reduces the random variation attached to any single estimate, the same logic as averaging several measurements of a length, so accuracy goes up, not down. No psychometrician would set out to make an estimate less accurate. It tempts because it mirrors the correct option with one word reversed, catching candidates who read quickly or misremember the direction.","c":"Averaging splits leaves the test untouched; every item is used in every split. Reducing items is a separate decision made during item analysis, when weak items with low item-total correlations are dropped, and shortening a test usually lowers reliability, as the Spearman-Brown formula shows. It tempts because item analysis and reliability are taught together, and candidates confuse refining a test with estimating its reliability."}},{"q":"__________ is the tendency for an individual surrounded by a group of strangers to hesitate and not help because none of the bystanders knows for sure what is happening","concept":"The bystander effect is the finding that a person is less likely to help in an emergency when other people are present. It was studied by John Darley and Bibb Latane after the 1964 murder of Kitty Genovese in New York, reported at the time as witnessed by 38 neighbours, a figure later shown to be exaggerated. In their decision model (Latane and Darley, 1970), a bystander must notice the event, interpret it as an emergency, accept personal responsibility, decide how to help, and then act. Others can block help at different steps through distinct processes. Pluralistic ignorance works at the interpretation step. When a situation is ambiguous, each person looks to others for cues; everyone is trying to look calm, so each concludes from the others' calm that nothing is wrong. In the smoke-filled room study (Latane and Darley, 1968), about 75 percent of people alone reported the smoke, but only 10 percent did so when sitting with two passive confederates. The term itself comes from Daniel Katz and Floyd Allport (1931). Diffusion of responsibility works at the responsibility step, when the emergency is clear but each person feels less personally obliged; in the seizure study (Darley and Latane, 1968), 85 percent of lone participants helped before the seizure ended, against 31 percent who believed four others could hear. A third process, audience inhibition, is fear of looking foolish in front of others. Option b is right because the stem describes not helping because none of the bystanders knows for sure what is happening, which is pluralistic ignorance.","traps":{"a":"The bystander effect, studied by John Darley and Bibb Latane (1968), is the general finding that the presence of others reduces helping. It is the outcome, an umbrella term, while pluralistic ignorance and diffusion of responsibility are the processes that explain it. The stem asks for the specific mechanism, uncertainty about what is happening. It tempts because it is the most familiar term and is not exactly wrong, only less precise.","c":"Diffusion of responsibility is the process in which each bystander feels less personal obligation because responsibility is shared among everyone present. It operates after the event is recognised as an emergency, as in the 1968 seizure study of Darley and Latane, where the need was clear. The stem stresses that no one knows what is happening, a problem of interpretation, not of responsibility. It tempts because it is the best known explanation of bystander apathy.","d":"Amotivation is a term from self-determination theory (Edward Deci and Richard Ryan, 1985), meaning a complete lack of motivation, where a person sees no reason or value in acting. 'Amotivation to help' is not a recognised bystander process and says nothing about uncertainty or the presence of others. It tempts because 'not helping' seems to imply low motivation, and the technical-sounding word suggests an established concept."}},{"q":"Which of the following are powerful sources of provocation","concept":"Provocation means words or actions by another person that we perceive as deliberate attacks on us, and it is one of the strongest social triggers of human aggression. Social psychology textbooks, notably Robert A. Baron and Donn Byrne's Social Psychology, describe direct provocation as producing retaliation: people usually answer aggression with matching or slightly stronger aggression. Two forms are singled out as especially powerful. Condescension is an expression of arrogance or disdain, being talked down to or treated as inferior. Harsh, unjustified criticism, especially criticism that attacks the person rather than the behaviour, is the second. Teasing and sneering also provoke, but the pair the textbook labels powerful is condescension plus unjustified criticism. Whether provocation leads to aggression depends on attribution: if we judge the harm intentional we retaliate, while a credible excuse or apology reduces anger. Kenneth Dodge (1980) showed that aggressive children carry a hostile attribution bias, reading ambiguous acts as hostile. Examiners build variants from neighbouring causes of aggression. The frustration-aggression hypothesis of John Dollard and colleagues (1939) said frustration always leads to aggression; Leonard Berkowitz (1989) revised it, arguing that frustration produces aggression mainly when it creates negative affect. Dolf Zillmann's excitation transfer theory (1971) explains how leftover arousal, for example from exercise, intensifies anger at a later provocation. The General Aggression Model of Craig Anderson and Brad Bushman (2002) combines person factors, situational factors such as provocation, and appraisal. Option c is right because condescension and harsh, unjustified criticism are the two provocations the social psychology literature names as especially powerful triggers of aggression.","traps":{"a":"Teasing means remarks that call attention to a person's flaws or mistakes, and research does show it can provoke aggression, especially when it is seen as hostile rather than playful. However, this pair drops unjustified criticism, which the textbook names alongside condescension as the especially powerful provocations. It tempts because condescension is correct and teasing is a real, familiar provocation.","b":"Harsh, unjustified criticism is one of the two powerful provocations, so half of this pair is right. The pair leaves out condescension, the expression of arrogance or disdain that the textbook lists first. Teasing does provoke, but it is not one of the two forms highlighted as especially powerful. It tempts because it contains one correct element and one plausible element.","d":"'None of the above' would be correct only if no listed pair matched the textbook. Choice c gives exactly the pair named as powerful sources of provocation, so this choice fails. It tempts candidates who know that teasing also provokes aggression, feel unable to choose between three pairs that each contain a real provocation, and retreat to the escape option."}},{"q":"splintering of a group into distinct factions is called","concept":"A schism is the splintering of a group into distinct factions, a term used in group dynamics textbooks. The word comes from the Greek for 'split' and was first used for religious divisions, such as the Great Schism of 1054 between the Eastern and Western churches. In a schism a group does not just argue: it divides into rival subgroups, each with its own identity, and these often end up as separate groups. Fabio Sani and Stephen Reicher (1998), studying the split of the Italian Communist Party, showed that schism follows when members believe a change has subverted the group's core identity; Sani's later work added that schism is more likely when dissenters feel they have no voice. Schism is the end point of intragroup conflict. Neighbouring terms an examiner may substitute: a faction or coalition is a subgroup inside a group that has not broken away; group polarization, shown by Serge Moscovici and Marisa Zavalloni (1969), is the shift of a whole group's opinion toward a more extreme position after discussion; groupthink, described by Irving Janis (1972), is the opposite problem, excessive cohesion that suppresses dissent; and deviance means one member departing from group norms. Bruce Tuckman (1965) placed conflict in the storming stage of group development, which a group may or may not survive. Intergroup conflict, as in Muzafer Sherif's Robbers Cave study (1954), is conflict between two groups rather than division inside one. Option a is right because schism is the technical name for a group splintering into distinct factions.","traps":{"b":"A chapter is a local branch of a larger organisation, such as a city chapter of a professional association. Chapters are created by the parent body and remain loyal parts of it; they involve no conflict and no breakaway. It tempts because a chapter is a smaller unit carved out of a larger whole, which superficially resembles a group being split into parts.","c":"'Division' is an everyday word for any part or section, or for the act of dividing, as in a company's sales division. It has no technical meaning in group dynamics and does not imply factions, rival identities or conflict. It tempts because a schism is literally a kind of division, so the general word looks like a safe synonym, but the stem asks for the specific term.","d":"Deviation, or deviance, is one member's departure from the norms of a group. The group responds by pressuring, persuading and finally rejecting the deviate, as Stanley Schachter (1951) showed in his studies of opinion deviates. It concerns an individual, not the splintering of the whole group into factions. It tempts because deviance is a familiar group dynamics term that is linked with conflict."}},{"q":"Fiedler's contingency theory does NOT include which of the following as dimensions","concept":"Fred Fiedler's contingency model of leadership (first proposed in 1964 and set out in his 1967 book A Theory of Leadership Effectiveness) was the first major theory to say that no single leadership style is best: effectiveness depends on the match between the leader's style and the situation. Style is measured by the Least Preferred Co-worker (LPC) scale. A leader who describes their least preferred co-worker harshly (low LPC) is task-oriented; one who still describes that person favourably (high LPC) is relationship-oriented. Fiedler treated style as a fairly fixed trait, so he advised changing the situation rather than the leader. Situational favourableness, or situational control, has exactly three dimensions, in order of importance: leader-member relations (trust and loyalty between leader and group), task structure (how clearly goals and procedures are defined), and position power (the formal authority to reward, punish, hire and fire). Combining these gives eight octants. Task-oriented leaders do best when the situation is very favourable or very unfavourable; relationship-oriented leaders do best in moderately favourable situations. Variant questions use neighbouring models. The path-goal theory of Robert House (1971) does make follower motivation central, since the leader clarifies paths to rewards. The situational leadership model of Paul Hersey and Kenneth Blanchard (1969) matches style to follower readiness or maturity. The model of Victor Vroom and Philip Yetton (1973) concerns how far subordinates should share in decisions. Option a is right because employee motivation is not one of Fiedler's three situational dimensions, while the other three options name them.","traps":{"b":"Leader-member relations is the first and most important of Fiedler's three dimensions. It refers to the degree of trust, respect and loyalty the group gives the leader, rated simply as good or poor. Because it is a genuine dimension of the model, it cannot be the answer to a NOT question. It tempts candidates who skim the stem and miss the word NOT.","c":"Task structure is Fiedler's second dimension: how clearly the goals, procedures and success criteria of the task are spelled out. A highly structured task, like assembling a standard product, gives the leader more control than a vague, creative one. It is part of the model, so it does not answer this NOT question. It tempts because 'structure of the task' is worded differently from the textbook term.","d":"Position power is Fiedler's third dimension: the formal authority that comes with the leader's role, including the power to reward, punish, hire and fire. The wording 'intrinsic power and authority' is an unusual paraphrase, since this power belongs to the position rather than the person. It is still a genuine dimension, so it is not the exception. It tempts because the odd wording sounds unfamiliar."}},{"q":"Identify the correct order of cellular layers to the retinal receptors","concept":"The vertebrate retina is built 'inside out'. The photoreceptors, rods and cones, lie at the very back of the eye against the pigment epithelium, so light entering through the pupil must first pass through several layers of neurons before it reaches them. Travelling from the vitreous humour toward the receptors, light meets the retinal ganglion cells, then the amacrine cells, then the bipolar cells, then the horizontal cells, and finally the rods and cones. Neural signals flow the opposite way: receptors pass the signal to bipolar cells, which pass it to ganglion cells, whose axons gather at the optic disc and leave the eye as the optic nerve. Because the axons exit there, the optic disc has no receptors and forms the blind spot, which the brain fills in by completion. Horizontal and amacrine cells are the lateral neurons: horizontal cells link receptors and bipolar cells sideways, and amacrine cells link bipolar and ganglion cells sideways, which supports lateral inhibition and contrast enhancement. At the fovea the inner layers are thinned out so that light reaches the densely packed cones with little distortion, which is why acuity is highest there. Stephen Kuffler (1953) recorded the centre-surround receptive fields of ganglion cells that this circuitry produces. The duplex theory adds that rods (about 120 million) serve dim, scotopic vision and cones (about 6 million) serve colour and detail in photopic vision. Option d is right because ganglion, amacrine, bipolar and horizontal cells is the order of the layers that light crosses on its way to the receptors.","traps":{"a":"This sequence starts with horizontal cells, which actually lie deepest among the retinal neurons, right next to the receptors. It then places ganglion cells second and bipolar cells last, mixing inner and outer layers. Horizontal cells link receptors to bipolar cells sideways, so they cannot be the first layer light meets. It tempts because horizontal cells are often described first when textbooks explain receptor-level circuitry.","b":"This sequence begins with bipolar cells and puts amacrine cells last, so it does not follow the physical layering at all. Bipolar cells sit in the middle of the retina, between the receptor-level horizontal cells and the ganglion-level amacrine cells. It tempts because bipolar cells are the direct link in the main receptor, bipolar, ganglion pathway, so candidates thinking of signal flow put them first.","c":"This sequence starts with amacrine cells and ends with ganglion cells, close to the reverse of the true arrangement. Ganglion cells form the innermost layer, the first that light meets, and their axons run across the retinal surface to the optic disc. Placing them next to the receptors is wrong. It tempts candidates who picture the retina the 'right way round', with receptors facing the light."}},{"q":"Tendency of acoustic stimuli of different frequencies (of sound) to cause different parts of the basilar membrane to flex back and forth is","concept":"Place coding, the basis of place theory, holds that the pitch of a sound is signalled by where along the basilar membrane the vibration peaks. The idea goes back to Hermann von Helmholtz (1863), who pictured the membrane as a set of tuned resonators. Georg von Békésy, who received the Nobel Prize in 1961, showed that sound sets up a travelling wave along the basilar membrane inside the cochlea. The membrane is narrow and stiff at the base, near the oval window, and wide and flexible at the apex. High frequencies produce maximum flexing near the base; low frequencies peak toward the apex. Hair cells of the organ of Corti at the point of greatest flexing fire most, so frequency is mapped onto place. This tonotopic layout is kept all the way to primary auditory cortex in the temporal lobe. The rival frequency theory (temporal coding), proposed by William Rutherford (1886), says the auditory nerve fires in step with the sound wave, so the timing of firing signals pitch. Single neurons cannot fire much faster than about 1000 times per second, so Ernest Glen Wever and Charles Bray (1930) proposed the volley principle: groups of neurons fire in staggered turns. The modern view combines both: temporal coding handles low frequencies, place coding handles high frequencies, and both operate in the middle range. Loudness is coded mainly by the rate and number of firing neurons. Option b is right because place coding is exactly the tendency of different frequencies to flex different parts of the basilar membrane.","traps":{"a":"Rate coding means that stimulus intensity is signalled by how fast neurons fire: a stronger stimulus produces a higher firing rate. In hearing, louder sounds increase firing rate and recruit more neurons. It describes a property of neural firing, not the location on the basilar membrane that vibrates most. It tempts because rate coding also appears in hearing chapters and sounds like a general answer to any coding question.","c":"Frequency coding, or temporal coding, is the claim of frequency theory, first put forward by William Rutherford (1886): auditory nerve fibres fire in time with the sound wave, so firing frequency signals pitch. It was extended by the volley principle of Ernest Glen Wever and Charles Bray (1930). It concerns timing, not place on the membrane. It tempts because the stem mentions different frequencies of sound.","d":"'Pitch coding' is not a standard technical term. Pitch is the perceived quality that corresponds mainly to frequency, and both place coding and temporal coding are theories of how pitch is coded. Choosing this option names the problem, not the mechanism the stem describes. It tempts because sounds of different frequencies are heard as different pitches, so the label feels intuitively right."}},{"q":"__________ layer is sensitive to color and ________ layer is sensitive to movement","concept":"The lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN) of the thalamus, the main relay between retina and primary visual cortex, has six layers, and each layer receives input from only one eye. The bottom two layers are magnocellular (large cell bodies) and the top four are parvocellular (small cell bodies). Thin koniocellular (very small cell) layers lie between them. These layers begin parallel channels that stay partly separate up to the cortex. The parvocellular channel takes its input mainly from cones, has small receptive fields, and is sensitive to colour, fine pattern detail and stationary or slowly moving objects. The magnocellular channel takes much of its input from rods and is sensitive to movement, flicker and small differences in brightness, but is essentially colour blind. The koniocellular channel carries signals including those from short-wavelength (blue) cones; its full role is less well understood. Margaret Livingstone and David Hubel (1988) proposed that these channels segregate the processing of form, colour, movement and depth. Their outputs feed two cortical streams described by Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin (1982): the dorsal stream to parietal cortex, the 'where' pathway dominated by magnocellular input, and the ventral stream to inferotemporal cortex, the 'what' pathway with heavy parvocellular input. Melvyn Goodale and David Milner (1992) later recast them as vision for action versus vision for perception. A memory hook: parvo for particulars such as colour and detail, magno for motion. Option d is right because the parvocellular layers are sensitive to colour and the magnocellular layers are sensitive to movement.","traps":{"a":"This option reverses the true pairing. Magnocellular neurons have large cell bodies and large receptive fields and are sensitive to movement but essentially colour blind; parvocellular neurons are sensitive to colour and fine detail. So magnocellular cannot fill the colour blank. It tempts because both correct terms are present, and a candidate who has memorised the two names but not their functions has a fifty-fifty chance.","b":"Parvocellular for colour is correct, but koniocellular is wrong for movement. Koniocellular cells form thin layers between the main LGN layers and carry signals including those from short-wavelength (blue) cones, so if anything they relate to colour, not motion. Movement belongs to the magnocellular layers, which this option leaves out. It tempts because the first half is right and koniocellular is an unfamiliar term.","c":"Both halves fail. Magnocellular is the motion channel, not the colour channel, and the koniocellular layers do not specialise in movement; they carry signals including those from blue cones. The option puts a real term in the wrong blank and adds a distractor in the other. It tempts because magnocellular is genuinely linked to one of the two functions in the stem, so it looks familiar."}},{"q":"Correct order to the auditory receptor is","concept":"Sound is a wave of air pressure, and the ear converts it into neural signals through a fixed sequence. The pinna (outer ear) collects sound and funnels it down the external auditory canal to the tympanic membrane (eardrum), which vibrates. In the middle ear three tiny bones, the ossicles, carry the vibration: the malleus (hammer) is attached to the eardrum, the incus (anvil) sits in the middle, and the stapes (stirrup) presses on the oval window, a membrane opening into the cochlea of the inner ear. Because the eardrum is many times larger than the oval window and the ossicles act as levers, pressure is amplified; this impedance matching lets airborne sound move the cochlear fluid. Vibration of the oval window sets up a travelling wave in that fluid, the round window bulges to release pressure, and the basilar membrane flexes. On the basilar membrane sits the organ of Corti, the auditory receptor organ: its hair cells are bent against the tectorial membrane and transduce motion into signals carried by the auditory nerve (cranial nerve VIII). The central pathway then runs through the cochlear nuclei, superior olives, inferior colliculi and medial geniculate nuclei to primary auditory cortex in the temporal lobe. Damage before the cochlea causes conductive hearing loss; damage to hair cells or the auditory nerve causes sensorineural loss. Georg von Békésy (Nobel Prize 1961) described the travelling wave. Option a is right because it gives the true outside-to-inside order: pinna, tympanic membrane, ossicles, oval window, cochlea, organ of Corti.","traps":{"b":"This sequence puts the cochlea second, straight after the pinna, and the ossicles after the oval window. The cochlea is the snail-shaped inner ear structure deep in the temporal bone; sound can reach it only after the eardrum and the middle ear bones have passed on the vibration. It tempts because the cochlea is the best-known hearing structure, so candidates who think of it as central may place it too early.","c":"This sequence places the oval window before the tympanic membrane and the cochlea before the ossicles. The oval window is the membrane between the middle and inner ear, pushed by the stapes, so it must come after both the eardrum and the ossicles. It tempts because all six structures are real and the pinna and organ of Corti are correctly placed at the ends, so only careful ordering of the middle exposes the error.","d":"This is the closest distractor: it only swaps the ossicles and the oval window. The ossicles (malleus, incus and stapes) must come before the oval window, because the footplate of the stapes is what pushes on the oval window. Only then does the motion pass into the cochlear fluid. It tempts because candidates often remember the oval window as part of the middle ear chain and misplace it."}},{"q":"The Anterolateral system system in somatosensory pathway carries ___________ information","concept":"Somatosensation, the body senses, reaches the brain through two main ascending systems. The dorsal-column medial-lemniscus system carries fine discriminative touch, vibration and proprioception (the sense of limb position). Its large, fast fibres enter the spinal cord and ascend on the same side in the dorsal columns, synapse in the dorsal column nuclei of the medulla, cross the midline, and travel in the medial lemniscus to the ventral posterior nucleus of the thalamus and on to primary somatosensory cortex. The anterolateral system carries nociceptive information (pain) and temperature, plus some crude touch. Its small fibres, the faster A-delta fibres for sharp pain and the slow C fibres for dull aching pain, synapse soon after entering the cord; the second neurons cross over at once and ascend in the anterolateral part of the cord. It has three tracts: the spinothalamic tract to the thalamus, the spinoreticular tract to the reticular formation, and the spinotectal tract to the tectum. Because the two systems cross at different levels, damage to one half of the spinal cord (Brown-Séquard syndrome) causes loss of touch and proprioception on the same side but loss of pain and temperature on the opposite side, a classic exam item. Pain is also modulated on its way up: the gate control theory of Ronald Melzack and Patrick Wall (1965) proposed that touch input and descending signals from the brain can close a spinal gate on pain. Option d is right because the anterolateral system is the pathway for nociceptive (pain) and temperature information.","traps":{"a":"Proprioception is the sense of the position and movement of the limbs, coming from receptors in muscles, tendons and joints. It travels in the dorsal-column medial-lemniscus system, on large myelinated fibres that ascend on the same side and cross in the medulla. It is not an anterolateral modality. It tempts because the two systems are taught together in the same somatosensory chapter, so candidates often swap their contents.","b":"Vibration is detected mainly by Pacinian corpuscles, fast-adapting receptors deep in the skin, and travels in the dorsal-column medial-lemniscus system along with fine touch and proprioception. Clinically it is tested with a tuning fork to check dorsal-column function. It is not carried by the anterolateral system. It tempts because vibration feels like a basic, crude sensation that a candidate might wrongly group with pain and temperature.","c":"Touch, especially fine discriminative touch such as two-point discrimination, is carried by the dorsal-column medial-lemniscus system. The anterolateral system carries only some crude, poorly localised touch, a minor part of its function. It tempts because that minor role is real, so a candidate who remembers crude touch in the spinothalamic tract may pick this, but the defining content of the anterolateral system is pain and temperature."}},{"q":"The part of the nervous system responsible for conservation of energy resources is ___________ and that responsible of allocation of resources for expenditure is _________","concept":"The peripheral nervous system has two parts. The somatic nervous system interacts with the external environment: its afferent nerves carry signals from the skin, skeletal muscles, joints, eyes and ears, and its efferent nerves move skeletal muscles under voluntary control. The autonomic nervous system regulates the internal environment, serving internal organs, smooth muscle, cardiac muscle and glands. Its efferent nerves form two divisions with largely opposite effects. Sympathetic nerves leave the thoracic and lumbar regions of the spinal cord and stimulate, organise and mobilise energy resources in threatening situations, the 'fight or flight' response named by Walter Cannon (1915): heart rate and blood pressure rise, pupils dilate, glucose is released and digestion slows. Parasympathetic nerves leave the brain (for example the vagus nerve) and the sacral cord and act to conserve energy, the 'rest and digest' pattern: heart rate slows and digestion and salivation increase. Most organs receive both inputs, so autonomic control works by balance between opposing divisions. A structural detail examiners like: sympathetic preganglionic neurons are short and postganglionic neurons long, mostly releasing norepinephrine at target organs, while parasympathetic preganglionic neurons are long and postganglionic neurons short, releasing acetylcholine. Hans Selye (1936) later described the general adaptation syndrome, in which sustained sympathetic and hormonal arousal under chronic stress wears the body down. Option a is right because the parasympathetic system conserves energy resources and the sympathetic system allocates and mobilises them for expenditure, in that order.","traps":{"b":"This choice reverses the pairing. The sympathetic system does not conserve energy; it spends it, mobilising glucose, raising heart rate and diverting blood to the muscles for fight or flight. The parasympathetic system does the conserving, slowing the heart and promoting digestion and storage. It tempts because both correct terms appear and the two blanks invite an order mistake, especially under time pressure.","c":"The somatic nervous system carries sensory signals from the skin, muscles and sense organs and controls voluntary skeletal muscle; it is not the energy-conserving system. The autonomic system regulates internal organs, but as a whole it both conserves and spends energy through its two divisions. So neither term fits its blank. It tempts because somatic versus autonomic is the first split of the peripheral nervous system.","d":"This reverses the previous pair and is still wrong. The autonomic nervous system is the umbrella that contains both the conserving parasympathetic and the spending sympathetic divisions, so it cannot be one side of the contrast. The somatic system controls voluntary skeletal muscle and has no special role in energy expenditure. It tempts because autonomic is correctly linked with internal energy regulation in general."}},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter undergoes recycling by enzymatic degradation exclusively","concept":"After a neurotransmitter is released and binds to receptors, its action must be ended quickly, or the next signal would be blurred. There are two deactivation mechanisms. Reuptake is the most common: transporter proteins in the presynaptic membrane, and sometimes in glial cells, draw the transmitter back out of the synaptic cleft so it can be repackaged and reused. Enzymatic degradation is the second: an enzyme breaks the transmitter down in the cleft. Acetylcholine is the classic transmitter deactivated this way. The enzyme acetylcholinesterase, present in the cleft, splits acetylcholine into choline and acetate within milliseconds; the choline is then taken back into the presynaptic terminal and recycled to make new acetylcholine. That is why textbooks describe acetylcholine as recycled by way of enzymatic degradation. The monoamines (dopamine, norepinephrine, serotonin) and the amino acids (glutamate, GABA) are deactivated mainly by reuptake; enzymes such as monoamine oxidase (MAO) and catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) also break down monoamines, but reuptake is their main route of clearance. Acetylcholine was the first neurotransmitter identified, through Otto Loewi's (1921) frog heart experiment, in which fluid from a vagus-stimulated heart slowed a second heart. Clinical links follow: acetylcholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil are used in Alzheimer's disease and related drugs treat myasthenia gravis, while organophosphate nerve gases and pesticides block the enzyme irreversibly. Reuptake blockers such as SSRIs and cocaine act on the other mechanism. Option c is right because acetylcholine is the transmitter cleared by enzymatic degradation, through acetylcholinesterase, rather than by reuptake.","traps":{"a":"Dopamine is a catecholamine whose synaptic action is ended mainly by reuptake through the dopamine transporter (DAT). Cocaine and amphetamine produce their effects by blocking or reversing this transporter. Dopamine is also broken down by MAO and COMT, but that degradation is not the main way its signal ends. It tempts because candidates know MAO inhibitors exist and assume enzymes are the main route.","b":"GABA, the main inhibitory transmitter of the brain, is cleared from the synapse by GABA transporters into neurons and nearby astrocytes. Only inside cells is it broken down by GABA transaminase, and the antiepileptic drug tiagabine works by blocking GABA reuptake. So its action ends through reuptake, not enzymes in the cleft. It tempts because GABA is partly recycled through glial cells, which sounds like the stem.","d":"Glutamate, the main excitatory transmitter, is cleared mainly by excitatory amino acid transporters, largely into astrocytes. There it is converted to glutamine, which returns to neurons and is turned back into glutamate (the glutamate-glutamine cycle). Enzymes are involved, but inside cells after reuptake, not in the cleft. It tempts because the word 'recycling' in the stem fits this well-known glial cycle."}},{"q":"Which of the following are not catecholamines?","concept":"Catecholamines are a family of monoamine neurotransmitters that share a catechol nucleus (a benzene ring carrying two hydroxyl groups) and are made from the amino acid tyrosine in a single chain. Tyrosine hydroxylase, the rate-limiting enzyme, converts tyrosine to L-DOPA; DOPA decarboxylase turns L-DOPA into dopamine; dopamine beta-hydroxylase converts dopamine to norepinephrine (noradrenaline); and in the adrenal medulla and a few neurons, norepinephrine is converted to epinephrine (adrenaline). So the three catecholamines are dopamine, norepinephrine and epinephrine. The monoamines are a larger class that also includes the indolamines, chiefly serotonin, made from tryptophan. Small-molecule transmitters are usually grouped into four classes: amino acids (glutamate, aspartate, glycine, GABA), monoamines, acetylcholine, and unconventional transmitters such as nitric oxide and the endocannabinoids. Neuropeptides form a separate class of large-molecule transmitters. GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid) is an amino acid transmitter, made from glutamate by glutamic acid decarboxylase; it is the main inhibitory transmitter of the brain and has no catechol ring. The synthesis chain explains a clinical fact: L-DOPA is given in Parkinson's disease because, unlike dopamine, it crosses the blood-brain barrier and is then converted to dopamine. This treatment grew from the work of Arvid Carlsson (1958), who showed that dopamine is a transmitter in its own right and later shared the 2000 Nobel Prize. Monoamine oxidase and COMT break catecholamines down. Option a is right because GABA is an amino acid neurotransmitter, not a catecholamine, while the other three are the catecholamine family.","traps":{"b":"Dopamine is the first catecholamine in the synthesis chain, made from L-DOPA, which is made from tyrosine. It acts in the nigrostriatal pathway, whose degeneration causes Parkinson's disease, and in the mesolimbic reward pathway. Since it is a catecholamine, it is not the answer to a 'not' question. It tempts candidates who misread the stem as asking which ones are catecholamines.","c":"Norepinephrine (noradrenaline) is a catecholamine made from dopamine by dopamine beta-hydroxylase. It is the transmitter of the locus coeruleus in the brainstem and of most sympathetic postganglionic neurons, and is linked to arousal and vigilance. Because it belongs to the catecholamine family, it does not answer the stem. It tempts candidates who file it only under the sympathetic nervous system and forget its chemical class.","d":"Epinephrine (adrenaline) is the last catecholamine in the chain, made from norepinephrine mainly in the adrenal medulla. It acts chiefly as a hormone in the stress response, and only a few brain neurons use it as a transmitter. It is still chemically a catecholamine. It tempts because its role as a circulating hormone makes some candidates think it cannot belong to a neurotransmitter family."}},{"q":"According to existentialists, what is the basic unity of person and environment expressed in?","concept":"Existential psychology applies the philosophy of Martin Heidegger (Being and Time, 1927) to the study of persons. Heidegger's key term is Dasein, literally 'being there', which he described as being-in-the-world. The hyphens matter: person and world are not two separate things that later interact; they form one indivisible unity, and each exists only through the other. This rejects the subject-object split of Cartesian thinking and of much scientific psychology. The Swiss psychiatrist Ludwig Binswanger (1942) built his Daseinsanalysis on this idea, and Rollo May, with Ernest Angel and Henri Ellenberger, brought it to English-speaking psychologists in the book Existence (1958). Binswanger distinguished three simultaneous modes of being-in-the-world. Umwelt is the 'around world' of biological needs, drives and the physical environment. Mitwelt is the 'with world' of relationships with other people. Eigenwelt is the 'own world' of self-awareness and the relationship to oneself. Some later writers add a fourth, Uberwelt, the spiritual world of ideals and beliefs. Examiners often ask which mode matches a description, so learn the three by their prefixes: Um for around, Mit for with, Eigen for own. Related existential themes include thrownness (being born into circumstances one did not choose), authenticity, freedom and responsibility, and the four ultimate concerns of Irvin Yalom (1980): death, freedom, isolation and meaninglessness. Option b is right because Dasein, being-in-the-world, is the term that expresses the basic unity of person and environment, while the other three are only modes within it.","traps":{"a":"Umwelt, literally the 'around world', is the biological and physical environment: bodily needs, drives, instincts and natural surroundings. It is one of Ludwig Binswanger's three modes of being-in-the-world, the mode that behaviourism and biology study best. It is only one aspect of existence, not the basic unity of person and world. It tempts because 'environment' in the stem sounds like a direct translation of Umwelt.","c":"Mitwelt, the 'with world', is the mode of being-in-the-world made up of relationships with other people: love, community, social roles and shared meaning. It is one of Ludwig Binswanger's three modes, not the overall unity of person and world. It tempts because existentialists stress that a person always exists in relation to others, so the social world can seem to capture that unity.","d":"Eigenwelt, the 'own world', is the mode of being-in-the-world made up of the relationship to oneself: self-awareness, self-reflection and the personal meaning things have. Rollo May argued it was the mode most neglected by modern psychology. It is still only one of three modes, not the overall unity. It tempts because it sounds the most personal and existential of the three German terms."}},{"q":"What is the third level of moral reasoning according to Kohlberg?","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg (doctoral dissertation, 1958) extended the work of Jean Piaget (1932) on moral judgement by presenting boys, and later adults, with moral dilemmas. The best known is the Heinz dilemma, in which a man considers stealing an overpriced drug to save his dying wife. Kohlberg scored the reasoning behind an answer, not the answer itself, and described three levels, each with two stages. Level I, preconventional morality: stage 1, obedience and punishment orientation; stage 2, individualism and exchange, where right is what serves one's own interests. Level II, conventional morality: stage 3, good interpersonal relationships, the 'good boy, nice girl' orientation; stage 4, maintaining social order, the 'law and order' orientation. Level III, postconventional morality: stage 5, social contract and individual rights, where laws are seen as agreements that can be changed; stage 6, universal ethical principles such as justice and human dignity. Kohlberg sometimes described Level III as the postconventional, autonomous or principled level. The stages are claimed to be invariant in sequence and universal, and only a minority of adults reach Level III; stage 6 was later treated as a theoretical ideal. Piaget's earlier two-stage scheme, heteronomous morality (moral realism) followed by autonomous morality, is a common source of distractors. Carol Gilligan (1982) criticised Kohlberg's justice orientation as male-biased and proposed an ethic of care. Option d is right because postconventional morality is the standard name of the third and highest level in Kohlberg's theory.","traps":{"a":"Preconventional morality is Kohlberg's first level, typical of most children up to about nine. At stage 1 actions are judged by whether they are punished; at stage 2 by whether they serve one's own interests through fair exchange. Morality is external, based on consequences imposed by authority. It is the first level, not the third. It tempts candidates who count the levels in the wrong direction.","b":"Conventional morality is Kohlberg's second level, typical of adolescents and many adults. At stage 3 people seek approval and want to be seen as good; at stage 4 they uphold laws and social order because society needs them. The rules of the group are accepted as right. It is the middle level, not the third. It tempts because it is the level most adults actually reach.","c":"'Autonomous moral principles' is not the standard name of a Kohlberg level. Kohlberg did describe Level III as autonomous or principled, but its accepted name is postconventional. The word 'autonomous' also recalls the autonomous morality of Jean Piaget (1932), the second of his two stages, where rules are seen as changeable agreements. It tempts because it describes the third level's content and borrows Piaget's terminology."}},{"q":"Which style of contact in gestalt therapy is characterized by a high need for acceptance and liking, resulting in enmeshment?","concept":"Gestalt therapy, developed by Fritz Perls with Laura Perls and Paul Goodman (key text Gestalt Therapy, 1951, co-written with Ralph Hefferline), sees healthy functioning as good contact at the boundary between self and environment. Contact means meeting the world with full awareness while keeping a sense of separateness. When contact is avoided or distorted, Gestalt therapists speak of resistances, interruptions or styles of contact, which Erving and Miriam Polster (1973) set out systematically. Introjection is uncritically swallowing beliefs and standards from others, such as parental 'shoulds', without making them one's own. Projection is disowning aspects of oneself and attributing them to other people or the environment. Retroflection is turning back onto oneself what one would like to do to others, or doing for oneself what one wants from others, as in self-blame or psychosomatic tension. Deflection is avoiding direct contact through humour, vagueness or changing the subject. Confluence is the blurring of the boundary between self and others, so the person no longer experiences where they end and others begin. It is driven by a high need for acceptance and to be liked, so the person avoids conflict, hides different feelings and agrees in order to keep the relationship safe. The result is enmeshment, and the therapist counters it with questions such as 'What do you want right now?' Other examined Gestalt ideas are here-and-now awareness, unfinished business and the empty chair. Option b is right because confluence is the contact style marked by a high need for acceptance and liking that dissolves boundaries and produces enmeshment.","traps":{"a":"Projection, in Gestalt therapy, is disowning parts of oneself, such as anger or attraction, and seeing them in other people or the environment, so the person avoids responsibility for those feelings. It keeps self and other apart rather than merging them. It tempts because projection is a familiar psychoanalytic defence mechanism, so it is often chosen whenever a question mentions a disturbance in relationships.","c":"Introjection is passively swallowing the values, rules and beliefs of others, typically parents, without examining or digesting them, so they remain foreign 'shoulds' inside the person. It concerns what is taken in, not the dissolving of the boundary in close relationships. It tempts because introjection also involves adopting other people's views, which can look like the agreeable, conforming behaviour of confluence.","d":"Retroflection is turning back onto oneself what one would like to do to another person, for example directing anger inward as self-criticism, or doing for oneself what one wants from others. Its signs include guilt, self-harm and physical tension. It turns energy inward rather than merging with others. It tempts because it also arises from avoiding conflict with people, which a need for acceptance can motivate."}},{"q":"Which step in systematic desensitization involves gradually exposing clients to anxiety-arousing situations?","concept":"Systematic desensitization was developed by Joseph Wolpe and set out in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958). It rests on reciprocal inhibition: a response incompatible with anxiety, usually deep muscle relaxation, is paired with anxiety cues so that the anxiety is gradually weakened. This is a form of counterconditioning, foreshadowed by Mary Cover Jones (1924), who reduced a boy's fear of a rabbit by pairing it with eating. The procedure has three steps. First, relaxation training, usually a shortened form of the progressive muscle relaxation of Edmund Jacobson (1938), taught over several sessions. Second, construction of the anxiety hierarchy: client and therapist list roughly ten to twenty feared situations and rank them with the Subjective Units of Disturbance (SUD) scale from 0 to 100. Third, systematic desensitization proper: while deeply relaxed, the client imagines the lowest item; if anxiety appears they signal, stop and relax again, and only when an item can be imagined calmly do they move up the hierarchy. Exposure can also be in vivo (in real life). Variant questions contrast this graded method with flooding, prolonged exposure to the most feared situation at once without escape, and with the implosive therapy of Thomas Stampfl (1967), which floods in imagination with exaggerated scenes. Exposure and response prevention for OCD and the participant modelling of Albert Bandura (1969) are related techniques. Option c is right because systematic desensitization proper is the step in which the relaxed client is gradually exposed to the anxiety-arousing situations of the hierarchy.","traps":{"a":"Relaxation training is the first step of systematic desensitization. The client learns to relax deeply, usually by tensing and releasing muscle groups in the progressive muscle relaxation of Edmund Jacobson (1938), and practises at home. It supplies the response that will compete with anxiety, but no feared material is presented yet. It tempts because relaxation runs through the whole procedure, so it can seem like the active step.","b":"Developing the anxiety hierarchy is the second step. Client and therapist list feared situations and rank them from least to most disturbing, often with Subjective Units of Disturbance ratings from 0 to 100. This is planning, not exposure: nothing is yet imagined while relaxed. It tempts because the hierarchy is a graded list of anxiety-arousing situations, which closely echoes the wording of the stem.","d":"Cognitive restructuring is a technique from cognitive therapy, associated with Aaron T. Beck (1976) and the rational emotive approach of Albert Ellis (1962), in which the client identifies and challenges irrational or distorted thoughts. It is not a step in Joseph Wolpe's (1958) systematic desensitization, which is a purely behavioural counterconditioning method. It tempts because modern cognitive behavioural treatment of anxiety often combines both."}},{"q":"Which stage is characterized by the development of sex-role identity and adult social relationships?","concept":"Sigmund Freud (Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality, 1905) proposed that personality develops through psychosexual stages, each defined by the body zone through which the libido, the sexual life energy, seeks gratification. Oral stage (birth to about 18 months): pleasure from sucking, biting and swallowing; fixation is linked to dependency, overeating or smoking. Anal stage (about 18 months to 3 years): pleasure from retaining or expelling faeces, with toilet training as the key conflict; fixation produces anal retentive (orderly, stingy, stubborn) or anal expulsive (messy) traits. Phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years): the genitals become the focus, the Oedipus complex arises, and the child resolves it by identifying with the same-sex parent, which forms the superego. Latency period (about 6 years to puberty): sexual impulses are repressed or sublimated into school, sport and same-sex friendships; Freud did not treat it as a true stage. Genital stage (puberty onward): sexual energy returns and is now directed toward peers outside the family, and the person develops a mature adult sex-role identity and the capacity for adult social, sexual and working relationships, summed up in the phrase attributed to Freud that the healthy adult can love and work. Fixation at an earlier stage weakens this outcome. Erik Erikson (1950) recast the stages as psychosocial crises; his adolescent crisis of identity versus role confusion and young adult crisis of intimacy versus isolation overlap with the genital stage. Option d is right because the genital stage is the stage marked by adult sex-role identity and adult social relationships.","traps":{"a":"The oral stage runs from birth to about 18 months. The mouth is the erogenous zone, and the infant gains pleasure from sucking, feeding and later biting. The central issue is dependency on the caregiver, and fixation is linked to traits such as dependence, overeating, smoking or verbal aggression. It has nothing to do with adult sex-role identity. It tempts only as the first, most familiar stage.","b":"The anal stage runs from about 18 months to 3 years. The anus is the erogenous zone, and the key conflict is toilet training, the child's first experience of external rules imposed on a bodily pleasure. Fixation produces anal retentive traits (orderliness, stubbornness, stinginess) or anal expulsive traits (messiness). It concerns control, not adult relationships. It tempts because it is about learning social rules.","c":"The phallic stage runs from about 3 to 6 years. The genitals become the erogenous zone, and the child experiences the Oedipus complex (for girls, the Electra complex, a term from Carl Jung, 1913). Resolving it by identifying with the same-sex parent gives an early gender identification and the superego. It tempts because of this early gender link, but mature sex-role identity and adult relationships come later."}},{"q":"What is the primary focus of the phallic stage of psychosexual development?","concept":"The phallic stage is the third of the psychosexual stages of Sigmund Freud (Three Essays on the Theory of Sexuality, 1905), lasting from about 3 to 6 years. The genitals become the main erogenous zone, and the child notices the anatomical difference between the sexes. Its central event is the Oedipus complex, named after the Greek king who unknowingly killed his father and married his mother: the child develops incestuous fantasies toward the opposite-sex parent and sees the same-sex parent as a rival. In boys, fear that the father will punish these wishes produces castration anxiety. In girls, Freud described penis envy and a turn toward the father; Carl Jung (1913) called the girl's version the Electra complex, a term Freud did not adopt. The complex is resolved by identification with the same-sex parent, a form of identification with the aggressor, which leads to internalising that parent's values, forming the superego, and to an early sense of gender. Unresolved conflict here was thought to underlie later neurosis and phallic character traits such as vanity or recklessness. Examiners build variants by attaching each stage to its focus: oral (feeding and dependency), anal (toilet training and control), phallic (Oedipus and Electra complexes), latency (repression and sublimation of sexual impulses into school and friendships), genital (mature sexuality and adult relationships). Karen Horney (1926) criticised penis envy as a male-centred idea and proposed womb envy. Option c is right because incestuous fantasies and the Oedipus/Electra complex are the primary focus of the phallic stage.","traps":{"a":"Toilet training and gratification from defecation describe the anal stage, about 18 months to 3 years, which comes before the phallic stage. The anus is the erogenous zone, and the child faces the first major demand from society to control a bodily pleasure. Fixation leads to anal retentive or anal expulsive traits. It tempts because it is the stage immediately before the phallic stage.","b":"Development of sex-role identity and adult social relationships describes the genital stage, from puberty onward, when sexual energy is directed toward peers outside the family and mature love and work relationships become possible. The phallic stage only lays down early gender identification. It tempts because identification with the same-sex parent in the phallic stage does begin gender identity, so candidates link sex-role identity to it.","d":"Sublimation of the sex instinct describes the latency period, from about 6 years to puberty, after the Oedipus complex is resolved. Sexual impulses are repressed or channelled into socially valued activities such as schoolwork, sport and same-sex friendships. Sublimation is the defence mechanism of redirecting impulses into acceptable outlets. It tempts because latency directly follows the phallic stage and involves the same sexual energy."}},{"q":"Who developed the basic behavioral procedure of systematic desensitization?","concept":"Joseph Wolpe, a South African psychiatrist, developed systematic desensitization and described it in Psychotherapy by Reciprocal Inhibition (1958). He first studied cats that developed 'experimental neuroses' after receiving shocks in a cage; their fear faded when they were fed in settings progressively more similar to the original cage. From this he formed the principle of reciprocal inhibition, a term borrowed from the reflex physiology of Charles Sherrington (1906): if a response incompatible with anxiety, such as deep muscle relaxation, is made to occur in the presence of anxiety cues, the bond between those cues and anxiety weakens. The procedure has three steps: relaxation training, building an anxiety hierarchy rated in Subjective Units of Disturbance (0 to 100), and graded exposure to the hierarchy items, usually in imagination, while relaxed. It became a founding technique of behaviour therapy, a label popularised by Hans Eysenck (1959). Earlier, Mary Cover Jones (1924) had reduced a boy's fear of a rabbit by gradual exposure paired with food, so she is often called the mother of behaviour therapy, and John B. Watson and Rosalie Rayner (1920) had shown with Little Albert that fear can be conditioned. Neighbouring exposure methods with other founders are common distractors: flooding, the implosive therapy of Thomas Stampfl (1967), and the participant modelling of Albert Bandura (1969). Option a is right because Joseph Wolpe developed systematic desensitization as the basic behavioural procedure built on reciprocal inhibition.","traps":{"b":"Carl Rogers founded client-centred, later person-centred, therapy, set out in Client-Centered Therapy (1951). It is a humanistic, non-directive approach in which the therapist offers unconditional positive regard, empathy and genuineness, trusting the client's actualising tendency. It uses no hierarchies or conditioning. It tempts because Rogers is among the most famous psychotherapists of the same era, and candidates may link any well-known therapy to him.","c":"Albert Bandura developed social learning theory, with the Bobo doll studies (1961) on imitation of aggression and the concept of self-efficacy (1977). His participant modelling, in which the therapist models handling the feared object and then guides the client to do the same, treats phobias and resembles graded exposure. It tempts because Bandura is a learning theorist who also worked on phobia treatment.","d":"Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis and explained phobias as products of unconscious conflict, as in his case of Little Hans (1909), whose fear of horses he traced to the Oedipus complex. Treatment works through free association and interpretation, not conditioning. Behaviour therapists, Wolpe included, explicitly rejected this model. It tempts because Freud is the most famous name in the list and wrote at length on phobias."}},{"q":"Ionotropic receptors bind to","concept":"Receptors are protein molecules in the postsynaptic membrane that bind neurotransmitters, and they come in two broad types. Ionotropic receptors are ligand-gated ion channels: the binding site and the channel are parts of one protein, so when the transmitter binds, the channel opens at once and ions flow, producing a fast postsynaptic potential lasting milliseconds. Examples are the nicotinic acetylcholine receptor, the AMPA, kainate and NMDA glutamate receptors, the GABA-A receptor, the glycine receptor and the 5-HT3 serotonin receptor. Metabotropic receptors are G-protein-coupled: binding activates a G protein, which may open a nearby channel or trigger a second messenger such as cyclic AMP, producing slower, longer-lasting and more widespread effects, including changes in gene expression. Neurotransmitters themselves come in two sizes. Small-molecule transmitters (amino acids, monoamines and acetylcholine) are made in the terminal and released quickly. Large-molecule transmitters, the neuropeptides such as endorphins, substance P and oxytocin, are short chains of amino acids made in the cell body and packaged in large vesicles. Small-molecule transmitters can act on either ionotropic or metabotropic receptors, but neuropeptides act through metabotropic receptors. So, in the standard textbook account, only small-molecule transmitters bind ionotropic receptors. Autoreceptors on the presynaptic neuron, which monitor and reduce release, are typically metabotropic too. Many drugs act at ionotropic sites: benzodiazepines and alcohol enhance GABA-A receptors, while ketamine and phencyclidine block NMDA receptors. Option a is right because ionotropic receptors are bound by small-molecule transmitters only, while large neuropeptides work through metabotropic receptors.","traps":{"b":"Large-molecule transmitters, the neuropeptides such as endorphins, substance P, oxytocin and neuropeptide Y, act on metabotropic, G-protein-coupled receptors, not on ligand-gated ion channels. So ionotropic receptors do not bind both sizes. It tempts because small transmitters do bind both receptor types, and a candidate can easily turn that fact around into 'both sizes of transmitter bind ionotropic receptors'.","c":"This reverses the truth. Large transmitters (neuropeptides) work through metabotropic receptors, which act slowly through G proteins and second messengers. Fast ionotropic receptors such as the nicotinic, AMPA, NMDA and GABA-A receptors are bound by small-molecule transmitters. It tempts candidates who associate 'large' with powerful, fast effects, when in fact neuropeptide effects are slow, diffuse and long-lasting.","d":"'None of the above' would be correct only if every listed statement were false. Small-molecule transmitters only is the accepted textbook answer, so this escape option fails. It tempts candidates who know that rare exceptions exist, such as a peptide-gated channel found in snails, or who are unsure about receptor classes and fall back on the safe-sounding choice."}},{"q":"Varicosities are ________ synapses and they release  ___________ neurotransmitters","concept":"Synapses can be classified by how precisely release is targeted. At directed synapses the site of release and the site of reception are very close, separated only by a narrow synaptic cleft a few tens of nanometres wide, as at classic axodendritic and axosomatic synapses. Fast small-molecule transmitters such as glutamate, GABA and acetylcholine typically act this way, point to point. At nondirected synapses the release site is some distance from the receptors. Transmitter is released from varicosities, bead-like swellings spaced along an axon and its branches, so the axon looks like a string of beads; these are also called string-of-beads or en passant synapses. The transmitter diffuses through the extracellular fluid and affects many cells over a wide area, a pattern known as volume transmission. Monoamines are the classic nondirected transmitters. Norepinephrine neurons of the locus coeruleus, serotonin neurons of the raphe nuclei and dopamine neurons of the midbrain have few cell bodies, clustered in the brainstem, but highly branched varicose axons that spread through much of the brain, which suits their modulatory roles in arousal, mood and reward. In the periphery, sympathetic postganglionic neurons release norepinephrine from varicosities onto smooth muscle. Neuropeptides are also often released diffusely. Examiners pair this topic with other synapse types: axoaxonic synapses, which mediate presynaptic inhibition or facilitation, dendrodendritic synapses, and electrical synapses formed by gap junctions. Option a is right because varicosities are nondirected synapses and the transmitters they classically release are monoamines.","traps":{"b":"Both halves are wrong. Directed synapses have release and receptor sites close together across a narrow cleft, and they are where fast amino acid transmitters such as glutamate and GABA usually act. Varicosities are the opposite case, releasing transmitter at a distance from their targets. It tempts because 'directed; amino acid' is itself a correct description of ordinary fast synapses, just not of varicosities.","c":"'Non-directed' is correct, but 'amino acid' is not. Amino acid transmitters (glutamate, GABA, glycine, aspartate) mostly act at directed synapses where fast, precise point-to-point signalling is needed. The varicose, string-of-beads release pattern is typical of monoamines such as norepinephrine and serotonin. It tempts because the first half is right, so a candidate who knows only the synapse type may stop there.","d":"'Monoamine' is correct, but 'directed' is wrong. Varicosities release transmitter into the extracellular fluid at some distance from target receptors, so they are nondirected by definition. A directed synapse needs the release site to face the receptor site across a narrow cleft. It tempts candidates who remember the monoamine half correctly but assume every chemical synapse is directed, as in the standard textbook diagram."}},{"q":"What is Error degrees of freedom for a randomized block design with 4 treatments and 5 blocks (1 sample per block)","concept":"The randomized block design (RBD) was developed by Ronald A. Fisher and set out in The Design of Experiments (1935). Similar units (plots of similar soil, participants matched on age or IQ, or the same person tested repeatedly) are grouped into blocks, and every treatment is randomly assigned once within each block. Blocking pulls a known nuisance source of variance out of the error term, so the F test becomes more powerful. The data are analysed with a two-way ANOVA without replication, and the degrees of freedom (df) are partitioned as: total = N - 1; treatments = t - 1; blocks = b - 1; error (residual) = (t - 1)(b - 1). With one score per cell, the treatment by block interaction serves as the error term. Here N = 20, so total df = 19, treatment df = 3, block df = 4, and error df = 19 - 3 - 4 = 12. Neighbouring designs: a completely randomized one-way ANOVA with the same 20 scores in 4 groups has error df = N - k = 16, because block variance stays in the error. A repeated-measures ANOVA is an RBD in which each subject is a block, so error df = (k - 1)(n - 1). A Latin square with t treatments has error df = (t - 1)(t - 2). Option a is right because the error df of a randomized block design is (t - 1)(b - 1) = (4 - 1)(5 - 1) = 3 x 4 = 12.","traps":{"b":"20 is N, the total number of observations (4 treatments x 5 blocks, one score per cell). Degrees of freedom are always fewer than N because each estimated mean uses up information: even the total df is N - 1 = 19, and the error df is what remains after treatment and block df are removed. It tempts because 4 x 5 is the first multiplication a student does with these numbers.","c":"15 is what you get from N - b (20 - 5), or from total df minus block df (19 - 4), which ignores the 3 treatment df. It would be the within-groups df if the five blocks were treated as the only grouping factor. It tempts because it looks like a proper subtraction from N, but in a randomized block design both the treatment df and the block df must be removed.","d":"16 is N - k = 20 - 4, the error (within-groups) df of a completely randomized one-way ANOVA with 4 groups of 5. That design does not remove block variance, so its error term keeps the 4 block df. It tempts because the one-way ANOVA formula is the one most students memorise first, and it gives a believable number close to the correct 12."}},{"q":"how many pairwise comparisons will have to be made post-hoc for an Independent variable with 4 levels?","concept":"A significant omnibus ANOVA F test says only that at least one group mean differs from another; post hoc (a posteriori) tests find which pairs differ. The number of distinct pairs among k means is the combination k(k - 1)/2, also written kC2. With k = 4 levels (call them A, B, C, D) the pairs are AB, AC, AD, BC, BD and CD: 4 x 3 / 2 = 6. Order does not matter, since comparing A with B is the same test as comparing B with A, which is why k(k - 1) is divided by 2. Quick values: 3 levels give 3 pairs, 5 give 10, 6 give 15. The count matters because of familywise error. If each test uses alpha = .05, the chance of at least one Type I error across c tests is roughly 1 - (1 - .05)^c, about .26 for 6 tests. The Bonferroni correction (from Carlo Emilio Bonferroni's inequality, applied to multiple comparisons by Olive Jean Dunn in 1961) divides alpha by c, so .05/6 = .0083 per comparison. Common post hoc tests are John W. Tukey's honestly significant difference test (all pairwise comparisons), Henry Scheffe's (1953) test (all possible contrasts, the most conservative), Ronald A. Fisher's least significant difference (the least conservative) and Charles Dunnett's (1955) test, which compares each treatment only with one control group, giving k - 1 = 3 comparisons. Option d is right because an independent variable with 4 levels yields 4 x 3 / 2 = 6 unique pairwise comparisons.","traps":{"a":"8 is 2 x k, a number with no basis in counting pairs. It may come from confusing the 4 levels with a 2 x 4 factorial layout or from adding comparisons carelessly. The correct count is the combination kC2 = k(k - 1)/2. It tempts because it is a round, plausible-looking number that sits between the true answer of 6 and the double count of 12.","b":"12 is k(k - 1) = 4 x 3, the number of ordered pairs. It counts A versus B and B versus A as two different comparisons, which is a permutation, not a combination. In post hoc testing the direction of a comparison does not change the test, so the product must be divided by 2. It tempts because students remember the numerator of the formula and forget the halving.","c":"4 is simply the number of levels, k. It may also be confused with the k - 1 = 3 comparisons in Dunnett's test, where each treatment is compared only with a control, or with the k - 1 degrees of freedom available for planned orthogonal contrasts. It tempts because it repeats a number from the stem, but testing all pairs after the ANOVA needs k(k - 1)/2 = 6 comparisons."}},{"q":"Which of the following rotation methods can be used for second order factor analysis","concept":"Factor analysis first extracts factors, for example by principal axis factoring, and then rotates them to make the loadings easier to interpret. Louis L. Thurstone (1947) set the goal of simple structure: each variable should load highly on one factor and near zero on the rest. Rotations come in two families. Orthogonal rotations keep the factor axes at 90 degrees, so the factors are uncorrelated. Varimax, from Henry F. Kaiser (1958), simplifies the columns of the loading matrix so that each factor has a few high loadings; Quartimax simplifies the rows so that each variable loads on as few factors as possible, often leaving a general factor; Equimax is a compromise between the two. Oblique rotations let the axes take any angle, so the factors may correlate. Promax, from Alan Hendrickson and Paul White (1964), starts from a Varimax solution and raises the loadings to a power to relax orthogonality; Direct Oblimin is the other common choice. An oblique solution gives a pattern matrix, a structure matrix and a factor correlation matrix. Second-order (higher-order) factor analysis factors that correlation matrix among the first-order factors to find broader factors above them. Classic examples are the global factors of Raymond B. Cattell's 16PF (1949) and the general factor g found above Thurstone's primary mental abilities. If the first-order factors are forced to be uncorrelated, their correlation matrix is an identity matrix and there is nothing left to factor. Option a is right because Promax is the only oblique rotation listed, and only correlated first-order factors can yield second-order factors.","traps":{"b":"Equimax is an orthogonal rotation proposed as a compromise between Varimax and Quartimax, balancing simplicity of the factors (columns) and of the variables (rows). Because it keeps the axes at right angles, the factors it produces are uncorrelated, so there is no factor correlation matrix to analyse at a second order. It tempts because its name is less familiar, which makes students guess it is the special one.","c":"Varimax, developed by Henry F. Kaiser (1958), is the most widely used orthogonal rotation. It maximises the variance of the squared loadings within each factor, so every factor has a few high and many near-zero loadings. Its factors are uncorrelated by design, which rules out higher-order factors. It tempts because it is the rotation students know best and the one reported in most published factor analyses.","d":"Quartimax is an orthogonal rotation that simplifies the rows of the loading matrix, trying to make each variable load on as few factors as possible. It often produces one large general factor plus smaller ones. Even so, its factors stay uncorrelated, so no second-order analysis is possible. It tempts precisely because its general-factor tendency resembles the idea of a higher-order g."}},{"q":"An uninvolved objective observation is done in ___________ approach of phenomenology; while an involved observation is done in ____________ approach of phenomenology","concept":"Phenomenology, founded by the philosopher Edmund Husserl (Logical Investigations, 1900-1901), is the study of experience as it appears to the person who lives it. Its rule is to suspend prior theory (epoche or bracketing) and describe the phenomenon itself. Karl Jaspers brought it into psychiatry in General Psychopathology (1913): the clinician tries to grasp the patient's inner experience through empathy and careful description, separating the form of a symptom (for example, a hallucination) from its content (what the voice says). The classification in this question sorts phenomenological observation by the observer's position. In the direct approach the observer is involved: he enters or shares the experience, as in first-person description of one's own experience or empathic, participant-style understanding of another person. In the indirect approach the observer stays uninvolved and records the person's reports, expressions and behaviour objectively from outside, as a third party. 'Semi-direct' is not a standard category. Related distinctions an examiner may swap in: participant versus non-participant observation in field research; introspection, which in the laboratory of Wilhelm Wundt (1879) was trained self-observation of sensations, versus phenomenological description, which keeps meaning intact; and the descriptive phenomenological method of Amedeo Giorgi (1970) versus the interpretative phenomenological analysis (IPA) of Jonathan A. Smith (1996). A memory aid: involvement means you are directly inside the experience; detachment means you see it indirectly from outside. Option a is right because uninvolved, objective observation belongs to the indirect approach and involved observation belongs to the direct approach.","traps":{"b":"This puts the uninvolved observation under the direct approach, which reverses the logic: direct means the observer is inside the experience, involved, not standing apart. It then adds 'semi-direct', which is not a recognised approach in phenomenology. It tempts because 'direct observation' in general research methods means watching behaviour as it happens, which can sound objective and uninvolved.","c":"The first half is correct: uninvolved, objective observation is the indirect approach. The second half fails because involved observation is the direct approach, and 'semi-direct' is an invented middle category, not a phenomenological term. It tempts because candidates who know only the first half pick any option starting with 'indirect', and 'semi-direct' sounds like a cautious compromise between involvement and detachment.","d":"This is the exact reversal of the key. It pairs detached, objective observation with the direct approach and involved observation with the indirect one. It tempts because in everyday research language 'direct observation' means an observer watching behaviour objectively, as in naturalistic observation, so students carry that meaning over. In this phenomenological classification, direct refers to being inside the experience, which is involvement."}},{"q":"Survey research does NOT","concept":"Survey research collects information from a sample, usually through questionnaires or interviews, in order to describe a larger population. The classic definition comes from Fred N. Kerlinger in Foundations of Behavioral Research (first published 1964): survey research studies large and small populations by selecting and studying samples drawn from them to discover the relative incidence, distribution and interrelations of sociological and psychological variables. Those three words map onto three of the options here. Relative incidence is how common something is, for example the prevalence of depression among college students. Distribution is how a trait spreads across age, gender or region. Interrelations are correlations between variables such as income and stress. Kerlinger classed surveys as non-experimental, ex post facto research: the researcher measures variables as they already exist and manipulates nothing. That is why survey data cannot isolate the factors that cause an event. Establishing causal factors needs an experiment, in which the independent variable is manipulated, participants are randomly assigned and extraneous variables are controlled, or at least a strong quasi-experimental design. Know the survey types an examiner may use: cross-sectional surveys (one point in time), longitudinal surveys (panel or trend studies over time), and the census versus the sample survey. Typical weaknesses are self-report bias, social desirability, non-response and sampling error. Option c is right because identifying the causal factors of an event lies outside survey research, while incidence, distribution and relationships between variables are exactly what Kerlinger's definition says surveys study.","traps":{"a":"Studying the distribution of a trait, for example how anxiety scores spread across age groups, states or income levels, is one of the three aims in Fred N. Kerlinger's definition of survey research. Surveys describe a population as it is, and distribution is a purely descriptive question that needs no manipulation. It tempts because NOT questions make candidates hunt for anything that sounds too simple, but this is a core survey function.","b":"Surveys routinely study relationships between variables, the 'interrelations' in Kerlinger's definition, using correlation and regression on the data collected, such as the link between screen time and sleep quality. What surveys cannot do is show that one variable causes the other. It tempts because students remember 'surveys cannot show causation' and wrongly stretch it to 'surveys cannot study relationships'.","d":"Relative incidence means how often a phenomenon occurs in one group compared with another, such as the rate of substance use among urban versus rural adolescents. Epidemiological prevalence surveys, including India's National Mental Health Survey, exist to answer exactly this kind of question. It tempts because 'incidence' sounds like a technical epidemiology term, but it appears word for word in the classic definition of survey research."}},{"q":"An exploratory research study is also a","concept":"Research designs are often classified by purpose, a scheme set out by Claire Selltiz, Marie Jahoda, Morton Deutsch and Stuart Cook in Research Methods in Social Relations (1959) and repeated in Indian research methodology texts. Exploratory, also called formulative, research is used when a problem is new or poorly understood. Its aim is to become familiar with the phenomenon, clarify concepts, set priorities and formulate precise questions and hypotheses for later work. It uses flexible methods: review of the literature, the experience survey (interviewing people with practical knowledge of the problem), analysis of insight-stimulating cases, focus groups and pilot studies. Because it starts without a firm hypothesis, it is hypothesis-generating. Descriptive research portrays the characteristics of a group or situation accurately, for example the prevalence of a disorder, and diagnostic research asks how often something occurs together with something else; both need more structured designs. Hypothesis-testing (experimental, confirmatory) research tests a causal hypothesis stated in advance, which normally means manipulating an independent variable, randomising and controlling extraneous variables. The same contrast appears in qualitative versus quantitative work: the grounded theory of Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss (1967) is inductive and builds theory from data, while a randomised experiment is deductive and checks a prediction. A useful sequence to remember is explore, then describe, then test. Option b is right because exploratory research is done to discover insights and produce hypotheses, not to manipulate variables or to test hypotheses that already exist.","traps":{"a":"Variable manipulation is the defining feature of experimental research, where the researcher changes an independent variable and measures its effect on a dependent variable under controlled conditions. Exploratory work comes before that stage: it uses flexible, open methods such as literature review, experience surveys and case analysis, and usually manipulates nothing. It tempts because many students treat the experiment as the standard model of all research.","c":"'All of the above' would require exploratory research to manipulate variables, generate hypotheses and test hypotheses at the same time. Manipulation and hypothesis testing belong to experimental, confirmatory research, so only one listed option fits. It tempts because exploratory work is flexible and may borrow many techniques, which makes 'all' feel safe, and because the option sits third rather than last, so it is easy to misread.","d":"Hypothesis-testing (confirmatory) research starts with a specific prediction, derived from theory or from earlier exploratory work, and tests it, usually through an experiment with manipulation, random assignment and significance testing. It is the stage that follows exploratory research, not a description of it. It tempts because the word 'hypothesis' appears in both b and d, so a candidate reading quickly may pick the more familiar phrase."}},{"q":"In grounded theory, the process of weaving the data back together around groups of concepts is known as","concept":"Grounded theory was introduced by Barney Glaser and Anselm Strauss in The Discovery of Grounded Theory (1967). It builds theory inductively from qualitative data, such as interview transcripts, rather than testing a theory chosen in advance. Its core tools are constant comparison (each new piece of data is compared with earlier codes), theoretical sampling (choosing the next cases to develop emerging categories), memo writing, and theoretical saturation (stopping when new data add no new properties). The three-step coding scheme most exams ask about comes from Anselm Strauss and Juliet Corbin in Basics of Qualitative Research (1990). Open coding breaks the data apart: lines and incidents are labelled with concepts, and similar concepts are grouped into categories with properties and dimensions. Axial coding then puts the data back together in new ways by making connections between a category and its subcategories, around an 'axis'. Its coding paradigm asks about causal conditions, context, intervening conditions, action and interaction strategies, and consequences. Selective coding finally chooses one core category and integrates all the others around it to tell the storyline of the theory. Versions differ: Glaser later rejected axial coding as forcing the data and kept substantive and theoretical coding, while Kathy Charmaz (2006), in constructivist grounded theory, uses initial coding and focused coding. Option b is right because axial coding is the stage that weaves the fragmented data back together by relating groups of concepts, the categories and subcategories, to one another.","traps":{"a":"'Early coding' is not a named stage in any major version of grounded theory. The first stage, which breaks the data into labelled concepts, is open coding in Anselm Strauss and Juliet Corbin (1990) and initial coding in Kathy Charmaz (2006). It tempts because it sounds like a plain-English description of the first step, and candidates who forget the technical terms may choose a word that feels chronological.","c":"Open coding is the first analytic step in Anselm Strauss and Juliet Corbin's (1990) scheme. The researcher reads the data line by line, fractures it into discrete incidents, labels them with concepts and groups those concepts into tentative categories. It takes the data apart rather than weaving it back together. It tempts because open coding does involve grouping concepts into categories, which looks close to the stem's wording.","d":"'Core coding' is not a standard grounded theory term. What exists is the core category, the central concept chosen during selective coding, around which all the other categories are integrated into a single theory. Selective coding integrates the whole theory around one idea, while axial coding links categories with their subcategories. It tempts because 'core' is a real grounded theory word, borrowed here into a fake label."}},{"q":"What is the degrees of freedom on a one-sample t-test with a sample size of 30","concept":"Degrees of freedom (df) are the number of values in a calculation that are free to vary once certain quantities have been fixed. The one-sample t-test, introduced by William Sealy Gosset writing as 'Student' (1908), compares a sample mean with a known or hypothesised population mean: t = (sample mean - population mean) / (s / square root of n). The sample standard deviation s is calculated around the sample mean, and fixing that mean uses up one piece of information: if you know the mean of 30 scores and any 29 of the scores, the 30th is determined. So df = n - 1. This is also why the sample variance divides by n - 1 rather than n, which gives an unbiased estimate of the population variance. As df increase, the t distribution, which has heavier tails than the normal curve, approaches the z distribution; at df = 29 the two-tailed .05 critical t is about 2.045, compared with 1.96 for z. Learn the neighbouring formulas: the paired-samples t uses n - 1 where n is the number of pairs; the independent-samples t uses n1 + n2 - 2; a Pearson r is tested with n - 2; chi-square goodness of fit uses k - 1 and the contingency test uses (r - 1)(c - 1); one-way ANOVA uses k - 1 between groups and N - k within groups. Option c is right because a one-sample t-test with n = 30 has df = 30 - 1 = 29.","traps":{"a":"30 is the sample size n itself. Degrees of freedom are not the number of scores but the number of independent pieces of information left after estimating parameters, and estimating the sample mean costs one. It tempts because 30 is also the sample size at which many textbooks say the t distribution becomes close to normal, so the number feels meaningful in this stem.","b":"28 is n - 2, the df for an independent-samples t-test with two groups of 15 (n1 + n2 - 2), for testing a Pearson correlation, or for a simple regression slope, where two parameters are estimated. A one-sample t estimates only one parameter, the mean, so only one df is lost. It tempts because n - 2 is a real and frequently tested formula, just for a different test.","d":"31 is n + 1, which has no statistical basis: estimating a parameter removes degrees of freedom and never adds them, so df cannot exceed n here. A student may reach this number by misremembering the sign in n - 1. It tempts mainly as a careless slip under exam pressure, sitting symmetrically on the other side of the sample size from the correct answer."}},{"q":"Which of the following measures of variability is affected by extreme scores in the sample","concept":"Measures of variability describe how spread out scores are, and they differ in how much an extreme score (an outlier) affects them. The range, highest minus lowest score, depends entirely on the two extremes, so it is the most affected. The variance and the standard deviation (SD), a term introduced by Karl Pearson (1894), are computed from every score's deviation from the mean, and those deviations are squared, so one very large deviation adds a disproportionately large amount. Example: the scores 4, 5, 5, 6, 5 have an SD of about 0.7; replace one 5 with 25 and the SD jumps to about 9, while the median stays at 5. The mean, on which the SD is built, is pulled the same way. Resistant (robust) measures ignore the tails. The interquartile range (IQR) is Q3 minus Q1, the spread of the middle 50 percent of scores, and the quartile deviation or semi-interquartile range is (Q3 - Q1)/2. Because quartiles depend on rank position, not on how far out an extreme score lies, outliers barely move them. The same logic applies to central tendency: the median resists outliers, the mean does not. So for skewed data or data with outliers, texts recommend the median with the IQR or quartile deviation, and for symmetric data the mean with the SD. The mean (average) deviation is also affected, though less than the SD because it does not square. Option c is right because the standard deviation uses every score's squared distance from the mean, so extreme scores inflate it.","traps":{"a":"The interquartile range is Q3 minus Q1, the spread of the middle 50 percent of scores. It ignores the lowest and highest quarters entirely, so an extreme score in either tail does not change it; the boxplot of John W. Tukey (1977) uses it to flag outliers. It tempts because it is a measure of variability students know well, but it is the classic resistant measure, the opposite of what the stem asks.","b":"The quartile deviation, also called the semi-interquartile range, is (Q3 - Q1)/2, half the interquartile range. Like the IQR it depends only on the positions of the quartiles, so extreme scores in the tails leave it unchanged. Indian statistics texts pair it with the median for skewed or open-ended distributions for exactly this reason. It tempts because its name contains 'deviation', which sounds like the standard deviation.","d":"'None of the above' would mean that no listed measure is affected by extreme scores. The standard deviation clearly is, because squaring the deviations magnifies large distances from the mean. It tempts candidates who know that two of the three listed measures are resistant and wrongly generalise that all measures of spread are, or who over-think the item because the answer feels too obvious."}},{"q":"On a Binet-Simon type test (mean IQ= 100, sd =16), a person has secured an IQ of 136, what percentage of people fall below him?","concept":"Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon published the first practical intelligence scale in France in 1905. Lewis Terman's Stanford-Binet (1916) reported a ratio IQ, mental age divided by chronological age times 100, building on William Stern's (1912) mental quotient. Ratio IQs fail for adults, so David Wechsler (1939) introduced the deviation IQ: a standard score showing where a person stands within his own age group's normal distribution. Wechsler scales use mean 100 and SD 15. The 1960 Stanford-Binet (Form L-M) used mean 100 and SD 16, which is why the stem says SD 16; the fifth edition (2003) uses 15. To find a percentile, convert to z: z = (X - mean)/SD = (136 - 100)/16 = 36/16 = 2.25. Then look up the area under the normal curve below that z. Benchmarks to memorise: z = 0 gives 50 percent, z = 1 about 84 percent, z = 1.28 about 90 percent, z = 1.645 about 95 percent, z = 1.96 about 97.5 percent, z = 2 about 97.7 percent, z = 2.25 about 98.8 percent, and z = 3 about 99.9 percent. The 68-95-99.7 rule gives the areas within 1, 2 and 3 SDs of the mean. Variants change the SD: the same IQ of 136 on a Wechsler scale (SD 15) gives z = 2.4, about the 99th percentile. Option c is right because z = 2.25 leaves about 98.8 percent of the normal curve below it, and 98.7 percent is the nearest option.","traps":{"a":"97.5 percent is the area below z = 1.96, the familiar two-tailed .05 critical value, which on this scale is an IQ of about 131 (100 + 1.96 x 16). An IQ of 136 lies further out, at z = 2.25, so more people fall below it. It tempts because 1.96 and 97.5 percent are the most memorised normal-curve pair, and 136 looks roughly like 'two SDs above the mean'.","b":"90 percent is the area below z = 1.28, which on this scale is an IQ of about 120. That is far too low for a score 36 points, or 2.25 SDs, above the mean. It tempts candidates who estimate by feel rather than computing z, or who mix up the 'top 10 percent' cut-offs used in gifted-education screening with the precise percentile for this score.","d":"84 percent is the area below z = 1, one SD above the mean, which here would be an IQ of 116. It is the most familiar benchmark (50 percent below the mean plus 34 percent between the mean and +1 SD). It tempts students who forget to divide by the SD and simply see 136 as 'one step above average', or who use the 68 percent rule without converting the score."}},{"q":"Ordered variables are considered to __________, while nominal variables are considered to be ___________","concept":"Stanley Smith Stevens (1946) defined four levels of measurement. Nominal scales only name or classify (gender, religion, diagnosis); numbers are labels and the only meaningful operation is counting frequencies. Ordinal scales rank (class rank, socioeconomic grade, Likert agreement); the order is meaningful but the gaps between ranks are not known to be equal. Interval scales have equal units but no true zero (Celsius temperature, standard IQ scores), and ratio scales have a true zero (reaction time, weight). A second, cruder division used in statistics and software is categorical versus continuous. Categorical (qualitative) variables place cases in groups with no inherent quantity; nominal variables are always categorical. Continuous (quantitative) variables can in principle take any value along a dimension. Ordered variables sit in between. Because ranks reflect an underlying dimension, many psychology texts treat ordered variables, especially summed rating scales, as continuous for analysis, and that convention is what this item tests. Strictly, though, ordinal data are discrete, and categorical-data books such as that of Alan Agresti (1990) call them ordered categorical variables. A related distinction: discrete (discontinuous) variables take only separate values, such as number of children, while continuous ones can take fractions, such as time. Examiners also ask which statistics suit each level: mode and chi-square for nominal; median, percentiles and Spearman's rho for ordinal; mean, SD, Pearson r and t-tests for interval and ratio. Option b is right because the item contrasts ordered variables, treated as lying on a continuum, with nominal variables, which are purely categorical labels.","traps":{"a":"'Categorical, Continuous' reverses the key. Calling ordered variables categorical has some technical support, since ordinal data are ordered categories, but calling nominal variables continuous is impossible: nominal numbers are labels such as 1 = male and 2 = female, with no quantity or order. It tempts because the first half sounds right to students who have read that ordinal data are 'ordered categorical'.","c":"'Discontinuous, Categorical' calls ordered variables discontinuous (discrete) and nominal variables categorical. Strictly, ordinal ranks are discrete, so this is not absurd; but nominal variables are also discontinuous, so the option sets up no real contrast between the two blanks. The item follows the common psychology convention of treating ordered variables as continuous. It tempts because 'ranks are discrete' is a true textbook statement.","d":"'Categorical; Discontinuous' calls ordered variables categorical and nominal variables discontinuous. Both labels describe the same broad family of non-continuous data, so the option does not separate the two types the stem contrasts. Nominal variables are best described as categorical; 'discontinuous' is the usual label for counts such as number of siblings. It tempts because both words can technically be applied to non-quantitative data."}},{"q":"Which of the following can NOT be used a test of homogeneity of variance before regression","concept":"Most parametric tests assume homogeneity of variance. In the t-test and ANOVA this means the groups have equal population variances. In regression the matching assumption is homoscedasticity: the spread of the residuals is the same at every level of the predicted value. When it fails (heteroscedasticity), the regression coefficients stay unbiased but their standard errors, and so the t and F tests, become unreliable. Tests of equal variances include Maurice Bartlett's (1937) test, which is powerful but very sensitive to non-normality, so a significant result may only reflect skewed data; Howard Levene's (1960) test, which runs an ANOVA on the absolute deviations of scores from their group mean and is robust to non-normality; the version by Morton Brown and Alan Forsythe (1974), which uses deviations from the median; and Herman Otto Hartley's (1950) Fmax, the largest group variance divided by the smallest. For regression residuals specifically, analysts also plot residuals against fitted values or use the test of Trevor Breusch and Adrian Pagan (1979). The test of Samuel Shapiro and Martin Wilk (1965) answers a different question: whether a sample comes from a normally distributed population. A significant W means the data depart from normality. Q-Q plots and skewness and kurtosis values serve the same purpose. Keep the assumption checks apart: normality (Shapiro-Wilk), equal variance (Levene, Bartlett), independence of errors (the statistic of James Durbin and Geoffrey Watson, 1950) and multicollinearity (variance inflation factor). Option c is right because Shapiro-Wilk tests normality, not equality of variances, so it cannot serve as a homogeneity-of-variance test.","traps":{"a":"Levene's test, from Howard Levene (1960), checks whether several groups have equal variances by running an ANOVA on each score's absolute deviation from its group mean. A non-significant result supports homogeneity. It is robust to non-normality and is printed automatically with the independent-samples t-test in SPSS. It tempts because in a NOT question a hurried candidate may simply pick the most familiar name.","b":"Bartlett's test, from Maurice Bartlett (1937), is a chi-square based test of equal variances across groups. It is powerful when the data are normal but very sensitive to departures from normality, so Levene's test is usually preferred. It is still a genuine homogeneity-of-variance test, so it can be used. It tempts because candidates remember its sensitivity to non-normality and confuse that with being a normality test.","d":"'None of the above' would mean all three listed tests can check homogeneity of variance. That fails because the Shapiro-Wilk test, by Samuel Shapiro and Martin Wilk (1965), tests the normality of a distribution, a separate assumption. It tempts because regression assumption checks are usually taught together in one list, and students blur normality, homoscedasticity and linearity into a single 'assumption testing' step."}},{"q":"The correlation between two variable where the common overlap with a third variable is statistically controlled is called","concept":"Correlation coefficients describe how two variables vary together, and Karl Pearson's product-moment r (1896) is the basic index for two continuous variables. Often, though, the link between X and Y partly reflects a third variable Z related to both: height and vocabulary are correlated in children because both rise with age. Partial correlation, written r12.3, removes the influence of the third variable from both X and Y and correlates what is left (the residuals). The first-order formula is r12.3 = (r12 - r13 x r23) / square root of [(1 - r13 squared)(1 - r23 squared)]. Controlling one variable gives a first-order partial, controlling two gives a second-order partial, and the plain r is a zero-order correlation. If the partial correlation drops near zero, the original relationship was spurious, explained by the third variable. Neighbouring indices an examiner may swap in: the semipartial or part correlation, r1(2.3), removes the third variable from only one of the two variables, and its square shows the unique variance a predictor adds in multiple regression; multiple correlation, R, is the correlation between one criterion and the best weighted combination of several predictors, and R squared is the variance they explain together; Charles Spearman's (1904) rho correlates ranks; the phi coefficient is used when both variables are true dichotomies (a 2 x 2 table); point-biserial r suits one true dichotomy and one continuous variable. Option b is right because partial correlation is precisely the correlation between two variables with their common overlap with a third variable statistically held constant.","traps":{"a":"Multiple correlation (R) measures the relationship between one criterion variable and a weighted combination of two or more predictors, as in multiple regression; R squared gives the variance they jointly explain. It combines variables rather than removing a third variable's influence from a two-variable relationship. It tempts because it also involves three or more variables, so it seems to fit a stem that mentions a third variable.","c":"Spearman's rank-order correlation, rho, from Charles Spearman (1904), applies Pearson's formula to ranks, so it suits ordinal data or monotonic relationships with outliers. It measures a simple two-variable relationship and does not control any third variable. It tempts because Spearman is the most famous name on the list and because his work on g and factor analysis involved correlating many variables at once.","d":"The phi coefficient measures association between two variables that are both genuine dichotomies, arranged in a 2 x 2 table, such as gender and pass or fail. It is a Pearson r computed on 0 and 1 codes and equals the square root of chi-square divided by N. It does not partial out a third variable. It tempts because it is another named special correlation taught in the same chapter."}},{"q":"Social loafing is also called __________","concept":"Social loafing is the reduction of individual effort when people pool their efforts toward a group goal, compared with working alone. Its roots lie in the work of the French agricultural engineer Max Ringelmann, whose rope-pulling studies (published 1913) showed that as group size grew, total force rose but force per person fell. This decline is called the Ringelmann effect. Alan Ingham and colleagues (1974) separated its two causes, coordination loss (people pulling at different moments) and motivation loss: blindfolded participants who only believed others were pulling with them still pulled less, showing a real loss of motivation. Bibb Latané, Kipling Williams and Stephen Harkins (1979) named this motivation loss social loafing in their clapping and shouting studies. Steven Karau and Kipling Williams (1993) explained it with the collective effort model: people work hard only when they expect their effort to matter and value the outcome. Loafing decreases when individual output is identifiable, the task is meaningful, the group is cohesive, and in collectivist cultures. Contrast social facilitation, the improvement in performance when others are present, first shown by Norman Triplett (1898) with cyclists and children winding fishing reels, and explained by Robert Zajonc (1965) as arousal strengthening dominant responses. Related group effects are the free-rider effect and the sucker effect described by Norbert Kerr (1983). Option a is right because social loafing is also known as the Ringelmann effect, after Max Ringelmann's rope-pulling findings.","traps":{"b":"The 'Triplett effect' is an informal name for social facilitation, from Norman Triplett (1898), who found that cyclists rode faster when racing others or with a pacer than alone, and that children wound fishing reels faster in pairs. That is an increase in performance in the presence of others, the opposite direction to loafing. It tempts because both Triplett and Ringelmann are classic early studies of performance in groups.","c":"The McGurk effect, reported by Harry McGurk and John MacDonald (1976), is an audiovisual speech illusion: when a video of a face saying 'ga' is dubbed with the sound 'ba', viewers often hear 'da'. It shows that vision changes what we hear and belongs to perception, not group behaviour. It tempts only because it is an eponymous 'effect' and fits the pattern of the other options.","d":"The free-rider effect, studied by Norbert Kerr and Steven Bruun (1983), is reduced effort because a member feels his contribution is dispensable, since others will achieve the goal anyway. It is one cause or form of motivation loss in groups, closely linked to loafing, but it is not the accepted alternative name for social loafing. It tempts because the meaning is close and the term sounds like everyday group-work slang."}},{"q":"What is the distinct type of motivation proposed by Maslow for self-actualizers?","concept":"Abraham Maslow set out his hierarchy of needs in 'A Theory of Human Motivation' (1943): physiological, safety, belongingness and love, esteem, and self-actualisation. He later grouped the first four as deficiency needs, or D-needs. A D-need is felt as a lack; satisfying it removes tension, and once satisfied it stops motivating. Behaviour driven by these needs is D-motivation (deficiency motivation). Self-actualisation is different: it is a growth need, and satisfying it does not end the motive but strengthens it. In 'A Theory of Metamotivation' (1967), Maslow argued that self-actualising people, whose basic needs are largely met, are moved by metamotivation, also called being-motivation, B-motivation or growth motivation. They are drawn to B-values (metaneeds) such as truth, goodness, beauty, wholeness, justice, aliveness, uniqueness and simplicity. When B-values are blocked, metapathologies such as apathy, cynicism and alienation result. Maslow drew the same D versus B contrast elsewhere: D-love (needing love from others) versus B-love (non-possessive love for another person's being), and D-cognition versus B-cognition, the latter typical of peak experiences. His self-actualisers, studied through figures such as Abraham Lincoln and Eleanor Roosevelt, showed accurate perception of reality, acceptance, spontaneity, problem-centredness and autonomy. Metamotivation overlaps with, but is not the same as, intrinsic motivation in the self-determination theory of Edward Deci and Richard Ryan (1985). Option d is right because Maslow named metamotivation as the distinct motivation of self-actualisers, in contrast to the deficiency motivation of people still meeting basic needs.","traps":{"a":"D-motivation, or deficiency motivation, is Maslow's term for behaviour driven by unmet lower needs: hunger, safety, belonging and esteem. It aims at reducing tension caused by a lack, and it stops once the need is met. Self-actualisers have largely moved beyond it, so it is the contrast to their distinctive motivation, not the answer. It tempts because it is a genuine Maslow term with the same letter-plus-motivation format.","b":"'E-motivation' is not a term in Maslow's theory or in mainstream motivation psychology. It may be built to suggest 'esteem', the fourth level of the hierarchy, but esteem is a deficiency need and its motivation is D-motivation. It tempts because it copies the format of the real term D-motivation, and a candidate who half-remembers 'letter motivations' may think there is a whole series of them.","c":"'F-motivation' is also not a Maslowian or standard psychological term. It may be designed to suggest 'fulfilment', which sounds like self-actualisation, but Maslow's word for the growth-driven motivation of self-actualisers is metamotivation, or B-motivation (being-motivation). It tempts because 'fulfilment' and 'self-actualisation' feel like synonyms, and the letter pattern of the options invites guessing."}},{"q":"According to self-determination theory, what are the three basic needs that lead to a state of well-being?","concept":"Self-determination theory (SDT) was developed by Edward L. Deci and Richard M. Ryan, whose main early statement was Intrinsic Motivation and Self-Determination in Human Behavior (1985). It grew from Deci's (1971) finding that paying students for solving puzzles reduced their later free-time interest in the task, an undermining effect also shown by Mark Lepper, David Greene and Richard Nisbett (1973) as the overjustification effect in children's drawing. SDT holds that people have three innate, universal psychological needs, and that well-being, intrinsic motivation and growth depend on satisfying them: competence (feeling effective and able to master challenges), autonomy (feeling that one's actions are self-chosen and endorsed, not controlled), and relatedness (feeling connected to and cared for by others). Autonomy here means volition, not independence: a person can autonomously choose to rely on others. SDT also places motivation on a continuum from amotivation through external, introjected, identified and integrated regulation to intrinsic motivation (organismic integration theory), and its cognitive evaluation theory explains how rewards and feedback raise or lower intrinsic motivation by affecting perceived competence and autonomy. Do not mix SDT needs with the needs for achievement, affiliation and power of David McClelland (1961), with Alfred Adler's striving for superiority, or with the hierarchy of Abraham Maslow (1943). Clinically, SDT informs motivational interviewing and autonomy-supportive therapy and teaching. Option b is right because SDT's three basic psychological needs are competence, autonomy and relatedness.","traps":{"a":"This option swaps competence for achievement. The need for achievement (nAch) comes from David McClelland (1961), building on Henry Murray's (1938) list of needs, and is measured with the Thematic Apperception Test. It is a learned motive to excel against standards of excellence, not one of SDT's innate needs. It tempts because autonomy and relatedness are correct, so two-thirds of the option matches.","c":"'Independence and superiority' replace autonomy and relatedness. Striving for superiority is Alfred Adler's master motive in Individual Psychology, and independence is not the same as SDT's autonomy, which means acting from one's own will, even while depending on others. Relatedness is missing entirely. It tempts because competence is right and 'independence' sounds like a synonym for autonomy.","d":"This option keeps competence and autonomy but replaces relatedness with excellence. SDT has no need for excellence; striving to meet standards of excellence is closer to McClelland's achievement motive. Leaving out relatedness removes SDT's social need, the sense of being connected to and cared for by others. It tempts because two of the three needs are correct and 'excellence' sounds like a positive growth term."}},{"q":"Gratitude increases prosocial behavior, primarily by enhancing helpers’ feelings of","concept":"Gratitude is the positive emotion felt when someone has intentionally done us good. Michael McCullough and colleagues (2001) described it as a moral affect: it registers kindness, rewards the helper and motivates the receiver to act prosocially, and Robert Emmons and Michael McCullough (2003) showed that keeping a weekly gratitude list improved well-being. But why does being thanked make the helper help again? Adam Grant and Francesca Gino (2010), in four experiments reported in 'A Little Thanks Goes a Long Way', proposed two routes. The agentic route is self-efficacy, Albert Bandura's (1977) term for belief in one's ability to succeed at a task: being thanked tells the helper that the help worked. The communal route is self-worth, or social worth: being thanked tells the helper that he is valued by others. In one study, participants who received a thank-you note from a student they had helped were about twice as likely to help him again as those who got a neutral reply, and thanked helpers also helped a different person more. Gratitude raised both feelings, but in their mediation analyses social worth carried the effect, not self-efficacy. Keep neighbouring terms apart: self-esteem is a global evaluation of oneself, measured by the scale of Morris Rosenberg (1965); self-confidence is a loose everyday word without a specific theory; reciprocity norms and the empathy-altruism hypothesis of C. Daniel Batson (1991) are other explanations of helping. Option a is right because it pairs self-worth, the feeling that carried the effect, with self-efficacy, the other mechanism Grant and Gino tested and textbooks report.","traps":{"b":"This option keeps self-worth, which is correct, but replaces self-efficacy with self-confidence. Self-confidence is a general, everyday term for trusting oneself, with no specific theory behind it. Self-efficacy, from Albert Bandura (1977), is a precise, task-specific belief that one can perform an action successfully, and it is the agentic mechanism Grant and Gino tested. It tempts because confidence and efficacy sound interchangeable.","c":"This option keeps self-efficacy but swaps self-worth for self-esteem. Self-esteem is a global, fairly stable evaluation of oneself as worthy, as measured by Morris Rosenberg's (1965) scale. The gratitude research measured social worth, the specific feeling of being valued by others in that interaction. It tempts because self-esteem and self-worth are often used as synonyms in everyday speech and in some textbooks.","d":"This option replaces both researched constructs with broader, everyday ones: self-esteem (a global self-evaluation) instead of self-worth (feeling valued by others), and self-confidence instead of Albert Bandura's (1977) task-specific self-efficacy. Neither was a mechanism tested in the gratitude studies. It tempts because it sounds like the most natural description of how being thanked makes someone feel good about themselves."}},{"q":"In a POX relationship triad, balance exists when there are","concept":"Fritz Heider set out balance theory in 1946 and developed it in The Psychology of Interpersonal Relations (1958). It is a cognitive consistency theory: people prefer their attitudes and relationships to fit together. The P-O-X triad has three elements: P, the perceiver; O, another person; and X, an object, idea or third person. Each of the three relations (P to O, P to X, O to X) is either positive (liking, agreeing, belonging) or negative (disliking, disagreeing). The rule is to multiply the three signs. If the product is positive the triad is balanced; if negative it is imbalanced. So balanced triads have all three positive (I like you, we both like cricket) or exactly one positive and two negatives (I like you and we both dislike a politician; or I dislike you, you dislike X, and I like X). Imbalanced triads have two positives and one negative (I like you, I like X, you hate X) or three negatives. Imbalance creates tension, and P restores balance by changing the weakest relation, for example liking O less or changing his view of X. Dorwin Cartwright and Frank Harary (1956) generalised the rule to larger networks using graph theory. Neighbouring theories: the A-B-X model of communication of Theodore Newcomb (1953); the congruity theory of Charles Osgood and Percy Tannenbaum (1955), which adds degrees of attitude; and the cognitive dissonance theory of Leon Festinger (1957). Option b is right because two negatives and one positive multiply to a positive product, which makes the triad balanced.","traps":{"a":"One negative and two positives gives a negative product, so the triad is imbalanced. Example: P likes O and P likes X, but O dislikes X. P feels strain, because a friend rejects something P values, and is pushed to change one relation. It tempts because two positives out of three looks 'mostly positive', and students assume that more liking means more balance, ignoring Fritz Heider's (1946) sign-multiplication rule.","c":"Three negatives multiply to a negative product, so the rule classes the triad as imbalanced. In the all-negative case the 'enemy of my enemy is my friend' logic predicts pressure for two members to become allies, which is why it is not stable. It tempts because complete mutual dislike sounds consistent in everyday terms, and Fritz Heider (1946) himself treated this case as less clear-cut than the others.","d":"'No positives' means all three relations are negative, which is the same triad as option c, and its product is negative, so it is imbalanced. Since c and d describe one situation, neither could be the single correct answer. Balance depends on the product of the three signs, not on the count of positives or negatives. It tempts because 'no positives' sounds like consistent agreement in dislike."}}],"met-mock-1":[{"q":"The all-or-none law of action potentials means that:","concept":"The all-or-none law states that once a stimulus depolarises the membrane to threshold (about -55 mV), a full-amplitude action potential fires; a stronger stimulus does not make a bigger spike. The self-regenerating opening of voltage-gated sodium channels guarantees the spike always reaches the same peak and propagates without decrement along the axon. Stimulus intensity is encoded instead by firing frequency (rate coding) and by the number of neurons recruited, not by spike size. This is why option (d) is correct.","traps":{"a":"This describes a graded, amplitude-coded response, exactly what the all-or-none law denies; amplitude is fixed once threshold is reached and intensity is carried by frequency.","b":"Sub-threshold stimuli produce only local, decremental graded potentials, not miniature action potentials, so no propagating spike occurs at all.","c":"Grading a spike by stimulus intensity describes receptor or postsynaptic potentials, not the fixed-amplitude axonal action potential."}},{"q":"Per DSM-5-TR, the minimum duration required for a diagnosis of major depressive episode is:","concept":"The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition, Text Revision (DSM-5-TR) requires five or more depressive symptoms, including at least depressed mood or anhedonia (loss of interest/pleasure), present nearly every day for a minimum of 2 weeks, causing clinically significant distress or impairment. The 2-week floor distinguishes a diagnosable episode from ordinary transient low mood. This makes option (a) correct.","traps":{"b":"One week is the duration threshold for a manic episode, not a major depressive episode.","c":"One month has no specific role here; it is closer to durations used in some trauma-related criteria, not depression.","d":"Six months evokes the chronicity of conditions like generalised anxiety disorder, not the 2-week major depressive episode."}},{"q":"According to Piaget, the ability to mentally reverse operations first appears in which stage?","concept":"Jean Piaget's concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years) is defined by the emergence of logical mental operations that can be reversed, enabling conservation, decentration, and classification. Reversibility, the ability to mentally undo an action (for example, recognising that pouring water back restores the original amount), is what allows conservation to succeed here. This makes option (d) correct.","traps":{"a":"The sensorimotor stage (0 to 2 years) is about acquiring object permanence through action, well before any reversible mental operations.","b":"Preoperational children (2 to 7 years) are precisely those who fail conservation because their thinking is irreversible and centred on one dimension.","c":"Formal operational thought adds abstract and hypothetical reasoning; reversibility of concrete operations appears earlier, so this is not the first appearance."}},{"q":"If a test has reliability r_xx = 0.81, the maximum possible validity coefficient with any other measure is:","concept":"A test cannot correlate with any external criterion more strongly than the square root of its own reliability, because measurement error caps how much true-score variance is available to correlate. The validity ceiling equals the square root of r_xx when the criterion is perfectly reliable: the square root of 0.81 is 0.90. This makes option (c) correct.","traps":{"a":"0.81 is the reliability coefficient itself, not its square root; the ceiling is the square root of reliability, which is higher.","b":"A validity of 1.00 would require a perfectly reliable test (r_xx = 1.00), which is not the case here.","d":"0.95 is the square root of 0.9025, not of 0.81, so it overstates the ceiling."}},{"q":"The 'empty chair' technique is most associated with which approach?","concept":"The empty chair technique belongs to Gestalt therapy, developed by Fritz Perls. The client speaks to an imagined person or a disowned part of the self seated in an empty chair, often switching seats to voice both sides, promoting awareness, contact, and integration of split-off feelings in the present moment. This makes option (b) correct.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive Behaviour Therapy uses techniques like cognitive restructuring and behavioural experiments, not experiential empty-chair dialogues.","c":"Person-Centred Therapy (Carl Rogers) relies on the therapeutic relationship and core conditions, not structured enactment techniques.","d":"Psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) uses free association and interpretation of transference, not chair work."}},{"q":"Choose the correctly punctuated sentence:","concept":"A non-restrictive relative clause, which adds extra information about an already-identified noun, must be enclosed by a comma on each side. In 'The patient, who was anxious, refused medication,' the clause is parenthetical and correctly bracketed by commas. This makes option (b) the only correctly punctuated sentence.","traps":{"a":"A semicolon cannot close a clause that a comma opened; mixing a comma and a semicolon around the clause is ungrammatical.","c":"Omitting both commas turns it into a restrictive clause (a grammatical reading), but the item asks for the correctly punctuated non-restrictive version.","d":"A single trailing comma with no opening comma leaves the relative clause unbalanced and incorrectly punctuated."}},{"q":"A Type I error in hypothesis testing occurs when:","concept":"A Type I error (alpha) is a false positive: rejecting the null hypothesis when it is actually true, that is, detecting an effect that does not exist. It is contrasted with a Type II error (beta), the false negative of retaining a false null. This makes option (d) correct.","traps":{"a":"Correctly retaining a true null is a correct decision, not an error of any type.","b":"Small sample size affects power and Type II error risk but is not itself the definition of a Type I error.","c":"Wrongly retaining a null that is false is the definition of a Type II error, not Type I."}},{"q":"During the absolute refractory period of a neuron:","concept":"During the absolute refractory period, the voltage-gated sodium channels are in their inactivated state and cannot reopen no matter how strong the stimulus, so no second action potential can be triggered. They reset to the closed-but-available state only after the membrane repolarises. This makes option (d) correct.","traps":{"a":"A stronger stimulus overcoming refractoriness describes the relative refractory period, not the absolute period where no spike is possible.","b":"Hyperpolarisation below rest characterises the after-hyperpolarisation phase, which coincides with the relative, not absolute, refractory period.","c":"Potassium efflux just beginning marks the start of repolarisation, whereas the absolute refractory period centres on sodium channel inactivation."}},{"q":"Schneider's first-rank symptoms are most associated with:","concept":"Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms (audible thoughts, voices arguing or commenting, thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcast, somatic passivity, made affect and impulse, and delusional perception) were historically treated as strongly suggestive of schizophrenia. Although DSM-5 removed their special diagnostic weight, they remain a clinically recognised marker of schizophrenia. This makes option (d) correct.","traps":{"a":"Bipolar disorder is defined by mood episodes (mania and depression), not by Schneiderian passivity phenomena.","b":"Borderline personality disorder centres on affective instability, identity disturbance, and impulsivity, not first-rank symptoms.","c":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder involves ego-dystonic obsessions and compulsions, not psychotic first-rank symptoms."}},{"q":"In Erikson's psychosocial theory, the crisis of middle adulthood is:","concept":"In Erik Erikson's psychosocial theory, middle adulthood (roughly 40 to 65 years) presents the crisis of Generativity versus Stagnation, resolved through contributing to the next generation and to society; the associated virtue is care. This makes option (a) correct.","traps":{"b":"Intimacy versus Isolation is the young-adulthood crisis about forming committed relationships, earlier than middle adulthood.","c":"Integrity versus Despair is the final, late-adulthood stage of reflecting on one's life.","d":"Identity versus Role Confusion is the adolescent crisis, well before middle adulthood."}},{"q":"A T-score of 70 on the MMPI corresponds to a deviation of:","concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI; Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) reports scores as T-scores with a mean of 50 and a standard deviation of 10. A T-score of 70 is therefore (70 minus 50) divided by 10, equal to 2 standard deviations above the mean, the conventional clinical cut-off. This makes option (a) correct.","traps":{"b":"1.5 SD would correspond to a T-score of 65, not 70.","c":"1 SD would correspond to a T-score of 60, not 70.","d":"3 SD would correspond to a T-score of 80, not 70."}},{"q":"Carl Rogers' three core conditions for therapeutic change are:","concept":"Carl Rogers held that three therapist-offered core conditions are necessary and sufficient for constructive personality change: unconditional positive regard, congruence (genuineness), and accurate empathic understanding. Together they create a safe relational climate for the client's self-actualising tendency. This makes option (c) correct.","traps":{"a":"Transference, free association, and interpretation are psychoanalytic techniques, not Rogers' relational conditions.","b":"Cognitive restructuring, behavioural activation, and exposure are Cognitive Behaviour Therapy methods.","d":"Working alliance, transference, and resistance interpretation belong to psychodynamic practice, not person-centred theory."}},{"q":"Identify the antonym of 'GREGARIOUS':","concept":"'Gregarious' means sociable and fond of the company of others. Its antonym is 'reclusive,' describing someone withdrawn and preferring solitude. This makes option (b) correct.","traps":{"a":"Sociable is a synonym of gregarious, not its antonym.","c":"Affable means friendly and approachable, a near-synonym rather than an opposite.","d":"Convivial means cheerfully sociable, again a synonym, not an antonym."}},{"q":"The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric equivalent of the:","concept":"The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric counterpart of the independent-samples t-test: it compares two independent groups using ranks rather than raw means, without assuming normality. This makes option (a) correct.","traps":{"b":"The paired t-test's non-parametric equivalent is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test, not Mann-Whitney U.","c":"The one-way ANOVA's non-parametric equivalent is the Kruskal-Wallis test.","d":"The chi-square test is itself non-parametric and analyses categorical frequencies, a different design from Mann-Whitney U."}},{"q":"The corticospinal tract decussates primarily at the:","concept":"About 85 to 90 percent of corticospinal tract fibres cross the midline at the pyramidal decussation in the lower medulla, forming the lateral corticospinal tract; this is why one hemisphere controls the opposite side of the body. This makes option (d) correct.","traps":{"a":"The optic chiasm is where visual (optic nerve) fibres partially cross, unrelated to the motor corticospinal tract.","b":"Only the small anterior corticospinal tract crosses near its level of entry; the main decussation is in the medulla.","c":"The pons carries corticospinal fibres but is not the site of their decussation."}},{"q":"In DSM-5-TR, persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) requires depressed mood for at least:","concept":"Under DSM-5-TR, persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) requires depressed mood for most of the day, more days than not, for at least 2 years in adults (and at least 1 year in children and adolescents), plus two or more additional symptoms. This makes option (b) correct.","traps":{"a":"6 months is too short and does not match the chronic-duration requirement for persistent depressive disorder.","c":"1 year is the threshold for children and adolescents, not for adults, so it is incomplete as a general answer.","d":"5 years overstates the requirement; the criterion is 2 years in adults."}},{"q":"In Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant who shows distress on separation but cannot be comforted on reunion and may resist contact is classified as:","concept":"In Mary Ainsworth's Strange Situation, the insecure-resistant/ambivalent (Type C) infant is highly distressed by separation and, on reunion, simultaneously seeks contact and angrily resists it, remaining hard to soothe. This pattern reflects an inconsistently responsive caregiver. This makes option (a) correct.","traps":{"b":"The insecure-avoidant (Type A) infant minimises distress and actively avoids the caregiver on reunion, the opposite of resistant clinging.","c":"The secure (Type B) infant is comforted and readily settled on reunion.","d":"The disorganised (Type D) infant shows contradictory, dazed, or freezing behaviours rather than the coherent seek-and-resist pattern."}},{"q":"In Wechsler's classification, an FSIQ in the range 70–84 falls under:","concept":"On the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition (WAIS-IV), 80 to 89 is the Low Average range and 70 to 79 is Borderline; a span of 70 to 84 therefore straddles both, so the label depends on the exact score and edition. This makes option (a), low average / borderline depending on edition, correct.","traps":{"b":"Labelling the whole 70 to 84 span simply 'Borderline' ignores that 80 to 84 falls in the Low Average band.","c":"'Average' is 90 to 109, above the 70 to 84 range.","d":"Mild intellectual disability requires an IQ below 70 together with adaptive-functioning deficits, so it does not fit 70 to 84."}},{"q":"Aaron Beck's cognitive triad in depression refers to negative views of:","concept":"Aaron T. Beck's cognitive triad in depression comprises persistently negative views of the self, the world (current experience), and the future. These interlocking negative schemas drive the automatic negative thoughts central to his cognitive model. This makes option (d) correct.","traps":{"a":"Id, ego, and superego are Freud's structural components of personality, not Beck's cognitive triad.","b":"Past, present, and future is a plausible-sounding distractor, but Beck's triad targets self and world, not the past.","c":"Mother, father, and self reflect object-relations or family themes, not Beck's cognitive model."}},{"q":"Choose the correct preposition: 'The therapist insisted ___ a follow-up after two weeks.'","concept":"The verb 'insist' takes the preposition 'on' when followed by the thing demanded: one insists on something. 'The therapist insisted on a follow-up' is the standard collocation in British and Indian English. This makes option (c) correct.","traps":{"a":"'Insist for' is not an accepted collocation in standard English.","b":"'Insist with' is ungrammatical here; 'with' does not mark the demanded object.","d":"'Insist to' is incorrect before a noun; 'insist' pairs with 'on,' not 'to.'"}},{"q":"If the p-value of a study is 0.03 and α was set at 0.05:","concept":"When the p-value is smaller than the pre-set significance level alpha, the result is statistically significant and the null hypothesis is rejected. Here p = 0.03 is less than alpha = 0.05, so H0 is rejected. This makes option (b) correct.","traps":{"a":"Retaining the null would be correct only if p were greater than alpha, which is not the case.","c":"The result is significant because p is below alpha, so calling it not significant is wrong.","d":"A significant p-value says nothing directly about power; high power is not automatic and depends on effect size and sample size."}},{"q":"Which cranial nerve is purely motor and supplies most extraocular muscles?","concept":"The oculomotor nerve, cranial nerve III, is a motor nerve that supplies four of the six extraocular muscles (superior, inferior, and medial recti and the inferior oblique), plus the levator palpebrae superioris and, via parasympathetic fibres, the pupillary constrictor. This makes option (c) correct.","traps":{"a":"The optic nerve (II) is purely sensory for vision and moves no muscles.","b":"The trochlear nerve (IV) supplies only the superior oblique, not most extraocular muscles.","d":"The abducens nerve (VI) supplies only the lateral rectus, again not most of the muscles."}},{"q":"The cardinal feature of obsessive-compulsive disorder under DSM-5 is:","concept":"Under DSM-5, the cardinal feature of obsessive-compulsive disorder is the presence of obsessions and/or compulsions that are time-consuming (more than one hour per day) or cause clinically significant distress or impairment. An insight specifier was added in DSM-5. This makes option (a) correct.","traps":{"b":"Persistent depressive symptoms define persistent depressive disorder, not OCD.","c":"A pervasive pattern of grandiosity and need for admiration describes narcissistic personality disorder.","d":"Recurrent unexpected panic attacks are the hallmark of panic disorder, not OCD."}},{"q":"The zone of proximal development is a concept proposed by:","concept":"The zone of proximal development was proposed by Lev Vygotsky. It is the gap between what a learner can accomplish independently and what they can achieve with guidance or scaffolding from a more knowledgeable other, highlighting the social basis of cognitive development. This makes option (c) correct.","traps":{"a":"Jean Piaget emphasised individual, stage-based construction of knowledge, not the socially mediated ZPD.","b":"Urie Bronfenbrenner developed ecological systems theory, concerning nested environmental contexts.","d":"Lawrence Kohlberg proposed stages of moral development, unrelated to the ZPD."}},{"q":"Which projective test uses incomplete cartoon-style frames in which the respondent fills in what the depicted character is saying in a frustrating situation?","concept":"Saul Rosenzweig's Picture-Frustration (P-F) Study presents 24 cartoon-style frames showing frustrating situations, and the respondent writes what the frustrated character would reply. Responses are scored by direction of aggression (extrapunitive, intropunitive, impunitive) and by reaction type. This makes option (b) correct.","traps":{"a":"The Thematic Apperception Test (Christiana Morgan and Henry Murray, 1935) uses ambiguous photograph-like pictures for whole story-telling, not cartoon frames to be filled in.","c":"The Rorschach uses inkblots and asks what they might be, not what a character is saying.","d":"The Sentence Completion Test uses verbal stems, not cartoon-style pictures."}},{"q":"Systematic desensitisation, developed by Joseph Wolpe, is based on the principle of:","concept":"Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation rests on reciprocal inhibition: because deep muscle relaxation is physiologically incompatible with anxiety, pairing relaxation with a graded fear hierarchy progressively inhibits and weakens the conditioned fear response. This makes option (b) correct.","traps":{"a":"Operant extinction involves withdrawing reinforcement of a behaviour, a different learning mechanism from pairing relaxation with fear cues.","c":"Modelling is learning by observing others (Bandura), not the relaxation-based counterconditioning Wolpe used.","d":"Habituation alone (simple response decline to repeated stimuli) does not capture the active relaxation-anxiety antagonism central to the technique."}},{"q":"Identify the synonym of 'EPHEMERAL':","concept":"'Ephemeral' means lasting for a very short time. Its closest synonym is 'transient,' which likewise means short-lived or passing. This makes option (c) correct.","traps":{"a":"Vivid means bright or intensely clear, unrelated to duration.","b":"Permanent is the antonym of ephemeral, meaning long-lasting.","d":"Ancient means very old, which concerns great age rather than brief duration."}},{"q":"A Solomon four-group design is used to control for:","concept":"The Solomon four-group design adds two groups that receive no pretest (one treated, one control) to the standard pretested experimental and control groups. Comparing pretested and non-pretested groups lets the researcher detect and separate pretest sensitisation, the interaction between taking the pretest and the treatment. This makes option (b) correct.","traps":{"a":"Maturation is controlled by any design with a proper control group; the Solomon design's distinctive purpose is testing for pretest effects.","c":"Random assignment, not the four-group structure specifically, addresses selection bias.","d":"Researcher bias is handled by blinding and standardisation, not by adding no-pretest groups."}},{"q":"Damage to the ventromedial hypothalamus typically produces:","concept":"The ventromedial hypothalamus functions as the satiety centre. Bilateral lesions abolish the stop-eating signal, producing hyperphagia (overeating) and consequent obesity, the classic VMH syndrome. This makes option (c) correct.","traps":{"a":"Aphagia and weight loss follow lateral hypothalamic (the feeding centre) lesions, the opposite site.","b":"Insomnia is linked to other regions such as the anterior hypothalamus and reticular systems, not VMH feeding regulation.","d":"Thermoregulation is governed mainly by the preoptic/anterior hypothalamus, not the ventromedial satiety centre."}},{"q":"A patient presents with sudden inability to recall important autobiographical information, beyond ordinary forgetting, with no medical cause. The most likely diagnosis is:","concept":"Dissociative amnesia is diagnosed when a person cannot recall important autobiographical information, typically of a stressful or traumatic nature, that is too extensive to be ordinary forgetting and is not attributable to a medical, neurological, or substance cause. The psychogenic nature and absence of organic cause point here to option (d).","traps":{"a":"Transient global amnesia is a sudden, brief, self-limited anterograde memory loss with a presumed vascular/neurological basis, not a psychogenic autobiographical loss.","b":"Korsakoff syndrome is an organic amnestic disorder from thiamine deficiency (often alcohol-related), featuring confabulation and anterograde amnesia.","c":"Alzheimer's disease is a progressive neurodegenerative dementia, not a sudden isolated loss of autobiographical memory with no medical cause."}},{"q":"Bronfenbrenner's bioecological model places the immediate family in the:","concept":"Urie Bronfenbrenner's bioecological model nests a child's development inside layered systems. The microsystem is the innermost layer: the immediate, face-to-face settings the child directly participates in, such as the family, school classroom, and peer group. Because the immediate family is a direct, day-to-day setting the child lives within, it belongs to the microsystem.","traps":{"a":"The macrosystem is the outermost layer of overarching cultural values, laws, and customs, not a direct setting like the family.","b":"The mesosystem is not a setting itself but the web of connections between two microsystems, for example the link between home and school.","c":"The exosystem is a setting that affects the child indirectly without the child being present, such as a parent's workplace."}},{"q":"An item discrimination index (D) of −0.20 indicates that the item:","concept":"The item discrimination index (D) measures how well a test item separates high scorers from low scorers, ranging from -1.0 to +1.0. A positive D means high scorers answered correctly more often than low scorers, which is desired. A value of -0.20 is negative, meaning low scorers outperformed high scorers on that item, which signals the item is confusing, mis-keyed, or measuring something opposite to the intended trait, so it should be revised or discarded.","traps":{"a":"A well-functioning item to retain would show a positive discrimination index (typically 0.30 or higher), not a negative one.","b":"Item difficulty is captured by the p-value (proportion correct), not by D; a negative D does not tell you the item is too easy.","c":"Ideal difficulty is also a p-value concept (around 0.50), which is unrelated to and cannot be inferred from a negative discrimination value."}},{"q":"The ABC model in Ellis' REBT stands for:","concept":"Albert Ellis's Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT) uses the ABC model to show that emotions are driven by beliefs, not events directly. A is the Activating event, B is the person's Beliefs (especially irrational ones) about that event, and C is the emotional and behavioural Consequences. The therapist then Disputes (D) the irrational beliefs to produce a new Effect (E), making beliefs the pivotal middle term.","traps":{"a":"This substitutes 'Behaviour' for 'Beliefs', losing the core REBT insight that it is the belief, not behaviour, that mediates the consequence.","b":"Antecedent-Behaviour-Consequence is the Skinnerian ABC of behavioural analysis, a different model concerned with observable contingencies rather than irrational cognitions.","d":"Affect-Behaviour-Cognition is a mnemonic for the three components of an attitude, not Ellis's therapeutic model."}},{"q":"Choose the sentence with the correct subject-verb agreement:","concept":"The subject of the sentence is the indefinite pronoun 'each', which is grammatically singular and therefore requires a singular verb. The intervening prepositional phrase 'of the participants' does not change the number of the subject. The correct singular past-tense form is 'was given'.","traps":{"a":"'Were given' is plural and wrongly agrees with the nearer noun 'participants' rather than the singular subject 'each'.","b":"'Are given' is plural present tense and also fails the singular agreement required by 'each'.","c":"'Have given' is both plural and active voice, which changes the meaning (participants doing the giving) and breaks agreement."}},{"q":"Cluster sampling differs from stratified sampling primarily in that:","concept":"Both cluster and stratified sampling are probability methods, but they differ in what is randomized and why. In cluster sampling, the population is split into naturally occurring groups (clusters such as schools or villages), a random set of whole clusters is selected, and then all or a random subset of units within the chosen clusters are studied. This makes it efficient when a full list of individuals is unavailable but groups are easy to identify.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the two methods: stratified sampling draws individuals from within every stratum, while cluster sampling is the one that selects entire groups.","b":"Both cluster and stratified sampling are probability (random) methods, not non-probability methods.","d":"Requiring a complete prior listing of every individual describes simple random or stratified sampling; avoiding that listing burden is precisely why cluster sampling is used."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a basal ganglia structure?","concept":"The basal ganglia are subcortical grey-matter nuclei involved in motor control and include the caudate nucleus, putamen, globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus, and substantia nigra. The red nucleus is not part of this group; it lies in the midbrain tegmentum and gives rise to the rubrospinal tract. Therefore the red nucleus is the exception.","traps":{"a":"The caudate nucleus is a core basal ganglia structure, forming part of the striatum with the putamen.","b":"The globus pallidus is a genuine basal ganglia nucleus and the main output relay of the striatum.","d":"The subthalamic nucleus is functionally part of the basal ganglia circuitry and is a target in Parkinson's deep-brain stimulation."}},{"q":"ICD-11 retained the category of 'gaming disorder' under:","concept":"The International Classification of Diseases, 11th Revision (ICD-11) formally introduced gaming disorder and placed it within the grouping 'disorders due to substance use or addictive behaviours', alongside gambling disorder as a behavioural (non-substance) addiction. This reflects the view that persistent, impairing gaming can follow an addiction pattern.","traps":{"b":"Disorders specifically associated with stress (such as PTSD and adjustment disorder) form a separate ICD-11 grouping unrelated to addictive behaviour.","c":"Mood disorders cover depressive and bipolar conditions, not the compulsive-reward pattern that defines gaming disorder.","d":"Classifying it under impulse control disorders only is the older framing; ICD-11 deliberately located it within the addictive-behaviours grouping instead."}},{"q":"Kohlberg's preconventional level of moral reasoning is dominated by:","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg's preconventional level (stages 1 and 2) bases moral judgment on external consequences to the self rather than internalized social norms. Stage 1 is punishment-and-obedience (avoid punishment) and stage 2 is instrumental exchange (satisfy one's own needs through reciprocal deals). So this level is dominated by avoidance of punishment and instrumental exchange.","traps":{"b":"Maintaining laws and social order is stage 4, which sits in the conventional level, not the preconventional one.","c":"Social contract and universal ethical principles are stages 5 and 6 of the postconventional level, the most advanced reasoning.","d":"Conformity to peer and others' expectations is stage 3 ('good boy, nice girl'), which belongs to the conventional level."}},{"q":"Cattell's distinction between fluid and crystallised intelligence is operationalised in the Cattell-Horn-Carroll model. Crystallised intelligence (Gc) is best measured by:","concept":"In the Cattell-Horn-Carroll model, crystallised intelligence (Gc) is the store of acquired knowledge, verbal skills, and culturally learned information that accumulates through experience. It is best tapped by tasks drawing on learned content, such as the Vocabulary and Information subtests. Fluid intelligence (Gf), by contrast, is on-the-spot reasoning with novel material.","traps":{"a":"Block design is a visuospatial reasoning task loading on fluid intelligence and perceptual organisation, not on acquired verbal knowledge.","b":"Matrix reasoning is a classic measure of fluid intelligence (Gf), requiring novel pattern induction rather than stored knowledge.","c":"Coding or digit-symbol measures processing speed (Gs), not crystallised knowledge."}},{"q":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) was developed primarily for:","concept":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) was created by Marsha Linehan specifically for chronically suicidal and self-harming women diagnosed with borderline personality disorder. It blends standard cognitive-behavioural change strategies with acceptance, mindfulness, and dialectical philosophy (balancing acceptance and change). Though now adapted for other conditions, its original and primary target was BPD with chronic suicidality.","traps":{"b":"Schizophrenia is treated primarily with antipsychotics plus psychosocial approaches like CBT for psychosis, not DBT.","c":"OCD is most specifically treated with exposure and response prevention, a behavioural therapy distinct from DBT.","d":"Specific phobias are treated with graded exposure or systematic desensitisation (Joseph Wolpe, 1958), again not the population DBT was built for."}},{"q":"Identify the part of speech of the underlined word: 'The data were INCONCLUSIVE.'","concept":"The underlined word 'inconclusive' describes a quality of 'the data', telling us what the data were like. A word that modifies or describes a noun (here functioning as a predicate after the linking verb 'were') is an adjective. So 'inconclusive' is a predicate adjective.","traps":{"a":"A noun names a person, place, thing, or idea; 'inconclusive' names no entity, it only describes one.","b":"The verb in the sentence is 'were'; 'inconclusive' is the complement it links to, not the action word.","c":"An adverb would modify a verb, adjective, or another adverb and typically answer how or when; here the word modifies the noun 'data', making it adjectival."}},{"q":"In a normal distribution, approximately what percentage of cases falls within ±1 SD of the mean?","concept":"In a normal (bell-shaped) distribution, the empirical rule (the 68-95-99.7 rule) describes how cases spread around the mean. Approximately 68% of cases fall within plus or minus one standard deviation of the mean, about 95% within two standard deviations, and about 99.7% within three. Hence the answer is roughly 68%.","traps":{"a":"50% corresponds to cases falling below the mean (the median split), not to the region within one standard deviation.","b":"95% is the proportion within plus or minus two standard deviations, not one.","d":"99.7% is the proportion within plus or minus three standard deviations, the widest of the three bands."}},{"q":"The cones of the human retina are MOST concentrated in the:","concept":"The human retina has two photoreceptor types: cones for colour and fine detail, and rods for dim-light vision. Cones are packed most densely at the fovea centralis, a small pit at the centre of the macula that provides the sharpest visual acuity. Rods dominate the peripheral retina.","traps":{"a":"The optic disc is the blind spot where the optic nerve exits and contains no photoreceptors at all.","b":"The peripheral retina is dominated by rods, not cones, which is why peripheral vision is poor for detail and colour.","d":"Although the macula is cone-rich, cone density peaks specifically at the fovea within it, so 'macula but not the fovea' is wrong."}},{"q":"Anorexia nervosa is differentiated from bulimia nervosa primarily by:","concept":"Anorexia nervosa and bulimia nervosa both involve disturbed body image and weight preoccupation, so those cannot distinguish them. The defining difference is body weight: anorexia nervosa requires a significantly low body weight relative to what is expected, whereas people with bulimia nervosa are typically of normal weight or above. That weight criterion is the primary differentiator.","traps":{"a":"Body image disturbance is present in both disorders, so its supposed absence in bulimia is false.","c":"Compensatory behaviours (purging, fasting, excessive exercise) occur in bulimia and in the binge-purge subtype of anorexia, so they are not exclusive to anorexia.","d":"Purging can occur in anorexia (binge-eating/purging subtype), so its absence does not define anorexia."}},{"q":"The Visual Cliff experiment (Gibson & Walk, 1960) demonstrated that depth perception emerges:","concept":"In the Visual Cliff experiment by Eleanor Gibson and Richard Walk (1960), infants were placed on a glass surface over an apparent drop-off. Infants who could crawl (around 6 to 8 months) refused to venture onto the 'deep' side, indicating they perceived depth by that age. So depth perception is evident by the time infants can crawl.","traps":{"a":"Depth perception appears well before age 2; crawling-age infants already showed avoidance around 6 to 8 months.","c":"Depth perception is not fully developed at birth; it emerges over the first months, though heart-rate studies show some early sensitivity.","d":"Depth perception develops through maturation and experience, not through explicit verbal instruction, which infants could not follow anyway."}},{"q":"The Bender Visual Motor Gestalt Test is primarily used to screen for:","concept":"The Bender Visual Motor Gestalt Test asks the examinee to copy a series of geometric designs, assessing visuomotor integration (how well perception and motor output coordinate). It is used primarily to screen for organic brain damage and neuropsychological dysfunction, and in children for developmental maturation. So its main purpose is screening organic brain damage and visuomotor function.","traps":{"a":"Personality disorders are assessed with clinical interviews and personality inventories, not a design-copying visuomotor task.","c":"Schizophrenia is diagnosed clinically; the Bender is not a specific test for it, though gross distortions may prompt further evaluation.","d":"ADHD is assessed with rating scales and continuous performance tests, not primarily with the Bender-Gestalt."}},{"q":"In psychoanalysis, transference refers to:","concept":"In psychoanalysis, transference is the process by which a client unconsciously redirects feelings, wishes, and attitudes originally connected to significant figures from their past (often parents) onto the therapist. Analysing this displacement gives insight into the client's early relationships. So transference is the client redirecting such feelings onto the therapist.","traps":{"a":"The therapist's own emotional reaction to the client is countertransference, the mirror image of transference.","b":"Free association is the technique of voicing whatever comes to mind, a method of accessing the unconscious, not transference itself.","d":"Resistance is the client's blocking or opposition to the therapeutic process, a separate defensive phenomenon."}},{"q":"Choose the best one-word substitution for 'a person who is fluent in many languages':","concept":"A one-word substitution should exactly capture 'a person fluent in many languages'. 'Polyglot' means someone who knows and uses several languages, matching 'many'. It is more precise than words that mean only two languages or that describe a different role.","traps":{"b":"'Bilingual' specifically means fluent in only two languages, not many.","c":"'Linguist' primarily denotes a scientific student of language, though it can loosely mean a language-speaker; 'polyglot' is the exact fit for 'many languages'.","d":"'Orator' means a skilled public speaker, which says nothing about knowing multiple languages."}},{"q":"Cohen's d of 0.2, 0.5, and 0.8 are conventionally interpreted as:","concept":"Cohen's d expresses the difference between two means in standard-deviation units. Jacob Cohen proposed conventional benchmarks so effect sizes can be interpreted: d of 0.2 is a small effect, 0.5 is a medium effect, and 0.8 is a large effect. Hence the ordering is small, medium, large.","traps":{"b":"These labels (trivial, weak, moderate) understate the values; 0.8 in particular is conventionally 'large', not merely 'moderate'.","c":"This mislabels 0.2 as medium; 0.2 is Cohen's benchmark for a small effect.","d":"Calling all three values negligible is wrong, since 0.5 and especially 0.8 represent meaningful medium and large effects."}},{"q":"Place theory of hearing (Békésy) best explains the perception of:","concept":"The place theory of hearing, associated with Georg von Bekesy, holds that different sound frequencies displace the basilar membrane maximally at different locations. High-frequency sounds peak at the stiff basal end near the oval window, while low frequencies peak at the flexible apex. Pitch for high frequencies is therefore coded by the place of maximal displacement.","traps":{"b":"Low-frequency coding by neural firing rate is the frequency (temporal) theory, not place theory.","c":"Horizontal sound localisation depends on interaural time and intensity differences between the two ears, a separate mechanism from place coding.","d":"Loudness is coded largely by the number and firing rate of activated neurons, not by place along the basilar membrane."}},{"q":"A patient firmly believes that a close family member has been replaced by an identical impostor. This is:","concept":"This is a delusional misidentification syndrome. Capgras delusion is the fixed belief that a familiar person (often a spouse or close relative) has been replaced by an identical-looking impostor. Because the described patient believes a family member is a duplicate impostor, this is Capgras delusion.","traps":{"a":"Cotard delusion is the nihilistic belief that one is dead, does not exist, or has lost one's organs, not that others are impostors.","b":"Folie a deux (Charles Lasègue and Jules Falret, 1877) is a shared delusion transmitted from one person to a close associate, describing transmission rather than the impostor belief itself.","c":"Fregoli delusion is the reverse: believing that different strangers are actually one familiar persecutor in disguise."}},{"q":"According to Baumrind, the parenting style with high warmth and high demandingness is:","concept":"Diana Baumrind classified parenting along two dimensions: warmth (responsiveness) and demandingness (control). The authoritative style combines high warmth with high demandingness, setting firm expectations while remaining supportive, and is linked to the best child outcomes. So high warmth plus high demandingness is authoritative.","traps":{"a":"Authoritarian parenting is high demandingness but low warmth, strict and cold rather than supportive.","c":"Permissive (indulgent) parenting is high warmth but low demandingness, lenient with few limits.","d":"Uninvolved (neglectful) parenting is low on both warmth and demandingness."}},{"q":"The Vineland Adaptive Behaviour Scales assess all of the following EXCEPT:","concept":"The Vineland Adaptive Behaviour Scales measure adaptive functioning, meaning everyday practical skills, not intelligence. Its domains are Communication, Daily Living Skills, Socialisation, and (for young children) Motor Skills. Verbal IQ is a cognitive construct measured by intelligence tests such as the Wechsler scales, so it is the exception the Vineland does not assess.","traps":{"a":"Communication is a core Vineland domain covering receptive, expressive, and written skills.","c":"Socialisation is a Vineland domain assessing interpersonal relationships, play, and coping skills.","d":"Daily Living Skills is a Vineland domain covering personal, domestic, and community self-care abilities."}},{"q":"Token economy is an example of:","concept":"A token economy (Teodoro Ayllon and Nathan Azrin, 1968) is a behaviour-modification system grounded in operant conditioning (B. F. Skinner). Desired behaviours earn tokens, which are conditioned (secondary) reinforcers because they have no intrinsic value but can be exchanged for primary reinforcers such as food or privileges. So it is operant conditioning using secondary reinforcers.","traps":{"a":"Classical conditioning (Ivan Pavlov) involves pairing stimuli to elicit involuntary responses, not reinforcing voluntary behaviour with earned tokens.","c":"Cognitive restructuring is a cognitive therapy technique that changes maladaptive thoughts, not a reinforcement schedule.","d":"Insight-oriented therapy aims at unconscious understanding rather than shaping behaviour through tangible reinforcers."}},{"q":"Identify the correct meaning of the idiom 'to bury the hatchet':","concept":"The idiom 'to bury the hatchet' means to settle a dispute and make peace. It derives from a Native American custom of literally burying weapons to mark the end of hostilities. So the correct meaning is to make peace and end a quarrel.","traps":{"b":"Starting a quarrel is the opposite of the idiom's meaning of ending one.","c":"Hiding one's intentions is unrelated; the idiom is about reconciliation, not concealment.","d":"Digging up an old issue is the reverse image (unburying), which contradicts 'burying' the conflict."}},{"q":"An ANOVA F-test is significant. To determine which specific group means differ, you would conduct:","concept":"A significant omnibus ANOVA F-test tells you that at least one group mean differs from the others, but not which specific pairs differ. To locate exactly which means differ while controlling the inflated family-wise error rate from multiple comparisons, you run post-hoc tests such as Tukey's HSD, Bonferroni, or Scheffe. Hence post-hoc tests are the correct follow-up.","traps":{"b":"Running another ANOVA would just repeat the same omnibus result without identifying which pairs of means differ.","c":"A chi-square test is for categorical frequency data, not for comparing means of a continuous outcome.","d":"A Pearson correlation measures linear association between two continuous variables, not differences between group means."}},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter is most directly implicated in the positive symptoms of schizophrenia (mesolimbic pathway)?","concept":"The dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia links positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions) to excess dopamine activity in the mesolimbic pathway. Antipsychotics that block dopamine D2 receptors reduce these symptoms, supporting the link. So dopamine is the neurotransmitter most directly implicated in positive symptoms.","traps":{"a":"Serotonin is more relevant to negative symptoms and is targeted by atypical antipsychotics, but it is not the primary driver of positive symptoms in the mesolimbic model.","b":"GABA is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter and is implicated in anxiety and seizure regulation, not directly in schizophrenia's positive symptoms.","d":"Acetylcholine is central to memory and is implicated in Alzheimer's disease, not the mesolimbic positive-symptom pathway."}},{"q":"Negative symptoms of schizophrenia include all EXCEPT:","concept":"Schizophrenia symptoms are divided into positive symptoms (added experiences like hallucinations and delusions) and negative symptoms (deficits in normal function). Negative symptoms are often summarised as the five As: avolition, alogia, anhedonia, asociality, and affective (emotional) flattening. Auditory hallucinations are a positive symptom, so they are the exception here.","traps":{"a":"Avolition, the loss of motivation and goal-directed activity, is a classic negative symptom.","c":"Affective flattening, reduced emotional expression, is a negative symptom.","d":"Alogia, poverty of speech, is a negative symptom."}},{"q":"Object permanence, in Piaget's theory, is fully achieved by approximately:","concept":"In Jean Piaget's theory, object permanence (knowing that objects continue to exist when out of sight) develops gradually across the sensorimotor stage. The A-not-B search error is overcome around 12 months, but fully mature object permanence, including mental representation of hidden objects, is achieved by the end of the sensorimotor stage, roughly 18 to 24 months. So full achievement is around 18 to 24 months.","traps":{"a":"At about 2 months infants are early in the sensorimotor stage and show no evidence of object permanence.","c":"8 to 12 months is when infants begin searching but still commit the A-not-B error, so object permanence is emerging, not fully achieved.","d":"At 4 months infants still act as though hidden objects cease to exist."}},{"q":"The Indian adaptation of the WAIS used commonly in clinical practice in India is the:","concept":"For adult intelligence assessment in India, the WAIS-IV India edition is the current standardised adaptation of the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale used in clinical practice. It is the culturally and normatively adapted adult scale, whereas the other listed tools are for children. So the WAIS-IV India edition is the intended answer.","traps":{"b":"The Binet-Kamat Test (BKT) is an Indian adaptation of the Stanford-Binet (Lewis Terman, 1916) used for children, not the adult WAIS.","c":"Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children (MISIC) is a child intelligence test adapted from the WISC, not an adult WAIS adaptation.","d":"'All of the above' is wrong because BKT and MISIC are child scales, so they are not adaptations of the adult WAIS."}},{"q":"Motivational Interviewing (Miller & Rollnick) uses the acronym OARS to refer to:","concept":"Motivational Interviewing, developed by William Miller and Stephen Rollnick, is a collaborative, client-centred method for strengthening a person's own motivation to change. Its foundational micro-skills are captured by the acronym OARS: Open questions, Affirmations, Reflective listening, and Summaries. These skills are used to build rapport, evoke and reinforce 'change talk', and roll with resistance rather than confront it.","traps":{"b":"Outcomes, Assessment, Rewards, Sanctions describes a behaviour-management or contingency framework, not the relational micro-skills of Motivational Interviewing.","c":"Observation, Analysis, Reframing, Suggestion sounds clinical but is a fabricated expansion; MI deliberately avoids directive 'suggestion' in favour of evoking the client's own arguments for change.","d":"Openness, Acceptance, Responsibility, Support lists therapeutic attitudes rather than the specific OARS communication skills Miller and Rollnick defined."}},{"q":"Read the passage and answer:\n\n'Cognitive behaviour therapy (CBT) rests on the premise that distorted thinking patterns sustain emotional distress. Identifying and modifying these patterns can therefore alleviate the distress, even when the original triggers cannot be changed. Empirical trials over five decades support its effectiveness across a range of disorders.'\n\nThe central idea of the passage is:","concept":"A main-idea question asks for the claim that the whole passage is built to support, not an incidental detail. This passage states that cognitive behaviour therapy works because distorted thinking patterns sustain distress, so modifying those patterns relieves the distress even when triggers cannot be changed. Option b captures that central mechanism precisely.","traps":{"a":"'Cures every psychological disorder' overstates the passage, which claims effectiveness across a range of disorders, not a universal cure.","c":"The passage actually says the opposite: it emphasises that CBT helps even when triggers cannot be removed, so it does not claim triggers are easy to remove.","d":"The passage cites five decades of empirical trials as support, so it treats trials as valuable, not unnecessary."}},{"q":"A correlation of r = +0.90 between hours studied and exam scores indicates that hours studied:","concept":"A correlation coefficient (Pearson's r) measures only the strength and direction of a linear relationship between two variables; it never establishes causation. An r of +0.90 signals a strong positive linear association. To find the proportion of variance shared you must square r: r squared here is 0.81, meaning 81 percent of the variance, not 90 percent.","traps":{"a":"'Cause higher scores' commits the classic correlation-equals-causation error; a confound such as motivation could drive both variables.","b":"'90% of the variance' confuses r with the coefficient of determination r squared; 0.90 squared is 0.81, so the correct figure is 81 percent.","d":"'Causally unrelated' is contradicted by the strong association, and correlation cannot be used to rule causation in or out either way."}},{"q":"Long-term potentiation (LTP) in the hippocampus is critically dependent on which receptor?","concept":"Long-term potentiation (Timothy Bliss and Terje Lømo, 1973) is the lasting strengthening of synapses that underlies learning and memory in the hippocampus. It depends critically on the NMDA receptor, which acts as a coincidence detector: it opens only when glutamate binds and the postsynaptic membrane is simultaneously depolarised enough to expel the magnesium ion blocking its channel. The resulting calcium influx triggers the molecular cascade that induces LTP.","traps":{"a":"AMPA receptors mediate fast baseline excitatory transmission and are inserted during LTP expression, but they do not initiate it; they lack the magnesium-gated coincidence detection NMDA provides.","b":"Muscarinic M1 receptors are metabotropic acetylcholine receptors that modulate cortical excitability; they are not the trigger for hippocampal LTP.","c":"GABA-A receptors are inhibitory chloride channels and dampen neuronal firing, working against the depolarisation LTP requires."}},{"q":"Borderline personality disorder is characterised primarily by:","concept":"Borderline personality disorder is a Cluster B disorder in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual defined by a pervasive pattern of instability in interpersonal relationships, self-image, and affect, together with marked impulsivity. Associated features include frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, recurrent suicidal or self-harming behaviour, chronic emptiness, and transient stress-related paranoia or dissociation. Option a states this core pattern directly.","traps":{"b":"Pervasive grandiosity and lack of empathy describe narcissistic personality disorder, a different Cluster B condition.","c":"Eccentric beliefs and odd speech without psychosis point to schizotypal personality disorder in Cluster A.","d":"Detachment from social relationships with restricted emotional expression describes schizoid personality disorder, not the emotional volatility of BPD."}},{"q":"Harlow's surrogate mother experiments with rhesus monkeys demonstrated that:","concept":"Harry Harlow's surrogate mother experiments gave infant rhesus monkeys a choice between a wire mother that dispensed milk and a soft cloth mother that gave no food. The infants clung to the cloth mother and returned to it for comfort, especially when frightened, seeking it far more than the feeding wire mother. This showed that contact comfort, not feeding, is the primary basis of attachment, refuting the 'cupboard love' secondary-drive theory.","traps":{"a":"'Feeding is the primary basis of attachment' is exactly the secondary-drive theory Harlow's results overturned.","b":"'Imprinting occurs only in birds' refers to Konrad Lorenz's work with geese and is unrelated to what Harlow's monkey study demonstrated.","d":"'Solely instinctive and does not require contact' is contradicted by the finding that contact comfort was decisive for the infants."}},{"q":"A test–retest reliability coefficient is most appropriate when assessing:","concept":"Test-retest reliability estimates the stability of a measure by administering the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlating the scores; it tells you whether scores hold steady over time. Each form of reliability answers a different question, so matching the coefficient to the target concept is key. Stability over time is the defining purpose of test-retest.","traps":{"a":"Internal consistency of items is assessed with Cronbach's alpha or split-half reliability, not test-retest.","b":"Inter-rater agreement is measured with Cohen's kappa or the intraclass correlation coefficient, comparing raters rather than occasions.","c":"Equivalence of two parallel forms is measured by parallel-forms (alternate-forms) reliability, using two different but equivalent versions of the test."}},{"q":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) aims primarily to increase:","concept":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy, developed by Steven Hayes within the third wave of behaviour therapy, aims primarily to increase psychological flexibility rather than to eliminate symptoms. It does this through six interrelated core processes forming the 'hexaflex': acceptance, cognitive defusion, present-moment awareness, self-as-context, values, and committed action. The goal is a rich, values-driven life alongside difficult internal experiences.","traps":{"a":"Symptom elimination is explicitly not the aim of ACT, which encourages acceptance of unwanted thoughts and feelings instead of struggling to remove them.","c":"Insight into childhood conflicts is a goal of psychodynamic therapy, not ACT.","d":"Cognitive disputation of irrational beliefs is the method of Albert Ellis's Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy; ACT deliberately uses defusion rather than disputing thought content."}},{"q":"Per the same passage, the author's tone toward CBT is best described as:","concept":"Tone questions ask about the author's attitude as revealed by word choice. This passage praises CBT and cites decades of supporting trials, but it uses careful qualifiers such as 'a range of' disorders and says distress 'can' be alleviated, avoiding sweeping claims. That combination of endorsement plus hedging makes the tone supportive but qualified.","traps":{"a":"'Dismissive' would require the author to downplay or reject CBT, but the passage endorses it.","b":"'Sarcastic' implies ironic mockery, which is absent from the straightforward, approving prose.","d":"'Polemical' means aggressively argumentative or combative, whereas the passage is measured and even-handed."}},{"q":"The CONSORT statement provides reporting guidelines for:","concept":"The CONSORT statement (Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials) is a set of evidence-based guidelines, centred on a checklist and flow diagram, for transparently reporting randomised controlled trials. Each major study design has its own reporting standard, so the disorder-specific guideline must be matched correctly. CONSORT is the one for RCTs.","traps":{"a":"Systematic reviews and meta-analyses are covered by the PRISMA statement, not CONSORT.","b":"Qualitative research reporting is guided by COREQ or SRQR standards.","c":"Diagnostic accuracy studies follow the STARD guidelines."}},{"q":"The hormone secreted by the posterior pituitary that promotes water reabsorption is:","concept":"The posterior pituitary stores and releases two hormones made by hypothalamic neurons. The one that promotes water reabsorption is antidiuretic hormone, also called vasopressin, which is synthesised in the supraoptic nucleus of the hypothalamus and acts on the collecting ducts of the kidney to conserve water. Option c names it correctly.","traps":{"a":"Oxytocin is also released from the posterior pituitary but drives uterine contraction and milk let-down, not water reabsorption.","b":"Cortisol is a glucocorticoid secreted by the adrenal cortex, not the pituitary.","d":"Prolactin is released from the anterior (not posterior) pituitary and stimulates milk production."}},{"q":"The most common comorbidity with autism spectrum disorder in DSM-5 is:","concept":"Autism spectrum disorder in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual very frequently co-occurs with other conditions. The most common comorbidities are intellectual disability, present in roughly half of cases, and attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder; DSM-5 notably lifted the earlier prohibition and now permits diagnosing ASD and ADHD together. Option d captures this correctly.","traps":{"a":"Conduct disorder is not a characteristic comorbidity of ASD; it is associated more with disruptive behaviour disorder pathways.","b":"Schizophrenia is a much rarer co-occurrence and was historically confused with autism, but it is not the common comorbidity.","c":"Bipolar I can co-occur but is far less frequent than intellectual disability or ADHD in ASD populations."}},{"q":"Telegraphic speech, characterised by two-word utterances such as 'mommy go', typically appears around:","concept":"Language development moves through predictable stages. Around 12 months children use single words (the holophrastic stage), and around 18 to 24 months they combine words into two-word 'telegraphic' utterances such as 'mommy go' that omit grammatical function words while preserving content words. This two-word telegraphic stage therefore appears at roughly 18 to 24 months.","traps":{"b":"12 months marks the one-word holophrastic stage, which precedes two-word combinations.","c":"At 6 months infants are babbling, well before meaningful word combinations.","d":"By 36 months children typically produce longer, more grammatically complete sentences, past the telegraphic stage."}},{"q":"The NEO-PI-R measures personality based on:","concept":"The NEO-PI-R, developed by Paul Costa and Robert McCrae, is the flagship measure of the Five-Factor Model of personality, summarised by the acronym OCEAN: Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism. It assesses each of the five broad domains through six specific facets, giving a detailed trait profile. Option c identifies this framework.","traps":{"a":"Cattell's 16 personality factors are measured by the 16PF questionnaire, not the NEO-PI-R.","b":"Eysenck's PEN model (Psychoticism, Extraversion, Neuroticism) is assessed by the Eysenck Personality Questionnaire.","d":"Murray's needs-press theory underlies the Thematic Apperception Test and the Edwards Personal Preference Schedule, not the NEO-PI-R."}},{"q":"EMDR was developed by:","concept":"Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing was developed by Francine Shapiro in 1987 as a structured therapy for trauma, in which the client attends to distressing memories while making guided bilateral eye movements or other rhythmic stimulation. It is now recommended for post-traumatic stress disorder by bodies including the American Psychological Association and the World Health Organization. Option a names its originator.","traps":{"b":"Marsha Linehan developed Dialectical Behaviour Therapy for borderline personality disorder, not EMDR.","c":"Aaron Beck founded Cognitive Therapy, a different modality.","d":"Edna Foa developed Prolonged Exposure therapy for PTSD, which is distinct from EMDR."}},{"q":"Find the error: 'Neither the patient nor the family members was informed about the diagnosis.'","concept":"When a subject uses the correlative 'neither...nor', the verb agrees in number with the nearer of the two subjects (the proximity rule). Here 'the family members' is plural and stands closest to the verb, so the correct verb is 'were', not 'was'. The error is therefore the word 'was'.","traps":{"a":"'Neither' is used correctly to introduce the first element of the neither/nor pair.","b":"'The patient' is grammatically fine; it is simply the more distant subject and does not govern the verb here.","c":"'No error' is wrong because the singular verb 'was' violates the proximity agreement rule."}},{"q":"REM sleep is characterised by all EXCEPT:","concept":"Rapid eye movement sleep is defined by low-voltage, mixed-frequency, fast EEG activity resembling wakefulness, together with rapid eye movements, near-complete skeletal muscle atonia, and genital tumescence. It is where most vivid dreaming occurs. Delta (slow) waves are the hallmark of deep NREM Stage 3 sleep, not REM, so the EXCEPT answer is the delta-wave option.","traps":{"a":"Rapid eye movements are a defining feature of REM sleep, so this is a true characteristic, not the exception.","c":"Skeletal muscle atonia genuinely occurs in REM, preventing acting out of dreams.","d":"Penile or clitoral tumescence is a normal accompaniment of REM sleep and is therefore also true."}},{"q":"PTSD per DSM-5-TR requires symptoms from how many symptom clusters?","concept":"Post-traumatic stress disorder in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition, Text Revision requires symptoms drawn from four clusters following exposure to a traumatic event: intrusion (Criterion B), avoidance (Criterion C), negative alterations in cognitions and mood (Criterion D), and marked alterations in arousal and reactivity (Criterion E). Symptoms must persist for more than one month. The answer is four clusters.","traps":{"b":"Three clusters reflects the older DSM-IV structure, before the negative-cognitions cluster was separated out.","c":"Two clusters undercounts and does not match any DSM-5 formulation of PTSD.","d":"Five clusters overstates it; there are four symptom clusters (B through E), with A being the stressor criterion, not a symptom cluster."}},{"q":"In Bowlby's theory, the phase during which an infant displays clear attachment behaviour to one specific caregiver and shows separation anxiety is:","concept":"John Bowlby described attachment as unfolding in phases. The clear-cut attachment phase, from roughly 7 months to 2 years, is when the infant shows focused attachment to one specific caregiver, protests at separation (separation anxiety), and displays wariness of strangers. Option b names this phase.","traps":{"a":"The pre-attachment phase (birth to about 6 weeks) involves innate signalling with no discrimination between caregivers.","c":"The attachment-in-the-making phase (about 6 weeks to 6-8 months) shows preference for familiar people but not yet strong protest at separation.","d":"The reciprocal relationships phase (from about 2 years) involves negotiation and the goal-corrected partnership, after separation anxiety declines."}},{"q":"If a raw score lies at the 84th percentile on a normally distributed test, the corresponding z-score is approximately:","concept":"A z-score expresses how many standard deviations a raw score lies from the mean of a normal distribution. Because the normal curve is fixed, the 50th percentile is the mean (z of 0), and about 84 percent of scores fall below one standard deviation above the mean. So a raw score at the 84th percentile corresponds to a z-score of approximately +1.","traps":{"a":"A z of 0 sits at the mean, the 50th percentile, not the 84th.","c":"A z of +1.5 lies near the 93rd percentile, above the 84th.","d":"A z of +2 corresponds to roughly the 97.5th percentile, far above the 84th."}},{"q":"In family therapy, the structural approach is associated with:","concept":"Structural family therapy was developed by Salvador Minuchin, who viewed family problems as arising from a dysfunctional family organisation. His approach maps subsystems, hierarchies, and the boundaries between members (rigid, clear, or diffuse), and the therapist actively restructures these to restore healthy functioning. Option a identifies him.","traps":{"b":"Murray Bowen founded intergenerational (Bowenian) family therapy, emphasising differentiation of self and triangulation.","c":"Virginia Satir developed the experiential, communication-focused approach to family therapy.","d":"Jay Haley is associated with strategic family therapy, which uses directives and paradoxical interventions."}},{"q":"Choose the correct passive form of: 'The committee will announce the results tomorrow.'","concept":"Converting an active sentence to the passive keeps the same tense. The original 'The committee will announce the results tomorrow' is future simple active, so its passive form must also be future simple: the object 'the results' becomes the subject, and the verb becomes 'will be announced', giving 'The results will be announced by the committee tomorrow.'","traps":{"b":"'Are being announced' is present continuous passive, which changes the tense and clashes with 'tomorrow'.","c":"'Have been announced' is present perfect passive and wrongly implies the action is already complete.","d":"'Were announced' is past simple passive, contradicting the future reference of the original sentence."}},{"q":"A patient with damage to the right parietal lobe is most likely to show:","concept":"The right (typically non-dominant) parietal lobe, especially the inferior parietal region, governs spatial attention across both sides of space. Damage here classically produces left hemispatial neglect: the patient fails to attend to, report, or respond to stimuli on the left side, and may ignore the left half of their own body and drawings. Option c describes this syndrome.","traps":{"a":"Broca's aphasia follows damage to the left inferior frontal lobe, not the right parietal lobe.","b":"Anterograde amnesia results from bilateral hippocampal or medial temporal damage, not parietal lesions.","d":"Prosopagnosia (face blindness) arises from lesions in the fusiform face area of the occipitotemporal cortex."}},{"q":"A delirium differs from a major neurocognitive disorder primarily in:","concept":"Delirium and major neurocognitive disorder (dementia) both involve cognitive impairment, but they differ chiefly in course and the domain affected. Delirium has an acute onset over hours to days, a fluctuating course, and a primary disturbance of attention and awareness, often with an identifiable medical cause. Major neurocognitive disorder has an insidious onset and a relatively stable, progressive decline, with memory or other cognition affected more than attention.","traps":{"a":"Hallucinations can occur in both conditions and are not exclusive to delirium.","b":"Memory impairment is prominent in major neurocognitive disorder too, so it does not distinguish the two.","d":"Reversibility is more characteristic of delirium (which can resolve when the cause is treated), not of dementia, so this reverses the actual pattern."}},{"q":"Theory of mind is most commonly assessed in 4–5 year olds using the:","concept":"Theory of mind (David Premack and Guy Woodruff, 1978) is the understanding that other people have mental states, including beliefs, that may differ from one's own and from reality. It is classically tested in 4 to 5 year olds with the false-belief task, of which the Sally-Anne task (Simon Baron-Cohen, Alan Leslie and Uta Frith, 1985) is the best known: the child must predict that Sally will look for her marble where she left it, not where it was secretly moved. Children reliably pass around age 4.","traps":{"a":"The A-not-B task assesses object permanence and inhibitory control in infants, not theory of mind.","b":"The conservation of liquid task measures Piagetian concrete-operational understanding of quantity, not mental-state reasoning.","d":"The Strange Situation (Mary Ainsworth, 1978) assesses infant attachment security, not false-belief understanding."}},{"q":"The Halstead-Reitan Battery is used for:","concept":"The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery is a fixed, comprehensive set of standardised tests (including the Category Test, Tactual Performance Test, and Trail Making Test) used to detect and characterise brain dysfunction. Its results are combined into an overall Impairment Index. It is therefore a tool for comprehensive neuropsychological assessment, as option c states.","traps":{"a":"Personality assessment uses instruments like the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) or NEO-PI-R (Paul Costa and Robert McCrae), not the Halstead-Reitan.","b":"Vocational interest assessment uses inventories such as the Strong Interest Inventory.","d":"Projective assessment of children uses tools like the Children's Apperception Test (Leopold Bellak and Sonya Sorel Bellak, 1949) or Rorschach, which are unrelated to this neuropsychological battery."}},{"q":"The therapeutic factor in group therapy described by Yalom as 'the realisation that one is not alone in their suffering' is:","concept":"Irvin Yalom identified eleven therapeutic factors that make group therapy effective. Universality is the member's realisation that they are not alone, that others share similar problems, fears, and feelings; this discovery reduces the sense of isolation and shame. Option b names this factor.","traps":{"a":"Catharsis is the relief that comes from openly expressing strong emotion within the group, a different factor.","c":"Altruism is the benefit members gain by helping and supporting one another.","d":"Instillation of hope is the encouragement members draw from seeing others who have improved."}},{"q":"Choose the word closest in meaning to 'AMBIVALENT':","concept":"A synonym question asks for the word closest in meaning. 'Ambivalent' means simultaneously holding opposing or conflicting feelings or attitudes toward something, so 'having mixed feelings' is the precise match. It is important not to confuse ambivalence (two feelings at once) with indifference (no feeling).","traps":{"b":"'Indifferent' means having no feeling or interest at all, the opposite of holding two strong feelings at once.","c":"'Decisive' means able to make firm choices quickly, which contradicts the internal conflict of ambivalence.","d":"'Hostile' denotes clear-cut antagonism, a single negative attitude rather than mixed feelings."}},{"q":"The autosomal dominant disorder caused by a CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion on chromosome 4 is:","concept":"Huntington's disease is an autosomal dominant neurodegenerative disorder caused by an expanded CAG trinucleotide repeat in the HTT gene on the short arm of chromosome 4. Having more than about 36 repeats causes the disease, and the repeat tends to lengthen across generations, producing earlier onset known as anticipation. Option d identifies it.","traps":{"a":"Down syndrome is caused by trisomy 21 (an extra chromosome 21), not a trinucleotide repeat.","b":"Phenylketonuria is an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder caused by a deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase, not a CAG expansion.","c":"Fragile X syndrome is caused by a CGG (not CAG) repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome."}},{"q":"The biopsychosocial model in psychopathology is most associated with:","concept":"The biopsychosocial model holds that health and illness result from the interacting contributions of biological, psychological, and social factors, rather than biology alone. It was proposed by the psychiatrist George L. Engel in 1977 as an explicit challenge to the reductionist biomedical model. Option d names him.","traps":{"a":"Emil Kraepelin pioneered the descriptive classification of mental disorders, laying groundwork for the biomedical, not biopsychosocial, approach.","b":"Aaron T. Beck founded cognitive therapy and the cognitive model of depression, not the biopsychosocial model.","c":"Eugen Bleuler coined the term 'schizophrenia' and described its symptoms, but did not formulate the biopsychosocial model."}},{"q":"The temperamental category in the New York Longitudinal Study (Thomas & Chess) characterised by withdrawal from new stimuli but gradual adaptation is:","concept":"In the New York Longitudinal Study, Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess described three temperamental clusters: easy, difficult, and slow-to-warm-up. The slow-to-warm-up child shows initial withdrawal and mild negative reactions to novelty and low activity, but with repeated, patient exposure gradually adapts and warms up. The defining pattern here is withdrawal from new stimuli followed by gradual adaptation, which is exactly the slow-to-warm-up profile.","traps":{"a":"Easy temperament describes a child with regular rhythms, positive mood, and quick adaptability who approaches (not withdraws from) novelty, so it misses the initial withdrawal in the stem.","c":"Difficult temperament involves irregular biological rhythms, intense negative reactions, and poor adaptation, not the gradual warming-up the question specifies.","d":"Inhibited only is not one of the three Thomas and Chess categories; it borrows from Jerome Kagan's behavioral inhibition work and is a distractor invented to sound plausible."}},{"q":"Standardisation in test construction primarily refers to:","concept":"Standardisation in test construction means fixing the conditions of a test so results are comparable across people: uniform administration instructions, uniform scoring rules, and a representative norm group against which raw scores are interpreted. It is the procedural foundation that lets a score mean the same thing for everyone. Reliability and validity are separate psychometric properties that build on, but are not the same as, standardisation.","traps":{"a":"High validity (whether a test measures what it claims) is a distinct psychometric property, not what standardisation refers to.","b":"Eliminating cultural bias relates to test fairness and item bias analysis; standardisation fixes procedures and norms but does not by itself remove cultural bias.","d":"Computer-administration is merely one delivery mode; a paper test can be fully standardised and a computer test can be poorly standardised, so the medium is irrelevant to the concept."}},{"q":"Interpersonal Therapy (IPT) for depression focuses primarily on:","concept":"Interpersonal Therapy, developed by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman, is a brief (roughly 12 to 16 session) evidence-based treatment for major depression. It links depressive symptoms to current relationship difficulties and works on one or more of four defined problem areas: grief, interpersonal role disputes, role transitions, and interpersonal deficits. Its focus is squarely on present interpersonal functioning rather than intrapsychic or cognitive content.","traps":{"a":"Childhood unconscious conflicts are the target of psychodynamic or psychoanalytic therapy, whereas IPT deliberately stays in the here-and-now of current relationships.","c":"Cognitive distortions are the focus of Aaron T. Beck's cognitive therapy, not the interpersonal problem areas that define IPT.","d":"Reinforcement schedules belong to operant behaviour therapy derived from B. F. Skinner, which is a different treatment model entirely."}},{"q":"Identify the correctly spelt word:","concept":"The correct spelling is conscientious, meaning careful, diligent, and guided by conscience. The word keeps the -sci- cluster from its root (conscience) and ends in -tious. Recognising the shared conscience root helps fix both the tricky -sci- and the ending.","traps":{"a":"Conscentious drops the 'i' after 'sc' and misplaces letters, so it is a misspelling.","b":"Concientious loses the 's' from the -sci- cluster and is therefore wrong.","c":"Consientious both drops the 'c' after 'con' and mangles the -sci- cluster, making it incorrect."}},{"q":"The Yerkes–Dodson law predicts that performance on a complex task is best at:","concept":"The Yerkes-Dodson law, formulated by Robert Yerkes and John Dillingham Dodson, describes an inverted-U relationship between physiological arousal and performance: performance improves as arousal rises up to an optimum, then declines with further arousal. A key refinement is that the optimal arousal level is lower for complex or difficult tasks and higher for simple, well-learned ones. So a complex task peaks at moderate arousal, with that optimum shifted lower than for easy tasks.","traps":{"a":"Very high arousal degrades performance on complex tasks, sitting on the falling side of the inverted-U, so it is not optimal.","b":"Very low arousal leaves the person under-activated and below the performance peak, so it is not the optimum either.","c":"Claiming the optimum is independent of task difficulty ignores the law's central refinement that difficulty shifts where the peak lies."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a Cluster A personality disorder?","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, personality disorders are grouped into three clusters. Cluster A (odd or eccentric) contains paranoid, schizoid, and schizotypal personality disorders. Cluster B (dramatic or erratic) contains antisocial, borderline, histrionic, and narcissistic. Cluster C (anxious or fearful) contains avoidant, dependent, and obsessive-compulsive personality disorder, so avoidant is the one that does not belong to Cluster A.","traps":{"a":"Paranoid personality disorder is a Cluster A (odd/eccentric) disorder, so it is not the exception.","c":"Schizotypal personality disorder is a Cluster A disorder marked by eccentricity and cognitive-perceptual distortions.","d":"Schizoid personality disorder, defined by social detachment and restricted emotion, is also Cluster A."}},{"q":"In Freud's psychosexual theory, fixation at the anal stage is hypothesised to produce:","concept":"In Sigmund Freud's psychosexual theory, the anal stage (roughly 1.5 to 3 years) centres on toilet training and control over elimination. Unresolved conflict here can lead to fixation producing either an anal-retentive character (orderly, rigid, stingy, obstinate) or an anal-expulsive character (messy, disorganised, careless). Hence anal fixation is classically tied to the anal-retentive or anal-expulsive personality.","traps":{"a":"Oral aggression arises from fixation at the oral stage (biting sub-phase), not the anal stage.","b":"The Oedipus complex is a feature of the phallic stage, when the child directs desire toward the opposite-sex parent.","d":"Genital identity confusion is not a Freudian anal-stage outcome; the genital stage concerns mature adult sexuality, and this option is a distractor."}},{"q":"Item response theory (IRT) differs from classical test theory primarily in that IRT:","concept":"Item response theory models the probability of a particular response as a mathematical (usually logistic) function of a person's latent trait level (theta) and item parameters such as difficulty, discrimination, and guessing. Unlike classical test theory, whose item statistics depend on the particular sample, IRT aims for parameters that are invariant across samples, which is what enables computer-adaptive testing. Its defining feature is this trait-by-item probability model.","traps":{"a":"Treating item statistics as sample-dependent is a limitation of classical test theory, the opposite of what IRT achieves.","b":"IRT models can include multiple parameters (one, two, or three-parameter models); assuming difficulty only describes just the simplest 1PL case, not IRT in general.","c":"IRT is precisely what makes adaptive testing possible, so saying it cannot be used for adaptive testing is false."}},{"q":"In CBT, a 'downward arrow' technique is used to:","concept":"The downward-arrow technique in cognitive behaviour therapy starts from an automatic thought and repeatedly asks a question like 'if that were true, what would it mean about you?' to peel back successive layers of meaning. Each answer is treated as if true and probed again until an underlying core belief or schema is reached (for example, 'I am unlovable' or 'I am worthless'). Its purpose is to uncover the core belief beneath a surface thought.","traps":{"b":"Identifying behavioural antecedents is done through functional or ABC analysis, not the meaning-probing downward arrow.","c":"Rating symptom intensity from 0 to 10 is a subjective-units or symptom-monitoring task, unrelated to uncovering beliefs.","d":"Constructing an exposure hierarchy belongs to systematic desensitisation (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) and exposure therapy, a different behavioural procedure."}},{"q":"Choose the option that best completes the sentence: 'Had she taken the medication, she ___ recovered sooner.'","concept":"This sentence is a third conditional, which expresses a counterfactual situation in the past. Its structure is 'Had + subject + past participle ... subject + would have + past participle', so the correct completion is 'would have recovered'. The inverted 'Had she taken' signals the unreal past condition that requires the 'would have + participle' result clause.","traps":{"a":"Would recover is the result form for a second (present/future unreal) conditional, not the past counterfactual required here.","b":"Had recovered is a past perfect that duplicates the condition clause and cannot serve as the result clause.","c":"Will recover is a first-conditional or future form and clashes with the unreal past set up by 'Had she taken'."}}],"met-mock-3":[{"q":"During an action potential, the absolute refractory period exists primarily because:","concept":"The absolute refractory period is the window during which no stimulus, however strong, can trigger a second action potential. It exists because the voltage-gated Na+ channels that carried the rising phase enter an inactivated state via their ball-and-chain inactivation gate; these channels physically cannot reopen until the membrane repolarises and resets them. This mechanism also enforces the one-way, non-decremental propagation of the action potential down the axon.","traps":{"b":"The Na+/K+ ATPase restores ionic gradients slowly over time but is not gated by a single spike, so its activity does not define the brief absolute refractory window.","c":"K+ channels are not permanently inactivated; their delayed opening drives repolarisation and the following relative refractory period, which is the opposite of a permanent shutdown.","d":"Cl- influx is associated with inhibitory postsynaptic potentials, not with the intrinsic refractoriness of an axon that has just fired."}},{"q":"The release of neurotransmitter at the synaptic terminal is most directly triggered by:","concept":"Neurotransmitter release is triggered when the action potential reaching the axon terminal opens voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, letting Ca2+ flow in. The incoming Ca2+ binds synaptotagmin, the calcium sensor, which drives SNARE-mediated fusion of synaptic vesicles with the presynaptic membrane, releasing transmitter into the cleft. Ca2+ is the direct trigger, which is why blocking these channels abolishes release.","traps":{"a":"K+ efflux through delayed rectifier channels repolarises the terminal and actually helps terminate the signal, rather than triggering vesicle fusion.","b":"The Na+/K+ ATPase maintains resting gradients over the long term and does not gate the moment-to-moment release of transmitter.","c":"Cl- influx through GABA-A receptors is a postsynaptic inhibitory event on the receiving neuron, not the presynaptic trigger for release."}},{"q":"The mesocortical dopamine pathway, when underactive, is most associated with:","concept":"The mesocortical pathway runs from the ventral tegmental area to the prefrontal cortex. When it is underactive (hypodopaminergic), it is linked to the negative symptoms (avolition, blunted affect, anhedonia) and cognitive deficits of schizophrenia. This contrasts with the mesolimbic pathway, whose overactivity is tied to positive symptoms, and explains why simply blocking dopamine everywhere can worsen negative and cognitive features.","traps":{"a":"Positive symptoms (hallucinations, delusions) are attributed to mesolimbic dopamine hyperactivity, not to mesocortical underactivity.","b":"Parkinsonian rigidity reflects dopamine loss in the nigrostriatal pathway, a motor circuit distinct from the mesocortical projection.","c":"Hyperprolactinaemia results from blockade of the tuberoinfundibular pathway, where dopamine normally inhibits prolactin release."}},{"q":"Which structure is the principal source of acetylcholine projections to the cortex and is severely depleted in Alzheimer's disease?","concept":"The nucleus basalis of Meynert, located in the basal forebrain, is the principal source of cholinergic (acetylcholine) projections to the cerebral cortex. Its degeneration is a hallmark of Alzheimer's disease and provides the rationale for cholinesterase inhibitors such as donepezil and rivastigmine, which boost remaining acetylcholine. Each brainstem/forebrain nucleus supplies a signature transmitter, so identifying the transmitter identifies the source.","traps":{"a":"The locus coeruleus is the main source of noradrenaline (norepinephrine), not acetylcholine, to the forebrain.","b":"The raphe nuclei supply serotonin throughout the brain, a different neuromodulatory system from the cholinergic one lost in Alzheimer's.","d":"The ventral tegmental area is a dopaminergic origin (mesolimbic and mesocortical pathways), unrelated to cortical acetylcholine."}},{"q":"The blood–brain barrier is formed primarily by:","concept":"The blood-brain barrier is formed by tight junctions (zonulae occludentes) between the endothelial cells lining brain capillaries, which seal the paracellular route and prevent free diffusion of most solutes. Surrounding astrocyte end-feet induce and maintain these tight junctions and help regulate the barrier. A few circumventricular organs (area postrema, OVLT, subfornical organ) deliberately lack this barrier so they can sample blood chemistry.","traps":{"b":"The pia mater is a thin meningeal covering and does not form the selective capillary barrier; fenestrations would defeat rather than create a barrier.","c":"Microglia are the brain's phagocytic immune cells; their clean-up role is separate from the physical endothelial barrier.","d":"Oligodendrocytes myelinate axons for faster conduction; they do not wrap capillaries or form the barrier."}},{"q":"Cerebrospinal fluid is produced primarily by the:","concept":"Cerebrospinal fluid is produced mainly by the choroid plexus, specialised vascular tissue located chiefly in the lateral ventricles. CSF flows through the ventricular system and subarachnoid space (roughly 500 mL made per day against a standing volume of about 150 mL) and is reabsorbed into the dural venous sinuses by the arachnoid granulations. Distinguishing production (choroid plexus) from reabsorption (arachnoid granulations) is the key.","traps":{"a":"Arachnoid granulations reabsorb CSF into the venous sinuses rather than produce it.","b":"The subarachnoid space is a conduit where CSF circulates, not a secretory structure.","c":"The dura mater is the tough outer meningeal layer and has no CSF-secreting function."}},{"q":"Lesions of the suprachiasmatic nucleus most directly disrupt:","concept":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus of the anterior hypothalamus is the body's master circadian pacemaker, entrained to the light-dark cycle by direct retinal input via the retinohypothalamic tract. Lesioning it abolishes the roughly 24-hour organisation of sleep-wake and other rhythms without necessarily reducing the total amount of sleep. Its defining role is timing, not the quantity of any single behaviour.","traps":{"a":"Thermoregulation is governed by the preoptic and posterior hypothalamic areas, not the SCN's timekeeping function.","b":"Anterior pituitary hormone release is controlled by hypothalamic releasing hormones via the portal system, a separate neuroendocrine role.","c":"Hunger and satiety are regulated by the lateral and ventromedial hypothalamic areas, not the circadian pacemaker."}},{"q":"Which feature most reliably distinguishes the cerebellum's role in motor learning from that of the basal ganglia?","concept":"The cerebellum and basal ganglia both shape movement but do different jobs. The cerebellum, using mossy and climbing fibre input to Purkinje cells with an inferior-olive error signal, supports error correction, timing, and motor coordination. The basal ganglia, through cortico-striato-pallido-thalamic loops, handle action selection and the consolidation of habits and procedural learning. The cleanest distinction pairs cerebellum with error/coordination and basal ganglia with selection/habit.","traps":{"a":"Climbing fibres and fine motor error signals belong to the cerebellum, not the basal ganglia, so this reverses the anatomy.","c":"Habit learning and reward-based action selection are basal ganglia functions, wrongly attributed here to the cerebellum.","d":"Neither structure sends the primary descending motor command to muscles; that arises from the motor cortex via the corticospinal tract, with these structures modulating it."}},{"q":"The lateral geniculate nucleus is most directly involved in:","concept":"The lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus is the main relay for vision, receiving retinal signals via the optic tract and projecting to the primary visual cortex (V1) through the optic radiation. Each sensory modality has its own thalamic relay, so pairing the LGN with vision follows the general rule of thalamic sensory gating (olfaction being the exception that bypasses the thalamus).","traps":{"a":"Audition is relayed by the medial geniculate nucleus, not the lateral geniculate.","b":"Somatosensation is relayed through the ventral posterior nuclei (VPL/VPM) of the thalamus.","d":"Olfaction is the one sense that bypasses the thalamus, projecting directly to the olfactory cortex."}},{"q":"Damage to which structure produces a contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing?","concept":"A contralateral homonymous hemianopia with macular sparing points to an infarction of the primary visual (calcarine) cortex, typically from posterior cerebral artery occlusion, with the occipital pole spared. The macular (central-vision) representation at the occipital pole often survives because it receives collateral blood supply from the middle cerebral artery. The pattern of field loss localises the lesion along the visual pathway.","traps":{"a":"An optic chiasm lesion produces a bitemporal hemianopia because it hits the crossing nasal fibres, not a homonymous field cut.","c":"An optic tract lesion just behind the chiasm gives a contralateral homonymous hemianopia but without the characteristic macular sparing of a cortical infarct.","d":"An optic nerve lesion before the chiasm causes monocular blindness in that eye, not a homonymous hemifield defect."}},{"q":"The dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia was supported originally by which observation?","concept":"The dopamine hypothesis of schizophrenia was originally supported by the finding, shown by Philip Seeman and colleagues, that the clinical potency of typical antipsychotics tracks their binding affinity for the D2 receptor across several orders of magnitude. Supporting evidence includes amphetamine, which releases dopamine and can induce a psychosis resembling schizophrenia. The pharmacological dose-affinity correlation is the classic pillar.","traps":{"a":"Typical antipsychotics act mainly by blocking D2 receptors, not by elevating serotonin, so this misstates their mechanism.","c":"Twin concordance for schizophrenia is well below 100% (around 50% in monozygotic twins), so this claim is factually false.","d":"Levodopa raises dopamine and can worsen or trigger psychotic symptoms rather than reliably improving negative symptoms."}},{"q":"The 'fight or flight' response is mediated primarily by:","concept":"The acute fight-or-flight response is driven by the sympathetic nervous system together with the adrenal medulla, which releases epinephrine (and norepinephrine) into the bloodstream, a mechanism described by Walter Cannon. This produces the rapid rise in heart rate, blood pressure, and glucose mobilisation. The slower, sustained stress response is handled separately by the HPA axis (CRH to ACTH to cortisol), studied by Hans Selye.","traps":{"a":"The parasympathetic vagal system promotes rest-and-digest and calming, the opposite of the fight-or-flight mobilisation.","b":"The enteric nervous system governs gut function locally and is not the driver of the whole-body stress reaction.","d":"The pituitary-thyroid axis regulates metabolic rate over the long term, not the seconds-scale emergency response."}},{"q":"Substance abuse disorders are most consistently associated with structural and functional changes in which neural circuit?","concept":"Substance abuse disorders converge on the mesolimbic dopamine reward circuit running from the ventral tegmental area to the nucleus accumbens (ventral striatum). Addictive drugs of all classes raise dopamine here, and with chronic use the dorsal striatum is recruited, shifting behaviour from goal-directed to compulsive habit (Everitt and Robbins). This shared reward pathway is the consistent site of structural and functional change.","traps":{"a":"The cerebellar vermis is involved in motor coordination and posture, not the reward-based core of addiction.","c":"The lateral geniculate nucleus is a visual relay and has no established role in drug reward.","d":"The vestibular nuclei process balance and head movement, unrelated to the addiction circuitry."}},{"q":"The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test is classically used to detect dysfunction of which region?","concept":"The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (David Grant and Esta Berg, 1948) measures cognitive set shifting, abstraction, and the inhibition of a prepotent response, all of which depend on the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex. Patients with DLPFC damage perseverate, continuing to sort by an old rule even after feedback signals that the rule has changed. Perseveration on the WCST is the classic sign of frontal executive dysfunction.","traps":{"b":"The hippocampus supports declarative and episodic memory (Endel Tulving, 1972) formation, which the WCST does not primarily test.","c":"The primary motor cortex executes voluntary movement and is not what set-shifting probes.","d":"The cerebellum supports motor coordination and timing, not the executive set-shifting captured by the WCST."}},{"q":"Long-term depression (LTD) of synaptic strength typically depends on:","concept":"Long-term depression is a lasting weakening of synaptic strength produced by prolonged, low-frequency stimulation (around 1 Hz) that causes only a modest rise in postsynaptic Ca2+. This modest Ca2+ signal activates phosphatases such as PP1, which dephosphorylate and internalise AMPA receptors, reducing responsiveness. It is the mirror image of long-term potentiation (Timothy Bliss and Terje Lømo, 1973), which needs high-frequency stimulation, a large Ca2+ influx, and CaMKII.","traps":{"a":"Brief high-frequency stimulation with a large Ca2+ rise induces long-term potentiation, not depression.","b":"Presynaptic GABA release produces fast inhibition and does not define the LTD mechanism at excitatory synapses.","c":"Mg2+ blocks the NMDA receptor at rest, not AMPA receptors, so this misidentifies both the ion target and the mechanism."}},{"q":"Which event-related potential component is most commonly elevated in oddball paradigms and reduced in schizophrenia?","concept":"The P300 is an event-related potential component elicited by rare target stimuli in the oddball paradigm; its amplitude indexes attentional resource allocation and the updating of working memory. In schizophrenia the P300 is reliably reduced in amplitude, a finding considered a candidate endophenotype. The pairing of oddball elicitation with reduced amplitude in schizophrenia identifies the P300.","traps":{"a":"The N100 is an early sensory/attentional response to stimulus onset and is not the hallmark oddball target component.","c":"P50 sensory gating reflects filtering of repeated stimuli and, although also reduced in schizophrenia, is not the oddball-elicited component asked for here.","d":"The Bereitschaftspotential (readiness potential) precedes voluntary movement and is unrelated to the oddball attention task."}},{"q":"Anterograde amnesia following bilateral mammillary body / dorsomedial thalamus damage with confabulation is most characteristic of:","concept":"Anterograde amnesia with confabulation following damage to the mammillary bodies and dorsomedial thalamus is the classic picture of Korsakoff's syndrome, caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, usually from chronic alcohol use. It often follows acute Wernicke's encephalopathy, whose triad is ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and confusion. The diencephalic lesion location plus confabulation is the giveaway.","traps":{"a":"Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease is a prion disorder causing rapidly progressive dementia and myoclonus, not the focal diencephalic amnesia described.","b":"Pick's disease (a frontotemporal dementia) presents with personality and language change from frontal/temporal atrophy, not mammillary-body amnesia.","d":"Huntington's chorea is a basal-ganglia degeneration marked by chorea and subcortical dementia, not thiamine-related confabulation."}},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter binds the receptor that benzodiazepines positively modulate?","concept":"Benzodiazepines are positive allosteric modulators of the GABA-A receptor, the ionotropic receptor whose natural ligand is GABA, the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter. By binding an allosteric site, benzodiazepines increase the frequency of Cl- channel opening when GABA is present, enhancing inhibition (barbiturates instead prolong channel opening). The receptor is defined by its endogenous agonist, GABA.","traps":{"a":"Glutamate is the principal excitatory transmitter acting on NMDA and AMPA receptors, the opposite of the inhibitory GABA-A system.","b":"Acetylcholine binds nicotinic and muscarinic receptors, not the benzodiazepine-modulated chloride channel.","d":"Glycine is an inhibitory transmitter but acts mainly at spinal glycine receptors, which benzodiazepines do not modulate."}},{"q":"Erikson's stage of 'autonomy vs shame and doubt' is associated with which age range and developmental task?","concept":"In Erik Erikson's psychosocial theory, autonomy versus shame and doubt is the second stage, occurring in toddlerhood (about 1 to 3 years), when the child exercises will and independence through tasks like toilet training. Success yields the virtue of will; failure breeds shame and self-doubt. Placing it correctly requires knowing the ordered sequence of Erikson's stages and their ages.","traps":{"a":"Age 0 to 1 with trust through consistent caregiving describes stage 1, trust versus mistrust, not autonomy.","b":"Ages 3 to 6 with initiating play and tolerating guilt describes stage 3, initiative versus guilt.","d":"Ages 6 to 12 with peer comparison and competence describes stage 4, industry versus inferiority."}},{"q":"Schaffer and Emerson's longitudinal study identified four phases of attachment. The 'specific attachment' phase typically begins around:","concept":"In Schaffer and Emerson's 1964 longitudinal Glasgow study, the specific attachment phase begins around 7 months, when the infant forms a focused bond with one primary caregiver and shows separation anxiety and stranger anxiety. This follows the asocial and indiscriminate phases and precedes multiple attachments. The onset of separation/stranger anxiety marks this phase at roughly 7 months.","traps":{"b":"5 months falls within the indiscriminate attachment phase, before a single specific figure is preferred.","c":"3 months is still the indiscriminate phase, when infants accept comfort from almost anyone.","d":"12 months is closer to the multiple attachments phase, after specific attachment has already been established."}},{"q":"Vygotsky considered private (self-directed) speech in young children to be:","concept":"Lev Vygotsky regarded private (self-directed) speech as a functional self-regulatory tool: children talk aloud to guide and organise their own behaviour, and this speech gradually goes underground, internalising around age 7 as inner speech or verbal thought. This directly opposes Piaget, who dismissed such talk as egocentric speech that simply fades. Empirical evidence has largely supported Vygotsky's self-regulation account.","traps":{"a":"Viewing private speech as a regression to autistic thinking to be discouraged is neither Vygotsky's nor a supported position.","b":"Treating it as mere imitation with no function ignores its demonstrated role in self-regulation.","d":"Calling it egocentrism that fades with schooling is precisely Piaget's rejected interpretation, not Vygotsky's."}},{"q":"Margaret Mahler's separation-individuation theory places 'rapprochement' at approximately what age?","concept":"In Margaret Mahler's separation-individuation theory, rapprochement occurs at about 15 to 24 months, when the toddler, having explored during the practising subphase, returns ambivalently to the mother, seeking closeness while asserting independence. It follows differentiation and practising and precedes object constancy. Knowing the ordered subphases and their ages places rapprochement correctly.","traps":{"a":"0 to 2 months corresponds to Mahler's normal autism phase, long before rapprochement.","c":"5 to 10 months is the differentiation subphase, when the infant begins to distinguish self from mother.","d":"3 to 5 years is past the separation-individuation subphases, overlapping the consolidation of object constancy rather than rapprochement."}},{"q":"Konrad Lorenz's imprinting in geese is best characterised as:","concept":"Konrad Lorenz's classic 1935 work on geese described imprinting as a rapid, largely irreversible attachment that forms during a narrow critical period (roughly 13 to 16 hours after hatching), during which goslings bond to the first moving object they see. It is biologically prepared and hard to undo, and it influenced John Bowlby's ethological theory of human attachment. The defining features are speed, irreversibility, and the critical period.","traps":{"a":"Slow, reversible lifelong learning is the opposite of imprinting's fast, time-limited, largely fixed nature.","c":"Imprinting is not operant conditioning (B. F. Skinner) with food reward; it occurs without reinforcement during the sensitive window.","d":"Imprinting is fundamentally an attachment/following behaviour, not a purely visual recognition system divorced from attachment."}},{"q":"Baumrind's authoritative parenting style is characterised by:","concept":"Diana Baumrind's authoritative style combines high demandingness with high warmth (responsiveness): parents set clear expectations and enforce them through reasoned, explained discipline while supporting the child's autonomy. This style is consistently linked to the best child outcomes. It is distinguished by crossing the two dimensions of demandingness and responsiveness.","traps":{"a":"High demands with low warmth and strict obedience describes the authoritarian style, not authoritative.","b":"Low demands with low warmth and disengagement describes the uninvolved or neglectful style.","c":"Low demands with high warmth and few rules describes the permissive style."}},{"q":"The 'still face' paradigm developed by Edward Tronick demonstrates that infants:","concept":"Edward Tronick's still face paradigm (1978) shows that when a previously responsive caregiver suddenly becomes expressionless, infants as young as 2 to 9 months become distressed, avert their gaze, and try to self-soothe or re-engage the parent. This demonstrates that infants are sensitive to contingent, reciprocal social interaction and actively expect it. The distress at sudden unresponsiveness is the key finding.","traps":{"a":"Infants clearly detect maternal facial cues well before 12 months, which is exactly what the paradigm demonstrates.","b":"The paradigm does not show stronger attachment to fathers than mothers under unresponsive conditions.","c":"Infants do not habituate immediately with no behavioural change; they visibly protest the blank face."}},{"q":"Piaget's term 'centration' refers to:","concept":"For Jean Piaget, centration is the preoperational child's tendency to focus (centre) on one salient feature of a situation while ignoring other relevant dimensions. For example, judging quantity by the height of a liquid column while overlooking its width, which is why conservation fails. Overcoming this, called decentration, together with reversibility, allows conservation in the concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years).","traps":{"b":"Coordinating schemas across modalities is a sensorimotor achievement, not the definition of centration.","c":"Hypothetical reasoning is a formal operational ability, the opposite end of Piaget's sequence from centration.","d":"A reversible mental operation is exactly what the centring preoperational child lacks; reversibility is what enables conservation."}},{"q":"Selman's stages of perspective-taking place the transition from 'subjective / differentiated' (level 1) to 'self-reflective / reciprocal' (level 2) around:","concept":"In Robert Selman's stages of social perspective-taking, the shift from level 1 (subjective/differentiated) to level 2 (self-reflective/reciprocal) occurs around age 7, within the 5 to 9 year band. At level 2 the child can step outside the self and reflect on how another person views their own thoughts and feelings. Mapping the numbered levels to their age ranges places this transition in early middle childhood.","traps":{"a":"3 to 5 years corresponds to the egocentric level 0, before differentiated perspective-taking.","c":"10 to 12 years corresponds to level 3 (mutual, third-person perspective-taking), which comes after level 2.","d":"Late adolescence corresponds to level 4 (societal perspective-taking), well beyond the level 1 to 2 transition."}},{"q":"Kohlberg's stage 3 (conventional level) reasoning is best summarised as:","concept":"In Lawrence Kohlberg's theory, stage 3 sits at the conventional level and is the good boy / nice girl orientation: moral reasoning centres on gaining approval, maintaining relationships, and living up to others' expectations. It is paired with stage 4 (law and order). Recognising that stage 3 values interpersonal approval, rather than punishment avoidance or abstract principle, is the key.","traps":{"a":"Obedience to avoid punishment is stage 1 of the pre-conventional level, not stage 3.","b":"Instrumental self-interest (what's in it for me) is stage 2 of the pre-conventional level.","d":"Universal ethical principles and human rights describe stage 6 of the post-conventional level."}},{"q":"Bowlby's concept of an 'internal working model' refers to:","concept":"John Bowlby's internal working model is the set of mental representations a child builds from early attachment experiences: a model of the self (as worthy of love or not) and of attachment figures (as available and responsive or not). Formed in infancy, these models guide expectations and behaviour in later relationships, an idea Hazan and Shaver extended to adult romantic attachment. It is a cognitive-emotional representation, not a physical structure.","traps":{"a":"A neural circuit between hippocampus and amygdala is anatomy, not the representational model Bowlby described.","c":"Genetic templates for fear conditioning are innate biological predispositions, not the experience-built relational model.","d":"The infant's reflex repertoire at birth is the newborn's inborn behaviour, not the learned internal representation of relationships."}},{"q":"Adolescent egocentrism (Elkind) is most directly associated with which two constructs?","concept":"David Elkind (1967) described adolescent egocentrism as expressed through two constructs: the imaginary audience, the belief that others are as preoccupied with one's appearance and behaviour as oneself, and the personal fable, the sense of being uniquely special and invulnerable. Both emerge with the onset of formal operational thought and are linked to adolescent self-consciousness and risk-taking.","traps":{"a":"Conservation and reversibility are Piagetian concrete-operational concepts, not features of adolescent egocentrism.","b":"Inner speech and scaffolding are Vygotskian ideas about learning and self-regulation, unrelated to Elkind's constructs.","c":"Object permanence and stranger anxiety are infancy phenomena, far removed from adolescent egocentrism."}},{"q":"Habituation paradigms in infants are often used to assess:","concept":"Habituation paradigms exploit the fact that infants look less at a repeated (familiar) stimulus and look more again (dishabituate) when something novel appears. Because a recovery of looking proves the infant treats the new item as different, researchers like Robert Fantz and Elizabeth Spelke use the method to probe preverbal discrimination and categorisation (telling stimuli apart and grouping them) before language exists to report on. It also underlies infant studies of numerical sense and physical knowledge.","traps":{"b":"Long-term declarative memory is tested with explicit recall or deferred imitation tasks, not with the moment-to-moment looking-time changes that habituation measures.","c":"Object permanence is probed with specific violation-of-expectation or search tasks (Piaget's hidden-object procedures), whereas habituation is a general discrimination tool, not tied to permanence alone.","d":"Moral reasoning is a much later verbal-cognitive achievement assessed by dilemmas (Piaget, Kohlberg), impossible to index by infant looking times."}},{"q":"The 'A-not-B error' is a hallmark of which Piagetian sub-stage and reflects:","concept":"The A-not-B error occurs in Jean Piaget's sensorimotor stage, around sub-stage 4 (roughly 8 to 12 months): after an object is repeatedly hidden and found at location A, the infant keeps reaching to A even after watching it hidden at B. It reflects incomplete object permanence plus immature working memory and inhibitory control (a prepotent reach to the previously rewarded spot). This makes 'sensorimotor; incomplete object permanence' the correct pairing.","traps":{"a":"Formal operational is the adolescent stage of hypothetical-deductive reasoning; the A-not-B error appears in infancy, long before abstract hypotheticals.","b":"Preoperational centration on appearance describes conservation failures in 2 to 7 year olds, a different error from the infant reaching mistake.","c":"Concrete operational reversibility failures belong to the transition out of preoperations, not to the sensorimotor object-search behaviour seen here."}},{"q":"DSM-5 requires symptoms of major depressive disorder to be present for at least:","concept":"The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, fifth edition (DSM-5) requires at least 2 weeks of symptoms for major depressive disorder: 5 or more of 9 symptoms present most of the day, nearly every day, with at least one being depressed mood or anhedonia. The 2-week minimum distinguishes an episode from transient sadness. Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia), by contrast, needs 2 years.","traps":{"a":"1 week is the minimum duration for a manic episode, not for major depressive disorder.","c":"1 month is the active-phase threshold used in psychotic disorders, not the depression duration criterion.","d":"6 months evokes durations like generalised anxiety disorder or total schizophrenia disturbance, not the 2-week MDD requirement."}},{"q":"Per DSM-5, schizophrenia requires continuous signs of disturbance for at least:","concept":"Under DSM-5, schizophrenia requires continuous signs of disturbance for at least 6 months, which must include at least 1 month of active-phase (Criterion A) symptoms such as delusions, hallucinations, or disorganised speech. The remaining time can be prodromal or residual. This 6-month rule separates schizophrenia from schizophreniform disorder (1 to 6 months) and brief psychotic disorder (under 1 month).","traps":{"a":"1 week is far too brief; even brief psychotic disorder allows up to a month, and schizophrenia needs six.","b":"1 month is only the active-phase symptom minimum, not the total disturbance duration required.","d":"12 months overstates the requirement; DSM-5 fixes the threshold at 6 months, not a full year."}},{"q":"First-rank symptoms of schizophrenia (Schneider) most prominently include:","concept":"Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms are specific psychotic experiences he considered highly suggestive of schizophrenia: passivity phenomena (thought insertion, withdrawal, and broadcasting; made feelings, impulses, and acts; somatic passivity) and particular auditory hallucinations (voices commenting or arguing in the third person). They had good inter-rater reliability but are no longer required for diagnosis in DSM-5 or ICD-11.","traps":{"a":"Avolition and flat affect are negative symptoms, important to schizophrenia but not part of Schneider's first-rank list.","b":"Memory deficits and disorientation to person point to organic or amnestic conditions, not Schneiderian first-rank symptoms.","d":"Catatonic excitement is a motor/behavioural feature, distinct from the passivity and auditory phenomena Schneider emphasised."}},{"q":"A patient with persistent intrusive thoughts of contamination, accompanied by ritualised hand-washing that consumes >1 hour/day, is best classified under DSM-5 as:","concept":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder (OCD) in DSM-5 involves obsessions (intrusive, unwanted thoughts such as contamination fears) and/or compulsions (repetitive behaviours like hand-washing) that are time-consuming (over 1 hour per day) or cause marked distress or impairment. The classic contamination-obsession-plus-washing-ritual picture is prototypical OCD, which DSM-5 moved out of the anxiety disorders into its own obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter.","traps":{"a":"Generalised anxiety disorder is excessive worry across many life domains, without the specific intrusive obsessions and ritualised compulsions seen here.","b":"Illness anxiety disorder is preoccupation with having a serious illness, not contamination obsessions relieved by washing rituals.","c":"Specific phobia is marked fear cued by a particular object or situation with avoidance, not compulsive rituals consuming over an hour a day."}},{"q":"Borderline personality disorder, per DSM-5, requires ≥5 of 9 criteria. Which is NOT among them?","concept":"DSM-5 borderline personality disorder requires 5 or more of 9 criteria centred on pervasive instability: frantic efforts to avoid abandonment, unstable intense relationships, identity disturbance, impulsivity, recurrent suicidal or self-mutilating behaviour, affective instability, chronic emptiness, inappropriate anger, and transient stress-related paranoia or dissociation. Cruelty to animals and pre-age-15 rule-breaking are conduct disorder features (a precursor of antisocial PD), so they are NOT among the BPD criteria.","traps":{"a":"Frantic efforts to avoid real or imagined abandonment is criterion 1 of BPD, so it is included, not the exception.","c":"Recurrent suicidal behaviour, gestures, or self-mutilation is a core BPD criterion, so it belongs to the set.","d":"Identity disturbance with an unstable self-image is another genuine BPD criterion, not the odd one out."}},{"q":"Capgras syndrome is characterised by:","concept":"Capgras syndrome is the delusion of doubles: the patient believes a familiar person (often a spouse or parent) has been replaced by a physically identical impostor. Ellis and Young linked it to a disconnection between intact face recognition and the normal affective response to that face, so the person looks right but feels wrong. It contrasts with Cotard, Fregoli, and Othello delusions.","traps":{"a":"Belief that one is dead or non-existent is Cotard delusion (nihilistic delusion), not Capgras.","b":"Delusional jealousy about a partner's fidelity is Othello syndrome, a different misidentification-adjacent delusion.","c":"The belief that a stranger is a familiar person in disguise is Fregoli syndrome, the mirror image of Capgras."}},{"q":"ICD-11 retains a category of dissociative neurological symptom disorder (also termed 'conversion'). Its core feature is:","concept":"In ICD-11, dissociative neurological symptom disorder (the older term is conversion disorder, or functional neurological symptom disorder in DSM-5) is defined by motor or sensory symptoms, such as non-epileptic seizures, paralysis, or blindness, that are internally inconsistent and incompatible with recognised neurological disease, and are not intentionally produced. The unconscious, non-feigned nature separates it from malingering and factitious disorder.","traps":{"a":"Intentional production of symptoms for external gain (money, avoiding duty) is malingering, not conversion, which is unintentional.","b":"A persecutory delusion with intact reality testing describes a psychotic phenomenon, unrelated to functional neurological symptoms.","c":"A persistent memory deficit from organic brain injury is an organic amnestic/neurocognitive picture, the opposite of symptoms incompatible with known disease."}},{"q":"PTSD per DSM-5 requires the symptoms to persist for at least:","concept":"DSM-5 post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD) requires symptoms to persist for more than 1 month following exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence, with disturbance across four clusters: intrusion, avoidance, negative alterations in cognition and mood, and altered arousal and reactivity. Symptoms lasting 3 days to 1 month are instead classified as acute stress disorder.","traps":{"a":"2 days is below even the acute stress disorder floor (which starts at 3 days), so it cannot mark PTSD.","b":"1 week falls within the acute stress disorder window, not the PTSD threshold.","c":"6 months is when PTSD may be specified 'with delayed expression,' but the minimum duration to diagnose is 1 month."}},{"q":"A patient presents with episodes of binge eating ≥1/week for ≥3 months, without compensatory behaviours, with marked distress about the binges. The DSM-5 diagnosis is:","concept":"Binge-eating disorder (BED) in DSM-5 is defined by recurrent binge episodes (eating an objectively large amount with a sense of loss of control) occurring at least once a week for at least 3 months, with marked distress, and crucially NO recurrent inappropriate compensatory behaviours. The absence of purging, fasting, or excessive exercise is what separates BED from bulimia nervosa. BED became a full diagnosis in DSM-5.","traps":{"b":"Bulimia nervosa requires recurrent compensatory behaviours (purging, fasting, or exercise), which this patient lacks.","c":"Anorexia nervosa, binge-purge subtype, requires significantly low body weight and compensatory behaviour, neither described here.","d":"Avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder involves avoidance of food without binge episodes or body-image concern, unlike this presentation."}},{"q":"Charles Bonnet syndrome is characterised by:","concept":"Charles Bonnet syndrome is the occurrence of vivid, complex visual hallucinations in people with significant vision loss (for example macular degeneration or glaucoma) who are cognitively intact and typically retain insight that the images are not real. It is understood as a release phenomenon: deafferented visual cortex generates spontaneous imagery. Intact insight and ocular pathology are its hallmarks.","traps":{"a":"Auditory hallucinations in the deaf are the auditory analogue (sometimes called musical release hallucinations), not Charles Bonnet syndrome, which is visual.","b":"Multimodal hallucinations in delirium occur with clouded consciousness and lost insight, opposite to the intact, insightful patient here.","d":"Olfactory hallucinations preceding temporal lobe seizures are uncinate/epileptic auras, unrelated to vision loss."}},{"q":"Delirium is most reliably differentiated from dementia by:","concept":"Delirium is distinguished from dementia chiefly by its acute onset, fluctuating course, and disturbance of attention and consciousness (level of awareness), often with a reversed sleep-wake cycle and an identifiable medical or toxic cause. Dementia is insidious and progressive, dominated by cognitive decline (memory, executive function) with consciousness relatively preserved until late. Attention/consciousness change is the key discriminator.","traps":{"a":"Older age at onset does not separate the two, since both delirium and dementia are more common in the elderly.","b":"Confabulation is more typical of amnestic syndromes such as Korsakoff's, not a reliable delirium-versus-dementia discriminator.","d":"Aphasia and apraxia as early features point toward specific dementias, not the defining feature of delirium."}},{"q":"Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) requires depressed mood for most of the day, more days than not, for at least:","concept":"Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia) in DSM-5 requires depressed mood most of the day, more days than not, for at least 2 years in adults (1 year in children and adolescents), plus at least 2 of 6 associated symptoms, with no symptom-free interval longer than 2 months. DSM-5 merged chronic major depression and the old dysthymic disorder into this single category.","traps":{"b":"6 months is far short of the chronic 2-year course that defines persistent depressive disorder.","c":"1 month is not a depression duration threshold at all; even acute MDD needs 2 weeks.","d":"5 years overstates the requirement; the minimum is 2 years in adults, not five."}},{"q":"Tourette's disorder per DSM-5 requires multiple motor and ≥1 vocal tic, with onset before age 18 and duration of at least:","concept":"Tourette's disorder in DSM-5 requires multiple motor tics and at least one vocal tic (not necessarily concurrent), present for more than 1 year since first tic onset, with onset before age 18. The 1-year duration distinguishes it from provisional tic disorder (under 1 year), while persistent (chronic) motor or vocal tic disorder involves only one type, motor or vocal but not both.","traps":{"a":"1 month is far too short; that duration would fall under provisional tic disorder, not Tourette's.","b":"3 months still falls within the provisional (under 1 year) window, not the Tourette's threshold.","d":"6 months likewise does not meet the required duration; Tourette's needs symptoms for more than a year."}},{"q":"Anorexia nervosa, restricting subtype, per DSM-5 is defined by:","concept":"Anorexia nervosa in DSM-5 is defined by restriction of energy intake leading to significantly low body weight, intense fear of gaining weight, and disturbance in the way body weight or shape is experienced. The restricting subtype means weight loss achieved mainly through dieting, fasting, or exercise, with no recurrent binge-eating or purging in the last 3 months. DSM-5 dropped the amenorrhoea criterion and the fixed BMI cutoff.","traps":{"a":"A BMI under 17.5 alone was an ICD-10 style marker; DSM-5 does not use a fixed BMI cutoff and requires fear and body-image disturbance too.","b":"Selective eating without weight loss suggests avoidant/restrictive food intake disorder, not anorexia nervosa.","c":"Recurrent binge eating with purging characterises the binge-purge subtype (or bulimia), not the restricting subtype."}},{"q":"Which symptom cluster is NOT among the DSM-5 PTSD criteria?","concept":"DSM-5 PTSD is organised into four symptom clusters: intrusion (flashbacks, intrusive memories, nightmares), avoidance of trauma reminders, negative alterations in cognitions and mood, and marked alterations in arousal and reactivity (hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, irritability, sleep disturbance). Magical thinking and ideas of reference are odd-belief features of schizotypal personality disorder, so that cluster is NOT part of PTSD.","traps":{"b":"Avoidance of trauma reminders is a genuine PTSD cluster, so it is included, not the exception.","c":"Negative alterations in cognitions and mood is one of the four PTSD clusters, part of the criteria set.","d":"Intrusion symptoms such as flashbacks and intrusive memories form the first PTSD cluster, clearly included."}},{"q":"Pseudobulbar affect (PBA) is best characterised by:","concept":"Pseudobulbar affect (PBA), or emotional lability/incontinence, is brief, involuntary, stereotyped outbursts of laughing or crying that are disproportionate to or decoupled from the person's actual mood, arising in neurological disease such as multiple sclerosis, stroke, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, or brain injury. It reflects disrupted corticobulbar control over brainstem emotional-motor pathways and responds to dextromethorphan/quinidine or SSRIs.","traps":{"a":"Mood-congruent emotional expression in major depression matches the underlying mood, unlike PBA where affect is disconnected from mood.","b":"Restricted (flat) affect in schizophrenia is a reduction of emotional expression, the opposite of PBA's exaggerated outbursts.","d":"Histrionic emotional shifts are personality-driven and situationally reactive, not involuntary neurological outbursts."}},{"q":"DSM-5 distinguishes mania from hypomania primarily by:","concept":"DSM-5 separates mania from hypomania mainly by severity and duration. A manic episode lasts at least 1 week (or any duration if hospitalisation is needed), causes marked functional impairment, and may include psychotic features, defining Bipolar I. Hypomania lasts at least 4 days, produces an observable change in functioning but no marked impairment and no psychosis; paired with a major depressive episode it defines Bipolar II.","traps":{"b":"Hypomania by definition excludes psychotic features; their presence would make the episode manic, not hypomanic.","c":"Mania is not always preceded by depression; a manic episode alone suffices for Bipolar I.","d":"Hypomania does require an observable change from baseline; the point is that the change is not severe enough to cause marked impairment."}},{"q":"Folie à deux (shared psychotic disorder) most often involves:","concept":"Folie a deux, or shared psychotic disorder, classically involves a dominant person with a primary psychotic illness (the inducer) and a more passive, often related person who comes to share the delusional belief, usually in conditions of close contact and social isolation. Separating the pair often resolves the induced belief. DSM-5 removed it as a discrete diagnosis, folding it under 'other specified schizophrenia spectrum and other psychotic disorder.'","traps":{"a":"Two patients independently developing identical delusions is not folie a deux (Charles Lasègue and Jules Falret, 1877), which requires transmission from a primary case to a passive recipient.","b":"A mother and infant sharing dissociative symptoms is not the phenomenon; folie a deux concerns shared delusions, not infant dissociation.","c":"Calling it purely cultural is wrong; folie a deux is a recognised psychiatric (induced delusional) phenomenon."}},{"q":"On the WAIS-IV (and WAIS-V), the mean and standard deviation of subtest scaled scores are:","concept":"On the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS-IV and WAIS-V), individual subtest scaled scores are standardised to a mean of 10 and a standard deviation of 3. The composite/index scores (Full Scale IQ and the index scores such as Verbal Comprehension and Processing Speed) instead use a mean of 100 and SD of 15. Knowing which metric applies to subtests versus composites is the key distinction.","traps":{"a":"Mean 100, SD 15 describes the composite/index and Full Scale IQ scores, not the individual subtest scaled scores.","b":"Mean 50, SD 10 is the T-score metric (used on the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943)), not Wechsler subtest scaling.","d":"Mean 0, SD 1 is the z-score (standard normal) metric, not the scaled-score system."}},{"q":"The MMPI-2 validity scale 'F' (Infrequency) elevated to a T-score >100 most plausibly indicates:","concept":"On the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory-2 (MMPI-2), the F (Infrequency) scale contains items endorsed by fewer than 10% of the normative sample. A very high F score (T over 100) most plausibly signals random or inconsistent responding, genuinely severe psychopathology, or symptom exaggeration (faking bad). VRIN and TRIN are then used to tell random responding apart from deliberate over-reporting.","traps":{"a":"Faking good or minimisation is indicated by elevated L and K scales, not by a high F score.","c":"A high F does not reflect excellent reliability; it flags an invalid or exaggerated profile.","d":"Above-average defensiveness is captured by the K scale; F elevation points the opposite way, toward over-reporting."}},{"q":"Cronbach's alpha is a measure of:","concept":"Cronbach's alpha estimates internal consistency reliability: it reflects the average intercorrelation among a test's items measuring the same construct, adjusted for the number of items. Values above 0.70 are generally acceptable and above 0.80 good. It answers whether the items hang together, not whether scores are stable over time or agree across raters.","traps":{"a":"Test-retest reliability is assessed by correlating scores from two separate administrations, not by Cronbach's alpha.","b":"Construct validity concerns whether the test measures the intended construct, a validity question distinct from internal consistency.","d":"Inter-rater reliability (agreement between raters) is indexed by Cohen's kappa or an intraclass correlation, not by alpha."}},{"q":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test uses 10 standardised cards; the Exner Comprehensive System scores responses on dimensions that include all of the following EXCEPT:","concept":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test uses 10 standardised cards, and John Exner's Comprehensive System codes each response on structured dimensions: location (Whole, common Detail, unusual Detail, Space), determinants (form, colour, shading, movement), form quality, content, popularity, special scores, and organisational activity. It yields structural summary indices but never an IQ score, so 'explicit IQ score' is the exception.","traps":{"a":"Location coding (W, D, Dd, S) is a genuine Exner dimension, so it is scored, not excluded.","b":"Determinants (form, colour, shading, movement) are core to Exner coding, part of what is scored.","c":"Form quality is another standard Exner scoring dimension, included in the Comprehensive System."}},{"q":"Sensitivity of a screening instrument is best defined as:","concept":"Sensitivity is the proportion of true cases that a test correctly identifies as positive: true positives divided by (true positives plus false negatives). A highly sensitive test rules conditions out when negative (few missed cases), so it is prized for screening. It is a fixed property of the test, unlike predictive values, which shift with the base rate of the condition.","traps":{"b":"The proportion of true negatives correctly identified is specificity, not sensitivity.","c":"The probability that a positive scorer truly has the condition is positive predictive value, which depends on prevalence.","d":"The probability that a negative scorer truly lacks the condition is negative predictive value, again base-rate dependent."}},{"q":"Stanine scores have a mean and SD of:","concept":"Stanine scores (from 'standard nine') form a single-digit scale from 1 to 9 with a mean of 5 and a standard deviation of 2, widely used in educational assessment to place a student's performance into one of nine bands. Recognising this metric prevents confusion with T-scores, scaled scores, and deviation IQs, which use different means and SDs.","traps":{"a":"Mean 50, SD 10 is the T-score metric, not the stanine scale.","c":"Mean 10, SD 3 is the Wechsler subtest scaled-score metric, not stanines.","d":"Mean 100, SD 15 is the deviation IQ / composite metric, not the 1-to-9 stanine scale."}},{"q":"The Beck Depression Inventory-II severity cutoff conventionally used to flag 'moderate' depression is approximately:","concept":"The Beck Depression Inventory-II (Beck et al., 1996) has 21 items scored 0 to 3, for a maximum of 63. Conventional severity bands in clinical samples are 0 to 13 minimal, 14 to 19 mild, 20 to 28 moderate, and 29 to 63 severe. So a score in the 20 to 28 range flags 'moderate' depression.","traps":{"b":"14 to 19 is the mild band, one step below moderate.","c":"0 to 13 is the minimal range, indicating little or no depression.","d":"29 to 63 is the severe band, above the moderate range."}},{"q":"Item Response Theory (IRT), unlike Classical Test Theory, models:","concept":"Item Response Theory (IRT) models the probability of a given response to each item as a function of an underlying latent trait (theta), using item-level parameters: a (discrimination), b (difficulty), and in the three-parameter model c (pseudo-guessing). Unlike Classical Test Theory, which works at the whole-test level, IRT places persons and items on the same scale, enabling computer adaptive testing and equating across forms (as in the GRE and USMLE).","traps":{"a":"Modelling only group-level means and variances describes a limited classical approach, not IRT's item-and-person latent-trait modelling.","b":"Test-retest stability is a reliability concept, not what IRT is designed to model.","d":"Inter-rater agreement on observed behaviour is an observational reliability issue, unrelated to IRT's latent-trait item modelling."}},{"q":"The Bender-Gestalt II is primarily used to assess:","concept":"The Bender-Gestalt II (Brannigan and Decker, 2003) presents 16 designs that the examinee copies and later recalls, primarily assessing visuomotor integration (the coordination of visual perception with motor output). It is used for developmental screening and as a brief neuropsychological screen for organic impairment. The original Bender Visual Motor Gestalt Test used 9 figures drawn from Max Wertheimer's Gestalt work.","traps":{"a":"Premorbid intelligence is estimated with reading-based tests (for example the National Adult Reading Test), not the Bender-Gestalt.","b":"Personality structure is assessed by projective or objective personality tests, not a visuomotor copying task.","c":"Verbal working memory is measured by tasks like digit span, not by copying geometric designs."}},{"q":"The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is classified as which type of test?","concept":"The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan in 1935, is a projective personality test: the examinee is shown ambiguous picture cards and constructs stories, which are interpreted to reveal underlying needs, presses, and recurring themes. Its ambiguous stimuli and interpretive scoring make it projective, and Murray's needs-press theory later informed David McClelland's measurement of need for achievement.","traps":{"b":"An objective personality test uses standardised, fixed-response items (like the MMPI), unlike the open-ended storytelling of the TAT.","c":"An intelligence test measures cognitive ability with scored problems, not projective story construction.","d":"A neuropsychological battery assesses brain-behaviour functions, not the personality dynamics the TAT targets."}},{"q":"Concurrent validity is best demonstrated when:","concept":"Concurrent validity is a form of criterion validity established when a new test correlates highly with an already-established, validated measure of the SAME construct, both administered at the same point in time. The 'concurrent' part means the criterion is measured now, not in the future (that would be predictive validity). Option d captures both requirements: same construct and same time.","traps":{"a":"This describes correlating with a DIFFERENT construct at the same time, which is the opposite of what validity needs; high correlation with an unrelated construct signals poor discriminant validity, not good concurrent validity.","b":"Predicting future performance is predictive validity, the other flavour of criterion validity, distinguished by the criterion being measured later rather than concurrently.","c":"Stable internal consistency over time describes reliability (test-retest or internal consistency), not validity against a criterion at all."}},{"q":"The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery includes all of the following EXCEPT:","concept":"The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery is a fixed battery of standardized tests (Category Test, Tactual Performance Test, Trail Making A and B, Finger Tapping, Speech-Sounds Perception, Rhythm/Seashore Test, Sensory-Perceptual Examination) used with the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale and the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) as adjuncts. It measures brain-behaviour relationships across cognitive and sensorimotor domains. The Rorschach is a projective personality test and is not part of the HRNB.","traps":{"a":"The Category Test is a core Halstead-Reitan measure of abstraction and concept formation, so it is genuinely included.","b":"The Tactual Performance Test (form board done blindfolded) is a central Halstead-Reitan subtest of tactile and spatial learning.","c":"Trail Making A and B are part of the Halstead-Reitan battery, assessing visual scanning and set-shifting."}},{"q":"The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales primarily measures:","concept":"The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales (Sparrow and colleagues) is a semi-structured caregiver interview or rating that measures adaptive functioning, meaning how well a person copes with everyday demands. Its core domains are Communication, Daily Living Skills, and Socialisation, plus a Motor Skills domain for young children. It is required alongside an intelligence test to diagnose intellectual disability, because that diagnosis needs both low IQ and deficits in adaptive behaviour.","traps":{"b":"Intellectual quotient is measured by IQ tests such as the Wechsler or Stanford-Binet (Lewis Terman, 1916), not by the Vineland, which specifically assesses adaptive behaviour that IQ tests do not capture.","c":"Psychopathology in adolescents is assessed by instruments like the Youth Self-Report or MMPI-A, not by an adaptive behaviour scale.","d":"Premorbid personality is estimated through clinical history or personality inventories, and has nothing to do with the Vineland's adaptive-functioning focus."}},{"q":"If a test has reliability of 0.80 and a standard deviation of 15, the standard error of measurement (SEM) is approximately:","concept":"The standard error of measurement quantifies how much an observed score is expected to fluctuate around the true score due to measurement error. It is computed as SEM = SD times the square root of (1 minus reliability). Here that is 15 times the square root of (1 minus 0.80) = 15 times the square root of 0.20 = 15 times 0.447, which is approximately 6.7. Higher reliability produces a smaller SEM.","traps":{"b":"1.5 would only arise from a calculation error such as forgetting to take the square root or misplacing a decimal; it does not follow from the SEM formula with these values.","c":"12.0 comes from mistakenly using square root of reliability (0.80) rather than square root of (1 minus reliability), inflating the estimate.","d":"15.0 is simply the standard deviation itself, which would only equal the SEM if reliability were zero (a completely unreliable test)."}},{"q":"The Wechsler Memory Scale (WMS-IV) yields five Index scores. Which is NOT among them?","concept":"The Wechsler Memory Scale, Fourth Edition, yields five index scores: Auditory Memory, Visual Memory, Visual Working Memory, Immediate Memory, and Delayed Memory. These capture memory across modality (auditory versus visual), working memory, and time (immediate versus delayed recall). Mechanical Reasoning is not among them.","traps":{"a":"Auditory Memory is a genuine WMS-IV index reflecting recall of verbally presented material.","c":"Visual Working Memory is a real WMS-IV index measuring temporary manipulation of visual-spatial information.","d":"Mechanical Reasoning is a subtest of the Differential Aptitude Test, an occupational aptitude measure, not a memory index."}},{"q":"In CBT, the term 'Socratic questioning' refers to:","concept":"Socratic questioning, also called guided discovery in cognitive behaviour therapy (associated with Aaron Beck and Christine Padesky), uses a series of open-ended questions that lead clients to examine the evidence for their own automatic thoughts and reach new conclusions themselves. The therapist does not lecture or tell the client they are wrong; the client reformulates their own thinking. It is often paired with the downward-arrow technique and behavioural experiments.","traps":{"b":"Direct confrontation of beliefs is the antithesis of Socratic questioning, which deliberately avoids telling clients their thoughts are wrong and instead guides self-examination.","c":"Free association of childhood memories is a psychoanalytic technique, not a CBT method of thought examination.","d":"Hypnotic suggestion of new beliefs bypasses the client's own reasoning, which is exactly what guided discovery is designed to avoid."}},{"q":"Exposure and Response Prevention (ERP) is the first-line behavioural intervention for:","concept":"Exposure and Response Prevention (Edna Foa and Michael Kozak) is the first-line behavioural treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder. The client is exposed in a graded way to feared or triggering stimuli while refraining from performing the compulsive ritual, which breaks the cycle in which the compulsion is negatively reinforced by anxiety relief. Over repeated trials, anxiety habituates and the compulsion loses its grip.","traps":{"b":"Schizophrenia is treated primarily with antipsychotics and psychosocial rehabilitation, not ERP; exposure targets anxiety-driven avoidance, not psychosis.","c":"Bipolar I disorder requires mood stabilisers; ERP has no role in managing manic or depressive episodes.","d":"Antisocial personality disorder is not an anxiety disorder and does not respond to exposure; there is no compulsive fear cycle to break."}},{"q":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT), developed by Marsha Linehan, blends CBT with:","concept":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy, developed by Marsha Linehan, blends standard cognitive behavioural change strategies with acceptance-based mindfulness practices drawn from Zen Buddhism and a dialectical philosophy that holds opposing truths together (accepting the client as they are while working toward change). Its four skills modules are mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness. It is a first-line treatment for borderline personality disorder.","traps":{"b":"Psychoanalytic free association is not part of DBT; DBT is a structured, skills-based therapy, not an insight-oriented one.","c":"Pharmacological augmentation is a medication strategy, not the therapeutic tradition DBT integrates with CBT.","d":"Bowen family systems theory concerns multigenerational family dynamics and is unrelated to DBT's acceptance-and-change dialectic."}},{"q":"Rogers' three core conditions for client-centred therapy are:","concept":"Carl Rogers (1957) proposed that three therapist-provided conditions are necessary and sufficient for therapeutic change in client-centred therapy: unconditional positive regard (nonjudgmental acceptance of the client), accurate empathic understanding, and congruence (the therapist being genuine and integrated in the relationship). These humanistic conditions later shaped motivational interviewing (William Miller and Stephen Rollnick).","traps":{"a":"Confrontation, interpretation, and transference are psychoanalytic and confrontational techniques, opposite in spirit to Rogers' nondirective acceptance.","c":"Rational disputation, behavioural homework, and cognitive restructuring are cognitive-behavioural methods, not Rogerian core conditions.","d":"Free association, dream analysis, and transference are the tools of psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud), not client-centred therapy."}},{"q":"In psychoanalysis, the term 'working through' refers to:","concept":"In psychoanalysis, 'working through' (Sigmund Freud) is the extended process by which a patient repeatedly recognises, tolerates, and applies an insight or interpretation across many different situations and transferences until it produces lasting characterological change. A single correct interpretation, no matter how accurate, rarely alters personality; the repeated application is what does the work.","traps":{"b":"Telling the client the correct interpretation once is exactly what working through is NOT; insight must be applied repeatedly, not delivered in a single statement.","c":"A brief intake assessment is an initial evaluation, unrelated to the mid-to-late-phase process of consolidating insight.","d":"Termination of therapy is the ending phase; working through is an ongoing process that precedes and enables lasting change."}},{"q":"Aaron Beck's cognitive triad in depression consists of negative views of:","concept":"Aaron Beck's cognitive triad describes the three domains of negative thinking that characterise depression: a negative view of the self (worthless, inadequate), of the world or one's ongoing experience (defeating, demanding), and of the future (hopeless). Underlying maladaptive schemas activated by stress generate these biased cognitions, which are targeted through cognitive restructuring and behavioural activation.","traps":{"a":"Past, present, and future is a plausible-sounding time triad but misstates Beck; his triad is about self, world, and future, not the past.","c":"Mother, father, and self reflects a psychodynamic/object-relations framing, not Beck's cognitive model.","d":"Body, mind, and spirit is a holistic wellness phrase with no place in Beck's cognitive theory of depression."}},{"q":"Motivational Interviewing (Miller & Rollnick) is grounded in which spirit-of-MI principles?","concept":"Motivational Interviewing (William Miller and Stephen Rollnick) is grounded in an underlying 'spirit' summarised by the acronym PACE: Partnership (collaboration rather than expert-driven), Acceptance, Compassion, and Evocation (drawing out the client's own motivation rather than installing it). Its core techniques are OARS (open questions, affirmations, reflections, summaries) and eliciting change talk. It is a first-line approach in substance use.","traps":{"a":"Confrontation, persuasion, expert authority, and compliance are precisely the directive, authoritarian stance MI was designed to replace.","b":"Free association, transference, and abstinence belong to psychoanalytic tradition, not the collaborative spirit of MI.","d":"Behavioural shaping and contingency management are operant-conditioning techniques, distinct from MI's evocative, client-centred spirit."}},{"q":"Family therapy's 'enmeshment' (Minuchin) refers to:","concept":"In Salvador Minuchin's structural family therapy, 'enmeshment' describes a family with diffuse, overly permeable boundaries in which members are excessively involved in each other's lives at the cost of individual autonomy and differentiation. Its opposite is 'disengagement', marked by rigid boundaries and little emotional contact. Healthy families maintain clear but permeable boundaries with an appropriate hierarchy.","traps":{"b":"Rigid boundaries with little emotional contact describes disengagement, which is the opposite pole from enmeshment.","c":"Healthy reciprocal closeness is normal, well-bounded intimacy, whereas enmeshment specifically denotes pathological over-involvement that erodes autonomy.","d":"Pathological scapegoating of one member is a different family dynamic (identified-patient phenomenon), not the boundary concept of enmeshment."}},{"q":"Yalom's 'here-and-now' focus in group therapy refers to:","concept":"Irvin Yalom's 'here-and-now' focus treats the therapy group as a social microcosm in which members reproduce their characteristic interpersonal patterns in the room. The therapist works with those patterns as they emerge and then, through process illumination, helps members reflect on what just happened. This experiencing-then-reflecting loop is the core therapeutic engine of interpersonal group therapy.","traps":{"a":"Discussing only events outside the group ('there-and-then') is exactly what the here-and-now focus moves away from.","b":"Strictly limiting the group to didactic content makes it a class, not the experiential interpersonal work Yalom describes.","d":"Cognitive disputation of irrational beliefs is a CBT/REBT (Albert Ellis) technique, not Yalom's interpersonal process-oriented approach."}},{"q":"Systematic desensitisation (Wolpe) is based on the principle of:","concept":"Systematic desensitisation (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) rests on the principle of reciprocal inhibition: because a relaxation response is physiologically incompatible with an anxiety response, one cannot be fully anxious and fully relaxed at the same time. The client is guided up a graded fear hierarchy while maintaining relaxation, so the conditioned anxiety is progressively counter-conditioned. It has largely been superseded by in-vivo exposure.","traps":{"a":"Operant extinction involves withdrawing reinforcement of a behaviour, whereas systematic desensitisation works by counter-conditioning via incompatible relaxation, a classical-conditioning mechanism.","c":"Cognitive disputation is a cognitive-therapy technique, not the behavioural counter-conditioning that underlies Wolpe's method.","d":"The two-factor theory (Mowrer) explains how avoidance is acquired and maintained, but it is not the principle on which systematic desensitisation is built."}},{"q":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) targets which six core processes (the 'hexaflex')?","concept":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (Steven Hayes, Kelly Wilson, Kirk Strosahl) targets six interrelated core processes, arranged as the 'hexaflex': cognitive defusion, acceptance, contact with the present moment, self-as-context, values, and committed action. Together these promote psychological flexibility, the capacity to stay present and act on values even in the presence of difficult thoughts and feelings.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive restructuring, exposure, relaxation, homework, role-play, and contingency management are traditional CBT/behavioural techniques; ACT deliberately does NOT try to restructure thoughts.","c":"Free association, dream analysis, and transference are psychoanalytic methods, entirely outside the ACT model.","d":"Mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness are the four DBT (Marsha Linehan) skills modules, not the ACT hexaflex."}},{"q":"Interpersonal Therapy (IPT) for depression typically organises treatment around which problem areas?","concept":"Interpersonal Therapy (Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman) is a time-limited treatment (usually 12 to 16 sessions) that links depressive symptoms to the client's current interpersonal context. It organises treatment around one of four problem areas: grief (complicated bereavement), interpersonal role disputes, role transitions, and interpersonal deficits. It does not target unconscious conflict or, primarily, cognitive distortions.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive distortions and dysfunctional schemas are the targets of cognitive therapy (Beck), not the interpersonal problem areas of IPT.","b":"Trauma reprocessing through bilateral stimulation describes EMDR (Francine Shapiro), an unrelated trauma therapy.","c":"Free association and transference interpretation are psychoanalytic techniques; IPT focuses on present-day relationships, not unconscious conflict."}},{"q":"The 'empty chair' technique is most closely associated with which therapy?","concept":"The 'empty chair' technique belongs to Gestalt therapy, developed by Fritz Perls. The client speaks to an imagined person or a disowned part of the self placed in an empty chair, often switching seats to voice both sides, in order to integrate polarities and complete 'unfinished business'. It is an experiential, present-focused method.","traps":{"b":"Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (Albert Ellis) uses the ABC model and disputation of irrational beliefs, not chair work.","c":"Person-centred therapy (Carl Rogers) relies on empathic reflection and the core conditions, not enactment techniques.","d":"Family systems therapy (Murray Bowen) uses genograms and differentiation of self, not the empty chair."}},{"q":"Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing (EMDR) was developed by:","concept":"Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing was developed by Francine Shapiro in the late 1980s. It uses an eight-phase protocol in which the client recalls distressing memories while engaging in bilateral stimulation (side-to-side eye movements, tapping, or tones). It is endorsed by the World Health Organization and the American Psychological Association for post-traumatic stress disorder, though its mechanism remains debated.","traps":{"a":"Edna Foa developed Prolonged Exposure for PTSD and co-developed ERP for OCD, not EMDR.","b":"Bessel van der Kolk is a prominent trauma researcher and author, but he did not develop EMDR.","c":"Pat Ogden founded Sensorimotor Psychotherapy, a body-oriented trauma approach, not EMDR."}},{"q":"A Type II error occurs when:","concept":"In null-hypothesis significance testing, a Type II error (beta) occurs when the null hypothesis is not rejected even though it is actually false, that is, a real effect is missed (a false negative). This contrasts with a Type I error (alpha), rejecting a true null (false positive). Statistical power equals 1 minus beta, the probability of correctly detecting a true effect, conventionally targeted at 0.80.","traps":{"a":"Rejecting a true null hypothesis is a Type I error (false positive), the opposite of a Type II error.","b":"A confidence interval being too wide relates to precision and sample size, not to the definition of a Type II error.","d":"A large sample size actually reduces the chance of a Type II error by increasing power, so it cannot define one."}},{"q":"Which test is appropriate for comparing the means of three or more independent groups on a continuous outcome with normal distribution?","concept":"One-way analysis of variance (ANOVA) is the appropriate parametric test for comparing the means of three or more independent groups on a continuous, normally distributed outcome. It partitions total variance into between-group and within-group components and tests whether group means differ more than expected by chance. If normality is violated, the Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric alternative.","traps":{"a":"A paired t-test compares two related (matched or repeated) measurements, not three or more independent groups.","b":"The chi-square test analyses associations between categorical variables, not means of a continuous outcome.","d":"The Mann-Whitney U test is a non-parametric test for two independent groups, not three or more, and is not the normal-distribution parametric choice."}},{"q":"An odds ratio of 1.0 means:","concept":"The odds ratio compares the odds of an outcome in an exposed group to the odds in an unexposed group. An odds ratio of exactly 1.0 means the odds are equal in the two groups, indicating no association between exposure and outcome. Values above 1.0 suggest a positive (harmful) association and values below 1.0 suggest a protective effect; significance is judged by whether the 95% confidence interval excludes 1.","traps":{"a":"A strong positive association corresponds to an odds ratio well above 1.0, not equal to 1.0.","b":"A strong protective effect corresponds to an odds ratio well below 1.0 (approaching 0), not 1.0.","d":"Perfect correlation is a property of correlation coefficients (r = 1), a different statistic; an odds ratio of 1 actually means no association at all."}},{"q":"Cohen's d of approximately 0.5 indicates:","concept":"Cohen's d expresses the standardised difference between two means, calculated as the mean difference divided by the pooled standard deviation. By Jacob Cohen's conventional benchmarks, d around 0.2 is a small effect, around 0.5 is a medium effect, and around 0.8 is a large effect. A d of approximately 0.5 therefore indicates a medium effect.","traps":{"b":"A small effect corresponds to d around 0.2, not 0.5.","c":"A large effect corresponds to d around 0.8 or higher, well above 0.5.","d":"No effect would be d near 0; a d of 0.5 reflects a moderate, non-trivial difference between means."}},{"q":"Stratified random sampling is preferred over simple random sampling when:","concept":"Stratified random sampling divides the population into homogeneous subgroups (strata) defined by an important characteristic, then draws a random sample from within each stratum. It is preferred over simple random sampling when the researcher wants to guarantee adequate representation of key subgroups and to reduce sampling variance when those subgroups differ on the outcome of interest.","traps":{"a":"If the population is homogeneous there is little to gain from stratifying; simple random sampling suffices, so homogeneity is not the reason to stratify.","b":"Stratification is about representativeness and precision, not about minimising sample size; it does not inherently reduce the number needed.","d":"When no sampling frame exists, methods like cluster or snowball sampling are used; stratified sampling actually requires knowing subgroup membership."}},{"q":"A scale with high internal consistency but low criterion validity:","concept":"Reliability and validity are distinct. A scale with high internal consistency is reliable, meaning it measures something consistently, but if it has low criterion validity it is not measuring the intended construct. Thus a test can be reliable but not valid (consistently measuring the wrong thing). The reverse is not possible: validity presupposes reliability, since you cannot validly measure what you cannot measure consistently.","traps":{"b":"Being both reliable and valid would require high criterion validity, which the stem explicitly denies.","c":"Valid but not reliable is logically impossible, because validity requires some level of reliability as a precondition.","d":"The scale is described as internally consistent, so it is reliable; 'neither reliable nor valid' contradicts the given high internal consistency."}},{"q":"Intent-to-treat analysis means that:","concept":"Intent-to-treat analysis analyses every randomised participant in the group to which they were originally assigned, regardless of whether they completed the intervention, adhered to it, or crossed over. This preserves the balance created by randomisation and yields a conservative, real-world estimate of effectiveness. Per-protocol analysis, by contrast, includes only completers and risks selection bias.","traps":{"a":"Analysing only those who completed as planned is per-protocol analysis, which breaks randomisation, the opposite of ITT.","b":"Excluding non-completers to reduce noise reintroduces selection bias and is precisely what ITT avoids.","d":"Limiting analysis to per-protocol completers is a distinct, non-ITT approach that estimates efficacy under ideal adherence, not real-world effectiveness."}},{"q":"A 95% confidence interval for a mean difference that includes zero indicates that:","concept":"A 95% confidence interval for a mean difference that includes zero means the data are compatible with no true difference, so the null hypothesis cannot be rejected at the two-sided 0.05 significance level; the result is not statistically significant. The confidence interval and the p-value give equivalent decisions here.","traps":{"a":"Including zero does not mean the difference is exactly zero; the point estimate may be nonzero, but the range of plausible values simply cannot exclude zero.","c":"Low power can produce a wide interval that includes zero, but including zero by itself indicates non-significance, not specifically insufficient power.","d":"A large sample tends to narrow the interval, making it less likely to include zero, so it does not explain an interval that spans zero."}},{"q":"A non-parametric alternative to the independent-samples t-test is:","concept":"The Mann-Whitney U test (also called the Wilcoxon rank-sum test) is the non-parametric alternative to the independent-samples t-test, used to compare two independent groups when the assumptions of the parametric test are not met. It ranks all observations and compares rank sums between groups rather than comparing means directly.","traps":{"a":"The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is for two RELATED (paired) samples, the non-parametric analogue of the paired t-test, not the independent-samples t-test.","c":"The Friedman test is for three or more related samples, not two independent groups.","d":"The McNemar test is for paired dichotomous (categorical) data, not for comparing two independent continuous distributions."}},{"q":"Number Needed to Treat (NNT) is calculated as:","concept":"Number Needed to Treat is the number of patients who must receive the treatment for one additional beneficial outcome to occur. It is calculated as 1 divided by the Absolute Risk Reduction, where ARR is the difference in event rates between control and treatment groups. A smaller NNT indicates a more efficient treatment. The parallel harm measure is Number Needed to Harm, equal to 1 divided by the Absolute Risk Increase.","traps":{"a":"Relative risk reduction times 100 gives a percentage, not the count of patients needed to treat; NNT uses the ABSOLUTE risk reduction.","c":"1 divided by Relative Risk is not a defined outcome measure; NNT uses absolute, not relative, risk.","d":"Odds Ratio times 100 has no meaningful interpretation as NNT; the odds ratio is a measure of association, not a treatment-efficiency count."}},{"q":"The 'Bonferroni correction' is most appropriate when:","concept":"The Bonferroni correction controls the family-wise Type I error rate when many comparisons are made simultaneously, by dividing the significance threshold alpha by the number of comparisons (for example 0.05 divided by 10 equals 0.005). It is most appropriate when conducting many independent pairwise comparisons and wanting to keep the overall chance of any false positive at the nominal level. It is conservative, especially when comparisons are correlated.","traps":{"a":"A single planned comparison involves no multiplicity, so no correction is needed; Bonferroni addresses multiple tests.","b":"Reporting effect sizes concerns magnitude of effect, not control of Type I error across multiple tests.","c":"Whether a test is non-parametric is irrelevant to the need for a multiple-comparison correction; Bonferroni applies to the number of tests, not their parametric form."}},{"q":"Choose the option that best replaces the underlined portion: 'Neither the manager nor the staff <u>were</u> aware of the new policy.'","concept":"With correlative conjunctions 'neither ... nor' (and 'either ... or'), the verb follows the rule of proximity: it agrees with the subject nearer to it. Here the nearer subject is 'staff', treated as a singular collective noun taking a singular verb, so the correct form is 'was'. This is a subject-verb agreement point tested constantly in language sections.","traps":{"a":"'Were' is the seed error itself: it wrongly treats the whole 'neither the manager nor the staff' construction as plural instead of agreeing with the nearer singular subject.","b":"'Is' is singular but present tense, which clashes with the past-tense narrative frame of the sentence about awareness of a policy.","c":"'Are' is both plural and present tense, doubly wrong: it neither agrees with the singular nearer subject nor matches the past-tense context."}},{"q":"Identify the sentence with correct punctuation:","concept":"'However' used as a conjunctive adverb at the start of a clause takes a comma after it, and two independent clauses joined without a coordinating conjunction (and, but, or) must be separated by a semicolon, not a comma. Option (a) applies both rules: 'However,' then a semicolon between the two complete clauses. A comma alone between two independent clauses is the classic comma-splice error.","traps":{"b":"This omits the comma after 'However' and joins two independent clauses with only a comma, committing a comma splice.","c":"A semicolon after 'However' is wrong because a semicolon must separate two independent clauses, not a single introductory adverb from its clause.","d":"The stray comma after 'results' wrongly splits the subject from its verb 'were', and the clauses are still comma-spliced."}},{"q":"Choose the most appropriate word: 'The findings of the study are not <u>____</u> to the broader population because the sample was small and self-selected.'","concept":"After a linking verb like 'are not', a predicate adjective is needed to describe the subject 'findings'. The adjective 'generalisable' (capable of being generalised) fits: the findings are not generalisable. Recognising the part of speech required by the sentence frame is the key skill here.","traps":{"a":"'Generalisation' is a noun naming the act or product of generalising, so it cannot serve as the adjective describing 'findings' after 'are not'.","c":"'Generality' is an abstract noun (the quality of being general), again a noun where an adjective is required.","d":"'Generally' is an adverb, which would modify a verb or adjective, not function as the predicate describing the findings."}},{"q":"Which sentence uses the conditional correctly?","concept":"The third conditional expresses an unreal or hypothetical situation in the past and follows the pattern: If + past perfect (had + past participle), main clause with would have + past participle. Thus 'If I had known, I would have called you.' A very common error is putting 'would have' in the if-clause, which is nonstandard in formal English.","traps":{"a":"This inserts 'would have' into the if-clause ('If I would have known'), the classic conditional error; the if-clause should take the past perfect 'had known'.","b":"'If I knew earlier' mixes tenses and 'I would called' drops the required 'have', so neither clause matches any correct conditional pattern.","c":"'If I would knew' is ungrammatical (would cannot precede a past-tense verb) and 'I called you' lacks the 'would have' result form."}},{"q":"Choose the word closest in meaning to 'ubiquitous':","concept":"'Ubiquitous' means present or found everywhere at the same time. Its closest synonym is 'omnipresent', which literally means present in all places. Vocabulary questions reward matching the core sense (everywhere-ness), not a loosely associated feature.","traps":{"b":"'Rare' means seldom found or occurring, the near-opposite of being everywhere.","c":"'Obscure' means unclear or little-known, which concerns visibility or fame, not spatial pervasiveness.","d":"'Uniform' means consistent or unvarying in form; it shares a sense of sameness but not the 'everywhere' meaning of ubiquitous."}},{"q":"Identify the error: 'The patient, along with her two children, <u>were</u> waiting in the consulting room.'","concept":"Parenthetical phrases introduced by 'along with', 'as well as', 'in addition to', or 'together with' do not change the grammatical number of the subject. The true subject is 'the patient' (singular), so the verb should be 'was waiting'. Only a compound subject joined by 'and' would make the verb plural.","traps":{"a":"'No error' misses the agreement mistake: the singular subject 'the patient' cannot take the plural verb 'were'.","c":"Replacing 'with' with 'and' would create a genuine compound plural subject, but that rewrites the sentence rather than correcting the actual verb-agreement error.","d":"Replacing 'her' with 'their' introduces a pronoun disagreement (the children belong to the singular patient) and does not fix the verb."}},{"q":"Choose the option closest in meaning to 'salient':","concept":"'Salient' means standing out, most noticeable, or conspicuous, so its closest synonym is 'prominent'. The word derives from Latin 'salire', to leap, capturing the idea of a feature that leaps out at the observer.","traps":{"a":"'Trivial' means of little importance, the opposite of a feature that stands out.","c":"'Concealed' means hidden from view, directly contrary to being conspicuous.","d":"'Uncertain' means doubtful or not clearly established, which describes confidence, not conspicuousness."}},{"q":"Select the correctly punctuated sentence:","concept":"When quoting a direct question, the quoted sentence begins with a capital letter and the question mark goes inside the closing quotation mark because the question belongs to the quoted material. A comma sets off the reporting tag ('asked,') before the opening quote. Option (b) follows all three conventions.","traps":{"a":"The quoted question is not capitalised and the question mark is placed outside the closing quote, misattributing the question to the whole sentence rather than the quoted words.","c":"Ending the quoted direct question with a full stop instead of a question mark misrepresents the sentence as a statement.","d":"This omits the comma after 'asked' and fails to capitalise the start of the quoted question."}},{"q":"Reading comprehension: 'Although early studies suggested that lithium was uniformly effective, more recent meta-analyses have qualified this view: lithium reduces suicide and prevents relapse but is less reliable for acute mania than second-generation antipsychotics.' The passage primarily implies that:","concept":"The passage tempers early enthusiasm about lithium without dismissing it: it credits lithium with reducing suicide and preventing relapse while noting it is less reliable than second-generation antipsychotics for acute mania. The primary implication is therefore that lithium remains valuable but is not the optimal agent for every phase of bipolar disorder. Reading comprehension rewards the balanced inference the text actually supports, not an extreme restatement.","traps":{"a":"The passage explicitly names ongoing benefits (suicide reduction, relapse prevention), so claiming lithium has no role overstates the qualification.","b":"The text qualifies the early studies' conclusion, not their methodology; it never says the early studies were flawed.","c":"It says SGAs are more reliable only for acute mania, which is far from replacing lithium completely across all phases."}},{"q":"Choose the option that best replaces the underlined portion to be grammatically correct: 'Each of the participants <u>have</u> been informed about the risks.'","concept":"'Each' is a singular indefinite pronoun and always takes a singular verb, regardless of an intervening prepositional phrase like 'of the participants'. So the correct form is 'Each of the participants has been informed'. The same singular rule governs 'every', 'either', and 'neither'.","traps":{"a":"'Have' is plural and is drawn incorrectly to the nearer plural noun 'participants' rather than to the singular subject 'Each'.","c":"'Had been' shifts to the past perfect tense, changing the meaning and time frame instead of simply correcting the number agreement.","d":"'Is' is singular but does not fit the passive-perfect construction '... informed about the risks', which requires the auxiliary 'has been'."}}],"met-mock-4":[{"q":"GABA-A receptors differ from GABA-B receptors primarily in that GABA-A:","concept":"GABA is the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter and acts through two receptor families. GABA-A receptors are ionotropic, ligand-gated chloride channels: when GABA binds, Cl- flows in, hyperpolarizing the cell and producing a fast inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP). This is why option d is correct, and it explains why benzodiazepines, barbiturates, and alcohol all act here as positive modulators. GABA-B, by contrast, is metabotropic and G-protein coupled, producing slow inhibition via K+ efflux and Ca2+ channel closure.","traps":{"a":"This describes an excitatory ionotropic receptor like the nicotinic or AMPA receptor; GABA-A is inhibitory and passes Cl-, not Na+.","b":"This actually describes the GABA-B receptor (metabotropic, G-protein coupled, acting via K+ and Ca2+ channels), not GABA-A.","c":"GABA-A is ligand-gated, opened by GABA binding, not voltage-gated and never independent of its neurotransmitter."}},{"q":"The NMDA glutamate receptor is unique among ionotropic receptors because:","concept":"The NMDA glutamate receptor is a 'coincidence detector,' which makes it unique among ionotropic receptors. At resting potential its pore is plugged by an Mg2+ ion, so glutamate binding alone cannot open it; the cell must also be depolarized (usually by AMPA receptors) to expel the Mg2+ block. Only when both conditions coincide does the channel open and admit Ca2+, the trigger for long-term potentiation (Timothy Bliss and Terje Lømo, 1973) and synaptic plasticity. This dual requirement is why option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"The opposite is true: NMDA opens only when the membrane is depolarized, which relieves the Mg2+ block, not at hyperpolarized potentials.","b":"Cl- permeability characterizes GABA-A and glycine receptors; NMDA is a cation channel notable for its high Ca2+ permeability.","d":"NMDA is an ionotropic ligand-gated channel; the metabotropic glutamate receptors (mGluRs) are the G-protein coupled family."}},{"q":"Bilateral medial temporal lobectomy (as in patient H.M.) produces:","concept":"Patient H.M. (Henry Molaison), described by William Scoville and Brenda Milner in 1957, had both medial temporal lobes including the hippocampi removed to treat epilepsy. The result was a dense anterograde amnesia for declarative memory (both episodic and semantic), meaning he could not form new conscious memories, while his procedural learning stayed intact (he improved on the mirror-tracing task without recalling having practiced). This dissociation established the hippocampus as critical for consolidating declarative memory, making option d correct.","traps":{"a":"H.M.'s motor coordination was intact; medial temporal structures serve memory, not motor control, which depends on the cerebellum and basal ganglia.","b":"This reverses the deficit: H.M. had profound trouble with new learning (anterograde), not primarily loss of old memories, and retrograde loss was limited.","c":"The amnesia spanned all declarative memory, both episodic and semantic; it was not selective for semantics alone."}},{"q":"The amygdala is most directly implicated in:","concept":"The amygdala, especially its central and basolateral nuclei, is the brain's hub for emotional salience, fear conditioning, and threat detection, making option a correct. Joseph LeDoux described a fast 'low road' running from the thalamus directly to the amygdala, bypassing the cortex to trigger rapid fear responses. Bilateral amygdala damage produces Kluver-Bucy syndrome (placidity, hyperorality, hypersexuality, visual agnosia).","traps":{"b":"Motor sequence learning depends on the basal ganglia and cerebellum, not the amygdala.","c":"Conscious recall of facts is a declarative memory function tied to the hippocampus and neocortex, not emotional processing.","d":"Circadian rhythm is generated by the suprachiasmatic nucleus of the hypothalamus, unrelated to amygdala function."}},{"q":"The Papez circuit includes which sequence?","concept":"James Papez (1937) proposed a closed loop as the anatomical substrate of emotion, now called the Papez circuit. The correct sequence runs hippocampus to fornix to mammillary bodies to anterior thalamic nuclei (via the mammillothalamic tract) to cingulate gyrus and back to the hippocampus, making option a correct. Damage to the mammillary bodies in Korsakoff's syndrome disrupts this circuit and impairs memory.","traps":{"b":"This is the basal ganglia (cortico-striato-pallido-thalamo-cortical) motor loop, not the limbic Papez emotion circuit.","c":"This is the visual pathway (retina to lateral geniculate nucleus to primary and secondary visual cortex), unrelated to emotion.","d":"This is a fabricated sequence; the cerebellum is not part of the Papez circuit, and the true loop passes through the mammillary bodies and anterior thalamus."}},{"q":"Wernicke's area is classically located in:","concept":"Wernicke's area sits in the posterior superior temporal gyrus (Brodmann area 22) of the dominant hemisphere, usually the left, making option c correct. It underlies language comprehension, so damage produces a fluent aphasia with poor understanding and meaningless speech. Broca's area, by contrast, lies in the inferior frontal gyrus and governs speech production.","traps":{"a":"The occipital pole houses primary visual cortex, not a language region.","b":"The inferior frontal gyrus is Broca's area (motor speech), not Wernicke's area.","d":"The postcentral gyrus is the primary somatosensory cortex, unrelated to language comprehension."}},{"q":"The key feature distinguishing Broca's aphasia from Wernicke's aphasia is that Broca's aphasia involves:","concept":"Broca's aphasia results from damage to the inferior frontal gyrus and is defined by effortful, non-fluent, telegraphic speech with relatively preserved comprehension and impaired repetition; patients are often aware and frustrated. This makes option d correct. It contrasts sharply with Wernicke's aphasia, where speech is fluent but empty and comprehension is lost.","traps":{"a":"Fluent speech with intact comprehension describes normal or anomic speech, not Broca's aphasia, which is non-fluent.","b":"Loss of comprehension with fluent, meaningless speech describes Wernicke's aphasia, the opposite condition.","c":"Selective alexia (impaired reading) with no other deficit is a specific disconnection syndrome, not the picture of Broca's aphasia."}},{"q":"Which function is most reliably lateralised to the right (non-dominant) hemisphere in right-handers?","concept":"In right-handers the left hemisphere is dominant for language (in roughly 95%), so the right hemisphere specializes in visuospatial processing and prosody, making option b correct. The right hemisphere also handles face recognition (fusiform face area) and holistic, gestalt perception, while the left handles analytic and sequential processing.","traps":{"a":"Phonological processing is a left-hemisphere language function.","c":"Grammatical syntax is lateralized to the left hemisphere, alongside other core language operations.","d":"Verbal memory is predominantly a left-hemisphere (and left medial temporal) function; the right specializes in visuospatial memory."}},{"q":"Damage to the orbitofrontal cortex is most likely to produce:","concept":"The orbitofrontal cortex regulates emotion, reward valuation, and social judgment, so its damage produces disinhibition, impulsivity, and socially inappropriate behavior, making option d correct. The classic illustration is Phineas Gage (case reported by John Martyn Harlow, 1848), whose personality changed dramatically after a tamping iron destroyed his orbitofrontal region. Such patients also fail the Iowa Gambling Task, reflecting impaired decision-making.","traps":{"a":"Pure aphasia follows damage to perisylvian language areas (Broca's or Wernicke's), not the orbitofrontal cortex.","b":"Cortical blindness results from bilateral occipital (visual cortex) damage.","c":"Hemiplegia follows damage to the primary motor cortex or its descending corticospinal tracts, not the orbitofrontal region."}},{"q":"Dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (DLPFC) damage most specifically impairs:","concept":"The dorsolateral prefrontal cortex (Brodmann areas 9 and 46) is the seat of working memory and executive function, so its damage specifically impairs planning, set-shifting, and response inhibition, making option b correct. Such patients perform poorly on the Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (David Grant and Esta Berg, 1948), the Stroop task, and n-back tasks. This contrasts with the orbitofrontal cortex (reward and impulse) and the anterior cingulate (error and conflict monitoring).","traps":{"a":"Color perception depends on the ventral occipitotemporal visual cortex (area V4), not the prefrontal cortex.","c":"Basic motor reflexes are mediated by the spinal cord and brainstem, not the DLPFC.","d":"Long-term declarative memory depends on the hippocampus and medial temporal lobe, not on dorsolateral prefrontal working memory circuits."}},{"q":"Slow-wave (deep) sleep is characterised on EEG by:","concept":"Slow-wave sleep (stage N3, the deepest non-REM stage) is defined on EEG by high-amplitude, low-frequency delta waves (0.5 to 4 Hz), making option b correct. This is the stage of greatest restorative and consolidation activity. Lighter stage N2 shows sleep spindles and K-complexes, while REM shows low-voltage mixed-frequency activity.","traps":{"a":"Sawtooth waves accompanied by rapid eye movements characterize REM sleep, not slow-wave sleep.","c":"Beta waves signal alert wakefulness and active concentration, the opposite of deep sleep.","d":"Sleep spindles and K-complexes are the hallmark of stage N2, not the delta-dominated N3 slow-wave sleep."}},{"q":"REM sleep behavior disorder (RBD) is characterised by:","concept":"REM sleep behavior disorder (RBD) is a failure of the normal muscle atonia (paralysis) of REM sleep, so the person physically enacts dream content by kicking, punching, or vocalizing, making option b correct. It is strongly linked to the alpha-synucleinopathies (Parkinson's disease, dementia with Lewy bodies, multiple system atrophy) and often predates motor symptoms by years. It is typically treated with melatonin or clonazepam.","traps":{"a":"RBD patients do enter REM normally; the problem is loss of atonia during REM, not absence of REM.","c":"Bedwetting during N3 is sleep enuresis, a different non-REM parasomnia.","d":"Excessive daytime sleepiness without nocturnal enactment points to narcolepsy or hypersomnia, not RBD."}},{"q":"SSRIs typically take 2-4 weeks for clinical antidepressant effect despite rapid synaptic serotonin elevation. The most-supported explanation is:","concept":"Although SSRIs raise synaptic serotonin within hours, the clinical antidepressant effect takes 2 to 4 weeks because of downstream adaptive changes, making option b correct. Initially, presynaptic 5-HT1A autoreceptors sense the extra serotonin and suppress firing; over weeks these autoreceptors desensitize, firing normalizes, and slower processes (BDNF upregulation, hippocampal neurogenesis, dendritic remodeling) build up in parallel with symptom improvement.","traps":{"a":"Liver enzyme induction alters drug metabolism and clearance but does not explain the therapeutic lag.","c":"SSRIs cross the blood-brain barrier rapidly, which is why synaptic serotonin rises almost immediately.","d":"Steady-state plasma levels are reached within days, far faster than the several-week clinical delay."}},{"q":"Lithium is classically associated with which narrow therapeutic concern?","concept":"Lithium's defining clinical concern is its narrow therapeutic index: the effective range (about 0.6 to 1.2 mEq/L) sits dangerously close to toxic levels, making option a correct. Toxicity brings tremor, ataxia, and nephrogenic diabetes insipidus, and levels climb with dehydration, NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, and thiazide diuretics. Routine monitoring of serum lithium, renal function, and thyroid function is therefore standard.","traps":{"b":"Constipation is not lithium's dose-limiting toxicity; the concern is a narrow window where neurologic and renal toxicity emerge.","c":"Lithium is renally cleared and unsafe in renal impairment without monitoring, since impaired excretion raises levels toward toxicity.","d":"Lithium is renally cleared and does not cause acute fulminant liver failure; hepatotoxicity is not its characteristic hazard."}},{"q":"Benzodiazepines and barbiturates both potentiate GABA-A receptor activity but differ in that:","concept":"Benzodiazepines and barbiturates are both positive modulators of the GABA-A receptor but act by different mechanisms, making option a correct. Benzodiazepines increase the frequency of chloride channel opening and require GABA to be present, giving a ceiling effect that makes them relatively safe in overdose alone. Barbiturates increase the duration of channel opening and at high doses directly open the channel without GABA, so they have no ceiling and can cause fatal respiratory depression.","traps":{"b":"Barbiturates act on GABA-A, not GABA-B; baclofen is the GABA-B agonist.","c":"Benzodiazepines work at the GABA-A receptor, not at NMDA glutamate receptors.","d":"This reverses the two mechanisms: benzodiazepines increase frequency of opening while barbiturates increase duration."}},{"q":"Long-term potentiation (LTP) in the hippocampus most directly involves:","concept":"Long-term potentiation (LTP), first described by Timothy Bliss and Terje Lomo in 1973, is the leading cellular model of memory. High-frequency stimulation depolarizes the postsynaptic neuron, relieving the NMDA receptor's Mg2+ block so Ca2+ flows in; this Ca2+ activates CaMKII, which drives insertion of more AMPA receptors into the membrane, strengthening the synapse. This calcium-triggered AMPA insertion is why option b is correct.","traps":{"a":"LTP is associated with increased dendritic spine density and growth, not a decrease.","c":"LTP strengthens transmission and does not deplete glutamate stores.","d":"LTP builds synaptic connections; apoptosis (programmed cell death) is a destructive process unrelated to potentiation."}},{"q":"Which hypothalamic nucleus is most directly involved in oxytocin and ADH synthesis?","concept":"Oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone (ADH, vasopressin) are synthesized by magnocellular neurons of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus, making option c correct. Their axons travel down to the posterior pituitary, which stores and releases these hormones into the bloodstream. Parvocellular neurons of the paraventricular nucleus additionally make CRH to drive the anterior pituitary.","traps":{"a":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus is the master circadian clock, not a site of oxytocin or ADH synthesis.","b":"The mammillary bodies function in memory (Papez circuit), not hormone synthesis.","d":"The arcuate nucleus regulates appetite and releases hormones like GnRH and dopamine, not oxytocin or ADH."}},{"q":"Which neurotransmitter is synthesised from the amino acid tryptophan?","concept":"Serotonin (5-HT) is synthesized from the amino acid tryptophan, making option d correct. The pathway runs tryptophan to 5-HTP by tryptophan hydroxylase (the rate-limiting step) and then to serotonin by aromatic L-amino acid decarboxylase. Knowing precursor pathways is a common exam point, since each transmitter has a distinct starting amino acid.","traps":{"a":"Acetylcholine is made from choline and acetyl-CoA by choline acetyltransferase, not from an amino acid precursor like tryptophan.","b":"Dopamine is synthesized from tyrosine (via L-DOPA), not tryptophan.","c":"GABA is synthesized from glutamate by glutamic acid decarboxylase, not from tryptophan."}},{"q":"Erikson's first psychosocial stage (0-18 months) involves the conflict of:","concept":"Erik Erikson's first psychosocial stage, spanning roughly birth to 18 months, is Trust versus Mistrust, making option a correct. Consistent, responsive caregiving lets the infant develop basic trust in the world; neglect breeds mistrust. The remaining seven stages unfold across the lifespan, each defined by its own central conflict.","traps":{"b":"Initiative versus Guilt is Erikson's third stage (roughly ages 3 to 6), not the first.","c":"Industry versus Inferiority is the fourth stage (roughly ages 6 to 12).","d":"Autonomy versus Shame and Doubt is the second stage (roughly 18 months to 3 years), the one that follows Trust versus Mistrust."}},{"q":"In Piaget's theory, object permanence is acquired during the:","concept":"In Jean Piaget's theory, object permanence (understanding that an object still exists when it cannot be seen) is acquired during the sensorimotor stage, around 8 to 12 months, making option c correct. Its emergence is imperfect at first, shown by the A-not-B error, and is fully mastered by about 18 to 24 months. It is a signature achievement of infancy.","traps":{"a":"The concrete operational stage (roughly ages 7 to 11) is when conservation and logical operations appear, long after object permanence.","b":"The formal operational stage (from about age 11) brings abstract and hypothetical reasoning, not object permanence.","d":"Object permanence develops in infancy, not at age 4; the preoperational stage is marked by egocentrism and symbolic play."}},{"q":"Vygotsky's concept of the Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) refers to:","concept":"Lev Vygotsky's Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) is the gap between what a child can do independently and what the child can achieve with guidance from a more knowledgeable other, making option d correct. It implies that teaching should target this zone, and it underlies the practice of scaffolding (a term later developed by Wood, Bruner, and Ross in 1976). It highlights the social nature of learning.","traps":{"a":"The influential peer group relates to socialization theories, not Vygotsky's ZPD.","b":"The window for language acquisition is the critical period concept (Lenneberg), not the ZPD.","c":"The ZPD is a psychological and educational construct about potential learning, not a neural region."}},{"q":"Kohlberg's pre-conventional level of moral reasoning is characterised by:","concept":"Lawrence Kohlberg's pre-conventional level of moral reasoning bases judgments on external consequences, specifically avoiding punishment (Stage 1) and gaining rewards or instrumental exchange (Stage 2), making option c correct. It is the dominant mode in young children. The later conventional and post-conventional levels move toward social approval, law, and abstract ethical principles.","traps":{"a":"Conformity to peer and social norms is Stage 3 (good boy/good girl) of the conventional level, not pre-conventional.","b":"Maintaining social order and law is Stage 4 (law and order) of the conventional level.","d":"Universal ethical principles define Stage 6 of the post-conventional level, the highest and most abstract stage."}},{"q":"In Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant who shows little distress on separation, ignores the caregiver on reunion, and treats stranger and caregiver similarly is classified as:","concept":"In Mary Ainsworth's Strange Situation, an infant who shows little distress at separation, ignores or avoids the caregiver at reunion, and treats stranger and caregiver alike is classified as insecure-avoidant (Type A), making option b correct. This pattern is thought to reflect a caregiver who is consistently unresponsive or rejecting. It contrasts with the secure Type B infant, who protests but is readily comforted.","traps":{"a":"Disorganized (Type D), described by Mary Main, shows contradictory, freezing, or fearful behavior, often linked to maltreatment, not calm avoidance.","c":"Insecure-resistant/ambivalent (Type C) infants show intense distress and cannot be soothed, seeking then resisting contact, the opposite of the avoidant pattern.","d":"Secure (Type B) infants protest separation but actively seek and accept comfort on reunion, unlike the indifferent avoidant infant."}},{"q":"Bowlby's concept of the internal working model refers to:","concept":"John Bowlby's internal working model is the mental representation a child builds of the self, attachment figures, and relationships, formed in infancy from repeated interactions, making option c correct. It encodes whether the self is lovable, whether caregivers are available, and whether relationships are predictable, and it serves as the template guiding future attachment behavior. This is Bowlby's proposed mechanism for the continuity of attachment across the lifespan.","traps":{"a":"A cognitive map of physical space is a spatial-memory concept (Tolman), not Bowlby's relational model.","b":"The neural substrate of language acquisition is unrelated to attachment representations.","d":"Object permanence is Piaget's cognitive concept, distinct from Bowlby's emotional working model of relationships."}},{"q":"Baumrind's authoritative parenting style is characterised by:","concept":"Diana Baumrind's authoritative parenting style combines high demandingness with high responsiveness (warmth), making option b correct. These parents set clear standards and enforce them while remaining warm and communicative, and this style is associated with the best child outcomes. Maccoby and Martin later completed the 2x2 model by adding the neglectful (uninvolved) cell.","traps":{"a":"Low demandingness with high responsiveness is the permissive (indulgent) style, associated with impulsive children.","c":"Low demandingness with low responsiveness is the neglectful (uninvolved) style, linked to the poorest outcomes.","d":"High demandingness with low responsiveness is the authoritarian style, associated with withdrawn or hostile children."}},{"q":"The concept of 'goodness of fit' in temperament research (Thomas & Chess) refers to:","concept":"In the temperament research of Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess (the New York Longitudinal Study), 'goodness of fit' refers to the match between a child's temperament and the demands and expectations of the environment or caregiver, making option a correct. Their key insight was that outcomes depend not on temperament alone (easy, difficult, slow-to-warm-up) but on how well it fits the surrounding environment. A difficult child in a patient, structured home may thrive.","traps":{"b":"The IQ difference between identical twins concerns heritability research, not the temperament-environment fit.","c":"The number of siblings is a family structure variable, unrelated to goodness of fit.","d":"Genetic concordance between parent and child is a behavior-genetics measure, not the temperament-environment match Thomas and Chess described."}},{"q":"Harry Harlow's rhesus monkey experiments demonstrated that:","concept":"Harry Harlow's rhesus monkey experiments (1958) showed that contact comfort, not feeding, is the primary basis of attachment, making option b correct. Infant monkeys preferred a soft cloth surrogate 'mother' over a wire one that dispensed milk, clinging to the cloth figure especially when frightened. This directly challenged the behaviorist, feeding-based (cupboard love) account of attachment.","traps":{"a":"Rapid, time-limited imprinting in the first hours characterizes Lorenz's precocial birds, not Harlow's primate work.","c":"Harlow's work showed the opposite: maternal deprivation produced severe, lasting behavioral disturbance in the monkeys.","d":"The whole point of Harlow's findings was that operant conditioning (B. F. Skinner) and feeding do not fully explain attachment."}},{"q":"Lorenz's concept of imprinting is best demonstrated by:","concept":"Konrad Lorenz's concept of imprinting is best shown by greylag goslings forming a rapid, durable following response to the first moving object they encounter shortly after hatching, making option b correct. This bonding occurs only within a narrow sensitive (critical) period, roughly 13 to 16 hours in geese, and established the idea of critical periods in development. The response is fast, lasting, and relatively general to the stimulus.","traps":{"a":"Habituation is a decline in response to repeated stimuli, a form of non-associative learning, not imprinting.","c":"A 5-year-old's playmate preference reflects social development, not the hatchling imprinting Lorenz studied.","d":"Reflexive neonatal smiling is an innate reflex, not the learned attachment-during-a-critical-period seen in imprinting."}},{"q":"Theory of Mind, classically tested with the false-belief task (Sally-Anne), typically emerges around:","concept":"Theory of Mind, the ability to attribute mental states (including false beliefs) to others, is classically tested with the Sally-Anne false-belief task and typically emerges around age 4, making option a correct. In the task of Wimmer and Perner (1983), later adapted by Simon Baron-Cohen (1985), children under about 4 answer based on reality rather than Sally's mistaken belief. Children with autism spectrum disorder often show delayed or impaired performance, the basis of the Theory of Mind deficit hypothesis.","traps":{"b":"At 12 months infants lack the representational capacity to reason about others' false beliefs; ToM emerges years later.","c":"By age 8 (concrete operational) most children have long passed false-belief tasks; the milestone is around age 4.","d":"Theory of Mind appears in early childhood, not adolescence; adolescence brings more advanced perspective-taking, not first-order false belief."}},{"q":"Marcia's identity statuses are defined by two dimensions:","concept":"James Marcia (1966), building on Erikson's fifth stage, defined four identity statuses using two dimensions: crisis (active exploration of alternatives) and commitment (a firm choice), making option b correct. Crossing these yields Identity Diffusion (neither), Foreclosure (commitment without crisis), Moratorium (crisis without commitment), and Identity Achievement (crisis resolved into commitment). The typical adolescent path runs from diffusion through moratorium to achievement.","traps":{"a":"IQ and emotional intelligence (Peter Salovey and John Mayer, 1990) are ability constructs, not the dimensions Marcia used to define identity statuses.","c":"Internal and external locus of control is Rotter's construct, unrelated to Marcia's exploration-and-commitment model.","d":"Approach and avoidance motivation is a motivational dimension, not the crisis-and-commitment framework of identity statuses."}},{"q":"Selman's stages of perspective-taking describe progressive shifts from:","concept":"Robert Selman's stages of social perspective-taking chart how a child moves from being unable to separate self from other to coordinating multiple viewpoints. It runs from Stage 0 (egocentric, roughly 3-6 years, where the child cannot distinguish their own view from another's) up through Stage 3 (mutual, third-person perspective, 10-12 years) and Stage 4 (societal perspective-taking in adolescence). The progression is egocentric to multiple, mutual perspective-taking, and it parallels Jean Piaget's account of declining egocentrism and the growth of theory of mind (David Premack and Guy Woodruff, 1978).","traps":{"a":"Concrete to abstract numerical reasoning describes Piaget's shift from concrete-operational to formal-operational thought, a cognitive-logic sequence, not Selman's social perspective-taking model.","c":"Inductive to deductive logic is a reasoning distinction (specific-to-general versus general-to-specific), unrelated to how a child comes to understand other people's viewpoints.","d":"Procedural to declarative memory is a memory-systems contrast (skills versus facts), which has nothing to do with Selman's stages of social cognition."}},{"q":"Erikson's psychosocial conflict of late adulthood is:","concept":"Erik Erikson's eighth and final psychosocial stage, occurring in late adulthood (roughly 65 and older), is Ego Integrity versus Despair. The person reviews their life and either accepts it as meaningful and well-lived (integrity) or is overwhelmed by regret and bitterness about missed chances (despair). Successful resolution yields the virtue of wisdom.","traps":{"a":"Identity versus Role Confusion is Erikson's fifth stage, occurring in adolescence, centred on forming a coherent sense of self.","c":"Generativity versus Stagnation is the seventh stage, middle adulthood, about contributing to and guiding the next generation.","d":"Intimacy versus Isolation is the sixth stage, young adulthood, about forming committed close relationships."}},{"q":"The DSM-5 minimum duration for a Major Depressive Episode is:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), a Major Depressive Episode requires at least five of nine symptoms (including depressed mood or anhedonia) present most of the day, nearly every day, for a minimum of two weeks, causing impairment and not attributable to a substance or medical condition. The two-week threshold is the defining minimum duration. Longer chronic low mood points instead to Persistent Depressive Disorder.","traps":{"a":"Six months does not match any depressive episode criterion; it resembles durations used in disorders like generalized anxiety, not an MDE.","b":"One month is too long for the MDE minimum and does not correspond to any DSM-5 depression threshold.","c":"One week is too short; a single week of low mood does not meet the two-week MDE requirement."}},{"q":"DSM-5 requires symptoms of schizophrenia to be present for at least:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), schizophrenia requires at least two characteristic symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, disorganised speech, grossly disorganised or catatonic behaviour, or negative symptoms) present for at least one month of active-phase symptoms, with at least one being from the first three. Additionally, continuous signs of disturbance must persist for at least six months. Durations under one month indicate brief psychotic disorder, and one to six months indicates schizophreniform disorder.","traps":{"a":"Three months is not a schizophrenia threshold; it falls within the schizophreniform range (one to six months), not the full diagnosis.","c":"Two weeks of active phase only understates the required one-month active phase and omits the six-month continuous-disturbance criterion.","d":"One year of any psychotic symptom is neither the required duration nor specific enough; a single symptom for a year does not satisfy the two-symptom, six-month structure."}},{"q":"Schneider's first-rank symptoms classically include:","concept":"Kurt Schneider (1959) identified first-rank symptoms once believed to be pathognomonic for schizophrenia. These include thought insertion, withdrawal, and broadcasting; audible thoughts (Gedankenlautwerden); voices arguing or commenting; delusional perception; somatic passivity; and made affect, will, or actions. The DSM-5 no longer treats them as pathognomonic, but they remain clinically useful markers of the schizophrenic experience.","traps":{"a":"Echolalia and stereotyped movements are catatonic or autistic-spectrum features, not Schneiderian first-rank symptoms.","b":"Olfactory hallucinations and tactile illusions point toward organic or temporal-lobe pathology, not the passivity and thought-alienation phenomena Schneider described.","d":"Pure anhedonia and avolition are negative symptoms of schizophrenia, which Schneider explicitly distinguished from his first-rank (largely positive) symptoms."}},{"q":"DSM-5 requires obsessive-compulsive disorder to take up at least:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), obsessive-compulsive disorder requires obsessions and/or compulsions that are time-consuming (taking more than one hour per day) OR that cause clinically significant distress or impairment. The DSM-5 loosened the older DSM-IV strict time criterion by adding the distress/impairment alternative. It also introduced an insight specifier (good/fair, poor, or absent/delusional).","traps":{"a":"Six hours per day is an arbitrary overstatement; the threshold is more than one hour, not six.","c":"Requiring only that obsessions are present ignores that compulsions alone can qualify and that the time/distress criterion must be met.","d":"Two weeks of continuous symptoms is a depressive-episode style duration criterion and is not part of OCD diagnosis."}},{"q":"Which feature most reliably distinguishes Borderline PD from Conduct Disorder?","concept":"Borderline Personality Disorder and Conduct Disorder differ most reliably in who is diagnosed and what the core disturbance is. BPD is a cluster B personality disorder diagnosed in adults, centred on instability of identity, relationships, and affect, marked impulsivity, self-harm, and intense fear of abandonment. Conduct Disorder is diagnosed before age 18 and centres on a persistent pattern of rule violations, aggression, deceit, and antisocial behaviour; childhood CD typically precedes Antisocial (not Borderline) Personality Disorder.","traps":{"a":"Saying both are identical conditions is plainly false; they differ in age of onset, core features, and developmental trajectory.","b":"CD does not always become BPD; when it progresses to a personality disorder, the typical adult outcome is Antisocial Personality Disorder.","c":"Neither disorder is exclusively biological or exclusively environmental; both arise from gene-environment interaction, making this dichotomy inaccurate."}},{"q":"Capgras delusion is the belief that:","concept":"Capgras delusion is a delusional misidentification syndrome in which the patient believes a familiar person (often a spouse or close relative) has been replaced by an identical-looking impostor. It is thought to arise from a disconnect between face recognition and the emotional familiarity response. It belongs to a family of misidentification delusions alongside Fregoli, Cotard, and Othello syndromes.","traps":{"a":"The belief that one is dead or does not exist is Cotard (nihilistic) delusion, not Capgras.","b":"The belief that one's spouse is unfaithful without evidence is Othello syndrome (delusional jealousy).","c":"The belief that strangers are familiar people in disguise is Fregoli delusion, the mirror image of Capgras."}},{"q":"The defining feature distinguishing conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) from malingering is that conversion disorder involves:","concept":"Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) is defined by neurological symptoms (motor or sensory) that are genuinely experienced, produced unintentionally and unconsciously, and are not feigned for any gain. This distinguishes it sharply from malingering (deliberate faking for external reward) and factitious disorder (deliberate faking to assume the sick role). The key is the absence of conscious intent and the absence of external incentive.","traps":{"a":"Intentional production of symptoms for external gain defines malingering, the opposite of conversion disorder's unconscious mechanism.","b":"Conscious production of symptoms for the sick role defines factitious disorder, which involves deliberate deception without external reward.","c":"Faked symptoms in legal proceedings only is a narrow instance of malingering, not the unconscious, non-feigned process of conversion disorder."}},{"q":"DSM-5 requires PTSD symptoms to persist for at least:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder requires exposure to actual or threatened death, serious injury, or sexual violence, followed by symptoms persisting for at least one month across all four symptom clusters (intrusion, avoidance, negative alterations in cognition/mood, and alterations in arousal/reactivity). Symptoms under one month indicate Acute Stress Disorder. A delayed-expression specifier applies when full criteria are not met until at least six months post-event.","traps":{"a":"One week is far too short and corresponds to no PTSD threshold; symptoms this brief fall under Acute Stress Disorder.","c":"Six months is the cutoff for the delayed-expression specifier, not the minimum duration for diagnosis.","d":"Three days is the minimum for Acute Stress Disorder, not PTSD, which requires at least one month."}},{"q":"DSM-5 criteria for Binge Eating Disorder require binge episodes to occur on average at least:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), Binge Eating Disorder requires recurrent binge episodes occurring on average at least once per week for at least three months, marked by a sense of loss of control and at least three associated features (eating rapidly, until uncomfortably full, when not hungry, alone, or with guilt afterward). Unlike Bulimia Nervosa, there are no compensatory behaviours. The once-weekly-for-three-months frequency mirrors the bulimia frequency criterion.","traps":{"a":"Once weekly for six months overstates the duration; the requirement is three months, not six.","b":"Twice monthly for one month understates both frequency and duration; the DSM-5 requires roughly weekly episodes for three months.","d":"Once daily for a year is far more frequent and prolonged than required; daily bingeing is not the diagnostic threshold."}},{"q":"Charles Bonnet syndrome is characterised by:","concept":"Charles Bonnet syndrome consists of complex visual hallucinations (faces, figures, or patterns) occurring in cognitively intact people who have significant visual impairment, such as macular degeneration. Crucially, insight is preserved: the person knows the images are not real. It is thought to reflect cortical release or deafferentation, where visually deprived cortex generates spontaneous imagery.","traps":{"a":"Auditory hallucinations in dementia are unrelated; Charles Bonnet syndrome is specifically visual and occurs without cognitive decline.","b":"Tactile hallucinations in alcohol withdrawal describe delirium tremens (formication), a very different aetiology.","d":"Olfactory hallucinations preceding seizures describe an uncinate or temporal-lobe aura, not the visual release phenomenon of Charles Bonnet syndrome."}},{"q":"The most reliable clinical feature distinguishing delirium from dementia is:","concept":"Delirium and dementia are most reliably distinguished by course and attention. Delirium has an acute onset (hours to days), a fluctuating course, prominent inattention as the cardinal feature, altered consciousness, and an often reversible underlying cause (infection, drugs, metabolic disturbance). Dementia has an insidious onset (months to years) and a progressive course, with memory impairment prominent early and attention relatively preserved until later. The two commonly co-occur in hospitalised elderly patients.","traps":{"a":"Delirium occurs only in young patients is false; it is in fact especially common in the elderly, particularly those with underlying dementia.","b":"Memory loss being greater in delirium misidentifies the cardinal deficit; the hallmark of delirium is inattention, and prominent memory loss is more characteristic of dementia.","d":"Dementia is always reversible is incorrect; most dementias are progressive and irreversible, whereas delirium is the potentially reversible condition."}},{"q":"DSM-5 Persistent Depressive Disorder (dysthymia) requires symptoms to be present for at least:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), Persistent Depressive Disorder (dysthymia) requires depressed mood most of the day, more days than not, for at least two years in adults and at least one year in children and adolescents, plus at least two of six associated symptoms. The person is never symptom-free for more than two months, and there is no manic or hypomanic episode. DSM-5 merged chronic major depression and dysthymia into this single category.","traps":{"a":"Five years overstates the requirement; the adult threshold is two years, not five.","c":"Two weeks is the criterion for a Major Depressive Episode, not the chronic course of Persistent Depressive Disorder.","d":"Six months is too short for PDD, which requires two years in adults (one year in youth)."}},{"q":"Tourette's disorder requires:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), Tourette's disorder requires multiple motor tics plus at least one vocal tic (though they need not occur concurrently), present for at least one year, with onset before age 18 and not attributable to a substance or medical condition. Coprolalia, though famous, occurs in only about 10-15 percent of cases and is not required. Comorbid OCD and ADHD are very common.","traps":{"a":"Coprolalia as a required feature is a popular misconception; it is present in a minority of cases and is not diagnostic.","b":"Vocal tics only for over one month describes provisional or a persistent vocal tic disorder, not Tourette's, which requires both motor and vocal tics.","d":"Only motor tics for six months describes a persistent (chronic) motor tic disorder, missing Tourette's requirement of a vocal tic and its one-year duration."}},{"q":"Anorexia nervosa, restricting subtype, is defined in DSM-5 by:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), the restricting subtype of Anorexia Nervosa is defined by the absence of recurrent binge-eating or purging behaviours during the current three-month episode, with weight loss achieved through dietary restriction, fasting, and/or excessive exercise. The binge-eating/purging subtype, by contrast, involves recurrent such behaviours. DSM-5 removed the amenorrhea criterion and softened the strict weight threshold.","traps":{"a":"Required compensatory vomiting contradicts the restricting subtype, which is defined precisely by the absence of purging.","c":"Binge-purge cycles within three months describe the binge-eating/purging subtype, the opposite of the restricting subtype.","d":"Required weekly laxative use is again a purging behaviour and would place the case in the binge/purge subtype, not the restricting one."}},{"q":"The four DSM-5 PTSD symptom clusters are:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), PTSD is organised into four symptom clusters: Intrusion (flashbacks, nightmares, intrusive memories), Avoidance (of internal or external reminders), Negative alterations in cognition and mood (negative beliefs, blame, detachment, diminished interest), and Alterations in arousal and reactivity (hypervigilance, exaggerated startle, irritability, sleep disturbance). DSM-IV had three clusters; DSM-5 split the negative-cognition/mood cluster off from avoidance to create four.","traps":{"b":"Hyperarousal only, with mood changes optional, collapses four required clusters into one and wrongly makes mood alterations optional.","c":"Flashbacks, denial, anger, bargaining mixes one true PTSD feature with the Kubler-Ross stages of grief, which are not PTSD clusters.","d":"Dissociation, anxiety, depression, mania are separate symptom domains or disorders, not the four defined PTSD clusters."}},{"q":"Pseudobulbar affect (PBA) is characterised by:","concept":"Pseudobulbar affect (pathological laughing and crying) is characterised by sudden, brief, involuntary outbursts of crying or laughing that are disproportionate to, or incongruent with, the person's underlying mood. It results from disruption of corticobulbar pathways and is associated with neurological conditions such as stroke, multiple sclerosis, ALS, traumatic brain injury, and dementia. It is treated with dextromethorphan-quinidine or SSRIs and is distinct from post-stroke depression.","traps":{"a":"Anxious affect in generalized anxiety disorder is a mood/anxiety symptom, not the involuntary emotional expression of a corticobulbar lesion.","b":"Affect blunting in schizophrenia is a reduction of emotional expression, the opposite of the exaggerated, involuntary outbursts of PBA.","d":"Mood-congruent affect in major depression means the emotional display matches the mood, whereas PBA is defined by incongruence between expression and mood."}},{"q":"The key DSM-5 feature distinguishing mania from hypomania is:","concept":"In the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), mania and hypomania differ mainly in severity, duration, and consequences. Mania lasts at least seven days (or any duration if hospitalisation is required), causes marked impairment, and may include psychotic features, defining Bipolar I. Hypomania lasts at least four days, causes no marked impairment, involves no psychosis, and is observable by others, and with a major depressive episode defines Bipolar II. By definition, hypomania never includes psychotic features.","traps":{"b":"Saying mania has no euphoria is false; euphoric (or irritable) mood is a core feature of a manic episode.","c":"Hypomania lasting longer than mania is backwards; hypomania has a shorter minimum duration (four days versus seven).","d":"Saying only hypomania can include psychotic features inverts the truth; psychosis occurs in mania and is explicitly excluded from hypomania."}},{"q":"Folie à deux (shared psychotic disorder) describes:","concept":"Folie a deux (shared psychotic disorder), described by Lasegue and Falret in 1877, is a condition in which a delusion develops in a previously healthy person who is in a close relationship with, and often isolated alongside, a 'primary' individual who has an established psychotic illness. The delusion is transmitted from the dominant partner to the secondary one. DSM-5 folds this into 'Other Specified Schizophrenia Spectrum and Other Psychotic Disorder.'","traps":{"a":"Group hallucinations in cult settings describe mass or collective phenomena, not the two-person delusional transmission that defines folie a deux.","b":"Two unrelated patients with identical hallucinations lacks the required close relationship and the primary-to-secondary transmission that is the essence of the syndrome.","d":"Schizophrenia transmitted via genetic inheritance describes heritability of a disorder, not the psychosocial induction of a delusion in a close contact."}},{"q":"On the WAIS-IV, the four index scores are:","concept":"The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition (WAIS-IV, 2008) replaced the older Verbal IQ and Performance IQ structure with four index scores: the Verbal Comprehension Index (VCI), Perceptual Reasoning Index (PRI), Working Memory Index (WMI), and Processing Speed Index (PSI). The Full Scale IQ is the overall composite derived from these indices. This four-index model reflects a more factor-analytically grounded structure of intelligence.","traps":{"a":"Crystallised, Fluid, Visual, Quantitative are broad Cattell-Horn-Carroll or Woodcock-Johnson style abilities, not the WAIS-IV index labels.","c":"VIQ, PIQ, Memory, Attention mixes the outdated Verbal/Performance IQ terms with domains that are not WAIS-IV indices.","d":"Verbal IQ, Performance IQ, Full Scale IQ, and Memory Quotient describe the older Wechsler and Wechsler Memory Scale structure that WAIS-IV replaced."}},{"q":"On the MMPI-2, the F (Infrequency) scale is used to detect:","concept":"On the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory-2 (MMPI-2), the F (Infrequency) scale detects random responding or the over-reporting of unusual or severe symptoms, often called 'faking bad.' It is one of several validity scales, alongside L (Lie), K (Correction), and the VRIN and TRIN inconsistency scales. An elevated F must be interpreted cautiously, ruling out random responding via VRIN/TRIN before concluding symptom exaggeration.","traps":{"a":"Religious bias is not what any MMPI-2 validity scale measures; the F scale targets infrequent symptom endorsement, not belief content.","b":"Item endorsement based on social desirability (faking good) is captured by the L and K scales, not the F scale.","d":"Defensiveness only is the domain of the L and K scales; the F scale detects the opposite tendency, over-reporting."}},{"q":"Cronbach's alpha is most commonly used to estimate:","concept":"Cronbach's alpha, introduced by Lee Cronbach in 1951, is the most commonly used index of internal consistency reliability, estimating how closely the items of a multi-item scale measure the same underlying construct. It ranges from 0 to 1, with values of roughly 0.70 acceptable, 0.80 good, and 0.90 excellent, though very high alpha can indicate item redundancy. It is distinct from inter-rater reliability (Cohen's kappa) and test-retest reliability (Pearson r over time).","traps":{"a":"Construct validity concerns whether a test measures the intended construct, which is a validity question, not the internal-consistency reliability that alpha estimates.","c":"Predictive validity concerns how well scores forecast a future criterion, again a validity issue rather than reliability.","d":"Test-retest reliability is estimated by correlating scores across two time points (Pearson r), not by Cronbach's alpha."}},{"q":"The Exner Comprehensive System is the major scoring system for:","concept":"John Exner's Comprehensive System (1974) is the major standardised scoring system for the Rorschach Inkblot Test. Exner integrated five earlier, competing Rorschach systems into a single approach with norms, coding location, determinants, content, populars, and special scores into a structural summary. It has been largely superseded by the Rorschach Performance Assessment System (R-PAS, 2011).","traps":{"a":"The MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) is an objective self-report inventory scored by validity and clinical scales, not by the Exner system.","b":"The Thematic Apperception Test is scored by narrative/thematic methods (for example Murray's needs and press), not the Exner Comprehensive System.","d":"The Wechsler scales are intelligence tests scored by index and IQ composites, entirely unrelated to Rorschach coding."}},{"q":"Sensitivity of a screening test refers to:","concept":"Sensitivity is the true positive rate of a screening test: the proportion of people who actually have the condition whom the test correctly identifies as positive, calculated as true positives divided by (true positives plus false negatives). It contrasts with specificity, the true negative rate. A highly sensitive test is good for ruling out disease when negative (the SnNOut rule).","traps":{"a":"The probability that a positive test reflects true disease is positive predictive value, which depends on prevalence, not sensitivity.","b":"The probability of disease prior to testing is the pre-test probability or prevalence, not a test's sensitivity.","d":"The proportion of true negatives correctly identified is specificity, the complementary measure to sensitivity."}},{"q":"A stanine score of 5 corresponds to which position on the normal curve?","concept":"Stanine scores ('standard nine') are a normalised scale from 1 to 9 with a mean of 5 and a standard deviation of about 2, where each band is roughly half a standard deviation wide. A stanine of 5 is the central band, corresponding to the 40th to 60th percentile (the mean plus or minus about 0.25 SD). Stanines 1-3 are below average, 4-6 average, and 7-9 above average.","traps":{"a":"The 23rd-39th percentile corresponds to a stanine of 4 (just below average), not the central stanine of 5.","b":"Above the 95th percentile corresponds to a stanine of 9, the top band, far above the middle stanine of 5.","d":"The 1st-4th percentile corresponds to a stanine of 1, the lowest band, not the average middle band."}},{"q":"BDI-II severity bands are conventionally:","concept":"The Beck Depression Inventory-II (BDI-II, 1996) is a 21-item self-report scale with a total range of 0 to 63, conventionally banded as 0-13 minimal, 14-19 mild, 20-28 moderate, and 29-63 severe depression. Knowing these cutoffs distinguishes it from other instruments such as the PHQ-9 and the clinician-rated Hamilton Depression Rating Scale, which use different ranges.","traps":{"b":"The 0-5 / 6-10 / 11-15 / 16+ banding does not match the BDI-II's 0-63 range and fabricated small cutoffs.","c":"The 0-9 / 10-15 / 16-23 / 24-40 banding is incorrect for the BDI-II and does not reach its maximum of 63.","d":"The 0-20 / 21-40 / 41-60 banding invents three broad ranges that do not correspond to the established BDI-II severity thresholds."}},{"q":"Item Response Theory (IRT) differs from Classical Test Theory (CTT) primarily in that IRT:","concept":"Item Response Theory (IRT), developed by figures such as Georg Rasch, Frederic Lord, and Allan Birnbaum, models the probability of a correct response as a logistic function of a person's latent ability (theta) and item parameters: difficulty (b), discrimination (a), and guessing (c). The one-parameter (Rasch) model uses difficulty only, the two-parameter adds discrimination, and the three-parameter adds guessing. This item-level modelling is what enables computer-adaptive testing, unlike Classical Test Theory which focuses on whole-test scores.","traps":{"b":"Saying IRT is identical to CTT but relabelled is false; IRT models item-level probabilities and person ability, a fundamentally different framework from CTT.","c":"Claiming IRT cannot estimate item difficulty is wrong; difficulty (the b parameter) is central to every IRT model.","d":"Requiring only a single item parameter and no person parameter contradicts IRT, which always includes a person-ability parameter (theta) alongside item parameters."}},{"q":"The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test (Bender-Gestalt II) primarily assesses:","concept":"The Bender Visual-Motor Gestalt Test, Second Edition (Bender-Gestalt II, Brannigan and Decker 2003) primarily assesses visual-motor integration by having the subject copy geometric designs, scored for accuracy, organisation, and distortion. It is used to screen for developmental delays in children (for example school readiness) and for neuropsychological or organic brain dysfunction in adults, though its specificity is limited. It was historically sometimes interpreted projectively.","traps":{"a":"Auditory memory is assessed by verbal memory tasks, not by a design-copying visual-motor test.","c":"Verbal abstraction is measured by tasks like similarities or proverb interpretation, not by copying geometric figures.","d":"Assessing personality via projective interpretation only misrepresents the Bender-Gestalt; its primary, validated use is visual-motor screening, with projective use being a legacy secondary practice."}},{"q":"The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT) is classified as a:","concept":"The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan at Harvard in 1935, is a projective technique in which the subject constructs stories about ambiguous interpersonal scenes. Responses are interpreted for underlying needs, press, and recurring themes, grounded in Murray's Need-Press theory. As a projective measure it contrasts with objective, self-report inventories.","traps":{"a":"An objective achievement test measures acquired knowledge with right/wrong answers, unlike the open-ended, interpretive TAT.","b":"A self-report personality inventory (such as the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943)) uses fixed items scored objectively, the opposite of the TAT's projective storytelling.","c":"A structured neuropsychological battery assesses cognitive functions with standardised tasks, not the personality dynamics the projective TAT elicits."}},{"q":"Concurrent validity is established by:","concept":"Criterion validity asks whether a test correlates with an external gold-standard criterion. It has two forms: concurrent validity, where the criterion is measured at roughly the same time as the test, and predictive validity, where the criterion is measured later. Option a describes exactly the concurrent case, so it is correct.","traps":{"b":"Correlating a test with future performance is predictive validity, the other branch of criterion validity, not concurrent validity.","c":"Expert agreement that items 'look right' is face validity, a subjective judgment about appearance, not an empirical correlation with a criterion.","d":"Comparing scores across two cultures speaks to measurement invariance or cross-cultural bias, not to whether a test tracks a same-time criterion."}},{"q":"The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery includes which classic subtest?","concept":"The Halstead-Reitan Neuropsychological Battery, standardised by Ralph Reitan, is a fixed battery of sensitive tests that yield an overall Impairment Index for brain dysfunction. Its classic subtests include the Category Test, Tactual Performance Test, Trail Making A and B, Speech-Sounds Perception, Seashore Rhythm, and Finger Tapping. Option a lists these core Halstead-Reitan components, so it is correct.","traps":{"b":"The Beck Depression Inventory and Hamilton Depression Rating Scale are mood measures, not neuropsychological subtests of the Halstead-Reitan.","c":"The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale and Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) are general IQ and personality instruments that may accompany but do not constitute the Halstead-Reitan battery.","d":"The Bender-Gestalt is a single visuomotor screening test, not the multi-subtest Halstead-Reitan battery."}},{"q":"The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales primarily assess:","concept":"The Vineland Adaptive Behavior Scales, developed by Sara Sparrow and colleagues, use a caregiver interview or questionnaire to measure adaptive functioning across the lifespan. They assess four domains: Communication, Daily Living Skills, Socialisation, and Motor Skills, plus a Maladaptive Behaviour index. Because adaptive deficits are required alongside low IQ to diagnose intellectual disability under the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"Suicidal ideation severity is captured by scales like the Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale or Beck Scale for Suicide Ideation, not by an adaptive-behaviour instrument.","b":"Personality disorders are assessed with instruments such as the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) or SCID-5-PD, not the Vineland.","c":"Cognitive ability alone is measured by IQ tests like the Wechsler scales; the Vineland deliberately measures everyday adaptive skills, not intelligence."}},{"q":"The standard error of measurement (SEM) of a test is calculated as:","concept":"The standard error of measurement indexes how much an examinee's observed score is expected to fluctuate around their true score due to unreliability. It is computed as the standard deviation of scores multiplied by the square root of one minus the reliability coefficient, so SEM = SD times the square root of (1 minus r). As reliability approaches 1, the SEM shrinks toward 0, making option c correct.","traps":{"a":"Dividing SD by reliability is not a defined psychometric formula and would grow the error as reliability falls, which is backward.","b":"Multiplying SD by (1 plus reliability) inflates rather than reduces error as reliability rises, the opposite of the intended relationship.","d":"SD times reliability confuses reliability with its complement; the SEM depends on the square root of (1 minus reliability), not reliability itself."}},{"q":"The Wechsler Memory Scale-IV (WMS-IV) yields which primary indices?","concept":"The Wechsler Memory Scale, Fourth Edition, developed by David Wechsler's successors, is the standard adult memory battery. Its primary index scores are Auditory Memory, Visual Memory, Visual Working Memory, Immediate Memory, and Delayed Memory, built from subtests like Logical Memory, Verbal Paired Associates, and Visual Reproduction. Option c names these indices, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Verbal IQ and Performance IQ are older Wechsler intelligence-scale indices, not memory-scale outputs, and were themselves dropped in recent WAIS editions.","b":"Crystallised and fluid intelligence are Cattell-Horn constructs measured by ability tests, not by the WMS-IV.","d":"Attention, concentration, and vigilance are single cognitive processes tapped in part by the scale, not the named primary index structure of the WMS-IV."}},{"q":"Socratic questioning in CBT is used primarily to:","concept":"Socratic questioning, central to Aaron T. Beck's cognitive therapy, uses open-ended guided-discovery questions to help clients examine the evidence for and against their automatic thoughts. Rather than the therapist supplying conclusions, the client is led to weigh alternatives and reach more balanced, adaptive views themselves. Option d captures this collaborative evidence-testing, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Free-associating to childhood memories is a psychoanalytic technique, not the present-focused, thought-testing method of Socratic questioning.","b":"Socratic questioning complements rather than replaces exposure therapy; the two address different targets and are often used together.","c":"Telling the client what to think is directive instruction, the exact opposite of guided discovery, which draws conclusions from the client."}},{"q":"The first-line behavioural treatment for OCD is:","concept":"The first-line behavioural treatment for obsessive-compulsive disorder is Exposure and Response Prevention, formalised by Edna Foa and Michael Kozak. The client is exposed to feared triggers while refraining from compulsions, allowing the anxiety to extinguish and breaking the obsession-anxiety-compulsion cycle. Option a is therefore correct, with SSRIs as first-line medication.","traps":{"b":"Hypnotherapy has no established evidence base as a first-line OCD treatment and is not recommended by major guidelines.","c":"Free association is a psychoanalytic method, not the structured behavioural intervention proven for OCD.","d":"Systematic desensitisation (Joseph Wolpe, 1958) is a graded, relaxation-based procedure for phobias; ERP, which emphasises response prevention, is the specific OCD treatment."}},{"q":"Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha Linehan primarily for:","concept":"Dialectical Behavior Therapy was developed by Marsha Linehan primarily for chronically suicidal clients and those with Borderline Personality Disorder. It synthesises behavioural change strategies with acceptance and mindfulness drawn from Zen, delivered through four skill modules: mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness. Option d names its original target population, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Childhood ADHD is treated primarily with behavioural parent training and stimulant medication, not DBT.","b":"Panic disorder is treated first-line with cognitive behavioural therapy and interoceptive exposure, not DBT.","c":"Schizophrenia is managed with antipsychotics plus psychosocial interventions; DBT was not designed for it."}},{"q":"Rogers' core conditions for client-centered therapy are:","concept":"Carl Rogers specified three core conditions he called necessary and sufficient for therapeutic change: empathy, unconditional positive regard, and congruence (genuineness). These relational attitudes, uniquely emphasised in person-centered therapy, form the foundation of the common-factors literature. Option c lists all three, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Interpretation, transference, and free association are psychoanalytic techniques, not Rogers' humanistic core conditions.","b":"Modeling, role-play, and homework are behavioural and cognitive-behavioural methods, not the person-centered relational conditions.","d":"Confrontation, paradoxical intention, and prescription are directive strategic techniques that contradict Rogers' non-directive stance."}},{"q":"Working through in psychoanalytic therapy refers to:","concept":"Working through, Freud's Durcharbeiten, is the effortful psychoanalytic process of repeatedly re-encountering the same conflict, resistance, and interpretation across different contexts such as transference, dreams, and daily life. This repetition consolidates insight so that it moves beyond intellectual understanding into genuine emotional and behavioural change. Option b describes this process, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Solving problems by direct advice is a supportive or directive approach, not the insight-integrating work of psychoanalysis.","c":"Ending therapy after a single insight is the opposite of working through, which specifically requires sustained repetition.","d":"Free association without interpretation is the raw analytic method, but working through is precisely the repeated application of interpretation to that material."}},{"q":"Beck's cognitive triad in depression consists of negative views about:","concept":"Aaron T. Beck's cognitive triad describes the three negative belief domains that characterise depression: negative views of the self, of the world or current experience, and of the future. These pervasive negative schemas generate the automatic thoughts that cognitive therapy targets. Option c lists self, world, and future, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Mood, behaviour, and somatic state are symptom clusters of depression, not the belief domains of Beck's triad.","b":"Past, present, and future is a time framing; the triad specifically concerns self, world, and future, not the past.","d":"Self, mother, and father reflects an object-relations or psychoanalytic frame, not Beck's cognitive triad."}},{"q":"The 'spirit' of Motivational Interviewing is captured by the acronym PACE, which stands for:","concept":"William Miller and Stephen Rollnick describe the underlying spirit of Motivational Interviewing with the acronym PACE: Partnership, Acceptance, Compassion, and Evocation. This collaborative, client-honouring stance draws out the person's own motivation for change rather than imposing it. Option a spells out PACE, so it is correct.","traps":{"b":"Presence, Affirmation, Confrontation, Empathy is invented; confrontation in particular runs counter to the non-confrontational MI spirit.","c":"Practice, Active listening, Change, Engagement does not match the PACE acronym and mixes in unrelated terms.","d":"Plan, Act, Check, Evaluate is a quality-improvement or PDCA cycle, not the MI spirit."}},{"q":"In Minuchin's structural family therapy, enmeshment refers to:","concept":"In Salvador Minuchin's structural family therapy, families are organised into subsystems separated by boundaries that vary in permeability. Enmeshment refers to diffuse, overly permeable boundaries marked by over-involvement, intrusion, and loss of individual autonomy. Option a describes this, so it is correct; its opposite is disengagement with rigid boundaries.","traps":{"b":"Strategic paradoxical interventions belong to strategic family therapy (Haley, the Milan group), not to the structural concept of enmeshment.","c":"Rigid boundaries with disengagement is the opposite pole of enmeshment on Minuchin's boundary continuum.","d":"A complete lack of communication describes extreme disengagement, not the over-involvement that defines enmeshment."}},{"q":"Yalom's emphasis on the 'here-and-now' in group therapy is intended to:","concept":"Irvin Yalom treats the therapy group as a social microcosm in which members' habitual relationship patterns replay in real time. Emphasising the here-and-now means using these immediate in-group interactions, then reflecting on them through a self-reflective loop, as the primary engine of change. Option d captures this, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"The here-and-now focus does not forbid discussing outside life; it uses in-group events to illuminate those outside patterns.","b":"Yalom's approach complements rather than permanently replaces individual therapy.","c":"Examining group dynamics is central to the here-and-now method, not something it bypasses."}},{"q":"Joseph Wolpe's principle of reciprocal inhibition is the basis for:","concept":"Joseph Wolpe's principle of reciprocal inhibition holds that an anxiety response can be inhibited by simultaneously eliciting an incompatible response such as deep relaxation. This principle is the basis of systematic desensitisation: train relaxation, build a fear hierarchy, then progress through it while staying relaxed. Option b describes this, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (Steven Hayes) is rooted in Relational Frame Theory and psychological flexibility, not Wolpe's reciprocal inhibition.","c":"Paradoxical intention is a logotherapy/strategic technique (Frankl), unrelated to pairing relaxation with feared stimuli.","d":"Free association is a psychoanalytic method, not a behaviour-therapy application of reciprocal inhibition."}},{"q":"The 'hexaflex' of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) consists of:","concept":"The hexaflex of Acceptance and Commitment Therapy, developed by Steven Hayes, maps the six interrelated processes that produce psychological flexibility: acceptance, cognitive defusion, self-as-context, contact with the present moment, values, and committed action. Each process counters a corresponding rigidity such as experiential avoidance or cognitive fusion. Option d lists the six, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Six neurotransmitter systems is a neurobiology reference with nothing to do with the ACT hexaflex.","b":"Six DSM disorder clusters is a diagnostic grouping, not the ACT process model.","c":"Sensation, perception, attention, memory, emotion, behaviour are basic cognitive-psychology domains, not the ACT flexibility processes."}},{"q":"Interpersonal Therapy (IPT) targets four main problem areas:","concept":"Interpersonal Therapy, developed by Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman for major depression, is a time-limited treatment focused on the client's current interpersonal context. Therapist and client select one of four problem areas to target: grief, interpersonal role disputes, role transitions, and interpersonal deficits. Option d names these four, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Schemas, automatic thoughts, behaviours, and biological factors are cognitive-behavioural targets, not the four IPT problem areas.","b":"Id, ego, superego, and defences are psychoanalytic structures, not IPT foci.","c":"Cognitive, affective, motor, and somatic are symptom domains, not the interpersonal problem areas IPT works with."}},{"q":"The 'empty chair' technique is most associated with which therapeutic school?","concept":"The empty chair technique is most associated with Gestalt therapy, founded by Fritz Perls. The client dialogues with an absent person or a disowned part of the self imagined in an empty chair, often switching seats to voice each side. This surfaces unfinished business and integrates polarities such as top dog and underdog, so option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"Person-centered therapy (Carl Rogers, 1951) relies on the core conditions and non-directive reflection, not staged chair dialogues.","b":"DBT (Marsha Linehan) uses skills training and dialectics, not the empty-chair experiment.","d":"CBT uses thought records and behavioural experiments; the empty chair is a Gestalt, later emotion-focused, technique."}},{"q":"Francine Shapiro's EMDR (Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing) is recommended primarily for:","concept":"Eye Movement Desensitization and Reprocessing, developed by Francine Shapiro, is an eight-phase protocol that pairs brief recall of a distressing memory with bilateral stimulation such as guided eye movements. It is recommended primarily for post-traumatic stress disorder and trauma-related conditions and is endorsed by the American Psychological Association, NICE, and the World Health Organization. Option a is therefore correct.","traps":{"b":"Schizophrenia is treated with antipsychotics and psychosocial care; EMDR is not a primary treatment for it.","c":"Bipolar disorder is managed with mood stabilisers, not EMDR.","d":"OCD's first-line treatment is Exposure and Response Prevention, not EMDR."}},{"q":"A Type II error in hypothesis testing occurs when:","concept":"In null hypothesis significance testing, a Type II error (beta) occurs when a false null hypothesis is not rejected, meaning a real effect exists but the test fails to detect it: a false negative. Statistical power equals 1 minus beta and rises with larger samples, larger effect sizes, and lower variance. Option c describes this false negative, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Setting the p-value or alpha threshold is a design choice, not the definition of a Type II error.","b":"An excessively large sample tends to reduce Type II error, not cause it.","d":"Rejecting a true null is a false positive, which is a Type I error, not Type II."}},{"q":"ANOVA (analysis of variance) is most appropriate when comparing:","concept":"Analysis of variance compares means across groups by partitioning variance into between-group and within-group components, tested with an F ratio. One-way ANOVA is the appropriate choice when comparing three or more independent group means on a continuous outcome; a significant F is followed by post-hoc tests. Option d states this, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Two paired observations of the same subject call for a paired t-test, not ANOVA.","b":"Two categorical variables are analysed with a chi-square test, not ANOVA.","c":"Two independent group means are compared with an independent-samples t-test; ANOVA is reserved for three or more groups."}},{"q":"An odds ratio of 1.0 indicates:","concept":"An odds ratio quantifies the odds of an outcome in an exposed group relative to an unexposed group. When the odds ratio equals exactly 1.0, the odds are identical in both groups, indicating no association between exposure and outcome. Option d states this, so it is correct; the same null value logic applies to risk ratios and hazard ratios.","traps":{"a":"A protective exposure yields an odds ratio below 1.0, not equal to 1.0.","b":"An odds ratio, being observational, does not by itself establish causation, and 1.0 indicates no association at all.","c":"Doubling the odds corresponds to an odds ratio of 2.0, not 1.0."}},{"q":"Cohen's d of 0.5 conventionally represents:","concept":"Cohen's d expresses a between-group difference in standard-deviation units. By Jacob Cohen's conventional benchmarks, roughly 0.2 is a small effect, 0.5 is a medium effect, and 0.8 is a large effect. A d of 0.5 therefore represents a medium effect, making option b correct.","traps":{"a":"A trivial effect would be well below 0.2, far smaller than 0.5.","c":"A small effect corresponds to about 0.2, not 0.5.","d":"A large effect corresponds to about 0.8 or higher, not 0.5."}},{"q":"Stratified random sampling differs from simple random sampling in that it:","concept":"Stratified random sampling first divides the population into mutually exclusive subgroups (strata) defined by a relevant characteristic, then randomly samples within each stratum. This guarantees each subgroup is represented and typically yields lower variance than simple random sampling when strata are internally homogeneous. Option d describes this, so it is correct.","traps":{"a":"Selecting every nth subject from a list is systematic sampling, not stratified sampling.","b":"Selecting intact clusters such as whole schools is cluster sampling, which samples groups rather than sampling within strata.","c":"Using only volunteers is convenience sampling, a non-probability method, not stratified random sampling."}},{"q":"Reliability and validity differ in that:","concept":"Reliability and validity are distinct measurement properties. Reliability is the consistency or reproducibility of a measurement, while validity is whether the test actually measures the construct it claims to. A test can be reliable without being valid, but a valid test must be reliable, so option a is correct.","traps":{"b":"Reliability includes many forms such as test-retest and internal consistency, not only inter-rater agreement.","c":"Reliability and validity are conceptually different, not two names for the same thing.","d":"There is no rule that validity must be established before reliability; if anything reliability sets an upper bound on validity."}},{"q":"Intention-to-treat (ITT) analysis in an RCT means:","concept":"Intention-to-treat analysis in a randomised controlled trial analyses every randomised participant in the group to which they were originally assigned, regardless of adherence, drop-out, or crossover. This preserves the balance created by randomisation and prevents bias from differential attrition, giving a pragmatic effectiveness estimate. Option a states this, so it is correct.","traps":{"b":"Analysing only those who completed the intended treatment is per-protocol analysis, which breaks randomisation and tends to inflate efficacy.","c":"Analysing only responders is a biased selective approach, not intention-to-treat.","d":"Including only the control group ignores the intervention arm entirely and is not intention-to-treat."}},{"q":"A 95% confidence interval for a mean difference of 0.4 that ranges from −0.1 to 0.9 implies:","concept":"A confidence interval for a mean difference conveys both the effect estimate and its precision. When a 95% confidence interval for a difference includes 0 (the null value), the result is not statistically significant at alpha 0.05 because a zero difference remains plausible. The interval from minus 0.1 to 0.9 crosses 0, so option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"A result crossing 0 is not statistically significant, so it cannot be highly significant.","b":"A non-significant interval does not prove there is no effect; the study may simply be underpowered.","c":"A large sample would narrow the interval; a wide interval crossing 0 suggests limited precision, not an oversized sample."}},{"q":"The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric analogue of:","concept":"The Mann-Whitney U test (equivalently the Wilcoxon rank-sum test) is the non-parametric analogue of the independent-samples t-test, used to compare two independent groups when normality is violated or data are ordinal. It ranks all observations and tests whether the distributions differ. Option d correctly identifies its parametric counterpart.","traps":{"a":"The chi-square test of independence handles categorical associations; its rank-based counterpart is not the Mann-Whitney U.","b":"The non-parametric analogue of the paired t-test is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test, not Mann-Whitney U.","c":"The non-parametric analogue of one-way ANOVA is the Kruskal-Wallis test, not Mann-Whitney U."}},{"q":"Number Needed to Treat (NNT) is calculated as:","concept":"The Number Needed to Treat is the number of patients who must receive a treatment for one additional person to benefit compared with control. It is calculated as 1 divided by the absolute risk reduction, where the absolute risk reduction is the control event rate minus the treatment event rate. Option a gives this formula, so it is correct.","traps":{"b":"Odds ratio minus 1 is not a defined NNT formula and mixes a relative measure with an absolute count.","c":"Relative risk times the control event rate does not yield the NNT.","d":"Patients enrolled divided by number of events is an event-rate-type calculation, not the reciprocal of absolute risk reduction."}},{"q":"The Bonferroni correction adjusts the alpha threshold when conducting multiple comparisons by:","concept":"The Bonferroni correction controls the family-wise error rate that inflates when many comparisons are made. It divides the alpha threshold by the number of comparisons (alpha divided by k), so each individual test must meet a stricter significance level. This conservative adjustment, which lowers power, makes option a correct.","traps":{"b":"Bonferroni adjusts the alpha threshold; it does not replace the p-value with a Z-score.","c":"Ignoring the number of comparisons is exactly what Bonferroni corrects for, not what it does.","d":"Multiplying alpha by the number of comparisons would loosen the threshold and inflate false positives, the opposite of Bonferroni."}},{"q":"Choose the correctly punctuated sentence.","concept":"When a sentence opens with a dependent (subordinate) clause, standard English punctuation places a comma after that clause and before the main independent clause. Here 'Although it was raining' is a subordinate clause introduced by 'although', so it must be followed by a comma: 'Although it was raining, we decided to walk.' The subordinating conjunction 'although' itself is not followed by a comma, and a semicolon cannot be used because a semicolon joins two independent clauses, not a dependent clause to an independent one.","traps":{"a":"This wrongly inserts a comma after 'Although' and omits the needed comma after the subordinate clause, cutting the conjunction off from the clause it introduces.","b":"A semicolon joins two independent clauses, but 'Although it was raining' is dependent, so a semicolon is grammatically wrong here.","c":"The comma is misplaced inside the main clause ('we decided, to walk'), which incorrectly separates the verb 'decided' from its infinitive object."}},{"q":"Choose the option that correctly completes the sentence: 'The data ___ inconclusive.'","concept":"'Data' is historically the plural of the Latin singular 'datum', so in formal, academic, and scientific writing it takes a plural verb: 'The data are inconclusive.' Although everyday and increasingly some journalistic usage treats 'data' as a singular mass noun ('the data is'), exam and research contexts expect the traditional plural agreement. The same pattern applies to 'media' (plural of medium) and 'criteria' (plural of criterion).","traps":{"b":"'Was' is singular past tense, treating 'data' as a singular mass noun, which formal usage rejects.","c":"'Has been' is singular present perfect and mismatches the plural subject 'data'.","d":"'Is' is the singular present verb and reflects the informal treatment of 'data' as singular, not the formal plural preferred here."}},{"q":"Identify the sentence that uses 'who' / 'whom' correctly.","concept":"'Who' is the subject form and 'whom' is the object form; the reliable test is to substitute he/him. In 'Who shall I say is calling?', the phrase 'shall I say' is a parenthetical interruption, and the real clause is 'who is calling', where the pronoun is the subject of 'is calling' (He is calling), so 'who' is correct. 'Whom' would only be right where the pronoun functions as an object.","traps":{"b":"'Whom is at the door?' wrongly uses the object form for the subject of 'is'; it should be 'Who is at the door?'","c":"'Who did you give the book to?' uses the subject form where the pronoun is the object of the preposition 'to', so strictly it should be 'whom' (colloquially accepted but not formally correct).","d":"'Whom shall I say is calling?' misreads the parenthetical 'shall I say' and wrongly makes the subject of 'is calling' an object; it should be 'who'."}},{"q":"Which sentence uses the conditional correctly?","concept":"The third conditional expresses an unreal or hypothetical situation in the past and follows the pattern: If + past perfect, would have + past participle. Thus 'If I had known, I would have come.' is correct. A common error is putting 'would have' inside the if-clause; the if-clause takes the past perfect ('had known'), and 'would have' belongs only in the main result clause.","traps":{"a":"This commits the classic error of using 'would have' in the if-clause; the condition should read 'If I had known'.","b":"This mixes tenses illogically, pairing a present-style 'would know' condition with a simple past 'came' result, matching no valid conditional pattern.","c":"This mismatches a second-conditional-style 'If I knew' with a present perfect 'have come', so the tenses do not form a coherent conditional."}},{"q":"Select the sentence with a misplaced modifier.","concept":"A misplaced or dangling modifier is a descriptive phrase positioned so that it appears to modify the wrong noun. In 'Running quickly, the marathon finish line came into view,' the participial phrase 'Running quickly' seems to describe 'the marathon finish line', which cannot run; the sentence needs a human subject, e.g., 'Running quickly, she saw the finish line come into view.' The other options place their modifiers directly beside the words they logically describe.","traps":{"a":"'He drove to the office in the morning' places 'in the morning' sensibly next to the action it modifies, so there is no misplaced modifier.","c":"'The teacher gave the test on Friday' correctly attaches 'on Friday' to the giving of the test, with no dangling phrase.","d":"'She ate the soup that was hot' places the relative clause 'that was hot' directly after 'soup', the noun it properly modifies."}},{"q":"Choose the word closest in meaning to UBIQUITOUS.","concept":"'Ubiquitous' means present, appearing, or found everywhere; pervasive or omnipresent. Its closest synonym among the options is 'universal / present everywhere'. Its antonyms are 'rare', 'scarce', and 'uncommon', and it is frequently confused with the similar-sounding 'iniquitous', which means wicked or grossly unjust.","traps":{"a":"'Quietly persistent' suggests something enduring or insistent, but ubiquitous is about being everywhere in space, not persisting over time.","b":"'Outdated' concerns being old-fashioned or obsolete and has no bearing on how widespread something is.","d":"'Rare' is the direct antonym of ubiquitous, describing scarcity rather than omnipresence."}},{"q":"Identify the sentence in passive voice.","concept":"In the passive voice the grammatical subject receives the action rather than performing it, formed with a form of 'to be' plus the past participle, and the doer (agent) appears in an optional 'by' phrase. 'The proposal was approved by the committee' is passive because 'the proposal' receives the approving and 'the committee' is the agent. The active equivalent is 'The committee approved the proposal.'","traps":{"a":"'The committee will approve the proposal' is active future tense: the committee (subject) performs the action.","b":"'Did the committee approve the proposal?' is an active interrogative sentence, with the committee as the doer, not a passive construction.","c":"'The committee approved the proposal' is active past tense, with the subject performing the action rather than receiving it."}},{"q":"Which option corrects the run-on sentence: 'She studied hard she passed the exam.'?","concept":"A run-on (fused) sentence joins two independent clauses with no punctuation or connector, as in 'She studied hard she passed the exam.' It can be fixed by a period, a semicolon (optionally with a conjunctive adverb like 'consequently' followed by a comma), or a comma plus a coordinating conjunction. 'She studied hard; consequently, she passed the exam' correctly uses a semicolon before the conjunctive adverb and a comma after it.","traps":{"a":"'She studied hard so she passed the exam' uses the coordinating conjunction 'so' but omits the comma that should precede it, so it is not the cleanest correction.","b":"'She studied, hard she passed the exam' inserts a comma in the wrong place and leaves the two clauses improperly fused.","d":"'She studied hard, she passed the exam' joins two independent clauses with only a comma, creating a comma splice, which is still an error."}},{"q":"Read the brief passage and answer:\n'Despite mounting evidence of climate disruption, public policy responses have been hampered by short electoral cycles and powerful incumbent interests, leaving long-horizon problems chronically under-addressed.'\nThe author's main point is best described as:","concept":"The passage uses a 'Despite X, public policy is hampered by Y and Z, leaving long-horizon problems under-addressed' structure, which explicitly blames short electoral cycles and entrenched incumbent interests for the inadequate response to climate change. The author's main point is therefore that short political horizons and vested interests prevent an adequate response to long-term problems. Reading comprehension requires identifying the claim the sentence actually asserts, not adding outside inferences.","traps":{"b":"'Climate change is not happening' directly contradicts the passage, which opens with 'mounting evidence of climate disruption'.","c":"'Renewable energy is more profitable than fossil fuels' introduces an economic claim the passage never makes.","d":"'Elections should be abolished' is an extreme prescriptive leap; the passage diagnoses short electoral cycles as a problem but never proposes ending elections."}},{"q":"Choose the correct option to complete: 'Neither the manager nor the employees ___ available for comment.'","concept":"With correlative conjunctions 'neither ... nor' (and 'either ... or'), the verb agrees with the subject nearer to it, called the rule of proximity. Here the closer subject is the plural 'employees', so the verb must be plural: 'Neither the manager nor the employees were available for comment.' If the order were reversed, with the singular 'manager' nearer the verb, it would take the singular 'was'.","traps":{"b":"'Has been' is singular and clashes with the nearer plural subject 'employees'.","c":"'Was' is singular and would be correct only if the nearer subject were singular, which it is not here.","d":"'Is' is singular present and ignores the proximity rule, which requires agreement with the plural 'employees'."}}],"diagnostic-1":[{"q":"A researcher rejects the null hypothesis when it is actually true. This is:","concept":"In null hypothesis significance testing there are two possible mistakes. A Type I error (alpha) is a false positive: you reject the null hypothesis when it is actually true, concluding an effect exists when it does not. A Type II error (beta) is a false negative: you retain a false null. The probability of a Type I error is set by the significance level alpha (commonly 0.05). Rejecting a true null is by definition a Type I error.","traps":{"b":"A Type II (beta) error is the opposite mistake, failing to reject a null that is actually false, not rejecting a true one.","c":"Low statistical power is (1 minus beta) being small, which raises the risk of Type II errors, not the act of rejecting a true null.","d":"A confounding error refers to a third variable distorting the observed relationship, a design/validity issue, not the decision error described here."}},{"q":"Which test is appropriate for comparing the means of three or more independent groups?","concept":"When comparing means across three or more independent groups, the correct test is a one-way analysis of variance (ANOVA), which uses the F-ratio (between-group variance over within-group variance). Running multiple t-tests instead inflates the family-wise Type I error rate, which is exactly why ANOVA exists. A single t-test can only compare two group means.","traps":{"a":"An independent samples t-test compares the means of only two independent groups, not three or more.","c":"Pearson correlation measures the linear association between two continuous variables, it does not compare group means.","d":"A chi-square test analyses associations between categorical (frequency) variables, not differences between group means."}},{"q":"The non-parametric equivalent of the independent-samples t-test is the:","concept":"Non-parametric tests are used when data violate the assumptions of parametric tests (e.g., non-normal, ordinal). The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric equivalent of the independent-samples t-test: it compares two independent groups by ranking all scores together. Its paired counterpart is the Wilcoxon signed-rank test.","traps":{"a":"The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is the non-parametric equivalent of the paired (related) t-test, not the independent-samples t-test.","b":"The Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric equivalent of one-way ANOVA (three or more independent groups).","d":"The Friedman test is the non-parametric equivalent of repeated-measures ANOVA (three or more related conditions)."}},{"q":"Cronbach's alpha is a measure of:","concept":"Cronbach's alpha, developed by Lee Cronbach, is an index of internal consistency reliability: it estimates how strongly the items of a scale intercorrelate, that is, whether they measure the same underlying construct. Values range from 0 to 1, with roughly 0.70 and above considered acceptable. It is a reliability index, not a validity index.","traps":{"a":"Criterion validity concerns how well a test predicts or agrees with an external criterion, a form of validity, not internal consistency.","b":"Test-retest reliability assesses stability of scores over time using two administrations, whereas alpha is computed from a single administration.","c":"Inter-rater reliability measures agreement between different raters/observers, not the internal coherence of items."}},{"q":"A correlation coefficient of −0.85 indicates:","concept":"A correlation coefficient conveys two things: its sign shows direction and its absolute value (closeness to 1) shows strength. A value of minus 0.85 has a negative sign (as one variable rises, the other falls) and a large magnitude, so it indicates a strong negative relationship. Strength is unrelated to the sign.","traps":{"b":"A weak negative relationship would be a small magnitude such as minus 0.15 or minus 0.20, not minus 0.85.","c":"No relationship corresponds to a coefficient near 0, whereas minus 0.85 is far from zero.","d":"A strong positive relationship would require a positive sign (e.g., plus 0.85); the minus sign here makes it negative."}},{"q":"Which of the following is a PROBABILITY sampling method?","concept":"Probability (random) sampling means every element has a known, non-zero chance of selection, which supports generalisation and estimation of sampling error. Stratified random sampling divides the population into strata and randomly samples within each, so it is a probability method. Quota, purposive, and snowball are non-probability methods that rely on the researcher's judgement or convenience.","traps":{"a":"Quota sampling fills preset category quotas using non-random selection, making it a non-probability method.","c":"Purposive (judgemental) sampling deliberately selects cases the researcher deems informative, so selection probabilities are unknown.","d":"Snowball sampling recruits participants through referrals from existing ones, a non-probability chain-referral technique."}},{"q":"For a one-sample t-test with a sample size of 20, the degrees of freedom are:","concept":"Degrees of freedom (df) are the number of values free to vary when estimating a parameter. For a one-sample t-test, one parameter (the mean) is estimated, so df equals n minus 1. With n equal to 20, df equals 19.","traps":{"a":"20 is the sample size n itself, not df; df subtracts one for the estimated mean.","b":"18 would be n minus 2, the df formula for an independent two-sample situation per adjustment, not a one-sample t-test.","d":"21 is n plus 1, which reverses the correct subtraction."}},{"q":"Which of the following is a PROJECTIVE test of personality?","concept":"Projective tests present ambiguous stimuli and assume the person 'projects' unconscious needs and conflicts onto them. The Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan, asks the person to tell stories about ambiguous picture cards, so it is projective. Objective inventories instead use structured, self-report items with fixed response formats.","traps":{"a":"The 16PF is Raymond Cattell's objective self-report personality inventory measuring 16 traits, not a projective test.","b":"The MMPI-2 is an objective true/false self-report inventory of psychopathology, not projective.","c":"The NEO-PI-R (Paul Costa and Robert McCrae) is an objective self-report questionnaire measuring the Big Five traits, not projective."}},{"q":"A T-score has a mean and standard deviation of:","concept":"Standardised scores rescale raw scores to a fixed mean and standard deviation. A T-score has a mean of 50 and a standard deviation of 10, so a T-score of 60 lies one SD above the mean. This distinguishes it from z-scores and deviation IQ scores.","traps":{"b":"Mean 100, SD 15 describes the deviation IQ metric (e.g., Wechsler scales), not a T-score.","c":"Mean 0, SD 1 describes the z-score (standard score) distribution, not a T-score.","d":"Mean 500, SD 100 describes large-scale test scaling such as the SAT/GRE metric, not a T-score."}},{"q":"The MMPI-2 contains how many clinical scales?","concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory-2 (MMPI-2) contains 10 clinical scales (e.g., Hypochondriasis, Depression, Hysteria, Psychopathic Deviate, Paranoia, Schizophrenia), alongside a set of validity scales (such as L, F, and K) that detect response distortion. The number of clinical scales is 10.","traps":{"a":"4 does not match; the validity indicators are several, and the core clinical scales number 10.","c":"16 confuses the MMPI-2 with Cattell's 16PF, which measures 16 personality factors.","d":"13 is not the count; the standard MMPI-2 clinical scale set is 10."}},{"q":"Sten scores (range 1–10) are most associated with which test?","concept":"A sten ('standard ten') score divides the distribution into ten units, ranging from 1 to 10 with a mean of 5.5 and a standard deviation of 2. This scoring metric is the signature of Raymond Cattell's 16PF personality questionnaire.","traps":{"a":"Wechsler scales report deviation IQs (mean 100, SD 15) and scaled subtest scores (mean 10), not stens.","b":"The Stanford-Binet (Lewis Terman, 1916) uses a deviation IQ metric, not the 1 to 10 sten scale.","d":"The Rorschach is a projective inkblot test scored by systems such as Exner's, not by stens."}},{"q":"Administering the same test to the same people on two occasions and correlating the scores assesses:","concept":"Reliability concerns consistency of measurement. Test-retest reliability specifically assesses the temporal stability of scores: the same test is given to the same people on two occasions and the two sets of scores are correlated. A high correlation indicates scores are stable over time.","traps":{"a":"Internal consistency (e.g., Cronbach's alpha) is computed from a single administration by examining item intercorrelations, not two occasions.","b":"Construct validity concerns whether a test measures the intended theoretical construct, a validity question, not consistency over time.","c":"Content validity concerns whether items adequately sample the domain, judged by expert review, not by re-testing."}},{"q":"Loosening of associations and 'knight's move' thinking are characteristic of:","concept":"Formal thought disorder is a disturbance in the form or flow of thought inferred from disorganised speech. Loosening of associations (ideas slipping onto unrelated tracks) and 'knight's move' thinking (illogical jumps between topics) are classic signs, seen most characteristically in schizophrenia. They reflect disorganised thinking rather than mood or anxiety pathology.","traps":{"b":"Major depressive disorder is a mood disorder marked by low mood and anhedonia, not by loosening of associations.","c":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder features intrusive obsessions and compulsions with generally intact thought form.","d":"Specific phobia involves marked fear of a specific object or situation, with no formal thought disorder."}},{"q":"Compared with DSM-IV-TR, the DSM-5 (2013):","concept":"The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, Fifth Edition (DSM-5, 2013) abolished the multiaxial system that DSM-IV-TR used. Former Axes I, II, and III (clinical disorders, personality disorders/intellectual disability, and medical conditions) were merged into a single list of diagnoses, Axis IV became separate notation for psychosocial and contextual factors, and Axis V (the Global Assessment of Functioning) was dropped.","traps":{"a":"DSM-5 did not add a sixth axis; it removed the axial system entirely.","c":"Retaining the five-axis system describes DSM-IV/DSM-IV-TR, not DSM-5.","d":"DSM-5 kept its own diagnostic criteria and did not replace diagnoses with ICD codes only, though it lists corresponding ICD codes."}},{"q":"Anhedonia refers to:","concept":"Anhedonia is the markedly diminished ability to experience interest or pleasure in activities that were previously enjoyable. It is one of the two cardinal (core) symptoms of a major depressive episode, alongside depressed mood, in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual.","traps":{"a":"Reduced need for sleep is a feature of a manic episode, not the definition of anhedonia.","b":"Excessive fear of social situations describes social anxiety disorder, not anhedonia.","d":"Loss of contact with reality describes psychosis, a different construct from the loss of pleasure."}},{"q":"First-rank symptoms of schizophrenia were described by:","concept":"The First-Rank Symptoms of schizophrenia (e.g., audible thoughts, thought insertion/withdrawal/broadcast, delusional perception, passivity phenomena) were described by Kurt Schneider in 1959 as symptoms with strong (though not pathognomonic) diagnostic weight. Other theorists shaped the concept differently: Kraepelin and Bleuler defined the disorder, and Jaspers advanced its phenomenology.","traps":{"a":"Emil Kraepelin coined 'dementia praecox' and its subtypes, not the First-Rank Symptoms.","b":"Eugen Bleuler coined the term 'schizophrenia' and described the four A's (associations, affect, ambivalence, autism), not first-rank symptoms.","c":"Karl Jaspers pioneered descriptive psychopathology and phenomenology but did not formulate the first-rank symptoms."}},{"q":"The minimum duration of symptoms required to diagnose schizophrenia under DSM-5 is:","concept":"Under the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition (DSM-5), a schizophrenia diagnosis requires continuous signs of disturbance for at least 6 months in total (which may include prodromal or residual periods), of which at least 1 month must contain active-phase symptoms. This contrasts with ICD, which requires about 1 month.","traps":{"b":"1 month is the active-phase requirement within DSM-5 and the ICD threshold, not the total duration DSM-5 demands.","c":"1 week is too short and corresponds to no standard schizophrenia criterion (it is closer to a brief psychotic episode boundary).","d":"2 years is not required; it overstates the duration criterion for schizophrenia."}},{"q":"Which cluster of DSM-5 personality disorders is described as 'dramatic, emotional, erratic'?","concept":"The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual groups personality disorders into three clusters. Cluster B, described as dramatic, emotional, and erratic, comprises Antisocial, Borderline, Histrionic, and Narcissistic personality disorders. (Cluster A is odd/eccentric; Cluster C is anxious/fearful.)","traps":{"a":"Cluster A is the 'odd or eccentric' group (Paranoid, Schizoid, Schizotypal), not dramatic/emotional.","c":"Cluster C is the 'anxious or fearful' group (Avoidant, Dependent, Obsessive-Compulsive personality disorders).","d":"There is no Cluster D in the DSM classification; only clusters A, B, and C exist."}},{"q":"Unconditional positive regard, empathy, and congruence are the core conditions described by:","concept":"Carl Rogers, founder of Person-Centred (client-centred) therapy, specified three core conditions he called necessary and sufficient for therapeutic change: unconditional positive regard, empathic understanding, and congruence (genuineness). These describe the therapist's way of being with the client rather than specific techniques.","traps":{"a":"Aaron Beck developed cognitive therapy, focused on identifying and testing automatic thoughts and schemas, not the Rogerian core conditions.","b":"Albert Ellis developed Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy centred on disputing irrational beliefs, not unconditional positive regard.","d":"Fritz Perls developed Gestalt therapy, known for techniques like the empty chair, not the three core conditions."}},{"q":"Systematic desensitization, based on reciprocal inhibition, was developed by:","concept":"Systematic desensitization was developed by Joseph Wolpe. It rests on reciprocal inhibition, the principle that a response incompatible with anxiety (typically deep muscle relaxation) can inhibit anxiety. The client is gradually exposed to a hierarchy of feared stimuli while relaxed, so the feared cues lose their power.","traps":{"a":"B. F. Skinner developed operant conditioning (reinforcement and punishment), not systematic desensitization.","b":"John Watson pioneered behaviourism and the 'Little Albert' fear-conditioning study but did not create systematic desensitization.","c":"Ivan Pavlov discovered classical conditioning, which underlies the phenomenon, but the therapy itself was Wolpe's."}},{"q":"The 'empty chair' technique is most associated with:","concept":"The 'empty chair' (and 'two-chair') technique is a hallmark of Gestalt therapy, developed by Fritz Perls. The client speaks to an imagined person or a disowned part of the self seated in an empty chair, promoting present-moment awareness and integration of conflicting feelings.","traps":{"b":"Psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) uses free association, dream analysis, and transference interpretation, not the empty-chair technique.","c":"Cognitive therapy focuses on identifying and restructuring maladaptive thoughts, not empty-chair dialogue.","d":"Behaviour therapy uses techniques like exposure and reinforcement, not the Gestalt empty chair."}},{"q":"In Ellis's REBT, the 'B' in the ABC model stands for:","concept":"In Albert Ellis's Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT), the ABC model holds that emotional consequences arise not directly from events but from beliefs about them. A is the Activating event, B is the Belief (especially irrational beliefs), and C is the Consequence (emotional or behavioural). Ellis later added D (disputing the belief) and E (the new effect).","traps":{"a":"Behaviour is not what B stands for; the model targets beliefs as the mediator between event and consequence.","c":"Baseline is a measurement concept from behavioural assessment, not a component of the ABC model.","d":"Bias is a general cognitive term, not the specific 'B' (Belief) element in Ellis's model."}},{"q":"Transference and free association are central techniques of:","concept":"Free association (saying whatever comes to mind), dream analysis, transference (redirecting feelings from past figures onto the therapist), and interpretation are the central techniques of psychoanalytic therapy founded by Sigmund Freud. They aim to make unconscious conflicts conscious.","traps":{"a":"Behaviour therapy uses conditioning-based methods such as exposure and reinforcement, not free association or transference.","b":"Person-centred therapy (Carl Rogers) relies on the therapist's core conditions (empathy, congruence, unconditional positive regard), not transference work.","d":"Reality therapy, developed by William Glasser, focuses on present choices and responsibility, not free association."}},{"q":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha Linehan primarily to treat:","concept":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy (DBT) was developed by Marsha Linehan primarily to treat borderline personality disorder and chronic suicidal/self-harming behaviour. It blends cognitive-behavioural change strategies with acceptance and mindfulness, organised around modules of mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness.","traps":{"a":"Schizophrenia is treated mainly with antipsychotics and psychosocial rehabilitation, not DBT as its primary indication.","b":"Autism spectrum disorder is addressed with behavioural and developmental interventions, not DBT.","c":"Specific phobias are treated with exposure-based behaviour therapy, not DBT."}},{"q":"Object permanence, according to Piaget, first develops during the:","concept":"Object permanence is the understanding that objects continue to exist even when they cannot be seen. According to Jean Piaget, it first develops during the sensorimotor stage (roughly birth to 2 years), as infants gradually learn to represent absent objects, shown by searching for a hidden toy.","traps":{"b":"The preoperational stage (about 2 to 7 years) is marked by symbolic thought and egocentrism, but object permanence is already established before it.","c":"The concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years) brings conservation and logical operations on concrete material, well after object permanence.","d":"The formal operational stage (about 11 years onward) introduces abstract and hypothetical reasoning, long after object permanence emerges."}},{"q":"In Erikson's theory, the psychosocial crisis of adolescence is:","concept":"Erik Erikson proposed eight psychosocial stages, each defined by a central crisis. Adolescence corresponds to the fifth stage, Identity versus Role Confusion, in which the person works to form a coherent sense of self and future direction; failure leaves confusion about one's roles and values.","traps":{"a":"Trust versus mistrust is Erikson's first stage, occurring in infancy, not adolescence.","c":"Industry versus inferiority is the fourth stage, spanning the school-age years before adolescence.","d":"Intimacy versus isolation is the sixth stage, the crisis of young adulthood, not adolescence."}},{"q":"The 'Strange Situation' procedure for assessing attachment was devised by:","concept":"The 'Strange Situation' is a structured laboratory procedure devised by Mary Ainsworth to assess infant attachment through a series of separations and reunions with the caregiver. It classified infants into secure, anxious-avoidant, and anxious-resistant (ambivalent) patterns; a disorganised category was added later by Mary Main.","traps":{"a":"John Bowlby founded attachment theory and its ethological framework but did not design the Strange Situation procedure.","b":"Harry Harlow conducted the rhesus-monkey surrogate-mother studies on contact comfort, not the Strange Situation.","d":"Konrad Lorenz studied imprinting in geese, an ethological phenomenon distinct from the Strange Situation."}},{"q":"The 'Zone of Proximal Development' is a concept proposed by:","concept":"The Zone of Proximal Development (ZPD) was proposed by Lev Vygotsky. It is the gap between what a learner can accomplish independently and what they can achieve with guidance from a more knowledgeable other. Support tailored to this zone, later termed 'scaffolding', is gradually withdrawn as competence grows.","traps":{"a":"Jean Piaget emphasised individual, stage-based cognitive construction, not the socially mediated ZPD.","b":"Lawrence Kohlberg developed a theory of moral development stages, unrelated to the ZPD.","c":"Jerome Bruner popularised the term 'scaffolding' and modes of representation but did not originate the ZPD concept."}},{"q":"The tendency for individuals to be less likely to help when others are present is the:","concept":"The bystander effect is the finding that people are less likely to help a victim when other people are present, largely because responsibility is diffused across the group and each person assumes someone else will act. It was studied experimentally by John Darley and Bibb Latané following the Kitty Genovese case.","traps":{"b":"Social facilitation (Norman Triplett, 1898) is the improvement of performance on simple tasks in the presence of others, not reduced helping.","c":"The halo effect (Edward Thorndike, 1920) is a bias where one positive trait colours overall impressions of a person, unrelated to helping behaviour.","d":"The just-world phenomenon (Melvin Lerner) is the belief that people get what they deserve, which can lead to victim-blaming, not diffusion of responsibility."}},{"q":"The theory of cognitive dissonance was proposed by:","concept":"The theory of cognitive dissonance was proposed by Leon Festinger in 1957. It holds that holding two inconsistent cognitions (beliefs, attitudes, or a belief and a behaviour) creates uncomfortable tension, which motivates the person to reduce it by changing an attitude, adding consonant beliefs, or minimising the inconsistency.","traps":{"a":"Stanley Milgram is known for the obedience-to-authority experiments, not cognitive dissonance.","c":"Solomon Asch is known for the conformity line-judgement experiments, not dissonance theory.","d":"Philip Zimbardo conducted the Stanford Prison Experiment on situational roles, not cognitive dissonance."}},{"q":"Overestimating dispositional causes and underestimating situational causes of others' behaviour is the:","concept":"The fundamental attribution error, described by Lee Ross (1977), is the tendency to explain other people's behaviour by their internal traits or personality (dispositional causes) while discounting the power of the situation they are in. If someone cuts you off in traffic, you decide they are rude rather than considering they might be rushing to a hospital. It reflects a systematic bias in how observers attribute the causes of others' actions.","traps":{"a":"Self-serving bias is taking personal credit for successes but blaming external factors for failures, which protects one's own self-esteem rather than judging others' behaviour.","b":"Actor-observer bias (Edward Jones and Richard Nisbett, 1971) is the broader pattern that we attribute our OWN behaviour to the situation but others' behaviour to their disposition, whereas this question is specifically about the disposition-over-situation error for others.","d":"Confirmation bias (Peter Wason, 1960) is seeking or interpreting information so it confirms one's pre-existing beliefs, a reasoning bias unrelated to attributing causes of behaviour."}},{"q":"The classic line-judgment experiments demonstrating conformity to group pressure were conducted by:","concept":"Solomon Asch's line-judgment experiments (1951) had participants match the length of a line to comparison lines while surrounded by confederates who deliberately gave the same wrong answer. About one third of responses conformed to the incorrect majority, demonstrating normative social influence, the pressure to go along with a group to fit in even when one privately knows it is wrong.","traps":{"a":"Stanley Milgram is known for the obedience-to-authority studies (shock experiments), which measured compliance with an authority figure, not conformity to peers.","b":"Philip Zimbardo ran the Stanford Prison Experiment on how situational roles shape behaviour, not perceptual conformity.","c":"Muzafer Sherif used the autokinetic effect to study the formation of group norms in an ambiguous task and the Robbers Cave study of intergroup conflict, not the unambiguous line-judgment conformity paradigm."}},{"q":"Parkinson's disease is primarily associated with a deficiency of which neurotransmitter?","concept":"Parkinson's disease results primarily from the degeneration of dopamine-producing neurons in the substantia nigra, which projects along the nigrostriatal pathway to the basal ganglia. Loss of this dopamine disrupts the control of movement, producing the classic triad of resting tremor, rigidity, and bradykinesia. This is why treatment relies on the dopamine precursor levodopa.","traps":{"b":"Serotonin deficiency is more associated with depression and anxiety disorders, not the motor degeneration of Parkinson's disease.","c":"Acetylcholine loss is centrally implicated in Alzheimer's disease; in Parkinson's the relative excess of acetylcholine activity is a downstream consequence, not the primary deficiency.","d":"GABA is the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter and is linked to anxiety and seizure disorders, not the dopaminergic loss defining Parkinson's."}},{"q":"The 'rest and digest' functions of the body are governed by the:","concept":"The parasympathetic nervous system is the branch of the autonomic nervous system responsible for 'rest and digest' functions: it slows the heart rate, stimulates digestion, and conserves the body's energy when it is at ease. It works in opposition to the sympathetic system, which mobilizes the body for 'fight or flight' (Walter Cannon). Both are subdivisions of the autonomic nervous system.","traps":{"a":"The sympathetic nervous system does the opposite, mobilizing the body for 'fight or flight' by raising heart rate and releasing adrenaline.","c":"The somatic nervous system controls voluntary skeletal muscle movement and carries sensory information, not the involuntary visceral 'rest and digest' functions.","d":"The central nervous system is the brain and spinal cord as a whole; 'rest and digest' is governed specifically by an autonomic (peripheral) branch, not the CNS at large."}},{"q":"Damage to Broca's area typically results in:","concept":"Broca's area lies in the inferior frontal gyrus of the (usually left) frontal lobe and is critical for speech production. Damage causes Broca's (expressive/non-fluent) aphasia: speech is slow, effortful, and agrammatic (missing small connecting words), yet the person can still largely understand what is said to them. The dissociation between poor output and preserved comprehension is the diagnostic clue.","traps":{"a":"Fluent speech with poor comprehension describes Wernicke's (receptive) aphasia from temporal-lobe damage, the near-opposite of Broca's.","b":"Complete loss of hearing is a sensory/auditory deficit, not a language-production disorder produced by frontal-lobe damage.","d":"Inability to recognize faces is prosopagnosia, caused by damage to the fusiform face area in the occipitotemporal region, unrelated to Broca's area."}},{"q":"The hippocampus is most critical for:","concept":"The hippocampus, in the medial temporal lobe, is essential for consolidating new declarative (episodic and semantic) long-term memories, converting short-term experiences into durable stored memories. The famous patient H.M. (studied by William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957), whose hippocampi were removed, could no longer form new long-term memories (anterograde amnesia) though older memories and skills survived. This shows the hippocampus builds new memories rather than storing all of them permanently.","traps":{"a":"Regulating body temperature is a function of the hypothalamus, not the hippocampus.","b":"Controlling balance and coordination is the role of the cerebellum.","c":"Producing speech is governed by Broca's area in the frontal lobe, not the hippocampus."}},{"q":"The working memory model with a phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, and central executive was proposed by:","concept":"Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch (1974) proposed the working memory model, replacing the passive short-term store with an active system. It comprises a central executive (an attentional controller) that directs two slave systems: the phonological loop for verbal and acoustic information and the visuospatial sketchpad for visual and spatial information; Baddeley later added the episodic buffer (2000).","traps":{"b":"Atkinson and Shiffrin (1968) proposed the multi-store (modal) model of sensory, short-term, and long-term memory, not the multi-component working memory model.","c":"Endel Tulving distinguished episodic from semantic long-term memory, a different contribution to memory theory.","d":"George Sperling studied the capacity and rapid decay of iconic (visual sensory) memory using partial-report experiments, not working memory structure."}},{"q":"When previously learned information interferes with the recall of newly learned information, it is called:","concept":"Proactive interference occurs when previously learned (older) information disrupts the recall of newly learned information; the learning moves forward in time to interfere. For example, an old phone number keeps intruding when you try to recall a new one. It contrasts with retroactive interference, where new learning disrupts the recall of old material.","traps":{"a":"Retroactive interference is the reverse: NEW learning interferes with the recall of OLD information (it works backward).","c":"Decay theory attributes forgetting to the fading of a memory trace over time through disuse, not to interference from other information.","d":"Encoding failure is forgetting because the information was never properly stored in the first place, not competition between two learned sets."}},{"q":"George Miller's classic estimate of the capacity of short-term memory is:","concept":"George Miller's 1956 paper 'The Magical Number Seven, Plus or Minus Two' estimated that short-term memory can hold about 7 plus or minus 2 (roughly 5 to 9) items at once. Crucially, the unit of capacity is the 'chunk', a meaningful grouping, so chunking can pack more raw information into each of those slots. This defines the limited capacity of short-term memory.","traps":{"a":"3 plus or minus 1 items is closer to Nelson Cowan's later, more conservative estimate of working memory capacity, not Miller's classic figure.","b":"12 plus or minus 3 items overstates short-term capacity well beyond any accepted estimate.","d":"Unlimited capacity describes long-term memory, not the sharply limited short-term store."}},{"q":"Negative reinforcement involves:","concept":"Negative reinforcement, an operant conditioning concept from B. F. Skinner, means removing or avoiding an aversive stimulus so that the behaviour that ended it becomes MORE likely to recur. Taking an aspirin to stop a headache reinforces pill-taking because the unpleasant pain is removed. 'Negative' refers to taking something away, not to punishment, and reinforcement always increases behaviour.","traps":{"a":"Presenting an aversive stimulus to decrease behaviour is positive punishment, not negative reinforcement.","b":"Removing a reward to decrease behaviour is negative punishment (for example a time-out or a fine).","c":"Presenting a reward to increase behaviour is positive reinforcement, which adds a pleasant stimulus rather than removing an aversive one."}},{"q":"The 'Little Albert' experiment, demonstrating the conditioning of a fear response, was conducted by:","concept":"John B. Watson, the founder of behaviourism, together with Rosalie Rayner conducted the 'Little Albert' experiment in 1920. They paired a white rat (a neutral stimulus) with a loud, frightening noise until the infant, Albert, came to fear the rat alone, demonstrating that emotional responses like fear can be classically conditioned. It applied Pavlovian principles to human emotion.","traps":{"b":"B. F. Skinner is associated with operant conditioning (the Skinner box and reinforcement), not the classical conditioning of Little Albert.","c":"Edward Thorndike formulated the law of effect using puzzle-box experiments with cats, a precursor to operant conditioning.","d":"Ivan Pavlov discovered classical conditioning with dogs and salivation; Watson applied Pavlov's principles to Little Albert, but Pavlov did not run the study."}},{"q":"Learning that occurs without reinforcement and is not demonstrated until there is an incentive (shown in Tolman's rat maze studies) is called:","concept":"Latent learning, demonstrated by Edward Tolman with rats in mazes, is learning that occurs without any reinforcement and remains hidden until an incentive gives the animal a reason to show it. Rats allowed to explore a maze with no reward later ran it quickly once food was introduced, revealing they had learned the layout all along. Tolman argued they had formed a cognitive map, challenging strict stimulus-response behaviourism (John B. Watson, 1913).","traps":{"a":"Insight learning, studied by Wolfgang Kohler with chimpanzees, is the sudden 'aha' grasp of a solution, not learning that stays hidden until rewarded.","c":"Observational learning, associated with Albert Bandura, is acquiring behaviour by watching a model, not the unreinforced latent learning of Tolman's mazes.","d":"Vicarious learning refers to learning from the consequences experienced by others (a facet of Bandura's social learning), not from unrewarded personal exploration."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the Big Five (OCEAN) personality traits?","concept":"The Big Five, remembered by the acronym OCEAN, are the five broad dimensions of personality supported by factor analysis: Openness to experience, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism. Intelligence is a cognitive ability measured separately and is not one of these trait dimensions. The model is one of the most empirically robust frameworks in personality psychology.","traps":{"a":"Openness to experience is a genuine Big Five trait (the 'O' in OCEAN), so it cannot be the exception.","b":"Conscientiousness is a genuine Big Five trait (the 'C' in OCEAN).","d":"Neuroticism is a genuine Big Five trait (the 'N' in OCEAN), capturing emotional instability."}},{"q":"In Freud's structural model, the part of the personality that operates on the 'pleasure principle' is the:","concept":"In Sigmund Freud's structural model of personality, the id is the primitive, unconscious reservoir of drives that operates on the pleasure principle, demanding immediate gratification of instincts regardless of reality or morality. The ego mediates using the reality principle, and the superego supplies moral standards. The id is present from birth and is entirely unconscious.","traps":{"a":"The ego operates on the reality principle, negotiating between the id's demands and external constraints, not on the pleasure principle.","b":"The superego embodies morality and conscience (the internalized rules of parents and society), the opposite of the pleasure-seeking id.","c":"The preconscious is a level of awareness (material that can be brought to mind), not a structural component driven by the pleasure principle."}},{"q":"The Rehabilitation Council of India (RCI) was established as a statutory body under the RCI Act of:","concept":"The Rehabilitation Council of India was established as a statutory body under the Rehabilitation Council of India Act, 1992. The Act makes registration with the RCI mandatory in order to practise as a clinical psychologist or other rehabilitation professional, and it regulates training standards and maintains a Central Rehabilitation Register. Practising without registration is a punishable offence.","traps":{"b":"1987 is the year of the old Mental Health Act, not the RCI Act.","c":"2002 is not the founding year of the RCI; no core disability-professional statute of that year applies here.","d":"2016 is the year of the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, a different piece of legislation from the RCI Act."}},{"q":"The concept of an 'Advance Directive' and a 'Nominated Representative' for mental healthcare in India comes from the:","concept":"The Mental Healthcare Act, 2017 introduced the rights-based provisions of an advance directive (letting a person state in advance how they wish to be treated if they lose capacity) and a nominated representative to act on their behalf. It also affirmed the right to access mental healthcare and effectively decriminalized attempted suicide. It replaced the older Mental Health Act, 1987.","traps":{"a":"The Mental Health Act, 1987 was the earlier, more custodial law that the 2017 Act repealed; it contained no advance directives or nominated representatives.","c":"The Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016 addresses disability rights broadly, not the specific advance-directive and nominated-representative mechanisms of mental healthcare.","d":"The RCI Act, 1992 regulates the training and registration of rehabilitation professionals, not patients' advance directives."}},{"q":"The 'scientist-practitioner' model of clinical psychology training (Ph.D.) originated at the 1949 conference held at:","concept":"The scientist-practitioner (Boulder) model of clinical psychology training was formulated at the 1949 Boulder Conference in Boulder, Colorado. It holds that clinical psychologists should be trained both as researchers who generate science and as practitioners who apply it, and it is associated with the Ph.D. degree. A later 1973 Vail Conference produced the practitioner-scholar (Vail) model tied to the Psy.D.","traps":{"a":"Vail (1973) gave rise to the practitioner-scholar model and the Psy.D., not the 1949 scientist-practitioner model.","b":"Salt Lake City is a distractor location not associated with either founding training-model conference.","d":"Adelphi is linked to early professional (Psy.D.-style) training programs, not the 1949 Boulder scientist-practitioner conference."}},{"q":"The legal principle that a therapist has a 'duty to warn/protect' an identifiable third party from a client's threats stems from:","concept":"The duty to warn or protect an identifiable third party stems from Tarasoff v. Regents of the University of California (1976), following the killing of Tatiana Tarasoff. The court ruled that when a therapist determines a client poses a serious danger to an identifiable victim, the therapist has a duty to take reasonable steps to protect that person, creating a recognized limit to client confidentiality.","traps":{"a":"The Durham rule is an insanity-defence standard (the 'product' test), concerning criminal responsibility, not a therapist's duty to warn.","b":"The Daubert standard governs the admissibility of expert scientific testimony in court, unrelated to protecting third parties.","c":"The M'Naghten rule is the classic 'right from wrong' test for the insanity defence, again about criminal responsibility rather than confidentiality limits."}},{"q":"'Dhat syndrome', characterized by anxiety over the loss of semen, is a culture-bound syndrome reported in:","concept":"Dhat syndrome is a culture-bound syndrome reported in the Indian subcontinent (South Asia), in which men experience anxiety, fatigue, and somatic complaints that they attribute to the loss of semen (dhatu), believed to be a vital bodily essence. It illustrates how cultural beliefs shape the presentation of distress. It is recognized in cultural formulations of illness such as those in the ICD and DSM.","traps":{"b":"Malaysia is the setting for culture-bound syndromes such as amok and latah, not dhat.","c":"Latin America is associated with syndromes like susto and ataque de nervios, not semen-loss anxiety.","d":"The Arctic is linked to pibloktoq (Arctic hysteria) among Inuit populations, not dhat syndrome."}},{"q":"In India, the Act that provides for the protection of children from sexual offences is the POCSO Act of:","concept":"The Protection of Children from Sexual Offences (POCSO) Act was enacted in 2012 to protect children from sexual assault, harassment, and pornography. It is gender-neutral, defines a child as any person below 18 years of age, and provides for child-friendly procedures for reporting, recording evidence, and trial through Special Courts. It filled major gaps in earlier laws on child sexual abuse.","traps":{"a":"2005 is the year of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, not POCSO.","c":"2009 is the year of the Right to Education Act, unrelated to child sexual offences.","d":"2015 is the year of the Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act, a distinct statute from POCSO."}},{"q":"In the Mental Status Examination (MSE), 'insight' refers to:","concept":"In the Mental Status Examination, 'insight' refers to the patient's awareness that they are ill and their understanding of the nature and cause of that illness. It is typically graded along a spectrum from complete denial of illness to intellectual and finally true emotional insight, and it has bearing on treatment adherence and prognosis. It is distinct from orientation, cognition, and perception, which are assessed separately.","traps":{"a":"Orientation to time, place, and person is a separate MSE component (part of the cognition/sensorium assessment), not insight.","b":"Serial subtraction (for example serial sevens) tests attention and concentration, a cognitive function distinct from insight.","d":"The presence of hallucinations falls under the perception section of the MSE, not insight."}},{"q":"The first psychological clinic and the term 'clinical psychology' are credited to:","concept":"Lightner Witmer founded the first psychological clinic at the University of Pennsylvania in 1896 and coined the term 'clinical psychology'. His clinic focused on assessing and helping children with learning and behavioural difficulties, marking the applied, individual-case beginnings of the discipline. He is therefore regarded as the founder of clinical psychology.","traps":{"a":"Sigmund Freud founded psychoanalysis, a theory and therapy of the unconscious, not the field of clinical psychology or its first clinic.","b":"Wilhelm Wundt founded the first experimental psychology laboratory (Leipzig, 1879), which was for research, not clinical service.","c":"G. Stanley Hall was a pioneer of developmental and educational psychology and the first APA president, but he did not coin 'clinical psychology'."}},{"q":"The founder of psychophysics, who studied the relationship between physical stimuli and sensations, is:","concept":"Gustav Fechner is regarded as the founder of psychophysics, the study of the relationship between physical stimuli and the sensations they produce. In his Elements of Psychophysics (1860) he built on Ernst Weber's findings to formulate the Weber-Fechner law, showing that sensation grows as the logarithm of stimulus intensity. This gave psychology one of its first quantitative methods.","traps":{"b":"Wilhelm Wundt founded the first psychology laboratory and structuralism-oriented experimental psychology, not psychophysics as a field.","c":"Ernst Weber discovered the just-noticeable-difference and Weber's law, providing the foundation Fechner extended, but Fechner is credited as the founder of psychophysics.","d":"Hermann Ebbinghaus pioneered the experimental study of memory and forgetting (the forgetting curve), not psychophysics."}},{"q":"The first experimental psychology laboratory (Leipzig, 1879) was established by:","concept":"Wilhelm Wundt established the first laboratory dedicated to experimental psychology at the University of Leipzig in 1879, an event conventionally treated as the birth of psychology as an independent scientific discipline. He used methods such as introspection to study conscious experience and trained many early psychologists. This separated psychology from philosophy and physiology.","traps":{"a":"William James was a founder of American psychology and functionalism, but he did not establish the first experimental laboratory at Leipzig.","c":"Edward Titchener was Wundt's student who brought structuralism to America; he did not found the original Leipzig lab.","d":"Sigmund Freud developed psychoanalysis clinically in Vienna and had no role in founding the first experimental psychology laboratory."}},{"q":"Functionalism, emphasizing the purpose of mental processes in adaptation, is most associated with:","concept":"Functionalism is the school of psychology, most associated with William James, that emphasized the purpose or function of mental processes in helping an organism adapt to its environment, drawing heavily on Charles Darwin's evolutionary thinking. Rather than dissecting consciousness into elements, it asked what mind and behaviour are for. James's 'Principles of Psychology' (1890) was its landmark text.","traps":{"a":"Wilhelm Wundt is linked to the experimental study of the structure of consciousness, not the adaptive-function emphasis of functionalism.","b":"Edward Titchener championed structuralism, which broke conscious experience into basic elements, the rival school to functionalism.","d":"John Watson founded behaviourism, which rejected the study of mental processes altogether in favour of observable behaviour."}},{"q":"The Gestalt principle stating that objects close together are perceived as a group is the law of:","concept":"The Gestalt law of proximity states that elements which are close together in space are perceived as belonging to the same group. It is one of the Gestalt grouping principles showing that the mind organizes perception into wholes rather than isolated parts, following the idea that 'the whole is different from the sum of its parts'. Spacing alone can create the impression of distinct clusters.","traps":{"a":"The law of similarity groups elements by shared features such as colour or shape, not by their nearness.","b":"The law of continuity (good continuation) makes us perceive smooth, continuous lines rather than abrupt breaks, not grouping by closeness.","c":"The law of closure fills in gaps so an incomplete figure is seen as whole, a different grouping principle from proximity."}},{"q":"When participants change their behaviour simply because they know they are being observed/studied, this is the:","concept":"The Hawthorne effect (Hawthorne studies by Elton Mayo and colleagues, 1924-1932; term by Henry Landsberger, 1958) is the tendency for people to change or improve their behaviour simply because they know they are being observed or studied, regardless of the actual experimental manipulation. It is named after the Western Electric Hawthorne Works studies, where worker productivity rose with almost any change in conditions. It is an important threat to the internal validity of research.","traps":{"b":"The placebo effect is improvement caused by the expectation of treatment when receiving an inactive substance, not by being observed.","c":"The halo effect (Edward Thorndike, 1920) is letting one positive trait bias overall judgment of a person, a social-perception bias unrelated to being watched.","d":"The Pygmalion effect (Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson, 1968) is when higher expectations from others (for example teachers) lead to improved performance, not a reaction to observation itself."}},{"q":"At the apex of Maslow's hierarchy of needs is:","concept":"At the apex of Abraham Maslow's hierarchy of needs, in the classic five-level pyramid, is self-actualization: the drive to realize one's full potential and become everything one is capable of becoming. Lower needs (physiological, safety, belongingness, esteem) must be reasonably met before this growth need is pursued. It represents the fulfilment of one's unique talents and purpose.","traps":{"a":"Esteem needs sit just below self-actualization in the pyramid, not at its apex.","c":"Safety needs are near the base of the hierarchy, second from the bottom, far below the top.","d":"Belongingness (love) needs occupy the middle of the hierarchy, below esteem and self-actualization."}},{"q":"The extent to which a test score predicts performance on a future criterion is called:","concept":"Predictive validity is the degree to which a test score forecasts performance on a FUTURE criterion measured later in time, for example an entrance exam predicting later academic success. It is one form of criterion-related validity. Its defining feature is the time gap between administering the test and collecting the outcome it is meant to predict.","traps":{"a":"Content validity is how well a test's items sample the full domain it is meant to cover, not its ability to forecast a future outcome.","b":"Concurrent validity correlates test scores with a criterion measured at the SAME time, not a future one.","d":"Face validity is merely whether a test appears to measure what it claims on the surface, the weakest and most superficial form of validity."}},{"q":"The two-factor theory of intelligence, proposing a general factor 'g' and specific factors 's', was given by:","concept":"Charles Spearman proposed the two-factor theory of intelligence, arguing that performance on any mental task reflects a general intelligence factor 'g' common to all tasks plus a specific factor 's' unique to that particular task. He derived this using factor analysis, a statistical technique he helped pioneer, after noticing that scores on diverse cognitive tests tend to correlate positively.","traps":{"a":"L. L. Thurstone rejected a single 'g' and proposed seven primary mental abilities instead of a two-factor structure.","b":"J. P. Guilford proposed the Structure of Intellect model with up to 120 or more distinct factors, not Spearman's two factors.","c":"Howard Gardner proposed the theory of multiple intelligences (linguistic, musical, spatial, and so on), a modern pluralistic view opposed to a single general factor."}}],"bio-testing-paper":[{"q":"The 12 cranial nerves in order from I to XII begin with which sensory nerve, the only one that does NOT pass through the thalamus before reaching its primary cortex?","concept":"The olfactory nerve (Cranial Nerve I) is the only sensory system whose afferents reach the cerebral cortex without first relaying in the thalamus: olfactory receptor axons synapse in the olfactory bulb, then project directly via the olfactory tract to the piriform (primary olfactory) cortex, amygdala, and entorhinal cortex. Every other sensory modality (vision, hearing, somatosensation, taste) synapses in a thalamic nucleus before reaching its primary cortex. This direct, phylogenetically old routing is why smell has such immediate links to emotion and memory.","traps":{"b":"The trigeminal nerve (V) carries facial somatosensation and does relay through the ventral posteromedial nucleus of the thalamus, so it is not the thalamus-bypassing pathway.","c":"The optic nerve (II) relays in the lateral geniculate nucleus of the thalamus before reaching the primary visual cortex, so vision does pass through the thalamus.","d":"The vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) sends auditory signals through the medial geniculate nucleus of the thalamus to the auditory cortex, so hearing is thalamically relayed."}},{"q":"The pyramidal (corticospinal) tract carries voluntary motor commands. Where does it decussate?","concept":"The pyramidal (corticospinal) tract carries voluntary motor commands from the motor cortex to lower motor neurons. About 80 to 90 percent of its fibres cross the midline at the pyramidal decussation in the lower (caudal) medulla, forming the lateral corticospinal tract that then descends on the contralateral side to synapse on anterior-horn motor neurons. This is why a lesion above the medulla produces contralateral weakness. The remaining 10 to 15 percent stay ipsilateral as the anterior corticospinal tract and decussate only at their spinal segmental level.","traps":{"a":"The third ventricle is a fluid-filled cavity in the diencephalon, not a site of motor fibre crossing; no tract decussates there.","b":"Claiming no decussation contradicts the defining feature of the corticospinal system and would wrongly predict ipsilateral rather than contralateral hemiplegia after a cortical lesion.","c":"The mid-pons is where corticopontine fibres synapse en route to the cerebellum, not where the corticospinal tract crosses; the crossing is lower, in the medulla."}},{"q":"The direct pathway through the basal ganglia is most accurately described as:","concept":"The direct pathway of the basal ganglia facilitates movement. Cortex excites striatal neurons bearing D1 receptors, which inhibit the internal globus pallidus and substantia nigra pars reticulata (GPi/SNr); because GPi/SNr normally inhibit the thalamus, this inhibition disinhibits the thalamus, which then drives the cortex and promotes movement. Dopamine from the substantia nigra pars compacta reinforces this by exciting the D1 direct pathway while inhibiting the D2 indirect pathway. Loss of dopamine impairs the direct pathway (Parkinsonian hypokinesia); overactivity produces hyperkinetic chorea.","traps":{"a":"This describes the indirect pathway (D2 receptors, external GP, subthalamic nucleus) whose net effect inhibits movement, not the movement-facilitating direct pathway.","c":"Hippocampus to fornix to thalamus is the Papez memory circuit, not a basal ganglia motor loop.","d":"The cortico-cerebellar loop coordinates movement but always relays through the thalamus back to cortex, so 'no thalamic relay' is wrong and it is not the basal ganglia direct pathway."}},{"q":"MAO-A and MAO-B enzymes principally metabolise:","concept":"Monoamine oxidase (MAO) enzymes catabolise monoamine neurotransmitters. MAO-A preferentially degrades serotonin, norepinephrine, dopamine, and dietary tyramine, and predominates in the gut and liver; MAO-B preferentially breaks down dopamine and phenylethylamine and predominates in the brain. This is why monoamine oxidase inhibitors raise monoamine levels and act as antidepressants, why selective MAO-B inhibitors (selegiline, rasagiline) help Parkinson's, and why tyramine plus a non-selective MAOI can trigger a hypertensive crisis.","traps":{"b":"GABA is inactivated mainly by GABA transaminase and reuptake, not by monoamine oxidase, since it is an amino-acid transmitter, not a monoamine.","c":"Glutamate and glycine are amino-acid transmitters cleared by transporters and other enzymes, not substrates of MAO.","d":"Acetylcholine is broken down by acetylcholinesterase, an entirely different enzyme from monoamine oxidase."}},{"q":"Astrocytes perform all of the following functions EXCEPT:","concept":"Astrocytes are the principal supporting glia of the central nervous system, but they do not conduct action propagating action potentials; they lack the density of propagating voltage-gated sodium channels needed and instead signal slowly via calcium waves. Their genuine roles include maintaining the blood-brain barrier through perivascular end-feet, buffering extracellular potassium, taking up and recycling glutamate through the glutamate-glutamine cycle, providing metabolic substrates, and forming glial scars. Conducting action potentials is the job of neurons.","traps":{"a":"Recycling glutamate via the glutamate-glutamine cycle is a genuine astrocyte function, so it cannot be the exception.","b":"Maintaining the blood-brain barrier with end-feet on capillaries is a real astrocyte role, so it is not the exception.","d":"Potassium buffering and supplying metabolic substrates to neurons are established astrocyte functions, so this is not the exception."}},{"q":"Microglia function chiefly as:","concept":"Microglia are the resident macrophages and immune cells of the central nervous system, derived from yolk-sac progenitors rather than neural tissue. They perform immune surveillance, phagocytose debris and pathogens, release cytokines, and prune synapses (especially during development). Activated microglia drive neuroinflammation and are implicated in Alzheimer's disease, multiple sclerosis, and chronic pain. Myelination is handled by oligodendrocytes in the CNS and Schwann cells in the periphery.","traps":{"a":"Myelin in the CNS is made by oligodendrocytes, not microglia, so this describes the wrong glial cell.","b":"Potassium buffering around neurons is a hallmark astrocyte function, not a microglial one.","d":"Sensing voltage is a property of neuronal ion channels, not a function of microglia."}},{"q":"Mirror neurons, first described in macaque ventral premotor cortex (area F5), fire when:","concept":"Mirror neurons, first described by Rizzolatti, Gallese, di Pellegrino and colleagues in macaque ventral premotor cortex (area F5) in the 1990s, fire both when the animal performs a goal-directed action and when it observes another individual performing the same action. This dual firing has been proposed as a neural substrate for action understanding, imitation, and empathy, with a putative human mirror system in the inferior frontal gyrus, inferior parietal lobule, and superior temporal sulcus. The correct option captures this defining execution-plus-observation property.","traps":{"b":"Firing only during self-executed action describes ordinary motor neurons, missing the observation component that defines mirror neurons.","c":"Firing only during observation describes purely visual or perceptual neurons, missing the execution component of mirror neurons.","d":"Restricting activity to REM sleep is unrelated to mirror neurons, which are defined by waking action and observation."}},{"q":"The locus coeruleus is the principal source of which neurotransmitter in the CNS?","concept":"The locus coeruleus, a small pigmented nucleus in the dorsal pons near the fourth ventricle, is the principal source of norepinephrine in the central nervous system, sending widespread projections to the cortex, hippocampus, and cerebellum. It governs arousal, attention, and the stress response. Knowing the other principal sources helps: the ventral tegmental area and substantia nigra supply dopamine, the raphe nuclei supply serotonin, and the nucleus basalis of Meynert and medial septum supply acetylcholine.","traps":{"b":"Serotonin originates from the raphe nuclei along the brainstem midline, not from the locus coeruleus.","c":"Acetylcholine in the CNS comes chiefly from the nucleus basalis of Meynert and medial septum, not the locus coeruleus.","d":"Dopamine arises mainly from the ventral tegmental area and substantia nigra pars compacta, not the locus coeruleus."}},{"q":"The raphe nuclei are arranged along which axis and are the major source of:","concept":"The raphe nuclei are clusters of serotonergic neurons arranged along the midline of the brainstem and are the major source of serotonin (5-HT) projections in the brain; the rostral group projects to the forebrain while the caudal group projects to the spinal cord. They modulate mood, sleep, appetite, and pain, and the dorsal raphe is the largest of them. Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors act downstream of these projections at serotonergic synapses.","traps":{"a":"The cerebellum and acetylcholine have nothing to do with the raphe nuclei, which are brainstem serotonergic clusters.","b":"The raphe nuclei lie along the brainstem, not the spinal cord, and release serotonin rather than dopamine.","d":"Oxytocin comes from hypothalamic neurons (paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei), not from the raphe nuclei."}},{"q":"The hypothalamus connects to the anterior pituitary primarily via:","concept":"The anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis) is controlled by releasing and inhibiting hormones that the hypothalamus secretes into the hypothalamo-hypophyseal portal venous system at the median eminence; these hormones (including CRH, TRH, GnRH, GHRH, somatostatin, and dopamine) travel through blood to reach the gland. This vascular link contrasts with the posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis), which receives direct axons from the paraventricular and supraoptic nuclei that release oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone straight into the circulation.","traps":{"a":"Cerebrospinal fluid is not the route by which hypothalamic hormones reach the anterior pituitary; a dedicated portal blood system is.","b":"Direct neural axons connect the hypothalamus to the posterior pituitary, not the anterior pituitary, which is controlled hormonally.","d":"The vagus nerve is a parasympathetic nerve to the viscera and has no role in anterior pituitary control."}},{"q":"Pre-ganglionic sympathetic neurons originate from spinal cord segments:","concept":"Preganglionic sympathetic neurons arise from the thoracolumbar spinal cord, specifically segments T1 to L2, where their cell bodies sit in the intermediolateral cell column. Sympathetic preganglionic fibres are relatively short (synapsing in nearby sympathetic chain ganglia) with long postganglionic fibres. This contrasts with the parasympathetic (craniosacral) outflow from cranial nerves III, VII, IX, and X plus sacral segments S2 to S4. The adrenal medulla is a modified sympathetic ganglion releasing epinephrine into the blood.","traps":{"a":"Cervical levels contain no lateral horn for sympathetic outflow; the sympathetic origin is thoracolumbar (T1 to L2).","c":"Cranial nerves carry parasympathetic (and other) fibres, not the sympathetic preganglionic outflow.","d":"S2 to S4 is the sacral parasympathetic outflow, not the sympathetic origin."}},{"q":"The somatosensory homunculus on the postcentral gyrus shows that the largest cortical representation belongs to:","concept":"Penfield's somatosensory homunculus on the postcentral gyrus is grossly distorted: body parts with dense sensory receptors and fine discriminative ability, namely the hand, lips, and tongue, occupy disproportionately large cortical territory, while the trunk and proximal limbs occupy small areas. This cortical magnification reflects receptor density and functional importance rather than physical skin area, which is why our fingertips and mouth are so sensitive.","traps":{"a":"The trunk and back have sparse sensory innervation and therefore a small, not large, cortical representation.","b":"The knee and hip are proximal, low-acuity regions with modest cortical territory.","c":"The dorsum of the foot has low receptor density and only a small cortical area, unlike the hand and face."}},{"q":"Parvocellular vs magnocellular layers of the LGN differ in that:","concept":"The lateral geniculate nucleus has six layers with a functional division of labour. Magnocellular layers (1 and 2) contain large cells that are fast-conducting, achromatic, and specialised for motion and contrast, feeding the dorsal 'where/how' stream. Parvocellular layers (3 to 6) contain small cells that are slower, colour-sensitive (red-green), and specialised for fine detail, feeding the ventral 'what' stream. Koniocellular layers between them handle blue-yellow signals. The correct option pairs each layer set with its true properties.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the roles: magnocellular handles motion and contrast, while parvocellular handles colour and fine detail.","b":"Magnocellular layers feed the dorsal motion stream, not exclusively area V4, which is a colour and form region of the ventral stream.","c":"The two layer types carry distinctly different information (motion versus colour and detail), so they are not identical."}},{"q":"Cochlear hair cells transduce sound waves into neural signals primarily via:","concept":"Cochlear hair cells transduce sound by mechanotransduction, not by chemical or purely electrical triggering. As the basilar membrane moves, the stereocilia of inner hair cells bend; this bending pulls tip-links that mechanically open cation channels, allowing potassium to flow in from the potassium-rich endolymph, which depolarises the cell and triggers glutamate release onto spiral ganglion fibres of cranial nerve VIII. Outer hair cells amplify the signal via the motor protein prestin, and frequency is coded tonotopically (high frequencies at the base, low at the apex).","traps":{"a":"Transduction opens cation channels via mechanical bending; it is not driven by closure of voltage-gated sodium channels.","b":"GABA is not the trigger; the depolarising event is potassium influx through mechanically gated channels, and hair cells release glutamate.","d":"Olfactory chemical signalling is an entirely different modality and has nothing to do with cochlear mechanotransduction."}},{"q":"The vestibular system's semicircular canals detect:","concept":"The three semicircular canals (anterior, posterior, and lateral) of the vestibular system detect rotational, that is angular, acceleration of the head in three planes: endolymph movement deflects the cupula within the ampulla, bending hair cells. This is distinct from the otolith organs (utricle and saccule), which detect linear acceleration and head tilt via otoconia. Vestibular signals travel through cranial nerve VIII to the vestibular nuclei, driving the vestibulo-ocular reflex and postural control.","traps":{"a":"Static head position and tilt are sensed by the otolith organs (utricle and saccule), not the semicircular canals.","c":"Auditory pitch is coded by the cochlea, not by the vestibular semicircular canals.","d":"Linear acceleration is detected by the otolith organs; the semicircular canals detect angular, not linear, acceleration."}},{"q":"BDNF (brain-derived neurotrophic factor) is most strongly implicated in:","concept":"Brain-derived neurotrophic factor (BDNF) is a neurotrophin that binds TrkB receptors and drives signalling supporting neuronal survival, dendritic growth, long-term potentiation, and hippocampal neurogenesis. It is central to the neurotrophic hypothesis of depression: BDNF is reduced in major depressive disorder, and selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, electroconvulsive therapy, exercise, and ketamine all raise it. The Val66Met polymorphism, which affects activity-dependent BDNF secretion, is studied as a risk variant for depression and cognitive outcomes.","traps":{"b":"Firing individual action potentials is a fast electrical event mediated by ion channels, not the trophic, plasticity-related role of BDNF.","c":"BDNF promotes survival and growth rather than being an 'apoptosis only' factor, so this is the opposite of its main action.","d":"Myelin synthesis is carried out by oligodendrocytes and is not the defining function of BDNF."}},{"q":"Long-term depression (LTD) at hippocampal synapses differs from LTP in that LTD involves:","concept":"Long-term depression (LTD) is the mirror image of long-term potentiation (Timothy Bliss and Terje Lømo, 1973) and weakens synaptic strength. At hippocampal synapses, brief low-frequency stimulation produces a smaller but more prolonged rise in postsynaptic calcium, which favours protein phosphatases (PP1 and PP2A) over kinases, leading to removal of AMPA receptors from the membrane and a sustained decrease in synaptic strength. Together LTP and LTD allow bidirectional, tunable plasticity; a distinct cerebellar LTD at climbing-fibre to Purkinje-cell synapses underlies motor learning.","traps":{"a":"LTD weakens a synapse but does not kill the cell; it is a reversible change in synaptic strength, not permanent cell death.","b":"Glutamate release does have a postsynaptic effect in LTD, namely a modest calcium rise that triggers AMPA receptor removal.","d":"High-frequency stimulation with NMDA blockade would prevent plasticity, whereas LTD is induced by low-frequency stimulation that still admits calcium through NMDA receptors."}},{"q":"The P300 event-related potential is most associated with:","concept":"The P300 (or P3) is a positive event-related potential appearing about 300 milliseconds after an infrequent target stimulus in the oddball paradigm, reflecting attention allocation, stimulus categorisation, and context updating. Its amplitude is reduced and its latency prolonged in schizophrenia and Alzheimer's disease, and it is studied as an endophenotype. Other components differ: N400 indexes semantic incongruence, P600 indexes syntactic violations, and mismatch negativity reflects pre-attentive auditory change detection.","traps":{"a":"Motor command generation relates to the readiness potential (Bereitschaftspotential), not the P300.","b":"REM sleep onset is not indexed by the P300, which is a waking attentional response to oddball stimuli.","c":"The initial visual cortex response is an early sensory component (such as P1 or C1), far earlier and different from the cognitive P300."}},{"q":"Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is most directly caused by:","concept":"Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome results directly from thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, classically in chronic alcohol use. Wernicke's encephalopathy is the acute phase with the triad of confusion, ophthalmoplegia, and ataxia; Korsakoff's is the chronic phase with anterograde amnesia, confabulation, and apathy, reflecting damage to the mammillary bodies and thalamus. Treatment is intravenous thiamine given before glucose, because administering glucose first can precipitate or worsen the syndrome.","traps":{"a":"Hypoxia causes diffuse ischaemic brain injury, not the specific thiamine-dependent mammillary and thalamic pathology of this syndrome.","c":"Lead poisoning causes peripheral neuropathy and encephalopathy but is not the cause of Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome.","d":"Vitamin B12 deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration and megaloblastic anaemia, a different deficiency from the thiamine loss that causes this syndrome."}},{"q":"Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics primarily exert clinical effects via blockade of which receptors, and which side-effect cluster results?","concept":"Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics such as haloperidol, chlorpromazine, and fluphenazine exert their antipsychotic effect mainly by blocking dopamine D2 receptors. Blockade in the mesolimbic pathway reduces positive symptoms, but blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway produces extrapyramidal symptoms (acute dystonia, akathisia, parkinsonism, and tardive dyskinesia), and blockade in the tuberoinfundibular pathway raises prolactin. Atypicals add serotonin 5-HT2A blockade (the Meltzer hypothesis), reducing extrapyramidal symptoms but increasing metabolic side effects.","traps":{"a":"5-HT2A blockade and metabolic syndrome characterise atypical antipsychotics, not the D2-driven typicals in question.","b":"NMDA glutamate involvement relates to models of psychosis and drugs like ketamine, not the mechanism of typical antipsychotics.","c":"GABA-A modulation with sedation describes benzodiazepines, not the dopamine-blocking action of typical antipsychotics."}},{"q":"The Yerkes-Dodson law describes the relationship between:","concept":"The Yerkes-Dodson law (Robert Yerkes and John Dillingham Dodson, 1908) describes the relationship between arousal and task performance as an inverted-U: performance improves as arousal rises to a moderate optimum and then declines with further arousal. Crucially, the optimum is lower for difficult or novel tasks and higher for simple, well-learned ones, which is why a little anxiety can help but too much impairs. Related ideas include the Easterbrook cue-utilisation hypothesis of attentional narrowing under arousal.","traps":{"b":"The relationship between IQ and emotional intelligence (Peter Salovey and John Mayer, 1990) is a separate individual-differences question, not the Yerkes-Dodson arousal law.","c":"Salary and job satisfaction is an organisational-psychology topic unrelated to the arousal-performance curve.","d":"Punishment severity and learning rate concerns reinforcement and conditioning, not the inverted-U of arousal and performance."}},{"q":"The 'two-streams' (dorsal/ventral) visual processing hypothesis (Ungerleider & Mishkin, 1982) proposes:","concept":"The two-streams hypothesis of Leslie Ungerleider and Mortimer Mishkin (1982) holds that after the primary visual cortex, visual information splits into two pathways: a dorsal stream running to the parietal lobe that handles 'where' and 'how' (spatial location and action guidance), and a ventral stream running to the inferior temporal lobe that handles 'what' (object recognition, including faces and places). Milner and Goodale later reframed this as an action versus perception distinction, supported by the double dissociation between optic ataxia (dorsal damage) and visual agnosia (ventral damage).","traps":{"a":"This reverses the mapping: the dorsal stream handles spatial and motion-related 'where/how' processing, while colour is a ventral-stream property.","b":"Both streams are visual; neither is auditory, so this mischaracterises the pathways entirely.","d":"The streams diverge after V1 rather than converging in it, so 'both converge in V1' is incorrect."}},{"q":"Tonic-clonic seizures involve:","concept":"A generalised tonic-clonic seizure (grand mal) involves loss of consciousness with widespread cortical hyperexcitation, unfolding as a tonic phase of generalised muscle stiffening (with apnoea and cyanosis, lasting about 10 to 30 seconds), followed by a clonic phase of rhythmic jerking (about 1 to 2 minutes), then post-ictal confusion. The EEG shows high-amplitude generalised spike-and-wave activity. Treatment options include valproate, levetiracetam, and lamotrigine.","traps":{"a":"A brief staring spell without loss of tone describes a typical absence seizure, not a tonic-clonic one.","c":"Motor symptoms confined to one limb with preserved awareness describe a simple partial (focal) seizure, not a generalised tonic-clonic event.","d":"A sleep-only seizure with no EEG correlate does not fit tonic-clonic seizures, which have a florid generalised spike-and-wave EEG signature."}},{"q":"Parkinson's disease pathology principally involves degeneration of:","concept":"Parkinson's disease is defined pathologically by degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta, with alpha-synuclein-containing Lewy body inclusions. Loss of nigrostriatal dopamine impairs facilitation of the basal ganglia direct pathway, producing bradykinesia, rigidity, a resting tremor (3 to 5 Hz), and postural instability. Treatment is symptomatic: L-DOPA combined with carbidopa (a peripheral DOPA decarboxylase inhibitor), dopamine agonists, and MAO-B inhibitors.","traps":{"a":"Degeneration of spinal motor neurons characterises amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, not Parkinson's disease.","b":"Loss of hippocampal pyramidal cells relates to amnestic and Alzheimer-type pathology, not the nigral degeneration of Parkinson's.","c":"Cholinergic basal forebrain neuron loss is a feature of Alzheimer's disease, not the dopaminergic loss of Parkinson's."}},{"q":"The amygdala receives a 'low road' input from the thalamus that bypasses the cortex (LeDoux). This pathway:","concept":"Joseph LeDoux's dual-route model of fear describes a fast subcortical 'low road' in which the sensory thalamus projects directly to the amygdala, bypassing the cortex, to permit rapid but crude detection of potential threat and quick defensive responses before conscious recognition. A slower 'high road' runs from the sensory thalamus through the cortex to the amygdala, providing more accurate identification. The low road's value is speed for survival-critical reactions, even at the cost of precision.","traps":{"a":"The low road provides only crude, fast threat signals, not the full detailed content of conscious vision, which depends on cortical processing.","c":"Voluntary motor output is generated by motor cortical and basal ganglia circuits, not by the thalamo-amygdala threat pathway.","d":"Slower, more accurate object identification is the function of the cortical 'high road', not the fast 'low road'."}},{"q":"Wakefulness is associated on EEG with which dominant frequencies?","concept":"On the EEG, alert wakefulness is dominated by beta activity (13 to 30 Hz), reflecting focused, active cognition, while relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed shows posterior alpha rhythm (8 to 13 Hz) that blocks (attenuates) when the eyes open. As one moves through drowsiness and sleep the frequencies slow: theta (4 to 8 Hz) in N1 drowsiness, sleep spindles and K-complexes in N2, and high-amplitude delta (0.5 to 4 Hz) in N3 deep sleep. The correct option pairs both waking states with their true bands.","traps":{"b":"Theta alone characterises drowsiness and light N1 sleep, not the fully awake states.","c":"Sleep spindles are a signature of N2 sleep, not of wakefulness.","d":"Delta predominance marks deep N3 sleep, the opposite of alert wakefulness."}},{"q":"Cannabinoid CB1 receptors are most densely concentrated in:","concept":"Cannabinoid CB1 receptors are the most abundant G-protein-coupled receptors in the brain, densely expressed in the hippocampus (memory), basal ganglia and cerebellum (motor control), cortex, hypothalamus, and amygdala. Acting through Gi/o proteins, they reduce presynaptic neurotransmitter release, and their endogenous ligands are anandamide and 2-arachidonoylglycerol. This central distribution explains cannabis's effects on memory, movement, and reward. By contrast, CB2 receptors are found mainly on immune cells and microglia.","traps":{"a":"CB1 receptors are widespread in the brain, not confined to spinal motor neurons, so limiting them to the cord is wrong.","c":"Immune cells express mainly CB2, not CB1, so 'peripheral immune cells exclusively' misattributes the receptor.","d":"The adrenal cortex is not the dense CB1 site; CB1 concentrates in brain regions like the hippocampus and basal ganglia."}},{"q":"The hippocampal formation is critical for:","concept":"The hippocampal formation is critical for encoding and consolidating declarative memory, especially episodic and spatial memory (its place cells earned John O'Keefe a share of the 2014 Nobel Prize). Importantly, it is not the permanent storage site of remote memories: over time, according to the standard consolidation model, memories become dependent on distributed neocortical stores, though multiple-trace theory argues the hippocampus stays involved for detailed episodic recall. Procedural and motor memory instead depend on the striatum and cerebellum.","traps":{"b":"Procedural and motor memory rely on the striatum and cerebellum, not the hippocampus, which handles declarative memory.","c":"Olfactory transduction occurs in the olfactory epithelium and bulb, not the hippocampus.","d":"Voluntary motor execution is a function of the motor cortex and basal ganglia, not the hippocampal formation."}},{"q":"Multiple sclerosis is characterised by:","concept":"Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune, T-cell-mediated demyelinating disease of central nervous system white matter, in which oligodendrocytes and their myelin are the target, producing lesions disseminated in time and space (the basis of the McDonald criteria). Typical lesions are periventricular, juxtacortical, infratentorial, and spinal, causing optic neuritis, paraesthesias, weakness, and ataxia; MRI shows T2 hyperintense plaques. Early on there is demyelination with relative axonal preservation, and treatment uses disease-modifying therapies such as interferon-beta, glatiramer, and monoclonal antibodies.","traps":{"a":"Alpha-synuclein deposits in the substantia nigra define Parkinson's disease, not the demyelinating pathology of multiple sclerosis.","c":"Cortical beta-amyloid plaques characterise Alzheimer's disease, not the white-matter demyelination of multiple sclerosis.","d":"Lower motor neuron degeneration describes disorders like amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, not the myelin-targeted autoimmunity of multiple sclerosis."}},{"q":"The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (WCST) is most sensitive to dysfunction of which region?","concept":"The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test (David Grant and Esta Berg, 1948) requires the subject to sort cards by an unstated rule (colour, shape, or number) and to shift sets when feedback signals the rule has changed. It is most sensitive to dorsolateral prefrontal cortex dysfunction, which produces perseverative errors (continuing to sort by the old rule), and it is widely used in schizophrenia, frontal lobe injury, and ADHD. For contrast, the Stroop taps the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex and anterior cingulate, the Iowa Gambling Task taps the orbitofrontal cortex, and the Tower of London taps planning.","traps":{"b":"The primary motor cortex governs movement execution, not the set-shifting and rule-updating measured by the card sorting test.","c":"The hippocampus supports declarative memory, whereas the card sorting test probes executive set-shifting.","d":"The cerebellum coordinates movement and some cognition, but the card sorting test's perseverative-error sensitivity localises to the dorsolateral prefrontal cortex."}},{"q":"The WAIS-IV Verbal Comprehension Index (VCI) consists of which core subtests?","concept":"On the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition (WAIS-IV), the Verbal Comprehension Index taps acquired verbal knowledge and reasoning, and its three core subtests are Similarities, Vocabulary, and Information (Comprehension is supplementary). Each of the four indexes draws on its own cluster of subtests, so the correct grouping is defined by which construct the index measures. VCI reflects crystallised verbal ability, distinct from perceptual reasoning, working memory, and processing speed.","traps":{"a":"Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, and Visual Puzzles are the core subtests of the Perceptual Reasoning Index (PRI), which measures nonverbal fluid reasoning, not verbal comprehension.","b":"Coding, Symbol Search, and Cancellation belong to the Processing Speed Index (PSI), tapping speed of simple visual scanning rather than verbal knowledge.","c":"Digit Span, Arithmetic, and Letter-Number Sequencing make up the Working Memory Index (WMI), which measures mental manipulation and attention, not verbal comprehension."}},{"q":"Raven's Progressive Matrices is considered a 'culture-fair' test because it measures:","concept":"Raven's Progressive Matrices, devised by John C. Raven in 1936, presents abstract visual patterns with a missing piece that the examinee completes by inferring the underlying rule. Because the task uses no words and minimal cultural content, it is regarded as a 'culture-fair' measure of fluid (nonverbal) reasoning, or Gf in Cattell-Horn-Carroll theory. It is one of the purest markers of general reasoning ability, though education and culture still exert some real-world influence.","traps":{"a":"Reading comprehension is a verbal, language-loaded skill; the whole point of Raven's is to avoid such content so it can be culture-fair.","c":"Crystallised verbal intelligence (Gc) reflects acquired knowledge and vocabulary, the opposite of the nonverbal fluid reasoning Raven's is designed to isolate.","d":"Memory alone is not what the matrices assess; the task requires active rule induction and pattern completion, not simple recall."}},{"q":"On the Stanford-Binet 5 (SB5), the five factors are:","concept":"The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scales, Fifth Edition (SB5, Gale H. Roid, 2003), is organised around five Cattell-Horn-Carroll factors, each assessed in both a verbal and a nonverbal domain, giving ten subtests. The five factors are Fluid Reasoning, Knowledge, Quantitative Reasoning, Visual-Spatial Processing, and Working Memory. This yields a Full Scale IQ plus Verbal IQ, Nonverbal IQ, and factor index scores.","traps":{"a":"Sensory, Motor, Memory, Attention, and Language describe a neuropsychological domain breakdown, not the CHC factor structure of the SB5.","b":"The Verbal IQ / Performance IQ split belongs to older Wechsler editions (e.g. WAIS-III); the SB5 crosses verbal and nonverbal domains onto five factors instead.","d":"The Big Five personality factors measure personality, not intelligence, and have nothing to do with the SB5 cognitive structure."}},{"q":"The MMPI-2 clinical scale 'Pd' (Psychopathic Deviate, Scale 4) classically elevates in:","concept":"On the Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory, Second Edition (MMPI-2), Scale 4, Psychopathic Deviate (Pd), was originally developed to identify social maladjustment and rebelliousness. High scorers characteristically show antisocial behaviour, conflict with family and authority, impulsivity, and a sense of alienation. It is not a mood or anxiety scale; it captures a behavioural-dyscontrol and rule-defiance profile.","traps":{"a":"Mania is indexed by Scale 9 (Ma, Hypomania), not by Scale 4; elevation on Pd is not exclusive to mania.","b":"Depression is captured by Scale 2 (D); Pd is not a depression-specific scale even though depressive features can co-occur.","d":"Obsessive-compulsive and anxiety features load on Scale 7 (Pt, Psychasthenia), not on the Psychopathic Deviate scale."}},{"q":"The MoCA (Montreal Cognitive Assessment) differs from the MMSE in that MoCA:","concept":"The Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA; Ziad Nasreddine, 2005) is a 30-point, roughly 10-minute cognitive screen designed to catch mild cognitive impairment that the Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE; Marshal Folstein, Susan Folstein and Paul McHugh, 1975) often misses. It samples executive function (trail-making, alternating sequence), clock-drawing, abstraction, and delayed recall, giving it greater sensitivity to frontal-executive and early deficits. A common screening cut-off is a score of 25 or below out of 30, with an education adjustment.","traps":{"a":"The MoCA tests memory (delayed recall) plus multiple other domains; claiming it omits memory is simply false.","c":"The MoCA is a brief screen usable by trained clinicians, nurses, and general practitioners, not something restricted to neurologists.","d":"The MoCA and MMSE take comparable time (about 10 minutes each); the real distinction is sensitivity and executive-function coverage, not a 5-versus-30-minute gap."}},{"q":"The PANSS (Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale) is used to assess:","concept":"The Positive and Negative Syndrome Scale (PANSS; Stanley Kay, Abraham Fiszbein, and Lewis Opler, 1987) is a 30-item, clinician-rated instrument for grading symptom severity in schizophrenia. Items divide into a 7-item Positive subscale, a 7-item Negative subscale, and a 16-item General Psychopathology subscale, each rated 1 to 7. It is the standard outcome measure in schizophrenia trials, distinct from scales targeting mood, personality, or cognition.","traps":{"a":"Personality disorders are assessed with instruments like the SCID-5-PD or PCL-R (Robert Hare), not the PANSS, which is schizophrenia-specific.","b":"Depression severity is rated by scales such as the Hamilton or Beck inventories; the PANSS measures psychotic and general psychopathology, not depression per se.","c":"Cognitive impairment is screened by tools like the MoCA or MMSE (Marshal Folstein, Susan Folstein and Paul McHugh, 1975); the PANSS rates symptom severity, not cognitive status."}},{"q":"The Y-BOCS (Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale) rates:","concept":"The Yale-Brown Obsessive Compulsive Scale (Y-BOCS; Wayne Goodman et al., 1989) is the gold-standard clinician-rated measure of OCD severity. It has 10 items, five rating obsessions and five rating compulsions, each scored 0 to 4 across the dimensions of time occupied, interference, distress, resistance, and control, for a total of 0 to 40. Crucially it rates severity independent of symptom content, which a preceding symptom checklist catalogues.","traps":{"a":"Suicidal ideation severity is measured by scales like the Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale, not the Y-BOCS, which is OCD-specific.","b":"Depression severity is captured by instruments such as the Beck Depression Inventory; the Y-BOCS does not rate mood.","c":"Hallucination frequency is a psychotic symptom rated on scales like the PANSS (Stanley Kay, Abraham Fiszbein and Lewis Opler, 1987) or SAPS, unrelated to the obsessive-compulsive focus of the Y-BOCS."}},{"q":"The PCL-R (Psychopathy Checklist–Revised) was developed by:","concept":"The Psychopathy Checklist-Revised (PCL-R) was developed by Robert D. Hare in 1991, building on Hervey Cleckley's clinical description of psychopathy in 'The Mask of Sanity' (1941). It comprises 20 items scored 0 to 2 by a trained clinician using a semi-structured interview plus file review, giving a maximum of 40, with 30 the conventional North American cut-off for psychopathy. It is a central tool in forensic risk assessment.","traps":{"a":"Hervey Cleckley (1941) provided the foundational clinical concept of psychopathy but did not create the PCL-R; Hare operationalised it decades later.","b":"Aaron T. Beck developed cognitive therapy and its depression and anxiety inventories, not a psychopathy checklist.","d":"Hans Eysenck built dimensional personality theory (the PEN model) and its questionnaires, not the PCL-R."}},{"q":"Cohen's kappa (κ) is used to estimate:","concept":"Cohen's kappa is a chance-corrected index of agreement between two raters classifying cases into nominal or categorical categories, computed as kappa = (Po minus Pe) divided by (1 minus Pe), where Po is observed and Pe is expected-by-chance agreement. It answers how reliably two raters assign the same category beyond what coincidence would produce. Landis and Koch benchmarks label values above 0.61 as substantial and above 0.81 as almost perfect.","traps":{"b":"Predictive validity concerns how well a test forecasts a future criterion, a validity question, not the inter-rater agreement kappa quantifies.","c":"Effect size for ANOVA is captured by statistics like eta-squared or Cohen's f, not by kappa.","d":"Internal consistency (how well items hang together) is measured by Cronbach's alpha or KR-20, not by a chance-corrected agreement coefficient."}},{"q":"Spearman-Brown prophecy formula predicts:","concept":"The Spearman-Brown prophecy formula predicts how a test's reliability changes when its length is altered by a factor k, via r_new = (k times r_old) divided by (1 plus (k minus 1) times r_old). Adding parallel items raises reliability with diminishing returns. Its classic use is correcting split-half reliability, estimating the full test's reliability from a single half, and planning how many items are needed to hit a target reliability.","traps":{"a":"The validity of a new test concerns its correlation with a criterion or construct, which the Spearman-Brown formula does not address; it deals with reliability and length.","b":"Effect size in ANCOVA is a separate statistical quantity; the formula makes no prediction about experimental effect sizes.","c":"The factor structure of the WAIS is a matter for factor analysis, not for a reliability-by-length formula."}},{"q":"Kuder-Richardson 20 (KR-20) differs from Cronbach's alpha in that KR-20:","concept":"Kuder-Richardson 20 (KR-20) is an internal-consistency reliability coefficient that applies specifically to dichotomously scored items, those marked right/wrong or yes/no. It is in fact the special case of Cronbach's alpha for two-category items; when items are polytomous (e.g. Likert scales) Cronbach's alpha generalises the same idea. KR-21 is a simplified variant assuming equal item difficulty.","traps":{"b":"KR-20 does estimate internal consistency; saying it cannot is the opposite of its purpose.","c":"KR-20 and Cronbach's alpha are not identical in scope: KR-20 handles only dichotomous items, whereas alpha extends to multi-point scoring.","d":"KR-20 is used for right/wrong dichotomous items (often ability tests), not exclusively for personality items."}},{"q":"Confirmatory Factor Analysis (CFA) differs from Exploratory Factor Analysis (EFA) primarily in that CFA:","concept":"Confirmatory Factor Analysis (CFA) tests a factor structure that the researcher specifies in advance, fixing which items load on which latent factors and then evaluating model fit with indices such as CFI, TLI, RMSEA, and SRMR. Exploratory Factor Analysis (EFA), by contrast, is data-driven: it discovers how many factors exist and how items cluster without an a priori model. CFA is the measurement component of structural equation modelling.","traps":{"a":"CFA does not always use oblique rotation; rotation is an EFA concern, and CFA instead constrains loadings within a specified model.","c":"CFA is not limited to dichotomous data; it handles continuous and ordinal indicators with appropriate estimators.","d":"Having no a priori model describes EFA, not CFA; CFA is defined precisely by testing a pre-specified model."}},{"q":"A Likert-type scale differs from a Thurstone scale in that Likert items are:","concept":"A Likert-type scale, introduced by Rensis Likert in 1932, asks respondents to rate their agreement with each statement on a symmetric continuum (for example 1 to 5 or 1 to 7), and the endorsed ratings are summed into a total score assuming roughly equal item weights. The key contrast with a Thurstone scale is that Thurstone items are pre-scaled by judges before administration, whereas Likert items are weighted equally and scaled by the respondents themselves.","traps":{"a":"Likert items are not dichotomous; they use a graded multi-point agree-disagree continuum, which is what distinguishes them.","c":"Pre-scaling of items by judges before administration is the defining feature of the Thurstone equal-appearing-intervals method, not the Likert method.","d":"Drawing responses from a sentence-completion stimulus describes a projective technique, not a Likert rating scale."}},{"q":"Q-sort methodology was developed by William Stephenson and Carl Rogers to:","concept":"Q-sort methodology, developed by William Stephenson and adopted by Carl Rogers, has a respondent sort a set of descriptive statements (such as self-descriptors) into a forced quasi-normal distribution from most to least characteristic. This captures idiographic, within-person structure rather than comparing one person against others. Rogers famously used it to measure the congruence between self-concept and ideal self before and after therapy.","traps":{"a":"Q-sort was not designed to replace the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943); it is a distinct idiographic sorting method, not a substitute clinical inventory.","c":"Infant reflex testing belongs to neurodevelopmental examination, entirely unrelated to Q-sort card sorting.","d":"Q-sort is not a diagnostic instrument for schizophrenia; it measures self-perception and within-person variation."}},{"q":"The Hawthorne effect refers to:","concept":"The Hawthorne effect, named after the Western Electric Hawthorne plant studies (Roethlisberger and Dickson, 1924 to 1932), is the tendency of people to change their behaviour, typically improving performance, simply because they know they are being observed or studied, regardless of the actual intervention. It is a form of reactivity or observer effect. Recognising it matters because apparent treatment gains may reflect being watched rather than the manipulation itself.","traps":{"a":"Demand characteristics arising from instructions are cues about the hypothesis (Orne's concept), distinct from the mere effect of being observed.","b":"Experimenters unconsciously biasing the data describes experimenter expectancy or the Rosenthal/Pygmalion effect, not the Hawthorne effect.","d":"The placebo effect is improvement from an inert treatment believed to be active, a different mechanism from reactivity to observation."}},{"q":"Demand characteristics, as described by Martin Orne, are:","concept":"Demand characteristics, described by Martin Orne in 1962, are the cues within an experimental setting, including instructions, the setting itself, rumours, and procedures, from which participants infer what the experimenter expects and then adjust their behaviour accordingly. They threaten internal validity by turning the participant into a hypothesis-guesser. Controls include blinding, unobtrusive measures, deception with debriefing, and post-experimental inquiry.","traps":{"a":"Demand characteristics are cues in the situation, not properties of the experimental hypothesis itself.","b":"Statistical demands of a test refer to assumptions or requirements of an analysis, not to situational cues that shape participant behaviour.","c":"The amount of effort an item requires is an item-difficulty or workload notion, unrelated to Orne's concept of situational expectancy cues."}},{"q":"A ceiling effect on a test means:","concept":"A ceiling effect occurs when so many examinees score at or near the maximum that the test can no longer discriminate among the highest performers, compressing variance at the top. Its mirror image, the floor effect, bunches low performers at the minimum. Both restrict range, lower reliability and validity for the affected end, and can mask genuine differences or intervention effects; the remedy is adding items of appropriate difficulty.","traps":{"b":"Items being too difficult overall would drive scores down toward the minimum (a floor effect), which is the opposite of a ceiling effect.","c":"Reliability cannot exceed 1, and a ceiling effect actually reduces reliability at the top rather than being about impossible reliability values.","d":"A normal distribution of scores implies good spread; a ceiling effect specifically produces a skewed pile-up at the maximum, not normality."}},{"q":"Convergent validity is demonstrated when:","concept":"Convergent validity is evidence for construct validity shown when a test correlates highly with other measures of the same construct, for example a depression scale correlating strongly with another depression measure. It is one half of construct validation (Cronbach and Meehl, 1955), paired with discriminant validity (low correlation with unrelated constructs). Campbell and Fiske's multitrait-multimethod matrix (1959) evaluates both at once.","traps":{"b":"Two raters agreeing on a score is inter-rater reliability, not convergent validity across measures of a construct.","c":"A test predicting future criteria is predictive (criterion) validity, a separate form of validity from convergent evidence.","d":"Failing to correlate with measures of unrelated constructs defines discriminant validity, the complement of convergent validity, not convergent validity itself."}},{"q":"Criterion contamination refers to:","concept":"Criterion contamination occurs when the predictor, or knowledge of the predictor's scores, influences the criterion measure, so that the observed predictor-criterion correlation is spuriously inflated. A classic case is a supervisor who knows employees' test scores and then rates their job performance partly on that knowledge. Remedies include blinding raters and using independent criteria; it differs from criterion deficiency, where the criterion omits relevant content.","traps":{"a":"Random measurement error in the criterion is unreliability, which attenuates (lowers) correlations, the opposite of the inflation criterion contamination causes.","b":"Replacing the criterion mid-study is a design change, not the overlap-driven artefactual inflation that defines contamination.","d":"A criterion with too few items concerns criterion reliability or breadth, not the predictor-into-criterion leakage that constitutes contamination."}},{"q":"A z-score of +1.5 on a normally distributed test corresponds to approximately which percentile?","concept":"On the standard normal distribution a z-score expresses how many standard deviations a score lies above or below the mean, and it maps directly onto a percentile via the cumulative normal curve. A z of +1.5 corresponds to roughly the 93rd percentile (about 93.3 percent of the area lies below it). Useful anchors are z = 0 at the 50th, z = +1 at the 84th, and z = +2 at about the 98th percentile.","traps":{"a":"The 99th percentile corresponds to about z = +2.33, well above +1.5.","b":"The 84th percentile corresponds to z = +1.0, not +1.5.","c":"The 50th percentile is z = 0 (the mean), far below a z of +1.5."}},{"q":"T-scores (as used on the MMPI, NEO-PI-R) have a mean and SD of:","concept":"T-scores are a standardised score scale with a mean of 50 and a standard deviation of 10, used on instruments such as the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943), the NEO-PI-R (Paul Costa and Robert McCrae), and the ASEBA. Converting to a common metric lets scores across scales be compared, with values above 65 (1.5 SD) typically flagged as clinically elevated on the MMPI. This differs from the Wechsler IQ metric (mean 100, SD 15) and the z-score metric (mean 0, SD 1).","traps":{"a":"Mean 100, SD 15 is the deviation IQ scale (Wechsler, Stanford-Binet), not the T-score metric.","c":"Mean 500, SD 100 is the old SAT/GRE scaling, not the T-score.","d":"Mean 0, SD 1 is the z-score, the raw standard-normal metric from which T-scores are derived, not the T-score itself."}},{"q":"On the MMPI-2-RF, the RC (Restructured Clinical) scales differ from the original clinical scales in that RC scales:","concept":"On the MMPI-2-RF the Restructured Clinical (RC) scales, developed by Auke Tellegen and colleagues (2003), were built by first extracting the pervasive 'demoralisation' factor into its own scale (RCd) so that the remaining RC scales each capture a more distinctive core dimension of psychopathology with cleaner discriminant validity. This addressed the heavy intercorrelation and construct overlap of the original clinical scales. The RC scales serve as the core of the MMPI-2-RF (2008) and MMPI-3 (2020).","traps":{"a":"The RC scales were not dropped in the MMPI-2-RF; they are its core scales.","b":"The RC scales are not defined by using only true/false items (the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) item format is already true/false); their innovation is removing demoralisation variance.","d":"The RC scales are deliberately not identical to the original clinical scales; they were restructured to reduce overlap and improve discriminant validity."}},{"q":"The NEO-PI-R measures personality on which model?","concept":"The NEO-PI-R (Paul Costa and Robert McCrae, 1992) is a 240-item self-report questionnaire operationalising the Five-Factor Model, or Big Five: Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, and Neuroticism (OCEAN), with six facets measured under each domain. The five factors recur across languages and cultures, consistent with the lexical hypothesis. A 60-item short form, the NEO-FFI, measures the domains without facets.","traps":{"a":"The Cattell 16PF measures 16 primary factors from Cattell's model, not the five-factor structure the NEO-PI-R is built on.","b":"The MBTI's 16-type typology derives from Jungian dichotomies, a categorical model distinct from the continuous Big Five.","d":"Eysenck's PEN model has three dimensions (Psychoticism, Extraversion, Neuroticism), not the five factors of the NEO-PI-R."}},{"q":"Cattell's 16PF (Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire) is most associated with:","concept":"Raymond Cattell's Sixteen Personality Factor Questionnaire (16PF) is a factor-analytically derived inventory that reduces the natural-language descriptors of personality to 16 primary 'source' traits (such as Warmth, Reasoning, and Emotional Stability). These primary traits in turn combine into five second-order global factors that broadly approximate the Big Five. Cattell's lexical, factor-analytic approach makes it a landmark trait instrument.","traps":{"a":"The 16PF is a general normal-personality measure, not restricted to diagnosing personality disorders.","b":"Q-sort methodology is Stephenson's forced-distribution sorting technique, unrelated to Cattell's factor-based questionnaire.","d":"Projective interpretation applies to instruments like the Rorschach or TAT; the 16PF is an objective, self-report questionnaire."}},{"q":"Generalizability theory (G theory, Cronbach et al. 1972) extends Classical Test Theory by:","concept":"Generalizability theory (Lee Cronbach and colleagues, 1972) extends Classical Test Theory by decomposing observed-score variance into multiple 'facets' of measurement, such as raters, items, and occasions, using analysis of variance to estimate each source of error separately. Rather than lumping everything into one undifferentiated error term, a G-study quantifies variance components and a follow-up D-study projects how reliability would change under different administration designs. It complements but does not replace Item Response Theory.","traps":{"b":"Treating measurement error as a single undifferentiated term is precisely the Classical Test Theory limitation that G theory was created to overcome.","c":"G theory does not eliminate the need for reliability estimation; it provides a richer, multi-faceted way of estimating it.","d":"Modelling item response curves as a function of latent ability is the province of Item Response Theory, not generalizability theory."}},{"q":"The Item Characteristic Curve (ICC) in IRT plots:","concept":"In Item Response Theory the Item Characteristic Curve (ICC) plots the probability of a correct (or keyed) response on the y-axis against the examinee's latent ability, theta, on the x-axis, producing an S-shaped logistic curve. Item parameters shape it: b (difficulty) locates the curve along the theta axis, a (discrimination) sets its steepness, and c (guessing) sets the lower asymptote. The 1PL/Rasch model uses only b, the 2PL adds a, and the 3PL adds c.","traps":{"b":"Validity coefficients across studies are the material of validity generalisation or meta-analysis, not what an ICC plots.","c":"Internal consistency by item is a reliability concern (item-total statistics), not the ability-versus-probability relationship an ICC displays.","d":"Test reliability against sample size is a different relationship entirely; the ICC's x-axis is latent ability, not sample size."}},{"q":"Test-retest reliability is most appropriate to estimate when:","concept":"Test-retest reliability is estimated by administering the same test twice and correlating the scores, so it is only appropriate when the construct being measured is expected to remain stable across the retest interval, as with intelligence or trait personality. For state-like constructs such as current mood or anxiety, genuine change over time would lower the correlation and be misread as unreliability. Short intervals also risk memory carryover, while long ones confound true change with error.","traps":{"a":"A current mood state is exactly the kind of unstable construct for which test-retest reliability is inappropriate, since real change would depress the correlation.","b":"Having no opportunity to administer twice makes test-retest impossible to compute, not appropriate; it points toward alternative reliability methods.","c":"Having only one rater speaks to whether inter-rater reliability applies, not to when test-retest reliability is appropriate."}},{"q":"The Standard Error of Measurement (SEM = SD × √(1 − r)) for a test with SD = 15 and reliability = 0.91 is approximately:","concept":"The Standard Error of Measurement quantifies the expected spread of an individual's observed scores around their true score, computed as SEM = SD times the square root of (1 minus reliability). With SD = 15 and reliability = 0.91, SEM = 15 times the square root of 0.09 = 15 times 0.3 = 4.5. Higher reliability and lower SD both shrink the SEM, tightening the confidence interval around a score.","traps":{"a":"15.0 would result only if reliability were 0 (all variance error); with reliability 0.91 the SEM is far smaller.","b":"10.0 does not follow from the formula; the square root of 0.09 is 0.3, giving 4.5, not a value near two-thirds of the SD.","c":"1.5 underestimates the SEM; it would require a reliability of 0.99, not 0.91."}},{"q":"Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) analysis plots:","concept":"Receiver Operating Characteristic (ROC) analysis plots sensitivity (the true positive rate) on the y-axis against 1 minus specificity (the false positive rate) on the x-axis across every possible cut-off value of a test. The Area Under the Curve (AUC) summarises overall discrimination as the probability that a randomly chosen positive case scores higher than a randomly chosen negative, with 0.5 meaning chance and values above 0.9 outstanding. ROC curves guide selection of clinical cut-off scores.","traps":{"a":"Predictive validity plotted against reliability is not what ROC displays; ROC plots true positive against false positive rates.","c":"Item difficulty versus discrimination is an item-analysis plot from IRT or classical analysis, not an ROC curve.","d":"Score versus age describes a developmental norming or growth curve, unrelated to the sensitivity-specificity trade-off of ROC."}},{"q":"The Vineland-3, K-SADS, ADI-R, and ADOS-2 are best classified as:","concept":"The Vineland-3, K-SADS, ADI-R, and ADOS-2 are all structured or semi-structured interview and observation instruments used in developmental and psychiatric assessment rather than self-report questionnaires or lab paradigms. The Vineland-3 is a caregiver interview for adaptive behaviour, the K-SADS is a semi-structured interview for childhood psychiatric disorders, the ADI-R is a caregiver autism interview, and the ADOS-2 is a direct interaction-based autism observation, with ADI-R plus ADOS-2 forming the gold-standard autism diagnostic combination.","traps":{"a":"EEG paradigms are electrophysiological recordings; none of these four instruments involves brain-wave measurement.","b":"Personality projective techniques (like the Rorschach) present ambiguous stimuli for interpretation, unlike these structured diagnostic interviews and observations.","c":"Self-report cognitive tests are completed directly by the examinee; these instruments instead rely on caregiver interview or clinician observation of the child."}}],"nimhans-mock-1":[{"q":"In a cohort study investigating the association between a new psychological intervention and relapse rates in schizophrenia, the risk ratio is calculated as 1.0. This finding indicates that:","concept":"The risk ratio (relative risk) divides the incidence of the outcome in the exposed group by the incidence in the unexposed group. When RR equals exactly 1.0, the two incidences are identical, meaning the exposure (the intervention) is not associated with any change in relapse risk. By convention RR greater than 1 indicates increased risk (harm) and RR less than 1 indicates reduced risk (protection).","traps":{"a":"An RR of 2.0, not 1.0, would mean the exposed group's incidence is double that of the unexposed group.","b":"A significant protective effect would require RR below 1.0 with a confidence interval that does not cross 1, which is not the case here.","d":"This describes the interpretation of an odds ratio of 1.0 framed by exposure odds, whereas the question specifically asks about a risk ratio."}},{"q":"A researcher conducts a systematic review on the efficacy of CBT for insomnia. To ensure transparency and replicability in the methodology, the researcher should adhere to which of the following guidelines?","concept":"PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses) is the reporting guideline designed specifically to make systematic reviews transparent and replicable, covering search strategy, study selection, and the flow of records. Each major study design has its own reporting standard, so matching the guideline to the design is the key exam skill here.","traps":{"a":"The CONSORT Statement is the reporting standard for randomized controlled trials, not for systematic reviews.","c":"The STROBE checklist governs the reporting of observational studies (cohort, case-control, cross-sectional).","d":"CARE guidelines are for writing up individual case reports, not synthesized reviews of many studies."}},{"q":"To investigate a rare psychological outcome (e.g., a specific dissociative disorder) and its potential past exposures (e.g., childhood trauma), which of the following epidemiological study designs would be the MOST feasible and appropriate?","concept":"A case-control study starts by identifying people who already have the outcome (cases) and comparable people without it (controls), then looks backward at prior exposures. Because it recruits on the basis of the outcome, it is the most efficient and feasible design for rare outcomes, where waiting for cases to appear in a cohort would be impractical.","traps":{"a":"A prospective cohort would require following an enormous sample forward for years to accumulate enough cases of a rare disorder, making it impractical.","b":"A cross-sectional study measures exposure and outcome at a single time point, so it cannot establish temporal order and captures too few rare cases.","d":"A randomized controlled trial deliberately assigns an intervention and cannot ethically or practically assign a harmful past exposure like childhood trauma."}},{"q":"A researcher randomly selects 5 districts from each state, then 10 schools per district, then 30 students per school. This sampling technique is known as:","concept":"Multi-stage cluster sampling proceeds through a hierarchy of naturally occurring groups (clusters), selecting randomly at each level: first districts, then schools within chosen districts, then students within chosen schools. It is used when a full sampling frame of individuals is unavailable but the population is organized into nested units.","traps":{"a":"Stratified random sampling divides the whole population into strata and samples from every stratum, rather than randomly discarding entire clusters at each stage.","b":"Quota sampling is a non-probability method that fills preset category targets by convenience, with no random selection at any level.","d":"Purposive sampling deliberately handpicks participants judged to be informative, unlike the random multi-level selection described here."}},{"q":"Which sampling method relies on the researcher's judgment to select participants who are 'typical' or 'representative,' and does NOT allow for the calculation of sampling error?","concept":"Quota sampling is a non-probability technique in which the researcher uses personal judgment to select participants believed to be typical or representative until preset category quotas are filled. Because selection is not random and each unit's probability of inclusion is unknown, sampling error cannot be estimated.","traps":{"a":"Simple random sampling gives every individual a known equal chance of selection, so sampling error is calculable.","b":"Stratified random sampling is a probability method that randomly samples within defined strata and permits sampling error estimation.","d":"Cluster sampling randomly selects intact groups and is a probability method, allowing sampling error to be quantified."}},{"q":"On a Scree plot, eigenvalues drop sharply from 3.5 to 1.8, then plateau at 0.9, 0.7, 0.5. By the Kaiser-Guttman rule (eigenvalue > 1) and the elbow, how many factors should be retained?","concept":"The Kaiser-Guttman rule retains only factors whose eigenvalue exceeds 1.0, because such factors explain more variance than a single standardized variable. Here only the eigenvalues 3.5 and 1.8 exceed 1.0, so two factors are retained, and the scree plot's elbow (the point where the curve flattens after 1.8) agrees.","traps":{"a":"Retaining all five would ignore the eigenvalue-greater-than-1 criterion, keeping factors (0.9, 0.7, 0.5) that explain less variance than one original variable.","b":"Retaining three would incorrectly include the 0.9 eigenvalue, which falls below the Kaiser threshold of 1.0.","d":"Retaining only one drops the 1.8 eigenvalue, which clearly exceeds 1.0 and lies above the scree elbow."}},{"q":"When comparing two independent samples and the data does not meet assumptions for parametric testing, which non-parametric test is most commonly used?","concept":"The Mann-Whitney U test is the standard non-parametric alternative to the independent-samples t-test, comparing two unrelated groups by ranking all observations rather than assuming normality. It is chosen whenever two independent samples must be compared and parametric assumptions (normality, interval data) are not met.","traps":{"a":"The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is for two related or paired samples, not independent groups.","c":"The Kruskal-Wallis test extends the logic to three or more independent groups, more than the two specified here.","d":"The Friedman test handles three or more repeated (within-subjects) measures, not two independent samples."}},{"q":"Cohen's Kappa is a statistical measure used to assess which of the following?","concept":"Cohen's Kappa quantifies the agreement between two raters classifying items into categories, correcting for the agreement that would be expected purely by chance. It is the standard index of inter-rater reliability for categorical judgments.","traps":{"a":"Test-retest reliability is assessed by correlating the same measure across two time points, typically with Pearson's r, not Kappa.","b":"Internal consistency (how well items on a scale hang together) is measured by Cronbach's alpha.","d":"Criterion validity concerns how well a test predicts an external standard, which is a validity question, not rater agreement."}},{"q":"When two predictors in a regression analysis have a high correlation with each other, this statistical problem is known as:","concept":"Multicollinearity occurs when two or more predictor variables in a regression are highly correlated with each other, which inflates the standard errors of the coefficients and makes the individual effects unstable and hard to interpret. It is diagnosed with the Variance Inflation Factor (VIF above 10) or tolerance below 0.1.","traps":{"a":"Heteroscedasticity refers to non-constant variance of the residuals across levels of the predictor, not correlation between predictors.","c":"Autocorrelation is the correlation of residuals with each other across observations (common in time-series data), not correlation among predictors.","d":"Homogeneity of variance is an assumption of equal variances across groups, relevant to ANOVA and t-tests, not a predictor-correlation problem."}},{"q":"In statistical hypothesis testing, rejecting a true null hypothesis represents which type of error?","concept":"A Type I error is rejecting the null hypothesis when it is actually true, that is, a false positive; its probability is the significance level alpha. This contrasts with a Type II error, which is failing to reject a false null hypothesis (a false negative).","traps":{"b":"A Type II error is failing to reject a null hypothesis that is actually false, the opposite mistake to the one described.","c":"Sampling error is the natural variability between a sample statistic and the true population parameter, not a hypothesis-testing decision error.","d":"Standard error is a measure of the precision of a sample estimate, not a category of decision error."}},{"q":"Which statistical measure tells how far individual data points are dispersed from the mean expressed in standard deviation units?","concept":"A Z-score (standard score) expresses how far a raw score lies from the mean in units of standard deviation, calculated as (X minus the mean) divided by the standard deviation. Because it is scaled in standard deviation units, it allows scores from different distributions to be compared directly.","traps":{"a":"Variance is the average of squared deviations from the mean and is expressed in squared units, not standard deviation units per data point.","c":"The coefficient of variation is the ratio of the standard deviation to the mean, used to compare relative dispersion across datasets, not to locate a single point.","d":"Standard error describes the variability of a sample statistic (like the mean) across repeated samples, not the position of an individual score."}},{"q":"When the assumption of normal distribution is met and you want to compare the means of two independent groups, which statistical test is most appropriate?","concept":"When data are normally distributed and the goal is to compare the means of two independent (unrelated) groups, the independent-samples t-test is the appropriate parametric test. If the assumption of equal variances is violated, Welch's t-test is used instead.","traps":{"a":"The Mann-Whitney U test is the non-parametric alternative, used when normality cannot be assumed, so it is unnecessary here.","c":"The Chi-square test compares frequencies of categorical variables, not means of continuous data.","d":"The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is non-parametric and for paired data, not independent groups under normality."}},{"q":"Interaction effects between two or more independent variables can be detected and analyzed using which statistical technique?","concept":"Factorial ANOVA involves two or more independent variables (factors) and can test not only their separate main effects but also their interaction, that is, whether the effect of one IV on the dependent variable depends on the level of another. Detecting interaction effects requires this factorial structure.","traps":{"a":"Correlation analysis measures the strength and direction of a linear association between variables and cannot partition main effects from interactions.","b":"The independent-samples t-test handles only one independent variable with two levels, so it cannot test interactions.","d":"The Chi-square test examines associations between categorical variables and does not model interaction effects on a continuous outcome."}},{"q":"If a researcher wants to subtract out the influence of number of children from the relationship between marital stress and length of marriage, which statistical method is most appropriate?","concept":"Partial correlation measures the association between two variables (marital stress and length of marriage) while statistically holding constant, or subtracting out, the influence of a third variable (number of children). This isolates the direct relationship after removing the confound.","traps":{"a":"Biserial correlation estimates the relationship between a continuous variable and an artificially dichotomized one, not the control of a confounder.","b":"Multiple correlation relates one dependent variable to several predictors jointly, rather than removing the effect of a single control variable.","c":"Linear correlation simply measures the bivariate association without controlling for any third variable."}},{"q":"A researcher wants to calculate the degrees of freedom (df) for a 3 × 4 contingency table in a Chi-square test. The df is:","concept":"For a chi-square test on a contingency table, degrees of freedom equal (number of rows minus 1) multiplied by (number of columns minus 1). For a 3 by 4 table this is 2 multiplied by 3, giving 6.","traps":{"a":"12 is simply the total number of cells (3 times 4), not the degrees of freedom.","b":"7 does not follow the (r minus 1)(c minus 1) formula and likely comes from adding rather than the correct calculation.","d":"11 is the total number of cells minus 1, which is the wrong rule for a two-way contingency table."}},{"q":"In Phenylketonuria (PKU), the body fails to convert phenylalanine into which substance due to a deficiency of phenylalanine hydroxylase?","concept":"Phenylketonuria is an autosomal recessive disorder in which a deficiency of the enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase prevents the conversion of phenylalanine into tyrosine. Phenylalanine therefore accumulates to toxic levels while tyrosine falls, and untreated cases develop intellectual disability, which newborn screening and a low-phenylalanine diet prevent.","traps":{"a":"Urea is the nitrogen waste product of the urea cycle and is unrelated to the phenylalanine hydroxylase reaction.","c":"Tryptophan is a separate essential amino acid and the precursor of serotonin, not the product blocked in PKU.","d":"Histidine is another amino acid and is not the substance that phenylalanine is converted into."}},{"q":"What is the alternative name for adrenaline, the hormone released by the adrenal medulla during the fight-or-flight response?","concept":"Adrenaline and epinephrine are two names for the same catecholamine hormone released by the adrenal medulla during sympathetic fight-or-flight (Walter Cannon) activation. Epinephrine is the term standardized in medical and pharmacological usage.","traps":{"a":"Norepinephrine (noradrenaline) is a closely related but distinct catecholamine and is not simply the alternative name for adrenaline.","c":"Dopamine is a precursor catecholamine and neurotransmitter, not another name for adrenaline.","d":"Cortisol is a glucocorticoid released by the adrenal cortex, not the medulla, and is chemically unrelated to adrenaline."}},{"q":"Which of the following hormones is NOT secreted by the anterior pituitary gland?","concept":"The anterior pituitary secretes growth hormone, adrenocorticotropic hormone, thyroid stimulating hormone, follicle stimulating hormone, luteinizing hormone, and prolactin. Melatonin is not among them; it is produced by the pineal gland to regulate the sleep-wake cycle.","traps":{"a":"Growth hormone is a genuine anterior pituitary hormone, so it is not the exception.","b":"Adrenocorticotropic hormone is secreted by the anterior pituitary to drive the adrenal cortex, so it is not the exception.","d":"Thyroid stimulating hormone is an anterior pituitary product acting on the thyroid, so it is not the exception."}},{"q":"Which brain structure serves as the primary control center for integrating the endocrine, visceral, and autonomic nervous systems?","concept":"The hypothalamus is the master integrating center that links the nervous and endocrine systems, controlling the pituitary gland and governing homeostatic functions such as temperature, hunger, thirst, autonomic outflow, and the sleep-wake cycle. This coordinating role over endocrine, visceral, and autonomic systems makes it the answer.","traps":{"a":"The cerebellum coordinates movement, balance, and motor learning, not endocrine and autonomic integration.","c":"The thalamus is chiefly a sensory relay station directing signals to the cortex, not the endocrine control center.","d":"The brainstem controls vital reflexes like breathing and heart rate but does not serve as the master endocrine-autonomic integrator."}},{"q":"In neuroimaging, which technique directly measures the electrical activity of the brain with high temporal resolution, akin to ECG in cardiology?","concept":"Electroencephalography (EEG) records the summed electrical activity of cortical neurons through scalp electrodes with millisecond temporal resolution, directly analogous to how the ECG records the heart's electrical activity. Its strength is temporal precision, though its spatial resolution is poor.","traps":{"a":"fMRI measures the blood-oxygen-level-dependent signal indirectly and offers high spatial but poor temporal resolution, and it is not electrical.","b":"PET tracks metabolic or receptor activity using radioactive tracers, not direct electrical activity, and has slow temporal resolution.","d":"MEG measures the magnetic fields generated by neural currents rather than the electrical potentials themselves, and is far less commonly used."}},{"q":"The lateral ventricles, the largest of the ventricles in the brain, are filled with:","concept":"All four ventricles of the brain, including the large paired lateral ventricles, are filled with cerebrospinal fluid produced by the choroid plexus. CSF cushions the brain, removes waste, and supports nutrient exchange, flowing from the lateral ventricles to the third and fourth ventricles.","traps":{"a":"Blood circulates in vessels and sinuses, not within the ventricular cavities.","c":"There is no fluid called 'synaptic fluid'; the ventricles contain CSF.","d":"Myelin is the lipid sheath insulating axons, not a fluid that fills ventricles."}},{"q":"Patient H.M. underwent surgical removal of the medial temporal lobes (incl. hippocampus) for epilepsy. His most profound deficit was in:","concept":"After bilateral removal of his medial temporal lobes, including the hippocampus, patient Henry Molaison (H.M.) was unable to form new declarative (conscious) memories, a condition called anterograde amnesia. This case established the hippocampus as essential for consolidating new long-term memories, while sparing working memory and most older memories.","traps":{"a":"H.M.'s procedural (skill) memory was preserved, as shown by his improvement on motor tasks, so it was not his profound deficit.","b":"Semantic memory acquired before surgery remained largely intact; his core problem was forming new memories, not accessing old facts.","d":"His retrograde amnesia was comparatively limited; the hallmark and most profound deficit was the anterograde loss."}},{"q":"Despite his severe amnesia, H.M. learned and improved on a mirror-tracing task across days, with no conscious recollection of practising it. This demonstrates the preservation of:","concept":"Despite being unable to consciously recall practising, H.M. (studied by William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957) steadily improved on the mirror-tracing task, demonstrating intact procedural (skill) memory. Procedural memory depends on the basal ganglia and cerebellum rather than the hippocampus, and this dissociation is key evidence for separate memory systems.","traps":{"a":"Episodic memory (recollection of specific events) was severely impaired in H.M., as he could not remember the practice sessions.","b":"Declarative memory, the conscious memory of facts and events, was exactly the system he had lost.","d":"Semantic memory, general knowledge of facts, is a declarative system and was not what the mirror-tracing task tapped."}},{"q":"Loss of memories for events that happened BEFORE a brain injury, such as a concussion or stroke, is termed:","concept":"Retrograde amnesia is the loss of memories for events that occurred before the brain injury, whereas anterograde amnesia is the inability to form new memories after it. According to Ribot's law, more recent memories are typically lost first while remote memories are relatively spared.","traps":{"a":"Anterograde amnesia is the inability to lay down new memories after the injury, the opposite temporal direction to what is described.","c":"Source amnesia is forgetting where or how information was learned while retaining the information itself, not a time-based loss around an injury.","d":"Dissociative amnesia is a psychogenic memory loss for personal information triggered by trauma or stress, not caused by a physical brain injury."}},{"q":"The primary brain region responsible for the comprehension of spoken and written language is:","concept":"Wernicke's area, in the left superior temporal gyrus, is responsible for the comprehension of spoken and written language. Damage produces fluent but meaningless speech (word salad) with impaired understanding, and its connection to Broca's area via the arcuate fasciculus supports the flow from comprehension to production.","traps":{"a":"Broca's area in the left inferior frontal gyrus governs speech production, not comprehension.","c":"The primary auditory cortex (Heschl's gyrus) detects and processes sound, but does not extract linguistic meaning.","d":"The angular gyrus contributes to reading, writing, and integrating information, but it is not the primary language-comprehension center."}},{"q":"Damage to Broca's area typically results in:","concept":"Broca's aphasia is an expressive (non-fluent) aphasia resulting from damage to Broca's area, marked by effortful, telegraphic, agrammatic speech while comprehension remains relatively intact. Because they understand their errors, patients are typically aware and frustrated.","traps":{"a":"Fluent speech with poor comprehension describes Wernicke's (receptive) aphasia, not Broca's aphasia.","c":"Anomia (word-finding or naming difficulty) is a broader symptom seen across many aphasias and is not the defining feature of Broca's aphasia.","d":"Prosopagnosia, the inability to recognize faces, results from damage to the fusiform face area, unrelated to Broca's area."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] The reticular activating system (RAS), located in the brainstem, is crucial for the regulation of:","concept":"The ascending reticular activating system (Giuseppe Moruzzi and Horace Magoun, 1949) in the brainstem regulates cortical arousal, wakefulness, and attention, and controls transitions between sleep and waking. Severe damage to it can produce coma, underscoring its role in maintaining consciousness.","traps":{"a":"Fine motor coordination is a function of the cerebellum and basal ganglia, not the reticular activating system.","c":"Emotional memory is associated with the amygdala and hippocampus, not the RAS.","d":"Balance and posture are governed by the cerebellum and vestibular system, not the arousal-regulating RAS."}},{"q":"A 45-year-old man with chronic schizophrenia on long-term antipsychotics presents with repetitive, involuntary lip-smacking, tongue movements, and grimacing. This is most likely:","concept":"Tardive dyskinesia is a late-onset, often irreversible movement disorder caused by chronic dopamine D2 receptor blockade from long-term antipsychotic use, presenting with repetitive involuntary oro-facial movements such as lip-smacking, tongue protrusion, and grimacing. Risk is higher with typical (first-generation) antipsychotics, and it is monitored using the Abnormal Involuntary Movement Scale.","traps":{"a":"Akathisia is a subjective inner restlessness with an urge to move, not the repetitive oro-facial dyskinesia described.","b":"Acute dystonia is a sudden, early sustained muscle contraction (such as torticollis or oculogyric crisis) occurring soon after starting medication, not a late-onset movement.","d":"Drug-induced parkinsonism presents with tremor, rigidity, and bradykinesia, not the choreoathetoid oro-facial movements of tardive dyskinesia."}},{"q":"What is the primary function of the myelin sheath surrounding nerve axons?","concept":"The myelin sheath insulates axons and enables saltatory conduction, in which the action potential jumps between the Nodes of Ranvier, dramatically increasing the speed of nerve impulse conduction (roughly 10 to 100 times). Demyelinating diseases such as multiple sclerosis slow this transmission and cause neurological deficits.","traps":{"a":"Structural support to neurons is provided mainly by glial cells and the cytoskeleton, not the primary role of myelin.","c":"Neurotransmitters are stored in synaptic vesicles at axon terminals, not within the myelin sheath.","d":"Action potentials are generated at the axon hillock and Nodes of Ranvier by ion channels; myelin speeds conduction rather than generating the impulse."}},{"q":"A patient presents with severe memory deficits, confabulation, and nystagmus, with a history of chronic alcohol use. This clinical picture is classic for:","concept":"The triad of severe anterograde memory deficits, confabulation, and eye signs such as nystagmus in a chronic alcohol user is classic for Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency. Wernicke's encephalopathy presents acutely with confusion, ataxia, and ophthalmoplegia, and if untreated progresses to the amnestic, confabulating Korsakoff psychosis; treatment is intravenous thiamine given before glucose.","traps":{"a":"Alzheimer's disease is a gradual degenerative dementia of older age without the nystagmus or alcohol-linked thiamine deficiency seen here.","c":"Vascular dementia produces stepwise cognitive decline linked to cerebrovascular events, not the confabulation-plus-nystagmus picture of thiamine deficiency.","d":"Frontotemporal dementia presents with early personality change and language decline, not the acute ocular signs and confabulation described."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] 'Folie à deux,' or Shared Psychotic Disorder, is characterized by:","concept":"Folie a deux (Shared Psychotic Disorder) occurs when a dominant, psychotic 'inducer' transmits a fixed delusional belief to a passive, closely associated 'recipient' who normally would not be psychotic. The relationship (often shared isolation and dependency) is the vehicle: separating the pair frequently resolves the recipient's symptoms. The Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition removed it as a standalone entity (folded under delusional disorder / other specified psychotic disorder), but the term remains clinically useful.","traps":{"a":"Two independent simultaneous psychoses describes coincident illness, not the defining transmission of one person's delusion to another within a relationship.","c":"A delusion shared by a whole cultural group is closer to a culturally sanctioned belief or mass phenomenon, not the dyadic inducer-recipient pattern of folie a deux.","d":"A brief stress-triggered psychosis in two people describes shared acute stress reactions, missing the core mechanism of belief transmission from a dominant inducer."}},{"q":"The specific phobia of pain is known as:","concept":"The specific phobia of pain is algophobia, from the Greek 'algos' meaning pain. Specific phobias are named by their Greek or Latin root plus '-phobia', so knowing the root reliably identifies the fear. Algophobia is a marked, persistent fear of experiencing or anticipating physical pain.","traps":{"a":"Nosophobia is the fear of having or developing a specific disease (from 'nosos', disease), not the fear of pain.","c":"Cynophobia is the fear of dogs (from 'kyon/kynos', dog), an animal-type specific phobia.","d":"Hematophobia is the fear of blood (from 'haima', blood), part of the blood-injection-injury phobia cluster."}},{"q":"A client reports persistent admiration-seeking but presents with excessive emotionality, attention-seeking, and seductive behavior, rather than grandiosity. This is most characteristic of:","concept":"Histrionic personality disorder (Cluster B) is defined by pervasive excessive emotionality and attention-seeking: theatrical, seductive or provocative behavior, shallow and rapidly shifting affect, suggestibility, and discomfort when not the center of attention. The key discriminator here is that the person seeks admiration through dramatic emotional display rather than through beliefs of superiority. That absence of grandiosity and entitlement rules out narcissism.","traps":{"a":"Narcissistic personality disorder also craves admiration but does so through grandiosity, entitlement, and a need for status, not theatrical seductiveness and shallow emotionality.","c":"Borderline personality disorder centers on identity disturbance, abandonment fears, affective instability, and impulsivity, not primarily attention-seeking through seductive display.","d":"Dependent personality disorder involves clinging, submissive behavior and needing others to take responsibility, not dramatic attention-seeking."}},{"q":"A client reports persistent and frightening experiences of their surroundings seeming unreal, distorted, or dreamlike. This symptom is best described as:","concept":"Derealization is a dissociative experience in which the external world feels unreal, distorted, dreamlike, foggy, or lifeless, though the person retains intact reality testing (they know it only feels that way). It is distinguished from depersonalization, which targets the self rather than the surroundings. Both can co-occur in depersonalization/derealization disorder, panic attacks, and post-traumatic stress disorder.","traps":{"b":"Depersonalization is feeling detached from or unreal within one's own self, thoughts, or body, whereas the described symptom concerns the surroundings.","c":"Deja vu is a fleeting sense that a novel situation has been experienced before, not a persistent dreamlike unreality of the environment.","d":"A hallucination is a false sensory perception without an external stimulus; here perception is accurate but feels unreal, so reality testing is preserved."}},{"q":"A patient presents with a fixed delusional belief that their spouse is being unfaithful, despite no evidence. This specific form of delusional jealousy is known as:","concept":"Othello syndrome (morbid or delusional jealousy) is a fixed, false belief that one's partner is being unfaithful, held with delusional conviction despite absent or contradictory evidence. It is named after Shakespeare's jealous Othello and can drive dangerous stalking or violence. It is classified among the delusional misidentification and content-specific delusional syndromes.","traps":{"a":"Capgras syndrome is the delusion that a familiar person has been replaced by an identical impostor, not a belief about infidelity.","c":"Fregoli syndrome is the delusion that different strangers are actually one persecutor in disguise, a misidentification of identity rather than jealousy.","d":"De Clerambault's syndrome (erotomania) is the delusion that another person, often of higher status, is secretly in love with the patient, the opposite theme to jealous suspicion."}},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with mild cognitive impairment insists that his wife of 40 years is an identical 'duplicate' impostor, despite acknowledging she looks exactly like her. Most likely diagnosis:","concept":"Capgras syndrome is the delusional belief that a familiar person (here the wife) has been replaced by an identical-looking impostor or 'duplicate', even while the patient admits the resemblance is perfect. It is thought to arise from a disconnection between intact face recognition (ventral visual stream) and the emotional response that normally accompanies recognizing a loved one, so the face looks right but 'feels' wrong. It commonly appears in dementia, schizophrenia, and other neurological conditions.","traps":{"a":"Fregoli syndrome is the reverse error: believing strangers are one familiar person in disguise, not that a familiar person is an impostor.","c":"Cotard syndrome is a nihilistic delusion of being dead or nonexistent, unrelated to misidentifying another person.","d":"Reduplicative paramnesia is the delusion that a place has been duplicated or relocated, concerning locations rather than identifying a person as an impostor."}},{"q":"Cotard syndrome, a rare neuropsychiatric condition, is primarily characterized by which type of delusional belief?","concept":"Cotard syndrome (walking corpse or nihilistic delusion) is characterized by the fixed belief that one is dead, does not exist, is decaying, or has lost internal organs or one's soul. It reflects an extreme nihilistic delusional content and is most often seen in severe psychotic depression, but also in schizophrenia and neurodegenerative disease. The defining feature is denial of one's own existence or life.","traps":{"a":"Grandiose delusions of special powers are the opposite in content and typify mania or grandiose-type delusional disorder, not Cotard.","b":"Persecutory delusions of being followed define paranoid presentations, not the nihilistic self-denial of Cotard.","d":"Somatic delusions of harboring a serious illness point to illness-themed delusional disorder, distinct from the belief that one is already dead or nonexistent."}},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman presents with sudden right-arm paralysis after an argument; neuro exam is normal and she appears strikingly unconcerned ('It's not really bothering me'). This presentation is most characteristic of:","concept":"Conversion disorder (functional neurological symptom disorder) presents with neurological symptoms such as sudden paralysis, blindness, or seizures that are incompatible with recognized neurological disease and are often precipitated by psychological stress (here an argument). A classic accompanying sign is la belle indifference, a striking lack of concern about a dramatic deficit. The symptoms are not consciously produced, distinguishing it from feigning.","traps":{"b":"Borderline personality disorder involves affective instability and interpersonal chaos, not the sudden loss of a neurological function with normal exam findings.","c":"Major depressive disorder is a mood syndrome; it would not produce isolated limb paralysis with intact neurology and indifferent affect.","d":"Factitious disorder involves deliberate fabrication or induction of symptoms to assume the sick role, whereas conversion symptoms are unconsciously produced."}},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman exhibits distinct personalities with their own names, memories, and behaviour, with amnesia between episodes; she finds clothes she does not remember buying. Most consistent diagnosis:","concept":"Dissociative identity disorder is defined by two or more distinct identity states (each with its own name, memories, and behavior) accompanied by recurrent gaps in autobiographical memory that exceed ordinary forgetting, such as finding purchases one does not remember making. It is strongly linked to severe, chronic early childhood trauma. The presence of multiple identities plus inter-identity amnesia is the diagnostic hallmark.","traps":{"a":"Dissociative amnesia is an inability to recall important personal information without distinct alternate identities, so it cannot explain the separate named personalities.","c":"Depersonalization/derealization disorder involves feelings of unreality of self or surroundings without amnesia or distinct identity states.","d":"Dissociative fugue involves sudden purposeful travel with amnesia for one's past, a single episode rather than recurring distinct identities."}},{"q":"Persistent preoccupation with perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance that are not observable to others is characteristic of which disorder?","concept":"Body dysmorphic disorder is a preoccupation with one or more perceived defects or flaws in physical appearance that are unobservable or appear only slight to others, coupled with repetitive behaviors such as mirror checking, grooming, or reassurance seeking. It sits within the obsessive-compulsive and related disorders chapter of the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition and carries a notably high suicide risk. The focus on appearance flaws distinguishes it from illness- or symptom-focused disorders.","traps":{"a":"Somatic symptom disorder centers on distressing physical symptoms and excessive health-related thoughts and behaviors, not on perceived appearance defects.","c":"Illness anxiety disorder is preoccupation with having or acquiring a serious illness, not with a flaw in how one looks.","d":"Conversion disorder involves neurological-type functional deficits, unrelated to preoccupation with appearance."}},{"q":"Which of the following best describes the concept of 'ego-dystonic' as it applies to obsessions in OCD?","concept":"Ego-dystonic means an experience is felt as alien, intrusive, unwanted, and inconsistent with one's sense of self, which is exactly how obsessions in obsessive-compulsive disorder are experienced: the person recognizes the thoughts as their own but resists and is distressed by them. This contrasts with ego-syntonic experiences, which feel congruent with the self (as in many personality disorder traits). The distress and rejection of the thought define ego-dystonia.","traps":{"a":"Experiencing obsessions as a core part of identity describes ego-syntonic content, the opposite of the ego-dystonic quality of OCD obsessions.","c":"Deriving pleasure from the thoughts contradicts the distressing, unwanted nature of obsessions.","d":"Being completely unaware that the obsessions are unreasonable describes poor or absent insight, which is a separate specifier, not the meaning of ego-dystonic."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] 'Dementia praecox,' which translates to 'premature dementia,' is historically most directly associated with which contemporary diagnostic category?","concept":"Dementia praecox ('premature dementia') is the historical forerunner of schizophrenia. Emil Kraepelin coined the term to describe an early-onset, deteriorating psychotic illness, and Eugen Bleuler later renamed it schizophrenia in 1908, emphasizing the splitting of psychic functions and his four A's (loosening of associations, affect disturbance, ambivalence, autism). The term maps onto the modern category of schizophrenia, not a dementia.","traps":{"a":"Alzheimer's disease is a neurodegenerative dementia of late life; despite the word 'dementia' in the old term, dementia praecox is a psychotic, not neurodegenerative, illness.","b":"Bipolar I disorder derives historically from Kraepelin's separate category of manic-depressive insanity, which he explicitly distinguished from dementia praecox.","d":"Frontotemporal dementia is a genuine neurodegenerative dementia and is unrelated to the historical concept of dementia praecox."}},{"q":"According to the ICD-10, what is the minimum duration of symptoms required for a definitive diagnosis of Schizophrenia?","concept":"Under the International Classification of Diseases, Tenth Revision, a definitive diagnosis of schizophrenia requires the characteristic symptoms to be present for most of the time during at least one month. This one-month threshold is a common exam contrast with the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition, which requires at least six months of total disturbance including at least one month of active-phase symptoms. The question specifies ICD-10, so the answer is one month.","traps":{"a":"One week corresponds roughly to brief psychotic disorder or acute presentations, not the ICD-10 threshold for schizophrenia.","c":"Three months matches ICD durations for some other conditions but is not the schizophrenia requirement.","d":"Six months is the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual, Fifth Edition criterion, not the ICD-10 one that the question asks about."}},{"q":"Which of the following is considered a negative symptom of schizophrenia, characterized by reduced quantity and quality of verbal expression?","concept":"Alogia (poverty of speech) is a negative symptom of schizophrenia, reflecting a reduction in the quantity and content of spontaneous verbal output. Negative symptoms represent a loss or diminution of normal function and are often summarized as the five A's: alogia, anhedonia, avolition, affective flattening, and asociality. This contrasts with positive symptoms, which are additions to normal experience.","traps":{"a":"Auditory hallucinations are a positive symptom, an added abnormal perception rather than a reduction of function.","c":"Persecutory delusions are positive symptoms, representing added false beliefs.","d":"Disorganized behavior is generally classed among positive or disorganization symptoms, not a negative symptom of reduced speech."}},{"q":"A patient with Korsakoff's psychosis fabricates detailed and believable stories about their past to fill memory gaps, without intent to deceive. This is:","concept":"Confabulation is the production of false or distorted memories without any conscious intent to deceive, typically to fill gaps left by amnesia; the patient genuinely believes the fabricated account. It is a hallmark of Korsakoff's syndrome and is also seen with frontal lobe damage and anterior communicating artery aneurysm. The absence of deceptive intent distinguishes it from lying and malingering.","traps":{"b":"Confusion is a global disturbance of clear thinking and orientation, not the specific filling of memory gaps with fabricated stories.","c":"Delirium is an acute, fluctuating disturbance of attention and awareness, a broader state rather than the specific memory phenomenon described.","d":"Malingering is the deliberate fabrication of symptoms for external gain, which requires the conscious intent to deceive that confabulation lacks."}},{"q":"According to Thomas and Chess's temperament model, a child who is generally negative in mood, slow to adapt, and has irregular biological rhythms would be classified as:","concept":"In Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess's New York Longitudinal Study temperament model, the 'difficult' child is characterized by predominantly negative mood, slow adaptability to change, withdrawal from new stimuli, high intensity of reactions, and irregular biological rhythms (sleep, feeding). Their framework also describes 'easy' and 'slow-to-warm-up' patterns, with 'goodness of fit' between temperament and environment predicting adjustment. The cluster of negativity, poor adaptability, and irregular rhythms defines the difficult temperament.","traps":{"a":"The easy temperament shows positive mood, regular rhythms, and ready adaptability, the opposite of the described profile.","c":"The slow-to-warm-up child is mild and initially withdrawn but has more regular rhythms and adapts gradually, lacking the pervasive negativity and irregularity of the difficult type.","d":"Insecure attachment is a relational classification from attachment theory (John Bowlby), not a temperament category in the Thomas and Chess model."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] The 'Marshmallow Test,' pioneered by Walter Mischel, primarily measures a child's capacity for:","concept":"Walter Mischel's marshmallow test presented preschoolers with an immediate small reward and the option to wait alone for a larger one, measuring the capacity for delay of gratification, that is, forgoing an immediate reward for a greater future one. Longer wait times predicted later self-regulation and achievement outcomes, though later replications with socioeconomic controls found smaller effects. The construct assessed is specifically delay of gratification.","traps":{"a":"Sustained attention concerns maintaining focus over time, which is not what the wait-for-a-bigger-reward paradigm was designed to measure.","b":"Resistance to temptation is a loose description, but the precise psychological construct Mischel named and measured is delay of gratification.","d":"Impulse control in social settings narrows the construct to interpersonal contexts, whereas the task measured general self-imposed delay for a future reward."}},{"q":"A child sees two identical balls of clay; one is rolled into a sausage. When asked if the amount is the same, the child says 'yes.' Per Piaget, this child is most likely in which stage?","concept":"Conservation, the understanding that quantity stays the same despite changes in shape or arrangement, emerges in Jean Piaget's concrete operational stage (about 7 to 11 years). A child who correctly says the rolled-out clay still has the same amount has decentered and can mentally reverse the transformation, hallmarks of concrete operations. Preoperational children typically fail this by centering on one dimension such as length.","traps":{"a":"The sensorimotor stage (birth to about 2 years) is dominated by object permanence and motor exploration, far too early for verbal conservation reasoning.","b":"A preoperational child would usually be misled by the change in shape and deny that the amount is conserved, so success here points beyond this stage.","d":"The formal operational stage adds abstract and hypothetical reasoning; conservation of mass is mastered earlier, in concrete operations."}},{"q":"A 5-year-old sorts cards by colour but cannot then re-sort the same deck by suit. This inflexibility is an example of:","concept":"Centration is Jean Piaget's term for the preoperational tendency to focus on a single salient feature of a situation while ignoring other relevant dimensions. The child who can sort only by color and cannot flexibly re-sort by suit is fixed on one attribute, unable to shift, which is centration. Overcoming it (decentration) is part of the transition into concrete operations.","traps":{"a":"Egocentrism is the inability to take another person's perspective, not the fixation on one feature of a task.","b":"Animism is attributing life and intentions to inanimate objects, unrelated to sorting inflexibility.","d":"Transductive reasoning is drawing links between specific events without true logical cause, not the focusing-on-one-dimension error described."}},{"q":"According to Erikson, the primary crisis to be resolved in adolescence (12-18 years) is:","concept":"In Erik Erikson's psychosocial stages, adolescence (roughly 12 to 18 years) centers on the crisis of identity versus role confusion, in which the young person works to form a coherent, integrated sense of self. Successful resolution yields the virtue of fidelity. James Marcia later operationalized this into four identity statuses (diffusion, foreclosure, moratorium, achievement).","traps":{"a":"Industry versus inferiority is the school-age stage (about 6 to 11 years), concerning competence and productivity, not the adolescent identity task.","c":"Intimacy versus isolation is the young-adult stage focused on forming committed relationships, which follows adolescence.","d":"Generativity versus stagnation is the middle-adulthood crisis about contributing to the next generation, much later than adolescence."}},{"q":"A middle-aged adult finds meaning by mentoring younger colleagues and contributing to the next generation. According to Erikson, this individual is successfully navigating:","concept":"Mentoring younger colleagues and contributing to the next generation is the essence of generativity in Erik Erikson's middle-adulthood stage (about 40 to 65 years), the crisis of generativity versus stagnation. Successful resolution produces the virtue of care, whereas failure leads to self-absorption and stagnation. Guiding and investing in others is the defining marker of generativity.","traps":{"a":"Intimacy versus isolation is the earlier young-adult stage about forming close partnerships, not about mentoring the next generation.","c":"Ego integrity versus despair is the late-life stage of reviewing and accepting one's life, which comes after middle adulthood.","d":"Identity versus role confusion is the adolescent stage of self-definition, far earlier than the described middle-aged mentoring."}},{"q":"According to Erikson, the primary virtue that emerges from successfully resolving 'Trust vs. Mistrust' in infancy is:","concept":"In Erik Erikson's first psychosocial stage, trust versus mistrust in infancy, consistent and responsive caregiving lets the infant develop basic trust, and the virtue that emerges is hope. Each Erikson stage yields a specific virtue when resolved well, and hope is the earliest of these. Reliable care teaches the infant that the world can be depended upon.","traps":{"a":"Will is the virtue of the autonomy versus shame and doubt stage in toddlerhood, not of infancy.","b":"Purpose arises from the initiative versus guilt stage in the preschool years.","d":"Competence is the virtue of the industry versus inferiority stage in the school-age years."}},{"q":"According to Piaget, abstract reasoning and hypothetical thinking abilities are primarily developed during which developmental stage?","concept":"Abstract reasoning, hypothetical and hypothetico-deductive thinking, and propositional logic are the defining achievements of Jean Piaget's formal operational stage, beginning around 11 years and into adolescence. At this stage a person can reason about possibilities and abstractions rather than only concrete, present realities. This capacity for 'what if' systematic reasoning is what distinguishes formal from concrete operations.","traps":{"a":"The sensorimotor stage involves early sensory and motor learning and object permanence, with no abstract thought.","b":"The preoperational stage features symbolic play and language but is limited by egocentrism and centration, lacking logical reasoning.","c":"The concrete operational stage allows logical reasoning only about tangible, concrete material, not abstract or hypothetical propositions."}},{"q":"Which parenting style is characterized by emotional support, clear boundaries, and accountability for children's behaviour?","concept":"In Diana Baumrind's typology, the authoritative parenting style combines high warmth and emotional support with clear, firm boundaries and expectations of accountability. It consistently predicts the best child outcomes in competence, self-regulation, and social adjustment. The balance of responsiveness plus reasonable demandingness is its defining signature.","traps":{"a":"Authoritarian parenting is high in control but low in warmth, demanding obedience without emotional support, so it lacks the warmth described.","c":"Permissive parenting is high in warmth but low in control, indulgent and lacking the clear boundaries and accountability described.","d":"Neglectful (uninvolved) parenting is low in both warmth and control, the opposite of the supportive, boundaried style described."}},{"q":"Mary Ainsworth is best known for her work on which aspect of developmental psychology?","concept":"Mary Ainsworth is best known for her contributions to attachment theory, particularly the Strange Situation procedure, which classified infant-caregiver attachment into secure, insecure-avoidant, and insecure-resistant patterns. Her work operationalized and extended John Bowlby's attachment ideas, and a disorganized category was later added by Mary Main and Judith Solomon. Attachment is the domain that defines her legacy.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive development is associated with Jean Piaget, not Ainsworth.","b":"Moral development is associated with Lawrence Kohlberg (and Jean Piaget's earlier work), not Ainsworth.","d":"Language acquisition is linked to theorists such as Noam Chomsky, not to Ainsworth's attachment research."}},{"q":"A 10-year-old illiterate child from a rural area is brought for intellectual assessment. Which test would be MOST appropriate to minimize cultural and educational bias?","concept":"Bhatia's Battery of Performance Tests of Intelligence is the most appropriate choice for an illiterate rural child because its five performance subtests require no reading or writing and it carries Indian norms, minimizing cultural and educational bias. Performance-based, nonverbal assessment avoids penalizing a child for lack of schooling or literacy. This makes it the standard Indian tool for low-literacy assessment.","traps":{"b":"The Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children, Fourth Edition, is heavily verbal and Western-normed, disadvantaging an illiterate child from a rural Indian background.","c":"The Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale, Fifth Edition, is also verbally loaded and Western-normed, poorly suited to minimizing cultural and educational bias here.","d":"Malin's Intelligence Scale for Indian Children, though Indian-adapted, contains substantial verbal subtests requiring language competence that an illiterate child may lack."}},{"q":"A test developer administers a new anxiety scale to a group, then re-administers the same scale two weeks later, correlating the two sets of scores. This establishes:","concept":"Administering the same test to the same group on two occasions and correlating the two sets of scores establishes test-retest reliability, an index of the stability or consistency of scores over time (usually a Pearson correlation). It answers whether the measure yields the same result when repeated. The two-week interval and re-administration of the identical scale are the giveaways.","traps":{"a":"Split-half reliability is estimated from a single administration by correlating two halves of the test, a measure of internal consistency, not stability over time.","b":"Inter-rater reliability concerns agreement between different scorers or observers, not repeated administration to the same people.","d":"Parallel forms reliability uses two equivalent versions of the test rather than the same test re-administered."}},{"q":"A clinician wants a client to reveal characteristic ways of thinking and feeling in close relationships by telling stories about ambiguous pictures. The MOST appropriate test is:","concept":"Henry Murray's Thematic Apperception Test presents ambiguous interpersonal pictures and asks the person to tell stories about them; the narratives reveal characteristic needs, drives, conflicts, and relationship themes through projection. It is the projective test of choice when the goal is to elicit interpersonal and motivational content. The storytelling-to-ambiguous-pictures format defines the TAT.","traps":{"a":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test uses inkblots and focuses on perception and form quality rather than eliciting interpersonal narratives.","c":"The Sentence Completion Test uses stems to complete rather than storytelling about pictures, giving less rich narrative material about relationships.","d":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) is an objective, self-report questionnaire, not a projective storytelling task."}},{"q":"On the WAIS-IV, a scaled score of 10 on a subtest indicates performance that is:","concept":"On the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition, individual subtest scaled scores have a mean of 10 and a standard deviation of 3, so a scaled score of 10 is exactly average, at the 50th percentile. This is distinct from the index and full-scale composite scores, which use a mean of 100 and standard deviation of 15. A score of 10 sits right at the center of the distribution.","traps":{"a":"The 16th percentile corresponds to a scaled score of about 7 (one standard deviation below the mean), not 10.","c":"The 84th percentile corresponds to a scaled score of about 13 (one standard deviation above the mean), not 10.","d":"The 2nd percentile corresponds to about two standard deviations below the mean (a scaled score near 4), far below the average score of 10."}},{"q":"In the WAIS-IV, how many CORE subtests are there in the Verbal Comprehension Index and the Perceptual Reasoning Index respectively?","concept":"On the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale, Fourth Edition, the Verbal Comprehension Index has three core subtests (Similarities, Vocabulary, Information) and the Perceptual Reasoning Index has three core subtests (Block Design, Matrix Reasoning, Visual Puzzles), giving three and three. The Working Memory Index and Processing Speed Index each add two more core subtests. So the correct pairing is three verbal comprehension and three perceptual reasoning.","traps":{"a":"Three and four misstates the perceptual reasoning core count, which is three, not four.","c":"Four and three overstates the verbal comprehension core, which has three core subtests, not four.","d":"Two and four is wrong on both indices, understating verbal comprehension and overstating perceptual reasoning."}},{"q":"Cattell's 16PF (16 Personality Factor Questionnaire) is primarily designed to measure:","concept":"The 16PF (16 Personality Factor Questionnaire), built by Raymond Cattell, uses factor analysis to reduce the surface language of personality into 16 underlying source traits (for example warmth, reasoning, dominance, vigilance). It is a self-report inventory of normal-range personality, organized hierarchically so the 16 primary traits load onto 5 global factors that broadly map to the Big Five. Its purpose is describing personality structure, not measuring cognitive ability, so the correct answer is that it measures personality traits.","traps":{"a":"Fluid intelligence is Cattell's own construct (reasoning with novel problems independent of learning), but that belongs to his theory of intelligence, not to the 16PF personality inventory.","b":"Crystallized intelligence, also a Cattell concept, refers to acquired knowledge and vocabulary and is assessed by ability tests, not by a personality questionnaire.","d":"General intelligence (Spearman's g-factor) is captured by IQ batteries such as the Wechsler scales, whereas the 16PF deliberately measures traits rather than overall cognitive capacity."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT a recognized Rorschach inkblot test scoring system?","concept":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test spawned several competing scoring systems in the twentieth century: Samuel Beck, Bruno Klopfer, Zygmunt Piotrowski, Marguerite Hertz, and the Rapaport-Schafer approach, later consolidated by John Exner into the Comprehensive System and, more recently, R-PAS. Henry Murray is associated with the Thematic Apperception Test (TAT), a different projective built from ambiguous interpersonal pictures, not inkblots. Therefore the Murray system is not a recognized Rorschach scoring system.","traps":{"a":"The Beck system is a genuine early Rorschach scoring approach developed by Samuel Beck, so it is a recognized system, not the exception.","b":"The Exner Comprehensive System is the most widely used empirically grounded Rorschach scoring method, so it does not fit a NOT question.","d":"The Klopfer system is a legitimate Rorschach scoring tradition emphasizing experiential and qualitative aspects, so it is a valid system rather than the odd one out."}},{"q":"The original Stanford-Binet Intelligence Scale used which method for calculating IQ scores?","concept":"The original Stanford-Binet, adapted by Lewis Terman from the Binet-Simon scale, expressed intelligence as the Ratio IQ using William Stern's formula: mental age divided by chronological age, multiplied by 100. This works in childhood but breaks down in adulthood because chronological age keeps climbing while mental age plateaus, deflating scores. David Wechsler later replaced it with the Deviation IQ (mean 100, standard deviation 15), now the standard, so the original method was the Ratio IQ.","traps":{"a":"Deviation IQ with standard scores is Wechsler's later innovation that fixed the ratio method's flaws, not the technique the original Stanford-Binet used.","c":"A percentile ranking system reports relative standing but was not the computational method used to derive the original Stanford-Binet IQ score.","d":"T-score transformation (mean 50, standard deviation 10) is a scaling used on some personality and clinical measures, not the intelligence-quotient formula of the early Stanford-Binet."}},{"q":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) is best described as a(n):","concept":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) is the prototypical objective personality test: it uses a large fixed set of true/false items, standardized scoring, and built-in validity scales (L, F, K) to detect response distortion. Its scales were derived by empirical (criterion) keying, contrasting clinical groups with normals. Because responses are structured and scored to a fixed key rather than interpreted from ambiguous stimuli, it is objective, not projective.","traps":{"a":"Projective personality tests (Rorschach, TAT) rely on ambiguous stimuli and open interpretation, which is the opposite of the MMPI's fixed items and scoring.","c":"An intelligence test measures cognitive ability; the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) measures psychopathology and personality, so this misreads its purpose.","d":"A neuropsychological screening battery assesses brain-behavior functions such as memory and attention, not the self-reported personality and symptom domains the MMPI covers."}},{"q":"A 35-year-old presents with persistent sadness, anhedonia, weight loss, insomnia, worthlessness, and concentration difficulties for 2 months. Which assessment would be MOST appropriate to further evaluate her symptoms?","concept":"The vignette describes a two-month depressive picture (persistent sadness, anhedonia, weight loss, insomnia, worthlessness, poor concentration). When you already suspect depression and want to quantify its severity, the most appropriate instrument is a targeted, validated depression measure. The Beck Depression Inventory (BDI), Aaron T. Beck's 21-item self-report, does exactly this, directly grading symptom severity, so it is the best fit.","traps":{"b":"The Rorschach Inkblot Test is a broad projective measure of personality and thought process, far less efficient or specific than a symptom scale for grading a clear depressive presentation.","c":"The MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) is a wide-band personality and psychopathology inventory; it is overly broad and time-consuming when the goal is simply to quantify depression severity.","d":"The Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale measures IQ and is irrelevant to evaluating mood symptoms in this case."}},{"q":"Which therapeutic modality was pioneered by Maxwell Jones and is characterized by democratic involvement of staff and patients in decision-making and daily activities?","concept":"The therapeutic community was pioneered by Maxwell Jones at the Henderson Hospital and described in his 1953 work. Its defining features are a flattened hierarchy in which staff and patients share decision-making, plus the principles of communalism, permissiveness, democratization, and reality confrontation (later summarized by Rapoport). Because the question stresses democratic involvement of staff and patients, the answer is the therapeutic community.","traps":{"a":"Encounter groups grew from the humanistic and Carl Rogers tradition, focused on intense interpersonal experience and personal growth, not on Jones's democratic ward structure.","c":"Group analysis is S. H. Foulkes's psychoanalytically informed group method, not a whole-ward democratic community model.","d":"Milieu therapy uses the treatment environment therapeutically and overlaps conceptually, but it is a broader later concept, whereas the specific model Maxwell Jones founded is the therapeutic community."}},{"q":"A client with a phobia of dogs is asked to relax and then imagine a graded series of fear-provoking interactions with a dog. This technique is called:","concept":"Pairing relaxation with a graded, imagined hierarchy of feared situations is Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitization. It works through reciprocal inhibition: a relaxation response incompatible with anxiety is conditioned to progressively stronger fear cues, so anxiety is displaced step by step up the hierarchy. The graded, relaxed, imaginal quality is what distinguishes it, making it the correct answer.","traps":{"a":"Flooding exposes the client to the full-intensity feared stimulus at once, without a graded hierarchy or relaxation, which is the opposite of the described technique.","c":"Implosive therapy (Stampfl) uses intense imagined exposure to exaggerated, often psychodynamic fear themes without graduation or relaxation.","d":"Modeling teaches new responses by having the client observe someone else cope with the feared stimulus, not by relaxing through a personal imagined hierarchy."}},{"q":"Viktor Frankl's concept of the 'will to meaning,' the fundamental motivational force in humans to find purpose, is central to which therapeutic school?","concept":"The 'will to meaning,' the idea that the primary human motivation is to find purpose, is the cornerstone of Viktor Frankl's logotherapy, set out in Man's Search for Meaning. Frankl proposed three pathways to meaning: creative work or deeds, experiences and love, and the attitude one takes toward unavoidable suffering. So the correct school is logotherapy.","traps":{"a":"Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (Albert Ellis) targets irrational beliefs through the ABC model, not the search for existential meaning.","c":"Existential-Humanistic therapy shares themes with Frankl but is a broader tradition (Yalom, May); the specific term 'will to meaning' is Frankl's logotherapy.","d":"Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (Steven Hayes) uses values-based action and psychological flexibility within a behavioral framework, distinct from Frankl's concept of the will to meaning."}},{"q":"A therapist helps a client identify and challenge irrational beliefs such as 'I absolutely must be approved by everyone' (musterbation). This therapist is most likely practising:","concept":"Challenging absolutist demands like 'I must be approved by everyone,' which Albert Ellis called musturbation, is the signature of Rational Emotive Behavior Therapy (REBT). Ellis's ABC model holds that an Activating event does not cause the Consequence directly; irrational Beliefs (musts, shoulds, awfulizing, low frustration tolerance) do, and the therapist actively disputes them. The focus on rigid 'must' demands identifies REBT.","traps":{"a":"Beck's Cognitive Therapy targets automatic thoughts and cognitive distortions through collaborative empiricism and gentle Socratic questioning, not the forceful disputation of absolutist 'musts' that is Ellis's trademark.","c":"Dialectical Behavior Therapy (Marsha Linehan) balances acceptance and change with skills training for emotion dysregulation, not disputation of irrational beliefs.","d":"Reality Therapy (William Glasser) focuses on present choices and responsibility to meet basic needs, not on challenging irrational 'must' beliefs."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024, 2025] The Premack Principle states that:","concept":"The Premack Principle, formulated by David Premack in 1965, states that a more probable (higher-frequency) behavior can serve as a reinforcer for a less probable (lower-frequency) behavior. This is 'Grandma's rule': eat your vegetables (low probability) and then you get dessert (high probability). So access to the preferred activity reinforces the less-preferred one, making option a correct.","traps":{"b":"This reverses the principle; a low-probability behavior cannot reinforce a high-probability one, since it is the preferred (high-probability) activity that does the reinforcing.","c":"Reinforcement after every correct response describes a continuous reinforcement schedule, a separate operant concept unrelated to the Premack relationship between behaviors.","d":"The claim that punishment beats reinforcement is a general (and incorrect) behavioral assertion, not a statement of the Premack Principle."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] A therapist uses 'time-out' to manage a child's aggressive outbursts. Time-out is technically classified as:","concept":"In operant terms, the label depends on whether something is added or removed and whether behavior goes up or down. Time-out (time-out from positive reinforcement) removes access to a desirable stimulus such as attention or activity, and it is used to decrease behavior. Removing a pleasant stimulus to reduce behavior is negative punishment, so option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"Positive punishment adds an aversive stimulus (for example a reprimand) to decrease behavior, whereas time-out takes something away rather than adding it.","b":"Negative reinforcement removes an aversive stimulus to increase behavior, the opposite of time-out's goal of reducing behavior.","d":"Positive reinforcement adds a desirable stimulus to increase behavior, which is the reverse of removing reinforcement to suppress aggression."}},{"q":"Which therapeutic approach focuses on the 'real relationship' and the therapist's unconditional positive regard, rather than the working alliance per se?","concept":"Carl Rogers's Person-Centered Therapy holds that the therapeutic relationship itself is the engine of change. Its three core conditions are unconditional positive regard, congruence (genuineness), and accurate empathic understanding. Because the emphasis is on the genuine, prizing relationship and unconditional positive regard rather than on a task-focused working alliance (Edward Bordin, 1979), the answer is Person-Centered Therapy.","traps":{"a":"Psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) works through transference interpretation and insight into unconscious conflict, not through Rogers's unconditional positive regard.","b":"Cognitive-Behavioral Therapy centers on modifying maladaptive thoughts and behaviors, using the relationship as a vehicle rather than the primary change agent.","d":"Interpersonal Therapy (Gerald Klerman and Myrna Weissman) is a structured, time-limited treatment focused on current relationship problems (grief, role transitions, disputes), not on unconditional positive regard as the core mechanism."}},{"q":"In Dialectical Behavior Therapy (DBT), the 'core mindfulness' skills are primarily derived from which tradition?","concept":"Marsha Linehan built Dialectical Behavior Therapy by integrating standard cognitive-behavioral change strategies with acceptance practices drawn from Zen Buddhism. The core mindfulness skills (observe, describe, participate; nonjudgmentally, one-mindfully, effectively) come directly from that Zen tradition. DBT's four skill modules are mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness, so the source of core mindfulness is Zen Buddhism.","traps":{"a":"Stoicism influenced cognitive therapy's view that beliefs shape distress, but it is not the source of DBT's mindfulness skills.","c":"Psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) contributed concepts of the unconscious and defenses, unrelated to DBT's mindfulness practices.","d":"Existentialism informs meaning-focused therapies like logotherapy (Viktor Frankl), not the Zen-derived mindfulness training in DBT."}},{"q":"Borderline Personality Disorder is most characteristically associated with which primary defense mechanism?","concept":"Borderline Personality Disorder is characterized in object-relations theory (Otto Kernberg, building on Melanie Klein) by splitting, the failure to integrate good and bad images of self and others. People are experienced as all-good or all-bad, and these views flip rapidly, driving the idealization-devaluation cycle in relationships. So splitting is the defense most characteristic of BPD.","traps":{"a":"Repression, keeping unacceptable material out of awareness, is a broad neurotic-level defense central to psychoanalysis (Sigmund Freud) generally, not the hallmark of BPD.","c":"Sublimation, channeling drives into socially valued activity, is a mature adaptive defense, not the primitive defense typical of borderline organization.","d":"Rationalization, offering plausible justifications for behavior, is a neurotic-level defense and not the signature mechanism of BPD."}},{"q":"Affirmative therapy is specifically designed to support which population of clients?","concept":"Affirmative therapy is specifically designed to support LGBTQ+ clients. It validates non-heteronormative sexual orientations and gender identities, addresses minority stress arising from stigma and discrimination, and explicitly rejects conversion or reparative therapy. Because its defining aim is affirming these identities, the correct population is LGBTQ+ individuals.","traps":{"a":"Individuals with anxiety disorders are treated with modalities like CBT or exposure; affirmative therapy is defined by the population it serves, not by an anxiety focus.","b":"Elderly clients with cognitive decline are served by geriatric and cognitive interventions, not by the identity-affirming framework of affirmative therapy.","d":"Children with developmental delays receive developmental and behavioral services, which is unrelated to the LGBTQ+ focus of affirmative therapy."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] The bystander effect, where the presence of others inhibits helping, is primarily explained by two factors:","concept":"The bystander effect, that people are less likely to help when others are present, was explained by Bibb Latane and John Darley through two main mechanisms. Diffusion of responsibility means each onlooker assumes someone else will act, so personal obligation is diluted. Pluralistic ignorance means each person reads others' apparent calm as a sign that nothing is wrong. Together these give option b.","traps":{"a":"Social loafing (reduced effort in groups) and deindividuation (loss of self-awareness in crowds) are real group phenomena but are not the standard explanation for bystander non-intervention.","c":"Social comparison (Leon Festinger, 1954) and normative influence concern how we evaluate ourselves and conform to be liked, not the specific dynamics of emergency helping.","d":"Group polarization (Serge Moscovici and Marisa Zavalloni, 1969) and groupthink (Irving Janis, 1972) describe how group discussion shifts and distorts decisions, which is unrelated to why individual bystanders fail to help."}},{"q":"The classic 'bystander apathy' effect was demonstrated in the laboratory following the real-life murder of:","concept":"The laboratory study of bystander apathy was prompted by the 1964 murder of Kitty Genovese in Queens, New York, reportedly witnessed by many neighbors who did not intervene. Although the original claim of 38 unresponsive witnesses has since been historically disputed, the case still motivated Latane and Darley's foundational research on bystander intervention. So the answer is Kitty Genovese.","traps":{"a":"Martin Luther King Jr. was assassinated in 1968, a politically motivated killing unconnected to bystander-effect research.","c":"Emmett Till's 1955 murder was a landmark in civil-rights history, not the case that inspired bystander-intervention experiments.","d":"Malala Yousafzai survived a 2012 attack and is an education activist, entirely unrelated to the origins of bystander research."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] The 'just-world hypothesis' explains victim-blaming as a result of:","concept":"The just-world hypothesis, developed by Melvin Lerner, is the belief that the world is orderly and fair so that people get what they deserve and deserve what they get. When an innocent victim's suffering threatens this comforting belief, observers often restore it by derogating or blaming the victim rather than accepting that bad things happen randomly. So victim-blaming stems from the need to believe the world is fair, option b.","traps":{"a":"Attributing victim-blaming to inherent sadism misreads the motive; the theory says it comes from a need for a predictable, fair world, not a wish to see suffering.","c":"Attributing behavior to dispositional factors describes the fundamental attribution error (Lee Ross, 1977), a related but distinct bias, not the just-world motive.","d":"Comparing oneself with a less fortunate other is downward social comparison, a self-esteem mechanism, not the fairness belief behind just-world reasoning."}},{"q":"The phenomenon where qualified individuals (especially women / minorities) are blocked from advancing to higher career positions by an invisible barrier is called:","concept":"The glass ceiling is the invisible, often informal barrier that keeps qualified people, especially women and minorities, from rising into top organizational positions despite their competence. The term was popularized in a 1986 Wall Street Journal article by Carol Hymowitz and Timothy Schellhardt. Because the question describes an unseen barrier to advancement, the answer is the glass ceiling effect.","traps":{"a":"The Matthew effect is the accumulation of advantage in which the already successful gain more, not a barrier blocking advancement.","c":"The halo effect (Edward Thorndike, 1920) is a bias where one positive impression colors judgments of other traits, unrelated to career-advancement barriers.","d":"The Pygmalion effect (Robert Rosenthal and Lenore Jacobson, 1968) is the self-fulfilling prophecy by which higher expectations boost performance, the opposite of a barrier holding people back."}},{"q":"Learned helplessness, in social learning theory, refers to:","concept":"Learned helplessness, demonstrated by Martin Seligman and Steven Maier (1967), is the passivity that develops after exposure to uncontrollable aversive events. Dogs given inescapable shocks later failed to escape shocks they could have avoided, having learned that responding was futile. The reformulated model (Lyn Abramson and colleagues) linked it to a depressogenic attributional style (internal, stable, global). So the correct description is repeated unavoidable aversive events leading to later passivity, option c.","traps":{"a":"Performing a behavior to escape an aversive stimulus is escape (negative reinforcement) learning, effectively the opposite of the helpless failure to escape.","b":"Observing a model rewarded for aggression describes Bandura's observational learning, not the effect of uncontrollable aversive events.","d":"Failing to receive reinforcement for a previously learned behavior describes extinction, which is not the same as helplessness produced by uncontrollability."}},{"q":"[Repeated 2023, 2024] The 'Type D' personality, identified as a risk factor for poor cardiovascular prognosis, is characterized by:","concept":"The Type D ('distressed') personality, identified by Johan Denollet, combines two stable traits: high negative affectivity (a tendency to experience worry, gloom, and irritability) and high social inhibition (suppressing the expression of these emotions in social settings). This combination roughly doubles the risk of cardiac morbidity and mortality independent of disease severity. So the correct answer is negative affectivity plus social inhibition, option b.","traps":{"a":"Hostility and competitiveness describe the Type A behavior pattern (Friedman and Rosenman), a different cardiovascular risk profile, not Type D.","c":"An optimistic, resilient outlook is protective, the opposite of the distressed Type D constellation.","d":"Perfectionism and rigidity are personality features, but they do not define the specific two-factor Type D pattern of negative affect and social inhibition."}},{"q":"The 'halo effect' is a cognitive bias in which:","concept":"The halo effect, first documented by Edward Thorndike in 1920, is a cognitive bias in which a global impression of a person spills over into ratings of their specific, unrelated traits. For example, someone judged attractive is often assumed to be more competent or kinder. Because the described bias is an overall impression coloring specific trait judgments, the answer is option a.","traps":{"b":"Taking credit for success while blaming external factors for failure is the self-serving bias, an attributional bias, not the halo effect.","c":"Overestimating how much others share one's opinions is the false consensus effect (Lee Ross and colleagues, 1977), a different social-perception error.","d":"Letting an initial impression anchor later judgments describes anchoring or the primacy effect, related but distinct from the trait-spillover of the halo effect."}},{"q":"The 'looking-glass self,' introduced by Charles Horton Cooley, posits that our self-concept is primarily formed by:","concept":"Charles Horton Cooley's looking-glass self (1902) proposes that our self-concept is socially constructed from how we believe others see us. It has three steps: we imagine how we appear to another person, we imagine that person's judgment of that appearance, and we react with a self-feeling such as pride or shame. So self-concept is formed by imagining how we appear to others and how they judge us, option c.","traps":{"a":"Innate unconscious drives shaping the self is a psychoanalytic (Freudian) view, not Cooley's socially reflected self.","b":"Comparing ourselves with similar others is Leon Festinger's social comparison theory, a different account of self-evaluation.","d":"Resolving psychosocial crises at each stage is Erik Erikson's developmental theory, not Cooley's looking-glass self."}},{"q":"In social psychology, the concept of reactance refers to which of the following phenomena?","concept":"Reactance, described in Jack Brehm's reactance theory, is the motivational arousal that arises when a person perceives that a specific freedom is threatened or eliminated. The restricted option becomes more attractive, and the person is driven to reassert the lost freedom, which explains backlash against bans and paradoxical failures of persuasion. So reactance is increased motivation to perform a behavior when freedom to do it is threatened, option b.","traps":{"a":"Complying with authority figures is obedience (studied by Stanley Milgram), essentially the opposite of the defiance seen in reactance.","c":"Changing one's attitude to match the majority is conformity, a distinct social-influence process, not reactance.","d":"Evaluating one's abilities by comparison with others is social comparison (Leon Festinger, 1954), unrelated to freedom-threat and reactance."}},{"q":"The sociological concept of anomie, as introduced by Émile Durkheim, refers to which of the following?","concept":"Anomie, introduced by Emile Durkheim, refers to a state of normlessness in which social norms become unclear, weak, or inapplicable, often during rapid social change. In this condition the usual regulation of behavior breaks down, increasing deviance and, in Durkheim's analysis, suicide. Robert Merton later extended the idea into strain theory (a gap between cultural goals and legitimate means). So anomie is a state of normlessness, option a.","traps":{"b":"The progression from traditional to modern societies is a broad theme of social evolution and modernization, not the meaning of anomie.","c":"Adapting to the values of a dominant culture is acculturation or assimilation, a different sociological process.","d":"Internalizing societal norms and values describes socialization, which is essentially the opposite of the normlessness that anomie names."}},{"q":"In Baddeley's model of working memory, the subsystem responsible for integrating information from the phonological loop, visuospatial sketchpad, and long-term memory into a single coherent episode is the:","concept":"In Alan Baddeley's model of working memory, the episodic buffer (added in 2000) is the component that integrates information from the phonological loop, the visuospatial sketchpad, and long-term memory into a single, coherent, multimodal episode. It is a limited-capacity temporary store controlled by the central executive. Because the question asks about binding across subsystems, the answer is the episodic buffer.","traps":{"a":"The central executive is the attentional control system that allocates resources and coordinates the subsystems, but it is not itself the storage buffer that binds them into an episode.","c":"The visuospatial sketchpad holds visual and spatial information only, so it is one of the slave systems being integrated, not the integrator.","d":"The phonological loop stores and rehearses verbal and acoustic information, again a slave system feeding into the buffer rather than the binding store."}},{"q":"The correct sequence of the four stages of creative thinking, as proposed by Graham Wallas, is:","concept":"Graham Wallas, in The Art of Thought (1926), described four stages of the creative process in this order: Preparation (gathering information and defining the problem), Incubation (unconscious processing while attention is elsewhere), Illumination (the sudden 'aha' insight), and Verification (testing and refining the idea). So the correct sequence is Preparation, Incubation, Illumination, Verification, option c.","traps":{"a":"This places Illumination before Incubation, which is impossible since insight follows the unconscious incubation period.","b":"This starts with Incubation before Preparation, but one cannot incubate before gathering and defining the problem in the preparation stage.","d":"This begins with Illumination and ends with Incubation, completely reversing the logical flow of the creative process."}},{"q":"According to Albert Bandura, which of the following is NOT a necessary component process for observational learning to occur?","concept":"Albert Bandura's model of observational (social) learning specifies four necessary sub-processes: Attention to the model, Retention of what was observed, Reproduction (motor reproduction) of the behavior, and Motivation to perform it. Crucially, direct external reinforcement is not required; vicarious reinforcement (seeing the model rewarded) can suffice. This distinguished Bandura from Skinner, so the component that is NOT necessary is reinforcement, option c.","traps":{"a":"Attention is one of Bandura's four essential processes; without attending to the model, learning cannot begin, so it is required.","b":"Motivation is a genuine component; it determines whether the learned behavior is actually performed, so it is necessary rather than the exception.","d":"Reproduction (motor reproduction) is required because the learner must be able to enact the observed behavior, so it belongs to the model."}},{"q":"The backward digit span test, where participants must repeat a sequence of numbers in reverse order, is primarily used to assess which type of memory system?","concept":"Digit span comes in two forms. Forward digit span, simply holding and repeating a sequence, taps short-term storage (the phonological loop). Backward digit span requires holding the numbers while mentally reversing their order, which demands active manipulation of stored information. That manipulation engages the central executive (Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch, 1974), making backward digit span a measure of working memory, option b.","traps":{"a":"Long-term memory stores information over minutes to years; the brief, immediate reversal task of backward digit span does not tap it.","c":"Sensory memory is a very brief, high-capacity trace (iconic or echoic) that fades in under a second, not the manipulated span being tested.","d":"Procedural memory is implicit memory (Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter, 1985) for skills and habits, unrelated to the conscious manipulation of a digit sequence."}},{"q":"In psychoanalytic theory, what term describes the mental representation of psychic energy being invested or attached to a particular object, person, or idea?","concept":"In Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic theory, cathexis (German Besetzung) is the investment or attachment of psychic energy (libido) onto a mental representation of an object, person, or idea. The withdrawal of that energy is decathexis. Because the question asks for the term describing psychic energy invested in an object, the answer is cathexis.","traps":{"a":"Libido is the psychic energy itself (the drive energy), not the process of attaching that energy to a particular object.","c":"Fixation is the arrest of development at an earlier psychosexual stage due to over- or under-gratification, not the investment of energy in an object.","d":"Sublimation is a defense mechanism that redirects unacceptable drives into socially acceptable outlets, not the concept of energy attachment."}},{"q":"'Crystallized intelligence' is best defined as:","concept":"In the Cattell-Horn-Carroll model, Raymond Cattell distinguished two broad intelligences: crystallized intelligence (Gc) is the store of accumulated factual knowledge, vocabulary, and verbal skills built up through education and life experience, and it tends to rise or stay stable across the lifespan. Fluid intelligence (Gf) is the capacity to reason and solve novel problems independent of prior learning, and it peaks in early adulthood then declines. Option c captures Gc because it names acquired knowledge and verbal skill derived from experience, which is the defining feature of crystallized intelligence.","traps":{"a":"Solving novel problems is the definition of fluid intelligence (Gf), the reasoning ability that is independent of accumulated knowledge, so it describes the opposite construct.","b":"Speed of neural processing refers to mental or processing speed (Gs), a distinct CHC ability sometimes underlying Gf, not the stored knowledge that defines crystallized intelligence.","d":"The capacity of working memory is short-term/working memory (Gsm), a separate cognitive resource for holding and manipulating information, not the experiential knowledge base of Gc."}},{"q":"Which personality inventory is directly based on Carl Jung's theory of psychological types?","concept":"The Myers-Briggs Type Indicator was developed by Katharine Cook Briggs and Isabel Briggs Myers directly from Carl Gustav Jung's theory of psychological types. It sorts people on four dichotomies (Extraversion/Introversion, Sensing/Intuition, Thinking/Feeling, Judging/Perceiving) to yield sixteen types, operationalizing Jung's attitudes and functions. Option b is correct because among these inventories only the MBTI was built explicitly on Jungian typology.","traps":{"a":"The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) is an empirically keyed clinical psychopathology inventory, not a measure derived from Jung's type theory.","c":"The 16PF was developed by Raymond Cattell using factor analysis of trait descriptors, an empirical/statistical origin rather than Jungian typology.","d":"The NEO-PI-R by Costa and McCrae measures the Big Five (Five-Factor Model) traits, a lexical/dimensional framework unrelated to Jung's types."}},{"q":"According to Aaron Beck's cognitive theory of depression, the pattern of negative thoughts about self, world, and future is known as:","concept":"Aaron Temkin Beck's cognitive theory of depression proposes that depressed individuals hold pervasive negative views across three domains: the self ('I am worthless'), the world/current experience ('everything is against me'), and the future ('nothing will improve'). Beck named this pattern the negative cognitive triad, and it is fueled by underlying schemas and surfaces as automatic thoughts. Option b is correct because the triad specifically refers to negative cognition about self, world, and future.","traps":{"a":"Cognitive distortions are the faulty processing styles (for example overgeneralization, catastrophizing) that bias thinking, not the three-domain content pattern named by the triad.","c":"Automatic thoughts are the involuntary, situation-specific negative thoughts that pop up in the moment; they are a product of the triad, not the triad itself.","d":"Dysfunctional assumptions are the deeper conditional rules or intermediate beliefs ('If I fail, I am worthless') beneath automatic thoughts, distinct from the self-world-future triad."}},{"q":"The phonological loop, a component of Baddeley's model of working memory, is primarily responsible for:","concept":"In Alan Baddeley and Graham Hitch's working memory model, the phonological loop is the subsystem that handles speech-based and verbal information. It has two parts: a phonological store that briefly holds sound-based traces (about two seconds) and an articulatory rehearsal process that refreshes them by subvocal repetition. Option b is correct because storing and rehearsing auditory/verbal material is precisely the loop's function, supported by the word-length effect, articulatory suppression, and phonological similarity effect.","traps":{"a":"Visual and spatial processing is the job of the visuospatial sketchpad, the separate slave system for imagery and spatial location, not the phonological loop.","c":"Coordinating and integrating information from different sources describes the central executive (and the later episodic buffer), the attentional controller rather than the verbal store.","d":"Managing long-term memory retrieval is not the loop's role; the episodic buffer, added later, links working memory to long-term memory."}},{"q":"Weber's Law is best described by which of the following statements?","concept":"Weber's Law, from Ernst Heinrich Weber, states that the just-noticeable difference (JND) between two stimuli is a constant proportion of the original stimulus intensity, expressed as the Weber fraction delta-I over I equals k. So a heavier baseline weight or brighter baseline light requires a proportionally larger change to be detected. Option b is correct because it states this proportional (constant-fraction) relationship; Gustav Fechner later extended it into a logarithmic law relating sensation to intensity.","traps":{"a":"A fixed absolute JND for any stimulus is exactly what Weber's Law denies; the JND grows with stimulus magnitude rather than staying a constant amount.","c":"Detection depending solely on exposure duration is a temporal-summation idea, not Weber's Law, which concerns the ratio of intensity change to baseline intensity.","d":"Claiming perception is unaffected by intensity changes contradicts the whole premise of difference thresholds and Weber's proportional relationship."}},{"q":"Wolpe's systematic desensitisation pairs graded exposure to a feared stimulus with deep muscle relaxation. It is based on the principle of:","concept":"Joseph Wolpe's systematic desensitisation is grounded in the principle of reciprocal inhibition: a response that is physiologically incompatible with anxiety, deep muscle relaxation, is paired with a graded hierarchy of feared stimuli so that relaxation displaces and inhibits the anxiety response. Because relaxation and anxiety cannot occur simultaneously, repeated pairing weakens the conditioned fear. Option b is correct because reciprocal inhibition is the explicit mechanism Wolpe proposed.","traps":{"a":"Operant extinction involves withholding reinforcement to reduce a behavior; systematic desensitisation works by counter-conditioning with an incompatible response, not by removing a reinforcer.","c":"Aversive conditioning pairs an unwanted behavior with an unpleasant stimulus to suppress it, the opposite aim of desensitisation, which reduces anxiety through relaxation.","d":"Flooding is a competing exposure technique using intense, ungraded exposure without relaxation, so it relies on habituation/extinction rather than reciprocal inhibition."}},{"q":"The presence of two or more distinct personality states together with recurrent gaps in autobiographical recall is the core feature of:","concept":"Dissociative identity disorder (DID) is defined by the presence of two or more distinct personality (identity) states, each with its own pattern of experiencing, together with recurrent gaps in the recall of everyday events and personal information that exceed ordinary forgetting. In both the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual (DSM-5) and ICD-11, the combination of distinct identities plus dissociative amnesia is the core. Option c is correct because only DID pairs distinct personality states with autobiographical memory gaps.","traps":{"a":"Depersonalisation/derealisation disorder involves feelings of detachment from oneself or unreality of surroundings while reality testing stays intact, without distinct identities or amnesia.","b":"Dissociative amnesia features the memory gaps but lacks the two or more distinct personality states that define DID, so it captures only half the described picture.","d":"Schizophrenia is a psychotic disorder marked by hallucinations, delusions, and disorganization, not a dissociative disorder with alternating identities and autobiographical amnesia."}},{"q":"The extrapyramidal side effects (dystonia, parkinsonism) of typical antipsychotics arise mainly from dopamine D2 blockade in which pathway?","concept":"Typical (first-generation) antipsychotics block dopamine D2 receptors throughout the brain. Extrapyramidal side effects such as acute dystonia, drug-induced parkinsonism, akathisia, and tardive dyskinesia arise from D2 blockade in the nigrostriatal pathway, which normally regulates motor control. Option d is correct because the nigrostriatal (substantia nigra to striatum) dopamine route governs movement, so its blockade produces EPS.","traps":{"a":"The mesolimbic pathway's D2 blockade reduces the positive symptoms of psychosis (hallucinations, delusions); it is the therapeutic target, not the source of movement side effects.","b":"The mesocortical pathway, when blocked, tends to worsen negative and cognitive symptoms; it does not cause the dystonia and parkinsonism of EPS.","c":"Tuberoinfundibular D2 blockade removes dopamine's inhibition of prolactin, causing hyperprolactinaemia (galactorrhoea, menstrual changes), not extrapyramidal motor effects."}},{"q":"The Vineland Adaptive Behaviour Scales are primarily used to assess:","concept":"The Vineland Adaptive Behaviour Scales, developed from Edgar Doll's work, assess adaptive functioning: the everyday practical skills a person actually performs across communication, daily living skills, socialisation, and motor skills. They are administered via informant interview and are central to diagnosing intellectual disability, where deficits in adaptive behaviour must accompany low IQ. Option b is correct because the Vineland measures adaptive functioning in daily living rather than any single cognitive ability.","traps":{"a":"Intelligence quotient (William Stern, 1912) is measured by IQ tests like the Wechsler or Stanford-Binet scales; the Vineland complements these but does not itself yield an IQ.","c":"Personality structure is assessed by inventories or projective tests (for example the MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943) or Rorschach), not by an adaptive behaviour scale.","d":"Neuropsychological memory deficits are evaluated with memory batteries (for example the Wechsler Memory Scale); the Vineland targets real-world adaptive skills, not memory."}},{"q":"Which sampling method gives every member of the population a known, non-zero probability of being selected?","concept":"Probability (random) sampling is defined by the fact that every member of the population has a known, non-zero chance of being selected, which is what allows the sample to be representative and sampling error to be estimated. Its forms include simple random, stratified, cluster, and systematic sampling. Option c is correct because only probability sampling guarantees a known non-zero selection probability for each unit.","traps":{"a":"Quota sampling fills preset category targets using the researcher's judgment, so selection probabilities are unknown; it is a non-probability method.","b":"Snowball sampling recruits participants through referrals from existing subjects, giving unknown and unequal selection chances, typical of hard-to-reach populations.","d":"Purposive sampling deliberately selects specific units based on the researcher's judgment of relevance, so members have no known probability of inclusion."}}]});
